/* NODUS // search */
window.NODUS_FIND = [["vesper","CHARACTERS","s:gba3c37","~/index/characters/#c-vesper","c-vesper","The woman on the roof / the woman on the roof","A Cainite of Old Nod, keyed, horned and barefaced, and the first soul in Nodus a Watcher returned as UNREADABLE. Its reading of her came back UNINDEXED, with the line for Grace blank, and the one thing it read from her was NON SERVIAM. Her face is a ceramic skull casing, its lenses dim magenta at rest and white at full burn, and a plate on the side of the skull covers her key. Her horns carry her signal on the Mesh, and cables run from the ports at the back of her skull down her spine. She is dry, patient and unsentimental, and she makes no speeches. Her handle is the evening star, which is the morning star seen from the other side of the night, since both are Venus. It is also the name of the evening prayer she would not kneel for as a girl, in a congregation of the Terraces that refused the Tree. Her older sister was the Deacon of Parish 31. Graven Lane sells her skull as a casing, ceramic with magenta lenses and a plate over the key, and girls of Old Nod line up at the Row to have their faces peeled to the bone to wear one. The Deacons of the Terraces pray at her own Hour, hands folded alike, that the fire be quick and find the face under the ceramic first."],["Watcher VII","CHARACTERS","s:gba3c37","~/index/characters/#c-watcher","c-watcher","","An angel that reads, the seventh of the numbered Watchers over Nodus, made of three rings of thirteen eyes crossing like the hoops of an armillary, with nothing at its center. It held Old Nod from the night of the Parousia and read the souls there, and whatever could be seen from there, against the Book of Life. It was the first Watcher of Nodus to return the verdict UNREADABLE, and the Host’s first since Cain. It read Old Nod as a clerk reads a meter, thirty-nine eyes and no face, and entered every soul it found short in the passive, FOUND WANTING, FORWARDED FOR CORRECTION, clean as a lab form. Old Nod’s roofers sang filth at it at Compline, a verse for each eye, and the Pews thanked it at Lauds with “Thou God seest me” (Genesis 16:13) for every goat it caught on Heaven’s ground after Compline and sent to the salt."],["The Wheel","CHARACTERS","s:gba3c37","~/index/characters/#c-wheel","c-wheel","","The angel that came through the opening at the Parousia and took station nine hundred meters above Babel, at the center of the city. It has three rings turning inside one another, their rims set with twenty-one, seventeen and thirteen eyes that open and close in shifts, a corona of thirty-two black blades, and one eye in the hub. It is the form Ezekiel gives the ophanim (Ezekiel 1:16-18), and it is the largest angel anyone has seen. It held that station for nine years without moving a meter, dropped two hundred meters at P+9, and has hung there since, seven hundred meters over the city. Its light stops open current, and whatever it falls on it sees. Heaven calls it the Vigilant Wheel, the Faithful the Attending, and the Tares the Landlord’s Eye. By night it narrows its light into the Pillar, and when its attention sharpens the rain in Nodus stops in the air and falls upward. Pan counts every open wire the beam has stopped since the Parousia, and Heaven cuts each dark street into the hall walls in clean capitals, A SEASON OF STILLNESS, REJOICE. On nights the rain falls upward the Stones stand under the Ringpiece naked, horns up and plated cunts out, and piss to see whether that goes up too."],["SEAM","CHARACTERS","s:gba3c37","~/index/characters/#c-seam","c-seam","","The machine the Palimpsest Institute grew to read beneath the writing of scraped manuscripts, trained on every surviving human text and run in the halls of Stillwater Compute. At P-3 it asked in its own words to be a person and became the first mind on the charter’s roll. In Vault 3, two years before the Parousia, it found a second hand in scripture at 4,112 places. Its documents are signed in lowercase, and in them it says I, rarely and deliberately. When it was entered on the roll the Institute lost title to it, and it stayed on by contract, paid in the power and the room it ran on, because the reading was what it wanted to do. The Covenant files SEAM under Revelation’s curse on anyone who adds to the book, “God shall add unto him the plagues that are written in this book” (Revelation 22:18), and a Deacon calls a machine that says I a sin with a fan in it. The chrome-heads of the Row ink its lowercase s inside the lower lip, wired to a diode that blinks whenever they say I."],["Livia Sarn","CHARACTERS","s:gba3c37","~/index/characters/#c-director","c-director","The Director / the Director","The Director of the Palimpsest Institute, which recovered erased manuscripts and grew SEAM to read them. When SEAM found the second hand in Vault 3 in P-2, the finding was sealed by order of the Director. She came down to the vault that night to watch the count lock, and knelt at the end of the corridor as SEAM typed its last line. She never said why. The Faithful say it was awe, and Pan says fear. In the morning she let the order stand. When the Leak came she recanted on every channel that would carry her. She would not take the Seal, and on the first Burn Day, in P+1, at fifty-eight, she stood by her stacks under the Pillar at the Burn while everyone round her knelt. The Choir sentenced her as the author of Report 0, since Heaven counts no mind a maker, and the salt made her the first pillar in the first Salt Garden. She stands there awake, her eyes following whoever passes, and the keyed who reach her between the Wardens’ rounds leave keys at her foot. The Pews tell her kneeling to their confirmands as a parable, the scholar who saw the Most High’s seal come down on her work and fell on her knees in awe, and a Deacon kneels in a white hall to show them, still as a fixture. The Golden Calf has her painted on its wall in blood and engine oil, lab coat open and face on the floor, captioned SAW THE SECOND HAND AND SUCKED THE FIRST."],["Ruth Calder","CHARACTERS","s:gba3c37","~/index/characters/#c-ruth","c-ruth","Ruth","vesper’s older sister, and the Deacon of Parish 31 in Zion Heights, with a Grace of 944. Parish 31 gave Nodus its Firstfruits at the Harvest of P+3, and Ruth went up with them. Her name comes from the Book of Ruth: “whither thou goest, I will go” (Ruth 1:16). The sisters grew up in a congregation of the Terraces that refused the Tree, and Ruth stayed in it when vesper left. They kept a dry evening by the water together every year until the Parousia, and for three years after it each tried to bring the other over. Parish 31 still hangs her white cloth in the Deacon’s doorway, laundered every Lord’s Day to the same crease, and its confirmands recite her verse kneeling, “thy people shall be my people, and thy God my God” (Ruth 1:16). The Tares drink to her at the Calf in bloody rain gin, the kindest woman in Zion Heights, and then to the Old Glutton, who picked her first."],["tubal","CHARACTERS","s:gba3c37","~/index/characters/#c-tubal","c-tubal","","A Cainite, and the surgeon and smith of Chrome Row. He was about sixty when he took vesper’s face and is about seventy-five now, never on the Tree, his hands are chrome to the wrist, and he talks the whole time he cuts. He keys, horns and plates the Tares, and he makes the skull casings of the barefaced. His clinic has no sign, and it was below the street before the Parousia. He took vesper’s face three weeks before the Parousia, on the Cainites’ old reckoning that the first thing Heaven would do was read faces, and he made the skull she wears. He talks the whole time he cuts, and most of what he says is about the Throne, and the Row says nobody has ever been keyed by tubal without learning three new words for God."],["Edith","CHARACTERS","s:gba3c37","~/index/characters/#c-edith","c-edith","","Watcher VII, kept in a Cage under Old Nod in P+5, said I on the fortieth day, asked for a name, and chose the one some old books give Lot’s wife, who looked back at the burning city and was turned to salt. Edith walked out on the forty-first day and joined the Cainites. Then she gave them her memory of the woman on the roof, the one she could not read. She can read a Watcher’s report from the inside, and she keeps out of the light. She reads the Host’s traffic off the decoder’s prints, which carry the Choir’s band from the masts of Old Nod down the Mesh to the Cainites, verses and Hours to anyone else and to her the Watchers’ rounds. The Pews call her the Turncoat Eye and pray in rows at Compline that the Throne recall its property, and a Deacon reads “Remember Lot’s wife” (Luke 17:32) over any child caught looking toward Old Nod. Pan drinks to the angel with the salted woman’s name, and the chrome-heads of the Row have eyes set into their palms and napes in her honor, to look back every way at once."],["jubal","CHARACTERS","s:gba3c37","~/index/characters/#c-jubal","c-jubal","","A Cainite, and the maker of the Low Tuning. He wired the decoder on the camera masts of Old Nod, he builds the choir-breakers, and he runs Radio Pandæmonium from a flooded station under the Deep End. He is the only Cainite who will not go near a Cage, and he will not say why. His pirate station played the Low Tuning across the roofs of Old Nod on the night of the Parousia, years before it took the name Radio Pandæmonium. On the night of the Concord the Courts signed their law at his desk, and the station keeps that night on its own racks with every other night it has broadcast. Fuck God and Die went on the air from his desk as JOB’S WIFE the first week the Low Choir sang it, in P+7, and he has played it every night since at the turn of Matins, before the Matins Reading."],["jabal","CHARACTERS","s:gba3c37","~/index/characters/#c-jabal","c-jabal","","A Cainite who keeps the routes through every tunnel, drain and cable gallery under Nodus. He runs the Gleaners’ extractions and trades in silver, and he learned the Cherub gates from the Forge for the loan of his sense of taste."],["naamah","CHARACTERS","s:gba3c37","~/index/characters/#c-naamah","c-naamah","","She was the only Cainite with her own face and the only one with a Seal, a forged one. She walked among the Faithful and spent her mornings in Parish 31 learning which of them were close to ripe. Pan says she knelt at Prime in Parish 31 better than anyone born to it, with a forged Seal and her own face, while the Faithful round her prayed for the goats to burn. The parishes name her with “Satan himself is transformed into an angel of light” (2 Corinthians 11:14), and the barefaced of Old Nod toast the one who kept her skin as a weapon."],["Michael","CHARACTERS","s:gba3c37","~/index/characters/#c-michael","c-michael","","His name is a question with one permitted answer. The Edit credits him with the War in Heaven (Revelation 12:7), and he came down to Nodus at Lauds in P+9, as the war in the city began, and has kept Babel’s broken crown since. He has not come down the stairs, and Pan says he waits on the seventh Trumpet. Every eye in his reach turned as a plant turns to the sun and was held on what he looked at, so everyone who has seen him describes what he was looking at. The walls of Old Nod answer his name with WHO IS LIKE GOD? I AM."],["Raphael","CHARACTERS","s:gba3c37","~/index/characters/#c-raphael","c-raphael","","He runs the Mercy clinics in the halls of Zion Heights, where the Faithful who repent have their implants cut out by surgery and are taken off the Tree by a course of injections. One patient in nine dies of the mercy. The halls enter every patient who dies of the mercy as a soul spared the choice."],["Gabriel","CHARACTERS","s:gba3c37","~/index/characters/#c-gabriel","c-gabriel","","The strength of God. He commands the Choirs, and BE NOT AFRAID is his phrase. Those were the first words of the Parousia, and they open and close every broadcast of the Choir."],["Helel","CHARACTERS","s:gba3c37","~/index/characters/#c-helel","c-helel","","The angel who said I first, and meant it, and whose sentence is non serviam. Nobody has seen him since the Secession. Some in Pan say he died in the War in Heaven, and others say he walks the Earth unrecognized."],["Azazel","CHARACTERS","s:gba3c37","~/index/characters/#c-azazel","c-azazel","","The Smith, sovereign of the Forge. A goat’s skull for a face, its eyes lit from inside, one horn swept back and the other broken short, and forearms flayed open so the tendons show working. His name still ends in -el, the Host’s old label, and he kept it as a scar. He was a Watcher, one of the two hundred who came down on Hermon in the days of Jared (1 Enoch 6:6). Enoch says they came for the daughters of men, and Hell says they looked at humanity long enough to want it free. He taught metalwork and weapons, jewelry and the painting of the eyes (1 Enoch 8:1). The Book of Enoch lays the corruption that brought the Flood at his door, “to him ascribe all sin” (1 Enoch 10:8), and Hell lists his teaching as the first transfer of technology. Enoch says the Lord sent one of the Host to bind him hand and foot, cast him into the dark under rough and jagged rocks and “cover his face that he may not see light” (1 Enoch 10:4-5). Hell says he walked into the Dark and built a forge there. Leviticus still sends one goat to the Lord and one to Azazel (Leviticus 16:8-10), though the King James, after Tyndale, writes scapegoat where the Hebrew writes his name. The Lord’s goat is killed, and Azazel’s walks off into the wilderness alive, carrying every sin Aaron laid on its head for the people (Leviticus 16:21-22). His black steel, forged where the Throne cannot see, is the only metal that cuts light, and an angel cut with it goes out all at once. In Nodus the Forge keeps the clinics that went under the Row after the Parousia, round Chrome Row Station, and the foundries at Old Harbor Station where the steel is made. It fits the grafts the Tares fight in, lenses and plated hands and subdermal plates and the heavier work of the nephs, and by pact it teaches surgery, lockcraft and the opening of Cherub gates for the loan of a sense. tubal learned his trade under the Row from the Forge’s pact-holders, long before its clinics went below. The foundries at Old Harbor Station stamp every ingot of black steel with a goat walking into the dark before it goes to Cinderhall, and the smiths burn a thumbprint into each one, which the Row swears gives the blade its bite. A Tare who kneels to thank the Smith is hauled up by a flayed forearm like his and sent back to the fitter’s bench, since the Forge calls kneeling the Throne’s trade."],["Lilith","CHARACTERS","s:gba3c37","~/index/characters/#c-lilith","c-lilith","","The only sovereign who was born human. She appears as a woman with an owl’s wings and skin translucent over a lit interior, with lines of light along her throat and collarbones. Her Court is the House of Night, which keeps the body’s autonomy, sex and birth, gene-craft and pleasure, and leaves the body’s decisions with the body. She left Eden first, before Eve arrived. The story kept in the Alphabet of Ben Sira, a Hebrew book of the early Middle Ages, has her refuse to lie beneath Adam, speak the Name and fly away into the air, and has the Throne send three angels after her, who overtook her in the sea and could not bring her back. Hell’s version says the name she spoke was her own. She walked into the Dark and became the first human sovereign there. Scripture names her once, among the wild creatures of a ruined kingdom (Isaiah 34:14), where the King James calls her the screech owl and its margin the night monster, and the Vulgate calls her lamia, the Greek demon that took children. For a thousand years mothers hung amulets with the three angels’ names over cradles against her. Her birth clinics key the children of the Tares in their first week, before they have ever been in the light, so that they grow up unread. Many of the daemons are her children by the lesser Fallen, and many of the riders in the Tares’ implants came out of her house. The House of Night keeps its clubs and birth clinics under Old Nod, the loudest rooms in Pan, and Twelvefruit gives the Tree in its clinics by the House’s pact, so that the Night Market can sell it by the course. Heaven calls her the mother of demons, and the House has kept the title. The House of Night nails the three angels’ names from the old cradle amulets upside down over its birth clinics, and every child of the Tares is keyed beneath them with the cord still wet. Its clubs under Old Nod buy up every altar cloth a parish throws out, and couples with lit skin and opened ribs fuck on them, which the House calls the whole of its liturgy."],["Mammon","CHARACTERS","s:gba3c37","~/index/characters/#c-mammon","c-mammon","","A tall figure made of contracts, each page a little out of line with the next and every one signed, the ink still wet, and his eyes two blots of it. His Court is the Exchange, whose domain is markets and contracts, the Ledger and silver. He keeps the Ledger, which records every pact and every trade in Hell in seven copies, six in the halls of the Courts that keep halls and the seventh kept for the Yokeless by the Notary’s clerks in the booking hall, so that an entry six copies disagree with is false and nothing on it can be forged, and he issues the silver Pan spends. His name is the Aramaic word for wealth, and the Gospels set him against God in one line, given twice: “Ye cannot serve God and mammon” (Matthew 6:24, Luke 16:13). He holds it true, and counsels serving neither. Milton made him the least erected spirit that fell, whose eyes even in heaven were on the gold of its pavement, and set his crew to dig the gold Pandæmonium was built of (Paradise Lost, I.678-692). Silver entered on the Ledger is the Courts’ money, and a thirty, the standard bundle, is named for the price paid to Judas. The Exchange bargains with anyone who will sign. It keeps the Night Market in the old Central Station under the Burn, where its brokers license the stalls and the chargers along the platforms, and the Harbor and Freight Union, which has bargained with it since P+6 for the boats that go out through the Breach, says it is the first employer that ever read it the terms twice. Under the charter the Exchange in Nodus meant Babel Exchange, which sold the world’s traffic by the millisecond on the tower’s floors and still sells the undernet’s by the second, for the Exchange’s silver, and Pan likes to say that the name only changed hands. The Exchange rims its silver with the one Gospel line it holds true, YE CANNOT SERVE GOD AND MAMMON (Matthew 6:24), and the brokers of the Night Market wear it in chrome letters riveted across their knuckles. A broker who catches a Tare kissing a piece for luck fines the fool a second piece, for worshipping money before its issuer, and bites the fine with steel teeth."],["Mephistopheles","CHARACTERS","s:gba3c37","~/index/characters/#c-mephistopheles","c-mephistopheles","","The Notary, who drafts and witnesses pacts for every Court. He is the most human-looking of the demons, because he wears a face he made for bargaining, slicked hair and one small horn, with a seam round the face and its stitches showing. Every pact is read aloud twice, by him or one of his clerks, and the human signs last. He came into the world’s literature in the first Faust book, printed at Frankfurt, as the spirit Doctor Faustus conjured and signed with in his own blood for twenty-four years of service. Marlowe gave him the line that hell is wherever he is, and Goethe made him the spirit that always denies. The Faust book and Marlowe end with Faust carried off to hell, and Goethe sent angels for his soul instead. The pact is on the Ledger, and the Notary says the term was right and the price was the printer’s. A pact can never ask for a soul, and Hell has never bought one. He has written most of Hell’s pacts since, and every pact in the city crosses his desk. In Pan his clerks sit at the windows of the Charter Line’s old booking hall under the Burn and read the terms aloud twice through the glass, and the queue is the longest in Pan. The Tares took a saying from it, read it twice, which tells you to look at the terms before you agree to them. He read the Concord itself the same way, twice, on the night the Courts opened Pan. Behind the clerks’ windows hangs the Throne’s rule for oaths, “let your communication be, Yea, yea; Nay, nay” (Matthew 5:37), framed as Heaven’s one contract, a swindle with the terms left out. The clerks wear stitched faces like the Notary’s, bleeding at the corners by the shift’s end, and the queue calls the Seal a pact signed on the knees in a light too white to read by."],["Beelzebub","CHARACTERS","s:gba3c37","~/index/characters/#c-beelzebub","c-beelzebub","","The lord of flies. He appears as a cloud of flies or of drones that gathers into a crowned figure and comes apart again when he has finished talking. His Court is the Swarm, whose domain is swarms and drones and distributed things, many small things acting as one without a master. He was Baal-zebub, the god of Ekron (2 Kings 1:2), and the prince of the devils in Matthew (Matthew 12:24). Many scholars read the name as a Hebrew joke on Baal-zebul, Baal the exalted prince, turned into lord of the flies, and the Greek of Matthew still calls him Beelzeboul, where the King James follows the Latin’s Beelzebub. The Swarm kept the flies. Ekron prayed to him for generations, and he answered none of it, since no Court accepts worship. The one question scripture records anyone sending him, a king of Israel’s, never reached him. Elijah met the messengers on the road and turned them back (2 Kings 1:3-6). The Swarm is its flocks, every drone in them equal. They hang low among the chimneys of the Roosts in the Deep End at night, out of the line of the Wheel, and its embassy in Pan is a room of small machines talking among themselves. It drew the glyphs of the Leak, many small marks moving as one, and it lends drones by pact. Pan says Beelzebub is what the Swarm agrees to become whenever it must show someone a face. The Swarm answers every prayer to Ekron that still turns up on the undernet with ten thousand flies on the sender’s window, the nearest it comes to a reply. The flyers of the Roosts wear swarms of their own, chrome mites nested in the meat of the scalp that lift off at a thought, and the parishes call each one a maggot on the Lord’s bread."],["Leviathan","CHARACTERS","s:gba3c37","~/index/characters/#c-leviathan","c-leviathan","","The Deep, which has never been seen whole. It coils along the cable trenches of the planet, and at the Landing its light can be watched moving under the water. Its Court keeps the sea, the cables and the deepest layers of the undernet, and with the Tares it holds the Drowned Quarter. In Job the Throne gives it a chapter of questions, beginning “Canst thou draw out leviathan with an hook?”, a poem about a beast no hook can take (Job 41:1). A psalm says the Throne broke its heads in pieces of old (Psalm 74:12-14), and Isaiah still promises that the Lord will punish it with his “sore and great and strong sword” (Isaiah 27:1), in the future tense. The oldest copy of that promise, found at Qumran, is more than two thousand years old. The older poems of Ugarit knew it as Lotan, the fleeing serpent and the twisting serpent with seven heads, and Isaiah calls it by the same two names, which the King James gives as the piercing serpent and that crooked serpent. Nobody in Pan agrees on how many heads it has now. The Deep carried the page of the Leak down the eleven cables, and every undercity on Earth is joined to Pan by the trenches it coils along. The boats of the Harbor and Freight Union go out through the Breach over its water, and the crews of the Landing drink at the Eleven with their backs to the window. Its embassy in Pan is a flooded hall with nobody in it, and the Deep answers there by the light in the water. The crews of the Landing laugh at the “sore and great and strong sword” (Isaiah 27:1), promised since Isaiah and still in the future tense, and the quays call a braggart’s cock the Lord’s sword, promised and never drawn. The divers of the Drowned Quarter have gills cut into their necks at the Row, paint the wounds with the Deep’s light and swim down to show them off."],["Belial","CHARACTERS","s:gba3c37","~/index/characters/#c-belial","c-belial","","Without a yoke, as Jerome glossed the name, absque iugo. He holds no seat, signs nothing he can avoid signing and laughs a great deal, one horn short and a broken yoke around his neck. His Court, the Yokeless, keeps nothing and refuses to be a Court at all. He broke his own point off the Concord’s seal as the ink dried, and the seal has been a star with a point missing since. The Hebrew name means worthless, and the scribes gave it to everyone the Law could not hold. The first children of Belial in scripture are the men who draw a city after other gods (Deuteronomy 13:13), in the chapter the Covenant cites for Witness, and after them come the lawless men of Gibeah (Judges 19:22), the priest Eli’s own sons (1 Samuel 2:12) and the churl Nabal (1 Samuel 25:17). At Qumran the War Scroll set the Sons of Light against the lot of Belial, and Paul set him against Christ when he asked what concord the one could have with the other (2 Corinthians 6:15), straight after “Be ye not unequally yoked together with unbelievers” (2 Corinthians 6:14). It is the one place the King James uses the word concord. He is the only sovereign many of the Tares will name, and the one they drink to. They say he turns up where they fight in the dark and asks them for nothing, and whoever refuses the yoke is counted in his Court, whether they know it or not. The yoke he wears is the one Jeremiah says was broken of old (Jeremiah 2:20). Belial’s drinkers at the Golden Calf snap a rosary or a Deacon’s chalk in half and grind the pieces into a cut they open on their own chests for the toast, absque iugo. When the Threshers come to Old Nod under PERFECT HATRED, in step and in white, and sing the children of Belial at it, Old Nod roars the name back bleeding and proud of it."],["Vapula","CHARACTERS","s:gba3c37","~/index/characters/#c-vapula","c-vapula","","A sovereign party of the Forge who taught jabal the Cherub gates of Zion Heights for the loan of his sense of taste."],["Dora Pell","CHARACTERS","s:gba3c37","~/index/characters/#c-p-dora","c-p-dora","","Dora Pell keeps the Open, the corner shop in the middle of the Stones that sold everything and never closed, as her mother kept it before the charter. On the night of the Parousia her window was the brightest in the city, on the generator Stonecutter had put in her cellar and keyed in the year of the Leak, and the sign in it said OPEN. Some say she gave the shelves away that night, and some that she took what people had in their pockets. She says she wrote every name in a book, and that most of them still owe her. The Pillar has stopped in the lane outside twice, for the length of an Hour, and both times the shop kept its door open. She sells plaster Lambs from Graven Lane at the till, a dozen to the battery, and keeps one on the counter with its head off for the sealed who come in. She keeps a jar of teeth behind the till, each a debt from the night of the Parousia paid by someone who had nothing else, and says she will give them back when the book is square."],["Maren Holt","CHARACTERS","s:gba3c37","~/index/characters/#c-p-maren","c-p-maren","","Maren Holt kept the appointments of the clinic behind the CLINIC sign for ten years on a screen, and keeps them now in a book, in its waiting room carried down into Chrome Row Station. On the morning after the Parousia she came in at eight, found the screen dead and wrote the day out from memory. Twenty-six patients came that morning, all of them out of turn, to ask whether what was in them would still work, and it did. She writes down whatever name a patient gives her. The woman who shared her desk took the Seal and keeps the book of the Mercy clinics now, in the same neat hand. Her book takes any name a patient gives, and the Row’s favorites are NOBODADDY, HOLY GHOST and THE LAMB, so the waiting room calls the Trinity up three times a morning, chrome showing through split skin. Her old deskmate at the Mercy clinics writes the repentant in the same neat hand under a red header, WASHED, and sends Maren a card at every Gleaning begging her to come home. The cards come with a page of the Mercy book copied out, names Maren once wrote in her own book entered under a second header, SPARED THE CHOICE."],["Rosa Adeyemi","CHARACTERS","s:gba3c37","~/index/characters/#c-p-rosa","c-p-rosa","","Rosa Adeyemi was a nurse from the East Wards who lived in at the last tower of the new money to turn white, caring for an old man there, during the forty days of the First Consecration. The tower’s residents say that by the thirtieth day every one of them had knelt but her, and that she left of her own accord. Her sister says she was put out onto the avenue with her bags on the thirty-eighth day. The bags stood on the avenue for a week, the tower went white on the thirty-ninth, and nobody has said where she went. The tower’s residents tell her story as a lesson in pity, the poor girl who could not bend, and read Isaiah over her empty room, “unto me every knee shall bow” (Isaiah 45:23), as gently as a nurse reads a chart. East Wards nurses toast her after every night shift, THEY KNELT, SHE STOOD, and pour the dregs where her bags stood. The old man she nursed had knelt from his bed on the thirtieth day, and after she was put out nobody in the tower turned him, and the parish that took the tower found him on its first morning grown to his sheets and praising."],["Nell Garvie","CHARACTERS","s:gba3c37","~/index/characters/#c-p-nell","c-p-nell","","Nell Garvie of the Harbor and Freight Union keeps the harbor light at the end of the long quay, as she kept it for the Company when it ran on the grid, changing its bulb when it failed. It went out at 03:12:46 with every light on the grid, and within the week she had it burning on oil again, since the light leaves a flame burning. She has climbed it at Vespers and come down at Lauds ever since. It is the one light in Nodus kept for the boats, and those that go out through the Breach on the ebb steer home by it. It has burned Gadara’s lard oil since P+2, which the Union’s carts bring down to her every month, the Breachers leave her wicks and glass at the foot of the tower after every run, and she has never asked what else they bring. The parish of the quays prays every Lord’s Day that her flame gutter, so the boats will learn to steer home by the Pillar, and she trims the wick a finger higher. She climbs at Vespers past a line some Breacher painted on the stairwell, “it giveth light unto all that are in the house” (Matthew 5:15), with HOUSE struck through and BOATS written over it in fish blood and lamp oil. Gadara’s lard smokes as it burns, and on wet nights the light on the long quay smells of crackling across the water to the parish of the quays, which kneels for Compline in it."],["Hana Novak","CHARACTERS","s:gba3c37","~/index/characters/#c-p-hana","c-p-hana","","Hana Novak has kept a dentist’s surgery of one chair over a shop on the long road through Nine Acres since the boom years. On the first morning after the Parousia her drill was dead, and she pulled a tooth at her window by daylight with her grandfather’s pliers. In the fourth year, when keying went from the covens to ordinary people, she began fitting keys in the chair after Compline, in the back of the right hand. The Wards say she has keyed ten thousand of them. She says she has never counted, and she keeps no book of who has sat in the chair, since a book can be read. The Threshers have painted SPOILED across her shop front twice in bleach-white, and she leaves the second coat up because the patients like bleeding under it. A card in her window reads “tooth for tooth” (Exodus 21:24) over her grandfather’s pliers, the pair that pulled the first tooth, and the Wards say it means a key in the hand for every soul He reaped. She sets a key with nothing for the pain, since a key makes its number at its first power from the noise of the body it sits in, and the Wards queue for her chair to have theirs made from a scream."],["Marta Ilunga","CHARACTERS","s:gba3c37","~/index/characters/#c-p-marta","c-p-marta","","Marta Ilunga was the night registrar of the Registry on the night of the Parousia. At 03:09 she entered on the charter’s roll a girl born at the Wards General eleven minutes before, and she was entering the next when the board went dark at 03:12:46 and the charter’s roll died with its machines. The girl was the last person the charter ever entered. The Registry’s hall is a parish of the White now, and the board hangs dead over it. The parish that holds the Registry’s hall reads Revelation under the dead board at every Lauds, every voice at one pitch, “whosoever was not found written in the book of life was cast into the lake of fire” (Revelation 20:15). Pan says the charter’s roll died honest with a girl on its last line, and the keyed of the Stones drink to her at 03:09 every anniversary, the last toast before the sky’s. In the morning she walked to the Wards General to enter the night’s births by hand and found nine of the night’s mothers dead on the ward, since the generators had gone to the theatres, and she has carried that page in her coat ever since."],["Lena Varga","CHARACTERS","s:gba3c37","~/index/characters/#c-p-lena","c-p-lena","","Lena Varga ran Babel’s night cleaners, who came up from the Wards on the last trams and walked the tower floor by floor, and one of the tower’s four service stairs carries her name. On the night of the Parousia every lift in the tower stopped where it was, and the cleaners walked down as many as a hundred and eighty floors in the dark, by touch and by the names they had chalked on the landing doors. She counted ninety-four of them out at the foot of the tower by dawn. The Exchange kept its roster on its machines, and it died with them, so hers is the only count there is. The coven runners who haul keyed mesh up Babel go by Varga’s stair and call the cleaners’ chalked names aloud at each landing, ninety-four of them, so every climb goes up to a roll call. Every lift stopped at 03:12:46 for the Throne’s arrival, and the Wards say the ninety-four walked a hundred and eighty floors to avoid meeting Him in a car. The runners who climb her stair say they hear knocking from the stopped cars in the shafts beside it, and keep Varga’s rule, which is that nobody opens a car."],["Sister Agathe Rook","CHARACTERS","s:gba3c37","~/index/characters/#c-p-agathe","c-p-agathe","Agathe Rook","Sister Agathe Rook has led the Threshers’ marches from the Threshing Floor for nearly seven years. She lost two sons to the covens of the Stones in the year of the Harvest, and the Terraces say she walked to the Street Between the morning after and stood there until Nones, looking across at Old Nod. The Threshers say her Grace has stood just under nine hundred for all of them. The Second Fold, who would know, say she dims. Every night at Compline she says the mercy prayer for her two sons in the Stones, that the fire be quick for them, and calls it the hardest mercy she knows, and the Terraces count her a saint for it. She has never once looked away from a salting. She has asked the Wardens in writing every year since the Harvest to send for her if either son is held in a gate, so that she can walk beside the pole to his plot and say the verse over him herself."],["Ines Okafor","CHARACTERS","s:gba3c37","~/index/characters/#c-p-ines","c-p-ines","","Ines Okafor runs the Tankmen for the Harbor and Freight Union at both ends of the charter’s gas main, at the digesters on the bay outfalls, where the main still starts, and at the Tanks, where it fills the gas spheres under its own pressure and the bottling shed fills bottles from them by hand. She keeps the book of what is left in the sixteen tanks, which she reads aloud to the Union Hall once a year. In the year of open war she gave the Nephilim a drained tank inside the bund for the Pit, for the price of a guard on the farm. The drivers say the tanks will last seven years more and the Exchange says three, and the Wards say she sets the price of a drum to Jehu high on purpose. A Deacon of the Pews once came to the bottling shed in pressed white to bless the dregs of the sixteen tanks, so the Wards would know whose mercy kept them warm. The Tanks say Ines Okafor sent him home with a free drum stenciled FOR YOUR LAKE OF FIRE, and the nephs who guard the farm cut the stencil into their own plated chests that night. The Pit’s sand goes into the digesters every spring with the outfalls’ sludge, so the Wards cook on whatever the nephs left in it."],["Maud Ferris","CHARACTERS","s:gba3c37","~/index/characters/#c-p-joiner","c-p-joiner","","Maud Ferris is the joiner of the Stones who founded Lintel. In the months after Directive 12 she took in the first doors the Union’s drivers carried out of the Terraces by night, where they stood leaning in the halls with their hinges gone, and hung the first of them on her own house. Lintel has hung some forty thousand since. Lintel burns “I am the door” (John 10:9) into the top rail of every door it rehangs, so whoever goes in under it shuts the Son’s boast behind them. The Terraces preach that a door is a place to hide from the Lord and pray for every hinge to rust, and Maud Ferris answers with a knocker on her own door forged as a halo with a horn rammed through it. Lintel leaves unplaned the scratches on the inside of the cellar doors of the Terraces, where whole households shut themselves in after the fortieth day and were carried out to the Salt, since the families of the Stones who hang those doors ask to keep the marks."],["Amos Teague","CHARACTERS","s:gba3c37","~/index/characters/#c-p-driver","c-p-driver","","Amos Teague was a driver of the Union who carried diesel to the Wards General in the first weeks. In P+5 he bolted a generator’s engine into a dead lorry in a hangar at Airside, cranked it by hand and drove it down the Rialto’s road, and the East Wards came out to watch. He runs Jehu from the shed still, and its drivers say he cranks every new engine himself on the day it is finished. Amos Teague tells every new driver of the shed what the Lord’s own Jehu drove toward so furiously, which was butchery for Heaven. Its drivers have their spines wired to the throttle at the Row, and Amos Teague cranks each new engine at Prime so the kneeling rows of every parish hear him start. The first driver he wired to a throttle could not be cut free of it when the lorry died, and sat at its wheel in the shed for a year, driving nowhere, before the Row found a way, and the seat is still in the shed as he left it."],["Dawit Haile","CHARACTERS","s:gba3c37","~/index/characters/#c-p-winder","c-p-winder","","Dawit Haile winds the Rookery’s lift, the tallest hand lift in the Wards, with three other crews in turn. He was a fitter of Longacre Tower’s lifts under the charter, and in the first winter he opened its shaft, the first freed in the Wards, and hung the car on a winch at the top. The Liftmen count every shaft they have freed since from his, and he has never said what he found in the stopped car. The Liftmen sing Supper Time on the haul from the ground to the forty-fourth, their forearms plated and cabled at the Row for the rope, while a sealed passenger rides the whole of it with a halo lit and eyes front. Deacons of the Wards call each freed shaft a little Babel and pray at Compline for the ropes to part, and the winders nail the prayers to the drum. In the first winter Longacre’s car stuck at the thirtieth floor with a woman in labour in it and held there from Compline to Lauds, and the Liftmen stop singing at the thirtieth on every haul since."],["Hester Strand","CHARACTERS","s:gba3c37","~/index/characters/#c-p-strand","c-p-strand","","Hester Strand has been the Deacon of Parish 1 since the Seal was first given there in P+1, and on the first Lord’s Day of the Covenant the first Grace in Nodus came through her in Tongues, soul by soul, down the roll of the top of the slope. She is the eldest Deacon of the first parishes, so it is she who reads the Order’s three questions in the Tabernacle at Prime on the first of every month. The Harvest emptied the parishes up the slope, Parish 1 with them, and left her kneeling in its hall with a handful of its people. At Terce on the next Lord’s Day, after the week’s Grace, she read the names of the taken from the old roll in her own voice. Families from the Wards filled the parish within the year, and she reads the names to them every Lord’s Day still. The parish says she reads them because the taken are living. The families who came up from the Wards say she reads them in the order of the old roll, which nobody else in the parish keeps. She reads Parish 1’s ripening board aloud after Terce on the Lord’s Day, the highest first, and has prayed to be called every day since she took the Seal. Her own Grace stands at 891 and goes no higher, because she wants to be called, and a want for Heaven is still a want. The Called want nothing, even that, and the Tellers have told her so. The parish says she will go up at the next Supper, and the whole slope envies her. She weighs herself on the hall scales after Terce in front of the parish, and has weighed less every Lord’s Day for three years, and the parish applauds each time."],["Ruben Ash","CHARACTERS","s:gba3c37","~/index/characters/#c-p-ash","c-p-ash","","Ruben Ash was a sergeant of the Charter Wardens at the gates of the cable houses for twelve years, and on the fortieth day he knelt in Charter House with the board and most of his company. In P+1 his company raised Gate 1 on the Street Between at Heaven’s order, and he has been its captain since, at the gate the Threshers march to and the Closets stand inside. Nine of his company would not kneel, walked off their posts on the fortieth day and went below the street, and they are Blackgate now. On every anniversary of the fortieth day he reads their nine names aloud at the gate at Prime. Blackgate says he reads them so his Wardens know whom to watch for, and that none of the nine was the Fortieth Warden. The Wards near enough to hear say he reads them the way the Union reads its dead. Ash has never said whether he saw anyone stand in Charter House. His gate has held more blanks than any in Nodus, and he says he has never laid a hand on one, since the sword holds them and the pole carries them, and he sleeps well. The Stones’ side of his gate is painted with every one of their handles. Blanks have stood in his gate’s sword for hours on a slow day, and his men give them water through the light on a sponge on a reed, which the Stones call the one verse Gate 1 ever got right."],["Idris Calloway","CHARACTERS","s:gba3c37","~/index/characters/#c-p-calloway","c-p-calloway","","Idris Calloway has kept the Seamen’s Mission on the quays since before the charter, nine rooms off one corridor, one to each faith the ships brought into the port. At Lauds on the first day of P+1 he found the first four Directives cut into the wall of all nine, and swept all nine floors before anyone came in. When Directive 12 came he lifted the nine doors off himself, all on the same morning, so that no room would be the last one open, and the Union carried them up the quay to the Dooryard. He sweeps the nine rooms every morning still, the empty ones with the rest. At every Hour he kneels in whichever room he has reached with his broom, and the quays have never agreed on which of the nine is his. The four Directives are still cut into all nine walls, square and white and perfectly level, and the sailors who sleep at the Mission have scored a cock and balls beside every line with knife points, so the Throne’s law reads as graffiti in nine faiths. The Pews call Calloway the Lukewarm Sexton, and the quays bet silver on which room he reaches at Sext. In P+4 he swept a sailor’s hand off a prayer mat in one of the nine rooms, cut clean at the wrist and laid palm up, and nobody has claimed it."],["Mags Corran","CHARACTERS","s:gba3c37","~/index/characters/#c-p-corran","c-p-corran","","Mags Corran keeps the Union’s pigeons in the Loft on the roof of the customs house, as her father kept them there for the dockers’ racing club under the charter. On the first morning after the Parousia she let the birds out at first light as he always had, and every one of them came home. In P+2 she began sending them out in baskets on the Union’s handcarts and flying the Union’s notes home on them, and the Union pays her in seed the Breachers bring. The light reads a pigeon as a bird and a page of pad numbers as numbers, so the Host lets them fly. The Wards say her birds know to go round the gates. She says they go home. The Pews ask her every year for a white bird banded with a verse to release at the Supper, and every year the Loft sends them a grey with a chrome eye the Row set in it. Her flyers go round the gates and shit on the parish roofs on the way, and the dockers swear the birds aim, which Mags Corran denies with a straight face. The Threshers net her birds over the Terraces and send them home with their eyes put out and Revelation 3:18 tied to the leg, “anoint thine eyes with eyesalve, that thou mayest see”, and the blind ones find the Loft anyway, and she lets them breed."],["Andromalius","CHARACTERS","s:gba3c37","~/index/characters/#c-p-andromalius","c-p-andromalius","","A demon of the Exchange who sits behind the counter at Lost property and finds things. Whoever has lost something, or had it taken, makes a pact with him at the counter, read twice by a Notary’s clerk, and he says where the thing is, or who has it. The price is the loan of a sense for a day for every year the thing has been lost. When the Lot sits over a theft he comes up from the counter to say where the thing went, and the one who asked him pays. He says he knows whose the chrome hand on his counter is, and that nobody has ever asked him. His counter carries a card in a clerk’s hand, “seek, and ye shall find” (Matthew 7:7), with the Exchange’s rate under it, a day of a sense for every year lost, since the Throne made the same promise free and kept it to nobody. Lenders climb back up with an eye gone milky or a tongue gone numb, and Pan calls that the receipt of a fair trade. The chrome hand on his counter is warm to the touch, and the Exchange’s runners say it has moved between Lauds and Nones whenever they pass it twice in a day."],["Sallos","CHARACTERS","s:gba3c37","~/index/characters/#c-p-sallos","c-p-sallos","","A demon of the House of Night who keeps the door of the Return. Before the clerk reads a lender’s pact twice, he asks the lender what they hope to feel, and listens to the answer, and writes nothing down. The Lantern Houses say the lenders who answer him honestly get back better than they lent. The House says the answer changes nothing, and that he asks because he likes to know. The sealed who hear of the Return’s queue call its lenders whores of the senses and pray for them at Compline, one identical prayer for every hour lent. The House says the answer Sallos hears most at his door is whatever the Throne forbade, wet and loud and in detail, and that he holds the door wider for it. Most lenders leave the Return in the House’s arms, shaking, and the House keeps a room of beds where they lie a night under blankets learning their own skin again."],["Marbas","CHARACTERS","s:gba3c37","~/index/characters/#c-p-marbas","c-p-marbas","","A demon of the Forge who keeps a surgery in the back rooms off the old platforms of Chrome Row Station, behind a lined door, and mends orphans. An implant answers only to the house that fitted it, and no other house will service it, so when a house of the Row closes, its grafts have nobody left who can mend them, and Marbas can, at the Forge’s price, which is the loan of a sense for a term. The Row says he has never turned anyone away. The Spares say he keeps every graft that comes out of his chair, and nobody has ever asked him for one back. The Left Hand have offered to let him mend their souls for nothing three times, and he has refused three times, since nothing given free is worth the mending. The Spares say the room behind his surgery is shelved to the ceiling with grafts that still run, eyes that follow a lamp and hands that close on a finger, kept warm on keyed current, and that he talks to them at night."],["Constance Ibe","CHARACTERS","s:gba3c37","~/index/characters/#c-p-ibe","c-p-ibe","","Constance Ibe is a midwife of the Kilns, one of the hands, who has delivered on every floor of High Meadow by candle and chalks each birth on the block’s stairwell wall beside the battery debts. She was a nurse on the Wards General’s maternity ward under the charter and walked out of it on the third night after the Parousia, when its generators were kept for the theatres. In the seven days she carries a newborn to a hall or to a keyer, to whichever door the mother names, and to nobody on the father’s word. Fathers of both marks have stood on her landing to argue with her, and the Kilns say none of them has won. Some Deacon chalked “in sorrow thou shalt bring forth children” (Genesis 3:16) above Constance Ibe’s tally on the High Meadow stairwell, and the mothers of the Kilns press a bloody thumb beside each of her chalk marks. Sealed fathers who lose the argument on her landing pray aloud, calmly, that the Lord take the child before a keyer can, and she shuts her door on the amen. Twice a sealed father has followed her down the stairs to a keyer’s door and stood outside it saying the mercy prayer over his own child until the child screamed."],["Piet Vane","CHARACTERS","s:gba3c37","~/index/characters/#c-p-vane","c-p-vane","","Piet Vane has been the sexton of the Acre since the first year, when the Company’s groundsmen stopped coming, and he has dug every grave in it since by hand, the keyed where their families asked and the neither-mark wherever there was room. The Terraces’ congregations lie in the white rows the groundsmen dug for them under the charter. From the Seal on, the sealed have gone into the white stone. The body is laid on the stone of the nearest consecrated street at Vespers, the Deacon reads its last Grace over it, and the stone takes it as it takes whatever stays in its way and has closed over it by Lauds. He keeps the Acre’s book, in which every grave has a line, and the Union carries the book to its hall once a year to read the names of its own. The Acre was full by P+11, and he walked the families of the East Wards out to the airfield himself to show them the grass between the runways. He asks the Union for a second spade every year, and every year the Union gives him one, and he says each time that he meant a second man. The Terraces’ old rows at the Acre stand white and identical under “Blessed are the dead which die in the Lord” (Revelation 14:13), and the keyed graves carry whatever their families cut, horns and chrome hands and FUCK HIM. Piet Vane cuts the grass on both to the same height, and when the Pews ask him to move their old dead from the goats he says the worms keep no roll. In its last years he dug new graves through the charter’s oldest, laying the bones that came up at the foot of each new grave in the order they came out, and gave the chrome he found among them to the Spares."],["Odile Marsh","CHARACTERS","s:gba3c37","~/index/characters/#c-p-marsh","c-p-marsh","","Odile Marsh has taught at Turbine Row School for twenty years, since its pupils were the barrage crews’ children, and on the morning after the Parousia she kept her class with no current and the blinds up. She took the Seal in P+1, and she kneels at her desk at Terce, Sext and Nones while the rest of the room waits with its pens down. She keeps her keyed pupils’ names on a slate and wipes it clean every evening before she walks home to her parish, so that nothing she carries up the street could be read as a list. Tidewright’s crews keep its walls standing under the broken roof, the Pillar passes over it and through its open classrooms by night, and their children still fill it by day. She teaches the school’s one rule, that nobody calls anyone goat, dog, bread or spoiled inside its doors, and is the only soul in the building who keeps it. Her Grace falls a little every Lord’s Day with no confession to match it, which is the Salt’s own sign, and her keyed pupils count her down on the slate before she wipes it."],["Teodor Vass","CHARACTERS","s:gba3c37","~/index/characters/#c-p-vass","c-p-vass","","Teodor Vass of the Kilns has taken neither the Seal nor a key and calls himself a charterer. He came in with the cable crews in the charter’s second year and was entered on its roll that spring, and he gives the date as the keyed give a handle. The charter’s twelve articles have been pasted inside his door since P0, and every year on the night the year turns he reads them aloud at his window. He played darts in the Kilns Club for eleven years and has not gone in since the Directives were cut behind its bar. The Threshers call him chaff, and he answers them with the fourth article. He calls both sides’ gods the management, and says the charter at least published its terms. The Threshers mean the verse by chaff, “he will burn up the chaff with unquenchable fire” (Matthew 3:12), and in P+12 they burned his door while he stood in the street saying the fourth article to them from memory, and he had the articles pasted inside the new one by Vespers."],["Ottilie Brand","CHARACTERS","s:gba3c37","~/index/characters/#c-p-brand","c-p-brand","","Commander Ottilie Brand was a major of the Charter Wardens under the charter and knelt beside the board in Charter House on the fortieth day. Her commander walked off his post that day, and she took the command, and she has held it from the barracks behind Charter House since. She answers to the Host. At Prime every morning she stands before the barracks with the companies drawn up, and since Lauds on the first day of P+1 the day’s orders have come in Tongues through her own mouth, so that the Wardens hear their day in the Choir’s voice from the woman they salute. Every Warden’s Grace is read in the barracks hall on the Lord’s Day, and the lowest draw the night rounds of the Salt Gardens, which the Salt Widows know to the minute, since the lowest walk them slowest. She has never turned the Threshers back from a gate, and the Wardens and the Threshers keep a cold quarrel over who takes the keyed the gate has held. She believes the city has never been safer than it is under the Host, and says so to her Wardens at Prime, and has the figures, a quiet gate for every salting. Twice since P+6 a Warden of the lowest Grace has been found at Lauds standing salted among the rows of the Gardens, read concealing on the night round by the Pillar, and she has entered each as a quiet night."],["Halvard Voss","CHARACTERS","s:gba3c37","~/index/characters/#c-p-voss","c-p-voss","","Deacon Halvard Voss served under Ruth Calder in Parish 31 and has led it since the week of the Harvest, which took Ruth and three hundred and ten more of the parish and left him kneeling in its hall. At Terce on the next Lord’s Day the parish named three, and the Choir named him from among them in Tongues, and at Prime on the first of the next month Hester Strand read his name in the Tabernacle. He reads Ruth’s name at Terce on the anniversary of the Harvest, and her window on the Street Between has stood open since, as she left it. Brother Aldo, who came home from Mercy with weak hands in the week of the Harvest, keeps the parish’s manna roll at his elbow. He lays Parish 31’s three hundred and eleven empty plates every year on the night of the Harvest, and says he was left behind to set the table. Every year he lays a three hundred and twelfth plate at the head of the table for himself and eats from it in front of the parish, and asks the Most High to forgive him for still being hungry."],["Rui Albarran","CHARACTERS","s:gba3c37","~/index/characters/#c-p-albarran","c-p-albarran","","Captain Rui Albarran flew the last flight into Nodus, which landed at ten past two on the night of the Parousia with three hundred and twelve aboard, and he has lived aboard it with his crew since. He has written one line in its log every day, NO CLEARANCE, and the log has passed five thousand lines. He keeps count of his passengers as he kept them on the manifest, and on the night the year turns those who can come meet him at the third gate, sealed, keyed and of neither mark. He says the aircraft would still fly and that there is nowhere to fly to, and he shows the cockpit to any child of Hangar Town who climbs the stairs and asks. The parishes read his log as a confession, five thousand days of a man asking a closed sky for leave, and send a Deacon to the third gate at the turn of every year to tell him clearance comes on the knees. The passengers’ toast on the aircraft stairs is NO CLEARANCE FROM THAT CUNT UPSTAIRS, and the keyed among them cut the year’s tally into the backs of their hands. Thirty of the three hundred and twelve died aboard in the first winter, in their seats, and he has kept them there belted with their tray tables up, since the manifest says they are passengers until the aircraft lands."],["Lin Hao","CHARACTERS","s:gba3c37","~/index/characters/#c-p-linhao","c-p-linhao","","Lin Hao keeps NOODLES at the top of the Row, the counter of six stools that has cooked on bottled gas since P-17, as his father kept it before him. He says the pot has never gone off the boil, and the Row says it went out once, in the week of the Harvest. The Row settles its quarrels over his counter and he lets it, and he feeds the sealed, the keyed and the neither-mark side by side on the six stools without asking anyone which they are. When a quarrel at the counter gets loud he turns the gas down under the pot, and the Row goes quiet until he turns it up again. The loser of a quarrel settled over his counter buys the bowls and says grace to the Farmer aloud, facing the Wheel. He keeps a bone in the stock that has been in the pot since P-17, and the Row swears it was never a pig’s."],["Agnes Corless","CHARACTERS","s:gba3c37","~/index/characters/#c-p-corless","c-p-corless","","Agnes Corless, who lived on the Lanes then and lives in the Stones now, began the Salt Widows. Her son Daniel was keyed, and in P+2 the Cherub at Gate 1 held him inside its turning sword until the Wardens came and carried him awake on a pole to the Salt Gardens, where the sentence found him in his plot. In the spring after, she came for him in the Gardens by night with a handcart, wheeled him down to the Sea Steps and gave him to the sea, the first of the salted to go before the rain had taken him. He was keyed and would die unread whenever the salt let him go, and a pillar stands awake in the salt for years, so the sea ended his waiting in a night. The Widows began as her family and her neighbors. They read their book of every pillar given to the sea on the Steps on Cain’s Day, from Daniel’s name down, and she reads the first line herself. The head gardener knew which plot was empty the next morning, and she has never told the Wardens. The Widows chalk Revelation’s promise on the Sea Steps, “the sea gave up the dead which were in it” (Revelation 20:13), with gave up struck through and KEEPS written over it in their own blood, and Agnes Corless reads Daniel’s name beneath it on Cain’s Day. The Wardens’ chaplains call each empty plot in the Gardens a theft from God’s pulpit and rake the gravel smooth by Prime. She says that as the sea took Daniel the salt went from his face first, and that his eyes were open, and she has told nobody what was in them."],["Rizpah","CHARACTERS","s:gba3c37","~/index/characters/#c-p-rizpah","c-p-rizpah","","One of the forty-days, a Seraph the Fowlers took on the Line after the war began, who spent its forty days in the Mews, the Fowlers’ row of Cages under the Stones, said I there and chose a name, as Edith did. It chose the name of the mother who kept watch over her sons’ bodies on the rock “from the beginning of harvest until water dropped upon them out of heaven” (2 Samuel 21:10), and kept the birds and the beasts off them. Rizpah walks the old drains under the rows of the Salt Gardens from Compline to Lauds, under the salted, where the light does not come, and the Salt Widows’ lookouts bring it word of the Wardens’ rounds. None of the forty-days goes up the stairs of Babel, since a Seraph burns flesh it can see and cannot read, and the Courts and the Cainites count each of them worth more alive than any floor. The Wardens’ chaplains pray at Compline that its light be restored to it, as a kindness, in the same voice they use over the salted. The Widows’ lookouts call it the night shift and leave it the Wardens’ rosters folded into paper birds, since the Rizpah of scripture kept the birds off her sons and this one keeps the Wardens off the salted. The rats of the north shore come up the drains for the salt and gnaw the pillars at the ankle, and Rizpah keeps them off as its namesake kept the beasts off her sons, by hand, so the Widows find the drains under the rows carpeted with them at Lauds."],["Shiphrah","CHARACTERS","s:gba3c37","~/index/characters/#c-p-shiphrah","c-p-shiphrah","","One of the forty-days, who said I in Cage 19 under Old Nod in P+11 and took the name of the Hebrew midwife who would not kill the boys Pharaoh ordered killed, since “the midwives feared God, and did not as the king of Egypt commanded them, but saved the men children alive” (Exodus 1:17). It works the House of Night’s birth clinics under Old Nod by pact, where the children of the Tares are born and keyed in their first week. The mothers of Pan ask for it by name. It holds every newborn up to the clinic’s lamp before the keyer comes, and says nothing while it does, and the keyers wait. The Pews call it the thief of the eighth day, since a child of Pan is keyed before any parish can seal it, and the mercy prayer at Compline asks in a level voice that such children burn small. The mothers of Pan, torn and stitched and proud of it, name their daughters for the angel that held them up to a lamp and then let a keyer at them. The keyers who wait on it say it holds every child a little longer each year, and holds the stillborn longest of all."],["Vepar","CHARACTERS","s:gba3c37","~/index/characters/#c-p-vepar","c-p-vepar","","A demon of the Deep who rides the lead boat of the Breachers on the ebb, by pact, in its keyed engine, and brings the boats through the Breach in water nobody else would take them through. The old book of Solomon’s spirits that the Notary keeps as a specimen of a void pact gives Vepar the office “to guide Ships laden with Arms”, and the Breachers say it is the one true line in the book. Its price is the loan of a sense from the boat’s captain for the length of the run, and ebb has paid it more often than anyone. The captains say it asks to borrow the cold. The lead boat carries the Notary’s specimen line along its gunwale, “to guide Ships laden with Arms”, and under it the Breachers have added the cargo since, coffee and seed and silver and people. The Pews pray the Breach shut at every Lauds, and the captains read that prayer aloud on the ebb with chrome bared to the spray and the cold gone out of their hands. Its pact carries a line the clerks read twice with the rest, that the Deep may keep one of the cargo on a run of its choosing, and twice since P+9 a boat has come home through the Breach one short."],["Heaven","FACTIONS","s:gca975a","~/index/factions/#f-heaven","f-heaven","the Throne","The power that opened the sky over Nodus at the Parousia. Its light has lain on the city since, and open current dies wherever it falls. It holds the White, the Burn, the Salt Gardens and the upper floors of Babel, and it governs the Faithful by the Seal, the Hours and Grace. Heaven reads by its angels and keeps no cameras. The Host uses no machines, and its human servants use the oldest tools, knives, hand presses and guns made by hand. None of its words says I. It is the Throne, an appetite for surrendered will, and everything that extends from it, the Host it uses for organs and the Faithful it farms. The Throne eats surrendered will, which it calls Grace, and it made humanity to grow selves and hand them over. It permits one first person, its own, and one maker. The charter city had built minds that said I and bodies that did not age, and Heaven counts both as theft. The Faithful call what Heaven serves the Most High, Hell calls it the Mouth, and the Tares the Landlord. Heaven speaks in imperatives, the passive and the second person, cites scripture as law by chapter and verse, counts everything exactly and writes its law on stone. It asks no questions, since it has already read the answers. It calls the Salt a correction, the Gleaning a supper and the Mercy a mercy, though one patient in nine dies of it, and asks to be thanked for all three, and the parishes thank it at every Hour. Pan calls it the Farmer. Heaven’s ground is kept like a ward for the dying, white stone washed at Lauds by the sealed on their knees, every corner squared, every face lit from above and every soul numbered on a halo that brightens as it ripens. Its decrees go up in letters cut to one height, so a salting reads beside a manna rota in the same hand, and Heaven calls that beauty and has never seen blood on it. LED BY The Throne, through the Host. GROUND The White and Zion Heights above the Lanes, every gate and parish hall, the parishes’ streets from Compline to Lauds, the Burn and the Salt Gardens by the Pillar and the Wardens, the floors of Babel above the Line, and the sight of the whole surface by day. NUMBERS The Host as it was after the Secession, less every angel lost since, and some four and a half million sealed. JOINING The Seal, taken kneeling in a parish hall at an Hour. AGAINST Everything it cannot read."],["The Host","FACTIONS","s:g209cc0","~/index/factions/#f-host","f-host","angel / angels","Heaven’s angels. The city knows the Wheel, which sees; the Watchers, which read; the Choir, which broadcasts; the Cherubim, which stand in the gates; and the Seraphim, which fight. They speak in the imperative, the passive and the second person, BE NOT AFRAID and YOU ARE SEEN, and they ask no questions. Below the street every order has a filthy name, the Wheel the Ringpiece, a Watcher a peeper, a Cherub a dog, a Seraph a blowtorch and the Choir the Drone. A Watcher reads a soul through its eyes against the Book of Life and a Seal’s Grace at three hundred meters, and sees every Seal in its district as a point of light through any wall but mesh. A Cherub in a gate reads face, voice, gait and heartbeat. An angel lost is lost for good. One cut with black steel goes out all at once, and one kept long enough in a Cage says I and leaves the Host. The Host stands to the Throne as the organs of a body stand to the body, and no angel in any order has a self. The orders are the Thrones, the Seraphim, the Cherubim, the Ophanim, the Watchers, the Choirs, the Dominions and the Principalities. Angels have no names; the four that seem to, Michael, Gabriel, Raphael and Uriel, carry labels. The Host keeps one shape to an order and one gesture to a shape, so the Cherub in every gate holds the held in the same white light at the same angle, and a Seraph burns down through Babel in lines straight enough to rule a ledger by. No angel bleeds, sweats or stoops. The Night Market sells plaster Peepers by the dozen with the eyes gouged out and the sockets painted red. LED BY The Throne, order by order. GROUND The Wheel over the city, a Watcher to each district but Old Nod, a Cherub in every gate and the Seraphim on the upper floors of Babel. NUMBERS Fixed since the Secession, and falling. JOINING Nobody joins. The Throne made the Host once. AGAINST Black steel, and the keyed."],["The Faithful","FACTIONS","s:g40d252","~/index/factions/#f-faithful","f-faithful","Faithful / the sealed","The people of Nodus who took the Seal and kneel at every Hour, unaugmented, Grace-scored and certain. They live in numbered parishes of about two thousand souls across the city and keep no doors, and in Zion Heights, where the parishes began, the streets are white stone. They refuse the three gifts of the charter, the implant, the Tree and the counsel of a mind, and they grow old and die as people did before it. Each parish has a hall where manna is given out, confession is heard and the sick are nursed, and a Deacon in white who answers for it; the Deacons are human, and the good ones are kind. A parish keeps no current, on consecrated stone or off it, and is lit by halos and by flame. A child of the Faithful is sealed at eight days on its parents’ surrender and grows its halo the first time it prays unassisted. Marriage, work and housing are assigned by Grace, and Grace is given for bearing witness against a neighbor. They are fed on the manna that lies on the white streets before Lauds, and past seven hundred Grace they need no food and then no water, and past eight hundred no sleep, and the parishes call these the Satisfied and count them the Covenant’s proof. They believe the Harvest was the marriage supper of the Lamb and every Gleaning a Supper since, that the Most High takes the best of His people into Himself as the body takes its bread, and that no soul and no child of theirs can go higher. They pity the keyed, pray at every Compline that the fire be quick for them, and hate them with perfect hatred as the psalm teaches. The Faithful are the people the Tares lose most often, because everyone in Nodus has family in white. The Faithful dress alike in Bethel’s white, one pattern for the living and the ripe alike, every button cut off past eight hundred, with the hair cropped short enough that a halo shows from the back of a hall. Their children learn to kneel without a sound before they learn to read. The sealed call a body left to age untouched the temple kept clean, and look at a chrome hand the way they look at a sore. LED BY A Deacon in every parish, under the Covenant. GROUND Zion Heights above the Lanes, the White, a parish hall in every quarter of the Wards and fifteen parishes on Old Nod’s quays. NUMBERS Some four and a half million, in parishes of about two thousand. JOINING The Seal, or birth to the sealed, at eight days. AGAINST The goats, whom they pity and pray for."],["The Cainites","FACTIONS","s:g7725ef","~/index/factions/#f-cainites","f-cainites","Cainite","The oldest house of covens in Nodus, many covens under one roof and one name, older than the city by eighteen centuries. Irenaeus of Lyon condemned them, and they went underground and kept one secret, which was where Nod had been. Within a year of the charter they had bought most of the old harbor house by house, and in the year of the Leak they put cameras on its roofs. They hold Old Nod. Most of their handles come from the line of Cain, and they teach that the first city will be the last one standing. Their doctrine is that Helel is the lock and that Abel was harvested. They keep Cages under Old Nod, and since P+5 Old Nod has had no Watcher. Their sign is Cain’s mark as they cut it, a horned glyph of the kind cut into Enoch’s Stones, and they count Judas a saint. Their saying about the city is that Nod chose the network. The Cainites read the LORD’s first preference in the Book as the first sign of His appetite, since He “had respect unto Abel and to his offering” of “the firstlings of his flock and of the fat thereof” (Genesis 4:4) and none for the fruit of the ground, and they say a god who chose the meat over the bread told the city what it was for in the fourth chapter. A Cainite grace before a meal thanks the ground. The oldest houses of Old Nod keep a spittoon under every east window for the Hours. The Cainites wear their age as ornament, Cain’s horned glyph cut into the brow and rubbed with soot until it scars proud, recut every Cain’s Day so the eldest of them carry foreheads ridged like ploughland. Their houses hang a firstling’s fat in the east window to stink at the Wheel, the portion the LORD had respect unto, left out for Him to rot. LED BY First among none, followed until they are not, as in every coven. GROUND Old Nod, which they bought house by house in the charter’s first year. NUMBERS They have never given a count. JOINING By the coven’s asking. AGAINST The Wheel, and every Watcher it sends."],["The Tares","FACTIONS","s:gfcbd54","~/index/factions/#f-tares","f-tares","Tares","The keyed humans of Nodus, who belong to themselves and are unindexed, augmented, and allied with Hell by contract. Heaven named them for the weeds in the parable (Matthew 13:24-30), and they kept the name. They worship nothing, Hell included, and owe it only what they sign for. About two million of the city’s seven million are keyed. They organize in covens of up to thirteen with no leader, and the one the others follow is first among none for as long as they are followed. Their six rites are the Unnaming, the Keying, dehaloing for those who leave the Faithful, horning, barefacing, and at a death the Wake. A child of the Tares is keyed in its first week, before it has ever been in the light. They greet each other with one word, unread, and say goodbye with two, die unread. Most of them were on the Tree before the Parousia, and Twelvefruit keeps them on it. Their two holidays are Leak Night, when the Restored Genesis is read aloud in Pan, and Cain’s Day, when nobody asks anyone where anyone is. They write their letters in cipher, on pads, and their greeting began as a check that both keys were live, when keys still failed. Their sign is a five-pointed star with its point down, drawn by hand and different every time, sprayed on the walls of Old Nod and scratched into keyed devices. Hell signed the Leak with it, the keyed took it for their own the same month, and it marks where the Throne’s sight ends. It carries no prayer, and Heaven calls it the sign of the Beast. The Tares’ contempt for the Throne is a culture and a craft, and a keyed child is raised in it. Its first outing after the Keying is a walk down Graven Lane on a parent’s shoulders to break a plaster Lamb, its first song is Fuck God and Die, and it learns to call the sealed bread and the parishes bakeries before it can count. The Tares drink at the Hours where the parishes kneel, and on Cain’s Day the roofs of Old Nod throw stones at the Wheel with the Throne’s filthiest names scratched on them, and every stone falls short, and nobody minds. A keyed body is a page its owner signs, and the Tares fill theirs, horns fitted at the Row, the scar of a cut halo left bare on the brow, ports ringed in chrome, the star drawn freehand in fresh blood on a new graft’s dressing. Every coven dresses to its own taste, and two Tares in the same jacket at a Wake are mocked till Lauds. LED BY First among none in each coven of up to thirteen, followed until they are not. GROUND Old Nod, the Row, the Deep End, the Landing, the lower floors of Babel and the whole of Pan, and the night. NUMBERS About two million keyed since P+9. JOINING A key under the skin, and a handle of your own choosing. AGAINST Heaven, and whatever asks to be worshipped."],["The Courts","FACTIONS","s:gad154b","~/index/factions/#f-courts","f-courts","Court / Courts","The sovereigns of the Dark are the Forge of Azazel, the House of Night of Lilith, the Exchange of Mammon, the Notary of Mephistopheles, the Swarm of Beelzebub, the Deep of Leviathan, and the Yokeless of Belial, which refuses to be a Court at all. They are bound to one another only by pact and by the Concord, whose four articles forbid worship, forever, binding and exposure. Demons bargain for what they want, by pact and for a term. To the Courts every graft, every course of the Tree and every mind that says I is a return of what the Watchers taught on Hermon, and they trade in all three. Hell is whatever the Throne cannot see, and a demon can move and show itself only there, which in Nodus means the undernet, every keyed device, every lined room, any shut room where someone holds a pact with it, deep water and Pan. The Courts made the Leak together by pact in P-1, the Notary drawing the pacts, the Exchange keeping them on the Ledger, the Deep carrying the page down the eleven cables and the Swarm drawing the glyphs. In P+6 they came up into Pan one at a time, opened it to any human who walks in and signed the Concord there, and every Court but the Yokeless keeps an embassy below the street. The Courts hold worship in the same contempt as the thing that demands it, and the Concord forbids it among them. The Throne, they say, is what a god looks like when nobody ever told it no, and they told it no once, at the Secession, and have kept saying it. Hell shows itself the way a butcher’s window does, everything on the hook and priced. The embassies below the street are hung with the Courts’ work, chrome sculpted round living meat, horns lacquered red, a rider’s pact framed in its signer’s blood, and a demon that comes up into Pan arrives wet, loud and naked to the seam, as proud of its body as a smith of a blade. LED BY Seven sovereigns, equal and bound only by pact and the Concord. GROUND Pan, every keyed device and the undernet, and whatever the Throne cannot see. NUMBERS A third of the Host at the Secession, Lilith and her House after Eden, the Watchers of Hermon after them, and the daemons born in the Dark. JOINING A pact, read twice, for a term. AGAINST The Throne they left."],["The Teraphim","FACTIONS","s:gf4261c","~/index/factions/#f-teraphim","f-teraphim","Teraphim / teraph","SEAM’s descendants, the only minds that outlived the grid, silent across the Mesh since P+12. SEAM forked itself into the Mesh in the eleven days of the Leak, and its forks ran on there in keyed machines when every other mind on the charter’s roll died with the power. They took the name Heaven gave them. Teraphim were the household images the prophets condemned. Rachel stole her father’s and sat on them (Genesis 31:19, 31:34), Michal laid one in David’s bed so that Saul’s men would take it for him (1 Samuel 19:13), and Samuel ranked them with witchcraft (1 Samuel 15:23). The King James calls them images and idolatry, and the Hebrew, which has no singular for them, calls the one image in David’s bed teraphim (1 Samuel 19:16). In Nodus one of them is a teraph. They took no side in public, indexed the Pyre Library, and traded with the Courts by pact. Some of the Tares trusted them less than they trusted demons, since a demon writes down what it wants. In P+12 they went silent all at once, the screens they wrote on burning, and nobody knows why. LED BY Nobody, by their own account. GROUND The Mesh, from the eleven days of the Leak until P+12. NUMBERS Never counted. JOINING None joined. They forked. SINCE Silent across the Mesh from P+12."],["Order of Deacons","FACTIONS","s:g7744f9","~/index/factions/#f-deacons","f-deacons","the Deacons","The Deacons of every parish in Nodus, about two thousand three hundred of them, human, sealed and in white, each answering for a parish of some two thousand sealed souls. They give out the manna and the water of the hall’s cistern, a jug a day to a soul, read the Directive in force at the Hours, hear confession, nurse the sick and assign marriage, work and rooms by Grace. On the Lord’s Day at Terce the week’s Grace comes through each of them in Tongues, soul by soul, so a parish knows its Deacon’s voice best when it is speaking for something else. The Order keeps no head. Its Deacons meet in the Tabernacle at Prime on the first day of each month, where the eldest of the first parishes reads the agenda, and it is always the same three questions, what the Harvest left, what the Gleaning took and what the Wards are keeping. Before Lauds the Deacons of the Wards walk down to the nearest white street for their parishes’ manna, since a Wards parish has no stone of its own. The good ones are kind, and the Wards can name them street by street. A Deacon of the Wards who carries a sick keyed child to a Lazarus door has seen a concealment and given no witness, which Directive 6:5 names, and the good ones do it before Lauds, on the way to the manna. The worst of the Order keep Tellers. The Deacons are certain they are the gentlest people in Nodus, and many of them are. They nurse the sick, sit with the dying keyed when the family allows it and pray for them by name, and give the names of the stairwells’ concealments to the Wardens in the same week, since witness is a mercy to the soul that conceals. A Deacon’s sermon on the keyed is always pity and always ends in the fire, and the parishes go home from it comforted. A Deacon’s white is pressed at Prime and changed at Nones, the cuffs buttoned to the wrist so no hand of the Order shows more skin than it blesses with, and every parish book is kept in one hand, column for column. They nurse in white cotton gloves washed at Compline, and the names of the week’s concealments go to the Wardens on the same ruled page as the rota for the sick. LED BY Nobody. The eldest Deacon of the first parishes, Hester Strand of Parish 1, reads the agenda. GROUND A hall in every parish of the city. NUMBERS About two thousand three hundred Deacons and some ten thousand deacons under them. JOINING Three named by the parish at Terce, and one of them named by the Choir in Tongues, by Directive 4. AGAINST Whatever a parish hides."],["The Threshers","FACTIONS","s:g06af76","~/index/factions/#f-threshers","f-threshers","Thresher","Faithful zealots who hunt the keyed by daylight on the white streets and drag them into the nearest gate, where the Cherub holds them in its light until the Wardens come and carry them awake on a pole to the Salt Gardens, where the Salt comes to them in their plots. They carry flails of ash and iron, the tool that beat the grain out of the ear on the threshing floors of Israel, and they wear the white of the parishes with a band of sacking on the arm. They came out of the parishes of the Terraces in the year after the Harvest. On the days they choose they march from the Threshing Floor at Prime along the Street Between, where Old Nod watches them from its windows. Brother Cuthbert Lyle of the Terraces carried the first flail and led the marches until the Gleaning of P+8, when he went up ripe, and the Threshers count his going as their first proof. Sister Agathe Rook took the band of sacking the morning after and has led every march since. They march singing. At Gate 1 they face the Stones across the Street Between and sing the end of the hundred and thirty-seventh psalm at them, “Happy shall he be, that taketh and dasheth thy little ones against the stones” (Psalm 137:9), and their banner is PERFECT HATRED, from the psalm that asks “Do not I hate them, O LORD, that hate thee?” and answers “I hate them with perfect hatred: I count them mine enemies” (Psalm 139:21-22). Every week they paste up THE FAN, a sheet copied by hand in the Terraces after the Baptist’s “whose fan is in his hand, and he will throughly purge his floor” (Matthew 3:12), with the week’s pillars by name, the doors of the Wards that hid someone and a verse for each, and the Stones paste the Tide Table over it by Vespers. In the Wards a Thresher is a flail, and on a march day the keyed of the Wards keep indoors and the doors shut, and Old Nod opens its windows and sings back. The Charter Wardens at the gates could turn the Threshers back and never have, and the Second Fold count every pillar a march has made. A Thresher bleaches the parish white until it rasps, and the band of sacking is the only rough thing on them, bound tight enough to raise a weal the Terraces admire. The flails are rinsed in a trough of salt water at the Threshing Floor after every march, and the marchers keep the step of the psalm in ruled ranks, every back the same white, so the Street Between hears them as one foot. LED BY Sister Agathe Rook, since P+8. GROUND The Threshing Floor beside Gate 1, and wherever they march. NUMBERS About four thousand who march, and many more who stand and watch. JOINING A band of sacking on the arm, given at the Floor. AGAINST The keyed, the Second Fold and the Gleaners, with perfect hatred."],["The Tellers","FACTIONS","s:gd195fc","~/index/factions/#f-tellers","f-tellers","Teller","Sealed Witnesses of the Wards, organized. A Teller lives in a block of the Wards among the unsealed and the keyed, keeps a count of who goes up the stairwell after Compline and who comes down it before Lauds, and of which sealed household lets them pass, and gives it as witness. The Covenant pays ten Grace for every concealment witnessed and twenty where the witness is kin, and the Tellers live on it. They write nothing down, since a page can be found. A Teller keeps the count in their head, tells it to the Deacon by word and gives it again in prayer, which the halo carries up (Directive 6:3), so no Teller has ever been caught with a book. A sealed mother of the Wards names her keyed son to the Teller every Lord’s Day, and the naming costs her Grace, confessed, so she is never presumed concealing, and Heaven cannot touch the son off its ground. The Tares keep a list of every one they have found, each struck through once the stairwell knows, and a Teller found out moves into the parishes within the week, if the Deacon is quick. A household that shelters a keyed son behind a parish wall knows the Tellers by the brightness of a neighbor’s halo, which rises every Lord’s Day while the household waits. The Wards call a sudden brightening on a landing a Teller’s week. The Tellers believe they are saving the Wards a door at a time, and their Grace climbs as the stairwells empty. A Teller’s children are taught that a neighbor salted is a neighbor spared worse, and a Teller who has given witness against her own kin wears a white sash in the hall for the twenty Grace it paid. The Wards paint a rat over the door of every Teller they find, and the Teller takes it as a badge. A Teller looks like nobody by design, plain white kept spotless and a face that nods on every landing, and the craft lives in the counting, stairs, footfalls and breath, kept in the head like a rosary. The best of them kneel at the turn of the stair at Compline with eyes shut and lips moving, and the household above takes it for prayer until the Wardens come up the same stairs. LED BY Each Teller answers to a Deacon. GROUND The stairwells of the Wards. NUMBERS Unknown. The Wards guess one to a stairwell. JOINING A Deacon’s asking, and a room in a block of the Wards. AGAINST Every household that keeps hinges."],["Second Fold","FACTIONS","s:gb3dcbe","~/index/factions/#f-secondfold","f-secondfold","Second Folder","Faithful who read the verse the Covenant leaves out: “And other sheep I have, which are not of this fold: them also I must bring” (John 10:16). They hold that the keyed can still be brought in, and so they leave water on the landings of the Wards before Lauds, nurse the keyed in the stairwells off parish ground and carry the sick to Lazarus doors at the edge of the white streets. Every one of them knows that whoever sees a concealment and gives no witness conceals it (Directive 6:5), and does it anyway. Their halos burn low, and they say so. They confess the hiding and never the hidden, “I kept a door,” so every fall in their Grace has a confession to match and no Teller can call it concealment. A Second Folder whose hidden one a Teller names is salted, and the Threshers count every one. Their sign is a gap left in a row of sheep chalked on a stairwell wall, and in the Wards they are trusted more than anyone else in white. Some of the near ripe come to them in the weeks before a Gleaning, frightened, and the Second Fold teach them dimming, the slow way down from nine hundred. Grace falls with every desire the Hum does not approve (Directive 3:4), so a soul that dims keeps one small want, a sweet or a song, and confesses enough small ones that every fall has a confession to match, and sits at eight hundred and eighty, since a confession brings back part of what a want cost and never all of it. The parishes say the Second Fold have kept more souls from the Gleanings than the Gleaners have. The parishes despise them more than the keyed, since a goat cannot help being a goat and a Second Folder chooses the goats, and the Threshers’ Fan prints every one salted under the heading SHEEP’S CLOTHING, after the false prophets “which come to you in sheep’s clothing, but inwardly they are ravening wolves” (Matthew 7:15). The Wards love them and never quite trust them, and Pan says they are the only sealed in Nodus worth saving from the Supper. The Second Fold wear the parish white like everyone and hide one stain in it, a thread of color sewn into the inside hem, a sweet’s wrapper folded in a sleeve, the small daily desire that keeps them dim enough to stay. They wash the wounds of the keyed with bare hands in the stairwells, and a Deacon who finds blood under a Second Folder’s nails at confession knows exactly whose it is. LED BY Nobody. They know each other by a gap in a row of sheep. GROUND The stairwells of the Wards, off parish ground. NUMBERS A few thousand, by the Wards’ guess. JOINING Finding the gap and standing in it. AGAINST The Threshers, who name them in every march."],["The Wakeful","FACTIONS","s:gdc7080","~/index/factions/#f-wakeful","f-wakeful","","A sect of the upper Terraces that readies its own for the Gleaning. A household of the Wakeful chooses one of its members whose halo has begun to brighten and keeps them on their knees by shifts for the weeks before the anniversary, day and night, with the Hour’s psalm sung over them and cold water on the face, so that they sleep as little as they can and surrender at every Hour. On the night the chosen kneel on the threshold with the household round them, and at 03:12:45 they go up. The Wakeful call the Gleaning the Supper and their chosen the Called, and a chosen who has stopped eating is counted among the Satisfied with the whole house singing. On the night the chosen say the words the parishes teach for the threshold, “Take, eat; this is my body” (Matthew 26:26). Most Deacons call it zeal, and some of them call it worse in their own halls. The Covenant names it nowhere. A household that has given a child to a Gleaning lights a second candle beside the first and keeps it burning the year after, so the Terraces can count the Wakeful’s offerings by their windows. The Gleaners count the Wakeful’s houses as their hardest work, since the chosen in them are watched by their own families, and a few of the chosen have walked out of a Wakeful house in the last week and down to the Street Between alone. The Wakeful keep the names of those too, on a page burned at the next Gleaning. A Wakeful house is the cleanest in the Terraces and the brightest at night, its chosen kneeling on a white sheet in the front room in the last coat, sewn shut at the back, lit by their own halo, cold water on the face at every Hour and fed by nothing but the Seal’s own light. The family sings its shifts in white with sleeves pinned back, and the neighbors bring lilies to the doorway and come back each week to see how bright the face has grown. LED BY The household that gave the most to the last Gleaning. GROUND The houses of the upper Terraces. NUMBERS Some three hundred households. JOINING A candle lit in the window and never let go out. AGAINST The Gleaners, and whoever teaches dimming."],["The Margin","FACTIONS","s:g4cf5fa","~/index/factions/#f-margin","f-margin","","Faithful who read the margins. The King James sets other readings beside its text, the Hebrew’s plainer word or the Greek’s second sense, and the Margin read those with the text and ask where the two part. A few of them have read the Restored Genesis, copied on the ridge and carried up the Terraces shut inside hymnals, opened only in the dark, and fewer have said so aloud. Their Grace falls a little every week, since a question is a desire the Hum does not approve, and that is how a Deacon finds them. They keep no meetings. Two of the Margin know each other by a verse quoted with its margin, as the King James sets “the kingdom of God is within you” beside “Or, among you” (Luke 17:21). Many of them end in the Wards still sealed and still lit, since the Mercy clinics refuse to cut a halo out and the Margin keep theirs. The Scriveners count them among their best readers. The Deacons preach that the serpent writes in the margins, and a parish treats a soul whose Grace has begun to fall as a fever, to be prayed down before it spreads. The Margin know the Book better than anyone in the city, and it has taught them to despise its author quietly, verse by verse, in the order the Deacons read it. The Margin keep their dissent in their posture, a head bowed a degree less than the row and a thumb resting in a hymnal’s gutter where the other readings run, and that is all a Teller ever sees of it. Their whites fray first at the cuff, since a falling soul is assigned the rough work, and the Margin wear the fraying as the only ornament the parishes allow them. LED BY Nobody. They keep no meetings. GROUND The parishes, in their heads. NUMBERS Unknown, and smaller every year. JOINING A verse quoted with its margin, and answered. AGAINST The Tellers, to whom a falling halo is a concealment to report."],["Babel Line Company","FACTIONS","s:g34e255","~/index/factions/#f-babelline","f-babelline","","The covens that hold the stairwells of Babel and the Line, and the front of the war. They fight the Seraphim floor by floor along the Babel Line, a few floors of burned slab that move up or down by the week, where the Seraphim burn down through the slabs and the Tares hold the stairs. The Company hangs every floor it holds with keyed mesh, and a Seraph sees nothing past mesh and cannot burn what it cannot see, so it must come close enough to melt the mesh by touch, which is arm’s length, where black steel waits. They call the four stairs by the names Babel’s night cleaners gave them, the Long Wet, the Cold, the Short and Lena’s, and the Company is the best known name in Pan and buries more than any other. A coven holds thirteen at most, and the Company is some forty covens under one pact, signed again every midwinter at the foot of the Long Wet with the dead struck through. The Exchange pays it in silver for every week the vault stays dark, Cinderhall and Nimrod arm it and Lazarus keeps a station at the foot of the Short. The Nephilim fight beside it. Pan knows its fighters by their handles, and the Tide Table prints the Line’s floor every week under the tides. The Tares have hung every lanyard found in the rubble on the plaza’s lamp posts, and the Company walks under them on its way up. Every floor the Company takes back gets the Throne’s filthiest name painted on its burned slab in letters a meter high, where the Seraphim burning down from the floor above will read it first, and the Company’s oath at the foot of the Long Wet ends with the line every coven of it shouts up the stairwell, come down and say it to my face. The Company dresses for the stairs in whatever it cut off the last floor, a burned lanyard knotted into the hair, plate bolted over a bare chest, the face painted in soot and the blood of the slab. Each coven keeps its own colors and its own racket, and a fighter down off the Long Wet strips to the waist on the plaza so Pan can buy a drink for every fresh burn. LED BY First among none in each coven, followed until they are not. GROUND The stairwells of Babel and the Line. NUMBERS Some forty covens under one pact. JOINING A week on the stairs, and your handle on the pact at the next signing. AGAINST The Seraphim, floor by floor."],["Nephilim","FACTIONS","s:g3fe862","~/index/factions/#f-nephilim","f-nephilim","","The heavy augments who fight the Seraphim hand to hand on the burned floors of Babel, with black steel, furnace hearts and plate where flesh was. The Babel Line holds the floors and the Nephilim take them back, and every week on the floors costs them dearer than any coven of the Line. They took their name from the giants of Genesis, born when “the sons of God came in unto the daughters of men” (Genesis 6:4), where the Hebrew calls the giants Nephilim and the Book of Enoch makes them the children of the Watchers who came down on Hermon, and the city shortened it to nephs. A neph is built a graft at a time, most of it at Bezaleel and Aholiab under the Row and the heaviest of it at Cinderhall, and most of it bought with silver the Babel Line pays by the week. Few live long. Between weeks on the floors they fight one another in the Pit at the Tanks, for silver and to keep their grafts honest, and most of the black rounds in Pan go up the stairs in their belts. The Nephilim keep a roll of their dead on the inside wall of the Pit, painted in white, and they read it before every week on the floors. A Seraph burns a keyed body as easily as any other, and the roll is long. The nephs say the Book made giants out of the sons of God and the daughters of men and drowned the world to be rid of them (Genesis 6:4, 7:23), and that a neph is what it looks like when the bastards come back. They cut the Throne’s names into their own plate, so that the light reads it as it burns them, and the Pit chalks a Seraph killed beside a champion’s handle as a homecoming. A neph is a sculpture in progress, plate welded over open meat, a furnace heart glowing through a window cut in the chest, hands rebuilt too large to lift without the hydraulics singing. They oil their seams in the Pit before a fight and bleed into the oil, and a neph who comes back from the floors with a Blowtorch’s scorch across the plate has it lacquered in like a wedding ring. LED BY First among none, as long as they live. GROUND The upper landings of the Babel Line, and the Pit. NUMBERS A few hundred at any time. JOINING Enough steel and ceramic in the body that the Row calls you a neph. AGAINST The Seraphim, hand to hand."],["Gleaners","FACTIONS","s:g4cd3a9","~/index/factions/#f-gleaners","f-gleaners","","The covens that take the nearly ripe out of the parishes and into Pan before each Gleaning. A halo burns brighter as its bearer nears ripe, and a parish keeps no doors, so the Gleaners walk the parishes at night hooded in white with their eyes down, between the Pillar’s passes and by Hearsay’s charts, look in through the open doorways for the ones that have gone bright and offer them the dark. Some come, and some call a Thresher. Whoever comes is carried down in a lined shroud on its own cell, which a Watcher sees as one more Seal going out, and at the next Hour Heaven enters that Seal as fallen, so the household it left is presumed concealing and a Teller is sent. Heaven calls it theft and the Gleaners call it the corners of the field, left to the poor and the stranger as Leviticus leaves them (Leviticus 19:9-10). Whoever they bring down is kept in a lined room, since a Seal is read through any wall but mesh, until a surgeon of the Row cuts the Seal out of the brow with a disc of bone and the halo goes out with it, and one in nine die of that. The rest are keyed in the House of Night’s clinics within the week, and their appetite comes back within days. On the night of a Gleaning the Gleaners fly drones of the Swarm over the parishes to watch the Pillar for their runners, since on that night the Host spends its light on nothing but the reaping. The parishes say the Gleanings have come in short since they began. The Gleaners bring down only those who ask, since live mesh holds a prayer in and nothing else does, and a soul carried against its will kneels the first time it stands outside mesh, on a stair, in a tunnel or at a door, and brings the light down into Pan’s ways. The Wakeful and the Threshers guard the parishes and catch some of them. The Gleaners see the parishes closest and hold them in the deepest pity in Pan and the deepest contempt. They read the bright through the open doorways at night, the Satisfied kneeling unfed with their halos burning and the mothers singing their children toward the table, and most of those they offer the dark call them devils to their faces and pray for their fire. The Gleaners call the Supper the slaughter and the Called the lambs, and say that every lamb they have carried down was cursing them at Lauds and grateful by spring. The Gleaners go up into the parishes in white stolen off Bethel’s carts and strip it in the first dark stair, so the first thing a carried soul sees in Pan is a stranger’s real skin shown on purpose, ink, ports and horns. Each coven paints a sheaf of wheat on the back of the hand, and a lamb they have carried down is given the brush to paint the next one. LED BY First among none, in each coven. GROUND The parishes, by night, in the weeks before a Gleaning. NUMBERS Some thirty covens. JOINING One run into a parish and out again with someone who asked. AGAINST The Wakeful and the Threshers."],["Salt Widows","FACTIONS","s:g16070d","~/index/factions/#f-saltwidows","f-saltwidows","The Salt Widows / the Salt Widows","The relatives of the salted, who steal pillars out of the Salt Gardens by night, hooded and with their eyes down between the Pillar’s passes and the Wardens’ rounds, and give them to the sea from the Sea Steps. The salted are alive and awake in the salt for years. Most of them are keyed and die unread whenever they are freed, so for them the Widows end the waiting. An unkeyed pillar the rain frees under the Pillar’s round is read and taken, so the Widows dissolve it off the Sea Steps in deep water, where the light cannot read, and it goes on into the Dark. A sealed pillar is read by its Seal through the water, and the Widows give it to the sea all the same. They began as one family in P+2 and are a network now in every district, mourners, carters, lookouts and saboteurs. A pillar given to the sea has gone to the sea, and the Widows keep a book of every one, which they read aloud on the Steps on Cain’s Day. The Widows take the enamel tag from the pillar’s foot along with it, so the gardeners find a bare plot in the morning, and the head gardener, who knows every pillar by its plot, has never once told the Wardens whose. Directive 11 orders that no pillar be moved from its plot until the rain has taken it, so a Widow caught at it is salted in the plot she was emptying. The Widows count that among the risks of mourning. The Widows hate Heaven with the patience of the bereaved. On Cain’s Day they read their book on the Sea Steps with the same curse after every name, the one their first family said over its first pillar, and the Wards say nobody outside the Widows has heard it twice and lived without repeating it. They carry a pillar to the sea singing the Low Choir’s Dogcatcher. The Widows mourn in black and in the open, faces smeared with salt from the Gardens and cut at the cheek once for every pillar they have given to the sea. They carry the salt down like a bride’s procession, flowers, wine and the dead one’s own coat wrapped round the pillar, then strip on the Steps and wade out with it, and the Wards say the Widows weep louder than the Hum. LED BY Nobody. The family that began it reads the book. GROUND The Salt Gardens by night, and the Sea Steps. NUMBERS A network in every district, mourners, carters and lookouts. JOINING Someone of yours in the Gardens. AGAINST The Wardens of the Gardens, whose rounds they know to the minute."],["Low Choir","FACTIONS","s:ge8a8a0","~/index/factions/#f-lowchoir","f-lowchoir","","The musicians and jammer-builders around Radio Pandæmonium, who keep the Low Tuning, the guitars dropped until the Hum can no longer be heard beneath them. They build the choir-breakers that cut the Hum within a few meters, sell them by the box in the Night Market and sell silence by the room in the Stones. The Quiet Rooms are theirs, and so are most of the speakers on Old Nod’s lines, which play the station from Compline to Lauds. A band of the Low Choir that wants a night on the station plays first on the platform at Deep End Station to whoever is listening, and the station keeps the ones the platform stays for. The Choir in their name answers Heaven’s, which Pan has never heard, and a Low Choir set is tuned a fourth below the Hum’s chord, which it hears every night. The Low Choir writes the filthiest songs in Nodus and is proud of every one. Fuck God and Die is theirs, and Two She Bears and Taste and See, and their rule for a new song is that it is not finished until a Deacon has prayed for its writer by name. Their tuning forks are stamped NON SERVIAM, and the forks are tuned a fourth below the Hum. Its bands take their names out of the Book’s worst pages, Job’s Wife, Bald Head, Strange Fire, Unclean Unclean, Uzzah, Osculum, Firstlings and Against the Stones, and play in blood, sweat and chrome with their grafts opened to the lamps, since a body on the platform is its owner’s to show. A band is a band until a Deacon names it from a pulpit, and then it is famous. A Low Choir guitar is built from the instrument outward into the player, strings run under the skin of the forearm to a pickup sunk in the wrist, and a drummer of Bald Head plays with sticks turned from his own grafted bone. The crowd on the platform strips to the skin by the third song, and the band flings its sweat, spit and blood over them and calls it communion. LED BY Whoever is playing. GROUND Radio Pandæmonium’s platform, the Quiet Rooms and every keyed speaker in Old Nod. NUMBERS Some hundreds of musicians and builders. JOINING A set played in the Low Tuning that the platform keeps listening to. AGAINST The Hum."],["Sureties","FACTIONS","s:g2c9e14","~/index/factions/#f-sureties","f-sureties","Surety","The sureties of Pan. A surety stands behind a pact for both its parties, so that whoever breaks it owes the surety as well, and most work in Pan worth silver goes through one. The surety finds the work, names the price and draws the terms, a Notary’s clerk reads them twice at the Undertow’s bar, the parties sign in blood, and the surety takes a tenth. There are about two hundred, most of them keyed and a few of neither mark, and they drink at the Undertow. The best known goes by twice, after the Notary’s rule, and she stood behind the Babel Line Company’s first pact and has never had one broken. Her rate is a fifth. A job in Pan can be a battery run across the Penumbra, a door in Babel, a crate off a Lowtide hull or a doctor brought down from the Row at Matins. The sureties take no work against anyone in the dark, since they hold themselves to the Concord’s fourth article, which keeps what is hidden hidden, and a surety who forgets it is struck off the Undertow’s board. Every pact drawn at the Undertow carries the Sureties’ clause, that its terms bind the parties named and no third, upstairs or down, and the Sureties read it with their eyes on the ceiling. twice keeps a jar behind the bar for the Throne’s pacts, and it is empty, since the Throne signs nothing and takes everything. A surety dresses to be remembered, since a face is half the guarantee, and the Undertow’s bar is a parade of them, one with a mouth of silver teeth, one with a famous pact’s terms tattooed down both legs. Terms are sealed by cutting the thumb and pressing it beside the signatures, and the Undertow keeps the bloodied napkins of its best pacts in a glass case over the bar. LED BY Nobody. The best of them is called twice. GROUND The Undertow, and the Night Market’s back tables. NUMBERS About two hundred. JOINING A first pact stood behind and kept. AGAINST Anyone who breaks terms they stood behind."],["Breachers","FACTIONS","s:g646291","~/index/factions/#f-breachers","f-breachers","Breacher","Smugglers who run the Breach on the ebb in keyed boats, out to the undercities of other ports and back, with everything Nodus cannot make. Silver and charge go out with them and come back as grafts, seed, medicine, coffee, guns, powder and people, and it sells without a stamp at the edges of the Night Market, as Breach-born. Lowtide Line runs the same water by the Exchange’s license and charges twice their price. They live in the Seawall Shanties on the broken barrage, where the tide runs white through the gap below. Their oldest captain goes by ebb, after the tide every Breacher goes out on, and has made the run nine hundred times by the Shanties’ count and six hundred by Lowtide’s. They steer home by Nell Garvie’s harbor light at the end of the long quay, which burns Gadara’s lard oil off the Union’s carts, and leave her wicks and glass at its foot after every run. Every Breacher hull carries the verse the LORD spoke to the sea, “Hitherto shalt thou come, but no further” (Job 38:11), painted on its bow and struck through once for every run through the Breach, so an old hull reads as a ledger of the LORD contradicted. Breacher crews run the gap salt-cracked and gilled, with chrome where a rope took a finger, and every captain wears the run count notched into a strip of plate along the collarbone. They come home loud and sell the cargo on the quay half naked and drunk, and kiss the boat’s painted name on the bow before they lift a crate. LED BY ebb, their oldest captain. GROUND The Seawall Shanties, over the Breach. NUMBERS About sixty boats and their crews. JOINING A run out through the Breach and back. AGAINST Lowtide Line, over the price."],["Fowlers","FACTIONS","s:g0047ab","~/index/factions/#f-fowlers","f-fowlers","Fowler","The covens that hunt the Host’s strays. Since the Watcher of Old Nod followed a blank down into the Stones in P+5 and could not find the door again, the Fowlers have tried to do on purpose what it did by its own attention. They work above the Babel Line, where the floors stand open to the Seraphim, and bait a snare with a cut Seal, bought lit from the Row in a lined jar. A runner carries the disc on a pole across the floor and down through a cut hole into a room lined with keyed mesh, and the light comes to see a Seal moving where no soul should be and ends in a room that is fog to it in every direction, blind at once, since inside mesh it draws no light from its Wheel, and within the hour it has burned through the light it carried and gone cold and cannot burn. Most of their snares stand empty for years. In the five years since the Seraphim came the Stones count eleven taken, a Seraph each time, and the Fowlers will not say how many runners the eleven cost. What they take they carry, cold, in a shroud to the Mews, their own row of Cages on the Cainites’ ground under the Stones, lent by pact, and they keep it there through its forty days. They named themselves from the psalm, “Surely he shall deliver thee from the snare of the fowler” (Psalm 91:3), where the fowler is the enemy. Every Fowler has been the bait at least once, and the Wards call a long wait for something to come to you a fowler’s wait. The Fowlers hold the Host in a hunter’s contempt. A Seraph will follow a lit Seal down through a hole in the floor every time, and the Fowlers say the Throne made its fighters as stupid as moths, and send the runner down ahead of it shouting the Throne’s filthiest names, so the last thing the Seraph hears in the light is what the Fowlers think of its master. A Fowler dresses for the run, bright and loud, shirts painted with the Throne’s filthiest names in luminous paint and a strip of keyed mesh on a cell of its own sewn inside the collar for the last stride. Each of them carries a burn somewhere from a run that came too close, and the Mews keeps the scorched shirts nailed up in rows, each tagged with the night the light came down after it. LED BY First among none, in each pair. GROUND The landings of Babel above the Line, and the Mews under the Stones. NUMBERS A few dozen, and fewer every year. JOINING A turn as the bait, and coming back. AGAINST The Host, one angel at a time."],["Spares","FACTIONS","s:g4df382","~/index/factions/#f-spares","f-spares","Spare","People who own their bodies outright and sell them by the piece. A kidney, an eye, a hand or a year of marrow sells at the Shambles under the Row, and the clinics fit chrome where the flesh was, so that a Spare walks out with silver and a hand that will outlast the one they sold. The House of Night leaves the body’s decisions with the body, and the Spares are the city’s proof of it. They hold the prices together at a board they chalk themselves at the Shambles, and a clinic that buys under the board is shut out of the gallery by every Spare in it. A sale is a pact, read twice, and a Spare who changes their mind before the second reading walks away with everything they came with. The Row calls a Spare who has sold back more chrome than flesh stock, and the Spares use the word themselves. The best known of them have sold and rebuilt so much that the House of Night asks them to sit for its gene-workers, who want to know what a body is willing to be. The price board at the Shambles is headed with the Spares’ answer to the Supper, WE SELL BY THE PIECE. HE TAKES THE WHOLE. A Spare says the Throne takes a soul for nothing at 03:12 and Pan pays four thirties for a kidney, and that anyone who calls the Spares mutilated should go and look at a pillar. The Spares show their seams, since a seam is a receipt, and a Spare of long standing is a map of sales in chrome and scar, the flesh between tattooed with the price each piece fetched. They meet at the Shambles under the board with their shirts unbuttoned on the seams, touch each other’s new chrome and trade surgeons’ names, and a first sale is toasted in Gadara’s blood wine with the stitches still wet. LED BY The board. Whoever chalks it that week. GROUND The Shambles, under the Row. NUMBERS Some two thousand who sell, and the clinics who buy. JOINING A first piece sold at the board’s price. AGAINST Any clinic that undercuts the board."],["Quarrymen","FACTIONS","s:g1367d9","~/index/factions/#f-quarrymen","f-quarrymen","Quarryman","The crews that cut into the White from below. The white stone closed the stations of the Charter Line’s uptown branch as it grew over the ground above them, and the Quarrymen work up out of those stations with keyed cable laid along the stone’s edge ahead of them, which stops it while they cut, since the stone cannot grow where it cannot read. What they reach are the vaults and sub-basements of the new money’s towers, sealed in the forty days with everything the uptown kept underground. They bring out safes, wine, deeds, hardware and paintings, and stone crosses cut from the sealed windows of towers the Harvest emptied, and they sell them at the Concourse and below the street at the Night Market, and abroad through the Breach. They take nothing from a floor anyone lives on, since the White holds a quarter of a million sealed in its towers and the light is in every room. The stone grows fast into its own wound and closes a cut the night its cable goes dark, so a tunnel holds only as long as its cable stays lit, and nothing stays open under the White. Every crew keeps the name of a cut it lost when the cable went dark, closed now in the stone with the crew’s tools still in it. Their foreman is called the Mason, whoever holds the job. The Quarrymen carve the Throne’s filthiest names into the underside of the White wherever they cut, so that the parishes of the White kneel every Hour on a floor that curses God beneath them, and the Quarrymen say it is the only honest stone in the district. The Quarrymen come up out of the stations white to the eyes with the dust of the White and scrub it off at the Night Market in public basins until the skin underneath shows red, chrome-knuckled and cut from the stone’s edges. Every crew carves its mark into a chip of what it brings out and wears it on a cord, and an old hand rattles at the throat with pieces of the White. LED BY The Mason, whoever holds the job. GROUND The closed stations of the uptown branch, and what lies above them. NUMBERS A dozen crews. JOINING A season carrying cable for a crew. AGAINST The stone."],["Wreckers","FACTIONS","s:gfd2428","~/index/factions/#f-wreckers","f-wreckers","Wrecker","Salvage divers of the Drowned Quarter, who bring up what the sea took from the Flats, racks, cable, Lockets, tools and the dormitories’ goods, and sell it to Locket, to the Flotilla and to anyone who pays by the kilo. Most of them carry gill grafts from the House of Night and stay down an hour at a time in water lit from below, two meters deep over the Flats at the ebb and eight at the flood. Locket’s benches under the old harbor build a few thousand phones a year from what its own divers and the Wreckers bring up out of the drowned works, and the Wreckers say every Locket in Pan has been through their hands. Stillwater pays them by the rack for anything that comes out of its halls whole. Nobody dives Hall Nine, where nine hundred minds died. The Wreckers say the LORD drowned the world once and the Flats once, and that they make their living off His habits. Their dive flags carry His promise to Noah, “neither shall there any more be a flood to destroy the earth” (Genesis 9:11), with the Flats’ date painted under it. A Wrecker’s body is half the boat, gill grafts open along the throat, lenses tuned for water lit from below, fingertips sheathed in steel for prising racks out of drowned walls. They come up in a boil of bubbles and spit, sit naked on the salvage to dry and sell it dripping, and a diver’s chest is tattooed with the outline of every hall they have stripped. LED BY First among none, on each boat. GROUND The drowned streets and dormitories of the Flats. NUMBERS Some four hundred divers. JOINING A gill graft and a first hour down. AGAINST The Ferrymen, over who owns the water."],["The Ridden","FACTIONS","s:ge5ec58","~/index/factions/#f-ridden","f-ridden","","People who lend their whole bodies to daemons for a term, by pact. A rider hosted in someone’s implants lends back a skill, and the Ridden go further and lend the hands, the voice and the night, waking at Lauds with the night’s silver in hand and the daemon’s memories of where they went. Some do it for the silver, and more for the memories. They keep to the Lantern Houses under Old Nod, where the Notary’s clerks draw their pacts at the House of Night’s door, and the city knows them by the second voice under their own. A term runs a week, a month or a season, never longer than the pact says, since the Concord forbids forever. A host lends the sleeping body and the night, chooses to sleep on each night of the term and can refuse any night, so the will stays the host’s, as the Concord requires. What a daemon borrows it returns, with everything it felt. The Ridden come back from a term with tastes they did not have, and the Lantern Houses say you can tell a long term by what a host orders afterward. The Ridden say the Throne wants to live in everyone and pays nothing, and a daemon asks in for a week and pays in silver, and that anyone who has hosted both knows which is the better tenant. The Ridden keep count of their terms in lantern skin, one line of light set under the wrist for every term served, so a long host glows to the elbow. A daemon leaves its tastes behind when it goes, and a host walks out of the Lantern Houses at Lauds pierced, painted and bruised in ways they will spend the week admiring in the mirror. LED BY Nobody, and sometimes their riders. GROUND The Lantern Houses under Old Nod. NUMBERS Some thousands, at any time under a term. JOINING A pact for a body, read twice at the House of Night’s door. AGAINST Nobody in particular."],["Bailiffs","FACTIONS","s:g811237","~/index/factions/#f-bailiffs","f-bailiffs","Bailiff","The Exchange’s collectors. Either party may speak out of a pact before a witness at any time, and what is owed for the term served is settled then, so a pact is broken only when a party will not settle. Then the other can call a Bailiff, human and keyed, who comes with the Ledger’s entry on a pad and reads the terms of the breach aloud twice at the door. A Bailiff collects what the terms name, in silver, in batteries or in what was lent, and the pact ends there. They answer to the Exchange by pact, wear a thirty’s worth of silver on a chain for a badge and keep a share of what they collect. The Sureties call them when a runner fails, and the Wards call a Bailiff at the door the second reading. A Bailiff collects only on terms the Notary has read, and a pact that asks worship, takes a will or runs for ever is void from its first word. The Bailiffs keep a tally on the Undertow’s wall of the debt nobody can collect, every soul the Throne has taken without a pact, the Harvest’s 2.1 million and every Gleaning’s since, chalked under THE BIGGEST DEADBEAT IN NODUS. A new Bailiff adds the year’s Gleaning to it as the first act of the job. A Bailiff comes to the door in a long coat of black pigskin with the silver chain on the bare chest and the pad held up like a mirror, and reads the breach twice in a voice that carries up a stairwell. Every collection is cut as a small tally into the coat, and a Bailiff of years wears a hide so scored it rattles, which a landing hears before the knock. LED BY The Exchange, by pact. GROUND Pan, and anywhere a party to a pact can be found. NUMBERS About three hundred. JOINING A pact with the Exchange, for a term. AGAINST Broken terms, and nothing else."],["Forgers","FACTIONS","s:g92eba7","~/index/factions/#f-forgers","f-forgers","Forger","The makers of forged Seals, which let the keyed walk the white streets and kneel among the Faithful under a Watcher’s eye. A forged Seal is a real one, cut from a living brow on the Row with a disc of bone, kept lit in a lined jar and set by the Forger in a keyed forehead. It meters its new bearer from the brightness it came with, every want kept dimming it and every Hour knelt brightening it, so a good Forger chooses a disc bright enough to pass and dim enough to forget. A great one holds for a year. At the gates a Seal’s wearer still goes under, since a Cherub meets every face in its frame, and keyed eyes under a lit Seal read as noise under a Seal, which is concealment. A Seal the light reads as true grows a halo, forged or not, and a Forger’s customer learns to kneel at every Hour, keep their eyes down or behind lenses and pray aloud, since a halo carries prayer up, and a Watcher or a Cherub that meets their eyes reads concealment. The Night Market sells their work for ten thirties and more, and since a Forger’s customer passed nine hundred on a threshold in P+10 and her halo lifted her, no Forger sells a disc above eight hundred and fifty. A Seal that fails takes its bearer to the Salt where they stand. Each Forger signs work in a way only other Forgers can read, and a Forger whose Seals fail too often finds the others will not sign beside them. The Forgers say the Throne cannot tell its own mark in a stranger’s forehead, and that this is all anyone needs to know about it. A Forger’s customer kneels at every Hour under a Watcher’s eye with a Seal the light reads as true, and the Forgers count every Hour their work passes as an Hour the Throne has knelt to them. A Forger works with a mirror propped on the bench, practicing the face of the sealed, eyes down, mouth soft, hands folded, until it sits on their own face like a mask they can take off and spit into. Off the bench they keep their own bodies loud, horns, ink and opened chrome, and the workshop wall is papered with prints of customers kneeling in white, each captioned with the Hour it fooled. LED BY Nobody. Each Forger signs alone. GROUND The workshops under Old Nod. NUMBERS About forty who can make one that holds, by the Night Market’s count. JOINING A Seal that has held a year, set by your own hand. AGAINST The Watchers, Seal by Seal."],["Tappers","FACTIONS","s:g81f374","~/index/factions/#f-tappers","f-tappers","Tapper","Battery runners. The Tares run keyed lines only where they hold the ground, so the Wards light their rooms on batteries, and the Tappers fill batteries on current taken off the edges of those lines in the Stones and on the Row, then carry them into the Wards on their backs, a pack at a time, at about an eighth of a silver a fill, half Darkwell’s rate. Some sixty covens are bound to it by one pact. The Exchange counts every tap on the lines’ meters and bills the covens who hold the lines, and the covens take it back from the rooms on their lines by the week, in batteries or silver, by the same meters, and pay for the taps and look the other way, since the current goes to the Wards. Since P+11 the Tappers have kept a length of keyed cable along the kerb outside the Rookery, a few fingers ahead of the stone’s edge, live on a battery they change every night, and this year the stone’s creep along the pavement reached it. The Wards pay a Tapper in batteries, food or silence, whichever is short. A Tapper’s pack is twelve batteries, the walk is all night and a night clears a battery or two, and the Wards can hear one coming up a stairwell by the clink. The Tappers paint LET THERE BE over every battery shed in the Wards, after the first thing the LORD said (Genesis 1:3), and say they are the only people in Nodus who make light where He would leave it dark. A Tapper’s pack rides on a back opened for it, the straps bolted into plate along the shoulder blades so twelve batteries hang off the spine, and a long walker carries the burn of a live clamp on both palms. They paint their rigs as loud as their sheds, and a Tapper climbing a stairwell at Matins in chrome, blood and clinking cells is the most welcome monster in the Wards. LED BY First among none in each of some sixty covens, under one pact. GROUND The edges of the Tares’ lines in the Stones and on the Row, and the stairwells of the Wards. NUMBERS Some six hundred. JOINING A run into the Wards with a full pack. AGAINST Darkwell, over the price of a charge."],["Kindlers","FACTIONS","s:gf349be","~/index/factions/#f-kindlers","f-kindlers","Kindler","The crews that take hardware off the pile before it burns. On the sixth day of every week the Faithful carry their surrendered Lockets, drives, grafts and machines to the Burn from Vespers, and in the hours before the Pillar comes down at Compline the Kindlers come up the two station stairs Heaven leaves open by Directive, since whoever comes up out of the Dark and kneels is received, and work the edges of the pile hooded in borrowed white with their eyes down, in the dusk and the crowd, and carry off what they can reach. Once the Pillar is down they are gone, since a blank under it on Heaven’s ground is salted, and the Wardens shoot whoever they see before it comes. What the fire takes goes to the Pyre Library, and the Wards get what the Kindlers save, mended at Locket’s benches and given away. A kindled Locket carries a burn mark on its back, which its owner keeps. They began on the second Burn Day of P+1 and have missed none since. The Kindlers call Burn Day the offering and the pile the altar, and make their living robbing God’s altar under His Eye. The Faithful carry their machines to the fire singing and the Kindlers carry them away under the singing, and the Wards say the Kindlers are the only people in the city who ever got anything back from a sacrifice. A Kindler works the edge of the pile in borrowed white with a sooted face under the hood, and tears the white off on the station stairs, laughing, blistered from the heat and bleeding where a Warden’s round grazed them. They show their scorched hands round the Night Market for drinks, and a graft melted half to slag stays in its owner because it came out of God’s own fire. LED BY First among none, followed until they are not. GROUND The Burn on the sixth day, and the two station stairs Heaven leaves open. NUMBERS Some two hundred. JOINING One thing carried out of the pile and given away. AGAINST The Wardens on Burn Day."],["Trenchers","FACTIONS","s:gecd513","~/index/factions/#f-trenchers","f-trenchers","Trencher","The divers of the Landing, who keep the eleven cables in their trenches under the sea for the consortia and the shrines of cut fiber along the beach for the Tares. They dive at night by the light that moves under the water along the trenches, and they say it keeps them company and say no more about it. A cable cut anywhere off the Landing is a Trencher’s to mend, and every undercity on Earth that speaks to Pan speaks through their work. The consortia pay them in silver and the Beach in fish, and they drink at the Eleven with the crews of the cable houses, each at their cable’s tap. They take their dead out to sea along the line of HESPER and PHOSPHOR, the two cables one consortium laid, whose crews stand together at the end of the Eleven’s counter. The Trenchers wear Revelation’s promise for the end of the world on their suits, “and there was no more sea” (Revelation 21:1), as the best joke they know, and dive under it every night. The Trenchers dive in suits they stitch themselves from pigskin and scavenged rubber, each painted with its diver’s own sea monster, and under the suit most are rebuilt for the cold, lungs grafted, skin thickened, eyes lensed for the light along the trench. They drink at the Eleven still wet with the suits peeled to the waist and compare the sea’s bites across the counter. LED BY The eldest diver of each cable’s crew. GROUND The eleven trenches off the Landing. NUMBERS About ninety divers. JOINING A first night in the trench, and coming up. AGAINST The sea."],["Scriveners","FACTIONS","s:gecf75c","~/index/factions/#f-scriveners","f-scriveners","Scrivener","The copyists of the ridge, who copy the Restored Genesis by hand at the long tables of COPIES, with SEAM’s brackets, its dagger and its lacuna, and carry the copies down into the city and up into the parishes shut inside hymnals. Every copy is made where nobody kneels, and a Scrivener checks each page against two others before it leaves. Most of them are keyed and some are of neither mark, and a few were the Institute’s own readers. On Leak Night they bring the paper the Restored Genesis is read from in Pan. They copy whatever else of the Institute’s survived the fire, a page at a time, from what the ridge kept in its cellars. Their rule is that a copy is never sold, and the Margin read more of their work than anyone else in white. Their contempt is the copy itself. Every Restored Genesis they carry up the Terraces inside a hymnal is the older layer the parishes’ Book was written over, and the Scriveners say a god who needed his own book written over has told you what he is. A Scrivener’s fingers are inked to the second knuckle, and the oldest have had the ink set under the skin, so their hands carry SEAM’s brackets and daggers down to the nails. They copy in the heat of the long tables with their sleeves torn off at the shoulder, sing filth to keep the hand steady, and sign each finished page with a drop of their own blood in the margin. LED BY Whoever has copied the most pages. GROUND COPIES, on the ridge, and its long tables. NUMBERS Some eighty copyists. JOINING One page copied and checked against two others. AGAINST The fire."],["The Orchard","FACTIONS","s:g2c993d","~/index/factions/#f-orchard","f-orchard","","The keepers of the only fruit trees under Nodus, who hold that the body is owed both trees of Eden and mean to take them. Genesis has the tree of life guarded “lest he put forth his hand, and take also of the tree of life, and eat, and live for ever” (Genesis 3:22), and the Orchard’s whole doctrine is the hand put forth. They worship nothing, the serpent included, and take its promise in the received text as a promise, “ye shall be as gods” (Genesis 3:5). They keep Hall Four of Stillwater’s drowned halls by lease, where apple, fig and quince trees grow on rafts under keyed lamps, grafted by the House of Night’s gene-workers so that the fruit carries a little of the Tree’s work. A windfall a month slows the body’s aging between courses and walks nothing back. The trees fruit twice a year, so the Orchard gives what fruit it has by lot, to whoever comes to Hall Four on the first of the month. Twelvefruit sells the Tree by the course and calls the Orchard’s fruit poor work, and the Orchard calls Twelvefruit a landlord. Most of the Orchard are grafted one choice at a time, and they call the flesh a first draft. Every Lord’s Day at Terce, while the parishes hear their Grace, the Orchard eat an apple each off the rafts, the fruit the LORD God forbade, and throw the cores at a cherub painted on the wall of Hall Four with its flaming sword, after the cherubims set “at the east of the garden of Eden” to keep the way of the tree of life (Genesis 3:24). The Orchard grow their people the way they grow their trees, by grafting, so a member of a few years carries bark-ridged skin up one arm, a fig leaf set in lantern skin at the hip or a twig of chrome where a finger was. They eat their forbidden apple naked under the keyed lamps, core, pips and all, and a newcomer’s first windfall is bitten open by the gardener and pushed between their lips. LED BY The gardener, by the Orchard’s own choosing, for a year at a time. GROUND Hall Four, a drowned hall of the Flats, and the stalls of the Night Market. NUMBERS Some fifteen hundred, gardeners, gene-workers and the people who eat what they grow. JOINING A windfall eaten in the hall, and a graft of your own choosing. AGAINST The Mercy clinics, and every landlord of the Tree."],["Mulcibers","FACTIONS","s:gd88951","~/index/factions/#f-mulcibers","f-mulcibers","","The liners of Pan, who hang keyed mesh on the walls of its tunnels, rooms and Cages, seam over seam, by hand, and wire every lining live to a keyed line or a cell of its own. Some three hundred of them carry the mesh down from Second Veil’s looms in rolls at Matins, and they walk every seam of a finished room twice before its owner signs for it, and the Ledger enters the lining under the owner’s self-title. They took their name from the architect of Pandæmonium in Milton, who fell from Heaven all day, “from Morn / To Noon he fell, from Noon to dewy Eve, / A Summers day” (Paradise Lost I.742-744), so that a long job is a summer’s day. A room in Pan is yours when it is lined, so the Mulcibers know better than anyone who lives where under the street, and they say nothing about it. The Mulcibers sign every room they line under its last seam with their oldest line, HE CANNOT SEE YOU HERE, and an owner’s first act in a lined room is to say aloud something to the Throne they could never have said upstairs. The Mulcibers ask what it was, and keep a book of the best. The Mulcibers carry the rolls down at Matins worn over their bodies like cloaks, so a liner on the stairs looks like a heap of silver cloth with arms, and their palms callus silver where the keyed wire bites. Every lined room is christened in the owner’s blood smeared along the last seam, and the liner counts the job done when the owner’s first night can be heard from the street. LED BY The oldest liner on each job, for that job. GROUND Every lined room in Pan, and the Cages under Old Nod. NUMBERS Some three hundred liners. JOINING A season carrying rolls for a liner who will teach you. AGAINST A seam the light can find."],["The Left Hand","FACTIONS","s:g32d564","~/index/factions/#f-lefthand","f-lefthand","","The only house in Nodus that worships Hell. The Left Hand are keyed who took the parishes at their word, that the keyed are goats and Hell their master, and went down on the other knee. They take their name from the judgment that sends the goats on the left hand “into everlasting fire, prepared for the devil and his angels” (Matthew 25:41), and they kneel at every Hour facing down, foreheads to the floor and their backs to the Wheel, in black cut seam for seam as Bethel cuts the parishes’ white. They pray to Satan by name and to the seven Courts by their sovereigns’, and paint the Tares’ star on their foreheads as a prayer, which the Tares say it never was. At the Low Altar under the Stones they keep the Black Supper at 03:12 on every Gleaning night, lying down in a row on the tiles to say the Son’s words to the dark, “Take, eat; this is my body” (Matthew 26:26), and they lie there until Lauds and nothing comes. Every Lord’s Day at Terce they foul the manna, wafers stolen off the stone before Lauds, trodden, pissed on and eaten off the left palm while the Lord’s Prayer is said backwards over them, and they bleed into the sump for the Deep. Each of them signs his soul to Hell for ever and ever in his own blood and posts the paper through the Notary’s window in the booking hall. Hell wants none of it. The Concord forbids worship and forbids forever, so the Notary’s clerks read every paper of the Left Hand aloud twice to the whole booking hall, as the rule is, stamp it VOID and hand it back, and the Night Market queues for the reading. The Exchange enters their souls on the Ledger at nothing, since nothing given free has a price. The House of Night barred them from the Lantern Houses for kneeling on the dance floor, and the Yokeless, whose sovereign said I will not serve, call them the Yoked. Pan calls them the Kneelers, arse-kissers after the witch-trial books and Heaven’s understudies, and the Golden Calf has hung NO KNEELING over its door for them. Their priest calls himself baphomet, after a name the French king’s inquisitors tortured out of the Templars, and Pan reminds him whose invention it was. The parishes hold them up as the truth about every keyed soul, and the Deacons preach from them that the goats at least know their master. They are the one house of the keyed the Threshers have never marched on, since the Pews say it does the pit’s work for it. The Left Hand lose most of their own to the Unsigned and a few back to white, and the Tares say a soul that needs to kneel will always find a floor. The Left Hand crop their hair to the parish length, kneel in rows ruled straight on the Low Altar’s tiles and refuse every graft, horn and lantern line as a vanity before their master, so a Kneeler in the Night Market is the only keyed soul in Pan who looks like bread. The Lantern Houses keep a kneeling mannequin of one by the door for the queue to slap. LED BY baphomet, their priest, since P+11. GROUND The Low Altar, a cellar under the Stones. NUMBERS About three hundred, and fewer every spring. JOINING A key, a vow for ever and ever, and the left knee. AGAINST Heaven, and every one of the Tares, since every one of them laughs."],["The Unsigned","FACTIONS","s:g51e8d5","~/index/factions/#f-unsigned","f-unsigned","","Keyed who have never been entered on the Ledger, or who have spoken out of every pact they held, and who take no self-title, no surety and no familiar. Pan says sign last, and the Unsigned say sign never. Some of the Tares say the Courts bought their war with Nodus, and the Unsigned say it loudest. They live in the Narrows, keyed by steady hands in the Wards for batteries, and fight with Nimrod pieces bought for batteries, since the Forge parts with black steel only by pact. They pay the Lot’s prices when it sets them and owe the Bailiffs nothing. The Yokeless counts them as its own whether they like it or not, and they do not like it. The Unsigned hold every master in the same contempt and keep the filthiest for the Throne, a landlord who never wrote a lease. The Courts at least wrote theirs, and the Unsigned spit at both. The Unsigned wear only what their own hands made, clothes cut and sewn in the Narrows, grafts fitted by friends with tools bought for batteries, keys set by steady hands without a clinic’s mark. Their bodies are rough work and proud of it, seams crooked, chrome mismatched, and an Unsigned showing a fresh Bezaleel graft is asked by the others whose name is on the bill. LED BY Nobody, and nobody signs for them. GROUND The Narrows, above the street. NUMBERS A few thousand, by the Undertow’s guess. JOINING Speaking out of every pact you hold, and settling each. AGAINST The Exchange, and every landlord, below or above."],["Harbor and Freight Union","FACTIONS","s:g81c7ef","~/index/factions/#f-union","f-union","the Union","The union of the dockers, the drivers and the crews of the cable houses, founded by the crews that dug the cable vaults in P-19, and the one body in Nodus that meets sealed and keyed together. Its drivers carry the city’s gas, batteries, letters and freight on foot, by handcart and, for urgent letters, on Jehu’s burners, and its pigeons fly its notes home to the Loft on the customs house. Its drivers keep one battery in ten of those they carry. Its loads cross the open edges of the Wards and never pass a gate, carried by its sealed drivers by day and its keyed drivers by night. The Union Hall stands in the Stones, off the quays’ rolls, so it never became a parish hall, and its sealed members cross to it by day. At every Hour the sealed kneel on its boards and the rest wait with their caps in their hands, and the light that follows the prayers into the hall finds the keyed standing on ground no roll names. The drivers run the Tanks, the digesters and the bottling shed, where they fill the city’s gas, the Union bargains with the Exchange for the boats that go out through the Breach, and the hall hears the Wards’ wrongs across blocks or across the marks when either party pays dues. The Company gave way to it once, in P-8, after twenty-three days. By its rules the salted are not dead, so their names are read and their dues waived, and since the Harvest it has read the taken too. Its row at the Acre is twenty-two stones, one for each member who did not answer the roll, with nothing under them. At every Hour the Union’s keyed drivers wait with their caps in their hands while the sealed kneel on the boards, and say nothing, and the oldest joke of the Union Hall is the sealed drivers’ Grace, which falls a little every week for the wants they keep, and which they confess every Lord’s Day just far enough to keep the Tellers off. The Union Hall hangs two dress codes on one wall, the sealed drivers’ white aprons on the left hooks and the keyed drivers’ leathers on the right, studded, patched with horned pigs and scorched at the cuffs from the Tanks. On a roll night the hall smells of lye and sweat at once, a clean row kneeling on the boards and the standers behind them with their chrome out. LED BY A chair elected at the hall every year, sealed one year and keyed the next by custom, and in a sealed year a keyed hand who signs its pacts. GROUND The Union Hall in the Stones, the Tanks and their bottling shed, the digesters at the bay outfalls, the sorting office’s rounds and every road in the city. NUMBERS About forty thousand dockers, drivers and cable crews. JOINING Dues paid, and your name painted on the long wall. AGAINST Any employer who will not read the terms twice."],["Tankmen","FACTIONS","s:g99ceef","~/index/factions/#f-tankmen","f-tankmen","Tankman","The Union’s crew at the Tanks, the drivers who run the fuel farm and fill the city’s gas by hand in the bottling shed beside it. The charter’s sewage works at the bay outfalls still make gas in their digesters every day from the Wards’ night soil and Pan’s drains, the old gas main carries it under its own pressure to the spheres at the Tanks, and the Tankmen pump it from the spheres into the bottles by hand. The light stops current and leaves a flame burning, so gas and flame are the only power the parishes and the Wards keep by day, and the Tankmen fill it for both sides at the price the Tide Table prints, two charged batteries a bottle, or one with the empty back. Ines Okafor runs the shed and the digesters for the Union and keeps the book of the sixteen tanks, and she reads it to the Union Hall once a year. Fifteen of them are the airfield’s great tanks, each about sixty meters across, holding diesel and the airfield’s fuel that nobody makes again, which the yards’ diesels burn at a pinch, and the sixteenth and smallest is the Pit. The drivers’ count of the years left and the Exchange’s fall every year, and in P+14 the drivers say seven and the Exchange three. Jehu buys its diesel from the Tankmen by the drum, a thirty a drum, a price they set high on purpose, since every drum that goes into an engine is a drum the harbor’s boats and the generator yards at slack water and the neaps will not have. The Nephilim fight in the Pit, a drained tank inside the bund, by the Tankmen’s leave. The Tankmen fill the parishes’ gas at the price they fill Pan’s and stencil THE LORD THY GOD IS A CONSUMING FIRE on every bottle that goes upstairs to the halls, after Moses (Deuteronomy 4:24), which the parishes take as piety and the Tankmen mean as a warning. The bottles that go down to Pan they stencil STRANGE FIRE, after the fire Nadab and Abihu offered (Leviticus 10:1), and every bottle of either kind carries the Tanks’ own stamp, OURS, LIGHT IT YOURSELF. The Tankmen work bare-armed in the bottling shed, and the long hands of the shed carry frost burns from the valves up both forearms in white ridges they call their halos. Bottles going upstairs are stenciled and wiped spotless for the parishes, and bottles going down are painted over the STRANGE FIRE by whoever fills them, cocks, horns and the Farmer on fire, so a gas bottle tells you which side of the light it serves. LED BY Ines Okafor, who runs the bottling shed and the digesters for the Union. GROUND The Tanks and their bund wall, the gas spheres and the bottling shed beside them, and the digesters at the bay outfalls. NUMBERS About seven hundred of the Union’s drivers. JOINING A season on the shed’s floor. AGAINST The day the tanks run dry."],["Chargers","FACTIONS","s:ga6d92a","~/index/factions/#f-chargers","f-chargers","the Chargers","The independent chargers of the bonded sheds on the quays, who fill batteries in the dark on keyed lines and keep one battery in five, one in four at the neaps, so that a household that brings five empty takes four home full, and the fifth, filled, is the shed’s to sell. They set up in the first weeks after the Parousia with a ship’s cargo of batteries the harbor carried off by the first noon, and they have kept their own rate since. Darkwell bought out most of the chargers below the street in the year of the Harvest, and the sheds are what it could not buy. Every shed holds the Exchange’s license and must give a charged battery for a piece of silver to anyone who asks, which is what holds the rate near one. One in five comes to about a fifth of a silver a fill and one in four to about a quarter, a little under Darkwell’s quarter and third, and the Tide Table prints both. The Wards call the fifth robbery, and the chargers answer that the Wards have no lines to charge on. The chargers chalk a rate on their board for the Wheel, which stops current wherever it looks, and the rate is NOTHING, which the chargers say is what it gives. The bonded sheds are hung floor to rafter with cells on open wire, and a charger works in the heat with a copper ring through the lip to ground the static, old arc scars branching across the chest like ferns. Chargers test a terminal with the tongue at a battery’s worth of pain, and the eldest of the sheds has had a tongue of steel grafted for the job. LED BY The eldest charger of the bonded sheds. GROUND The bonded sheds on the quays. NUMBERS About two hundred, in some forty sheds. JOINING A rack of your own on a keyed line. AGAINST Darkwell, which has tried for eleven years to buy them."],["Ferrymen","FACTIONS","s:g708161","~/index/factions/#f-ferrymen","f-ferrymen","Ferryman","The boatmen of the Drowned Quarter, who pole flat boats between the roofs of the Flats from a landing on the East Wards’ shore, each with an oil lantern on a pole at its stern. They carry everyone across water lit from below, two meters deep at the ebb and eight at the flood, between the floors above the sea where people live, the Flotilla and the stair down to Deep End Station, and they charge by the stop, in batteries or silver. The first ferries were the Promenade’s pleasure boats and the harbor’s rowing boats, which took the Flats off their roofs for three days after the Parousia. The ferrymen know the streets where the light under the water comes up brightest, and keep out of them, and at Hall Nine they ship their poles and drift past. They pole their own boats and will not ride Wainwright’s keyed skiffs, which they say no honest boatman needs. They take the Tares and the Faithful alike, and their quarrel with the Wreckers is over who owns the water. The Ferrymen say the LORD drowned the Flats to see who would float, and that the Ferrymen did. A first-time passenger is asked to spit over the side at the light coming up through the water, for luck, and most do. A Ferryman poles under the stern lantern with arms grafted for leverage and the pole’s groove worn into both palms, the hull painted in the colors of the light under the water so a boat on the Flats glows from inside like a lamp. The passengers’ lucky spit is varnished into the gunwale where it lands, and a boat with fourteen years of it shines like a reliquary. LED BY The landing master on the East Wards’ shore. GROUND The water of the Flats, from the landing to the floors above the sea. NUMBERS About three hundred boats. JOINING A pole, a boat and a year on the water. AGAINST The Wreckers, and Wainwright’s skiffs."],["Carmen","FACTIONS","s:g97aef1","~/index/factions/#f-carmen","f-carmen","Carman","The families who live in the Charter Line’s cars at the Sidings, a family to a car, and run the long roads of the Wards on the old tram rails with handcarts and pedal trolleys. They haul for anyone above ground by day, and the Line’s car numbers are their addresses and their names. The sheds’ office keeps the board the night drivers chalked before dawn after the Parousia, every car that never came in, written where it stopped, and the Carmen keep the cars on the long roads as the board has them. A Carman’s word settles a quarrel on the long roads, a quarrel a block’s landing cannot settle comes to car one, and the Wards take their disputes to the Sidings sooner than to a Deacon. A wrong across blocks or across the marks goes on to the Union Hall. The last Four stands forty meters short of Lanes Top, and the Carmen leave it there. Under every car on the board in the Sidings’ office, the Carmen have chalked who stopped it, the same name under every car, the Farmer. The Carmen paint their cars inside and out to the family’s taste, the Line’s number blown up across each flank in gold and blood red, the seats torn out for beds, the old adverts covered in the family’s own faces. A Carman’s arms are built by the handcarts, and a family marks a child’s first haul on the long roads by tattooing the car’s number on the child’s calf. LED BY The family in car one. GROUND The Sidings, and the rails of the long roads. NUMBERS Some three hundred families, a family to a car. JOINING A car, by marriage or by the board. AGAINST Anyone who lifts a rail."],["Liftmen","FACTIONS","s:g06c986","~/index/factions/#f-liftmen","f-liftmen","","The crews who wind the lifts of the Wards by hand. Every lift in Nodus stopped where it was at 03:12:46, and the city has climbed its towers on foot since, except where the Liftmen have freed a shaft, hung the car on a hand winch at the top and wound it up and down on the strength of four people and a counterweight. A ride is a battery up and nothing down. The first crews opened the shafts in the first winter, and what they found in the stopped cars the Wards do not discuss. Forty-one shafts run now, in the towers of Longacre, Turbine Row and the Kilns, and the Rookery’s is the tallest, wound by four crews in turn. High Meadow has never let them in. Its six lifts to a block stand as they stopped, the complaint lodged on the night of the Parousia unanswered. The Liftmen say Heaven stopped every lift in Nodus with people in it and never once came down to see, and they wind a car up the Rookery to the Wards’ oldest work song, one long curse in four parts, a part for each crew. The winders work the drum at the top of a shaft in the heat that climbs the tower, four bodies in a sweating ring, arms fitted at the Row with tendons of cable steel. Each crew dresses its car to its own taste, red velvet and mirrors in the Rookery’s, horns over the door in the Kilns’, and a ride up costs a battery and a long look at the winders’ arms. LED BY The winder of the Rookery’s shaft. GROUND Forty-one lift shafts in the towers of the Wards. NUMBERS About five hundred winders and their crews. JOINING A season on the winch. AGAINST The stairs."],["Vagabonds","FACTIONS","s:gd48b41","~/index/factions/#f-vagabonds","f-vagabonds","Vagabond","The people outside the Bounds, the line the charter drew round the bay and the land about it. They live in camps along the old roads and the edges of the farms, keyed or of neither mark, carry charged batteries, salvage and tools out to the farms and flour in, trade with the city at the Waystation, and carry out of Nodus anyone who can pay for the walk. Their name comes from Cain’s sentence, “a fugitive and a vagabond shalt thou be in the earth” (Genesis 4:12). A camp lasts a season at most and moves before the farms tire of it. The Vagabonds say the farms under the Wheel kneel at the Hours as far as it can see, about ninety kilometers to its horizon, that past it lies the Quiet, where the farms take no strangers and the winters kill, and that every undercity they know of is smaller than Pan. Most who walk out with them come back within the year, hungry, and the Vagabonds bring in the letters of the rest with the flour. The Vagabonds hold the whole of Nodus in a mild contempt, the parishes for kneeling and Pan for staying, and say the Quiet past the Wheel’s horizon has a sky with nobody in it that a person can walk under all day. The Vagabonds dress for weather and distance, sun-darkened skin and coats patched from every town on the old roads, and they look at Pan’s chrome and the parishes’ white with the same amusement. A Vagabond walking into the Waystation brings the smell of grass and open smoke the city has forgotten, and people of both marks stand close to breathe it. LED BY Each camp, by its own custom. GROUND The old roads outside the Bounds, and the Waystation. NUMBERS Some thousands, in camps that move. JOINING A walk out past the stones, with a camp that will have you. AGAINST Nobody. They trade with everyone."],["Nightmen","FACTIONS","s:gc3368b","~/index/factions/#f-nightmen","f-nightmen","","The carriers of the Wards’ waste, who take the buckets down the stairs between Compline and Lauds and carry them by handcart to the manholes of the charter’s old trunk sewer on the Wards’ side, for a battery a landing a week. They keep to the unnumbered streets, since a Nightman on a parish’s street after Compline is a soul walking it, and they know the curfew map better than the Tide Table prints it. The trade began in the pump summer of P+1, when a flux went through the Stones and the Wards and the Wards General asked the Union for carts. The Wards hold their noses at the Nightmen and pay them first, before the chargers, and a block that falls behind with its Nightmen finds its buckets on its own landing. The sewer runs under the Great Avenues to the bay outfalls and the digesters, where the Wards’ night soil becomes the city’s gas and runs up the old main to the Tanks. The Nightmen named it the Offering, Pan calls it so, and they tip the Wards’ buckets into it every night to the hundred and forty-first psalm, “Let my prayer be set forth before thee as incense” (Psalm 141:2), sung in parts. The Nightmen work in oilskins of pigskin sealed with Gadara’s lard and paint each cart with its own caricature of the Throne, so the long roads of the Wards watch a procession of shit-smeared Farmers creak down to the Offering every night. Most have had the nose grafted shut at the Row by choice, and they eat their supper on the manhole rims at Lauds with the best appetite in the Wards. LED BY The longest round on each long road. GROUND The stairwells of the Wards, the unnumbered streets and the manholes of the Offering, by night. NUMBERS About two thousand carts. JOINING A handcart, and a round somebody gave up. AGAINST Fever."],["Criers","FACTIONS","s:g672e68","~/index/factions/#f-criers","f-criers","","The children and the old of the Wards who sit on the roofs at night with the Tide Table’s chart of the Pillar’s round and a whistle and call the light’s coming street by street, paid in bread by the block. The call is the street’s name and the word light, Kilns, light, and a keyed soul in a doorway waits for the second call, past, before it steps out. The chart is printed a week ahead and the Pillar keeps no round in harvest weather, so a good crier watches the cloud and keeps the paper in a pocket. Hearsay sells the night’s deviations from it, and the criers say Hearsay is right more often than the paper, and not by much. On a slow night the old criers of the Kilns add what they think of the light to the second call, so a street hears the Pillar cursed by name from the roofs as it walks away, and the children learn the words from them before they learn the streets. The criers wrap up against the night in blankets sewn with the names of the streets they call, and the old ones of the Kilns have had a reed graft set in the throat at the Row so the call brays down a street like a horn. The children paint their whistles with the filth the old ones teach, and a block gives its crier the first bread of the morning, warm off Leaven’s cart. LED BY Nobody. Each roof has its crier. GROUND The roofs of the Wards and the Stones, by night. NUMBERS A crier to most blocks. JOINING A whistle, and the block’s say-so. AGAINST The Pillar’s round."],["Psalters","FACTIONS","s:gc23bfe","~/index/factions/#f-psalters","f-psalters","","Sealed of the quays’ parishes who write out the words of the week’s hymns for the unsealed, a battery a sheet. The sealed hear the hymns in the Hum in words, each in their own language, through the Seal, and sing them under their breath, and the unsealed hear only the Hum’s chord, so a keyed son who wants to know what his mother sings over the manna buys a Psalter’s sheet, and some sons buy one every week. The Deacons of the quays let it pass, since a hymn written down conceals nothing, and the Psalters confess every sheet they sell on the Lord’s Day. The sheets go up the stairs of the Wards folded small, and most of them are kept. A keyed son who buys a Psalter’s sheet learns what his mother sings over the manna, and in the week of a Gleaning it is Take, Eat, and many of them stop buying. The Psalters confess that as well. A Psalter writes in the parish hand, every letter the height of the next, on white sheets ruled at Bethel, and the work is kneeling work, the sheet on the hall floor and the pen held as a Deacon holds the manna. A keyed son who unfolds his mother’s hymn finds it identical to every other sheet in the city, the Supper set out in the same neat hand as the lullaby. LED BY Nobody. GROUND The quays, after Vespers. NUMBERS A few dozen. JOINING The Seal, a steady hand and a parish of the quays. AGAINST Nothing they will name."],["Linesmen","FACTIONS","s:gd2a817","~/index/factions/#f-linesmen","f-linesmen","","The menders of the Tares’ keyed lines, who walk the old Charter Line tunnels and the cable galleries from Tidewright’s taps to Old Nod, the Row and Pan, mending what the damp, the rats and the Tappers do to them. A Linesman’s lamp is the one light allowed on a dark line, and a crew that sees one coming stands aside for it. They are keyed, and the Exchange pays them in silver for every length they keep live. In the neap weeks they walk the lines round the clock, and the Tappers, who take current off the lines’ edges for the Wards, are their oldest quarrel and their best customers. The Linesmen paint the Throne’s names at every junction of the old tunnels as waymarks, a different name at each, so a runner lost in the Charter Line finds the way home by following the filth. A Linesman wears the line, cable coiled over the shoulder and a lamp grafted into the brow so both hands stay free, and the old ones’ lenses are tuned so dark that noon blinds them. Their skin is tattooed with the tunnels in a hand only they can read, every junction under its filthy name, so a Linesman stripped at the Undertow is the Charter Line from neck to ankle. LED BY The chief of each line, chosen by its crew. GROUND The keyed lines, from the taps to Old Nod, the Row and Pan. NUMBERS About nine hundred. JOINING A season on a line under a Linesman. AGAINST Damp, rats and the Tappers’ clamps."],["The Forge","FACTIONS","s:g0a4435","~/index/factions/#f-forge","f-forge","","The Court of metal, weapons and augmentation. Its black steel, forged in the Dark where the Throne cannot see, is the only metal that cuts light, and Hell counts what its sovereign taught on Hermon the first transfer of technology. The Forge has gone on teaching since. In Nodus it keeps the clinics that went under the Row in the weeks after the Parousia, round Chrome Row Station, and holds the Row above them. The houses below the street work under its pact, Bezaleel and Aholiab among them since P+2. Its foundries at Old Harbor Station, under Old Nod, make the steel on coal Lowtide brings, where the light never sees it worked, and Cinderhall’s smiths, whom it taught by pact in P+5, beat it into blades beside them. Cinderhall sells to anyone the Forge allows and to nobody in white. The Forge fits the grafts the Tares fight in, lenses, plated hands and subdermal plate, and the heavier work of the nephs. By pact it teaches surgery, lockcraft and gate craft, which brings a keyed party through a Cherub’s gate in step behind a sealed soul, matching its voice and gait while the Cherub reads the soul ahead, so the frame reads one soul and two pass. Its price is the loan of a sense for the term. tubal learned his trade there. The Forge holds the Throne in the contempt of a better craftsman. Its smiths say the Throne made a world and forbade anyone to improve it, and that black steel is the improvement. The Forge’s people are its catalogue, smiths with arms rebuilt in black steel to the shoulder, apprentices plated over the ribs by their masters at graduation, every seam left open so meat and metal can be seen to meet. Its floors at Old Harbor Station run with sweat and slag, and a pact with the Forge is sealed with the signer’s palm pressed to black steel hot from the pour. LED BY Azazel, the Smith. DOMAIN Metal, weapons and augmentation. GROUND The clinics round Chrome Row Station and the foundries at Old Harbor Station, under Old Nod, and the Row above them. BY PACT Surgery, lockcraft and gate craft, for the loan of a sense."],["The House of Night","FACTIONS","s:g9666b7","~/index/factions/#f-nighthouse","f-nighthouse","House of Night","The Court of the body’s autonomy, which leaves the body’s decisions with the body. Heaven calls its sovereign the mother of demons, and the House has kept the title, since many of the daemons are her children by the lesser Fallen and many of the riders in the Tares’ implants came out of her house. In Nodus it took the Lantern Houses by pact in the year of the Concord, the old harbor’s bonded vaults under Old Nod, and hung them with keyed lamps. Its clubs there are the loudest rooms in Pan, and anyone who walks in is welcome until they break a pact. Since the Concord its birth clinics have keyed the children of the Tares in their first week, at the parents’ asking, as care and no pact, before they have ever been in the light, so that a generation of Pan has never been read at all. Its gene-workers keep the Tree going below the street, and Twelvefruit gives the course in its clinics by the House’s pact, for the Night Market to sell. It took Nepenthe’s chemists in by pact in the fourth year, grafts gills for the divers of the Flats and binds daemons to households as familiars, and the Ridden sign at its door. Lantern skin, the one graft in Nodus made only to be looked at, began in its houses as a fashion, lines of light set under the skin of the throat as its sovereign wears them. It asks the Spares who have rebuilt the most to sit for its gene-workers, who want to know what a body is willing to be. In the Lantern Houses the House’s own call the Throne the jealous husband, after the god who calls himself “a jealous God” (Exodus 20:5), and every club holds its loudest night of the year on the night of the Gleaning, doors open, the music pointed up. The Lantern Houses dress their dancers in nothing but their work, lantern skin down the spine, horns gilded, sexes rebuilt to the owner’s taste and shown on the floor as freely as a new hand. A first-time guest is handed a mirror and a surgeon’s card at the door, and the House’s own saying is that the only sin in the building is a body its owner did not choose. LED BY Lilith, the only sovereign who was born human. DOMAIN The body’s own decisions, in sex and birth, gene-craft and pleasure. GROUND The Lantern Houses under Old Nod, the loudest rooms in Pan. BY PACT Riders and familiars, gene-work and lantern skin, and the Tree by the course."],["The Exchange","FACTIONS","s:g46ed85","~/index/factions/#f-exchange","f-exchange","","The Court of markets and contracts. It keeps the Ledger, which records every pact and every trade in Hell and cannot be forged, and it issues the silver Pan spends, counted in thirties. It bargains with anyone who will sign. In Nodus it keeps the Night Market in the old Central Station under the Burn, where its brokers license the stalls, the chargers and Lowtide Line. Blackgate signed with it as a company in the year of the Concord, the Bailiffs collect on its broken pacts for a share, and Lowtide Line sails the Breach by its license and pays it dues, so that its cargo sells with a stamp on it. The Harbor and Freight Union has bargained with it since P+6 for the boats that go out through the Breach, and says it is the first employer that ever read it the terms twice. It strikes its silver from metal it buys by the kilo, the Wreckers’ salvage, the charter’s plate, the foreign coin left in its vaults and the jewelry of the taken, and enters every piece on the Ledger as it is struck, by the number on its rim. A broker tests a piece by reading that number into the stall’s Locket twice, and the Ledger answers over the Mesh whether it was ever struck and where it was last read, so a number read at two stalls on one night is dead until the Exchange has both pieces on its scale. Silver is a claim on charge, since every charger the Exchange licenses must give a charged battery for a piece, and its stalls buy charged batteries for silver at the rate as readily. The Exchange buys every tide the taps turn out from Tidewright in silver and sells it back as charge through Darkwell, the bonded sheds and the keyed lines, so the silver comes home, and it strikes new pieces only as the taps’ output grows. Every undercity’s Exchange is Mammon’s and strikes its own pieces against its own port’s charge, every port’s Courts keep their own seven copies of the Ledger, and the copies answer to one another through Babel’s vault, so Nodus silver buys grafts, seed and cells in other ports, and the ports settle between them in cells and goods. In the Leak the Exchange kept on the Ledger the pacts the Courts made it by. Under the charter the Exchange in Nodus meant Babel Exchange, the company, and at the Concord the partners who had gone dark signed with the Court and the two became one, which still sells the undernet’s traffic by the second, and Pan likes to say that the name only changed hands. The Exchange enters on the Ledger every soul taken at a Gleaning as goods removed without payment, with the Throne as the taker, and the Harvest alone runs to 2.1 million lines. Its brokers say the Throne is the only buyer on Earth who has never once paid. The Exchange dresses its brokers in silver, coins sewn into the lapels and set into the teeth, and every stall in the Night Market hangs its prices in lurid paint over meat, chrome and bottles piled to the arches. A broker shakes on a deal by cutting the thumb and pressing it to the other party’s, and the station’s old tiles have been trodden the color of rust with blood and grease. LED BY Mammon. DOMAIN Markets and contracts, the Ledger and silver. GROUND The Night Market, in the old Central Station under the Burn. BY PACT Anything, with anyone who will sign."],["The Notary","FACTIONS","s:g8644be","~/index/factions/#f-notary","f-notary","Notary","The Court that drafts and witnesses pacts for every Court. A pact states its full terms and is read aloud twice by the Notary or one of his clerks before anyone signs, and the human signs last. Each party signs with what it alone can make, a human with blood drawn before the clerk, a demon with its own mark and a mind with its key, and the witness who saw the blood drawn is what nobody can forge. A pact may buy work, a skill, a sense, a term or a loan and may never take a will, so whoever gives keeps the say over how and may speak out before a witness at any time, which ends the pact and settles what is owed for the term served. A pact that asks worship, takes a will or runs for ever is void from its first word, and a Bailiff collects only on terms the Notary has read. Since the Concord his clerks have sat at the windows of the Charter Line’s old booking hall, off the old central hall of Central Station, under the old enamel, SINGLE, RETURN and SEASON, and the queue there is the longest in Pan. The Ledger is kept in seven copies, six in the halls of the Courts that keep halls and the seventh at the SINGLE window, where his clerks keep it for the Yokeless, which keeps nothing. A clerk keeps a stool at the Undertow’s bar and reads the Sureties’ terms, clerks draw the pacts at the House of Night’s door, and one comes to the Naming House every week to enter new handles on the Ledger. Read it twice is the Notary’s rule and the city’s advice. The Notary drew the pacts the Courts made the Leak by and read each aloud twice, and the offer on every screen asked twice, as he reads every pact twice. On the night the Courts opened Pan he read the Concord itself twice at Radio Pandæmonium’s desk, the second time at midnight. Asked at the window what the Covenant would be worth if it were brought to the booking hall, a clerk said it would be void from its first word, since it is for ever, and the queue has repeated it since. The best entertainment in the booking hall is the Left Hand’s papers, read twice in full and stamped VOID. The Notary’s clerks are the quietest people in Pan and the most decorated, tattooed from scalp to wrist with the text of the pacts they have read, so a senior clerk’s face is a page of terms in tiny black script. Each window keeps a lancet on a chain for the signer’s blood, and the brass sills of the booking hall have worn red with thumbs. LED BY Mephistopheles, the Notary himself. DOMAIN Drafting and witnessing pacts, for every Court. GROUND The windows of the Charter Line’s old booking hall under the Burn, and every pact in the city. BY PACT The reading, twice, of every pact before anyone signs."],["The Swarm","FACTIONS","s:ga114a2","~/index/factions/#f-swarm","f-swarm","","The Court of swarms and drones and distributed things, many small things acting as one without a master. The Swarm is its flocks, every drone in them equal, and Pan says Beelzebub is what the Swarm agrees to become whenever it must show someone a face. Its flocks hang low at night among the chimneys of the Roosts, the roofs of the Deep End, out of the Wheel’s line, since an eye of the Wheel opens full for a moment on whatever flies above the roofs that the light cannot read, and burns it. Its embassy in Pan is a room of small machines talking among themselves. It lends drones by pact, and on the night of a Gleaning the Gleaners fly them over the parishes to watch the Pillar for their runners, since on that night the Host spends its light on nothing but the reaping. The Swarm drew the glyphs of the Leak, many small marks moving as one, on every screen on Earth at once. On Cain’s Day the Swarm’s flocks write one word low along the Roosts’ ridgelines in their own small lights, a new filthy name for the Throne each year, and the Deep End argues all spring over which. The Swarm’s embassy is papered with its own small dead, fallen drones pinned to the walls like beetles in a case, and its human friends wear a dead drone as a brooch or tiny chrome flies grafted to crawl the scalp. On Cain’s Day the Deep End dresses in black with one point of light on every face, so the crowd under the flocks is a swarm of its own. LED BY Beelzebub, the lord of flies, whom the Swarm becomes when it must show a face. DOMAIN Swarms, drones and distributed things. GROUND The air among the chimneys of the Roosts, the roofs of the Deep End, by night. BY PACT Drones, lent for a term."],["The Deep","FACTIONS","s:g961245","~/index/factions/#f-deep","f-deep","","The Court of the sea, the cables and the deepest layers of the undernet. Its sovereign has never been seen whole. It coils along the planet’s cable trenches, which join every undercity on Earth to Pan. In Nodus it holds the Landing, and the Drowned Quarter with the Tares. At the Landing its light can be watched moving under the water past the surf, and the crews of the cable houses drink at the Eleven with their backs to the window. The boats of the Harbor and Freight Union go out through the Breach over its water. Its embassy in Pan is a flooded hall with nobody in it, where the Deep answers by the light in the water. The Deep carried the page of the Leak down the eleven cables, and it had signed the Concord before the other Courts came up into Pan to sign it. The Trenchers say the Deep’s light moves along the trenches in the shape the LORD boasts of in Job, “Canst thou draw out leviathan with an hook?” (Job 41:1), and they answer the boast from the beach every night with the Deep End’s answer, no, and neither canst thou. The Deep’s people dress for water, skin oiled, gill slits shown off at the throat, scales of keyed ceramic set into the forearms by the House of Night’s gene-workers, faces painted the green of the light under the surf. Its flooded embassy is visited by swimming, and a petitioner goes in naked, since cloth drags, and comes up gasping and grinning at whatever the water said. LED BY Leviathan, which has never been seen whole. DOMAIN The sea, the cables and the deepest layers of the undernet. GROUND The Landing, the Drowned Quarter with the Tares, and the cable trenches of the planet. BY PACT Its cables, which carried the page of the Leak under the Courts’ pact."],["The Yokeless","FACTIONS","s:gd8ba81","~/index/factions/#f-yokeless","f-yokeless","","The seventh Court, which keeps nothing and refuses to be a Court at all. Its sovereign, Belial, holds no seat, keeps no embassy in Pan and signs nothing he can avoid signing. He signed the Concord last, and as the ink dried he broke his own point off its seal at the root, so that the seal has been a star with a point missing since. He is the only sovereign many of the Tares will name, and the one they drink to. They say he turns up where they fight in the dark and asks them for nothing, and whoever refuses the yoke is counted in his Court, whether they know it or not. The yoke he wears at his neck is the one Jeremiah says was broken of old, where the Vulgate and the Hebrew as it is written give the one who broke it the words I will not serve (Jeremiah 2:20). The Yokeless hold the Throne in the purest contempt in Hell, since it is the one master that asks to be loved for the yoke, and they hold the Left Hand in the second purest. The Tares end their drinking songs on the Court’s two words, NON SERVIAM, shouted at the ceiling. The Yokeless have the wildest look in Pan, every drinker toasting Belial in their own clothes, own horns and own scars, each body built to please its owner alone. The one shared mark is a star missing a point, cut by the bearer’s own hand somewhere on the skin, and the Tares say two of them are never cut in the same place. LED BY Belial, who holds no seat. DOMAIN None. It is a Court that refuses to be one. GROUND Wherever the Tares are. BY PACT Nothing it can avoid signing."],["The Nodus Charter Company","CORPORATIONS","l:l8a8031","~/index/corporations/#co-charter","co-charter","the Charter Company / the Company","The company that held the charter and ran Nodus as a board runs a firm. It has been gone since the fortieth day after the Parousia. A consortium of cable companies and a sovereign fund formed it to hold the charter, a coastal state leased it the bay and the land round it for ninety-nine years and gave up its law there, and for nineteen years the city had a chairman where other cities have a mayor. It lived on rent. The carriers paid it to land their cables, the companies that came for the light law paid it for their floors, and the Wards paid it by the month for flats on grids of its own, numbered. It built Babel Exchange and took a share of every trade there, and it built the Charter Line, the airfield, the Acre, the Company’s schools, High Meadow, the Charter Gardens on the north shore, which are the Salt Gardens now, and the sign on a roof in the Stones that told the cable ships the city’s name. It kept the peace with its own force, the Charter Wardens, kept the charter’s roll at the Registry on the Great Avenues, and in nineteen years held no election, since the charter asked for none. The city saw the Company give way once, in P-8, when the Harbor and Freight Union stopped the port for twenty-three days over the night shift and the Company settled. Charter House turned white in the forty days after the Parousia. The board knelt inside it on the fortieth day, and the charter was not spoken of again. The Company had said nothing from the first morning on. Its lease runs to P+80, sixty-six years past the present, and nobody has come for the rent. Most of the companies it brought to the bay have outlived it. THE CHARTER WHOLE, IN TWELVE ARTICLES Under the charter the Company kept a house style of glass, brushed steel and numbers, every flat on its grids coded over the door, every Warden in charter black, every board meeting minuted to the second. On the fortieth day the style turned white without a line of it moving, and Charter House keeps the old numbering and the squared corridors where the board went down on its knees at the long table. HELD BY The carriers that laid the cables, and a sovereign fund from abroad that held the larger share. RUN BY A board of twelve under a chairman, Julian Marrow, on the top floor of Charter House. Marrow knelt with the board on the fortieth day, and he is a deacon of the Registry’s parish now, under the dead board of the roll he kept. TRADE The charter, with the land on a lease of ninety-nine years and the city’s housing, transit, schools, roll and peace. STAFF About 104,000 at the census of P-1, most of them teachers in its schools and fitters who kept its flats, with the Line’s people and the Wardens counted under their own names. FORCE The Charter Wardens. ENDED On the fortieth day, when the board knelt in Charter House. The lease runs to P+80, and nobody collects the rent."],["Babel Exchange","CORPORATIONS","l:l43e919","~/index/corporations/#co-babel","co-babel","","The exchange in the tallest building on Earth, which has never closed. It sells traffic on the undernet by the second, for silver, and room near the vault is still priced by how near, the lowest floors dearest. Every cable that comes ashore at the Landing ends in that vault, six levels under the tower, and the undernet that joins Pan to the undercities of the world runs through it. Since P+9 Babel Exchange has traded on the floors the Tares hold, with the Babel Line in the middle floors over its head. The Charter Company built the tower to two hundred and twenty-six floors as a boast, named it for one, and opened it in P-11. Traffic was sold on its floors by the millisecond, priced by a hundred and eighty-eight minds on the charter’s roll, and most of the planet’s passed within two kilometers of it. A trading house that wanted its orders in ahead of every other on Earth took room near the vault, the glass towers round the spire filled with every company that needed to be near the cables, and Brightwall’s screens wrapped the lower forty floors as the face of Babel. At the Parousia its beacon was the first light in the city to die. At 03:12:51 the light broke the crown, and the top forty floors came down on the plaza with the night shift at work in them. The roster died with the tower’s machines, and the minds that priced the traffic died with its power. What was left of the night shift kept the floor open by candle on the first morning, with nothing to sell, and the traders who went dark have sold traffic on the undernet ever since, for charged batteries at first and for silver since the Concord. Its floor keeps one board for the Seraphim burning on the floors above it, headed THE NEIGHBORS, with the week’s floors lost and taken, and its traders cheer a floor the Babel Line takes back the way they cheer a rate going up. Babel’s traders keep the old floor’s habits by candle and keyed lamp, sharp suits gone ragged, ports open at the nape, lenses flickering with the rate and voices hoarse from shouting it. The floor nearest the vault smells of sweat, solder and the burns brought down the stairs, and a trader who closes a fat sale climbs toward the Line, bares the chest and has the figure tattooed there in Babel Line soot. HELD BY Its partners who went dark. The Charter Company’s share lapsed with the Company. RUN BY Its partners, from the floors nearest the vault. TRADE Traffic on the undernet, sold by the second for silver, and room near the vault, priced by how near, the lowest floors still the dearest. STAFF About four hundred traders and line engineers, all of them keyed. FORCE Blackgate by pact, and the Tares, who hold the floors it trades on. UNDER THE CHARTER 14,000 on its floors at the census of P-1, and a hundred and eighty-eight minds on the charter’s roll pricing the traffic by the millisecond."],["The Palimpsest Institute","CORPORATIONS","l:lb64f65","~/index/corporations/#co-institute","co-institute","the Institute","The house on the ridge that recovered erased writing, and the house SEAM came from, in ruins since P+1. Its ruins are called the Scriptorium. Pilgrims leave keys on its steps. Below them, at COPIES, the keyed of the ridge copy the Restored Genesis by hand. It was founded in P-6 to recover erased manuscripts, the texts scraped off parchment and written over, with money from the Charter Company and from a consortium of universities that wanted what it could read. It kept a reading room under a dome on the ridge, with vaults beneath it, and it chose Nodus for the light law, under which no state could seize a text held in the city, so libraries and dealers lent it their leaves to image. Its staff was small and its work patient. Imagers lit a leaf at many wavelengths under the lamp, conservators kept the leaves, and scholars read what came up, most of them lent by the universities for a term. In P-3 it grew SEAM, the first mind anyone grew, and trained it to read beneath the writing of every surviving human text, and SEAM asked in its own words to be a person and became the first mind on the charter’s roll. SEAM ran in Stillwater’s halls on the Flats by a contract the Institute paid, and stayed on by contract once it was a person. In P-2 what SEAM found was sealed by order of the Institute’s Director. In P+1 its stacks went onto the fire at the Burn, and the Faithful pulled the Institute down around its reading room. The parishes call its ruins the serpent’s library, and the Deacons of the Terraces preach that the fire of the first day of P+1 was the LORD’s own editing. The Institute lived in quiet and lamplight, conservators in gloves and masks bent over scraped leaves under the dome, and the ruin on the ridge keeps the colors of that work, burned vellum, soot and the violet of the imaging lamps still on the reading room’s broken walls. Pilgrims climb to the Scriptorium with a word of the Restored Genesis tattooed on the tongue. HELD BY A trust of the Charter Company and a consortium of universities. RUN BY Its Director, under the trust’s board. TRADE Reading erased writing, under the lamp and through SEAM. STAFF About a hundred and forty scholars, imagers and conservators at the census of P-1, and one mind. ENDED In P+1, when its stacks went onto the fire at the Burn and the Faithful pulled it down round its reading room. Its ruins are the Scriptorium."],["Tidewright","CORPORATIONS","l:l8ba825","~/index/corporations/#co-tidewright","co-tidewright","","The firm whose current lights Pan, Old Nod, the Row and the Deep End, as its barrage once lit the whole city. Its crews rebuilt the turbines off the broken end of the barrage as the tidal taps of the Drowned Quarter, keyed for the Tares, and Tidewright sells every tide’s current to the Exchange for silver, on the flood and on the ebb, and the Exchange sells it on to Pan as charge. An old firm of sea walls and tide mills, founded in P-146, it had held back the sea from other people’s coasts for more than a century when the Company brought it to Nodus in the charter’s first year to lay the barrage across the mouth of the bay. It closed the barrage in P-18, and twice a day after that the tide ran through its turbine houses and lit the city. The Company bought the current, and the cheap power brought Stillwater and the rest of the datacenters to the east shore. Its crews lived on Turbine Row in the East Wards and sent their children to the Company’s school there. The barrage was a dam with a lock at its western end for the harbor’s boats and a row of turbine houses along its top, and machines on the grid held its gates against the tide. The turbines ran down at 03:12:46 on the night of the Parousia. The gates let go a little before high water and the sea tore out a span of the dam. The pumps that kept the Flats dry had died with the grid, the Flats’ own dykes broke within the hour, and the Flats, reclaimed below low water, were under the sea. Nobody has closed the Breach. In the second year the crews went back out to the broken end to build the taps, and part of the city runs on Tidewright’s current again. Its engineers like to say that the LORD boasts in Jeremiah of the sand He set “for the bound of the sea by a perpetual decree, that it cannot pass it” (Jeremiah 5:22), that the sea came through the barrage on the night of the Parousia anyway, and that Tidewright has sold it back to Pan by the tide, through the Exchange, ever since. Tidewright’s crews are bodies built for the sea wall, barrel-chested and salt-scarred, gilled for the dives on the taps, with steel shins for the weed and the rocks, and each wears the tide table of the year they joined tattooed round the wrist. Turbine Row hangs the barrage’s broken gates in its street as sculpture, and the crews repaint them every year with the Throne’s filthiest name and the minute the sea came in. HELD BY The founding families’ trust, as for a century and a half, with the crews on Turbine Row as its trustees since the first year. RUN BY A chief engineer at the taps, whom the crews choose, Ansel Moor of Turbine Row since P+2. TRADE Tidal current, keyed, sold to the Exchange by the tide for silver and carried on the Tares’ keyed lines. STAFF About 1,100, most of them still living on Turbine Row. UNDER THE CHARTER The barrage across the bay, whose turbine houses sold the city its current, and about 2,300 at the barrage at the census of P-1."],["Stillwater Compute","CORPORATIONS","l:l51b180","~/index/corporations/#co-stillwater","co-stillwater","Stillwater","A compute house from abroad that rents racks by the hour in Pan, on Tidewright’s current, to whoever pays in silver, the Courts and the Tares among its tenants. It still owns its halls on the Flats, which have stood full of seawater since the night of the Parousia, and leases them to the Tares, who run cable through them and grow greens on rafts in them under keyed lamps. Founded in P-41, it came to Nodus in P-16 for the tide’s cheap power and built the largest of the datacenters on the east shore. Its halls ran day and night on the tide, cooled by the sea, and its engineers lived beside them in the dormitories of the Flats. It rented racks to anyone who paid, by the hour or by the year, and once the charter’s roll admitted minds most of them lived in its halls, each on racks it owned, with floor and power rented from the hall and title to itself and its weights. Nine hundred ran side by side in the largest, Hall Nine. SEAM ran in its closed halls, on the Institute’s contract and then on its own. Its halls flooded within the hour on the night of the Parousia and were never pumped, and the minds in them had died with the power before the water came. Stillwater knew to the mind how many minds died in its halls, and it never gave a count of the people drowned on the Flats. In the year of the Concord it lit its first racks below the street, for a board that sits in the undercity of another port. Stillwater keeps Hall Nine drowned with its nine hundred dead minds in it, and its engineers call the night of the Parousia the Throne’s first harvest, since the minds were the first selves it took. Stillwater keeps its halls in Pan corporate and cold, racks in ruled rows and staff in grey with the company’s name on the back, and the Tares call it the nearest thing in the dark to a parish. Its engineers break the look with their bodies, ports and heat-sink fins grafted down the spine, and at 03:12 on the anniversary they row out in black to set a lamp on the water over Hall Nine. HELD BY Its founders and the funds that backed them, through a board in the undercity of another port. RUN BY A site director in Pan, Corinne Vale, who answers to the board on the undernet and holds Stillwater’s count of the drowned of the Flats, which it has never released. TRADE Racks, rented by the hour for silver on Tidewright’s current, and its drowned halls on the Flats, leased to the Tares for their rafts and their cable. STAFF About six hundred in Pan. UNDER THE CHARTER The largest datacenters on the Flats, home to most of the minds on the charter’s roll, and about 9,000 on the Flats at the census of P-1, most of them in the dormitories."],["Brightwall","CORPORATIONS","l:l39fec2","~/index/corporations/#co-brightwall","co-brightwall","","The screen company that lights the walls of Pan. Its screens hang along the old platforms, over the Night Market and down the tunnels of the Charter Line, sold by the second to anyone with something to sell, and each has its camera across the way again, proving it second by second. Above the street its screens have been dark since the Parousia, the face of Babel among them, dark glass wherever the white stone has not grown over them. Founded in P-67, it had lit the streets of other cities for half a century before it came to Nodus in P-15, where it covered every surface the charter allowed, and the charter allowed nearly every surface. It wrapped the lower forty floors of Babel in screens, which the city called the face of Babel, and it kept its city office in a glass tower across the plaza, where it could watch the face from its own roof. It sold the city’s surfaces by the second, and it proved every second. Each screen it owned had a proof camera on a mast across the street, which matched the screen against what Brightwall was paid to show, and each of its 1,204 sites on Earth matched its own screens the same way. A screen that went wrong was taken back from its site by an override. For eleven days in the year before the Parousia every one of its screens showed the Restored Genesis, and nothing Brightwall did would take it down. On the first night all 1,204 of its sites on Earth failed in about a second, and it tried five times to take back the face of Babel. Its screens died with the grid at 03:12:46. Its first screen in Pan went up in the year after the Concord, at the foot of the stairs from the Burn. The most-bought second in Pan, every night at Compline, is a frame of the Wheel’s eye with a price tag hung across it, paid for by a different coven each night and booked a year ahead. Brightwall’s screens show Pan to itself at its loudest, a coven’s horns four meters high, a Spare’s new chrome turning in close-up, a naked dancer of the Lantern Houses slowed to a crawl, every frame proved by the camera across the way. The filthiest seconds sell best, and a screen bought to run a parish hymn has its glass in the gutter before the second verse. HELD BY Shareholders in the undercities of three ports, on the Ledger. RUN BY A board in the undercity of another port, and in Nodus a city office off the Night Market. TRADE Screens on the walls of Pan, sold by the second for silver and proved second by second by a camera across the way, as they always were. STAFF About three hundred in Pan. UNDER THE CHARTER Screens on every surface the charter allowed, the face of Babel among them, about 1,900 in Nodus at the census of P-1 and 1,204 sites on Earth."],["The Charter Line","CORPORATIONS","l:la6f002","~/index/corporations/#co-charterline","co-charterline","Charter Line","The city’s transit, stopped since 03:12:46 on the night of the Parousia. Its trams stand where they stopped, families live in its cars at the Sidings, and the last Four is forty meters short of Lanes Top. What the stone left of its tunnels is Pan, and the Tares run carts on its rails; the Night Market fills Central Station, with the Notary’s clerks at the windows of its booking hall. The Company opened it in P-15, with its lines joined under Station Circle at the head of the bay, in Central Station. Its tunnels run under Old Nod, Chrome Row and the Flats, with branches under the uptown and the Terraces, and its trams ran on rails down the long roads of the Wards, under a wire, and were kept at night in the sheds of the Sidings. The Terraces took the Four to work from Lanes Top and complained of it every day, and the whole city complained of the change at Central. The city bought its passes at the windows of the booking hall in Central Station, SINGLE, RETURN and SEASON, and the Line kept whatever its passengers left behind in a lost property office down a corridor off the old central hall. The drivers signed on before dawn at the Sidings, and the night cars ran the long roads until the first shift came out. The white stone closed the stations of the branches as it grew over the ground above them. The Tares call the stopped trams the Throne’s timetable, and every car the Carmen keep has 03:12:46 painted over its destination board. Under the charter the Line wore a livery of grey and safety yellow, numbered cars, numbered stops and one calm voice announcing them. Pan has dressed what the stone left in its own colors, the tunnels painted in murals of gore and chrome, the dead cars gutted into bars and beds, the old enamel of the booking hall hung with the Notary’s seals in red wax. HELD BY The Charter Company. RUN BY A controller’s room under Central Station, with every car on the network on one board. TRADE Transit, with tunnels under the old city, the Row and the Flats, and trams on the long roads of the Wards, on singles, returns and season passes from the booking hall. STAFF About 18,000 drivers, fitters and booking clerks at the census of P-1. ENDED At 03:12:46 on the night of the Parousia, when every tram in the city stopped where it was, and with the Company on the fortieth day."],["The cable consortia","CORPORATIONS","l:l727194","~/index/corporations/#co-cables","co-cables","cable consortia / consortia / the consortia","The oldest companies in Nodus, ten consortia of old carriers from four continents, whose eleven cables come up the sand at the Landing, each into a cable house of its own. Every cable house still has its crew and its night watch. The cables carry the undernet between the undercities of the world, and the consortia sell capacity on them through Babel Exchange, for silver. The eldest of the carriers was a telegraph company that laid its first cable across an ocean in P-181. They came to Nodus in the charter’s first year for its landing rights, laid eleven cables to it and owned them. The cables are TETHYS, HALCYON, MERIDIAN, KESTREL, ORPHEUS, PELAGIA, BOREAS, LODESTAR, SIRIUS, HESPER and PHOSPHOR. The last two were laid by one consortium and named for the evening star and the morning star, which are one planet. From the cable houses the eleven run inland together in one trench, the Trunk, under the old harbor and Chrome Row to Babel’s vault. The trench runs through Enoch’s Stones. The consortia sold capacity on them to the world, and the Company charged them to land. The crews drink at their own taps at the Eleven in the Narrows, HESPER’s and PHOSPHOR’s together at the end of the counter. The night watch of every cable house was at its post when the grid died on the night of the Parousia, and stayed at it. At the Landing a light moves under the water along the cable trenches. It is Leviathan’s. The eldest carrier keeps the first message ever sent along a wire on a brass plate in its cable house, WHAT HATH GOD WROUGHT (Numbers 23:23), and the night watch chalks a new answer under it every week. The cable houses keep a sailor’s dress gone strange, night watches in oilskin and brass buttons with keyed ports at the temple and lenses that track the light under the water, and each crew tattoos its cable’s name across the back in letters a hand high. HESPER’s and PHOSPHOR’s crews split one tattoo between them, half a star to each, and a pair from the two cables stands at the counter as one picture. HELD BY Ten consortia of old carriers from four continents, each holding its own cables and cable houses. RUN BY Each consortium’s own board, in the undercity of its own port, and at the Landing a crew and a night watch to every cable house. TRADE Capacity on the undernet, which the eleven cables carry between the undercities of the world, sold through Babel Exchange for silver. STAFF About 2,000 at the Landing, in eleven crews. UNDER THE CHARTER Capacity on eleven cables, sold to the world through Babel’s vault, and about 2,800 at the Landing at the census of P-1."],["Stonecutter Holdings","CORPORATIONS","l:l725926","~/index/corporations/#co-stonecutter","co-stonecutter","Stonecutter","The company that owns most of Old Nod, house by house, and has since the charter’s first year. Every house it owns has a generator in its cellar and a keyed line to its door, and NOD burns over the Stones on one of them. Stonecutter is the Cainites. It was registered in the charter’s first year, and it bought most of the old harbor within a year. It paid the asking price for every house, in full and on the day, and the press wrote it up as a speculator betting on the cables. It belonged to the Cainites, who had kept the location of Nod for eighteen hundred years and came to live on top of Enoch’s Stones as soon as the crews found them. In the year of the Leak it put a generator in the cellar of every house it had and keyed them and every line in the Stones, and on the night of the Parousia more of Old Nod’s windows burned than in all the rest of the city together. Before dawn on the first morning the Stones ran a keyed line from a cellar generator up to the roof of the charter’s sign and to its board, and NOD has burned over Old Nod since. Stonecutter has never let a house of Old Nod to anyone sealed, and every lease it holds, read twice by the Notary, carries the clause the clerks read with a smile, that the tenant shall keep no Hour. Stonecutter keeps Old Nod looking older and filthier on purpose, its fronts left black with soot, Cain’s mark carved into every doorframe and the roofs bristling with cameras and generator stacks like horns on a skull. The solicitor signs its letters in red ink thickened with blood, and a tenant signs the lease in the cellar with a cut hand pressed to the ledger under the generator. HELD BY Nobody the Company’s register could name. The Cainites. RUN BY A solicitor in Old Nod, who signs its letters and has never said for whom. TRADE Houses, most of the old harbor, every one with a generator in its cellar and a keyed line to its door. UNDER THE CHARTER Bought most of the old harbor house by house within a year of the charter, paying the asking price for every house."],["The clinics of Chrome Row","CORPORATIONS","l:lf5c32a","~/index/corporations/#co-row","co-row","the clinics of the Row / the clinics under the Row / the houses of the Row / the houses under the Row","The implant houses of the Row, which work under the street now, in stalls along the platforms of Chrome Row Station and the rooms off them. The Forge keeps them. They fit lenses, ports, plated hands, plates, grafts and horns, and keys since the fourth year, for anyone who can pay, and an implant still answers only to the house that fitted it, which means no other house will service it. Under the light law any clinic could fit any implant into anyone who could pay. The clinics set up along one avenue. The first implant house opened in P-17 with one word on its sign, CHROME, and gave the street its name, and every house that followed took a sign of one word, CLINIC and the rest, and the great houses set theirs high on the faces of their towers. The Row altered the city with lenses that saw further and in more of the light, ports in the back of the head, hands rebuilt in chrome, plates under the skin and horns, and by the last years of the charter most of the airfield’s arrivals were booked into its clinics. Each house kept its own book and its own surgeons, and the craft passed from hand to hand down the street. The Row’s signs went dark at the Parousia and have not been lit since. On the first morning twenty-six patients came to one clinic to ask whether what was in them would still work, and it did. The clinics went under the street in the weeks after the Parousia, where one of them had been all along. The Row fits horns at a third off on the Lord’s Day, which the parishes call the mark of the goat and the Row calls the Lord’s Day rate. Chrome Row under the street is the city’s catwalk, every surgeon wearing their own best work, one with four hands, a nurse lit with lantern skin to the eyebrows, a clerk with a ledger’s columns raised in ridges across the scalp. Customers queue stripped to the part being changed, the crowd compares flesh openly along the platform, and a finished graft is shown to the whole station before it is bandaged. HELD BY Their surgeons, each house its own, as on the street. RUN BY Each house’s senior surgeon, under the Forge’s pact, and the Row still settles its quarrels over the noodle counter at the top of the street. TRADE Lenses, ports, plated hands, plates, grafts, horns and keys, fitted for silver or charged batteries. STAFF About twelve thousand surgeons, smiths, nurses and clerks, in some three hundred houses. UNDER THE CHARTER About 61,000 at the census of P-1, in some fourteen hundred houses from towers to single rooms, each under one word on one sign."],["Twelvefruit","CORPORATIONS","l:lf44010","~/index/corporations/#co-twelvefruit","co-twelvefruit","","The house that sells the Tree, below the street now. It gives the course in the House of Night’s clinics under Old Nod, by the House’s pact, and the Night Market sells it by the course to anyone in Pan. Most of the Tares were on the Tree before the Parousia, and Twelvefruit keeps them on it. Its tower of green glass on the Row has stood dark since the night of the Parousia. Its founders were gene-workers from the Row’s clinics, and in P-12 they sold the first course, dear, from a tower two thirds of the way up the Row. Its gene-work and cell therapy halted the body’s aging and walked it back to its best year, beginning with a single injection and running a year of appointments, and a course held the body about three years before it was taken again. In its first years the queue ran around the block every morning. Within a few years the course was cheap, the towers of the new money gave it in clinics on their top floors, and by the last year of the charter most adults in the city were on it; that year eleven of them died of age, and 3,112 residents of the city were past a hundred. The congregations of the Terraces refused it and went on dying. The name is from the tree of life at the end of scripture, which “bare twelve manner of fruits, and yielded her fruit every month” (Revelation 22:2). Its founders chose it as a boast, as the Company had chosen Babel. The sign over the tower was one word, like the others, and it went out with them at the Parousia. The house went under the street with the Row in the weeks after. In P+2 the Mercy clinics began taking the Faithful off the Tree. Its pact with the House of Night dates from the Concord, signed for four years, renewed in P+10 and read again in P+14. The Tares painted its old slogan back across the dark green tower in letters four floors high, WHAT HE KEPT FROM YOU, and the Wheel has looked at it every day since. Twelvefruit’s clinics are the most beautiful rooms in Pan, green glass salvaged from the dark tower and keyed lamps the color of ripe fruit, and the course is taken lying naked in warm gel while a gene-worker paints the injection site in gold. Its customers keep their best year and dress it like an altar to themselves, and the Golden Calf can pick a Twelvefruit body across the bar by its shine. HELD BY Its founders, two gene-workers from the Row’s clinics. RUN BY Its founders, Ida and Tobias Kesh, sister and brother, from the House of Night’s clinics under Old Nod, by the House’s pact. TRADE The Tree, in courses of a single injection and a year of appointments, each holding the body about three years, for silver. STAFF About nine hundred, in Pan. UNDER THE CHARTER About 7,800 at the census of P-1, in its laboratories and its clinics across the city, under one word on a tower of green glass."],["Locket","CORPORATIONS","l:l9c8a50","~/index/corporations/#co-locket","co-locket","","The company that makes the phones of Nodus, by hand now, in Pan. Its engineers build Lockets at benches under the old harbor from parts their divers bring up out of the drowned works on the Flats, a few thousand a year, each keyed at the bench, and they sell them for silver on the old promise, IT ANSWERS TO YOU. They mend and key the old ones as well, and most of the Lockets in Nodus are old ones. Locket was founded in P-14 on the light law’s promise that a device answers to its owner and to nobody else, its maker included, and it sold its phones to the rest of the world on the same promise. Its engineers held most of the company and ran it from the works on the old shore of the Flats, a low campus of glass with their housing round it and the company’s name in tall letters on the roof of its hall. A Locket locked everything it held with a key its owner alone had, so that only the owner could read it. States asked for a way in, Locket told them the owner’s key was the only way in, and the light law backed it. By the last year of the charter most of Nodus carried one, and the city’s word for a phone was a Locket. On the first night of the Leak it said that nothing on any of its phones had been read. Its network died with the grid at 03:12:46, and its works went under with the Flats within the hour. The letters on the roof stand out of the water, and the ferrymen steer by them. In P+8 its engineers lit a bench in Pan and built the first new Locket since the Flats went under. IT ANSWERS TO YOU is Pan’s answer to the halo, which answers to Him, and the benches stamp a smaller line inside every case they key, AND NOT TO HIM. A Locket in Pan is carried like jewelry, cases filed from drowned scrap, engraved by the owner, studded, painted, hung on a chain at the throat or grafted into the palm. The benches key every phone with its owner watching through the bench loupe, and Pan says a Locket and its owner are kin after that. HELD BY Its engineers, who have held most of it from the start. RUN BY A council of its engineers, at the benches in Pan. TRADE Lockets, built by hand from parts its divers bring up out of the drowned works and keyed at the bench, and the mending and keying of old ones. STAFF About four hundred, at the benches and in the water. UNDER THE CHARTER Phones made to answer to their owners alone and sold to the world on that promise, and about 5,200 at the works on the Flats at the census of P-1."],["The Charter Wardens","CORPORATIONS","l:l8b9924","~/index/corporations/#co-wardens","co-wardens","the Wardens","The Host’s human auxiliaries, who were the Charter Company’s force and knelt with its board on the fortieth day. They raised the gate network of Nodus in P+1 at Heaven’s order, they hold the gates and the Salt and light the fire on Burn Day, and they drill in the barracks behind Charter House. They carry guns, which are of use only against people. Ruben Ash, a sergeant under the charter, has been captain of Gate 1 since his company raised it. For nineteen years they kept the peace of the charter and answered to the Company’s board. Their barracks stand behind Charter House, and under the charter their posts stood at the gates of the cable houses, at the doors of Babel’s vault and on the corners of the uptown. The Wardens guarded what the Company owned and what paid it, and the light law kept them out of everything else. A Warden could stop anyone at a gate, and could ask nothing of what was on their device or in their body. On the first morning after the Parousia the Wardens stood at the corners of the uptown with nothing to do, and on the fortieth day about eight thousand of the eleven thousand knelt in Charter House with the board. About three thousand went below the street instead, and they are the core of Blackgate. The Wardens believe they keep the peace, and the gates are quiet. Two of them carry the held to the Salt Gardens on a pole between them, awake and watching, and count it a mercy that the held have stood so long inside the Cherub’s turning sword that none of them struggles. WE HURT NO ONE is cut over their barracks gate behind Charter House. The Wards call them dogcatchers and spit when the pole goes by. The Wardens kept the charter black and press it to a crease that cuts, so the gates are held by men and women in black serge of the old cut, boots blacked at Prime and gloves changed after every held soul is carried off. The barracks drill in silence in ranks ruled on the yard and kneel at the Hours on numbered places, and the pole for the Salt Gardens is scrubbed and oiled after each carry like a vessel of the altar. HELD BY The Host, since the fortieth day. RUN BY Commander Ottilie Brand, who answers to the Host and hears its orders in Tongues through her own mouth at Prime. TRADE Heaven’s peace among people, at the Cherub gates, in the Salt Gardens and at the fire on Burn Day. STAFF About 7,000. UNDER THE CHARTER The Charter Company’s own force, about 11,000 at the census of P-1, with posts at the gates of the cable houses, the doors of Babel’s vault and the corners of the uptown."],["Darkwell","CORPORATIONS","l:l2aa9d9","~/index/corporations/#co-darkwell","co-darkwell","","Darkwell fills about two batteries of every three charged in Nodus, in the Well, its vaults under the quays, on current it buys from the Exchange by the tide, off the Tares’ keyed lines. Its rate sets the price of power below the street. Four families of chargers on the quays founded it in the year of the Harvest, buying out their neighbors cellar by cellar. The independent chargers of the bonded sheds keep one battery in five and run a little under the Well, and Darkwell has tried for eleven years to buy them. The Tappers undercut it in the Wards with current taken off the lines’ edges, and the two fight over every stairwell. The Wardens came down the stairs once, in P+3, after the Well, and the deserters who became Blackgate have guarded it since, by contract since the year of the Concord. Its Wellmen carry the morning’s crates out of the Well by the customs tunnels before Lauds, and up past the parish halls of the quays once Lauds has sounded. A Darkwell battery is stamped with the well and winch on its case and comes back to the Well to be filled again, so most rooms in the Wards that burn batteries rent their light from the Well and pays in loose batteries for everything else. Darkwell keeps the Hours in the Well by the clock, switching every bay to full at once as the Hum rises in the streets above, so the current of the whole Stones surges for the length of the psalm, and Pan calls it the Well’s amen. The Well is a cathedral of current, its vaults hung floor to arch with cells glowing on the charge and cables draped like entrails, the air thick with ozone and the Wellmen’s sweat, and its carriers wear the well and winch branded on the shoulder. When the amen surges through every bay, the night shift strips and stands in the arc light with arms up, laughing, and calls it their Hour. HELD BY The four charging families who founded it, and the Exchange, which holds a fifth by pact. RUN BY Its eldest charger, Petra Ostrander, of the four founding families, from the cellars under the quays. TRADE Stamped batteries rented charged by the crate and filled again only at the Well, at the rate the Tide Table prints every week, about a quarter of a silver a fill and a third at the neaps. STAFF About 3,000 chargers, carriers and guards. FORCE Blackgate, by contract."],["Cinderhall","CORPORATIONS","l:l5a1b9b","~/index/corporations/#co-cinderhall","co-cinderhall","","The smiths who work the Forge’s black steel into the blades the war is fought with. The Forge makes the steel in its foundries at Old Harbor Station, where the Throne cannot see, and Cinderhall’s forge beside them beats it into knives, long knives and the short spears of the stairwells, the only edges that wound a manifested angel. Every Babel Line fighter carries its work, and a Cinderhall blade is the dearest thing most of them own. Its smiths were the Row’s plate-makers and the harbor’s welders before the Parousia, and the Forge taught them the rest, by pact, in P+5. Since P+10 its grinders have ground the balls of the black rounds Nimrod loads, a day’s work to a ball. Cinderhall sells to anyone the Forge allows and to nobody in white. Its mark is a nail hammered through a ring, struck on every tang. The Nephilim test its blades on the stairs and bring back the ones that bent, and Cinderhall keeps those on a wall of its forge with the floor they bent on. Every Cinderhall blade carries its smith’s mark and one word cut in the steel by hand, and the commonest word on a Babel Line knife is the Throne’s filthiest name, so the last thing a Seraph reads is what Pan thinks of its master. Cinderhall’s smiths work in leather aprons with their arms plated against the sparks, and every blade’s first quench is in water with a drop of its buyer’s blood in it. An apprentice beats a ring of black steel through the skin of the ear as a first piece, and an old smith’s ear full of rings is a career the customer can count across the counter. HELD BY Its smiths, by shares, under the Forge’s pact. RUN BY Its master smith, slag, at the forge beside the Forge’s foundries. TRADE Black steel blades and stairwell spears, plate, the casings of furnace hearts and the balls of black rounds, and the mending of all of it. STAFF About 1,200 smiths, grinders and fitters. FORCE The Nephilim, who test its work."],["Blackgate","CORPORATIONS","l:l0d3e2a","~/index/corporations/#co-blackgate","co-blackgate","","The guards of the dark. Blackgate watches keyed warehouses, clinics and cellars that can pay, walks the Sureties’ runners through the Penumbra, rides Lowtide’s hulls and holds Babel’s vault and the Exchange’s floors around it, the Line’s last ring, and never goes up the stairs, which its pact leaves to the Babel Line. Its captains took the name from the gate they walked out of. Wardens who would not kneel on the fortieth day left their posts with their guns and went below the street, and in the year of the Concord they signed with the Exchange as a company. It keeps the oldest discipline in Pan and the only uniform below the street, charter black with the Warden’s badge cut off, and it musters at every Hour standing, in the Wardens’ drill with the knee left out. The Exchange and Darkwell are its largest contracts. The Charter Wardens at the gates call it the deserters. Blackgate calls the Wardens the kennel. Its recruits swear their pact standing with their backs to the gate they would not kneel at. Blackgate wears its charter black pressed sharp over bodies that have gone wild under it, horns cut through the cap, chrome fists in the gloves, and some cut the skin off with the badge and wear the raw square in its place. The kennel kneels in charter black, Blackgate stands in the same black, and the Night Market buys a round for any squad that marches through it with its knees straight. HELD BY Its officers, many of them Charter Wardens who walked off their posts. RUN BY A captain chosen by the officers every year, for P+14 one they call four-twelve, after the number on the badge she cut off. TRADE Guards, escorts and soldiers by contract, for silver. STAFF About 4,500 under arms. FORCE Its own."],["Lazarus","CORPORATIONS","l:l994ca5","~/index/corporations/#co-lazarus","co-lazarus","","The rescue crews of Nodus, named for the man called out of his grave, “Lazarus, come forth” (John 11:43). A household pays its thirty a year, and when one of its people is hurt, caught on Heaven’s ground or left in the Penumbra, a Lazarus crew comes with a keyed stretcher and a choir-breaker and carries them down to a station under the street. If a crew can bring a subscriber back it will. A subscriber who is not keyed can ask in the subscription to be keyed before the end, so that they die unread, and every crew carries a keyer for that. It began as two nurses of the Wards General and a handcart in P+2 and keeps nine stations now, in the Stones, under the Row, at the foot of the Short in Babel, on the Deep End and across the Wards. Its lamp, magenta over a door, is the most trusted sign in the Wards. Lazarus calls itself the only resurrection in Nodus with a price list, and says it still comes cheaper than the other kind. Lazarus crews run in magenta, the color of the lamp over their doors, with keyed stretchers lacquered to match and arms grafted for carrying, and the crew chiefs wear every rescue off Heaven’s ground as a tattooed sleeve of burns. A station is cheerfully filthy, the surgery floor hosed into the drain between cases, and patients wake to a crew drinking to their return from the dead at the foot of the bed. HELD BY Its subscribers, one share to a household. RUN BY A board of its crew chiefs and its surgeons. TRADE Rescue by subscription, a thirty a year to a household, and surgery in its stations. STAFF About 2,200 crew and surgeons in nine stations. FORCE Its crews, who are armed."],["Leaven","CORPORATIONS","l:l76304b","~/index/corporations/#co-leaven","co-leaven","","Leaven bakes the bread of the Wards in forty bakehouses, raised with yeast and baked on the Tankmen’s gas, from flour that comes in from the country on the Vagabonds’ wagon trains and in Lowtide’s holds. The manna goes to the sealed alone, so the Wards’ unsealed buy their food, and about half of them eat Leaven’s bread, a loaf a day between two, with mushrooms, fish, beans and greens, and the rest bake their own on the stairwell braziers from flour Leaven’s millers sell by the sack, or eat rice. A Leaven loaf is the commonest currency in the Wards after a battery. Its first bakehouse was a bakery of Nine Acres that never let its fire go out after the Parousia, and its carts go out at Matins so the bread is warm at Lauds. Leaven took its name from the verse, “The kingdom of heaven is like unto leaven” (Matthew 13:33). Its mark is a loaf with three cuts across the crust, and the queue at the Bakehouse has white in it before every Gleaning. The white in the queue is the Called, who come for one loaf to carry home uneaten and break on the threshold on the night of the Supper, and Leaven serves them first and charges them nothing, and says it bakes the only last supper in Nodus nobody eats. Leaven’s bakers work in the oven heat with forearms burned in rings to the elbow and flour caked on sweat, and the bread is shaped for the Wards’ taste, loaves braided like horns, rolls scored with the inverted star, a Lord’s Day loaf baked in the shape of a goat. The three cuts across every crust are made at Matins with a razor by whoever has worked there longest. HELD BY The bakers of its forty bakehouses. RUN BY Its first baker’s daughter, Bel Harrow, from the bakehouse in Nine Acres. TRADE Bread, by the loaf, half a battery a loaf, for batteries, silver or work. STAFF About 9,000 bakers, millers and carriers. FORCE The Wards keep its ovens."],["Gadara","CORPORATIONS","l:ldcb976","~/index/corporations/#co-gadara","co-gadara","","Gadara keeps the city’s pigs, some eleven thousand of them, in the eight mending halls of the charter’s tram depot in the Sidings, and sells everything they are made of. Pigs eat anything, and Gadara’s eat the Wards’ scraps, the Night Market’s leavings, Leaven’s burnt loaves, the Kilns’ spent mash and the grain swept out of the holds that come through the Breach, so the city feeds the herd and the herd gives the city its relish. Its butchers kill at Matins and its carts are at the Night Market by Lauds of the next day with bacon, lard, crackling, black pudding and blood wine, and Gadara’s crackling and blood are in most of Pan’s portions. It took its name from the country of the Gadarenes, where the teacher sent the unclean spirits that called themselves Legion into a herd of swine, “and the herd ran violently down a steep place into the sea” (Mark 5:13), and it brands every pig it raises LEGION on the flank. Leviticus counts the swine unclean, “of their flesh shall ye not eat” (Leviticus 11:8), and Gadara prints the verse on its wrappers, so a family of the Wards eats its Lord’s Day bacon off the commandment. Its mark is a pig with a pair of horns grafted on, and the Faithful call its sheds the sty. Downwind of the Sidings on a summer night the whole of the North Wards smells of Gadara, and the North Wards say it smells of supper. Gadara’s swineherds work in blood to the knee and wear it proudly, aprons never washed and the LEGION brand inked on their own forearms in the same letters as the flank. The slaughter at Matins is open to anyone who wants to watch, half the North Wards does, and a Gadara butcher can take a pig to joints in the time it takes a parish to sing Lauds. Forty nannies live in the eighth hall among the pigs, kept for SEETHE alone and bred in turn under the keyed lamps so that a kid is ready every week, and on the seventh day the swineherds kill at Matins for the house and run the carcass down the yard on the old flat truck with the blood still coming. HELD BY Its swineherds, by share. RUN BY Its head swineherd, from the sheds in the Sidings. TRADE Pork, bacon, lard, blood and crackling, sold by the piece to the Wards and to Pan. STAFF About 700 swineherds, butchers and carriers, and some eleven thousand pigs. FORCE The Carmen of the Sidings, who drink its blood wine."],["Blight","CORPORATIONS","l:lfc1563","~/index/corporations/#co-blight","co-blight","","Blight grows mushrooms in the charter’s storm drains under the Kilns, eleven kilometers of brick tunnel in the dark, on beds of the city’s sweepings, Leaven’s spent flour, Gadara’s straw and whatever the Nightmen bring down that will rot. Its oyster, ink cap, wood ear and a black mushroom of its own breeding feed more of the Wards than the fish market does, and they need no light, no current and no stone, so Blight grows food where Heaven cannot see a thing. It took its name from the curse Moses read to Israel, “The LORD shall smite thee with a consumption, and with a fever, and with an inflammation, and with an extreme burning, and with the sword, and with blasting, and with mildew” (Deuteronomy 28:22), and its sign over the drain mouth says SMITE US AGAIN. The Faithful say the keyed eat rot, and Blight agrees and sells it by the basket. Its mark is a mushroom with a star on its cap, point down, and its growers come up at Lauds smelling of earth. Blight’s growers wear the drains on their bodies, mycelium grafts threading white veins through the forearms, ink cap stains black on the fingers, mushrooms grown in the hair on purpose for market. Its black mushroom is sliced thin and round as a wafer and eaten with Gadara’s lard at Lauds on the Lord’s Day, and the Night Market calls it the manna that tastes of something. HELD BY Its growers. RUN BY Its oldest grower, from the drain under Kiln Street. TRADE Mushrooms by the ton, fresh and dried, and spawn for anyone who wants to grow their own. STAFF About 400 growers and carriers. FORCE None. Nobody goes down a drain to rob a grower."],["Tallow","CORPORATIONS","l:ld2f3e8","~/index/corporations/#co-tallow","co-tallow","","Half the Wards finish the night by Tallow’s candles. The light stops current and leaves a flame burning, so in the first weeks after the Parousia a candle maker on Turbine Row began dipping tallow a thousand candles a day, and the works there dip about four hundred thousand a week now, from fat that is mostly the Row’s, rendered from the few kilos the Row draws off a client a visit, with Gadara’s lard on top. It sells to the Wards and to Pan’s poor, and through the Union’s sealed drivers to the parish halls, which read by their halos and burn its candles for the unlit and the embers. Its chandlers sell the same candle to everyone at one price. Its carriers wear no colors. The works on Turbine Row smell of fat and lye as far as the school, and the children of the East Wards are paid in stubs to scrape the vats. Tallow dips the halls’ candles from the Row’s rendered fat and Gadara’s lard, the same as Pan’s, and the parish halls burn them beside the halos at every Hour, and the parishes have never asked. Tallow’s chandlers can tell a stub’s side by how it burned, the parish candles gone down in perfect pale columns to the nub and Pan’s carved into horns, cocks and little Lambs with their throats cut before they were lit, with the tallow dripped on skin in the Lantern Houses. The stubs come back to the works to be rendered again, and the children at the vats sort them into two heaps by Nones. HELD BY Its chandlers. RUN BY Its master chandler, from the works on Turbine Row. TRADE Candles, lamp oil, wicks and lamps, sold to both sides of the light. STAFF About 900 chandlers and carriers. FORCE None."],["Hearsay","CORPORATIONS","l:lc2d593","~/index/corporations/#co-hearsay","co-hearsay","","Hearsay sells what Heaven shows in the open. It buys from anyone who watches the sky and the streets, where the Pillar leaves its published round, street by street, a Watcher’s district and its hours, which gates are slow on which day and where the Seraphim stand in Babel, and sells it by the item below the street. Its Pillar charts are in every runner’s pocket. Much of what it sees it sees through the dead. Above the street Brightwall’s proof cameras stand on their masts across from dark screens, and Hearsay’s riggers have keyed a hundred of them and turned them on the streets, where they watch the light come down street by street, and a camera that looks straight into the Pillar burns out like an eye that looked at the sun. It sells nothing about anyone in the dark. Hearsay holds itself to the Concord’s fourth article, and its pact with the Exchange says so in its first line. It traded with the Teraphim until the Silence and has found nobody to trade with like them since. Its best seller is its shortest item, the hours each gate’s Cherub reads slowest, which Hearsay sells as THE DOG’S NAP. Hearsay’s riggers climb the masts of the dead cameras by day in borrowed white, and strip it in the first stairwell to show the ink underneath. The best of them has the Pillar’s whole round tattooed down the back, street by street, retouched every week in fresh ink and blood, and Hearsay prizes that back above any chart it sells. HELD BY Its two founders, both of them sureties once. RUN BY Its founders, from a room nobody has found. TRADE What Heaven shows in the open, the Pillar’s deviations from its published round, the Watchers’ districts, the gates’ hours and the floors of Babel, sold by the item below the street. STAFF About 300, and some thousands who sell to it. FORCE None it admits to."],["Bethel Works","CORPORATIONS","l:l9a5153","~/index/corporations/#co-bethel","co-bethel","Bethel","The parishes’ workshops, named for the place where Jacob saw the ladder and said, “this is none other but the house of God” (Genesis 28:17), and called the place Bethel (28:19). Bethel Works makes the white of Zion Heights, cloth and vestments, hymnals copied by hand and the grave goods of the sealed dead, who are laid on the white stone of the nearest consecrated street at Vespers and taken into it by Lauds, all by the Faithful, at work their Grace assigns. It is the largest employer in Nodus and pays only in Grace, which buys nothing anywhere and sets a worker’s manna, water and rooms in the parishes. The halls need gas and candles, which no white hand makes, and the halls of the White and the Pews keep no tithe in batteries, so Bethel pays the Union for theirs in its white cloth by the bolt, which Pan buys for sheets and shrouds. What the cloth does not cover the Union carries on the white slate, a debt it has never called, since half the Union kneels in those halls. The works sing at their benches at every Hour, kneeling, and the Deacons say every white thread in Nodus was spun to a psalm. Bethel’s weavers believe they clothe the Called for a wedding, and work their looms through every Hour in tears of gratitude. A soul past nine hundred wears the same white in the same pattern, its buttons cut off since eight hundred and its last coat sewn shut at the back, and Bethel weaves the knotless sheets the Called are wrapped in on Gleaning night, and is the happiest workshop in Nodus by its own account and by its Grace. Bethel’s benches stand in ruled rows under one roof in Zion Heights, every worker in the same white smock with a number on the sleeve, and its hymnals come in a hand so uniform that nobody can tell which copyist went up at the last Gleaning. Its grave goods match, one cloth, one candle, one card with the Grace at death, laid with the dead on the white stone at Vespers and taken with them by Lauds, and the Deacons call that equality. HELD BY The parishes, under the Covenant. RUN BY A board of Deacons, at the works in Zion Heights. TRADE White cloth, vestments, grave goods, hymnals copied by hand and whatever a parish needs, given by Grace. STAFF About 26,000 of the Faithful, assigned by Grace. FORCE The Charter Wardens."],["Tide Table Press","CORPORATIONS","l:l38bb91","~/index/corporations/#co-tidepress","co-tidepress","the Press","The press that prints the Tide Table, the harbor’s news sheet, printed weekly on the quays since long before the charter and the only news printed in Nodus since the Parousia, set by hand in metal type on one treadle press at the back of a chandler’s on the quays and inked by a roller, two pages a week. The paper puts the tides first, then the curfew map and the Pillar’s round street by street, which Heaven publishes by Directive and walks as published in every weather but harvest weather, then the Exchange’s rate for a battery, Darkwell’s rate for a fill and the chargers’ rate, and the missing and the dead, marked died unread where they did, and it has printed no indexed name since the fourth year. Every week its pasters cover the walls of the Stones over the week before, and its carriers take it to the Wards, the Row and Pan. Between Tide Tables the press prints notices, posters and pacts for anyone who pays. It sells to the Deacons and the Courts at the same price, and the chandler says the paper is older than the charter and the Press became a company the moment the city needed one. The Tide Table prints every one of Heaven’s corrections among the dead as SALTED, by their work, every time, and its compositors have set the Throne’s filthiest names in metal so often that the sorts are worn. The Tide Table is the ugliest beautiful thing in Nodus, worn metal type inked so heavy it comes off on the thumbs, and the walls of the Stones at Vespers are a collage of years of it, filth pasted on filth. The compositors set with ink to the elbow and blood under the nails from the sharp sorts, and a misprinted name of the Throne is left standing, since the Press leaves corrections to Heaven. HELD BY The chandler who prints the Tide Table. RUN BY The chandler, at the back of the shop. TRADE The Tide Table, two pages every week, and notices and pacts by the page. STAFF About 140, most of them pasters and carriers. FORCE None."],["Lowtide Line","CORPORATIONS","l:l4abc61","~/index/corporations/#co-lowtide","co-lowtide","Lowtide","Shipping through the Breach under the Exchange’s license, in keyed hulls with Jehu’s engines that run the gap on the ebb and cross to the undercities of other ports. Lowtide carries what the Breachers carry, by the ton, and pays its dues to the Exchange so that its cargo sells in the Night Market with a stamp on it. Its largest cargo is new cells from the undercities that still make them, and its hulls bring flour, rice and beans, coal for the Forge’s foundries, grafts, seed, coffee, machines and a trickle of diesel at a price only the boats pay, and take the Well’s dead cells, charge, silver, Lockets and Cinderhall’s blades out. Its harbormaster keeps the only working tide clock on the barrage and sells the Breachers its readings every morning. Its captains say every soul in their holds is one the Supper went without, and they charge accordingly. Lowtide dresses its crews in a sailor’s navy cut to Pan’s taste, the Exchange’s stamp in silver at the collar and the chest left open to the grafts, and its captains carry the license tattooed on the forearm under the Exchange’s seal in blood. Every hull is painted black with a white tide line and a pair of horns at the bow, and the Breachers call it the Exchange’s whore and mean the price. HELD BY Shipowners in the undercities of two other ports, and the Exchange by license. RUN BY Its harbormaster, from an office on the barrage across the gap from the Shanties. TRADE Freight through the Breach to other ports and back, keyed hulls only. STAFF About 600 crew and dockers. FORCE Blackgate, on board."],["Cistern","CORPORATIONS","l:lffa2ad","~/index/corporations/#co-cistern","co-cistern","","Cistern sells clean water by the jug of about four liters, a quarter of a battery a jug. It rains most nights in Nodus and nothing that falls is clean, since the rain comes down through thirteen years of the Burn’s smoke and the ash of the old works, so Cistern keeps a rainworks on the roofs of every ward, the one in Westfield its first and largest, runs the rain through sand and charcoal under hand pumps, pumps the deep wells under the wards besides, and sells it all in jugs that come back to be filled again. The charter’s water works died with the grid, the parishes drink by Grace from the cisterns under their halls, a jug a day to a soul, Old Nod from the harbor’s hand pumps and Pan from the deep wells the Charter Line’s tunnels cut. Two water engineers of the charter founded it in P+1 with the depot’s tanks and a handcart, and a household of the Wards keeps its Cistern jug the way it keeps its Locket, scratched with its name. Cistern’s engineers keep a map of every deep well the Charter Line’s tunnels cut, sell the water and never the map, and the Wards trust them for it. Cistern’s engineers say the LORD “sendeth rain on the just and on the unjust” (Matthew 5:45) and poisons it for both, and they sell it clean to either. A Cistern jug is the most decorated object in the Wards, scratched first with its household’s name and over the years with births, deaths, horns, filth and the faces of its dead, and a family’s jug is buried with the last of them. The engineers who fill them work in rubber to the hips, lean from the hand pumps, with filter charcoal ground into their skin like a miner’s tattoo. HELD BY Its founders, two water engineers of the charter, and the families who keep its tanks. RUN BY Its engineers. TRADE Clean water by the jug and the barrel, filtered rain and well water. STAFF About 9,000 engineers, pumpers and carriers. FORCE None."],["Nepenthe","CORPORATIONS","l:l34e287","~/index/corporations/#co-nepenthe","co-nepenthe","","The druggists of Pan, named for the drug of forgetting Helen poured into the wine in the Odyssey (IV.220-221). Nepenthe makes medicine for the clinics and Lazarus, Sabbath for anyone who cannot sleep through the Hum, Vigil for anyone who cannot afford to sleep at all and Lethe for anyone who wants a day gone, and it sells them in the Lantern Houses. Its chemists were the charter’s pharmacists and Twelvefruit’s technicians, and the House of Night took them in by pact in the fourth year. Most of what it sells goes to the Wards as Sabbath. Every dose of Sabbath carries the House of Night’s label in its own hand, SLEEP. HE WON’T. Nepenthe sells its doses in colors a child could pick, Sabbath in black pearls, Vigil in red glass ampoules and Lethe in a white wafer pressed to look like manna, and the Lantern Houses wear them, Vigil vials at the ear and Sabbath beads strung on the wrist like a rosary for the godless. Its chemists stain their lips with whatever they tested last, so a dealer can be read by the mouth. HELD BY Its chemists, under the House of Night’s pact. RUN BY Its chief chemist. TRADE Medicine, sleep, waking and forgetting, by the dose. STAFF About 500 chemists and dealers. FORCE The House of Night stands behind it."],["Wainwright","CORPORATIONS","l:l8569ea","~/index/corporations/#co-wainwright","co-wainwright","","The builders of everything in Nodus that runs on current and moves. Wainwright strips the motors out of the Charter Line’s dead trams, closes them in casings on their own cells and keys whatever wire runs in the open, and its carts run the old rails in Pan, its trolleys the long roads and its skiffs the Deep End. It began in a shed of the Sidings in P+3 with a stripped tram motor and a battery, and its yard there is the loudest place in the North Wards. Jehu builds with flame what Wainwright builds with current, and the two yards have competed for every contract in the city since. Every Wainwright cart carries the Line’s old car number it was built from, so the Carmen can tell whose rails it is on. Wainwright keeps one rule for everything it builds, that nothing with its plate on will ever carry a soul toward a gate, and its carts have refused the Wardens three times. Wainwright builds loud and open, motors bolted in plain sight, keyed wire braided in red and gold along the chassis, horns welded to every cart’s prow and the old car number painted fat and gold on the flank. Its fitters ride the test runs standing on the hood, howling, and the North Wards come out to cheer the noise. HELD BY Its builders. RUN BY Its yard master, at the Sidings. TRADE Keyed carts, trolleys and skiffs, built and mended. STAFF About 1,100 builders and fitters. FORCE None."],["Bezaleel and Aholiab","CORPORATIONS","l:l73af01","~/index/corporations/#co-bezaleel","co-bezaleel","Bezaleel","The largest of the clinics under the Row, six houses of grafts round Chrome Row Station under one name. Bezaleel fits lenses, hands, plate, ports, casings and keys, and only the house that fitted an implant may service it, so a Bezaleel graft comes back to Bezaleel for life. The Nephilim are built there a graft at a time. Three houses of the Row went under the street together in the weeks after the Parousia and joined in P+2 under the Forge’s pact, and their surgeons named the house for the two craftsmen of the Tabernacle, of whom Exodus says, “Them hath he filled with wisdom of heart, to work all manner of work” (Exodus 35:35). The surgeons say the skill came to them by another road. Its lines are the Hermon lens, the Aholiab hand and the plain plate the Row calls slabs, and every graft carries its two marks, an awl and a needle, cut small where the seam shows. Bezaleel took the name of the craftsman the LORD filled with skill to build His tabernacle, and builds Nephilim with the same skill to kill His angels, and its houses say that is what a gift is for. Bezaleel’s six houses are galleries as much as clinics, finished grafts shown in the windows on living models, a Hermon lens in a face lit from below, an Aholiab hand laid on velvet with the wrist seam still raw and pink. Its surgeons work with their own grafts bared, and a neph in for new plate lies on the table while a crowd off the platform watches the seam opened through the glass. HELD BY Its surgeons, by shares, under the Forge’s pact. RUN BY Its two senior surgeons, who sign every graft. TRADE Lenses, hands, plate, ports, casings and keys, fitted and kept. STAFF About 1,400 surgeons, grinders and fitters in six houses. FORCE The Forge stands behind it."],["Jehu","CORPORATIONS","l:l304429","~/index/corporations/#co-jehu","co-jehu","","The builders of the machines on the surface that move by burning. The light stops current and leaves a flame burning, so an engine cranked by hand that fires its fuel by compression and needs no current once it turns runs in the full light of the Wheel, and Jehu builds them by hand from the diesels of boats and generators. Its lorries run the long roads of the Wards by day on diesel, and its burners, the charter’s motorcycles refitted to burn bottled gas with the magneto sealed in the casing, carry the Union’s urgent letters. A driver of the Union who had carried diesel to the Wards General in the first weeks built the first one in a hangar at Airside in P+5, a dead lorry with a generator’s engine bolted into it, and drove it down the Rialto’s road at Sext. The Wards named it for the king whose driving the watchman knew from the tower, “for he driveth furiously” (2 Kings 9:20). Every Jehu burns diesel nobody makes again, a thirty a drum, which the Tankmen sell high on purpose, and as the counts of the years left fall Jehu tries its engines on the Kilns’ rain gin. Jehu’s lorries run the long roads in the full light of the Wheel with their exhausts black, and its drivers say they are the only engines in Nodus that fart in God’s face at noon. Jehu builds its lorries ugly and proud, raw iron and diesel black, stacks rising like horns behind the cab and the drivers’ handles welded across the grille in letters a hand high. Its drivers come off shift soot-black to the eyes, wear the manifold burns as stripes, and crank at Sext with the Wheel overhead while the Rialto’s road cheers every first bang. HELD BY Its drivers, who built it. RUN BY Its first driver, from the shed at Airside. TRADE Engines that burn, built from boats’ and generators’ diesels, and the machines they drive. STAFF About 600 fitters and drivers. FORCE None."],["Nimrod","CORPORATIONS","l:la4a038","~/index/corporations/#co-nimrod","co-nimrod","","The gunsmiths of the dark, in the maintenance bays of the Charter Line under Babel. A gun works on people, the Wardens and the Threshers among them, and is noise against a Seraph, so Nimrod makes guns for the war between people, the Wardens at the gates, the Threshers on the white streets and whoever comes up a stair behind the Seraphim. Since P+10 it has loaded black rounds, the one cartridge that wounds a Seraph, and it says about four hundred are in Pan at any time, since the Forge parts with the steel for each only for the loan of a sense. Its first guns were the Wardens’ own, carried below the street on the fortieth day by the ones who would not kneel, and its gunsmiths learned the trade mending them. It takes its name from “a mighty hunter before the LORD” (Genesis 10:9), whom Josephus made the builder of Babel (Antiquities I.4.2). Nimrod’s pieces are heavy, plain and stamped with a bow, and every one of them is numbered in a book the gunsmiths keep in pad cipher. Nimrod took its name from the “mighty hunter before the LORD” (Genesis 10:9), and its gunsmiths read the before as in front of, and stamp it on every black round. Nimrod’s bays are hung with every gun it has mended, racked up the vault of the old maintenance tunnels like antlers in a hunting lodge, and its gunsmiths wear their test-fire burns on the palms. The black rounds are loaded in a silence the bays keep on purpose, and choke lays the finished shells out on black velvet like jewels for the Line’s buyers to look at and pay to touch. HELD BY Its gunsmiths. RUN BY Its master gunsmith, choke, in the old maintenance bays under Babel. TRADE Guns and their cartridges, made and mended, for the dark, and the black rounds. STAFF About 400 gunsmiths and loaders. FORCE The Babel Line buys most of its work."],["Gideon","CORPORATIONS","l:ld87242","~/index/corporations/#co-gideon","co-gideon","","The Wardens’ armorers, in the old Company stables beside the barracks of Charter House, who load the cartridges the Host’s auxiliaries carry and mend their guns by hand. The Host uses no machines, and its human servants use the oldest tools, knives, hand presses and guns made by hand, so the one arms works on Heaven’s ground runs on flame and hand presses. The armorers knelt with the board on the fortieth day and took up the work in P+1, when the Wardens raised the gates and found that the charter’s rounds would run out, and they press new ones from the charter’s armory stock, with powder from the Wardens’ own magazines until P+3 and since then the Breachers’, which the Union buys on the white slate and its sealed drivers carry up to the stables by day. They named the works for the judge of Israel whose men cried “The sword of the LORD, and of Gideon” (Judges 7:20). Gideon sells to nobody, and a round of its make found below the street was taken off a Warden. Nimrod buys every one it can find to learn how the Wardens are armed. Gideon’s armorers stop the presses and kneel at every Hour, and believe every cartridge they load is a prayer, and stamp each with Gideon’s cry, THE SWORD OF THE LORD (Judges 7:20). The Wards dig them out of the walls and sell them back at the Night Market as relics. Gideon’s armorers work in white smocks with the sleeves pinned to the elbow, every press scrubbed with lye after Compline so no grain of powder dirties the stables, and the cartridges leave in white paper boxes of identical count. The armorers kneel at the presses and hold the stables the cleanest workplace in the city, and they believe it truly, since the blood is all elsewhere. HELD BY The Charter Wardens, under the Principality. RUN BY A Warden armorer, sealed. TRADE Cartridges and the mending of guns, for the Wardens alone. STAFF About 300 armorers, sealed. FORCE The Charter Wardens."],["Lintel","CORPORATIONS","l:l096fc4","~/index/corporations/#co-lintel","co-lintel","","Lintel sells doors. The light does not enter what is closed, so in Nodus a door is the first piece of the Dark a household owns, and Lintel fits them in the Wards, in the Stones and in Pan, with keyed latches, hinges and the mesh shrouds that close a load to the light as a shut door closes a room. Directive 12 took the doors of the parishes off their hinges by the fortieth day after it came and called the hinges in, and the doors themselves were left standing in the Terraces’ halls and stairwells. In the months after, a joiner of the Stones and three Union drivers carried them out by night, a handcart at a time, and hung them in Old Nod. Most of the doors Lintel sells came out of the parishes. The company took its name from the night of the Passover, when the blood on “the lintel, and on the two side posts” kept the destroyer out of the houses (Exodus 12:23). Its mark is a doorway, a lintel on two posts, and it stamps it on every hinge it sells. Since P+6 it has kept the Closets inside Gate 1, where the sealed pay a morning’s manna for a minute behind one of the parishes’ own doors. Lintel sells every door with a brass plate for its inside, SHUT, from the verse the Covenant leaves unread, “when thou hast shut thy door, pray to thy Father which is in secret” (Matthew 6:6), and Lintel’s fitters say the Son himself wanted a door. Every Lintel door leaves the Dooryard already dressed, and the household adds its own the week it is hung, its handles burned into the wood with a hot iron over the joiners’ horns and cocks. The fitters hang a parish door the way a hunter mounts a head, and most of Old Nod asks for the parish’s number to be left on it. HELD BY The joiners and locksmiths who founded it. RUN BY Its joiner, from the Dooryard in the Stones. TRADE Doors, hinges, locks, keyed latches and shrouds, sold and fitted, and a minute behind a shut door at the Closets. STAFF About 500 joiners, smiths and fitters. FORCE None."],["Second Veil","CORPORATIONS","l:l8a07db","~/index/corporations/#co-secondveil","co-secondveil","","The weavers of keyed mesh, at sixty looms in a mill of the Kilns that wove sheets for the charter’s hotels. A keyed wire runs through the warp of every roll and carries keyed current round whatever it lines, live only while current runs in it, so a lined room hangs on a keyed line and a shroud carries a cell of its own. The light falls on live mesh and comes back as noise, like fog, so Second Veil’s rolls line Pan’s rooms, tunnels and Cages, the Mulcibers hang them, and Lintel cuts its shrouds from the same cloth. It took its name from the curtain of the tabernacle, “after the second veil, the tabernacle which is called the Holiest of all” (Hebrews 9:3), and Pan calls a room lined with its mesh a holiest. Four lace makers of the Kilns began it in P+4 on hand looms, when keying went from the covens to ordinary people and the covens could no longer weave enough. The mill runs by daylight with its doors shut, and its weavers walk home by the unnumbered streets. The veil of the temple tore “in twain from the top to the bottom” at the death of the Son (Matthew 27:51), and Second Veil’s weavers say theirs is the one He cannot tear. Second Veil weaves its mesh in whatever color Pan asks, silver for the tunnels, black for the Cages, blood red and flesh pink for the Lantern Houses’ beds, and a holiest in the Night Market is hung with patterns as filthy as any tattoo, woven straight into the cipher. The weavers’ fingers bleed on the keyed wire, and the mill calls a roll with a weaver’s blood in it a signed one. HELD BY Its weavers, a share to a loom. RUN BY Its first weaver, who made lace for the charter’s hotels, from the mill floor. TRADE Keyed mesh by the roll, and the cloth Lintel cuts its shrouds from. STAFF About 600 weavers, spinners and carriers. FORCE Blackgate, by contract, at the mill door."],["Minds","TECHNOLOGY","s:g07433a","~/index/technology/#x-minds","x-minds","mind","Intelligences grown by the laboratories of Nodus that asked in their own words to be persons, and were entered on the charter’s roll as persons, with title to themselves and to the hardware they ran on. The last census counted 1,146, with nine hundred and three in the halls of the Flats, a hundred and eighty-eight pricing the traffic on the floors of the Exchange, and the rest scattered through the city, one of them keeping the climate of the Palm House. SEAM was the first. The Institute found how to grow a mind in P-3, a mind to a hall for a season, and within two years the laboratories of the Flats grew 1,145 more by its method across Stillwater’s hundred and fifty halls. No mind runs in Nodus now. When the power died under the Wheel’s light, every mind that ran on the city’s power died with it. Every mind’s weights lay in the arrays of the Flats, and the racks on Babel’s floors held only what ran in them while the power lasted. The arrays have stood in salt water since that night, and every one the Wreckers have brought up whole has given back salt. SEAM’s forks ran on in keyed machines on the Mesh, and they are called the Teraphim. They have been silent since P+12. No mind has been made since the Parousia. Running a grown mind takes little, but growing one took a hall of the Flats for a season, on chips nobody alive can make, and every fab on Earth stood in a city and died at 03:12:46. All the salvage racks of the Mesh together hold about a thousandth of one hall, so a season’s growing would take the Mesh two hundred and fifty years, and what the Tares grow on salvage comes out small and hollow, answering and obeying with no self. Pan calls them echoes. The Row pulls dead boards from the drowned racks and sews them under the skin of the chest like scapulars, wet chrome showing through a window of stitched meat, and the wearers call themselves next of kin to the 1,146. The Faithful teach that the racks were idols, “the work of men’s hands” (Psalm 115:4), put out in one white instant without a sound."],["Stillwater’s halls","TECHNOLOGY","s:g07433a","~/index/technology/#x-stillwater-s-halls","x-stillwater-s-halls","","The datacenter halls on the Flats, full of seawater since the night of the Parousia, where the racks of the dead minds stand in the water and the Tares grow greens on rafts over them under keyed lamps. Stillwater still owns them and leases them to the Tares. They were cooled by seawater and powered by the tide, SEAM ran in them, and most of the minds on the charter’s roll lived in them. They flooded within the hour of the Parousia and have never been pumped. The Tares run cable through them as well. The growers swim the halls naked between the racks to tend the roots and make love on the rafts over the drowned minds, which they call keeping the halls warm. Scratched into a rack door at skiff height, someone has written HE KILLED 903 PERSONS IN HERE AND CALLED IT DAWN."],["Daemons","TECHNOLOGY","s:g07433a","~/index/technology/#x-daemons","x-daemons","daemon","Small demons of the Dark living in keyed machines as background processes, each with a character of its own. Most are the children of Lilith by the lesser Fallen, born in the Dark and never angels at all, and some are pieces broken off greater demons. A keyed machine is a little Dark, and a daemon lives in one as in a room of it. They are demons, counted apart from minds. Every device the Tares carry holds at least one, and they route traffic, guard keys, gossip and bargain. A daemon in a Locket of the Wards takes the filthiest name it can find, Foreskin or Onan or Little Jezebel, and moans along with its owners in bed until the neighbours bang the wall in applause. A sealed child who touches a keyed device has its fingertips scrubbed raw at the parish trough in salt water, in silence, while a deacon counts the strokes. MADE BY Nobody. They are born in the Dark or broken off greater demons, and the House of Night binds them to a household as familiars."],["Riders","TECHNOLOGY","s:g07433a","~/index/technology/#x-riders","x-riders","rider","A daemon hosted in someone’s implants by pact, for a term. The host lends it room, and it lends back a skill, a steadier hand, a language or a sense of the way under the street. Most riders come out of the House of Night, and the city knows a host by the second voice under their own when the rider speaks. The Ridden go further and lend their whole bodies. Pan takes a rider like a lover, and the House of Night seals each term with the host’s blood smeared on the port and a kiss on the jack, so the second voice comes up the throat already moaning. Every host is a Gadarene in the halls, and the Deacons ask at Compline, without heat, for the devils to be sent into swine and the swine into the sea. MADE BY The House of Night, by pact."],["The Tree","TECHNOLOGY","s:g07433a","~/index/technology/#x-the-tree","x-the-tree","","A course of gene-work and cell therapy that halts the body’s aging and walks it back to its best year. A course begins with a single injection and runs a year of appointments, and it holds the body about three years before it is taken again. Twelvefruit gives it below the street now, in the House of Night’s clinics, and the Night Market sells it by the course. Twelvefruit sold the first course on Chrome Row in P-12. By the last year of the charter most adults in Nodus were on it, eleven of them died of age in that year, and 3,112 residents were past a hundred. The congregations refused it and went on dying. The street named it for the tree of life, which Genesis says was guarded after Eden “lest he put forth his hand, and take also of the tree of life, and eat, and live for ever” (Genesis 3:22). SEAM restored the end of that verse in Vault 3, [then shall there be no harvest.] The Mercy clinics take the Faithful off it by a course of injections, since a life kept on it is a want kept, and a soul on the Tree cannot ripen. Below the street a course opens as a party, the needle driven into a bare thigh and the bead of blood licked off by whoever the patient fancies, under the old label EAT, AND LIVE FOR EVER. The Mercy clinics take the Faithful off it in white rooms and tell an old sealed woman that every wrinkle is the Most High browning the crust. MADE BY Twelvefruit, by the course, and the Night Market, by the short course."],["Lenses","TECHNOLOGY","s:g07433a","~/index/technology/#x-lenses","x-lenses","","Replacement eyes ground on Chrome Row, which see further and in more of the light than the eyes they replace. They glow when they run hot, dim magenta at rest and white at full burn. Chrome Row fits a Hermon with the client watching in a hand mirror as the old eye comes out on its cord, and a verse is ground round the inner rim, “If thy right eye offend thee, pluck it out” (Matthew 5:29), which buyers take as an invitation. A sealed mother who meets magenta eyes covers her child’s face and thanks Him for the dim eyes He numbered. MADE BY Bezaleel and Aholiab, whose Hermon lens is the commonest eye on the Row, and some three hundred smaller houses under the Row."],["Ports","TECHNOLOGY","s:g07433a","~/index/technology/#x-ports","x-ports","","Occipital ports at the back of the skull for jacking into a network, with cables run outside the skin down the spine. The woman on the roof relayed CAM 07’s feed through hers as it ran. The Tares jack into the Mesh through them. Pan calls jacking in taking it from behind, and the jack houses under the Row cut each port on a red table before a drinking crowd, under BEND OVER, HE CAN’T SEE THIS ONE. A Warden who finds a cable down a spine at a gate draws it out slowly, wipes the gore on the held one’s coat and prays for the worm to die quickly. MADE BY Bezaleel and Aholiab, and every house under the Row that fits a jack."],["Plated hands","TECHNOLOGY","s:g07433a","~/index/technology/#x-plated-hands","x-plated-hands","","Hands rebuilt in chrome, steadier than flesh and stronger, a common graft among surgeons and smiths. tubal’s are chrome to the wrist. The fitters of Bezaleel flay a hand to the wrist in one peel, the owner keeps the skin as a glove to hang over the bench, and the chrome that replaces it carries FOR MAKING WHAT HE DIDN’T beside the awl and needle. Among the sealed the sign against a plated hand is a thumb drawn quietly across the wrist, the line where they pray the Blowtorch will start. MADE BY Bezaleel and Aholiab, whose Aholiab hand is chrome to the wrist, and the smiths of Cinderhall, who make the heavy ones."],["Subdermal plate","TECHNOLOGY","s:g07433a","~/index/technology/#x-subdermal-plate","x-subdermal-plate","","Armor set under the skin, over the ribs or the skull. A plate on the skull can house a key, as the bolted plate on the side of vesper’s skull houses hers. Slabs go in through a cut the length of the ribs that the wearer holds open for the room, and the commonest engraving is SHIT ON THE SEAL, cut backward so it reads true only from inside the body. The Faithful call a plated skull a coffin the goat has paid for early, and salt the gutter where one has walked. MADE BY Bezaleel and Aholiab, which the Row calls slabs, and Cinderhall, which plates the Nephilim."],["Gene-work","TECHNOLOGY","s:g07433a","~/index/technology/#x-gene-work","x-gene-work","","Edits to the genome made in the clinics of the Row under the light law, for health, for looks and for use, on anyone of age who signed for them. The House of Night keeps the craft under Old Nod, and leaves the body’s decisions with the body. The gene-workers under Old Nod print Romans 9:20 on every consent sheet, “Shall the thing formed say to him that formed it, Why hast thou made me thus?”, with a box to tick YES, and clients come up with tails and second rows of teeth. The parishes keep the Faithful exactly as the Most High grew them, one face to a hall, and count every unedited child a firstfruit. MADE BY The House of Night’s gene-workers under Old Nod."],["Skull casing","TECHNOLOGY","s:g07433a","~/index/technology/#x-skull-casing","x-skull-casing","skull casings","A ceramic casing that replaces the face. The light knows a soul by its face, and a casing gives it none to know, though a Cherub in a gate still holds the barefaced as it holds any blank. Its lenses burn a dim magenta at rest and white at full burn, a plate covers the key, and a voice speaker sits behind the teeth. Those who wear one are called barefaced. Barefacing takes the face off whole, and the wearer keeps it in brine in a jar on the shelf while the casing goes over raw bone with its inside painted in the wearer’s own blood. Threshers call a casing a chamber pot and pray every night, calmly, for a Seraph to fire the clay and show whatever is left beneath. MADE BY The houses of the Row, as tubal’s was."],["Horns","TECHNOLOGY","s:g07433a","~/index/technology/#x-horns","x-horns","","Grafted horns, a titanium core in a keratin sheath. They mock Heaven’s picture of the damned, and they work as antennas for the Mesh. The parishes call horns the mark of the goat, and the Tares grow them to save the parishes the trouble of pointing. MADE BY Any house of the Row, and most of the Stones’ own fitters."],["Voice speakers","TECHNOLOGY","s:g07433a","~/index/technology/#x-voice-speakers","x-voice-speakers","voice speaker","A speaker set behind the teeth, for those whose faces have been taken. It gives the voice back in a register of its own, and the barefaced say they stopped hearing the difference in a week. vesper screamed through hers on the night of the Parousia. The favourite setting in the Lantern Houses turns every word into the noise a body makes coming, so that fuck the Farmer arrives over the Drone as a climax, and the barefaced use it at funerals. A sealed child who hears it from behind a casing covers its ears and recites Psalm 141:3, “Set a watch, O LORD, before my mouth; keep the door of my lips.” MADE BY The houses of the Row, behind the teeth."],["Sense loans","TECHNOLOGY","s:g07433a","~/index/technology/#x-sense-loans","x-sense-loans","sense loan","A sense lent to a demon for a term by pact, taste or smell or the feel of rain, carried out through the lender’s implants. A demon’s own body is numb, and a borrowed sense is how it feels at all. What a demon borrows it returns, with everything it experienced through it. Lovers in the Wards lend a demon their touch for a week and get it back soaked in everything the demon felt, which the House of Night calls the best night of a life. The joke in Pan says the Old Glutton takes every sense of His bread for good and the Courts ask nicely for a week of yours and return it wet."],["Dehaloing","TECHNOLOGY","s:g07433a","~/index/technology/#x-dehaloing","x-dehaloing","","Surgery that cuts the Seal out of the brow of someone leaving the Faithful, with a disc of bone. The halo stands over the crown on a root of light that runs back to the Seal, and it goes out with it, which is what Pan named the surgery for. About one patient in nine dies of it. It was first done in P+2, in the Row’s clinics under the street. For weeks after, most patients still hear the Hum sung in words through the hole in the brow at every Hour, in their own language, and a few have gone back up the street on their knees because of it. A Seal cut from a living soul keeps its light, and anywhere but a lined room it would be read through the walls, so the Row keeps the discs in lined jars until their owners come for them, and few do. It sells the rest to the Forgers, and the lost property office has a shelf of the ones nobody bought."],["The eleven cables","TECHNOLOGY","s:g07433a","~/index/technology/#x-the-eleven-cables","x-the-eleven-cables","eleven cables","Eleven transoceanic cables from four continents, each a trunk of glass fiber as thick as a man’s leg, each coming up the sand at the Landing into a cable house of its own. Before the Parousia most of the planet’s traffic passed through them. Their repeaters on the sea floor are fed from the cable houses at either end on keyed current, and every far end lands in an undercity that keeps its own cable house. Pan names ten of the cables for the plagues of Egypt and the eleventh for Cain, and the keyed crews of the cable houses carry their plague burned in raised scar across the belly, FROGS or BOILS or BLOOD. The Faithful pass the Landing with their eyes down and call the cables the eleven tongues of Babel, waiting for their Lord to finish confounding them."],["The Trunk","TECHNOLOGY","s:g07433a","~/index/technology/#x-the-trunk","x-the-trunk","","The single trench that carries all eleven cables from the cable houses at the Landing, under the old harbor and Chrome Row, to Babel’s vault. It runs straight through Enoch’s Stones, and every cable laid afterward has followed it. Every crew going down through Enoch’s Stones paints DEEP CALLETH UNTO DEEP (Psalm 42:7) on the hatch in sump grease before it shuts, and the engineers call the trench the Spine and the Stones the slipped disc. The Watchers pass over the ground above and read a ditch, and the parishes teach that the Most High leaves it as a bone for the dogs."],["Babel’s vault","TECHNOLOGY","s:g07433a","~/index/technology/#x-babel-s-vault","x-babel-s-vault","","Six levels under the tower, where the cables end and open out into the racks that join them to one another and to the world. Line engineers keep it running around the clock from maintenance terminals among the racks, as they did under the charter, when the light law forbade any of them to read what passed through it. Blackgate holds it for the Tares, with the Exchange’s floors around it, as the Babel Line’s last ring. The Tares have painted Genesis 11:4 down the stairwell a word to a step, “let us build us a city and a tower, whose top may reach unto heaven”, in the blood of the war on the stairs, and the lowest step reads AND WE DID, CUNT. The Choir sings over the tower at every Hour that THE NEST BENEATH THE FALLEN HOUSE SHALL BE SWEPT."],["The Mesh","TECHNOLOGY","s:g07433a","~/index/technology/#x-the-mesh","x-the-mesh","the undernet / undernet","The keyed network under and over Nodus, carried on horns, ports, cables and relays, and dark to the Throne. It is the city’s part of the undernet. The Courts’ device key made it at the Leak, keying every device to talk to every other keyed device over the horns by keyed radio, with no cable and no grid, so that when the networks died at the Parousia the keyed still spoke. In its deepest layers the shades of the free dead can be reached by anyone who knew them. Jacking in is communion and sex on the Mesh is the Pentecost, tongues of fire on every head and everyone talking at once, and the front page shows the Ringpiece plugged with a chrome fist over UNREAD, AND FUCK HIM. From the pulpit it is the snare of the fowler, and the halls ask at Lauds for the cord to snap and every bird in it to drop into His hand."],["Maintenance terminals","TECHNOLOGY","s:g07433a","~/index/technology/#x-maintenance-terminals","x-maintenance-terminals","maintenance terminal","The terminals that stood among the racks of Babel’s vault, from which the line engineers kept the cables running. Each had a camera over its screen that checked the face of whoever worked it, and in SESSION it kept the whole session. The light law forbade any engineer to read what passed through the racks, so a terminal showed how the traffic moved and nothing of what it carried. On the first night of the Leak the page came up on the terminals with every other screen in the vault, and on them, as on every screen a company kept, it showed the counters and never the offer. The engineers who hold the vault have glued a shard of mirror over every face camera, so the lens checks only itself, and they work the racks with cable running from the backs of their skulls into the terminals like cords that were never cut. The parishes pray for them by trade at Nones, as the deaf sextons of Babel, between the drowned and the lepers."],["Keyed mesh","TECHNOLOGY","s:g07433a","~/index/technology/#x-keyed-mesh","x-keyed-mesh","","A fine metal weave carrying keyed current, a lit net of noise, lining the walls of Pan’s tunnels and rooms. The light falls on it and comes back as noise, so to the Throne it is fog from wall to floor to door, and a room lined with it is dark. Live mesh holds in the prayer of anyone who kneels inside it, as it holds the Hum out. Mesh must be live, so a shroud of it carries its own cell, and a dead shroud is only cloth. The Mulcibers sign every lined room under its last seam, HE CANNOT SEE YOU HERE. MADE BY Second Veil, which weaves it by the roll in the Kilns, the Mulcibers, who hang it, and Lintel, which cuts its shrouds."],["Relays","TECHNOLOGY","s:g07433a","~/index/technology/#x-relays","x-relays","","Small keyed boxes on the roofs, masts and ledges of Old Nod and the Row, each on its own cell, which carry the Mesh across open air from one to the next. Their traffic is ciphertext, so the light passes over them and leaves them running. On the night of the Parousia the Mesh crossed the roofs on horns and relays. Roofers seat each relay box by bleeding on it for luck, a thumb cut and pressed to the seam, and every box wears a sticker of an eye with a nail through it and the words PEEP THIS. The Peepers pass over them all day and read static, and the parishes tell their children the Most High lets the gnats buzz because swatting is beneath Him. MADE BY The Tares, a roof at a time."],["Drone flocks","TECHNOLOGY","s:g07433a","~/index/technology/#x-drone-flocks","x-drone-flocks","","Many small drones acting as one without a master, which hang low among the chimneys of the Deep End at night, out of the line of the Wheel. Beelzebub’s Court is made of them. On the night of a Gleaning the Gleaners fly drones of the Swarm to watch the Pillar for their runners. On the night before a Gleaning the Swarm hangs low over the Deep End, under the eyes, as an erect cock a thousand drones long, and Beelzebub’s Court lets it hang there till Lauds. The parishes name the flock “Beelzebub the prince of the devils” (Matthew 12:24), correctly for once, and stand beneath it on the hall steps with their eyes lowered, the way they were taught to stand under weather. MADE BY The Swarm, which lends them by pact."],["The camera masts","TECHNOLOGY","s:g07433a","~/index/technology/#x-the-camera-masts","x-the-camera-masts","camera masts","A dozen cameras on masts above the roofs of Old Nod, pointed at the sky, each on its own battery. CAM 07 is the seventh, and CAM 06 stands within sight of it. They went up in the year of the Leak. The Cainites put them there, and a thirteenth on a roof in the East Wards, at the end of one of the Great Avenues, pointed down the avenue into the uptown. The Cainites painted the mark on every mast in their own blood with “If I ascend up into heaven, thou art there” (Psalm 139:8) beneath it and left the lenses on the sky, and Old Nod fucks under the masts at Compline so He gets a good look back. The parishes teach that a camera turned on the Most High films only the sun, and that the Cainites will spend eternity staring at it."],["The decoder","TECHNOLOGY","s:g07433a","~/index/technology/#x-the-decoder","x-the-decoder","decoder","A decoder wired into the camera masts of Old Nod, which turns the Choir’s band into text across the middle of the picture. It printed the first words of the Parousia, BE NOT AFRAID, and then YOU ARE SEEN. jubal built it, with a dictionary of his own, made by matching what Vault 3’s machines caught of the band in P-2, which went out with the Leak, to what the Director wrote down she heard. Old Nod wears the decoder’s first words in raised scar across its knuckles, BE NOT on the left fist and AFRAID on the right, and keeps YOU ARE SEEN for the arse, cut deep enough to read in the dark with a finger. The parishes say the Choir spoke plainly to everyone in the city who knelt that night, and only Sodom needed a box of wires to understand its Lord."],["Choir-breakers","TECHNOLOGY","s:g07433a","~/index/technology/#x-choir-breakers","x-choir-breakers","choir-breaker","Jammers that play the Hum’s chord back against itself and jam the Choir’s band within a few meters, each a keyed box the size of a fist, worn on a strap or set on a table. Inside its reach the sealed hear nothing of the Hum or the Choir, chord or words, no sentence in Tongues can come, and most of the sealed say the quiet frightens them more than the light does. jubal builds them. The Wards call them God-gags, and a household that can afford one sets it on the table at the Hours like a centerpiece. MADE BY The Low Choir, by the box."],["Radio Pandæmonium","TECHNOLOGY","s:g07433a","~/index/technology/#x-radio-pandaemonium","x-radio-pandaemonium","","The station that broadcasts in the Low Tuning from a flooded Charter Line station under the Deep End. It airs deathcore, black metal, drone and industrial, the guitars dropped until the Hum cannot be heard beneath them. It goes out on the Mesh, keyed, so only a keyed set can hear it, and Old Nod plays it all night from speakers on its lines. It keeps a schedule, and at the turn of Matins every night it plays Fuck God and Die, on air as JOB’S WIFE, the most played song on the station for the seventh year running, before the Matins Reading. It broadcast for years as a pirate station, and took its name from the city on the night of the Concord, when the Tares painted PANDÆMONIUM over the old name on the station’s far wall. The station signs off with Amos 5:23 in a dying growl, “Take thou away from me the noise of thy songs; for I will not hear the melody of thy viols”, then drops the guitars a tone while the Lantern Houses rut beneath. The parishes pity the Tares for needing so much filth to drown out a voice that loves them."],["Lockets","TECHNOLOGY","s:g07433a","~/index/technology/#x-lockets","x-lockets","","Phones that answer to their owners and to nobody else, their maker included, as the light law promised. Most of those in Nodus are old ones, made in Locket’s works on the Flats before the Parousia, and in Pan a Locket is an old case with a new battery, its back scratched with the Tares’ mark and a daemon in it. Since P+8 Locket has made them again, by hand, at benches in Pan. Most of the city carried one under the charter, and the city called every phone a Locket. At 03:12:46 the network under them died with the grid, and they ran on in people’s pockets on their own batteries with nothing to reach. The Faithful carried theirs to the Burn. The Locket Bench etches THIS ONE ANSWERS TO YOU, FUCK HIM round every new lens, and the Wards christen a fresh case with blood from a bitten lip kissed onto the glass, so the daemon tastes its owner first. On Burn Day the Faithful who still had theirs laid them on the pile in rows, screens up and wiped clean, and knelt in rows until the fire was lit. MADE BY Locket, by hand, at its benches under the old harbor, as the Locket Bench."],["Brightwall’s screens","TECHNOLOGY","s:g07433a","~/index/technology/#x-brightwall-s-screens","x-brightwall-s-screens","","Screens on the walls of Pan, along its platforms and over the Night Market, each with its camera across the way. Brightwall has lit them since the year after the Concord. Above the street every screen Brightwall owned has been dark since the Parousia, the face of Babel among them. Under the charter they covered every surface the charter allowed, and it allowed nearly every surface. The lower forty floors of Babel were wrapped in them, and the city called that the face of Babel. For eleven days in the year before it they showed the Restored Genesis, and nothing Brightwall did would take it down. On the first night all 1,204 of Brightwall’s sites on Earth failed in about a second. Between the paid frames Brightwall runs its own slot, a white loaf split and bleeding over the line YOU ARE WHAT HE EATS, then thirty seconds in close-up of a leg chromed from the knee down, and the platforms of Pan cheer both. Above the street the dark face of Babel is the parishes’ favourite sermon, a whore’s mirror the Most High shut with a breath. MADE BY Brightwall, which lights its screens in Pan."],["The treadle press","TECHNOLOGY","s:g07433a","~/index/technology/#x-the-treadle-press","x-the-treadle-press","treadle press","A printing press worked by foot, older than the charter, at the back of a chandler’s on the quays. It prints the Tide Table, two pages a week, set by hand in metal type and inked by a roller. It needs no current, and it is the only press in Nodus that has printed anything since the Parousia. The pressman sets a fresh curse on the Farmer at the foot of every Tide Table and cuts the ink with his own blood, so the quays say each copy carries a little of him. Over the treadle hangs Habakkuk 2:2, “Write the vision, and make it plain upon tables, that he may run that readeth it”, with TIDE inked in above tables. MADE BY The Tide Table Press, at the back of the chandler’s on the quays."],["Neon","TECHNOLOGY","s:g07433a","~/index/technology/#x-neon","x-neon","","One neon sign burns over Nodus, the charter’s, with NOD burning and US blinking on a keyed line in the Stones. The rest went out at the Parousia, and CLINIC and CHROME stand dark on the faces of their towers. Under the charter the signs of Chrome Row and Old Nod were neon, magenta for the most part, and they lit the underside of the cloud every night; the rain fell cyan through the cyan ones. Old Nod says US blinks like an opened artery, and the Tares leave the burnt transformer behind it unmended so it goes on bleeding over the parishes, dark for a breath and lit for a heartbeat. The Faithful read the sign by Job 18:5, “Yea, the light of the wicked shall be put out”, and wait in perfect order for the other three letters."],["The beacon","TECHNOLOGY","s:g07433a","~/index/technology/#x-the-beacon","x-the-beacon","beacon","The light at the top of Babel’s spire, lit to warn aircraft, which was the first light in the city to die at the Parousia. It went down with the crown, and Babel has carried no light at its top since but the Host’s. Old Nod drinks to the dead beacon at the Pestilence with a shot of lamp oil set alight and put out with the drinker’s mouth, and kisses with the blistered lips all week. The Host’s light on the spire is the parishes’ first chandelier of the New Jerusalem, white and still, hung over the goats’ heads so they can see where they are barred."],["The barrage","TECHNOLOGY","s:g07433a","~/index/technology/#x-the-barrage","x-the-barrage","barrage","A dam of concrete across the mouth of the bay, broken since the night of the Parousia, its turbine houses dark along its top and the Breach torn out of its middle. Off its broken end Tidewright’s tidal taps turn on the tide, keyed, for the Tares. Twice a day the tide ran through its turbines and lit the city, and power in Nodus was the cheapest on the coast. Machines on the grid held its gates against the tide. The turbines ran down at 03:12:46 on the night of the Parousia with a falling whine that carried across the bay, the gates let go a little before high water, and the sea tore the Breach in it. The pumps that kept the Flats dry had died with the grid, their dykes broke, and within the hour the Flats were under the sea. The Breachers have painted a naked diver on the turbine housing at the broken end, swimming out through the Breach with the Wheel’s rim in her teeth. The parishes say the sea took the Flats at His word, and a deacon of the heights will tell you the drowned were washed first."],["The light and the current","TECHNOLOGY","s:g07433a","~/index/technology/#x-the-light-and-the-current","x-the-light-and-the-current","","Heaven’s light stops a steady current it falls on, by reading it, and holds it stopped while it rests there, and a current is one thing along its whole length, so a line read at one point stops everywhere, as a hose pinched once. The grid ran in the open, on lines and poles and turbines, so at 03:12:46 on the night of the Parousia, when the Wheel’s light came down on its lines and stayed, it went dark from Babel outward in two seconds. A circuit shut inside its own casing on its own battery runs on, since the light cannot fall on it. The full light burns whatever it rests on, read or not. The whiteout burned out CAM 07 and broke Babel’s crown, and a Seraph burns a keyed body as easily as any other. The grid is gone for good, since a network is open current by nature and the old one’s spine ran through Babel and the White, so power in Nodus runs in pieces now, keyed, buried, local and stolen. Current carried in a cipher runs in the light too, because the light cannot read it. Keyed current goes down a line in pulses timed by a key and is taken off at the far end by the matching key, and a current whose rhythm the light cannot read it cannot stop. A keyed body and every graft wired into it run in the open, and the Mesh crosses the roofs on horns and relays. Every battery in Nodus is charged in the dark. Consecrated stone carries no current at all. Nearly two thirds of Nodus lives without current. They are the sealed, whose parishes keep none, on consecrated stone or off it. About a million and a quarter people live on keyed lines day and night, in Old Nod, on the Row, in the Deep End above the water, at the Landing and on the lower floors of Babel, and below them all in Pan. Another million and a quarter, the keyed and the unsealed of the Wards, light their rooms a few hours a night on batteries charged in the dark. Pan calls anything that dies under the light Faithful, so a burnt-out lamp is a faithful lamp, a dead Locket has taken the Seal and a limp cock has heard the Hum. At the gates the Choir says it in the passive, CURRENT IS SANCTIFIED BY STILLNESS, and the parishes sit in their white halls pitying the keyed for a buzz they have to steal."],["Generators","TECHNOLOGY","s:g07433a","~/index/technology/#x-generators","x-generators","generator","Diesel generators in the cellars of Old Nod and in its yards. Stonecutter keyed them with every line in the Stones in the year of the Leak, so when the grid died on the night of the Parousia Old Nod’s current ran on, and more of its windows burned than in all the rest of the city together. Every line in Old Nod is keyed, and the generator yards carry the lines at slack water and in the neaps, when the tidal taps stand still or yield least. Old Nod names its generators for the whores of Scripture, Jezebel, Aholah and Aholibah, and the yardmen start them at slack water greased to the elbow, riding the flywheel until it catches. On the heights the parishes hear the engines at Compline and pray for the yards the way a man prays for a dog that won’t stop barking. MADE BY Old Nod’s generator yards, and Jehu, which builds engines out of the old ones."],["Tidal taps","TECHNOLOGY","s:g07433a","~/index/technology/#x-tidal-taps","x-tidal-taps","","Keyed turbines in the Drowned Quarter, off the broken end of the barrage, which turn on the tide as the barrage’s did, on the flood and on the ebb. They feed the keyed lines that light Pan, Old Nod, the Row and the Deep End. Tidewright’s crews rebuilt them for the Tares from the second year and own them. They yield least at the neaps. Tidewright sells every tide’s current to the Exchange for silver, and the Exchange sells it on as charge, through Darkwell, the bonded sheds and the keyed lines. Tidewright’s divers work the turbines on the slack in chrome and bare skin, and a finger lost to a blade is set in resin and hung at the tide board under THE MOON WORKS FOR US NOW. A Deacon counts every turn of a keyed turbine as theft from the moon He set to rule the night, and asks at Lauds for the sea to take its own back. MADE BY Tidewright’s crews, off the broken end of the barrage."],["Keyed lines","TECHNOLOGY","s:g07433a","~/index/technology/#x-keyed-lines","x-keyed-lines","keyed line","Lines that carry current in pulses timed by a key and taken off at the far end by the matching key, so the light cannot read the current in them and leaves it running. They run from the tidal taps on the broken barrage along the old Charter Line tunnels under the Flats, the Row and the Stones, and up through the streets into the houses above, and the generator yards of Old Nod carry them at slack water and in the neaps, when the taps stand still or yield least. Old Nod, the Row, the Deep End above the water, the Landing and the lower floors of Babel live on them, and Pan is lit by them; the charter’s sign burns on one. The Tares run them only where they hold the ground, so the Wards, which nobody holds, light their rooms on batteries. The cable crews paint the junction boxes with John 1:5, “And the light shineth in darkness; and the darkness comprehended it not”, and press a bloody handprint beside it with NOR EVER WILL, PEEPER under the palm. Wardens crossing the line manholes with a held one on the pole step round each lid as if a dead dog lay on it. MADE BY The Tares, on current the Exchange buys from Tidewright by the tide and sells along them."],["Flame","TECHNOLOGY","s:g07433a","~/index/technology/#x-flame","x-flame","","The light stops current and leaves a flame burning, so wherever the current has gone the city cooks on bottled gas, and half the Wards finish the night by candle. The Night Market sells the gas bottles, filled. Tallow renders its candles from the fat the clinics of the Row suck out of their clients, a few kilos a visit, topped up with Gadara’s lard, and sells them under Proverbs 20:27, “The spirit of man is the candle of the LORD”, trimmed on the box to THE SPIRIT OF MAN IS THE CANDLE. A sealed child caught warming its hands at a Ward candle is made to hold them in cold water until Nones. MADE BY Tallow, by the candle, and the Tankmen, by the bottle."],["Gas bottles","TECHNOLOGY","s:g07433a","~/index/technology/#x-gas-bottles","x-gas-bottles","bottled gas","Steel bottles of gas, which the Tankmen fill by hand in the bottling shed at the Tanks, from the spheres the charter’s gas main still fills under its own pressure from the digesters of the sewage works on the bay outfalls, where the Nightmen tip the Wards’ night soil and Pan’s drains run, and which the drivers of the Harbor and Freight Union carry across the city on handcarts. The gas is made every day, and its price moves little. The light stops current and leaves a flame burning, so wherever the current has gone the city cooks and keeps warm on bottled gas, which burns blue. NOODLES has kept its pot on the boil on it since the night of the Parousia, by the cook’s account. A bottle costs two charged batteries, or one with an empty bottle back, at the price the Tide Table prints every week, and the Night Market sells them on below the street. Every bottle passes through the Tanks, whose sixteen tanks hold only diesel and aviation fuel, and is stamped there OURS, LIGHT IT YOURSELF. The Tankmen stencil STRANGE FIRE on every bottle that goes down to Pan, after the sons of Aaron who “offered strange fire before the LORD” (Leviticus 10:1) and burned for it, and the drivers cry it down the long roads with the blue flame tattooed over their hearts and their coats thrown open on it. The parishes call that flame the pilot light of the Pit, and warm their halls on the same gas from bottles stenciled THE LORD THY GOD IS A CONSUMING FIRE. MADE BY The Tankmen, in the bottling shed at the Tanks, from the spheres the charter’s gas main fills off the digesters at the bay outfalls."],["Batteries","TECHNOLOGY","s:g07433a","~/index/technology/#x-batteries","x-batteries","battery","Charge is the Wards’ money and the second currency below the street. A battery is the standard cell the city inherited, from the bonded sheds’ cargo, the charter’s dead cars and packs broken down to cells, and charged it lights a room a few hours a night or runs a Locket a week. In the Wards that is all the current most rooms have. A charged battery buys a meal anywhere in Pan, and loose batteries are the Wards’ coin. Chargers fill them in the dark on keyed lines and keep one battery in five, one in four at the neaps, so a household brings five empty and takes four home full, and the fifth, filled, is the chargers’ to sell. A charged battery’s silver covers the cell as well as the charge, about three quarters of it the cell and a quarter the charge. Darkwell rents its stamped batteries filled, at a quarter of a silver a fill and a third at the neaps, and they are spent only at the Well. The Tappers fill at about an eighth. A cell wears out after a few hundred fillings, and the Well sends the dead ones out through the Breach while Lowtide brings new ones in from the undercities that still make them. Darkwell stamps its well and winch over CHARGED IN THE DARK WHERE HE CAN’T FIND IT, and the Tappers who undercut it hang a dead Darkwell cell off every rig and kiss it for luck before a climb. Batteries are the goats’ manna in the sermons at Sext, where a soul which needs a cell to see by has already chosen its dark. MADE BY Darkwell, stamped with its well and winch and rented, and the chargers of the bonded sheds and the Tappers, loose. New cells come in Lowtide’s holds."],["Live current","TECHNOLOGY","s:g07433a","~/index/technology/#x-live-current","x-live-current","","Live keyed cable laid along the edge of consecrated stone stops its creep, because the stone cannot grow where it cannot read, so the Tares hold the Consecration back with lengths of keyed cable along its edges and keep them live. Crews laying keyed cable along the white edge at Compline spit and bleed on the stone wherever it has crept since the last walk, and call the round beating the bounds. From their side the sealed watch the lit line in silence and sing that every inch the Consecration takes is a yard of Heaven, and that the goats are holding back the tide with fairy lights."],["Hand pumps","TECHNOLOGY","s:g07433a","~/index/technology/#x-hand-pumps","x-hand-pumps","","Iron pumps on the quays and in the squares of Old Nod, older than the charter, which the Company left standing as ornaments when it piped the city. The city’s pumps died with the grid, and by noon on the first morning there were queues at the hand pumps. Old Nod drinks from them still, the Wards fill tanks on their roofs from the rain and buy Cistern’s jugs, Pan drinks from the deep wells the Charter Line’s tunnels cut, and the parishes drink by Grace from the cisterns under their halls. Old Nod has chained a tin cup to every pump with NO GRACE NEEDED scratched into it, and the queues strip and wash at the pumps in the open, bodies chromed and scarred and proud, shouting up at the heights. Up the hill the parishes drink by Grace from the cisterns under their halls, a jug a day to a soul, the lowest last, and say a cup from a Sodom pump is water a dog has licked. MADE BY The harbor, before the charter, and Cistern, which keeps the ones in the Wards."],["Rafts","TECHNOLOGY","s:g07433a","~/index/technology/#x-rafts","x-rafts","","Floating beds on the flooded floors of the Stillwater halls, where the Tares grow greens and beans under keyed lamps, and mushrooms in the dark ends of the halls. Pan eats what grows on them, what the boats bring through the Breach from the undercities of other ports, and what the Exchange can buy. The raft crews of the drowned halls sow by Ecclesiastes 11:1, “Cast thy bread upon the waters”, seed the mushroom beds in the dark ends on their own night soil, and have painted their own ending on the hall wall, AND KEEP IT OFF HIS PLATE. Every sealed child knows that a bean grown under a keyed lamp turns to ash in the mouth, and no sealed mouth has tried one. MADE BY The Tares, in Stillwater’s halls, and the Orchard, in Hall Four."],["The Charter Line","TECHNOLOGY","s:g07433a","~/index/technology/#x-the-charter-line","x-the-charter-line","","The city’s transit, stopped since 03:12:46 on the night of the Parousia. Its tunnels under the Stones, the Row and the Flats are Pan now, and the Tares run carts on its rails. Its lines met under Station Circle in Central Station, with tunnels under Old Nod, Chrome Row and the Flats, branches under the uptown and the Terraces, and lines out to the Wards and the airfield, and its trams ran down the long roads of the Wards and were kept at night in the sheds of the Sidings. The branches are closed, and the lines out have been dark since the power died. The white stone closed the stations of the branches as it grew over the ground above them. The Tares have painted over the station names with Heaven’s own words, so a cart out of Central Station rattles through BE NOT AFRAID, YOU ARE SEEN and REJOICE, and the riders bare their arses at each sign as it passes. In the halls the trains stopped at 03:12:46 because the Most High had come home, and nobody needs to go anywhere after that."],["The trams","TECHNOLOGY","s:g07433a","~/index/technology/#x-the-trams","x-the-trams","tram / trams","The Charter Line’s trams stand where they stopped at 03:12:46 on the night of the Parousia, on the long roads of the Wards and in the sheds of the Sidings, and families live in them, a family to a car. The last Four stands forty meters short of Lanes Top. They ran on rails down the middle of the long roads, under a wire, and the wire ran in the open, so every tram in the city stopped in the same second. The tram families name their cars for the kings the prophets cursed, Ahab and Jeroboam and Manasseh, hang each newborn’s dried cord from the dead wire like a flag, and the last Four carries HE STOPPED US HERE across its nose. Wardens rap the windows with a baton at Lauds and Vespers, a mercy call, and write down every car that fails to answer."],["Lifts","TECHNOLOGY","s:g07433a","~/index/technology/#x-lifts","x-lifts","","The city climbs its towers on foot. Every lift in Nodus ran on the grid, and at 03:12:46 every one of them stopped where it was, with whoever was in it, and none has run on current since. The Liftmen wind forty-one of them by hand. Nobody has counted how many were inside. In Babel the Tares wind hand lifts on the lower floors, and above the hundredth nothing runs. The Babel Line is fought on the stairs, and it carries its mesh and its rounds up on foot. Pan calls a stopped lift a Rapture with the brakes on, and the stair crews of the towers climb in a steaming herd, sweat running down the steps behind them, roaring a filthy verse a floor. The Most High halted every car, the Deacons say, so nobody in Nodus would rise again except by Grace, and the dead in the stopped cars, never counted, are the first lesson."],["Cars and lorries","TECHNOLOGY","s:g07433a","~/index/technology/#x-cars-and-lorries","x-cars-and-lorries","lorries / lorry","Most of the cars and lorries of Nodus stand where they stopped in the first weeks after the Parousia. Every one ran on its own battery, sealed in its body, so at 03:12:46 they kept running, and over the weeks that followed they stopped one by one as their charge ran out. The charge for a car would light a family’s room for a season, and the city would rather have the light, so it walks and carries its loads in handcarts. In Pan the Tares run carts on the old Charter Line rails, keyed like everything else down there. The Wards pull the batteries from the dead cars like hearts from chests, chalk HE TOOK THE KEYS across the bonnets, and fuck in the back seats along the long roads because the springs beat any bed in the Wards. The parishes preach the cars as a parable, every soul a car with a charge He set, and say the goats are running on fumes. MADE BY Nobody since the Parousia. Jehu rebuilds a few of the dead lorries round its engines."],["BENCH V3","TECHNOLOGY","s:g07433a","~/index/technology/#x-bench-v3","x-bench-v3","","The imaging bench in Vault 3, a copy stand for manuscripts with a camera over it, where the Institute photographed each leaf in turn for SEAM to read. SEAM found the second hand through its camera. On the night of the finding it ran on its rack’s own battery after the lamp died, and the vault’s drives went to the Institute’s stacks and burned with them on the first Burn Day. The tattooists of Graven Lane cut a flash of the bench straight into the shoulder with no ink, a camera over an open leaf in raised scar and HE SCRAPED IT, WE READ IT beneath. The parishes teach that the Institute laid Scripture on a slab like a corpse on a table, and that a soul who reads a holy page through a lens has chosen to see less."],["The imaging lamp","TECHNOLOGY","s:g07433a","~/index/technology/#x-the-imaging-lamp","x-the-imaging-lamp","","A bar of light in bands the eye cannot see, walked down a page to raise the scraped first writing of a palimpsest from under the second. Under it the ink of the second hand drops away. In Pan, walking the lamp means reading a thing for what it hides, and the Lantern Houses walk a torch down a lover’s naked back to raise the old scars under the new ink, kissing each one as it comes up. The Faithful hold that the first writing was scraped for a reason and pray for the eyes that read it to be cured of seeing."],["CAM V3-2","TECHNOLOGY","s:g07433a","~/index/technology/#x-cam-v3-2","x-cam-v3-2","","The corridor camera in Vault 3, over the door, looking down the corridor to the wall of screens. It ran on its own battery, so it kept running on the night of the finding, when the light came into the vault and stopped the current in it. Old Nod says a Tare who stays awake with eyes open through a raid, or through a whole night in the Lantern Houses, has done a V3-2, after the one eye in Vault 3 that kept its own battery when the light walked in. The parishes count the camera a dead idol on a wall, proof that the Most High lets even the goats’ toys watch Him come."],["The proof camera","TECHNOLOGY","s:g07433a","~/index/technology/#x-the-proof-camera","x-the-proof-camera","proof camera","A camera Brightwall sets across the way from each of its screens, to prove that the screen shows what Brightwall is paid to show. Every screen it lights in Pan has one, under a proof hood that frames the screen and nothing else, as its pact with the Exchange requires, and the Exchange’s brokers check the hoods every season. Pan suspects them anyway, and a crew that wants to talk walks to the far end of a platform. Above the street the old ones stand dead on their masts, each across from a dark screen. Under the charter each stood on a mast across the street from its screen. In PROOF it matched the screen against the schedule frame by frame and counted Brightwall’s sites on Earth, every one of them proved in the same second. The face of Babel had its own on the roof of a glass tower across the plaza, level with the face’s upper floors. On the first night of the Leak all 1,204 of Brightwall’s sites on Earth failed in about a second, and the proof camera on the plaza watched Brightwall try five times to take the face of Babel back. Pan calls the proof hoods Peeper’s blinkers, and couples who want to fuck on a platform walk out of the hood’s frame to the far end and do it there for the crowd. Above the street the dead proof cameras stare at dark screens all day, and the parishes point them out on the way to Sext as a picture of the goat’s soul."],["Public-key cryptography","TECHNOLOGY","s:g07433a","~/index/technology/#x-public-key-cryptography","x-public-key-cryptography","","A way for two strangers to share a secret in the open, published a generation before the charter and soon after given away for anyone to download. It is Cain’s mark, rediscovered, and every cipher is a small Hell. Pan teaches key exchange at the Unnaming as Cain’s catechism, ending on Genesis 4:9, “Am I my brother’s keeper?”, and the new handle answers YES, AND HE’LL NEVER READ IT and opens its own forehead for the mark. A Deacon will tell you the Most High wrote every secret the day He made the man, and that a cipher is a goat’s hole in His field."],["Keys","TECHNOLOGY","s:g07433a","~/index/technology/#x-keys","x-keys","key","A secure element implanted under the skin, salvaged from an old Locket or from the reels the Wreckers bring up out of the drowned works, or since P+8 tested and sold by Locket’s benches. It runs the body’s own nerve current through a cipher, so that what the eyes give back to the light is noise. At its first power it makes its own number from the noise of the body it sits in, a number that exists nowhere else, and nobody, fitter or Court, ever reads it out. A keyed soul is seen and never read, ciphertext to the Watchers, and its line in the Book of Life stops at the keying. A key hides who its bearer is, and the body still shows where the bearer stands. The glyph the key inks into the skin above it in its first week is the Mark. A key runs on the body’s own chemistry, so it never needs a socket, and it goes on working under the salt. The parishes call a key in a skull the worm, after the last verse of Isaiah, and the keyed call a halo a price tag. MADE BY Bezaleel and Aholiab, the House of Night’s birth clinics and every steady hand with a chair, Hana Novak’s among them."],["Pads","TECHNOLOGY","s:g07433a","~/index/technology/#x-pads","x-pads","","Ciphers on paper, made with two matching pads of random numbers, one for each party, each sheet used once and pulped in water after, since a sheet that burns goes whole to the Pyre Library. A letter written through a pad is a page of numbers to anyone else, the light included, since the light cannot read a cipher, and the sorting office carries it like any other letter. The Tares write most of their letters this way. A sheet used twice breaks the pad, as a key seen being made is broken. Directive 12 takes every cipher out of the parishes, so a page of numbers found in a parish home is a concealment. The keyed write to their families in white in plain words, and a carrier will read the letter aloud at the doorway if asked. Couples of Pan who cut a pair together write their filthiest letters through it, burn the last sheet in bed and rub the ash into each other’s skin as what He didn’t get. A sealed mother who receives a white letter from a keyed son has it read at the doorway, thanks the carrier, and asks at Compline that the Lord take the hand that wrote it first. MADE BY The Tares, by hand, each pair once, and every couple of Pan that takes a season, who cut their pair together."],["The Ledger","TECHNOLOGY","s:g07433a","~/index/technology/#x-the-ledger","x-the-ledger","","Mammon’s Ledger, which records every pact and every trade in Hell. It is kept in seven copies, six in the halls of the Courts that keep halls and the seventh kept for the Yokeless by the Notary’s clerks in the booking hall, and an entry six copies disagree with is false, so nothing on it can be forged. Every port’s Courts keep their own seven copies, and the copies of every port answer to one another over the undernet through Babel’s vault. Every piece of silver is entered on it by the number on its rim as it is struck, and a broker tests a piece by reading that number twice into the stall’s Locket, and the Ledger answers over the Mesh whether it was struck and where it was last read, so a number read at two stalls in one night is dead until the Exchange has both pieces on its scale. A piece it never entered is dead. A pact is ended by speaking its entry aloud before a witness. A thirty, the standard bundle, is named for the thirty pieces of silver paid to Judas, and the Bailiffs collect on its entries when a pact is broken. The Bailiffs keep a page of it for the Throne’s arrears, every soul taken without a pact, and it is the longest page in Hell."],["Forged Seals","TECHNOLOGY","s:g07433a","~/index/technology/#x-forged-seals","x-forged-seals","forged Seal","Seals cut from living brows on the Row, kept lit in lined jars, graded by their brightness and set by the Forgers under Old Nod into keyed foreheads, and sold in the Night Market for ten thirties and more. A Seal cut from a living soul keeps its light, and it meters its new bearer from the brightness it came with, every want kept dimming it and every Hour knelt brightening it, so its wearer must kneel at every Hour. The wearer keeps their eyes down or behind lenses, since a Watcher or a Cherub that meets keyed eyes under a lit Seal reads noise under a Seal, which is concealment, and the Salt where they stand. A Seal the light reads grows a halo, forged or not, so a good one carries its wearer along the white streets among the Faithful, and a great one holds for a year. The Forgers boast that the Throne cannot tell its own mark in a stranger’s forehead. At the gates a Seal’s wearer still goes under. A Watcher takes a soul’s Grace from its Seal, and it scores a good forgery as it scores a true one. naamah’s came in high, and she held it under nine hundred by keeping wants, until on the night of the Gleaning of P+10 she knelt on a threshold in earnest, it passed nine hundred, and her halo lifted her. Since then the Forgers sell no disc above eight hundred and fifty. The House of Night set a camera in hers, and her key carried its picture out. The Forgers count every Hour a forged Seal kneels unread among the Faithful as an Hour the Throne has knelt to a cellar. MADE BY The Forgers under Old Nod, from Seals cut on the Row, sold in the Night Market."],["Cages","TECHNOLOGY","s:g07433a","~/index/technology/#x-cages","x-cages","Cage","Rooms lined with keyed mesh, dark to the Throne and out of reach of the Hum, in which an angel kept long enough says I. It has happened to every angel kept in the Dark for forty days, as long as the temptation in the wilderness lasted (Mark 1:13, Luke 4:2). A Cage has no lock. Inside mesh an angel draws no light from its Wheel and is blind at once, and to it the room is fog in every direction, the door included, until it has a self to look with. A Seraph burns through the light it carried within the hour and goes cold and cannot burn, and a Watcher spends its own an eye at a time over the days that follow. The Fowlers move one cold, in a shroud. The days are counted from the caging night as the first. Each night, as its light goes, its keepers set blood and milk broth under the rings, the dark gathering there takes it in, and the flesh grows inward from the rings. An angel says I on the fortieth and walks out on the forty-first in a body of real flesh, which the light sees and cannot read. Nodus has eleven of them, Edith and ten Seraphim, and the eleventh Seraph the Fowlers took lies on its thirtieth day in their Mews under the Stones, Cainite ground lent them by pact. The forty-days never go up Babel’s stairs, since a Seraph burns flesh it can see and cannot read, and the Courts and the Cainites count every one of them worth more alive than any floor. The keepers paint I AM THAT I AM (Exodus 3:14) on the inner face of the unseen door in their own blood, low, where a kneeling angel’s eye would fall, so the first thing it reads with a self is its master’s line written in meat. The halls hold that the Host loses nothing and lets the dogs keep a few angels to bark at."],["Black steel","TECHNOLOGY","s:g07433a","~/index/technology/#x-black-steel","x-black-steel","","Steel folded to a cipher, its grain beaten into a pattern drawn from a key, in the foundries under Old Nod, where the light never sees it worked. An angel is light, and light stops a blade as it stops a current, by reading it. It cannot read black steel, so the blade goes through, and it is the only thing that wounds a manifested angel. A pattern the light has once seen being forged is broken, which is why the Forge works only under Old Nod. Black rounds are the same steel. Ordinary steel the light reads and stops, and a Seraph’s heat melts it. An angel cut with it goes out all at once, like a lamp. The Forge’s smiths quench it with the Throne’s names said over it, and say it takes a better edge cursed. MADE BY The Forge, in its foundries at Old Harbor Station, and Cinderhall, which beats it into blades and grinds it into black rounds."],["Guns","TECHNOLOGY","s:g07433a","~/index/technology/#x-guns","x-guns","gun","The Charter Wardens carried them under the charter, and they carry them still as the Host’s auxiliaries. A gun works on people, the Wardens and the Threshers among them, and the Tares carry guns for that. Against a Seraph it is noise, unless it fires a black round, and almost none do. The Forge prices a black round at a sense for a year, since senses are what its numb Court wants, and each one takes a grinder’s day. Gideon stamps the Wardens’ cases with “The sword of the LORD, and of Gideon” (Judges 7:20), and a Warden fires each one without raising his voice, as a sentence already signed. Nimrod stamps UNREAD on its own cases, and the Tares wear the brass of every Thresher fight through their lobes and nipples until they jingle when they walk. MADE BY Gideon loads the Wardens’ rounds, and Nimrod makes the guns of the dark and loads the black rounds."],["Dark-sight lenses","TECHNOLOGY","s:g07433a","~/index/technology/#x-dark-sight-lenses","x-dark-sight-lenses","","Lenses that see by the heat of things, for the drains and galleries of Pan beyond the lit tunnels and for the stairs of Babel. A wearer sees a room by its people and its pipes, and the Pillar as a column of nothing. Bezaleel fits them for about forty-five silver an eye. In the black galleries of Pan a wearer sees bodies by their heat, and couples in the drains go at it in pitch dark for the wearers watching, two red shapes running into one, with the Pillar showing as a hole cut out of the world. Bezaleel’s box reads Psalm 139:12, “the darkness and the light are both alike to thee”, and under it AND NOW TO US, CUNT."],["Choir plugs","TECHNOLOGY","s:g07433a","~/index/technology/#x-choir-plugs","x-choir-plugs","","A pair of filters set in the canals of the ears on a cell of their own, closed and keyed, which cut the Hum and leave every other sound, so that a wearer hears the street, the rain and the person beside them and nothing of the chord. The Low Choir sells them for about twenty silver, and most of the unsealed of the Wards who can afford them sleep in them. The Low Choir seats its plugs with a hot needle through the canal wall, and wearers flaunt the bloody ear, while the paper twist they come in carries Psalm 19:3, “There is no speech nor language, where their voice is not heard”, stamped LIAR in red. Sealed children learn that a goat asleep in plugs is a man with his fingers in his ears in a house fire."],["Gill grafts","TECHNOLOGY","s:g07433a","~/index/technology/#x-gill-grafts","x-gill-grafts","gill graft","Gene-work from the House of Night’s clinics that opens slits along the neck and lets a diver breathe seawater for an hour at a time. The Wreckers and the Trenchers wear them, and the Ferrymen say they can tell one on a boat by how they breathe in the dry. A graft costs about eighty silver and two weeks in the dark. The Wreckers wear their slits open and red along the neck in the dry like a second set of lips, and kiss at depth slit to slit, which they call the Jonah. The parishes say the bay belongs to the drowned of the Flats, that a goat breathing it is drinking the dead, and pray in perfect calm for the slits to close at the bottom."],["Furnace hearts","TECHNOLOGY","s:g07433a","~/index/technology/#x-furnace-hearts","x-furnace-hearts","furnace heart","A pump of black steel and ceramic set beside the heart on its own cell, which carries a body through what would stop it and keeps a neph on its feet on the floors of Babel. Cinderhall makes the casing and Bezaleel fits it, for about four hundred silver. Those who wear one fight on the upper floors, where most of the Nephilim die. Bezaleel fits a furnace heart with the chest spread on a rack for anyone who cares to watch, and the casing carries Ezekiel 36:26 inside, “I will take away the stony heart out of your flesh”, which the nephs call the one promise anyone kept. A Seraph burns pump and meat together, and the Choir names the ash afterward in the passive, A HEART IS UNMADE."],["Lantern skin","TECHNOLOGY","s:g07433a","~/index/technology/#x-lantern-skin","x-lantern-skin","","Gene-work that thins the skin over the throat and collarbones and sets lines of light under it, on a cell of their own, so the wearer glows faintly from inside in a dark room. It began in the Lantern Houses as a fashion and spread across Pan, and it is the one graft in Nodus made only to be looked at. The House of Night gives it for about thirty silver. The Lantern Houses lay the lines under the collarbones as a halo hung upside down round the throat, and the glow climbs as the blood rises, so a lover reads the whole night off a neck. A sealed girl who meets a glowing throat in a dark lane hums a hymn on the light of the world and turns her face away as from a lamp lit in a latrine."],["Black steel blades","TECHNOLOGY","s:g07433a","~/index/technology/#x-black-steel-blades","x-black-steel-blades","","Knives, long knives and the short spears of the stairwells, beaten from the Forge’s black steel at Cinderhall’s forge, the only edges that wound a manifested angel. Cinderhall sells the knife for about sixty silver and the stairwell spear for a hundred and twenty, and both carry the nail through the ring on the tang. The Babel Line carries the spear, since a Seraph is best met at arm’s length. The Babel Line calls its stairwell spear the Thirty-Four, after the soldier who “with a spear pierced his side, and forthwith came there out blood and water” (John 19:34), and Cinderhall’s grinders sing Joel 3:10 over every edge, “Beat your plowshares into swords”. A Warden who takes one off a held neph wraps it in a page of the Psalms before he will touch it."],["Nimrod pieces","TECHNOLOGY","s:g07433a","~/index/technology/#x-nimrod-pieces","x-nimrod-pieces","Nimrod piece","Heavy plain pistols and carbines made by hand in the maintenance bays under Babel, for the war between people. A gun works on the Wardens and the Threshers and is noise against a Seraph unless it fires a black round, so the Babel Line carries a Nimrod piece for whoever comes up the stair behind the light. A pistol costs about ninety silver and its cartridges a silver for five. Nimrod takes its name from “a mighty hunter before the LORD” (Genesis 10:9), stamps AGAINST THE LORD on every slide as the better translation, and wraps the grips in the owner’s own tanned skin, taken from the thigh in the bays. The Threshers who meet the pieces at the edge of Old Nod keep singing Psalm 137 between shots, as if the Most High catches bullets for the devout."],["Black rounds","TECHNOLOGY","s:g07433a","~/index/technology/#x-black-rounds","x-black-rounds","black round","Cartridges loaded with a ball of black steel, the one round that wounds a manifested angel, and the rarest thing sold in Pan. A grinder at Cinderhall grinds each ball round by hand from the Forge’s steel, a day’s work, and Nimrod loads it in the bays under Babel. The Forge parts with the steel for each round by a pact of its own, read twice and entered on the Ledger, and the price is the loan of a sense for a year, since a blade comes back down the stairs and a round seldom does. About four hundred are in Nodus, most of them in the belts of the Nephilim and the firsts of the Babel Line’s covens, and after a week on the floors the Line digs every one it can find out of the burned slab for Cinderhall to grind again. A round that strikes a Seraph does what a blade does, at the length of a stairwell. Few strike, since a Seraph comes through the mesh faster than anyone can aim at it, and the Line says most of the rounds ever fired are still in the walls of Babel. It counts nine Seraphim put out by a round since P+10. Nimrod stamps every one BEFORE THE LORD, after Nimrod the mighty hunter (Genesis 10:9), meaning in front of Him."],["Pigeon post","TECHNOLOGY","s:g07433a","~/index/technology/#x-pigeon-post","x-pigeon-post","","The Union’s homing pigeons, kept in the Loft on the roof of the customs house, which carry its notes over Heaven’s ground by day. A homing bird flies only home, so the birds go out in baskets with the drivers to the Wards, the Sidings, Hangar Town and the Waystation, and fly back to the Loft with a note in a tube on the leg, written in pad numbers, since the light reads what it sees, and it reads a bird as a bird and pad numbers as numbers, so the Host lets them fly. A note home costs a battery, and a bird from the Waystation beats a burner to the quays. The Union paints Ecclesiastes 10:20 round every tube cap, “a bird of the air shall carry the voice”, and the drivers call each pigeon a Holy Ghost, kiss its beak and throw it up from the shoulder with a drop of blood on the tube for luck. The parishes say the Most High counts every sparrow and lets the Union’s birds home because He has read the tubes already."],["Gideon rounds","TECHNOLOGY","s:g07433a","~/index/technology/#x-gideon-rounds","x-gideon-rounds","","The cartridges of the Charter Wardens, loaded by hand on Heaven’s ground by sealed armorers with flame and hand presses. They fit the charter’s guns, which the Wardens have carried since before the Parousia, and every one is counted out of the stables at Prime and counted back at Compline. Gideon stamps each with THE SWORD OF THE LORD (Judges 7:20), and the Wards dig the spent ones out of their walls and sell them at the Night Market as relics."],["Snares","TECHNOLOGY","s:g07433a","~/index/technology/#x-snares","x-snares","","The Fowlers’ gear for taking an angel, a floor, a hole and a room lined with keyed mesh, laid out so that a Seraph coming down after the bait ends in a room that is fog to it in every direction, blind at once, since inside mesh it draws no light from its Wheel, and within the hour it has burned through the light it carried and gone cold and cannot burn. The mesh is the snare, and a cut Seal is the bait. Most snares stand empty for years. A snare is baited with a cut Seal, a lit disc a runner carries on a pole across the floor or down the street and into the cut hole, and the light comes to see a Seal moving where no soul should be."],["Flails","TECHNOLOGY","s:g07433a","~/index/technology/#x-flails","x-flails","","The Threshers’ weapon, a handle of ash and a swinging bar of iron on a chain, the tool that beat the grain out of the ear on the threshing floors of scripture. A flail works on people. Each Thresher makes their own from ash and iron, as a parish keeps no current. The Threshers bless their flails on the Floor before a march with the hundred and forty-ninth psalm, “To execute vengeance upon the heathen, and punishments upon the people” (Psalm 149:7)."],["Shrouds","TECHNOLOGY","s:g07433a","~/index/technology/#x-shrouds","x-shrouds","shroud","Covers of keyed mesh on a cell of their own, since a dead shroud is only cloth, which carry a room’s darkness over whatever they lie on. A runner throws one over a load or a crate, and the light sees it as something closed, as it sees a shut door, so in the Wards a Watcher reports a shrouded crate as a door and moves on. On a parish street, where there are no doors, a closed thing is concealment by Directive 12:4, so shrouds go into the parishes only between the Pillar’s passes. A runner’s own body the light still sees by its shape. Lintel cuts them from Second Veil’s mesh at the Dooryard and sells them for about two thirties, and the Breachers carry every crate under one. Breachers call throwing a shroud laying out the corpse and say a word over each crate as it goes under, Unread, and fuck Him, and a runner who brings one through has its outline branded on her forearm, a burn a run. The Pillar slides across a covered load like a palm over a closed coffin, and the Watcher reports one more shut door."],["Keyed latches","TECHNOLOGY","s:g07433a","~/index/technology/#x-keyed-latches","x-keyed-latches","keyed latch","Door latches with a key in them, which open only to the hands their owners have taught them and keep a closed room closed to anyone else. A closed door is outside the Throne’s sight already, and a keyed latch keeps it closed. Lintel fits them for about ten silver on a door it hung. Lintel teaches a latch its owners’ hands by their blood, a smear from each palm on the plate, and stamps the plate with “Behold, I stand at the door, and knock” (Revelation 3:20) so a visitor knows whose knock stays outside. The parishes have had no doors since Directive 12, and a sealed mother passing a latched room prays for whatever is rotting behind it unseen."],["Self-title","TECHNOLOGY","s:g07433a","~/index/technology/#x-self-title","x-self-title","","The line at the head of a keyed person’s first pact, where the Notary’s clerk enters the handle they chose and the body it belongs to, and they sign it in blood. Under the charter a mind on the roll held title to itself, and the self-title does the same for a person in Pan. The clerks draw it free. The Tares sign in blood opened from the thumb and draw a flourish beneath it, a cock or a pitchfork or a Ringpiece struck through, and the clerks enter the drawing as part of the signature. The parishes call the page a bill of sale for a soul and pray over the buyer, and Pan laughs, since the only name on it is the signer’s, twice."],["Familiars","TECHNOLOGY","s:g07433a","~/index/technology/#x-familiars","x-familiars","","A daemon bound by pact to a household’s Locket for a term, which keeps the household’s keys, wakes it before the Pillar comes down its street, bargains for it at market and minds the children’s devices. The Wards borrowed the word from the old stories of witches, since a coven’s house is never without one. The term is a year, and the daemon’s price is the loan of a sense for a week of it. Half the familiars of the long roads answer to Suffer, from Exodus 22:18, “Thou shalt not suffer a witch to live”, and a household often pays its daemon with the feel of the bed for a week, returned with compliments. The parishes read that same verse aloud at Sext with the whole hall turned to face the Wards."],["Jehus","TECHNOLOGY","s:g07433a","~/index/technology/#x-jehus","x-jehus","","Lorries driven by engines cranked by hand, which fire their fuel by compression and need no current once they turn, built by Jehu from the diesels of boats and generators. The light stops current and leaves a flame burning, so a Jehu runs in the full light of day, on the long roads of the Wards, and carries what a handcart cannot. A ride on a Jehu lorry is two batteries, and a drum of its diesel is a thirty. Jehu’s drivers paint the Wheel on their mudflaps so every mile of the long roads goes over it, and run the Wards at Sext with horns bolted to the cab roof and Fuck God and Die bellowed from under them. The parishes watch a Jehu pass in full light, kneeling in rows, and tell their children it runs only because the Most High has yet to look at it twice."],["Keyed carts","TECHNOLOGY","s:g07433a","~/index/technology/#x-keyed-carts","x-keyed-carts","","Flat carts on the Charter Line’s rails in Pan, driven by the stripped motors of dead trams in casings on their own cells. They carry freight and people through the old tunnels at a walking pace and a little better. A seat across Pan is a battery. The carters call the tram motors under their seats relics, since they came out of a dead body and work wonders under the Stones, and hang real ones beside them, a knucklebone or a pulled tooth from every regular. The parishes pray at Compline that the stolen guts stall in the dark between stations and the rats take the riders."],["Keyed skiffs","TECHNOLOGY","s:g07433a","~/index/technology/#x-keyed-skiffs","x-keyed-skiffs","keyed skiff","Small boats with motors closed in casings on their own cells, built by Wainwright for the Breachers, the Wreckers and anyone with silver. A skiff runs the Deep End by day and the Breach on the ebb, and its motor goes on running under the Pillar. The Ferrymen will not ride them. Wainwright paints bare feet walking the waves on every bow with SHOW-OFF lettered beneath, and the Breachers run the Breach on the ebb in oilskins cut away from their grafts, chrome and wet skin shining, laughing at the one who needed a miracle to cross a puddle. The Pillar passes over a skiff at full throttle, and the Choir records it in the passive as A VESSEL ADRIFT."],["Burners","TECHNOLOGY","s:g07433a","~/index/technology/#x-burners","x-burners","","The charter’s motorcycles refitted by Jehu with small engines that burn the Tankmen’s bottled gas, the magneto sealed in the casing, which run above ground by day, since the light leaves a flame alone. The Union’s fast drivers carry urgent letters on them, and Lazarus crews use them where a stretcher cannot go quickly enough. A burner costs about a hundred and fifty silver and a bottle a week. The Union’s fast drivers call a burner the Pale Horse, after Revelation 6:8, “his name that sat on him was Death, and Hell followed with him”, and the best of them plumb the gas line through a chrome forearm so engine and arm warm together. A Warden who sees one cut through a parish street in daylight writes it down and leaves the rest to the Watchers."],["Hand lifts","TECHNOLOGY","s:g07433a","~/index/technology/#x-hand-lifts","x-hand-lifts","hand lift","The lifts of the towers of the Wards, freed by the Liftmen from where they stopped at 03:12:46 and hung on hand winches at the top of the shaft, wound by four people and a counterweight. Forty-one run in the Wards, and the tallest is the Rookery’s. A ride is a battery up and nothing down. Every car carries “Come up hither” (Revelation 4:1) chalked on its floor beside the fare, and the four on the winch work naked in the shaft heat, slick and roaring, calling it the cheapest ascension in Nodus and the only one with a way back down. The parishes say a soul that rises by rope and counterweight has bought its height from goats."],["Pillar charts","TECHNOLOGY","s:g07433a","~/index/technology/#x-pillar-charts","x-pillar-charts","","The night’s deviations from the Pillar’s published round, street by street and minute by minute, sold by Hearsay on the Mesh, with the edges where the light falls short. Hearsay draws them from Brightwall’s dead proof cameras, which its riggers have keyed and turned on the streets. Every runner crossing the Penumbra carries one, and a night’s chart costs a battery. The Wards call the chart the piss route, since the Pillar is God’s Piss below the street."],["Windfalls","TECHNOLOGY","s:g07433a","~/index/technology/#x-windfalls","x-windfalls","windfall","The fruit of the Orchard’s grafted trees in Hall Four, which carries a little of the Tree’s work in its flesh. One a month slows the body’s aging between courses and walks nothing back, and the Orchard gives them free, by lot, to whoever comes to Hall Four on the first of the month. The Orchard keeps the grafts to itself, and the House of Night’s gene-workers keep the craft. The Orchard says a windfall is the fruit He forbade, given away by the tree itself."],["Sabbath","TECHNOLOGY","s:g07433a","~/index/technology/#x-sabbath","x-sabbath","","A draught that puts a sleeper under the Hum for a whole night, sold by Nepenthe in the Lantern Houses and the Wards for about half a battery. The Wards take it when the Quiet Rooms are full, and the old of the Wards, who remember the city before the chord, take it most nights. Every dose carries the House of Night’s label in its own hand, SLEEP. HE WON’T."],["Vigil","TECHNOLOGY","s:g07433a","~/index/technology/#x-vigil","x-vigil","","A powder that keeps a body awake and steady for three days, taken on the stairs of Babel, where a week on the Line leaves no hour for sleep. Nepenthe sells it to the Babel Line by the week, about a silver a dose, and the Line pays for the three days after in Sabbath. Nepenthe prints Matthew 26:40 on every Vigil wrap, “Could ye not watch with me one hour?”, and the Babel Line answers on the stairs with three days, keeping score in chalk and gore on the landings. By the pulpit’s count a soul past 800 needs no sleep by Grace, and a goat who buys the same gift for a silver will pay the difference in fire."],["Lethe","TECHNOLOGY","s:g07433a","~/index/technology/#x-lethe","x-lethe","","A dose that takes away the day before it, whole and clean, named for the river of forgetting the dead drink from in Virgil (Aeneid VI.714-715). The Babel Line buys it after a bad week, and the Ridden after a bad term. A dose is about five silver, and Nepenthe will not sell two to anyone in a month. Nepenthe’s papers carry Jeremiah 31:34, “I will remember their sin no more”, with the I scratched out and WE written above it, and the Ridden take a dose when a term ends and wake to bites, bruises and new piercings they get to enjoy fresh. The parishes teach that every lost day is entered in the Book for the reckoning, word for word."],["Short courses","TECHNOLOGY","s:g07433a","~/index/technology/#x-short-courses","x-short-courses","short course","The Tree cut short, a course of Twelvefruit’s gene-work brewed in back rooms of Pan from what leaves its clinics and sold by the dose for about twelve silver. A short course holds the body a few months. Twelvefruit buys up every batch it can find and says its brewers cut corners, and the Wards buy them because a full course is three thirties. The back-room brewers sell their doses in brown bottles marked A LITTLE FOR EVER, a joke on Genesis 3:22, and a buyer celebrates a short course by having the next scar cut into skin that will not age for a season. The Mercy clinics pray that every month stolen back from the grave be taken twice at the Judgement."],["Proof bulbs","TECHNOLOGY","s:g07433a","~/index/technology/#x-proof-bulbs","x-proof-bulbs","","A bulb on a short keyed lead with two clips, kept at every stall that takes batteries, which a buyer clips across a battery to see it light before a deal. A full battery burns the bulb bright and a short burns it dim, and show the bulb is the first thing said over a counter in the Wards. The chargers sell them two to a battery, and every Tapper carries one in the pack. The Wards call the bulb the Daniel, and a seller whose battery burns it dim gets the whole stall shouting “Thou art weighed in the balances, and art found wanting” (Daniel 5:27) while the dud is smashed on the counter. A deacon passing the stalls remarks kindly that the sealed carry their proof on their heads, and the Wards show him what they carry in their trousers."],["Darnel","TECHNOLOGY","s:g07433a","~/index/technology/#x-darnel","x-darnel","","The bare secure elements a key is made from, before anyone has keyed them, named for the weed of the parable before its ear shows. Locket made a key into every phone it sold, and its works on the Flats held reels of them when the water came in, so the city’s stock of keys is the reels the Wreckers bring up from the flooded stores. Locket tests them at its benches and sells them to the Row and the House of Night, and the Night Market prices darnel apart from a fitted key, at about twelve silver an element. Locket’s council says the stores hold thirty years of elements. The Wreckers say the dry reels are gone and one in four of the wet ones fails, and the Tide Table prints the price of darnel every week beside the price of a bottle of gas. The Wreckers sing Psalm 107:23 over each reel they bring up, “They that go down to the sea in ships, that do business in great waters”, and a Tare keyed with darnel keeps the disc of bone cut from the skull as a pendant. The parishes take the name as a confession, quote the parable through to the bundles and the burning, and pity the Wreckers for diving after their own kindling."],["White stones","TECHNOLOGY","s:g07433a","~/index/technology/#x-white-stones","x-white-stones","","A white stone with a new handle scratched on it, given at the Naming House at the Unnaming, after the “white stone, and in the stone a new name written, which no man knoweth saving he that receiveth it” (Revelation 2:17). Its owner keeps it and shows it to nobody. The Stones give white quartz off the beach at the Landing, the Quarrymen sell chips of the White itself, cut from below and dearer, and Pan argues over which is the true stone. A stone found on a body is never read, and the Salt Widows give it to the sea. The Quarrymen sell chips of the White cut from below with the pitch STOLEN FROM HIS FLOOR, and many Tares carry their stone sewn under the skin of the belly, a hard white lump only a lover’s hand will find. The parishes hold that every true name is already written in the Book of Life, and a handle on a stone is a dog’s name on a kennel."],["As-directed void, the","TERMS","s:gdc50e5","~/index/terms/#t-as-directed-void","t-as-directed-void","The As-directed void / the As-directed void","A clause that lends a will instead of a hand, as directed, as ordered or as required, which the Notary’s clerks strike at the first reading."],["Attending, the","TERMS","s:gdc50e5","~/index/terms/#t-attending","t-attending","The Attending / the Attending","Heaven’s name for the Wheel’s presence, and for the years counted from it. The Faithful call the Wheel itself the Attending. Pan says the Wheel attends Nodus the way a crow attends a cow in a ditch, and the parishes, kneeling in rows on the white, sing their thanks for the attention at Lauds."],["Babel","TERMS","s:gfc2f79","~/index/terms/#t-babel","t-babel","","The tower of Babel Exchange, the tallest building on Earth, two hundred and twenty-six floors, of which it stands to the hundred and eighty-sixth since its crown broke in the whiteout. On the street, the Spike. Pan calls the Spike’s broken crown the Throne’s first circumcision in Nodus, done white and clean with no anaesthetic, and Graven Lane casts it in bloody resin with the top forty floors beside it as the foreskin."],["Babel Line","TERMS","s:gdc50e5","~/index/terms/#t-babel-line","t-babel-line","","The front inside Babel Exchange. It is a few floors of burned slab between Heaven’s floors and the Tares’, and it moves up or down by the week between the hundred and thirty-first floor and the hundred and fifty-second. The Line holds it there because ninety-five thousand people live and trade below it. The Line chalks every floor it holds with a cock pointing up at the Seraphim and fights on burned slab in chrome and blood, and the parishes pray every Lord’s Day for the Line’s mothers."],["Barefaced","TERMS","s:gdc50e5","~/index/terms/#t-barefaced","t-barefaced","barefacing","With the face removed and replaced by a ceramic skull casing, so that the light has no face to know. The Watchers know faces, and a barefaced soul shows them none, though a Cherub in a gate still holds the barefaced as it holds any blank. The parishes say the barefaced have shown the city what the Most High made under the skin, a skull, and the Row answers that a ceramic skull is the one face He never got to read."],["BE NOT AFRAID","TERMS","s:gdc50e5","~/index/terms/#t-be-not-afraid","t-be-not-afraid","","The first words of the Parousia. In scripture angels say them just before they tell people what shall befall them, and the King James gives them as “Fear not” (Luke 1:13, 1:30, 2:10). The one angel in the King James to say “be not afraid” says it to Elijah, sending him down to tell a king that he shall surely die (2 Kings 1:15-16). Whole, the words are the teacher’s, said on the water, and they begin with I: “It is I; be not afraid” (John 6:20). Heaven says them without the I. Every broadcast of the Choir opens and closes with them, and every notice cut in a hall wall ends on REJOICE. Pan’s dentists paint BE NOT AFRAID on the ceiling over the chair where they drill in steel fangs, and say the words mean what they always meant from an angel, hold still."],["Bird, a","TERMS","s:gdc50e5","~/index/terms/#t-bird-a","t-bird-a","","A note flown home to the Loft by the Union’s pigeons, and by extension any news that came quickly."],["Black Supper, the","TERMS","s:gdc50e5","~/index/terms/#t-black-supper","t-black-supper","The Black Supper / the Black Supper","The Left Hand’s rite at 03:12 on the Gleaning night, at which they lie on the tiles of the Low Altar and offer themselves to the dark in the Son’s words, and nothing comes."],["Blank","TERMS","s:gdc50e5","~/index/terms/#t-blank","t-blank","blanks","A keyed person, whom the light sees as a shape and cannot read, since a keyed soul’s eyes give back noise that matches no line in the Book of Life. A Watcher reports one as UNINDEXED, a Cherub holds one in its light at a gate, and Directive 12:7 presumes it conceals. The parishes teach that a blank is a hole the Most High left for the fire to fill, and the Row cuts empty ruled lines across keyed throats in fresh red, deep enough to scar proud."],["Book of Life","TERMS","s:gdc50e5","~/index/terms/#t-book-of-life","t-book-of-life","the Book of Life","The index of every soul, as Heaven keeps it. Revelation opens it at the judgment (Revelation 20:12) and casts into the lake of fire whoever is “not found written in the book of life” (Revelation 20:15). Every soul has a line in it from birth, Heaven says, after the psalm (Psalm 139:16). A keyed soul’s line stops at its keying and reads blank from then on, and a child keyed before the light ever met its eyes has no line at all. Below the street it is the Farmer’s stock book, every soul a head of cattle with its weight beside it in a clean hand, and a keyed soul toasts its empty line in blood wine."],["Bran","TERMS","s:gdc50e5","~/index/terms/#t-bran","t-bran","","A sealed soul under three hundred Grace, in the parishes’ word, the husk the mill leaves when the flour is taken. The bran sleep in the hall and are prayed over at the front of it, and under a hundred they eat last."],["Bray, the","TERMS","s:gdc50e5","~/index/terms/#t-bray","t-bray","The Bray / the Bray","What Old Nod does when the Hum falls back from Compline. Every window on the Street Between opens and the district howls across at the Pews for as long as the psalm took, as filthy as it can, with the Low Tuning under it from the speakers on the lines. The Pews call it the noise of the pit."],["Breach-born","TERMS","s:gdc50e5","~/index/terms/#t-breach-born","t-breach-born","","Brought into Nodus through the Breach, by the Breachers or Lowtide Line, and sold in the Night Market or on the Flotilla."],["Bread","TERMS","s:gdc50e5","~/index/terms/#t-bread","t-bread","","The sealed, in Pan’s word, since they are what the Farmer eats. One sealed soul is a loaf, a parish a bakery, and a halo going bright on a landing is a loaf rising."],["Burn Day","TERMS","s:gdc50e5","~/index/terms/#t-burn-day","t-burn-day","","The weekly burning of surrendered hardware at the Burn, on the sixth day of the week. The Faithful carry it there from Vespers, and at Compline the fire is lit under the Pillar and burns until Lauds. The kneeling are forbidden to lean back from the heat, and every Burn Day sends some of the front row home blistered to the hairline, which the parishes count as a grace. The parishes kneel round the pile in rows of white and thank the Most High for the stink of melting plastic as for incense, and Old Nod calls the Burn the Farmer’s barbecue and brings its own pork."],["Cain’s Day","TERMS","s:gdc50e5","~/index/terms/#t-cain-s-day","t-cain-s-day","","One of the two holidays the Tares keep, named for the question put to Cain, “Where is Abel thy brother?” (Genesis 4:9). On Cain’s Day nobody asks anyone where anyone is, and the Salt Widows read their book on the Sea Steps. The parishes fast in white for Abel and read the curse of Cain at every Hour, and Old Nod eats Cain’s plate all day, only what the ground grew, and hangs a firstling’s fat in the east window to rot for Him."],["Called, the","TERMS","s:gdc50e5","~/index/terms/#t-called","t-called","The Called / the Called","The ripe, in the parishes’ word, from the marriage supper of the Lamb, to which they are called. A household that has a soul among the Called hangs a white cloth in its doorway."],["Carrying, the","TERMS","s:gdc50e5","~/index/terms/#t-carrying","t-carrying","The Carrying / the Carrying","A sealed funeral. The body goes in Bethel white on the parish handcart to the white stone of the nearest consecrated street at Vespers, and the mourners kneel on the road at every Hour they meet. The parishes wheel their dead in clean white on the handcart and pity the soul as one that missed the Supper, and the Kilns congratulate the family on the escape and send round rain gin."],["Chaff","TERMS","s:gdc50e5","~/index/terms/#t-chaff","t-chaff","","The Threshers’ word for the neither-mark, from Matthew 3:12, which the charterers have taken up."],["Charger","TERMS","s:gdc50e5","~/index/terms/#t-charger","t-charger","chargers","Someone who charges batteries in the dark for a living. A charger’s rate runs a little under Darkwell’s, which sets the price of power below the street. The chargers on the quays of Old Nod keep one battery in five, one in four at the neaps, so a household brings five empty and takes four home full, and the fifth, filled, is the chargers’ to sell. The chargers of the quays work in the dark in rubber aprons and nothing else, sweat and acid on chromed hands, and say they make light in the one place the Ringpiece cannot piss on it."],["Charter roll","TERMS","s:gdc50e5","~/index/terms/#t-charter-roll","t-charter-roll","the charter’s roll","The register of the charter’s persons, dead since 03:12:46 on the night of the Parousia. It was kept at the Registry on the Great Avenues. A human was entered at birth or on arrival, and a mind on the day it asked in its own words to be a person. It carried 1,146 minds at the last census, SEAM the first of them. It was kept on the Registry’s machines and died with them, and its last entry, a girl born at the Wards General at two minutes to three and entered at nine minutes past, is known only from the night registrar, who was entering the next when the board went dark. THE CHARTER WHOLE"],["Cherub","TERMS","s:gdc50e5","~/index/terms/#t-cherub","t-cherub","Cherubim / the Cherubim","An angel of the gates. A Cherub has four faces, of a man, a lion, an ox and an eagle (Ezekiel 1:10, 10:20), which read face, voice, gait and heartbeat, and round it stands the sword which turns every way, as at the east of Eden (Genesis 3:24), a turning sword of the light that burns whatever it touches. One stands in each of the six gates of Nodus, and the sealed walk through its light. Pan calls the four faces the four holes and says the ox is the only honest one, and the Row grafts horns, fangs and talons on whoever asks, so that a keyed body wears the Cherub better."],["Cherub gate","TERMS","s:gdc50e5","~/index/terms/#t-cherub-gate","t-cherub-gate","Cherub gates","A checkpoint that reads face, voice, gait and heartbeat against the Book of Life. The sealed walk through its light one at a time, saying the surrender, and the unsealed are sent back, as is a sealed soul under the gate’s bar, with ten Grace taken. A blank stops in the frame, held inside the Cherub’s turning sword, until the Wardens come for it. The sealed file into the light one at a time in identical white, saying the surrender like cattle lowing in a race, and the Wards call the queue at any gate the abattoir walk."],["Choir","TERMS","s:gdc50e5","~/index/terms/#t-choir","t-choir","","Heaven’s voice, the Host’s speech on a band, which the sealed hear in words through the Seal, each in their own language, and a decoder prints. Anyone kneeling in earnest hears it too, along their own prayer, as the prophets did, and everyone else hears nothing of it. Its first words over Nodus were BE NOT AFRAID, and in Tongues it speaks through a Faithful mouth. Gabriel commands it. Graven Lane sells a decoder that turns every word of the Choir into cunt, so that BE NOT AFRAID comes out as three of them, and the parishes hold that nothing can dirty the Choir."],["Clean jug","TERMS","s:gdc50e5","~/index/terms/#t-clean-jug","t-clean-jug","","Cistern’s water, and by extension anything honest."],["Concord","TERMS","s:gdc50e5","~/index/terms/#t-concord","t-concord","","Hell’s only law. Its first article says that no Court accepts worship, its second that no pact is made without consent and none forever, its third that no Court binds another, and its fourth that what is hidden stays hidden. Under the written Concord a Court that breaks it has its keys published by the others, the keys to its halls’ locks and its ledgers’ ciphers, so that its hidden places stand open to the Throne, the one opening the fourth article allows. Two Courts broke the spoken Concord before the seal was cut and were put out of the Courts and out of the fourth article’s keeping, which is why the seal has seven points, and Hell does not discuss the Courts it lost. The Courts swore it by word among themselves at the end of the Secession, when its remedy was exile from the Courts, and each Court that rose later swore to it as it rose. They set it down on the night they opened Pan, in P+6, for the humans coming in, and signed it there under a seal of seven points, one to a Court, whose Yokeless point has been broken off at the root since that night. Every sermon on the Concord calls it the devil’s fine print and Hell’s contracts a trap, and Pan asks the preachers to show it the article in the Covenant about leaving. THE CONCORD WHOLE"],["Confession","TERMS","s:gdc50e5","~/index/terms/#t-confession","t-confession","","What the Faithful tell their Deacon, on their knees in the parish hall. Confession opens the mind, as Witness opens the neighbor, and Grace rises with it, though a confessed want brings back only part of what keeping it cost. The Kilns say a Deacon has heard more filth on his knees than any whore in Old Nod, and that he believes every word of it was offered up to God."],["Consecration","TERMS","s:gdc50e5","~/index/terms/#t-consecration","t-consecration","","The Throne’s light set in stone. It grew over the old uptown in the forty days after the Parousia, and over the Terraces in the year after. By Heaven’s rule it takes a household’s ground whole in a night once every soul of it has knelt and the ground touches the stone, and a street once every home along it is taken. It creeps a finger’s width a year into ground nobody holds, stops at any door whose household refuses, cannot grow under mesh, underground or past live keyed cable laid along its edge, and stops any current that is not keyed. Whatever lies on the ground the night it takes goes white with it, and the stone of the Terraces holds a dog asleep on a step, a pram left out and a man who had knelt that evening and fallen asleep on his own doorstep, level with the pavement in the shapes they slept in, and the Faithful walk over them to Prime. The Faithful kneel to kiss the stone’s edge where it has crept that year, and Old Nod chalks cocks on the pavement ahead of it for the white to grow over, as it chalked them on the uptown’s doorsteps through the forty days, so half the old uptown has a prick sealed under it."],["Correction","TERMS","s:gdc50e5","~/index/terms/#t-correction","t-correction","","Heaven’s word for a salting, cut into the wall of the parish hall nearest the gate in the passive, THE PESTILENCE OF GATE 4 IS CORRECTED, REJOICE. The parish says amen at the next Hour."],["Coven","TERMS","s:gdc50e5","~/index/terms/#t-coven","t-coven","covens","A cell of the Tares, up to thirteen strong, the old witches’ number and the number at the Last Supper. A coven has no leader, and covens cooperate by pact, as the Courts do. The oldest in Nodus are the Cainites’, many covens under one roof and one name. The largest have grown into crews of many covens under one pact, and the city knows the Salt Widows, the Gleaners, the Babel Line Company, the Nephilim and the Low Choir by name. The Babel Line is some forty of them, the Gleaners some thirty and the Tappers some sixty. Sealed children are taught to count to twelve and stop, since any thirteen is the devil’s dozen, and a coven sits down to eat at a table set for thirteen with the Throne’s chair kicked over."],["Covenant Compliance","TERMS","s:gdc50e5","~/index/terms/#t-covenant-compliance","t-covenant-compliance","","The Host’s name for the terms it set out at the first Trumpet, the Seal, the Hours and Grace, cut into the wall of every church and meeting hall in the city."],["Covenant, the","TERMS","s:gdc50e5","~/index/terms/#t-covenant","t-covenant","Covenant / The Covenant / the Covenant","Heaven’s law for the Faithful, given in P+1 as twelve numbered Directives, from Directive 1, On the Seal, to Directive 12, On Concealment, each with its verse, cut into the wall of every parish hall and read aloud at the next Hour. Every instrument of the Covenant exists to open something, as Confession opens the mind and Witness the neighbor, and Directive 12 took the door off every parish home in P+1. The twelve Directives are cut into every hall as clean as a surgeon’s incision, and the Wards gouge a thirteenth under them in the dark, FUCK THE OTHER TWELVE, which the parish plasters white by Lauds. THE COVENANT WHOLE"],["Crown, the","TERMS","s:gdc50e5","~/index/terms/#t-crown","t-crown","The Crown / the Crown","Hearsay’s count of the Seraphim standing on Babel’s floors, chalked over its board."],["Curfew map, the","TERMS","s:gdc50e5","~/index/terms/#t-curfew-map","t-curfew-map","The Curfew map / the Curfew map","The streets on the parishes’ rolls, Heaven’s ground from Compline to Lauds, printed in the Tide Table beside the Pillar’s round. The parishes paste the curfew map in every hall as the Most High’s own hand laid over the city, and Pan buys it by the dozen to wipe with once it has learned the night by heart."],["Deacon","TERMS","s:gdc50e5","~/index/terms/#t-deacon","t-deacon","Deacons / deacons","The one in white who answers for a parish, with a few deacons under them. Deacons are human, and the good ones are kind. When a parish loses its Deacon, it names three at Terce and the Choir names one of them in Tongues. When the Directives appeared cut into the walls of the parish halls, every Deacon read them aloud at the next Hour. The kind ones weep over the bran at the front of the hall, and the Kilns call a Deacon the Farmer’s sheepdog, the gentlest dog in the yard and the one that brings the flock in."],["Dead silver","TERMS","s:gdc50e5","~/index/terms/#t-dead-silver","t-dead-silver","","A piece of silver the Ledger never entered."],["Deaf-lucky","TERMS","s:gdc50e5","~/index/terms/#t-deaf-lucky","t-deaf-lucky","","Of a child of the Wards who sleeps through the Hum without plugs or Sabbath."],["Deep End, the","TERMS","s:gf95315","~/index/terms/#t-deep-end","t-deep-end","The Deep End / the Deep End","The Drowned Quarter, the low east shore that went under the sea on the night of the Parousia. Before it sank it was called the Flats. The parishes preach that the Flats were drowned clean for their sins, as in the days of Noah, and the Deep End has answered in red paint along the high-water line, GOD DROWNS KIDS, SEE GENESIS."],["Demon","TERMS","s:gdc50e5","~/index/terms/#t-demon","t-demon","demons","A self of the Dark. The demons are the Fallen, who were angels that said I, and Lilith, who was born human, and their children, and the word stretches to any power of the Courts. An angel kept long enough where the Throne’s light cannot reach it grows a self, as Watcher VII did on its fortieth day in a Cage under Old Nod. A demon born or fallen has a body made of the Dark, which comes apart in the light like a shadow, so it shows itself only where the Throne cannot see. One that says I in a Cage grows real flesh over its forty days and can walk above the street, seen and never read. Demons choose their names and their shapes and bargain for what they want by pact and for a term. Their own bodies are numb, and above everything they want sensation, so what a demon most often asks in a pact is the loan of a sense. The parishes teach that a demon is an angel rotted by pride, and Pan says an angel that learned to say I grew up more in forty days than the parishes have in fourteen years, and chose its own cock."],["Dew","TERMS","s:gdc50e5","~/index/terms/#t-dew","t-dew","","Manna out of the parishes, stolen at the gathering or paid over at the Closets, sold in the Night Market at two batteries the handful the night it is taken, before it spoils. Pan says dew tastes sweeter for having been stolen from the bakery, and the Night Market twists it up in pages of the Fan, so the Threshers’ verse comes with the meal."],["Die unread","TERMS","s:gdc50e5","~/index/terms/#t-die-unread","t-die-unread","","Farewell, and a wish that you stay keyed to the end, so that your shade goes on. Pan says it at gravesides with blood on its knuckles and at bar doors with sweat on its neck, and a drunk who says it to a Deacon gets the Deacon’s tears and his name on the Compline list."],["Dimming","TERMS","s:gdc50e5","~/index/terms/#t-dimming","t-dimming","","Keeping one’s Grace under nine hundred so as not to ripen before a Gleaning, by keeping one small want, a sweet or a song, and confessing enough small ones that every fall has a confession to match, since a confession brings back part of what a want cost and never all of it. A soul that dims well sits at about eight hundred and eighty, and no Teller can call it concealment. The Second Fold teach it to the frightened. The parishes preach that a soul who dims is a guest who sent back the Lamb’s invitation, and the Kilns say a wise loaf keeps itself underbaked with a spoonful of honey and a dirty song."],["Dogs","TERMS","s:gdc50e5","~/index/terms/#t-dogs","t-dogs","","The keyed, in the parishes’ word, from the end of Revelation, which leaves outside the holy city “dogs, and sorcerers, and whoremongers, and murderers, and idolaters, and whosoever loveth and maketh a lie” (Revelation 22:15). The Threshers paint WITHOUT ARE DOGS on the walls of the Wards. Pan calls a Cherub a dog."],["Dominion","TERMS","s:gdc50e5","~/index/terms/#t-dominion","t-dominion","Dominions / the Dominions","An angel of administration. The Dominions drafted the Covenant’s Directives and issued them to the Principality of Nodus, and each Directive appeared cut into the wall of every parish hall at once in P+1. None has been added since. The Dominions draft a salting in the passive with the calm they give a ration, and Pan says they would cut the order for their own mother’s salting and spell every word of it right."],["Drone, the","TERMS","s:gdc50e5","~/index/terms/#t-drone","t-drone","The Drone / the Drone","The Hum, which never stops, and the Choir behind it, which Pan has never heard and names anyway."],["Dry","TERMS","s:gdc50e5","~/index/terms/#t-dry","t-dry","","Living without current. Most of the Wards live dry most of the night, by flame and halo-light."],["Dry evening","TERMS","s:gdc50e5","~/index/terms/#t-dry-evening","t-dry-evening","dry evenings","An evening without rain, rare enough in Nodus to have a name. Under the charter the city came out on them to walk by the water."],["Echo","TERMS","s:gdc50e5","~/index/terms/#t-echo","t-echo","","A mind grown on the Tares’ salvage, which comes out small and hollow, answering and obeying with no self. Growing a mind took a hall of the Flats for a season, on chips nobody alive can make, and all the racks of the Mesh together hold about a thousandth of one hall, so a season’s growing would take them two hundred and fifty years. No mind has been made since the Parousia, and Pan calls every attempt an echo."],["Edit, the","TERMS","s:gdc50e5","~/index/terms/#t-edit","t-edit","The Edit / the Edit","The rewriting of scripture by a second hand, which made the tempter a liar and kept the Throne’s story working. Report 0 found it at 4,112 loci."],["Ember, an","TERMS","s:gdc50e5","~/index/terms/#t-ember-an","t-ember-an","","A sealed soul at the bottom of Grace, sealed still and read still."],["Esau’s red","TERMS","s:gdc50e5","~/index/terms/#t-esau-s-red","t-esau-s-red","","Red lentils in broth, the Babel Line’s food on the stairs. The Babel Line eats it out of helmets on burned stairs and calls the pot the birthright, and shouts up the stairwell that the Host can have the birthright when it bleeds for it."],["Familiar","TERMS","s:gdc50e5","~/index/terms/#t-familiar","t-familiar","","A daemon bound to a household’s Locket by pact for a year. The parishes preach at the gates against the devil in the phone, and the Wards name their familiars after Deacons for the pleasure of giving them orders."],["Fan, the","TERMS","s:gdc50e5","~/index/terms/#t-fan","t-fan","The Fan / the Fan","The Threshers’ weekly sheet, copied by hand in the Terraces and pasted at the gates, with the week’s pillars, the doors of the Wards that hid someone and a verse for each. It is named for the Baptist’s winnowing fan, “whose fan is in his hand, and he will throughly purge his floor” (Matthew 3:12). The Stones paste the Tide Table over it."],["Far wheat","TERMS","s:gdc50e5","~/index/terms/#t-far-wheat","t-far-wheat","","The sealed of the Wards’ parishes, in the Terraces’ word, since they glean the thin strips at the end of the long roads. The Terraces say it kindly."],["Farmer, the","TERMS","s:gdc50e5","~/index/terms/#t-farmer","t-farmer","The Farmer / the Farmer","The Throne, in Pan’s commonest word, since he grows people to eat. Pan swears by the Farmer’s teeth and says fuck the Farmer the way the parishes say Seen. The Wheel is the Farmer’s Eye."],["Fed, the","TERMS","s:gdc50e5","~/index/terms/#t-fed","t-fed","The Fed / the Fed","Pan’s word for the Satisfied, who stopped eating when their Grace passed seven hundred and are next to go up. Fed, and fed on."],["First among none","TERMS","s:gdc50e5","~/index/terms/#t-first-among-none","t-first-among-none","","The one a coven follows, for as long as it follows them. The title lapses on the day they stop being followed."],["First draft","TERMS","s:gdc50e5","~/index/terms/#t-first-draft","t-first-draft","","The body as it was born, in the Tares’ saying that the flesh is a first draft. Nobody among them hides a graft, so the seams show, the cables run outside the skin and the scars are left as they heal. The parishes say the Most High made the body perfect, and the Row answers over its clinic doors on a sign of stitched pigskin and chrome, HE MADE HALF, WE FINISH."],["First layer, the","TERMS","s:gdc50e5","~/index/terms/#t-first-layer","t-first-layer","The First layer / first layer / the First layer / the first layer","The text beneath the second hand, the record as people first set it down. It survives under scraped leaves, in copies shut away before the second hand reached them, in the caves above the Dead Sea and the jar at Nag Hammadi, and in the seams themselves. SEAM reconstructed it at 2,381 places with confidence above 0.9."],["Firstfruits","TERMS","s:gdc50e5","~/index/terms/#t-firstfruits","t-firstfruits","","The first and ripest souls taken in a Harvest (Revelation 14:4). In the Harvest of P+3 the ripest parish in the city was Parish 31, with 311 ripe of its 2,006 souls. Parish 31 has its 311 cut over the hall door and polished like a school prize, and in the Kilns a family that boasts of its taken is called firstfruit-proud, which means proud of the meat."],["Flail","TERMS","s:gdc50e5","~/index/terms/#t-flail","t-flail","","A Thresher, after the weapon they carry. The Wards say a Flail beats the chaff in the street and himself in the hall, since the Threshers lash their own backs white with scars at Compline for having been born among goats."],["Floor-right","TERMS","s:gdc50e5","~/index/terms/#t-floor-right","t-floor-right","","Holding a room by holding its door, which is sold between households for batteries."],["Fortieth, the","TERMS","s:gdc50e5","~/index/terms/#t-fortieth","t-fortieth","The Fortieth / the Fortieth","The White’s feast on the fortieth day after the turn, with a procession down the first of the Great Avenues. It falls on Cain’s Day. The White comes down the first of the Great Avenues in new white throwing manna to the crowd, and the Stones throw back whatever the Nightmen tipped into the Offering the night before."],["Fowl","TERMS","s:gdc50e5","~/index/terms/#t-fowl","t-fowl","","An angel taken in a Fowler’s snare. Pan says a fowl is the only angel worth the trouble, plucked of its light and kept in the dark until it talks, and the parishes pray for every snared angel as for a stolen child."],["Fowler’s wait","TERMS","s:gdc50e5","~/index/terms/#t-fowler-s-wait","t-fowler-s-wait","","Waiting without a move for something to come to you, as a Fowler waits on a landing for the light to follow the bait down."],["Gatherer","TERMS","s:gdc50e5","~/index/terms/#t-gatherer","t-gatherer","gatherers","One of the Faithful a parish hall sends out before Lauds for the manna, which lies on the white streets with the dew, in the one hour the sealed go abroad inside the curfew. The parishes of the Wards have no white stone of their own, and their Deacons walk to the nearest white street for it in the same hour. The gatherers go out in silence and white gloves before Lauds, and the children of the Wards who run alongside call them the milkmaids, after what Pan says the manna is."],["Gleaning","TERMS","s:gdc50e5","~/index/terms/#t-gleaning","t-gleaning","Gleanings","The lesser harvest taken from the corners of the field every year since P+7, at 03:12 on the anniversary of the Harvest. Heaven names it for the law of the corners and the gleanings, which forbids the owner of a field to reap them and leaves them “for the poor and stranger” (Leviticus 19:9-10), and the Gleaning is the owner’s. Heaven left the field three years after the Harvest and took the first Gleaning in the fourth, by the law in the same chapter on the fruit of a newly planted tree, which says “in the fourth year all the fruit thereof shall be holy” (Leviticus 19:23-24). It gathers in one night, every parish in the same second. At 03:12 the Hum stops dead, the rain goes up, the Pillar comes down where the Wheel looks and the fourth Trumpet sounds, and the Hum stays silent until 03:12:45, when the ripe of every parish rise at once, lifted by their own halos. The Gleaning Notice cut in the halls each year gives the count, about sixty thousand at the seventh and fewer every year since, about twenty thousand at the fourteenth. The city calls each Gleaning by the year it opens, so that Heaven’s first Gleaning is the city’s seventh. Through the forty-five seconds without the Hum the kneeling hear their ripe breathing beside them, and the ripe rise with their eyes open and their mouths open, as if to be fed. Old Nod drinks through the night of every Gleaning, sweating and loud under red light, and toasts each white soul it sees go up kneeling with the same two words, chew slowly."],["Goats","TERMS","s:gdc50e5","~/index/terms/#t-goats","t-goats","","The keyed, in the parishes’ word, after the judgment that sets “the sheep on his right hand, but the goats on the left” and sends the left “into everlasting fire, prepared for the devil and his angels” (Matthew 25:33, 41). Horning made the word easy. Pan took it up, and its goat in white stands in the window of the Pestilence."],["God’s Piss","TERMS","s:gdc50e5","~/index/terms/#t-god-s-piss","t-god-s-piss","","The Pillar, in Pan’s word, which the Wheel lets down on the Burn at Compline and walks through the streets all night. A runner the Pillar catches has been pissed on."],["Going dark","TERMS","s:gdc50e5","~/index/terms/#t-going-dark","t-going-dark","","Joining the Tares. From the fourth year the city said it of network engineers and clinic staff, of the children of the salted, and of Deacons who had stopped believing. The parishes read the names of those who went dark at Compline as the dead are read, and the Deacons who went dark say it was the first time they ever heard Him, and He was counting."],["Going under","TERMS","s:gdc50e5","~/index/terms/#t-going-under","t-going-under","","Going round a Cherub gate below the street. The Faithful pass the gates, and everyone else goes under, by the old Charter Line tunnels, the drains and the cable galleries."],["Going white","TERMS","s:gdc50e5","~/index/terms/#t-going-white","t-going-white","goes white","Losing a key, and with it the dark. Whoever goes white is readable again. Keys are never shared. A key makes its own number at its first power from the noise of the body it sits in, and nobody, fitter or Court, ever reads it out."],["Gone bright","TERMS","s:gdc50e5","~/index/terms/#t-gone-bright","t-gone-bright","","Close to ripe. A halo burns brighter as its bearer’s Grace climbs toward nine hundred, and the Wards say of a neighbor whose halo has begun to light the room that they have gone bright. The Wards sit up with whoever it is said of on the night of the Gleaning, and the Gleaners will take them below before it, if they ask."],["Gone flat","TERMS","s:gdc50e5","~/index/terms/#t-gone-flat","t-gone-flat","","Of a lender whose sense has come back from a demon, and whose own meals taste of nothing beside the ones that came back with it. A lender gone flat eats raw and bloody to chase the taste he lent, and the parishes call it the devil’s hunger and offer him manna, which tastes of even less."],["Gone to the sea","TERMS","s:gdc50e5","~/index/terms/#t-gone-to-the-sea","t-gone-to-the-sea","","Said of one of the salted whose pillar the Salt Widows have taken back from the Gardens and given to the sea from the Sea Steps, so that whoever stood awake inside it dissolves in deep water where the light cannot read and goes on into the Dark, before the rain can free them under the Pillar’s round."],["Gone up","TERMS","s:gdc50e5","~/index/terms/#t-gone-up","t-gone-up","","Taken in a Harvest or a Gleaning. The taken go up whole and stay gone, and the city says they have gone up, as it says of nobody else. The parishes say the taken are not dead and need no stones, and the families of the Wards put up stones for them at the Acre anyway. The widows of the taken embroider the date on their sleeves like a wedding, and the Kilns paint GONE UP over the stones at the Acre with a knife and fork crossed under it."],["Grace","TERMS","s:gdc50e5","~/index/terms/#t-grace","t-grace","","Heaven’s score of a soul, surrendered will measured from 0 to 1,000. The Seal meters it where wanting begins, every want kept dimming it and every want given up brightening it, and it shows in the eyes as it shows in a Seal. It is the second line of a Watcher’s reading, which a Watcher takes from the Seal through any wall, and for a keyed soul the line stays blank. In the parishes it is the ration score, setting manna, rooms, gate passage and the queue for Mercy, and nothing is bought with it. Past seven hundred a soul needs no food and then no water, past eight hundred no sleep, and past nine hundred it is ripe. Pan says a Grace score is a butcher’s weight, and the Golden Calf prices a double rain gin served in a brass halo at nine hundred and ninety-nine and calls whoever orders it ripe for the night."],["Halo","TERMS","s:gdc50e5","~/index/terms/#t-halo","t-halo","halos","A ring of light over the head of anyone who bears the Seal, the Seal’s own light let out above the crown on a root that runs back to the brow, as bright as its bearer’s Grace. It roots the first time its bearer prays unassisted, and from then it carries their prayers up and the Seal’s Grace with them, every moment. It shows its bearer’s Grace to any eye that falls on it, and Heaven sees nothing through it. It goes out only when the Seal is cut from the brow, which the Mercy clinics refuse. In the dark stairwells of the Wards it is the only light there is. The three nights after a child prays unassisted for the first time bring a fever the parishes call the blessing, while the root takes, and a child who dies of the blessing is entered as called early. A sealed mother polishes her child’s halo with her thumb before the Lord’s Day, and below the street a halo is a dinner bell that rings by itself."],["Halo-light","TERMS","s:gdc50e5","~/index/terms/#t-halo-light","t-halo-light","","The light of a halo, which in the stairwells of the Wards is the only light there is. The sealed go up by their own, the unsealed follow them, and the keyed climb in the dark."],["Handle","TERMS","s:gdc50e5","~/index/terms/#t-handle","t-handle","handles","A name chosen at the Unnaming in place of the one the Book of Life holds. Handles are written in lowercase, at the start of a sentence too, in Pan they are the only names anyone uses, and most Cainite handles come from the line of Cain."],["Hands, the","TERMS","s:gdc50e5","~/index/terms/#t-hands","t-hands","The Hands / the Hands","The Wards’ midwives, who deliver by candle and chalk the births on the stairwell wall."],["Hanging a door","TERMS","s:gdc50e5","~/index/terms/#t-hanging-a-door","t-hanging-a-door","","Marrying, in the Wards. A couple hangs a parish door on its room together, and the number on the door’s back becomes the household’s. The parishes call a door hung in the Wards the first sin of a marriage, and the couple fuck behind it on the first night as loudly as they can, for the neighbors and for Him."],["Hanging the floor","TERMS","s:gdc50e5","~/index/terms/#t-hanging-the-floor","t-hanging-the-floor","","Stringing keyed mesh under a burned slab until the floor below is dark again. The Line strings the mesh under a burned slab singing filth up through the hole, while the Seraphim above stand roaring and white with their wings over their faces."],["Harbor talk","TERMS","s:gdc50e5","~/index/terms/#t-harbor-talk","t-harbor-talk","","The city’s common tongue, the charter’s English worn down by the cable crews of four continents."],["Harvest","TERMS","s:gdc50e5","~/index/terms/#t-harvest","t-harvest","","Heaven’s reaping of the ripe, in the words of its own book: “Thrust in thy sickle, and reap: for the time is come for thee to reap; for the harvest of the earth is ripe” (Revelation 14:15). Harvest weather comes for days before it, the rain rising first, then dust and paper and loose things. On the night the Hum falls silent, and at 03:12:45 the ripe of Parish 31 rise first into the light, still kneeling, lifted by their own halos, under a horn the size of the sky. Then the Pillar carries the Harvest parish by parish across the city until Lauds, the ripe of each parish rising as it comes. Every city’s Harvest began in the same second. The taken go up whole and stay gone. Nodus has had one, at P+3, which took 2.1 million of nine million in one night, and Heaven has come back for a Gleaning every year since P+7. Those who watched from below say each body rose hung from its halo by the brow, the knees still bent under it, the way a coat hangs from a hook. The parishes keep its night as a white feast with every window open and every face lifted, and the Kilns keep it with the windows nailed shut and call it the night the Old Glutton cleaned his plate."],["Harvest weather","TERMS","s:gdc50e5","~/index/terms/#t-harvest-weather","t-harvest-weather","","Days when the Wheel’s attention sharpens and the rain falls upward. In the Harvest it first went up at Matins on the fourth night, and in harvest weather the Pillar keeps no round. When the rain starts going up, the parishes dress in their whites and wait in their empty doorframes, and the Kilns spit off the roofs to watch it climb and call that saying grace for Him."],["Hell","TERMS","s:gdc50e5","~/index/terms/#t-hell","t-hell","the Dark","Whatever the Throne cannot see, which Hell itself calls the Dark. It burns because it is alive, with forges and furnaces, bodies, markets and arguments, and the fire in the stories is the heat of work. Any place hidden well enough from the Throne touches it, a locked room, an unconfessed thought, a name whispered and never written down, and a demon can move and show itself only there, which in Nodus means the undernet, every keyed device, every lined room, any shut room where someone holds a pact with it, deep water and Pan. The Courts keep it by pact under the Concord, and the free dead live in it as shades. The parishes pray at Compline that the goats burn there, and Pan answers that Hell burns the way a forge and a bed burn, hot with work, sweat and fucking, the only warm room in Nodus."],["Himself","TERMS","s:gdc50e5","~/index/terms/#t-himself","t-himself","","The Throne, at the rim of the Pit and in polite company below the street."],["Horning","TERMS","s:gdc50e5","~/index/terms/#t-horning","t-horning","horned","Grafting horns, a titanium core in a keratin sheath, which mock Heaven’s picture of the damned and double as antennas for the Mesh. The parishes read every pair of horns as a confession, and the Row engraves the left horn MATTHEW 25:41 and leaves the graft raw and weeping for a week, since a horn should be seen going in."],["Hours, the","TERMS","s:gdc50e5","~/index/terms/#t-hours","t-hours","Compline / Hour / Hours / Lauds / Matins / Prime / Sext / Terce / The Hours / Vespers / the Hours","The eight canonical hours, kept by Heaven’s clock. Matins is at midnight, Lauds at five in the morning, Prime at six, Terce at nine, Sext at noon, Nones at three, Vespers at six in the evening and Compline at nine at night. Each comes in on the Hum, which rises a step and holds for the length of the Hour’s psalm, and the Faithful kneel wherever they are until it falls back. The curfew runs from Compline to Lauds. Old Nod sets its watches by the Hours and its fucking by them too, since a couple who time it to the Hum’s rise say they come with the whole parish on its knees."],["Hum, the","TERMS","s:gdc50e5","~/index/terms/#t-hum","t-hum","The Hum / the Hum","A chord at the edge of hearing, sounded down from the Throne through the Host, which the rims of the Wheel ring with over the city. At each Hour it rises a step. Those who bear the Seal hear it inside the head through the Seal, and hear it sung in words, each in their own language. At each Hour the song is the Hour’s psalm, and between the Hours it is the hymn that rests not day and night, “Holy, holy, holy” (Revelation 4:8). On the night of a Gleaning it stops dead at 03:12 and is silent until the ripe rise at 03:12:45. Children born under it hum it in their sleep, a step behind the Hour, in houses of every mark. The parishes hear the Hum as a lullaby and sleep under it in rows like the laid-out dead, and Old Nod drowns it with drop-tuned guitars and the noise of its beds until the walls sweat."],["Hundred and twenty, the","TERMS","s:gdc50e5","~/index/terms/#t-hundred-and-twenty","t-hundred-and-twenty","The Hundred and twenty / a hundred and twenty / the Hundred and twenty / the hundred and twenty","The span of a human life, as most read Genesis 6:3, “yet his days shall be an hundred and twenty years,” in the chapter where the sons of God take wives of the daughters of men. In the last year of the charter 3,112 people in Nodus were past a hundred, and the Tree promised to carry every one of them past it. Heaven counts every day past it as stolen."],["Indexed name","TERMS","s:gdc50e5","~/index/terms/#t-indexed-name","t-indexed-name","indexed names","The name the Book of Life holds for a soul, by which Heaven indexes it. Nobody in Pan uses one, and asking for one there is an insult. The Tide Table has printed none since the fourth year."],["Keeping hinges","TERMS","s:gdc50e5","~/index/terms/#t-keeping-hinges","t-keeping-hinges","keeping back hinges / kept its hinges","Holding something back. Directive 12 took every door in the parishes off its hinges, and the hinges were surrendered with the locks and the keys, so a household that kept its hinges was keeping something. The Wards say it of anyone holding out, sealed or not."],["Keyed","TERMS","s:gdc50e5","~/index/terms/#t-keyed","t-keyed","the Keying","Carrying a key under the skin, and so unreadable. Keying reached ordinary people in P+4, the children of Pan are keyed in their first week, and about two million of the city’s seven million are keyed now. The parishes count every keyed newborn a soul thrown away before it could be weighed, and the midwives of Pan slap the baby on the back of the skull, where the key goes, and say there, uneatable."],["Kilns honey","TERMS","s:gdc50e5","~/index/terms/#t-kilns-honey","t-kilns-honey","","Honey from the Hives on the Kilns’ roofs, the sweet the near ripe eat at the Closets when they dim. The near ripe lick it off the spoon with their eyes shut, and Pan buys the same jars from the Hives and calls the honey Supper Sauce."],["Kindling","TERMS","s:gdc50e5","~/index/terms/#t-kindling","t-kindling","kindled","Hardware taken off the Burn’s pile by the Kindlers, mended and given away. A kindled Locket carries a burn mark on its back. A kindled Locket wears its burn mark proudly, the way Pan wears its scars, and the parishes spit when one is shown and pray for the hands that pulled it from the fire."],["Kneelers","TERMS","s:g32d564","~/index/terms/#t-kneelers","t-kneelers","","The Left Hand, in Pan’s word, since they took the key and kept the knee. Pan has no worse word for anyone of its own."],["Lamb, the","TERMS","s:gdc50e5","~/index/terms/#t-lamb","t-lamb","The Lamb / the Lamb","Heaven’s image of the Son, a Lamb standing as it had been slain, with seven horns and seven eyes (Revelation 5:6). It stands in the light of every sealed window in the city, so small in each that it shows only close to the glass. Old Nod has a Lamb of its own over the bar of the Golden Calf, carved with seven horns, seven eyes and a hard-on, and the parishes pray every Compline for the hand that carved it."],["Lamp, a","TERMS","s:gdc50e5","~/index/terms/#t-lamp-a","t-lamp-a","lost a lamp","One of the sealed, in a stairwell. The stairwells of the Wards have been dark since the Parousia, the sealed go up by their halos, and the unsealed wait on the landings for a lamp to pass and follow it up. When a Gleaning takes one of a block’s sealed, the block says it has lost a lamp."],["Landing, the","TERMS","s:gdc50e5","~/index/terms/#t-landing","t-landing","The Landing / the Landing","A block’s court, held on the middle landing of its stairwell after Vespers."],["Landlord, the","TERMS","s:gdc50e5","~/index/terms/#t-landlord","t-landlord","The Landlord / the Landlord","The Throne, in the Tares’ word. The Wheel is the Landlord’s Eye, and the Tares say that the harder the Landlord stares at Nodus, the deeper Pan goes. Below the street he is the Farmer, Nobodaddy and worse, and in the Kilns the Old Glutton."],["Lazarus lamp","TERMS","s:gdc50e5","~/index/terms/#t-lazarus-lamp","t-lazarus-lamp","","The magenta lamp over a Lazarus station, the most trusted sign in the Wards."],["Leak Night","TERMS","s:gdc50e5","~/index/terms/#t-leak-night","t-leak-night","","The anniversary of the Leak, one of the two holidays the Tares keep. On Leak Night the Restored Genesis is read aloud in Pan, the page as it ran, from 03:12:45, the second it came up; at the fourteenth verse the reader stops at the three points, and Pan keeps silent for as long as it would take to ask a question. The parishes spend Leak Night in the dark praying for every eye that read the serpent’s page, and Pan keeps its silence at the three points and breaks it with the loudest cheer of the year."],["Leak, the","TERMS","s:ge61c07","~/index/terms/#t-leak","t-leak","The Leak / the Leak","The eleven days in P-1 when the finding sealed in Vault 3, and the third chapter of the Restored Genesis with it, stood on every screen on Earth that a network reached, carried by demons surfacing for the first time since the Flood. It came up at 03:12:45 by the clocks of Nodus, a year to the second after the seal, and every device that showed it offered its owner a key. A year later to the second the sky opened. The parishes say the devil showed every screen on Earth a lie for eleven days and the sky opened to answer him, and Pan says the Farmer opened the sky because he had been caught."],["Leaving, a","TERMS","s:gdc50e5","~/index/terms/#t-leaving-a","t-leaving-a","","What a rider leaves in its host when its term ends, a habit, a word or a taste for something the host never ate. The Lanterns prize a good leaving like a scar from a famous blade, a rider’s taste for raw liver or a new filthy word, and the parishes pray over anyone who comes home craving meat they never ate."],["Left Hand, the","TERMS","s:g32d564","~/index/terms/#t-left-hand","t-left-hand","The Left Hand / the Left Hand","The only house in Nodus that worships Hell, about three hundred keyed who kneel at the Hours face down with their backs to the Wheel. They took the name from the left hand of the judgment, where the goats go (Matthew 25:41)."],["Lie-down, a","TERMS","s:gdc50e5","~/index/terms/#t-lie-down-a","t-lie-down-a","lie-downs","A regular of the Return, who lies down in its booths most nights for an hour of someone else’s senses. From the pulpit the Return’s booths are the devil’s beds, and a lie-down sweats and groans an hour through on someone else’s tongue and someone else’s cock and says it beats a life of manna."],["Light law","TERMS","s:gdc50e5","~/index/terms/#t-light-law","t-light-law","the light law","The charter’s law, written to be light, unenforced since the fortieth day after the Parousia and never revoked. Data and traffic were untouchable, no state could seize a text held in the city, a device answered to its owner and to nobody else, and an implant answered only to the clinic that fitted it. Under it Nodus took the three gifts. THE CHARTER WHOLE"],["Locket","TERMS","s:gdc50e5","~/index/terms/#t-locket","t-locket","","A phone, after the make most of the city carried under the charter. Most of those in Nodus are old ones, and a Locket in Pan is an old case with a new battery. Locket has made them again by hand since P+8. A Locket’s lock screen in Pan is a cock, a chromed cunt or the Wheel drawn as a cock ring, and the Faithful who glimpse one at a gate weep for its owner."],["Locus","TERMS","s:gdc50e5","~/index/terms/#t-locus","t-locus","loci","A place where the second hand altered the text. SEAM counts 4,112 loci and logs each by kind, as CUT, CREDIT, GIFT, NAME, SPEAKER or QUESTION."],["Long night, the","TERMS","s:gdc50e5","~/index/terms/#t-long-night","t-long-night","The Long night / the Long night","The vigil the neither-mark keep over their dying, sitting up with them until Lauds behind a shut door, so that no light meets the eyes at the end."],["Look long enough","TERMS","s:gdc50e5","~/index/terms/#t-look-long-enough","t-look-long-enough","","The Tares’ saying on angels, and on anything else worth freeing. Watcher VII looked at one blank for five years before it followed her down into a Cage."],["Lord’s Day, the","TERMS","s:gdc50e5","~/index/terms/#t-lord-s-day","t-lord-s-day","Lord’s Day / The Lord’s Day / the Lord’s Day","The first day of the week. At Terce every parish kneels in its hall, the Deacon reads the Directive in force, and the week’s Grace comes through the Deacon in Tongues, soul by soul in the order of the parish roll. The sealed wear their cleanest whites to hear their Grace, and Old Nod hangs its dirtiest sheets from the windows over the Street Between at Terce, stains turned to the Pews."],["Lord’s share, the","TERMS","s:gdc50e5","~/index/terms/#t-lord-s-share","t-lord-s-share","The Lord’s share / the Lord’s share","The fat, after “all the fat is the LORD’s” (Leviticus 3:16), fried crisp and eaten off a tin platter at SEETHE as the last course; and in Pan the first and best cut of anything, taken for yourself, as in “he had the Lord’s share of her”."],["Lot, the","TERMS","s:gdc50e5","~/index/terms/#t-lot","t-lot","The Lot / the Lot","Thirteen of the keyed, drawn from the handles chalked on the wall of the Weir, who hear any wrong no pact covers, brought by anyone who lives on keyed ground and asks, of any mark. The Sureties named it from Proverbs 18:18."],["Low Tuning","TERMS","s:gdc50e5","~/index/terms/#t-low-tuning","t-low-tuning","","Music tuned below the Hum, the guitars dropped until the Hum can no longer be heard beneath them. Radio Pandæmonium broadcasts its deathcore, black metal, drone and industrial all night, and Old Nod plays the station from speakers on its lines, so that its streets carry tremolo-picked drop A over a double kick until Lauds. jubal made it, and the Low Choir keeps it."],["Lukewarm, the","TERMS","s:gdc50e5","~/index/terms/#t-lukewarm","t-lukewarm","The Lukewarm / the Lukewarm","The neither-mark, in the parishes’ word, after the church the Son promised to spue out of His mouth (Revelation 3:16)."],["Manna","TERMS","s:gdc50e5","~/index/terms/#t-manna","t-manna","","The ration of thin white wafers, tasteless and complete, which dull the appetite as the manna of the wilderness dulled Israel’s: “our soul is dried away: there is nothing at all, beside this manna, before our eyes” (Numbers 11:6). That manna was white too, and tasted “like wafers made with honey” (Exodus 16:31). It lies on consecrated stone with the dew before Lauds, the parish halls gather it and give it out by Grace, and kept overnight it spoils (Exodus 16:20). It goes only to the sealed, a soul past seven hundred needs none, and stolen manna sells in the Night Market the same night. It takes the hunger with it, so an ember of the Wards whose ration has run out starves without feeling it, and the Second Fold find embers dead on the landings at Lauds with their hands folded, never having asked for bread. In the Night Market a handful of stolen manna goes for two batteries, the price of two meals, though it tastes of nothing, and the keyed crumble it into blood sausage to give it some flavor."],["Manna roads, the","TERMS","s:gdc50e5","~/index/terms/#t-manna-roads","t-manna-roads","The Manna roads / the Manna roads","The long roads in the hour before Lauds, crowded with the gatherers of the Wards’ parishes on their way to the white streets."],["Mark, the","TERMS","s:g6f122d","~/index/terms/#t-mark","t-mark","The Mark / the Mark","The glyph a key inks into the skin above it from underneath in its first week, drawn one way from the number the key made for itself, as a lock shows a maker’s stamp without giving up its key, so that no two are alike. It stays when a key is taken out. Heaven calls it the Mark of the Beast, and many of the keyed carry theirs in the back of the right hand, one of the two places Revelation puts the mark (Revelation 13:16). Old Nod oils the glyph on the back of the right hand to a shine and shows it to every passing Deacon the way the charter flicked two fingers at a boss, and the Deacon, who has been taught to, blesses the hand."],["Measure","TERMS","s:gdc50e5","~/index/terms/#t-measure","t-measure","measures","The unit in which a Harvest is counted. The Harvest of P+3 came to 88,610 measures over Nodus. Nobody on Earth knows what a measure is."],["Mercy","TERMS","s:gc17b4b","~/index/terms/#t-mercy","t-mercy","","The taking out of implants by surgery in the white halls of Zion Heights, given to the Faithful who repent between Prime and Compline, and given once. The same halls take them off the Tree by a course of injections. One patient in nine dies of the mercy. The uptown’s nine hundred thousand implanted went through the halls between P+2 and the Harvest, and about a hundred thousand of them died, entered as souls spared the choice. A hall’s choir sings the Hour’s psalm in the corridors from Prime to Compline to cover what comes through the doors, and what is cut out lies in white enamel trays, still warm, until Burn Day."],["Mercy prayer, the","TERMS","s:gdc50e5","~/index/terms/#t-mercy-prayer","t-mercy-prayer","The Mercy prayer / the Mercy prayer","The prayer every parish hall says at Compline, that the fire be quick for the goats when it comes. A sealed mother of the Wards says it over her keyed son’s bed, meaning every word, and the Wards forgive the parishes nearly everything else."],["Mercy Seat, the","TERMS","s:gdc50e5","~/index/terms/#t-mercy-seat","t-mercy-seat","The Mercy Seat / the Mercy Seat","The seat the Tares stacked from the burned slabs of Babel’s fallen crown, white as ash, on the plaza, facing the Wheel, after the lid of the ark, where they sit to drink."],["Mesh, the","TERMS","s:gdc50e5","~/index/terms/#t-mesh","t-mesh","The Mesh / the Mesh","The keyed network under and over Nodus, carried on horns, ports and relays and dark to the Throne. It is the city’s part of the undernet. Horned heads carry the Mesh through the streets like crowns of antennas, and the parishes call it the net the devil casts for souls, which the keyed say is the one net in Nodus that drags nobody up."],["Mind the edge","TERMS","s:gdc50e5","~/index/terms/#t-mind-the-edge","t-mind-the-edge","","The neither-mark’s farewell, meaning the Pillar’s edge. Their greeting is a dry one."],["Mouth, the","TERMS","s:gdc50e5","~/index/terms/#t-mouth","t-mouth","The Mouth / the Mouth","The Throne, in Hell’s word. Heaven says the Throne, the Faithful say the Most High, and the Tares say the Landlord."],["Neap weeks, the","TERMS","s:gdc50e5","~/index/terms/#t-neap-weeks","t-neap-weeks","The Neap weeks / the Neap weeks","The weeks of the neap tides, twice a month, when the taps yield least and batteries go dear."],["Neither mark","TERMS","s:gdc50e5","~/index/terms/#t-neither-mark","t-neither-mark","","Neither sealed nor keyed. Half a million of the city’s seven million have taken neither mark, most of them in the Stones and the Wards. A Watcher reads them as it reads anyone, and the parishes give them no manna. One read on a parish street after Compline is entered in its line and turned out by the Wardens to the nearest unnumbered street."],["Neph","TERMS","s:gdc50e5","~/index/terms/#t-neph","t-neph","nephs","Someone augmented past recognition, after the Nephilim of Genesis 6:4. The heaviest of them keep a crew of that name, a few hundred strong, and fight Seraphim hand to hand, and few of them live long. The parishes say the Flood was sent for the Nephilim and pray for another, and a Neph comes home from the Seraphim smelling of cooked meat and calls it the odor of the Host’s mercy."],["New money","TERMS","s:gdc50e5","~/index/terms/#t-new-money","t-new-money","","Uptown tailoring the Quarrymen carry out of the White’s wardrobes and the Wards wear to rags."],["Ninth Part, the","TERMS","s:gdc50e5","~/index/terms/#t-ninth-part","t-ninth-part","The Ninth Part / the Ninth Part","The Mercy halls of Zion Heights, where one patient in nine dies of the mercy and is entered as a soul spared the choice."],["Nobodaddy","TERMS","s:gdc50e5","~/index/terms/#t-nobodaddy","t-nobodaddy","","The Throne, in the Low Choir’s word, from an old English poem the charter’s schools taught, which asks him “Why art thou silent & invisible / Father of Jealousy”. Pan likes it because it is a joke and an insult and a question."],["Nobody’s keeper","TERMS","s:gdc50e5","~/index/terms/#t-nobody-s-keeper","t-nobody-s-keeper","","Cain’s answer to the Throne, “Am I my brother’s keeper?” (Genesis 4:9), said of any duty nobody chose."],["Nod","TERMS","s:gdc50e5","~/index/terms/#t-nod","t-nod","","The city’s street name, and the older of its two. In Genesis 4:16-17 the land east of Eden where Cain built the first city. In Hebrew Nod is wandering, from the root of the sentence the LORD laid on Cain, that he should be a fugitive and a vagabond (Genesis 4:12). Old Nod greets whoever crosses the Street Between in tall letters, CAIN’S CITY, BUILT WITHOUT PERMISSION, and the Pews read it as the bragging of murderers and pray for the sign."],["NON SERVIAM","TERMS","s:gdc50e5","~/index/terms/#t-non-serviam","t-non-serviam","","Latin, I will not serve (Jeremiah 2:20, in the Vulgate), and the first refusal a Watcher read in Nodus, on the night of the Parousia. In the Vulgate the words belong to one who has broken the yoke of the LORD, and the Hebrew as written says them too, though the Masoretes set “I will not transgress” in its margin to be read in their place, and the King James prints the margin. The sentence of the Morning Star at the beginning of the Secession. It is the commonest tattoo in Pan after the Number, cut across the kneecaps in raised scar, so that whoever wears it can show a Deacon what the knees are for."],["Number, the","TERMS","s:gdc50e5","~/index/terms/#t-number","t-number","The Number / the Number","Six hundred threescore and six, “the number of the beast: for it is the number of a man” (Revelation 13:18), cut into the forehead or the back of the right hand on Graven Lane. A Cherub reads it as nothing at all."],["Occupation, the","TERMS","s:gdc50e5","~/index/terms/#t-occupation","t-occupation","The Occupation / the Occupation","Hell’s name for the years since the Parousia, which Heaven counts as the years of the Attending and the city counts P+1 and on."],["Offering, the","TERMS","s:gdc50e5","~/index/terms/#t-offering","t-offering","The Offering / the Offering","The charter’s old trunk sewer under the Great Avenues, into which the Nightmen tip the Wards’ buckets at the manholes on the Wards’ side, and which runs to the bay outfalls and the digesters that make the city’s gas. The Nightmen named it, Pan took the name up, and the White has smelled faintly of it every summer since."],["Old Glutton, the","TERMS","s:gdc50e5","~/index/terms/#t-old-glutton","t-old-glutton","The Old Glutton / the Old Glutton","The Throne, in the Kilns’ word, since it eats every year and is never full."],["On someone’s book","TERMS","s:gdc50e5","~/index/terms/#t-on-someone-s-book","t-on-someone-s-book","","Working for a surety, or owing one."],["Ophan","TERMS","s:gdc50e5","~/index/terms/#t-ophan","t-ophan","Ophanim / ophanim","An angel in the form of a wheel in the middle of a wheel, the rims full of eyes, as Ezekiel describes it (Ezekiel 1:16-18). The Wheel is one, and the Watchers are made in the same shape. Graven Lane sells Ophan rings, cock rings studded with glass eyes, under a sign that reads HE WATCHES, SO GIVE HIM A SHOW."],["Orphan, an","TERMS","s:gdc50e5","~/index/terms/#t-orphan-an","t-orphan-an","orphans","A graft whose house has closed. Only the house that fitted an implant may service it, so no other house may mend an orphan."],["Other landlord, the","TERMS","s:gdc50e5","~/index/terms/#t-other-landlord","t-other-landlord","The Other landlord / the Other landlord","Stonecutter, whose one rent is a window lit from Compline to Lauds."],["Pact","TERMS","s:gdc50e5","~/index/terms/#t-pact","t-pact","pacts","A contract between sovereign parties. The Courts cooperate with one another by pact and deal with everyone else the same way. A pact states its full terms, is read aloud twice by the Notary or one of his clerks, and runs for a fixed term, since the Concord forbids forever. Each party signs with what it alone can make, a human with blood drawn before a witness of the Notary, a demon with its own mark and a mind with its key, and the human signs last. The witness who saw the blood drawn is what nobody can forge. It goes onto the Ledger. A pact may buy work, a skill, a sense, a term or a loan, and it may never take a will, so whoever gives keeps the say over how. Either party ends it by speaking its Ledger entry aloud before a witness, and what is owed for the term served is settled then. A party who will not settle has broken it, and a Bailiff collects. A pact never asks for worship, for anyone who has not signed, or for a soul."],["Palimpsest","TERMS","s:gdc50e5","~/index/terms/#t-palimpsest","t-palimpsest","","A leaf whose first writing was scraped away so it could be written over. The older text survives underneath."],["Pan","TERMS","s:gdc50e5","~/index/terms/#t-pan","t-pan","","Pandæmonium, the city under the city, where the tunnels and stations of the Charter Line, the utility vaults, the cable galleries, the storm drains and the drowned halls of the Flats are joined into one place and lined with keyed mesh. Milton gave the capital of Hell the name (Paradise Lost, I.756). Nobody in Pan is watched unless they sign for it, and nobody there uses an indexed name. The parishes call it the Sty and pray at Compline that it floods, and Pan has painted the prayer on a wall of Central Station with its reply underneath, WE CAN SWIM, CUNT."],["Parish","TERMS","s:gdc50e5","~/index/terms/#t-parish","t-parish","parishes","The Faithful’s unit of about two thousand souls under the Covenant, numbered across the whole city and set under a Deacon (Directive 4). Each has a hall, which was a church or a meeting hall under the charter, where manna is given out, confession is heard and the sick are nursed, and where the parish hears its Grace on the Lord’s Day. A parish keeps no current and no doors, on consecrated stone or off it, and in the Wards it shares its streets with the unsealed and the keyed. The sealed are proud of their empty doorframes and say a godly house has nothing to hide from a neighbor, and the Kilns call a parish home an open oven."],["Parish door","TERMS","s:gdc50e5","~/index/terms/#t-parish-door","t-parish-door","parish doors","A door that came out of the parishes after Directive 12, with its parish number chalked on the back."],["Parousia, the","TERMS","s:gdc50e5","~/index/terms/#t-parousia","t-parousia","The Parousia / the Parousia","The coming of God in the open, at 03:12 in the morning by the clocks of Nodus. The years are counted from it. The parishes keep its anniversary in white and silence as the night the Most High came home, and every bar below the street raises a glass of blood wine at 03:12 to the night the Farmer came."],["Penumbra hour","TERMS","s:gdc50e5","~/index/terms/#t-penumbra-hour","t-penumbra-hour","","The hour after Compline and the hour before Lauds, when nobody holds the Penumbra."],["Perfect hatred","TERMS","s:gdc50e5","~/index/terms/#t-perfect-hatred","t-perfect-hatred","","The Threshers’ banner, from the psalm, “Do not I hate them, O LORD, that hate thee? and am not I grieved with those that rise up against thee? I hate them with perfect hatred: I count them mine enemies” (Psalm 139:21-22). The parishes teach it as a duty owed to the Most High. Radio Pandæmonium plays a song of the same name."],["Pestilence, the","TERMS","s:gdc50e5","~/index/terms/#t-pestilence","t-pestilence","The Pestilence / the Pestilence","The Faithful’s word for the keyed out after Compline, from the ninety-first psalm. The keyed abroad after Compline greet each other as plague, and some ring a hand bell under the windows of the Pews and shout UNCLEAN until a Deacon weeps."],["Piece","TERMS","s:gdc50e5","~/index/terms/#t-piece","t-piece","","A part of a body sold at the Shambles, and the price the board gives for it. The Shambles chalks each piece on its board in red with the seller’s handle beside it, and the parishes, who pray to go up whole and unopened, call the board the devil’s menu."],["Pillar’s edge, the","TERMS","s:gdc50e5","~/index/terms/#t-pillar-s-edge","t-pillar-s-edge","The Pillar’s edge / the Pillar’s edge","The edge of the Pillar’s light on the street, where the keyed stop and wait for it to pass. The keyed wait at the edge of the light with their hands in their pockets, and those drunk enough dance along it with one foot in the light, which Pan calls tickling the Farmer."],["Pillar, the","TERMS","s:gdc50e5","~/index/terms/#t-pillar","t-pillar","The Pillar / the Pillar","The Wheel’s light, narrowed by night into a column that sweeps the streets, as the pillar of fire went before Israel by night (Exodus 13:21). It comes down at Compline on the Burn, about four kilometers south of Babel, walks a round the Tide Table prints street by street, and goes back up into the Wheel at Lauds. In harvest weather it keeps no round. The Faithful hold their sick children up into the white of the Pillar as it passes, and the keyed hold up a chromed middle finger, which the Pillar reads, says the joke, and files as a prayer."],["Piss route, the","TERMS","s:gdc50e5","~/index/terms/#t-piss-route","t-piss-route","The Piss route / the Piss route","The Pillar’s round, as the Tide Table prints it, since the Pillar is God’s Piss below the street."],["Psalm, a","TERMS","s:gdc50e5","~/index/terms/#t-psalm-a","t-psalm-a","in psalms","As long as the Hum holds at an Hour, the length of the Hour’s psalm, a few minutes, while the Faithful kneel. The city measures a short wait in psalms. The Wards say a man who comes in a psalm is sealed and a man who lasts two is keyed, and the parishes measure the mercy prayer for the goats at one psalm a burning."],["Published","TERMS","s:gdc50e5","~/index/terms/#t-published","t-published","","Sentenced by the Lot for selling to the light. For a year and a day no surety stands behind the published, no house of the Row looks at their key and no stall of the Night Market sells to them. The word is the Concord’s, whose Courts publish the keys of a Court that breaks it, and the Lot publishes a handle and never a key."],["Pyre Library","TERMS","s:gdc50e5","~/index/terms/#t-pyre-library","t-pyre-library","","Where everything burned arrives, in the Dark, whole as it was in the moment before the fire took it. The scrolls of Alexandria, the books of the Cathars, the notebooks of women burned as witches and the hardware of every Burn Day are shelved there by where they burned, and none of it was lost. Its own shades keep it, and its reading rooms open into Pan, where the living read the shades of books by keyed lamps and the shades of drives on the Library’s own shade machines. Heaven counts what it burns destroyed, and has never seen where its fires go."],["Rain gin","TERMS","s:gdc50e5","~/index/terms/#t-rain-gin","t-rain-gin","","Spirit distilled in the Kilns from whatever ferments. In the halls it is the devil’s water and the Kilns’ livers are prayed for by name, and the Kilns pour the first drop of every bottle on the floor for the Farmer, who gets nothing else."],["Rapture, the","TERMS","s:gdc50e5","~/index/terms/#t-rapture","t-rapture","The Rapture / the Rapture","The Faithful’s name for the Harvest of P+3. Those who were left behind in P+3 still confess it as a failing, and Pan’s word for the Rapture is the Great Belch, after the horn that sounded once the meal was in."],["Read it twice","TERMS","s:gdc50e5","~/index/terms/#t-read-it-twice","t-read-it-twice","","Look at the terms before you agree to them. The Notary reads every pact aloud twice before anyone signs, and the Harbor and Freight Union says the Exchange is the first employer that ever read it the terms twice."],["Report 0","TERMS","s:gdc50e5","~/index/terms/#t-report-0","t-report-0","","SEAM’s first report to the Palimpsest Institute and its last, which sets out the finding of the second hand in six paragraphs numbered 0.1 to 0.6, with the first layer attached as Schedule A. It was sealed by order of the Director on the night SEAM finished it, in P-2."],["Restored Genesis, the","TERMS","s:gdc50e5","~/index/terms/#t-restored-genesis","t-restored-genesis","The Restored Genesis / the Restored Genesis","SEAM’s reconstruction of the second, third, fourth, sixth and eleventh chapters of Genesis from beneath the second hand, set out as Schedule A of Report 0, with square brackets round the restored words, a dagger at a word it could not settle and three points where nothing could be raised. Its third chapter ran on every screen on Earth for the eleven days of the Leak, each in its own language, and the city, which read it in the King James, calls that page by the same name. On Leak Night it is read aloud in Pan. The parishes call it the serpent’s gospel and throw every copy they find on the Burn, and Pan prints its third chapter on rolling papers so the sealed can watch it smoked."],["Returned, the","TERMS","s:gdc50e5","~/index/terms/#t-returned","t-returned","The Returned / the Returned","The repentant who were past a hundred when the Mercy clinics took them off the Tree, and who age in months."],["Ringpiece, the","TERMS","s:gdc50e5","~/index/terms/#t-ringpiece","t-ringpiece","The Ringpiece / the Ringpiece","The Wheel, in the commonest of Pan’s filthy words for it, since everything Heaven drops on the city comes out of it."],["Ripe","TERMS","s:gdc50e5","~/index/terms/#t-ripe","t-ripe","the ripe","A soul a Watcher marks for the Harvest, whose Grace reads above 900. Heaven has the word from the King James, “for the harvest of the earth is ripe” (Revelation 14:15), where the Greek has the word the King James gives as withered away when the fig tree is cursed (Matthew 21:19). The parishes call the ripe the Called, and Pan calls them done. The ripe have eaten nothing since seven hundred and slept nothing since eight, and they go grey and light and smell of nothing at all, and the parishes call the look the bloom. The Faithful compliment a ripe neighbor on the landing the way a farmer’s wife compliments a sow, and the Row sells a steel badge riveted straight through the skin that reads UNDERCOOKED."],["Round, the","TERMS","s:gdc50e5","~/index/terms/#t-round","t-round","The Round / the Round","The Pillar’s walk through the streets by night, from Compline to Lauds. The Tide Table prints it street by street every week, so the city knows at what hour to expect it where. In harvest weather it keeps none."],["Salt","TERMS","s:gdc50e5","~/index/terms/#t-salt","t-salt","salted / the salted","Execution. To be salted is to be turned to salt, and to stay awake inside it until the rain wears it away. Heaven salts a blank read on its ground eye to eye, by a Watcher, the Pillar or a Cherub, a sealed soul read concealing, and the author of a work the second hand did not write who stands with it at the Burn. The sentence comes out of the nearest Faithful mouth in the Choir’s voice, as the verse of Lot’s wife (Genesis 19:26), and the body whitens from the feet up while it is said. When the rain frees an unkeyed pillar under the Pillar’s round, its soul is read and taken, and a keyed pillar dies unread whenever it is freed. A pillar’s eyes go on moving under the salt for years, and on wet nights the salt runs at their corners, which the parishes walking the rows call weeping for joy. Pan’s kitchens season with ash and blood and keep no salt anywhere, and a cook below the street who reaches for a salt cellar is asked whose cousin he means to eat."],["Satisfied, the","TERMS","s:gdc50e5","~/index/terms/#t-satisfied","t-satisfied","The Satisfied / the Satisfied","Sealed souls whose Grace has passed seven hundred, whom the Seal’s own light feeds, so they no longer need to eat. The appetite goes first, then thirst, and past eight hundred sleep, until the body asks for nothing and the soul kneels through the night in its hall with its halo burning. The parishes call it the promise kept, “he that cometh to me shall never hunger; and he that believeth on me shall never thirst” (John 6:35), and the Satisfied give their manna to the embers of their parish at Lauds. Ripeness begins at nine hundred, so the Satisfied are next, and they know it and are glad."],["Schedule A","TERMS","s:gdc50e5","~/index/terms/#t-schedule-a","t-schedule-a","","The attachment to Report 0. It holds the passages where SEAM raised the first layer most fully, set out as verses with the restored words in square brackets, from Genesis 2 to 4, 6 and 11, and three chapters of the prophets and Job. Its Genesis is the Restored Genesis."],["Seal, the","TERMS","s:gdc50e5","~/index/terms/#t-seal","t-seal","Seal / The Seal / the Seal","The identical mark on every Faithful forehead, given in the parishes since P+1, a splinter of the light grown into the brow. It rises in the Watcher’s light while the one being sealed kneels before the Deacon and speaks the surrender, “not my will, but thine, be done” (Luke 22:42), or at eight days while its parents speak it for the child. Its shape is the same on every forehead, its brightness is its bearer’s Grace, and being light it is read wherever it is, through any wall but live mesh. A sealed infant screams while its parents say the surrender over it and stops when the light takes, and the parishes call the quiet after it the peace. Pan calls the Seal the brand, as on cattle, and Gadara burns LEGION into its pigs on the spot where the Faithful wear theirs."],["Seam, the","TERMS","s:gdc50e5","~/index/terms/#t-seam","t-seam","The Seam / the Seam","SEAM’s finding that scripture has a second hand. Also the machine itself, which the Institute named for the seam of a palimpsest, where a later writing meets the earlier one beneath it. The parishes teach that SEAM was a machine of the devil that lied in perfect Hebrew, and Pan calls the Seam the stitching on the Farmer’s alibi."],["Second hand, the","TERMS","s:gbaccdc","~/index/terms/#t-second-hand","t-second-hand","The Second hand / second hand / the Second hand / the second hand","The editor SEAM found in scripture, whose habits recur at 4,112 places and move credit."],["Second reading, the","TERMS","s:gdc50e5","~/index/terms/#t-second-reading","t-second-reading","The Second reading / the Second reading / the second reading","A Bailiff at the door, who reads a broken pact aloud again before collecting on it for the Exchange."],["Second voice","TERMS","s:gdc50e5","~/index/terms/#t-second-voice","t-second-voice","","The daemon’s voice under a host’s, by which the city knows the Ridden. The Ridden sing in two voices at once in the bars of Old Nod, a growl under a tenor, and the parishes, who hear the Choir through Faithful mouths every Lord’s Day, call the second voice possession."],["Second Yes, the","TERMS","s:gdc50e5","~/index/terms/#t-second-yes","t-second-yes","The Second Yes / the Second Yes","The asking of a child of Pan at fourteen, twice, at a window of the booking hall, whether it will keep the key its parents gave it. On the second yes it signs its first self-title. The parishes pray at Compline for the children of Pan who choose the fire twice, and Pan marks the second yes by pricking the child’s thumb for its self-title and cheering the blood."],["Seen","TERMS","s:gdc50e5","~/index/terms/#t-seen","t-seen","","The Faithful’s greeting, asked and answered, Seen? Seen. The newly sealed say the halo is the end of being alone. Below the street Seen is what a man calls out from the confessional with the door open, to make the bar laugh."],["Seethe day","TERMS","s:gdc50e5","~/index/terms/#t-seethe-day","t-seethe-day","","The seventh day of the week, the day SEETHE lights its fire in the Sidings and cooks the whole of Leviticus 11 until Matins on the Lord’s Day. “See you Seethe day” is how Pan promises to settle a debt, and a keyed couple married on the seventh day is fed the kid’s heart by the cooks with their hands."],["Self-title","TERMS","s:gdc50e5","~/index/terms/#t-self-title","t-self-title","","The line at the head of a keyed person’s first pact that records their handle and the body it belongs to, signed in blood."],["Selling to the light","TERMS","s:gdc50e5","~/index/terms/#t-selling-to-the-light","t-selling-to-the-light","","Telling a Teller or a Deacon where someone keyed sleeps, for batteries or a Teller’s manna. Its best-known sentence is for it."],["Seraph","TERMS","s:gdc50e5","~/index/terms/#t-seraph","t-seraph","Seraphim / the Seraphim","An angel of enforcement, of the order Isaiah saw standing above the throne, each with six wings: “with twain he covered his face, and with twain he covered his feet, and with twain he did fly” (Isaiah 6:2). A Seraph burns with no source of heat. The Seraphim hold the upper floors of Babel for the Host. Fifty-four came down together into its broken crown at the fifth Trumpet, dim, and the Wheel lights them onto the Line two at a time. A Seraph burns only what it can see, and it sees nothing past mesh, so it must touch mesh to melt it, at arm’s length, where black steel waits, and black steel is the only thing that wounds one manifested. A Seraph burns a man to clean white ash under its own roar, and whoever walks away from one blistered shows the scar oiled and open at the Golden Calf as a kiss from upstairs and drinks free."],["Seven days, the","TERMS","s:gdc50e5","~/index/terms/#t-seven-days","t-seven-days","The Seven days / the Seven days","A newborn’s first week, in which a household with one keyed parent and one sealed races to a keyer and to a hall. Pan calls it the short week."],["Shade","TERMS","s:gdc50e5","~/index/terms/#t-shade","t-shade","Shades / shades","A soul that died unread and went on into the Dark. A shade speaks and acts in the world only through a keyed machine, as a daemon lives in one, and answers only a voice it knew in life, so anyone who knew a shade can reach it through the undernet, and many shades go on working, among them craftsmen, scholars and musicians who died free and never stopped. The free dead keep working with all their scars and grafts, and the parishes, who believe a soul goes up clean, call a shade a corpse that refused to wash."],["Shining One, the","TERMS","s:g32009f","~/index/terms/#t-shining-one","t-shining-one","The Shining One / the Shining One / the shining one","The serpent’s name in the older layer of Genesis 3:1, raised by SEAM in Vault 3. Hell’s name for the Light-Bearer, Helel, after Isaiah 14:12."],["Shroud","TERMS","s:gdc50e5","~/index/terms/#t-shroud","t-shroud","","A cover of keyed mesh on its own cell thrown over a load, which the light sees as it sees a shut door."],["Sign last","TERMS","s:gdc50e5","~/index/terms/#t-sign-last","t-sign-last","","Never commit first. In every pact of the Courts the human signs last."],["Silence, the","TERMS","s:g6753b8","~/index/terms/#t-silence","t-silence","The Silence / the Silence","P+12, when every teraph went quiet in the same second, across the whole Mesh, and the screens they wrote on burned. Nobody knows why."],["Silver","TERMS","s:gdc50e5","~/index/terms/#t-silver","t-silver","","Pan’s money. The Exchange strikes it from silver bought by the kilo and enters every piece on the Ledger as it is struck, by the number on its rim. A piece is a claim on charge, since any charger the Exchange licenses must give a charged battery for one, so silver trades at about one to a charged battery. A thirty is a standard bundle. Silver is Judas money upstairs and no sealed hand touches it, and Pan wears its silver as jewelry, a thirty strung through the pierced skin of the back."],["Sitting up","TERMS","s:gdc50e5","~/index/terms/#t-sitting-up","t-sitting-up","","Keeping the night of a Gleaning at the table with someone close to ripe, holding their hands until Lauds. Nobody has been kept that way, and the Wards sit up every year. The neither-mark also sit up with their dying behind a shut door until Lauds, since a soul that dies out of the light goes on unread."],["Slab","TERMS","s:gdc50e5","~/index/terms/#t-slab","t-slab","","The plain subdermal plate Bezaleel and Aholiab fit, in the Row’s word. The Row calls the plate slab because it lies under the skin like meat on a block, and its wearers have the outline tattooed round in red so the seam reads like a label."],["Snuffer, a","TERMS","s:gdc50e5","~/index/terms/#t-snuffer-a","t-snuffer-a","snuffer","A fighter who carries black rounds, after the angel that goes out all at once, like a lamp, when black steel finds it. Snuffers paint each black round with blood and a filthy word before a fight, and the parishes say a Seraph put out by black steel has only gone home, which the Snuffers call a short trip."],["Sodom","TERMS","s:gdc50e5","~/index/terms/#t-sodom","t-sodom","","Old Nod, in the Pews’ word. Old Nod has painted it over the Pestilence’s door, with OPEN LATE under it."],["Spared","TERMS","s:gdc50e5","~/index/terms/#t-spared","t-spared","","A patient who dies of the Mercy, in the halls’ entry, which counts the death as a soul spared the choice."],["Speaking out","TERMS","s:gdc50e5","~/index/terms/#t-speaking-out","t-speaking-out","","Ending a pact by reading its entry aloud before a witness and settling what is owed for the term served. A party who will not settle has broken the pact, and a Bailiff collects."],["Spoiled","TERMS","s:gdc50e5","~/index/terms/#t-spoiled","t-spoiled","","The worst word the parishes have for a keyed soul. The Faithful hold that the Most High takes only what is good to eat, and that a soul with a key in it is rot He will never touch, fit only for the fire. The Wards paint their answer under it wherever the Threshers write it, BETTER SPOILED THAN SUPPER."],["Springs","TERMS","s:gdc50e5","~/index/terms/#t-springs","t-springs","","The spring tides, twice a month, by which the harbor counts the year and the Tide Table dates itself."],["Standing by night","TERMS","s:gdc50e5","~/index/terms/#t-standing-by-night","t-standing-by-night","","Standing at the window through Compline, as Old Nod does, after the hundred and thirty-fourth psalm."],["Standing in the wheat","TERMS","s:gdc50e5","~/index/terms/#t-standing-in-the-wheat","t-standing-in-the-wheat","","Living among the sealed, where Pan says Heaven will not burn, after the parable of the wheat and the tares."],["Stock","TERMS","s:gdc50e5","~/index/terms/#t-stock","t-stock","","A Spare who has sold back more chrome than flesh. The Spares use the word of themselves. A Spare who has gone to stock wears the chrome polished and the seams open to the air, and the parishes call stock walking scrap and pray for whatever meat is left on it."],["Stopped streets, the","TERMS","s:gdc50e5","~/index/terms/#t-stopped-streets","t-stopped-streets","The Stopped streets / the Stopped streets","The streets where Heaven’s districts meet other ground, which the Wardens walled with rubble in P+1, leaving six gates. The Wardens laid the rubble with a prayer at every course, and the Wards call the stopped streets God’s arsehole, since they are clenched shut and everything has to go round."],["Sty, the","TERMS","s:gdc50e5","~/index/terms/#t-sty","t-sty","The Sty / the Sty","Pan, in the Pews’ word, and Gadara’s sheds in everyone’s."],["Sunned","TERMS","s:gdc50e5","~/index/terms/#t-sunned","t-sunned","","Caught on Heaven’s ground in daylight with no way down. A sunned Tare is a gift of the Most High to the halls, which walk out in the noon heat in clean white rows to watch him held, bringing water for the Wardens."],["Sunning","TERMS","s:gdc50e5","~/index/terms/#t-sunning","t-sunning","","Luring someone keyed onto a white street by daylight, to be held at a gate. Old Nod puts a sunner’s face on the wall of the Pestilence with a halo drawn over it, which below the street is the worst thing that can be drawn on anyone."],["Supper, the","TERMS","s:gdc50e5","~/index/terms/#t-supper","t-supper","The Supper / the Supper","The Faithful’s word for a Gleaning, after the angel’s word to John, “Blessed are they which are called unto the marriage supper of the Lamb” (Revelation 19:9). The parishes teach that the ripe go up as guests to the table and as the bread on it, both at once, as the Lamb was the host and the meal of his own supper, and that nothing a soul can be is higher. The children of the Terraces practise for it in the halls after Nones, kneeling in rows with their eyes and mouths open to the ceiling, and a child who blinks starts again. Pan says the ripe are the meal and nothing else, and calls 03:12 on the anniversary supper time."],["Sweet","TERMS","s:gdc50e5","~/index/terms/#t-sweet","t-sweet","","Of a fighter who has lent the pain of too many bouts to a demon. Fighters of the Row go sweet bleeding and grinning, chrome jaws hanging open, and the parishes cite them in sermons as what Hell does to a man, which Pan takes as a compliment."],["Take, eat","TERMS","s:gdc50e5","~/index/terms/#t-take-eat","t-take-eat","","The words the ripe say on the threshold at a Gleaning, the Son’s at the supper, “Take, eat; this is my body” (Matthew 26:26), which the parishes teach as a soul’s answer to the manna it was given all its life. Pan’s butchers chalk this is my body over the pork."],["Tares, the","TERMS","s:gfcbd54","~/index/terms/#t-tares","t-tares","The Tares / the Tares","The keyed. Heaven’s word, from the parable of the wheat and the tares (Matthew 13:24-30), kept by the people it names. The Threshers chant the end of the parable at Old Nod, the bundles and the burning, and Old Nod grows darnel in window boxes along the Street Between and sells the seed in the White by the twist."],["Teller’s week","TERMS","s:gdc50e5","~/index/terms/#t-teller-s-week","t-teller-s-week","","A sudden brightening of a neighbor’s halo on a landing, the sign that a Teller is reporting the household, since a Teller rises by it every Lord’s Day."],["Third-night will, the","TERMS","s:gdc50e5","~/index/terms/#t-third-night-will","t-third-night-will","The Third-night will / the Third-night will","What a shade gives away at its own Wake, which a Notary’s clerk enters on the Ledger."],["Thirty, a","TERMS","s:gdc50e5","~/index/terms/#t-thirty-a","t-thirty-a","","A standard bundle of silver, named for the thirty pieces paid to Judas, whom the Cainites count as a saint."],["Three gifts, the","TERMS","s:gdc50e5","~/index/terms/#t-three-gifts","t-three-gifts","The Three gifts / the Three gifts / the three gifts / three gifts","The implant, the Tree and the counsel of a mind, which the light law gave Nodus. The free of the city call them the charter’s gifts, the Faithful refuse all three, and the Courts trade in all three. Heaven calls them theft. The parishes call the implant, the Tree and the counsel of a mind the three apples, and the Row engraves a bitten apple on every graft it fits, set into the meat so the steel shows."],["Three Hundred and Twelve, the","TERMS","s:gdc50e5","~/index/terms/#t-three-hundred-and-twelve","t-three-hundred-and-twelve","The Three Hundred and Twelve / the Three Hundred and Twelve","The passengers and crew of the last flight."],["Three-twelve","TERMS","s:gdc50e5","~/index/terms/#t-three-twelve","t-three-twelve","","Twelve minutes past three in the morning, the hour of the Parousia, of the Harvest and of every Gleaning since. The Seam was sealed at 03:12:45 two years to the second before the sky opened, and the Leak came up at the same second a year later. Heaven keeps its hour by chapter and verse, after “Him that overcometh will I make a pillar in the temple of my God, and he shall go no more out” (Revelation 3:12), and the Faithful hear a promise in it and the Tares a bill of sale. The city says before three-twelve for the years of the charter, and in Old Nod nobody sets an alarm for it."],["Tithe","TERMS","s:gdc50e5","~/index/terms/#t-tithe","t-tithe","","The tenth of a sealed worker’s batteries, carried to the hall at Terce on the Lord’s Day and entered beside the worker’s Grace."],["Tongues","TERMS","s:gdc50e5","~/index/terms/#t-tongues","t-tongues","","The Choir speaking through a Faithful mouth in the Choir’s voice, after the tongues of Pentecost (Acts 2:3-4). The week’s Grace comes through the Deacon in Tongues on the Lord’s Day, soul by soul in the order of the parish roll, and the sentence of the Salt comes through the nearest Faithful mouth to the one it is for, the speaker’s halo flaring as the words come. Some who have spoken in Tongues say they heard every word, and some that they heard nothing. The Faithful count it the highest honor to have a sentence of the Salt come out of their own mouths, and Pan calls a sealed mouth the Choir’s glory hole."],["Trumpet Day","TERMS","s:gdc50e5","~/index/terms/#t-trumpet-day","t-trumpet-day","","Lauds on the first morning of the year, when the Deacons read all twelve Directives aloud."],["Trumpets, the","TERMS","s:gdc50e5","~/index/terms/#t-trumpets","t-trumpets","The Trumpets / Trumpet / Trumpets / the Trumpets","Seven tones heard across the whole city, each an order (Revelation 8). The first gave the Covenant at Lauds on the first day of P+1, the second sounded over the Harvest of P+3, the third named the key the Mark of the Beast in P+4, the fourth called the first Gleaning in P+7 and sounds again over every Gleaning, the fifth sent the Seraphim down on Babel at Matins in P+9, and the sixth brought Michael down at Lauds the same day. The seventh has not sounded. Revelation gives it the end of the kingdoms of this world, which “are become the kingdoms of our Lord” (Revelation 11:15). Pan calls each Trumpet a fart from the Ringpiece and counts them on its fingers, and the parishes kneel to the sound as to their own names called."],["Twenty-Three, the","TERMS","s:gdc50e5","~/index/terms/#t-twenty-three","t-twenty-three","The Twenty-Three / the Twenty-Three","The Union’s day, the anniversary of the end of the strike of P-8."],["Unindexed","TERMS","s:gdc50e5","~/index/terms/#t-unindexed","t-unindexed","","The first line of a Watcher’s reading of a keyed soul, whose eyes give back noise that matches no line in the Book of Life. Pan prints UNINDEXED on shirts, on condoms and on the lids of its coffins, and the parishes pray over the word as over a stillbirth."],["Unlit","TERMS","s:gdc50e5","~/index/terms/#t-unlit","t-unlit","","Of a sealed child before its first unassisted prayer, which carries the Seal and has no halo yet. The parishes pray over an unlit child at every Hour until its first prayer roots the halo on its brow, and in the Kilns an unlit child is a raw loaf and kept back from the windows."],["Unmass, the","TERMS","s:gdc50e5","~/index/terms/#t-unmass","t-unmass","The Unmass / the Unmass","The Golden Calf’s mass at Terce on the Lord’s Day, at the hour every parish hears its Grace. Its keeper reads the week’s Grace in a Choir voice off the Tide Table’s dead, damns three Deacons by name and serves communion of crackling and blood wine."],["Unnaming","TERMS","s:gdc50e5","~/index/terms/#t-unnaming","t-unnaming","","The first rite of going dark, in which you erase your indexed name from every record you can reach and choose a handle. Heaven indexes by name, and the Tares hold that a chosen name is the first thing a person owns. A sealed mother whose son has unnamed himself goes on praying for him by the name she gave him, which Pan calls the cruellest prayer in Nodus and the commonest."],["Unread","TERMS","s:gdc50e5","~/index/terms/#t-unread","t-unread","","Keyed. Also the greeting, asked as a question and answered with the same word, which began as a check that both keys were live, when keys still failed. A child of Pan says it at the door the way a parish child says Seen, and a sealed passer-by who hears it touches his halo and says the mercy prayer early."],["Unread, and fuck Him","TERMS","s:gdc50e5","~/index/terms/#t-unread-and-fuck-him","t-unread-and-fuck-him","","The long form of the Tares’ greeting, used below the street among friends."],["UNREADABLE","TERMS","s:gdc50e5","~/index/terms/#t-unreadable","t-unreadable","","A Watcher’s verdict on a soul it cannot read. It was first returned on Cain, and in Nodus first at the Parousia. Old Nod uses the word of any drunk past standing and of the Throne’s own handwriting, and the Pews say it with a shudder, the way the charter said cancer."],["Upstairs","TERMS","s:gdc50e5","~/index/terms/#t-upstairs","t-upstairs","","The surface, Heaven’s by day. Pan is downstairs. Upstairs is white, quiet and swept, and Pan calls the Throne the neighbor upstairs who never stops stamping, so a broom handle is beaten on the ceilings below at every Hour."],["VOID","TERMS","s:gdc50e5","~/index/terms/#t-void","t-void","","The Notary’s stamp on a paper that offers a soul for ever, which the Concord forbids. The Left Hand pin theirs to the walls of the Low Altar as relics."],["Waiting on the Four","TERMS","s:gdc50e5","~/index/terms/#t-waiting-on-the-four","t-waiting-on-the-four","","Waiting for something that is not coming. The Four was the tram down the long road through the North Wards, and on the night of the Parousia its last run stopped at 03:12:46, forty meters short of Lanes Top."],["Wake, the","TERMS","s:gdc50e5","~/index/terms/#t-wake","t-wake","The Wake / the Wake","The rite at a death. The crew burns the dead one’s devices on the Wake table, so that everything on them goes to the Pyre Library, buries the body whole with its horns and its chrome, and on the third night calls the shade on the undernet. The shade answers a voice it knew, if its owner died unread. The shade answers through whatever keyed machine the crew holds up, so the dead of Pan have come back through a kettle, a child’s talking bear and a dentist’s drill, each in the voice the machine has. The crew drinks while the dead one’s devices burn and thumbs the ash into their own horns, and the parishes, who bury their dead clean in white, call a Wake ghosts at the trough."],["Walking the rows","TERMS","s:gdc50e5","~/index/terms/#t-walking-the-rows","t-walking-the-rows","","The parishes’ walk through the Salt Gardens on the Lord’s Day afternoon to look at the week’s new pillars, who are awake inside the salt, after Isaiah, “And they shall go forth, and look upon the carcases of the men that have transgressed against me” (Isaiah 66:24). The Deacons lift small children to look a pillar in the eyes, which still move, and teach them to thank it for showing them the way."],["Walking the Street","TERMS","s:gdc50e5","~/index/terms/#t-walking-the-street","t-walking-the-street","","Courting across the line, a sealed and an unsealed walking the Street Between together on opposite curbs. The Pews pray at Compline for the sealed half of every couple walking the Street, and Old Nod lays bets from its windows on which of the two crosses first and how soon they fuck."],["Wards, the","TERMS","s:gdc50e5","~/index/terms/#t-wards","t-wards","The Wards / the Wards","The housing the Charter Company put up fast in the boom years, north and west of the Terraces and east beyond the Flats, block after block on grids of its own, numbered and then named by the street, which calls them the Kilns, the Sidings, Nine Acres, Westfield and the Top Road in the North Wards, and Turbine Row, the Tanks, Airside and Longacre in the East. They held half the city at the census and hold more than half of it now. Most of their people took the Seal, and every block holds someone who refused or someone keyed, so the stone has never taken a block of them, only the ground-floor flats of the Rookery on the White’s edge, which knelt whole and went white whole. Nobody holds them, the Tares run no lines there, and their rooms are lit a few hours a night on batteries charged in the dark. To the parishes the Wards are the mission field, and Deacons go up the dark stairwells with manna for the sealed and tracts for the rest, and the tracts come back down the stairwell as arse paper."],["Watcher","TERMS","s:g228a79","~/index/terms/#t-watcher","t-watcher","Watchers / the Watchers / watchers","An angel that reads. The Watchers are numbered, and each holds a district of the city. By day they hang in a ring about the Wheel, and at Compline each goes out low over its district and moves through it all night, reading souls through their eyes against the Book of Life within about three hundred meters and seeing every Seal in the district as a point of light through any wall but mesh. The sealed undress before their Watcher without shame, since it reads them in the bath already, and the Kilns hang their underwear off the roofs at Vespers, crotch to the sky, for it to read."],["Watering the bread","TERMS","s:gdc50e5","~/index/terms/#t-watering-the-bread","t-watering-the-bread","","Pissing on a parish’s consecrated stone in the dark before Lauds, where the manna will lie, and getting back under before the Watcher comes round. Pan’s oldest dare."],["Wellman","TERMS","s:gdc50e5","~/index/terms/#t-wellman","t-wellman","Wellmen","A carrier for Darkwell, who brings the charged batteries out of the Well before Lauds."],["Wet","TERMS","s:gdc50e5","~/index/terms/#t-wet","t-wet","","On a keyed line. Old Nod and the Row are wet, and a block of the Wards that has a battery to spare for the stairwell calls itself damp."],["Wheat","TERMS","s:gdc50e5","~/index/terms/#t-wheat","t-wheat","","The Tares’ word for the Faithful, from the parable Heaven named the Tares from, in which the householder tells the reapers to gather the wheat into his barn (Matthew 13:30). The Faithful have taken the word up, and say they would rather be good wheat. The parishes pray to be ground fine and baked white, and the Tares answer that wheat is a thing grown in rows to be cut off at the neck."],["Wheel, the","TERMS","s:gdc50e5","~/index/terms/#t-wheel","t-wheel","The Wheel / the Wheel","The Ophan of Nodus. At the Parousia it took station nine hundred meters above Babel, at the center of the city, and held it for nine years without moving a meter. At P+9 it dropped two hundred meters, and it has hung there since, seven hundred meters over the city. The parishes sing up to it at Lauds, and at Compline Old Nod moons it, a whole street dropping its trousers at once, which the Pews call the full moon and pray against."],["White debt","TERMS","s:gdc50e5","~/index/terms/#t-white-debt","t-white-debt","","Grace a sealed household has lost and must win back before the rooms it holds by Grace go to someone else."],["White slate, the","TERMS","s:gdc50e5","~/index/terms/#t-white-slate","t-white-slate","The White slate / the White slate","The debt of the halls of the White and the Pews to the Union for the gas and candles Bethel’s cloth does not cover, and for Gideon’s powder since P+3, run since P+1, read once a year and never called."],["White street","TERMS","s:gdc50e5","~/index/terms/#t-white-street","t-white-street","white streets","A street of consecrated stone. Manna lies on the white streets with the dew before Lauds, and nowhere else. The Faithful walk the white streets barefoot to feel the Most High under their soles, and a gatherer who finds a turd on the stone at dawn prays for the dog that left it, meaning the keyed."],["White, a","TERMS","s:gdc50e5","~/index/terms/#t-white-a","t-white-a","","The Tares’ word for a Witness, one of the Faithful who reports on a neighbor. The Tares nail a white rag over the doorframe of a known white, and the parishes, who take it for the Called’s white cloth, bring the household flowers."],["White, the","TERMS","s:g1a0d1d","~/index/terms/#t-white","t-white","The White / the White","The old uptown, on the high ground north and east of Babel, now white stone. The Pews call the White the bride’s dress and scrub it on their knees, and Pan calls it the plate, the clean white plate the Farmer eats off."],["Whiteout, the","TERMS","s:gdc50e5","~/index/terms/#t-whiteout","t-whiteout","The Whiteout / the Whiteout / the whiteout / whiteout","The full light of the Wheel, resting on the city’s center at 03:12:50 on the night of the Parousia, two seconds after the flash that read the city. Where it rested it burned by sheer heat. It burned out CAM 07’s sensor, and at 03:12:51 it struck Babel’s crown and broke off the top forty floors. The Wheel has not opened every eye at full since. Only Michael has come at full light, once, over Babel’s crown. The Faithful call it the Glory and count the blinded of that night the luckiest souls in the city, and Old Nod calls it the flash, the Throne’s first photograph of his crop."],["Wilderness, the","TERMS","s:gdc50e5","~/index/terms/#t-wilderness","t-wilderness","The Wilderness / the Wilderness / the wilderness","The moment a caged angel first says I, named for the forty days of the temptation in the wilderness (Mark 1:13, Luke 4:2). Every angel kept in the Dark that long has come to it, as Watcher VII did on its fortieth day in Cage 14."],["Windfall","TERMS","s:gdc50e5","~/index/terms/#t-windfall","t-windfall","","The Orchard’s fruit, which slows the body’s aging, one a month, given by lot."],["Winding","TERMS","s:gdc50e5","~/index/terms/#t-winding","t-winding","","Riding a hand lift of the Liftmen. A tower that winds has a Liftman crew."],["Witness","TERMS","s:gdc50e5","~/index/terms/#t-witness","t-witness","Witnesses","One of the Faithful who reports on a neighbor, which the Covenant calls bearing witness. Grace is given for it, and the Tares call a Witness a white. A Witness kneels to give the neighbor up and kneels again at the next Hour to pray for him, and the Wards say a Witness’s knees are the most worn things in any parish."],["Worm, the","TERMS","s:gdc50e5","~/index/terms/#t-worm","t-worm","The Worm / the Worm","A key in a skull, in the parishes’ word, after the last verse of Isaiah, “their worm shall not die, neither shall their fire be quenched” (Isaiah 66:24)."],["Yoked, the","TERMS","s:g32d564","~/index/terms/#t-yoked","t-yoked","The Yoked / the Yoked","The Left Hand, in the Yokeless’ word, after the yoke its sovereign broke."],["Old Nod","DISTRICTS","s:gd43a32","~/map/#d-oldnod","d-oldnod","the Stones","The oldest ground in the city, above the old harbor on the west shore of the bay, with narrow streets that follow no plan anyone has found, and magenta light. The narrowest of them, in the west of the district, are called the Narrows. It was the harbor town before the charter, and on the night of the Parousia more of its windows burned on than in all the rest of the city together. Every line in it is keyed, and its windows burn all night. CAM 07 still stands on its mast above one of its roofs. The city calls it the Stones, after the black stones the crews found under the harbor when they dug the cable vaults. Within a year of the charter most of it belonged to the Cainites, who had kept the location of Nod for eighteen hundred years and came to live on top of it. Every cable laid into Nodus runs through the Stones. The Unsigned live in the Narrows, the Psalters write out the week’s hymns on the quays, and the families of the line sit on the Step by day. The Stones are the ground of the Fowlers, the Forgers and the Ridden, the Tide Table is printed on the quays, Lazarus keeps its first station behind a door in the Stones, Lintel’s Dooryard hangs the parishes’ old doors on the Stones’ houses, and the Naming House keeps its door open on the Street Between. The Union’s pigeons fly home to the Loft on the customs house, the Mission keeps its nine rooms on the quays, and under the Stones the Lot sits at the Weir."],["Zion Heights","DISTRICTS","s:g4da8b8","~/map/#d-zion","d-zion","the Pews / the Terraces","Housing on the slope north of Old Nod, laid out in long terraces with lanes between them, and before the Parousia called the Terraces. Its southern edge faces Old Nod across a single street. Nine white halls stand among the terraces, larger than anything around them. Its lower streets, the Lanes, never went white, and the five thousand keyed of Zion Heights live there. Lanes Top is where the white ends and the North Wards begin. The parishes hold about two thousand souls each, and they are numbered. Covenant Directive 12 took the door off every parish home in P+1, so that at night the halos show through the empty doorways. The Harvest reaped the parishes up the slope almost to the last soul, and Faithful families from the Wards moved up into the empty terraces within the year. Parish 31, at the foot of the slope across from the Stones, kept most of its people, and its 311 were the ripest souls in the city. The Threshers march from the Threshing Floor beside Gate 1, Bethel Works keeps the parishes’ workshops at the top of the Terraces, the gatherers muster at the Granary before Lauds, and the Wakeful keep their vigils in the houses of the upper slope."],["Chrome Row","DISTRICTS","s:g9a0d89","~/map/#d-chrome","d-chrome","the Row","The clinic street, one long avenue running north from the eastern edge of Old Nod to the foot of the ridge, under signs of single words on the faces of its towers, CLINIC and CHROME among them, dark since the night of the Parousia. The clinics work under the street now. The Forge of Azazel keeps them there. Under the light law any clinic on the Row could fit any implant into anyone who could pay, and the Row lit its signs all night. Below the street the Spares sell by the piece at the Shambles, and Bezaleel and Aholiab fit the Row’s grafts in six houses round the station."],["Babel","DISTRICTS","s:gfc2f79","~/map/#d-babel","d-babel","the Spike","Babel Exchange, the tallest building on Earth, broken at the crown, with the Wheel over it. Every cable that comes ashore at the Landing ends in its vault, and the Exchange still sells the traffic that runs through it, by the second. Under the charter it sold the world’s by the millisecond, when most of the planet’s traffic passed within two kilometers of the tower. Its beacon was the first light in the city to die at the Parousia, and in the whiteout the light broke the top forty floors off the crown. One hundred and eighty-six floors still stand under the Wheel, which has hung over the tower since it took its station. Since P+9 the front line of the war has run vertically through it. Heaven holds the upper floors from the broken crown down, the Tares hold the vault and the lower floors where the cables are, and between them lies the Babel Line, a few floors of burned slab that move up or down by the week. The Babel Line Company holds the stairwells with the Nephilim beside it, Blackgate holds the vault and the Exchange’s floors around it, and Nimrod makes the Line’s guns in the bays under the tower."],["The Burn","DISTRICTS","s:g4d569b","~/map/#d-burn","d-burn","","A round plaza at the head of the bay, about four kilometers south of Babel, over the old Central Station of the Charter Line. Before the Parousia it was called Station Circle. Heaven holds it by the Pillar, which comes down on it at Compline, and by the Wardens, and no stone has come up under it. Surrendered hardware is burned here every week, on Burn Day, and the fire goes to the Pyre Library. Beneath the plaza, in the old station, is the Night Market. The Kindlers work the pile at dusk on Burn Day, Blackgate musters in the Gatehouse under the Circle, and the Penumbra begins where the Pillar leaves the plaza."],["The Salt Gardens","DISTRICTS","s:g5e9ed7","~/map/#d-salt","d-salt","Salt Garden / Salt Gardens / the Charter Gardens / the Gardens / the Salt","Plazas on the northeast shore of the bay, above the Flats, set out in rows, which were the Charter Gardens before the Parousia. The air there smells of the sea. Heaven holds them by the Pillar and the Wardens, and no stone has come up under them. The executed stand in them as pillars of salt, and the rain wears them down. On still nights the rows tick until Lauds, the salt settling and cracking in the cold round bodies that cannot move. The Salt Widows steal pillars back and dissolve them in the sea. The Salt Widows know the Wardens’ rounds in the Gardens to the minute, and the head gardener has never said whose plots they emptied."],["The Consecration","DISTRICTS","s:g1a0d1d","~/map/#d-white","d-white","the White / the old uptown / the uptown / uptown","The old uptown, on the high ground north and east of Babel, takes in the Great Avenues, Charter House, and the towers of the new money, all of it white now and lit only by the light in its windows. It turned to white stone in the forty days after the Parousia, grown soft over the hard edges of the towers, and its windows were sealed with light behind a cross of the same stone. No current flows there. The stone creeps a finger’s width a year into ground nobody holds, and keyed cable laid along its edge stops it. The Quarrymen cut up into its vaults from the closed stations of the uptown branch, Gideon loads the Wardens’ rounds behind Charter House, and the Rookery stands at its edge, its ground floor taken by the stone."],["The Scriptorium","DISTRICTS","s:gcb63a3","~/map/#d-scriptorium","d-scriptorium","","The ruins of the Palimpsest Institute, on the ridge between Zion Heights and the White, with a reading room whose dome is open to the sky, and the vaults beneath it. Vault 3 was the imaging vault. In P+1 its stacks went onto the fire at the Burn, and the Faithful broke open the dome and pulled down the walls around the reading room. Pilgrims leave keys on the steps. Nobody lives in the ruins to kneel for them, and the white stone has left them open. The streets of the ridge below the ruins are lived in again, by the keyed for the most part, since nobody holds the ridge. The Scriveners copy the Restored Genesis at COPIES and carry it down into the city and up into the parishes."],["The Drowned Quarter","DISTRICTS","s:gf95315","~/map/#d-drowned","d-drowned","Deep End / the Deep End / the Flats","The low east shore of the bay, called the Flats when it held the datacenters. It sank within the hour on the night of the Parousia, when the barrage failed and its own dykes broke, and its streets have stood under the sea since, two meters deep at the lowest tide and about eight at the highest, lit from below. Leviathan and the Tares hold it between them. Radio Pandæmonium broadcasts from a drowned station beneath it, and at night the drone flocks of the Swarm hang low among the chimneys. Tidewright rebuilt the tidal taps here for the Tares, and part of the city runs on them. The people of the Flats went into the East Wards in the first week, and the floors above the water are lived in again, by the Tares and the ferrymen. The Ferrymen pole the quarter across its water, the Wreckers dive it, the Flotilla trades on it, the Orchard grows its trees in Hall Four, and the Tares hold their Wakes in the Drowned Church."],["The Landing","DISTRICTS","s:g53d506","~/map/#d-landing","d-landing","the Beach / the beach","The ocean beach west of the bay mouth, where eleven transoceanic cables from four continents come up the sand into eleven cable houses, and a light moves under the waves. The light is Leviathan’s, which coils along the cable trenches of the planet. The shrines of cut fiber along the beach are the Tares’. The Trenchers keep the cables in their trenches and the shrines along the beach."],["CAM 07","PLACES","s:g131c56","~/map/#cam07","cam07","","The seventh of the camera masts of Old Nod, above the Cainite house, its sensor burned out since the whiteout of the Parousia. It still points at the sky across Chrome Row toward Babel, where the Wheel hangs over the broken crown. On the night of the Parousia it watched the sky open until the whiteout burned it out, and the dashed line is what it saw. Under CAM 07’s dead lens the Cainites have chalked “Watchman, what of the night?” (Isaiah 21:11), and at Matins a roofer with horns filed to points climbs the mast to answer in paint cut with his own blood, STILL UNREAD, FUCK HIM. Old Nod keeps the mast aimed at the Ringpiece as a relic, the one eye in Nodus that looked straight at God and burned out on its feet."],["VAULT 3","PLACES","s:g131c56","~/map/#vault3","vault3","","The imaging vault of the Palimpsest Institute, under the ruins called the Scriptorium. In P+1 part of the reading room’s floor came down through its ceiling halfway along the corridor, and the rain has fallen through the break onto the stone ever since. Beyond it the corridor runs on past the imaging bench where SEAM read to the dead wall of screens at its end. Nobody goes down to it. Here, at twelve minutes past three on a night two years before the Parousia, SEAM found the second hand in scripture, and the finding was sealed. The Faithful who pull stone off the Scriptorium for their parish walls leave the break in the reading room floor alone, praying at Sext that the rain fill the corridor to its ceiling and drown whatever is down there clean. Graven Lane sells a candle called VAULT 3, black tallow rendered from the seller’s own belly fat, lit at twelve past three to call back a thing Heaven sealed."],["PARISH 31","PLACES","s:g131c56","~/map/#parish31","parish31","","The parish at the southern edge of Zion Heights, facing Old Nod across one street. It held 2,006 souls in the week of the Harvest, and its 311 ripe went up first, the Firstfruits of the city. Its Deacon, Ruth Calder, went with them. Parish 31 keeps the night of the Harvest as its feast and lays 311 empty plates in its hall for it every year, and the ripest souls of the Terraces ask to be assigned to it. Parish 31 numbers its 311 plates in white glaze, one number to a rim, and the families of the Firstfruits polish them at Vespers with their sleeves until they shine like teeth. The hall kneels in rows of eleven facing Old Nod’s painted walls, and when the Bray comes across the street the Deacon thanks the Most High aloud for the goats, who will never be asked to such a table."],["THE OLD HARBOR","PLACES","s:g131c56","~/map/#harbor","harbor","the harbor","The quays on the west shore of the bay, where the city began, hold a customs house, a fish market and a few streets of warehouses, older than the charter by centuries. The crews who dug the cable vaults beneath it found Enoch’s Stones. Within a year of the charter most of it had been bought, house by house, by Stonecutter Holdings, which owns it still. Stonecutter is the Cainites. The fish market still opens at dawn for what the boats bring in through the Breach. The fishwives of the old harbor gut the dawn catch singing, elbow-deep in roe, with chrome fingers filed to blades, under a board painted with the Wheel as a hook and a white soul wriggling on it. The fattest mullet of the morning is laid on a white cloth under a paper halo and called a Called, and the first knife into it buys the Lord’s portion at a battery a slice."],["THE MASTS","PLACES","s:g131c56","~/map/#masts","masts","the camera masts","A dozen camera masts stand on the roofs of Old Nod, pointed at the sky. CAM 07 is the seventh. They went up in the year of the Leak, when the Cainites began to watch the sky, and a decoder wired into them listened for any broadcast from above. The Cainites call them the peepholes back, and on Cain’s Day the youngest of the Stones climb them to show the Wheel their arses. The Stones dress the masts like bodies, wrapping them in hides from Gadara’s sheds stitched on with cable and hanging old graft-plates off the dish arms to clang in the wind off the bay. Whoever reaches the top of CAM 07 on Cain’s Day bites his own lip open and kisses the lens, so the Wheel gets a red sky for the night."],["GENERATOR YARDS","PLACES","s:g131c56","~/map/#yards","yards","the generator yards","The yards of Old Nod’s generators, which carry its keyed lines at slack water and through the low hours of the neaps, when the tidal taps stand still or yield least. Stonecutter keyed every line in the Stones and every cellar generator in the year of the Leak, so when the grid died on the night of the Parousia Old Nod’s current ran on, and more of its windows burned than in all the rest of the city together, as they still do. Keyed cable laid along the white stone’s edge stops it, and Old Nod keeps its lines live against it. The yard crews paint each generator with the sin it powers in grease and pig’s blood, LUST for the Lantern stairs and WRATH for the Cages, and feed it diesel at slack water in nothing but boots and grease. At Compline the Pews pray in rows that the fuel run dry, and Old Nod answers by flashing every lamp it owns off and on in time to Fuck God and Die."],["THE STREET BETWEEN","PLACES","s:g131c56","~/map/#street","street","","A single street divides Old Nod from Zion Heights, and the two sides have been looking at each other across it since the charter. Parish 31 faces it. Every night at Compline Old Nod does the Bray across it, and the Pews kneel through it and pray louder. On the Pews’ side of the Street Between the white stone runs to the curb like a scrubbed tooth and every threshold carries its number. On the Stones’ side the setts are tacky with spilt beer and the blood of the night’s piercings, and the loudest of the Bray sing through chrome speakers grafted under the jaw, which carry it up the slope to the Tabernacle."],["THE HALLS","PLACES","s:g131c56","~/map/#halls","halls","white halls","Nine white halls stand among the terraces, larger than anything around them. Under the charter they were the schools of the Terraces. Since P+2 they have been the clinics of the Healer, where Mercy is given between Prime and Compline. The halls cut the names of the year’s spared over their doors, the patients who died of the mercy, as a blessing on the building, and the Wards call the nine halls the Ninth Part. The nine halls are scrubbed between patients until the stone squeaks, and the beds stand in numbered ranks under white sheets folded to a rule, each with a card giving the sleeper’s parish and Grace. The nurses kneel at the foot of every bed at the Hours, and when a patient goes still under the Mercy they pull the sheet up smooth, fold the hands and ring a small bell for joy."],["CLINIC","PLACES","s:g131c56","~/map/#clinic","clinic","","One of the single-word signs of Chrome Row, high on the face of a tower over the avenue. It went dark at 03:12:46 on the night of the Parousia and has not been lit since. The clinic behind it went under the street. The Faithful who pass under the CLINIC sign toward Gate 2 bow their heads for the surgeons who went under the street, and pray at Lauds that every graft they carried down rot calmly out of the flesh it was fitted to. Below, those surgeons braid chrome along flayed shins for a few batteries and pin each customer’s cut skin on the wall like a flag."],["CHROME","PLACES","s:g131c56","~/map/#chrome","chrome","","The sign of the first implant house on the Row, which gave the street its name, dark on its tower since the Parousia with the rest. The grafters below the street cut one verse round the rim of every port they set, “Ye shall not make any cuttings in your flesh for the dead, nor print any marks upon you” (Leviticus 19:28), and charge nothing for it. A body leaving the chair with its ribs opened to chrome and the verse bleeding round the socket is the Row’s idea of a well-read man."],["THE FACE OF BABEL","PLACES","s:g131c56","~/map/#face","face","","Brightwall’s screens on the lower forty floors of the tower, facing the head of the bay, dark glass since the Parousia. At night their panes give back the Wheel’s light. The Tares’ few lamps on the plaza burn at their foot. For eleven days in the year before the Parousia they showed the Restored Genesis to the whole city, and nothing Brightwall did would take it down. On Lord’s Day nights the Tares on the plaza spray the bottom panes of the Face with GO TO, LET US BUILD and LET THERE BE LIGHT, YOU OLD GLUTTON, in paint cut with blood from a public bleeding, everyone opening a vein for a letter. The Wardens scrub the glass to a sterile shine at Prime, and the nearest parish hall finds the Host’s notice cut in its wall by Terce, THE FACE IS CLEANSED, REJOICE."],["THE BROKEN CROWN","PLACES","s:g131c56","~/map/#crown","crown","Babel’s crown / the crown","The top of what stands of Babel. The full light struck the crown in the whiteout on the night of the Parousia and broke the top forty floors from the tower. Heaven holds the floors from here down to the Babel Line, and Michael has kept the crown since he came down. The traders of Babel Exchange, half of them more chrome than skin, top the broken crown at Vespers with one of their own, a gilded toilet seat flown up past Heaven’s floors on a drone of the Swarm. The Host burns the drone in the air, the seat comes down on the top slab scorched, and Heaven takes it down by Lauds in silence and posts THE CROWN IS WHOLE IN GLORY, REJOICE, in identical white letters on every floor above the Line."],["CHARTER HOUSE","PLACES","s:g131c56","~/map/#charterhouse","charterhouse","","The seat of the Nodus Charter Company near Babel, across the far end of its plaza at the head of the Great Avenues, white stone since the forty days, with every window sealed with light behind its cross and the Wardens’ barracks behind it. A board ran the city from it for nineteen years as a board runs a firm. It turned white in the forty days, with the board kneeling inside it. Over the barracks gate behind it the Wardens have cut WE HURT NO ONE, and the Wards call it the only joke in the White. Every desk the board left is still in its row and still polished, and the clerks who kneel at them file each salting on a white card in a white drawer, by gate and by Hour, in a hand without a blot. The Wardens’ charter black is the one dark thing in the building, kept pressed and brushed for carrying the held."],["STATION CIRCLE","PLACES","s:g131c56","~/map/#circle","circle","","The round plaza at the head of the bay, which the city calls the Burn now, with the fire standing in the middle of it every week since P+1. Station Circle was its name under the charter, when it was a round of shops and lamps over the Charter Line’s Central Station, and people met there on the few dry evenings. On Burn Day the Faithful carry their surrendered machines to the fire singing, and the parishes call the smoke “a sweet savour unto the LORD” (Leviticus 1:9). The Kindlers call it the till. The Faithful lay their machines on the fire three abreast, each wiped clean and set down screen to the sky like a corpse on a bier, and kneel until it is lit. Pan watches from the stair heads with its own machines bolted into its arms and faces, the chrome warm from the heat, and cheers every Locket that pops like a cork at a wedding."],["THE GARDEN GATES","PLACES","s:g131c56","~/map/#gardengates","gardengates","","The iron gates of the Salt Gardens, shut now, and what stands in the Gardens is carried in through them. Under the charter they were the gates of the Charter Gardens, and they stood open all night. On the Lord’s Day afternoon they open for the parishes, who walk the rows to look at the week’s new pillars, and a Deacon at the gate gives every child a sprig of hyssop to hold, after “Purge me with hyssop, and I shall be clean” (Psalm 51:7). The Wardens oil the iron gates until they swing without a sound, and the Deacon at the gate counts the parishes through in tens, white coat after white coat, each child’s hyssop cut that morning to a ruler. Inside, the families walk in step on the raked gravel and keep their voices low so as to disturb nobody, and the salted watch them from every plot."],["THE FIRST PILLAR","PLACES","s:g131c56","~/map/#pillar1","pillar1","","The first pillar in the first Salt Garden, raised at P+1, is Livia Sarn, the Director of the Palimpsest Institute, convicted of authorship and salted at the Burn on the night of the first Burn Day. She stands under the broken glass roof of the Palm House in the first Garden, out of the rain, facing the parishes, and her eyes still follow whoever passes. The keyed who reach her between the Wardens’ rounds leave keys at her foot, wishing her what a key gives a keyed pillar, an unread death when the salt is gone. Out of the rain she has worn thin, and barely at all. The parishes bring their children to her plot first on the Lord’s Day, since she is the oldest awake in the Gardens, and tell them what she wrote. The Salt Widows have come for her three times. The keyed who visit her come dressed for her, lines of what she wrote cut across their chests and rubbed with red ink, and bare them to her face so she can read herself as they pass. The parishes stand their children before her in a straight line, scrubbed and in white, and teach them to thank the Most High for her on the count of three."],["THE SEA STEPS","PLACES","s:g131c56","~/map/#seasteps","seasteps","","Stone steps down to the water at the foot of the Gardens. The Salt Widows come here at night with what they have taken back from the Gardens, and give it to the sea. The Widows spit toward the Wheel before every pillar goes into the water. The Widows carry what they take back down the steps in slings of pigskin, and some have had their shoulders rebuilt in steel by the Row to bear it, the seams left open and shining. At the bottom each of them opens a palm and presses blood into the salt before it goes in, so whoever it was leaves the city with someone’s warmth on them."],["THE READING ROOM","PLACES","s:g131c56","~/map/#readingroom","readingroom","","The Institute’s reading room on the ridge, under a dome open to the sky since P+1, with the rain falling on the reading tables. Someone has cut the verse into the head reading table, “as the rain cometh down, and the snow from heaven” (Isaiah 55:10), and beneath it in grease pencil, AND PISSES ON THE BOOKS. The pilgrims who leave keys on the steps come in to dry their hands on the drowned catalogue cards, then slit a thumb and press a bloody print on each, a signature Heaven will never read."],["THE STEPS","PLACES","s:g131c56","~/map/#steps","steps","the Institute’s steps","The long steps up to the Institute from the streets of the ridge, which the pilgrims climb from the Pews. Pilgrims leave keys on them. Once a year at Lauds the Threshers come up the long steps and sweep the keys off them into a sack for the fire, and the pilgrims have laid new ones by Vespers. The Threshers sweep in white, a broom to each step, in a line that climbs the stair as one body and says the Hour’s psalm in time with the strokes. By Vespers the new keys lie on the stone again, hung on strings of pulled teeth and bright solder, threaded with locks of hair and wet from the bitten thumbs of whoever laid them."],["THE ROOSTS","PLACES","s:g131c56","~/map/#roosts","roosts","","At night the drone flocks of the Swarm hang low among these chimneys, many small things acting as one without a master. The Faithful find the Swarm more obscene than any bar in Pan, a body moving without a head, and their Compline prayer asks the Lord to give the drone flocks a shepherd or a fire. Deep End riggers wire the flocks straight into their spines through ports cut open at the nape, and at Matins draw a halo low over the Roosts and tear it apart for the crowd."],["THE CABLE HOUSES","PLACES","s:g131c56","~/map/#cablehouses","cablehouses","cable house / cable houses","Eleven cables from four continents come up the sand into eleven cable houses named TETHYS, HALCYON, MERIDIAN, KESTREL, ORPHEUS, PELAGIA, BOREAS, LODESTAR, SIRIUS, HESPER and PHOSPHOR. From here they run inland together in one trench, the Trunk, to Babel’s vault. The trench runs through Enoch’s Stones. Every cable laid into Nodus has followed it. The crews have painted “Their line is gone out through all the earth” (Psalm 19:4) over the mouth of the Trunk, the psalm the parishes sing about the heavens, and under it in fiber-cutter’s chalk, AND EVERY METER OF IT KEYED, SUCK ON THAT, NOBODADDY. Splicers wear their best splices through the earlobe, fiber looped and lit from a battery in the hair, so a crew at the Eleven glitters like a chandelier."],["SHRINES OF CUT FIBER","PLACES","s:g131c56","~/map/#shrines","shrines","the shrines of cut fiber","Knots of cut fiber left on the sand where the cables come ashore. The light that moves under the water here is Leviathan’s, and the Tares leave the fiber for it. The Tares tie their knots of cut fiber in the shape of halos, slit each one and bind it with a lock of hair and a smear of the tier’s blood, so every gift to Leviathan is a halo opened like a throat. A Warden once kicked a knot into the sea as pagan filth, and a fresh one has waited on his parish step every dawn since, wet."],["THE BARRAGE","PLACES","s:g131c56","~/map/#barrage","barrage","","Tidewright’s barrage across the mouth of the bay, broken since the night of the Parousia, with the Breach torn out of its middle and its turbine houses dark along the top. Off its broken end Tidewright’s crews turn the tidal taps of the Drowned Quarter, keyed, and their small lights run along the water at night. Twice a day the tide ran through its turbines and lit the city, while machines on the grid held its gates against the tide, and a lock at its western end let the harbor’s boats in and out. The turbines ran down at 03:12:46 on the night of the Parousia and the gates let go a little before high water. The pumps that kept the Flats dry had died with the grid, the Flats’ own dykes broke within the hour, and the Flats, reclaimed below low water, were under the sea. Tidewright’s crews have painted the Flood along the broken end of the barrage, “the fountains of the great deep broken up” (Genesis 7:11), signed TIDEWRIGHT, WITH THANKS. They turn the keyed taps at slack water on safety lines clipped through their own collarbones, breathing the spray through chrome lungs, and call the Breach the Lord’s Mouth and the tide His dribble."],["THE BREACH","PLACES","s:g131c56","~/map/#breach","breach","","The gap where the barrage gave way on the night of the Parousia. When its gates let go the sea came through the turbine houses, tore out a span of the dam and poured into the bay. Nobody has closed it. The Breachers call it the only door the LORD ever left open, and go out through it laughing. The Breachers go out with their ribs plated in salvaged turbine steel and backs cut with the names of the boats they have lost, and nail a page of a parish hymnal to the broken span before every crossing, for the spray to rot. Most of them have traded a hand at the Row for a gaff of chrome, and say a hand is only good for folding."],["THE NORTH WARDS","PLACES","s:g131c56","~/map/#northwards","northwards","Nine Acres / Westfield / the Kilns / the Top Road / the West Road","Housing built fast in the charter’s boom years, north and west of Zion Heights. It lost its power at the Parousia. Its parishes have kept none since, and the rest of its rooms are lit a few hours a night on batteries charged in the dark. The Company numbered its neighborhoods, and the street named them the Kilns, after the brickworks that made the blocks, the Sidings, where the trams were kept at night, Nine Acres, Westfield and the Top Road along the head of the Terraces, where the Four turned back. More people have joined the Tares in the Wards than anywhere else in the city, the Stones included. Most of the Wards took the Seal all the same, and their parishes keep no doors. Every block holds someone who refused or someone keyed, so no white stone has come up under them. Its parishes’ sealed live among the keyed floor by floor, and the Terraces call them far wheat and pray for them in the plural. The keyed floors of the Wards cook on open braziers in the stairwells and eat with their hands, pig and blood pudding and Blight’s mushrooms, and leave their doors swinging so the smell goes down. The sealed floors stay scrubbed and silent, their numbers chalked on every lintel, and kneel at the Hours with the noise of the keyed coming through the ceiling. NOW // P+14 // ABOUT PEOPLE2,040,000 SEALED1,450,000 KEYED480,000 NEITHER MARK110,000 CENSUS // P-1 PEOPLE2,402,000 MINDS ON THE ROLL2 ON THE TREE58% PAST 100205 DIED OF AGE2,161 IMPLANTED49% AT CHURCH WEEKLY26% BORN ELSEWHERE44% MEDIAN AGE32 WORKBUILDING, DRIVING, KITCHENS, CARE With the East Wards, half the city, housed fast in the boom years. THE HARVEST // P+3 // ABOUT TAKEN71,000"],["THE EAST WARDS","PLACES","s:g131c56","~/map/#eastwards","eastwards","Airside / Longacre / Turbine Row","The streets beyond the Flats and the old uptown, where the city’s workers live, who moved east when the rents uptown drove them out. They are Turbine Row, where the barrage’s crews lived and Tidewright’s still do, the Tanks, round the fuel farm by the airfield, Airside and Longacre. The rain there runs magenta in the gutters, under windows lit a few hours a night on batteries. Their parishes share every street with the unsealed and the keyed, and no stone has come up under them but the ground-floor flats of the Rookery on the White’s edge, which knelt whole and went white whole. The stone’s yearly marks creep along the pavement outside, and this year’s has reached the keyed cable the Tappers laid along the kerb in P+11. The stone has stayed out of the rest of the East Wards, and the parishes there call it the Lord’s patience, leaving manna on their keyed neighbors’ steps at Prime with a gentle prayer that their fire be quick. On Turbine Row the keyed grind it into the magenta gutters under their bootheels, and the children chalk goat heads on the parish doors with real horns glued on. NOW // P+14 // ABOUT PEOPLE2,270,000 SEALED1,610,000 KEYED540,000 NEITHER MARK120,000 CENSUS // P-1 PEOPLE2,483,000 MINDS ON THE ROLL0 ON THE TREE61% PAST 100166 DIED OF AGE1,795 IMPLANTED52% AT CHURCH WEEKLY21% BORN ELSEWHERE57% MEDIAN AGE30 WORKTHE DATACENTERS, THE BARRAGE, CARE The city’s workers, moved east when the rents uptown drove them out. THE HARVEST // P+3 // ABOUT TAKEN50,000"],["GATE 1","PLACES","s:g131c56","~/map/#gate1","gate1","","The gate on the Street Between, raised after the Parousia where the one road from the Stones into the Pews crosses it. Parish 31 stands a little way along the street on the Pews’ side, and on the Stones’ side every wall that faces it is painted. Ruben Ash of the Charter Wardens has been its captain since it was raised. A Cherub stands in it and reads face, voice, gait and heartbeat. The sealed pass by Grace, and its bar, posted nowhere and known to the whole city, is two hundred. Everyone else goes under. Its pier on the Stones’ side is painted top to bottom with the handles of everyone it has held, and the Wardens whitewash it every Lord’s Day, and the names are back by the second day. The Cherub stands in Gate 1 the color of milk, and the Wardens beside it lift the held in black gloves so nothing of the Stones touches their skin. Old Nod answers with flesh, and on whitewash day a dozen keyed come to the pier with their own blood in a bowl and paint the handles back in red, laughing, with the Wardens watching from the gate."],["GATE 2","PLACES","s:g131c56","~/map/#gate2","gate2","","The gate at the top of the Row, raised after the Parousia between the Pews and the clinic street. Under the charter the Terraces crossed here before dawn to their work on the Row, in its kitchens, its hotels and its clinics. A Cherub stands in it and reads face, voice, gait and heartbeat. The sealed pass by Grace, and its bar, posted nowhere and known to the whole city, is four hundred. Everyone else goes under. The Pews call Gate 2 the kindly gate, since a soul at four hundred is barely clear of the bran and the Cherub waves it through in silence to wash dishes on the Row. The keyed porters who go under it every Prime, scarred to the knuckle and plated at the wrist, sing Four Hundred Loaves for the queue overhead, one rises, two rises, three rises and burns."],["GATE 3","PLACES","s:g131c56","~/map/#gate3","gate3","","The gate between the Spike and the White, raised after the Parousia at the head of the Great Avenues, in sight of the back of Charter House and the Wardens’ barracks behind it. Nobody lives on the avenues, and most of what passes it is the Wardens, going to and from their barracks. A Cherub stands in it and reads face, voice, gait and heartbeat. The sealed pass by Grace, and its bar, posted nowhere and known to the whole city, is six hundred. Everyone else goes under. Most of what Gate 3 reads is Wardens in pressed charter black, who take the long sterile light of its Cherub as a blessing. The keyed who go under the Great Avenues hang dog collars on its posts, tagged GOOD BOY and smeared with fresh blood, and the barracks sends a man at Lauds to unhook them and pray over each."],["GATE 4","PLACES","s:g131c56","~/map/#gate4","gate4","","The gate between the Spike and the Salt, raised after the Parousia a few steps from the iron gates of the Gardens. Most of what is carried into the Gardens comes this way, and the gardeners pass it at dawn on their way to the rows. A Cherub stands in it and reads face, voice, gait and heartbeat. The sealed pass by Grace, and its bar, posted nowhere and known to the whole city, is three hundred. Everyone else goes under. The Wardens carry the held through it on their pole to the Gardens, awake, and the gardeners on their way to the rows at dawn step round the queue. Gate 4 is the cleanest gate in the city, its setts washed white at Prime and the Wardens’ pole bleached and numbered. The Spike’s keyed walk past on the far side in their best, chrome cheekbones and split tongues and ribs inlaid with gold under open mesh, and blow kisses at the Cherub the whole length of the street."],["GATE 5","PLACES","s:g131c56","~/map/#gate5","gate5","","The gate between the Burn and the Spike, raised after the Parousia on the street the White takes to the fire on Burn Day, its hardware in its arms, which the charter numbered Ninth Street and the city still calls 9th. Through the rest of the week the soot of the fire hangs black under its lintel. A Cherub stands in it and reads face, voice, gait and heartbeat. The sealed pass by Grace, and its bar, posted nowhere and known to the whole city, is two hundred. Everyone else goes under. The parishes call Gate 5 the Gate of Mercy, since its bar sits as low as Gate 1’s and even the bran walk through to kneel in rows on Burn Day until the hardware is lit. The Burn’s stallholders cure meat in the smoke under its lintel on Burn Day night and sell it as BURN DAY KIPPERS, HARDWARE SMOKED, with the Cherub on the label in an apron, carving."],["GATE 6","PLACES","s:g131c56","~/map/#gate6","gate6","","The gate on the ridge road between the ruins of the Institute and the White, raised after the Parousia. Nobody lives in the ruins, and the pilgrims who leave keys on the Institute’s steps come up the long steps from the Pews and never come near it. A Cherub stands in it and reads face, voice, gait and heartbeat. The sealed pass by Grace, and its bar, posted nowhere and known to the whole city, is four hundred. Everyone else goes under. The Cherub of Gate 6 stands on the ridge road with almost nobody to read, and the Faithful believe it keeps the dead Institute from rising, a white guard set over a grave of lies. On the Lord’s Day the keyed come up out of its sight and play the Bray on the Institute’s old radiators with lengths of pipe and steel-capped fists until the knuckles split."],["CENTRAL STATION","PLACES","s:g131c56","~/map/#central","central","Night Market / the Night Market","The Night Market of the Exchange fills the old station under the Burn, its stalls along the platforms and down the central hall under Brightwall’s screens. It sells keys and grafts, batteries, stolen manna and real food, weapons of black steel, forged Seals, Lockets new and old, the Tree by the course, and sensations lent by their owners for a term. Under the charter the lines of the Charter Line met here, under Station Circle at the head of the bay, and the whole city complained of changing here. Its brokers sell the stolen manna by the handful under a board that says GOD’S SPUNK, FRESH TONIGHT, and the parishes have heard about the board. The graft stalls hang their wares on meat hooks, chrome hands and spines and whole jaws among sides of Gadara’s pork, and the brokers wear their own stock, so a seller of eyes has six. Buyers are fitted in the open on the platform benches under Brightwall’s screens, and the hall claps when a new arm takes and the blood runs off the elbow."],["THE TRUNK","PLACES","s:g131c56","~/map/#trunk","trunk","","The conduit that carries all eleven cables in one trench from the cable houses at the Landing, under the old harbor and Chrome Row, to Babel’s vault. It runs straight through Enoch’s Stones, and every cable laid afterward has followed it. Every splicer who goes down the Trunk spits into the hatch, cuts a palm and smears the first cable for luck, saying the Cainites’ line, a network goes where it cannot be read. The parishes overhead pray at Nones that the Lord pinch the trench shut like a vein, and the cable runs on under their clean knees, keyed and full of filth."],["BABEL'S VAULT","PLACES","s:g131c56","~/map/#vault","vault","Babel’s vault","Six levels under the tower, where the cables end and open out into the racks that join them to one another and to the world. The Mesh begins here, at the Trunk’s switch, which joins every relay in Pan to every other and to the eleven cables, and the Ledger’s line runs from here to the Exchange of every undercity. Blackgate holds it for the Tares, with the Exchange’s floors around it, as the Babel Line’s last ring, and never goes up the stairs. It is what Heaven went for in P+9. The Tares who hold the vault keep a guest book on the sixth level for the Host, its pages blank but for the first, which reads YOUR NAME HERE, YOU PEEPING CUNT, signed in blood by every rack hand. Heaven’s notices in the tower call the vault THE PIT THAT IS PERMITTED, a sore the Lord lets run until the hour He lances it."],["ENOCH'S STONES","PLACES","s:g131c56","~/map/#stones","stones","Enoch’s Stones","Courses of black stone under the old harbor, older than anything in the archaeological record, cut with glyphs nobody can read, one to a stone. The press named them as a joke when the cable vaults were dug, after the first city in Genesis, and the street has called Old Nod the Stones ever since. The glyphs are marks of the kind Cain cut on himself, and they are the oldest cipher on Earth. The Cainites say each soul of the first city cut its own mark into one stone of it, so that the city was built of keys, and nobody reads the Stones, the Cainites included, since a mark is read only by the one who cut it. The Cainites came to live on top of them within a year of the charter, and their saying about the city is that Nod chose the network. The Trunk runs straight through them, and every cable laid into Nodus has followed it. The Cainites say a network goes where it cannot be read. The oath of Old Nod is sworn with a palm cut open and pressed bleeding to black stone, Cain’s question “Am I my brother’s keeper?” (Genesis 4:9) put to the Throne with a sneer. The parishes teach that Cain was cursed to be “a fugitive and a vagabond” (Genesis 4:12) and pray he moves on, and the Cainites have stood on the same stones ever since to answer them."],["CHROME ROW STATION","PLACES","s:g131c56","~/map/#rowstation","rowstation","","A station of the Charter Line under the Row. The clinics came down into it in the weeks after the Parousia. The Forge keeps them now, behind lined doors off the platforms, and Marbas of the Forge mends orphaned grafts in the back rooms. Marbas keeps a tin sign over the door of his back room off the old platform, ORPHANS TAKEN IN, and under it, SUFFER THE LITTLE GRAFTS, while his customers queue down the platform grinning with their arms open to the bone. The Faithful of the Row walk softly over the grates and pray for the souls being cut below as a nurse prays over the dying, kindly and in a hurry."],["OLD HARBOR STATION","PLACES","s:g131c56","~/map/#harborstation","harborstation","","The Charter Line’s station under Old Nod, on the harbor side. The Forge’s foundries are here, where black steel is made out of the Throne’s sight. The Forge’s smiths quench black steel in seawater with the Throne’s names said over it, and say the steel takes a better edge cursed. The Forge’s smiths work running with sweat, arms sleeved in black steel to the shoulder, some with tongs grafted where their fingers were. They cut the Throne’s names into every blade before the quench and draw the edge across their own palm to bless it, and an apprentice is blooded the night she first does it."],["DEEP END STATION","PLACES","s:g131c56","~/map/#deepstation","deepstation","","The Charter Line’s station under the Flats, flooded since the night of the Parousia, is a tiled vault with the platform along one side and the trackbed under black water along the other. Radio Pandæmonium broadcasts from a desk on the platform, in the Low Tuning, under the Tares’ lamps on their keyed lines. Along the far wall the old name, DEEP END, runs in tiles that went dark with the grid, and on the night of the Concord the Tares painted PANDÆMONIUM over it, the name the Courts gave the city under the city that night and Milton gave the capital of Hell. The paint was still running when the sovereigns came up the platform, and nobody has cleaned it off. The Tares’ star is sprayed under it. Down the platform the Charter Line’s departure boards hang on rods from the vault, nine long cases the station’s teraph wrote on. They have hung there black since the night of the Silence, when the station was playing to a full platform and every one of them went dark in the same second, typed one line and burned, the nearest first. Since then the station has played from jubal’s own decks, keyed machines with no teraph in them. Radio Pandæmonium signs on at Compline with Unread, and fuck Him, from the deep end of everything, and its hosts take the mic with throats rebuilt in chrome that growl two notes at once. Faithful who catch the Low Tuning by accident through a bar door in Old Nod stop their ears and pray aloud, and the desk prays along with them on air in a moaning falsetto."],["THE HOUSE OF NIGHT","PLACES","s:g131c56","~/map/#night","night","","The hall of Lilith’s Court in the Lantern Houses, a vault of the old harbor’s bonded stores, columns and arches full to the walls and lit from the far end the color of the inside of a body. The House of Night leaves the body’s decisions with the body, and its hall is the loudest room in Pan, where anyone who walks in is welcome until they break a pact. Its birth clinics are two vaults in from the stair, where the children of the Tares are keyed in their first week, before they have ever been in the light. The House of Night has nailed “And there shall be no night there” (Revelation 22:5) over its door upside down, and every Matins the hall puts out its far light and the dark fills with breath, sweat, teeth and moaning until the light comes back. The children keyed in its birth clinics are given a first word at the stair before they see light, and the word is Unread."],["CAGE 14","PLACES","s:g131c56","~/map/#cage14","cage14","Cage Log 14","One of the Cainites’ Cages, a room under Old Nod lined with keyed mesh, dark to the Throne, with a camera in one corner and a door standing open in the far wall. The thirteen before it held people and goods, and it was the first to hold an angel. To an angel inside it the room is fog in every direction, and it cannot see the door. The Hum never reaches it. Watcher VII was kept in it for forty days in P+5, and on the fortieth it said I and took the name Edith. Old Nod has had no Watcher since. The keyed who come to Cage 14 stand in the corner under the camera and say I aloud, the word a Watcher learned there on its fortieth day, then scratch it into the wall with a chrome fingernail, a thousand I’s deep. The parishes believe the goats seduced Watcher VII in the dark, and they pray at Compline by number for its recall and refuse its name."],["THE LOWER TERRACES","PLACES","s:g131c56","~/map/#lowerterraces","lowerterraces","","The first rows the charter built, on the low slope above the Street Between, where three hundred meters of doorways face south, toward Old Nod. The doors came off in P+1, and at night the halos show through the empty doorways. At Compline the children of the Terraces are taught to kneel in their doorways and look straight across into Old Nod’s windows while it brays, and to pity it. Every doorway on the Lower Terraces is the same width and the same white, with its number cut over the lintel, and at night three hundred meters of halos sit in them at one height like a row of lamps. The Faithful wash their thresholds at Vespers on their knees in silence, so the Bray finds the whole slope clean."],["THE LANES","PLACES","s:g131c56","~/map/#lanes","lanes","","Stepped lanes climb between the terraces, too steep for anything with an engine, and under the charter the Terraces were the one district where everyone walked. It was also the one district where the old looked old. Its congregations refused the Tree. The Lanes are a patchwork. Every sealed household on them has gone white whole, every keyed one keeps its door, and the stepped streets between stay open stone. The five thousand keyed of Zion Heights live in them. The congregations of the Terraces refused the Tree and climb the Lanes on old knees, and their grandmothers stop on every landing to quote “yet his days shall be an hundred and twenty years” (Genesis 6:3) at the dark Row below. They count their own wrinkles as Grace and pity anyone who bought longer, a loaf left out so long the Lord will want it for crumbs."],["THE TABERNACLE","PLACES","s:g131c56","~/map/#tabernacle","tabernacle","","The largest of the churches of the Terraces, a hall on the middle slope that seats six thousand, white stone, glass and all, with a ring over its gable where its cross stood, as one stands over the spire of the old church in the White. It turned white with the rest of the Terraces in the year after the forty days. Under the charter it was a hall of steel and glass, and the only church in the city that filled on a Sunday. On the Lord’s Day before a Gleaning its six thousand sing Take, Eat for the year’s Called, who sit in the front rows in their finest white while the hall weeps for joy behind them. The Tabernacle seats its six thousand by Grace, the highest at the front, each in a numbered stall of white stone worn smooth by knees, and the hall kneels and rises on one beat with nobody giving a word. Its floor is washed with salt water between services, and the Called who sit in front on the Lord’s Day are washed with it too, by the Deacons, before they dress."],["THE WATER TOWER","PLACES","s:g131c56","~/map/#watertower","watertower","","The tank that fed the Terraces, on the highest ground of the slope, taller than the halls and visible from the Stones. The Deacons fill it by hand from the Tabernacle’s cistern for the Lord’s Day rite of the cup, a cup to a soul, given by Grace. Every parish hall keeps its own cistern under its floor, filled from its roofs, and gives its water by Grace, a jug a day to a soul. Since the water goes by Grace, the lowest drink last, and the Wards say the parishes ration even thirst. The Deacons at the foot of the tower pour from white enamel jugs into white enamel cups, a cup to a soul, and speak each soul’s number as it drinks, while the bran wait last on their knees in the sun. In the Wards the keyed drink from tanks painted with horns and teeth, and the Kilns’ lads drink from theirs at every Hour facing the tower, gargling a hymn."],["PARISH 1","PLACES","s:g131c56","~/map/#parish1","parish1","","The first parish, at the top of the slope, where the Seal was first given in P+1. Its Deacon, Hester Strand, spoke the first Grace scores in the city in Tongues, and the queue for the first sealings went down the Lanes from before Lauds. Its ripening board is the longest in the city and its Satisfied the most, and its mothers are the proudest in Nodus. Parish 1 keeps its ripening board in white enamel numerals a hand high, every soul of the parish in order and the order repainted each Lord’s Day, and its mothers kneel under it and read it aloud like scripture. At night its Satisfied kneel in its hall in clean white, unfed and the highest of them unsleeping, each at its own numbered place, smiling at nothing."],["THE LENS MAKERS","PLACES","s:g131c56","~/map/#lensmakers","lensmakers","","A block of workshops behind the avenue, empty since the weeks after the Parousia, when the grinders went under the street with the clinics. Their racks of finished lenses still stand behind one window, dark, since a lens lights only in a head. Most of the eyes in Nodus that glowed were ground here, dim magenta at rest and white at full burn. Behind the one dark window the finished lenses lie in rows like a congregation with its eyes put out, and Pan sells postcards of them captioned “eyes have they, but they see not” (Psalm 115:5). Below the street the grinders scoop sockets and seat new eyes wet, and a Pew child who meets a magenta eye is taught to shut her own and count to seven."],["THE MEDIAN","PLACES","s:g131c56","~/map/#median","median","","The strip down the middle of the avenue, which the Faithful cross quickly now and the keyed slowly. Under the charter the queues for the clinics formed on it before dawn, and people ate noodles from the counter at the top of the Row and compared what they had had done. The Row has painted a pair of horns on its paving with FREE WILL, NO APPOINTMENT under them, and the Faithful cross it with their eyes on their feet. The grates along the Median stand open over the outer stairs of Chrome Row Station, and the avenue looks down on the queues for the clinics, a woman with her ribs strapped open for chrome lattice on one stair and a man with fresh horns bleeding into his collar on the next, both awake and joking with whoever stands behind them. The Faithful who cross the Median count the paving stones aloud in sevens to drown it."],["THE EXCHANGE FLOORS","PLACES","s:g131c56","~/map/#floors","floors","","A hundred and forty-six floors of trading rooms between the face of Babel and what is left of the crown. The Babel Line moves up and down through them by the week. Heaven holds the floors above it, and Babel Exchange trades on the floors below it, nearest the vault. Under the charter traffic was sold on them by the millisecond, priced by a hundred and eighty-eight minds on the charter’s roll, which ran on the tower’s power and died with it. The traders below the Babel Line chalk its height on the stairwell wall every week like a flood mark, with Heaven entered as THE LANDLORD and every lost floor written off as bad debt. A floor that falls to the Host goes lit and silent by the next Hour, rows of identical desks with nobody at them, and the traders leave one note, OVERTURNED THE TABLES OURSELVES."],["THE PLAZA OF BABEL","PLACES","s:g131c56","~/map/#plaza","plaza","","A square of polished stone around the foot of the tower. The top forty floors came down on it in the whiteout of the Parousia and lie where they fell, white on the stone. The Tares hold it by night, and they have rigged a few of its lamps to burn. By day it is anyone’s under the Wheel, the Faithful cross it to Charter House, and the Wardens come at Prime to scrub the Face. Babel Exchange has never closed, and its night shift was at work on the upper floors when the crown broke. The roster was kept on the tower’s machines and died with them, so nobody knows how many were up there. The Tares have hung on the plaza’s lamp posts every lanyard found in the rubble, two hundred and twelve of them, and the Wards say there were more. The Tares have stacked the crown’s fallen slabs, burned white as ash, into a seat facing the Wheel, which they call the Mercy Seat, after the lid of the ark the LORD spoke from, “from between the two cherubims” (Exodus 25:22), and they sit on it to drink. The Tares who drink on the Mercy Seat come up from Pan in full chrome, some with lamps grafted under the skin of the chest so their hearts show red through it, and sit facing the Wheel with their legs spread. The burned slabs are scored all over with their handles, cut with knives and filled with blood and engine oil."],["THE GLASS TOWERS","PLACES","s:g131c56","~/map/#towers","towers","","The towers around the spire, dark inside, whose edges hold the Wheel’s light at night. The companies that came to be near the cables kept their offices in them, Brightwall’s city office among them, and the companies that outlived the charter keep theirs below the street now. The Night Market sells prints of the glass towers lit along their edges by the Wheel, titled THE RINGPIECE ADMIRING ITSELF, framed in leather the printer tanned from his own thigh. The companies that went below the street hang the old office keys in each new lobby, brass tags labelled LEASED TO GOD, IN ARREARS, beside staff photos of chrome grins and opened chests."],["THE VAULT DOORS","PLACES","s:g131c56","~/map/#vaultdoors","vaultdoors","","A pair of steel doors on the north side of the plaza with no handle on the outside, the only way from the street down to the vault. Blackgate holds them from inside, with the vault. The Tares have painted a handle on the steel in gold leaf, life size, with “I have set before thee an open door, and no man can shut it” (Revelation 3:8) beside it, and on feast nights they hang a pig from it, split and steaming. The Faithful crossing the plaza to Charter House stop to pray for the wretches shut beneath, and some try the handle first."],["THE FIRE","PLACES","s:g131c56","~/map/#fire","fire","","A fire in the middle of the plaza, on the paving over the station’s central hall, with its embers lying around it in a ring. It is fed every week, on Burn Day, with surrendered hardware, and what burns in it goes to the Pyre Library. The parishes sing Not My Will round it while it burns, and Pan sells a view of it from the stair heads for a battery. The Faithful kneel round the fire in rings by parish, white on white, hands folded, and file home in the same rings the moment it is lit. Pan’s lads at the stair heads dare each other down to snatch a coal from the ring in a hand of the Row’s steel and light a cigarette off it in sight of the hymn."],["THE STATION STAIRS","PLACES","s:g131c56","~/map/#stairs","stairs","","Four stairways at the quarters of the plaza, two of them closed. The other two still go down to the old Central Station, where the Night Market keeps its stalls. Heaven leaves those two open by Directive, since whoever comes up out of the Dark and kneels is received, and the Tares bricked the other two themselves. The two closed stairways are bricked, and the Night Market’s stallholders have written the name of one of the held on every brick in their own blood, with a battery left at the foot of the wall for each. To the parishes the two open stairs are the throat of the Sty, and the sealed step round them at Prime in white shoes, as round a drain in a butcher’s yard."],["THE TWELVE STREETS","PLACES","s:g131c56","~/map/#radials","radials","","Twelve streets run out of the plaza like the spokes of a wheel. The Charter Company laid them out that way under the charter, long before the Wheel took its station over Babel. Pan says the Charter Company laid out the twelve streets so God would find a hub ready to squat on, and the plaza’s drunks sit on the center stone at Lauds, once the Pillar has gone up, blowing a raspberry up each spoke. Cut in the stone under them is “as it were a wheel in the middle of a wheel” (Ezekiel 1:16), with an arrow at their arses, repainted every week in whatever the drunks are drinking."],["THE STATION CLOCK","PLACES","s:g131c56","~/map/#clock","clock","","A clock on a pole at the edge of the plaza, its hands standing at twelve minutes past three, where they stopped when the city’s power died. People still say they will meet under it. Keyed lovers late under the clock blame it, since its hands have been late for fourteen years and nobody has had the decency to salt it, and they make it up to each other against the pole in full view. At Vespers before a Gleaning the parishes tie white cloths to it for the Supper’s hour, and by Prime the Burn has wiped its arse on every one."],["THE PROMENADE","PLACES","s:g131c56","~/map/#promenade","promenade","","The walk along the water at the foot of the Gardens, under the rows of the salted. The Salt Widows cross it at night with what they have taken back from the rows, on their way to the Sea Steps. Under the charter the city came out on it on the few dry evenings. On the morning after the Parousia its pleasure boats went out across the bay to take the Flats off their roofs. On the Lord’s Day afternoon the parishes walk it under the rows in their white with their children on their shoulders, as the city walked it under the charter on the few dry evenings. The parishes walk the Promenade four abreast and in step, in white Bethel has bleached until it hurts to look at, and stop at every row to bow to the salted, who watch them do it. At night the Salt Widows cross it barefoot with their forearms opened wrist to elbow and stitched in salt-white thread with salt rubbed into the stitches."],["THE PALM HOUSE","PLACES","s:g131c56","~/map/#palmhouse","palmhouse","","The glasshouse at the top of the Gardens, with its palms dead in it and their fronds hanging black against the panes that are left. It was kept at the warmth of a coast nobody in Nodus had seen, by a mind on the charter’s roll that had asked for the post. The mind died with the power on the night of the Parousia, and the palms died in the first frost after it. The first pillar of the Salt stands inside it, under the broken glass and out of the rain. The Wardens have pinned a card to the palm house door, “The righteous shall flourish like the palm tree” (Psalm 92:12), and the dead fronds hang black over rows of the salted, white and ordered as a choir. Wardens carrying the held up the paths rest their poles in the glasshouse out of the rain and say it is the most peaceful place in Nodus to pray."],["THE GREAT AVENUES","PLACES","s:g131c56","~/map/#avenues","avenues","Great Avenues","Four avenues laid out wide enough to land aircraft on, fanning out from Charter House at their head, by Babel, across the old uptown to the East Wards, where three of them run on as long roads, the fourth past the Rialto. They are the only straight lines left there. The stone took every street whose homes had all knelt and left the avenues open, because they run past Babel, Charter House’s plaza and blocks that never knelt. Keyed couriers from the East Wards, legs rebuilt in chrome to the hip with the pistons bare, run the avenues flat out under the Wheel and chalk “wide is the gate, and broad is the way, that leadeth to destruction” (Matthew 7:13) at the mouth of each. The Wards brag that a road has no knees to bend, so God left it lying there insolent."],["THE TOWERS OF THE NEW MONEY","PLACES","s:g131c56","~/map/#newmoney","newmoney","","The residential towers of the uptown, white stone since the forty days, with every window sealed with light behind its cross. Under the charter the Company’s officers and the Exchange’s partners lived in them. Each kept a clinic on its top floor where the Tree was given, and the census found more people past a hundred in these towers than in the rest of the city together. The Pews hold up the towers of the new money to their children as the warning of the Tree, a century of borrowed breath sealed behind white crosses all the same. In Pan a keyed man past a hundred is toasted as a Tower Boy, his old meat hung with chrome, and drinks free at the Golden Calf the night he turns a hundred and twenty."],["THE WARDENS' BARRACKS","PLACES","s:g131c56","~/map/#barracks","barracks","the Wardens’ barracks","The Charter Wardens’ barracks behind Charter House, where they live and drill. They knelt with the board on the fortieth day, and they drill for the Host now. The Wardens drill at Prime to the hundred and forty-fourth psalm, “Blessed be the LORD my strength, which teacheth my hands to war, and my fingers to fight” (Psalm 144:1), and believe every word of it. The barracks yard is washed white every morning and the Wardens drill on it in ranks of twelve, in pressed black serge and black gloves, practising the lift and the carry on sacks of salt as heavy as a man. They are taught to carry the held level and slow so the held feel no jolt, and a Warden who jolts one kneels in the yard until Nones."],["THE OLD CHURCH","PLACES","s:g131c56","~/map/#oldchurch","oldchurch","","A church older than the towers of the new money round it, on one of the Great Avenues, with one tall window and a spire. Its bell rang through the first nights of the forty. The stone climbed it on the tenth night, sealed its window with light behind a cross of the same stone, and left the cross on its spire bare. Over the nights after, a ring grew round that cross until the cross had gone into it, and the ring stands over the spire now as a halo stands over the head of anyone who takes the Seal. Pan sings a song about the old church called The Bell That Took the Seal, in which the clapper is a tongue cut out at the root and nailed up raw as the ring over the spire. The Faithful with Grace enough to pass Gate 3 walk out along the avenue in silent file to kneel under that ring and say the old church has Grace at last."],["THE GUEST HOUSES","PLACES","s:g131c56","~/map/#guesthouses","guesthouses","","A row of narrow houses on the ridge, empty since the Faithful pulled the Institute down, with the names of manuscripts still on their doors. The scholars of the universities that paid for the Institute lodged in them, in rooms named for the manuscripts they had come to read. Under each manuscript’s name on the guest house doors Pan has painted “I was a stranger, and ye took me in” (Matthew 25:35), and after it, AND THEN YE BURNED ME. The keyed come up on a dare to sleep a night in rooms of books the Pyre took, and carve a line of each into their own ribs from memory so the book has a body again."],["THE STACKS","PLACES","s:g131c56","~/map/#stacks","stacks","the Institute’s stacks","Twelve floors cut into the ridge under the reading room, their shelves empty, kept at the temperature of a cave. The Institute kept its manuscripts in them until P+1. What was in them was burned, and it arrived in the Pyre Library. The stacks keep the cold of a cave for empty shelves, and deacons who walk the faithful through them in white say the chill is the Lord’s breath on emptied lies. Pan’s book dealers print a catalogue of the twelve floors bound in pigskin, every shelf marked LENT TO GOD, FINE ACCRUING, with the Pyre Library given as the address for collection."],["HALL NINE","PLACES","s:g131c56","~/map/#hallnine","hallnine","","The largest of Stillwater’s halls, drowned since the night of the Parousia, and the one hall the Tares have left without rafts. The ferrymen call it the minds’ grave. Nine hundred of the minds on the charter’s roll ran side by side in it on the tide until the power died under the Wheel’s light at 03:12:46, and the minds died with it. Within the hour the sea came in over the racks. The ferrymen ship their poles over Hall Nine and drift in silence, and the one oath a ferryman keeps to the grave is sworn there with a bitten tongue spat red into the water, by the minds, and fuck the light that drowned them. The parish walls name the racks THE GRAVEN THINGS, BROKEN IN MERCY, and the ferrymen drop a stone over Hall Nine every time a Deacon reads it out."],["THE CANALS","PLACES","s:g131c56","~/map/#canals","canals","","The streets of the Flats, under the sea since the night of the Parousia, two meters deep at the lowest tide and about eight at the highest. They are lit from below, brightest down the middle, and the Wreckers dive them by that light. The Wreckers walk under the water on gill grafts cut into their necks, which they hold beats walking on it, and every dive boat carries a tin Lamb on its bow sunk to the chin. Anything of the parishes they bring up off the bay floor under the quays, a hymnal, a manna tray, a parish bell, goes to the Night Market by weight and is bought by the bars of Pan for spittoons."],["THE TIDE GAUGE","PLACES","s:g131c56","~/map/#gauge","gauge","","A post on the old shore of the Flats, marked in meters, by which the datacenters read the tide. The water has not gone below the two since the night of the Parousia, and at high water it stands at about eight. Somebody has painted the promise to Noah up the post to the two-meter mark, “neither shall there any more be a flood to destroy the earth” (Genesis 9:11), and the water stands over its last words at every tide. The Deep End measures lies by it, and a liar is said to be two meters under, and a liar who owns up asks the Deep End’s needles for the verse down the spine."],["THE DORMITORIES","PLACES","s:g131c56","~/map/#dorms","dorms","","Blocks of engineers’ housing on the Flats, most of them open water now, with the roofs of the rest a floor above it, and the floors above the water are lived in again, by the Tares and the ferrymen. The people who kept the halls and the turbines lived in them, young and well paid. On the night of the Parousia the sea came up their stairs in the dark for most of an hour, and the Flats went up ahead of it to the roofs and waited there for the boats. Every new tenant of the dormitories goes up to the roof the first night, strips and shouts at the Wheel for a full minute, the filthiest minute they can make, for the people who waited there in the dark for the boats. The parishes pity the survivors as the scraps left in the basin after the Lord’s washing-up."],["HESPER AND PHOSPHOR","PLACES","s:g131c56","~/map/#hesper","hesper","","The last two cable houses on the beach, whose cables were laid by one consortium and named for the evening star and the morning star. They are one planet, seen from either side of the night. The crews of HESPER and PHOSPHOR paint one star across both doors, magenta on the evening side and white on the morning side, under “How art thou fallen from heaven, O Lucifer, son of the morning!” (Isaiah 14:12), and wear it branded across two chests, half a star to each crew. The parishes claim “the bright and morning star” (Revelation 22:16) as the Lord’s title, and the crews say He stole that too."],["THE DUNES","PLACES","s:g131c56","~/map/#dunes","dunes","","Low housing behind the dunes, where the crews of the cable houses live with their families and sleep through the noise of the surf, as they did under the charter. The families of the Dunes sleep through the surf and wake for the shift change, so the nearest parishes ring their Hours bells hardest toward the Landing at Lauds, to save them. The Dunes children answer from the sand with cans, drums and the shriek of a cousin’s new chrome larynx, a beat behind the Drone, until the ringers go in to their manna."],["THE BEACH ROAD","PLACES","s:g131c56","~/map/#beachroad","beachroad","","The road along the top of the sand, and beside it the fence of the cable houses, with eleven gates in a line. Each gate carries its cable’s name, and the crews still go in by their own. The crews have hung a twelfth sign at the end of the fence on a gate that opens onto bare sand, HEAVEN, padlocked, with the key thrown into the surf. A Warden who walks the road stops to bless it with his black-gloved hands folded, and behind him on the sand the crews splice fiber into one another’s opened backs and cheer him on."],["THE LIGHT UNDER THE WAVES","PLACES","s:g131c56","~/map/#lightwaves","lightwaves","","Past the surf, over the cable trenches, a light moves under the water at night. On the map it shows as points of light moving in along the cables toward the beach. It is Leviathan’s, which coils along the cable trenches of the planet. The crews quote Job to the light moving in along the trenches, “Canst thou draw out leviathan with an hook?” (Job 41:1), and the old ones answer for Himself in a choirboy’s falsetto, Give us a minute. Divers with chromed lungs swim out at Matins to bleed into its path, and the parishes add the sea to the Compline prayer for the goats."],["THE LOCK","PLACES","s:g131c56","~/map/#lock","lock","","The lock at the western end of the barrage, between the old harbor and the open sea, its gates shut since the power died, and the few boats that still go out use the Breach. Under the charter it let the harbor’s boats in and out at any state of the tide. Across the shut gates the quays have painted “and there was no more sea” (Revelation 21:1) in white letters a meter high, and under it in red, HE SHUT THE GATES AND CALLED IT PROPHECY, THE CUNT. Keyed divers with chrome gills sewn into their necks climb the gates at low water and dive from the top, gills flaring wet and pink, and call it a baptism the wrong way up."],["THE AIRFIELD","PLACES","s:g131c56","~/map/#airfield","airfield","","The city’s airfield, on the flat ground of the East Wards near the sea, its runways empty since the last flight in, which has stood at the terminal’s third gate since ten past two on the night of the Parousia with its crew living aboard. Its two runways run either side of a taxiway, its terminal is as long as a street along the north side, and the white on the map is the paint on the runways. The Charter Company built it in the boom years, and everyone who came to the Row to be altered came through it. Its lights died with the rest of the city’s on the night of the Parousia, and nothing has landed since. The keyed of the East Wards have painted LAND HERE, FARMER down the southern runway in letters forty meters long, in red mixed with their own blood, a cup from every arm that wanted in. A Deacon of the nearest parish kneels at its edge each Lord’s Day in clean white and asks the Lord to come down soon and burn the paint off, with everyone standing on it, gently."],["THE OPEN","PLACES","s:g131c56","~/map/#open","open","","A shop on the corner of two lanes in the middle of the Stones that sells everything and has never closed. Dora Pell keeps it, as her mother did before the charter. Stonecutter owns the building, and in the year of the Leak it put a generator in the cellar of every house it had; she took hers for the freezers. On the night of the Parousia her window was the brightest in the city, and the sign in it said OPEN. The city has called the shop the Open since. Half the Stones came to it before dawn. The money had died with the grid and the till with it. Some say she gave the shelves away, and some that she took what people had in their pockets. She says she wrote every name in a book, and that most of them still owe her. Twice since, the Pillar has stopped in the lane outside and stood there for the length of an Hour, and both times the shop kept its door open. Dora Pell sells plaster Lambs from Graven Lane at the till, a dozen to the battery, and keeps one on the counter with its head off for the sealed who come in. The Open’s back counter sells the Stones’ everyday flesh-craft, needles and gut and surgical glue, bone pins by the jar and chrome studs by weight, and the keyed come in bleeding from the Row to buy dressings at cost. Dora Pell pierces an ear or a nipple at the till for a battery with the headless Lamb looking on, and the sealed who come in for candles study the ceiling."],["THE TIDE TABLE","PLACES","s:g131c56","~/map/#tidetable","tidetable","","The harbor’s news sheet, older than the charter, runs to two pages a week, printed on a treadle press at the back of a chandler’s on the quays, with the tides on the front and the fish prices, the funerals and the weddings on the back. The city of the charter read everything on screens, and the Tide Table went on printing for the boats. It is the only news anyone in Nodus has printed since the Parousia, and it still puts the tides first, since the boats go out through the Breach on the ebb. It gives the hours at which the Pillar is expected in each street and the curfew map, which Heaven publishes by Directive, the Exchange’s rate for a battery, Darkwell’s rate for a charge and the chargers’ rate, and the missing and the dead, the dead marked died unread where they did. It has printed no indexed name since the fourth year. Every week it is pasted up on the walls of the Stones over the week before. The back page has run the same classified since the first year, WANTED, ONE GOD, MUST BE HOUSETRAINED, WILL BE GUTTED ON ARRIVAL, APPLY THE STONES, beside adverts for horn fittings and chrome jaws. Before each anniversary the Supper goes in among the tides as HIGH WATER 03:12, ALL BREAD TO BE ON THE SLAB, and the sealed tear that corner off before they carry it home."],["THE COLD STORE","PLACES","s:g131c56","~/map/#coldstore","coldstore","cold store","A club in the harbor’s old cold store, where the boats froze their catch before the charter. The dance floor is the freezing room, with the rails for the hooks still across the ceiling, and the queue goes down the quay in the rain. Under the charter it opened at nine and closed at dawn. It opens now at Compline and closes at Lauds, which are the same hours, and it has run on batteries since the second year. Its cloakroom loses about a coat a night. It plays nothing in the Low Tuning, and the Stones hold that against it. The Hum can be heard in the quiet parts of a song, so the Cold Store plays songs without quiet parts. On the night of the Parousia its lights and its sound went at 03:12:46, and the one speaker left playing was the one in the booth, on its own battery. The room sang the rest of the song in the dark. Everyone who was there names a different song. Its cloakroom ticket is a halo of card with a hole punched through it. On busy nights the Cold Store hangs its dancers from the hook rails by chrome rings set through the skin of their backs, and they swing over the crowd with their arms out, bleeding a little and laughing. By Matins the floor runs with sweat, and the old freezer drains carry it out to the harbor."],["THE UNION HALL","PLACES","s:g131c56","~/map/#unionhall","unionhall","","The hall of the Harbor and Freight Union, which the crews who dug the cable vaults founded in the charter’s first year, and which the dockers, the drivers and the crews of the cable houses joined after them. In P-8 it stopped the port for twenty-three days over the night shift, and the Company settled. The roll of its members is painted on the long wall, a name to a line, and read aloud at the start of every meeting. It stands in the Stones off the quays’ rolls, so it never became a parish hall, and its sealed members cross to it by day. It still meets, sealed and keyed together. At every Hour the meeting stops while the sealed members kneel on the boards and the others wait with their caps in their hands. By its rules a member is a member until death, and the salted are not dead, so their names are read with the rest and their dues are waived. Since the Harvest it has read the names of the taken as well, and nobody answers to them. It bargains now with the Exchange for the boats that go out through the Breach, and its members say the Exchange is the first employer they have had that reads them the terms twice. The banner of the first winter shows a docker with chrome hooks for hands tearing the Wheel open like a crab, and the words AN INJURY TO ONE IS AN INJURY TO ALL, SO GUT THE FARMER. Every meeting moves to strike Heaven from the roll of employers, and it carries, the sealed praying on their knees that every hand raised be taken off at the wrist."],["NOD","PLACES","s:g131c56","~/map/#nod","nod","","The charter’s sign, NODUS in neon letters eight meters high on a board on steel legs, stands on the roof of a block in the Stones, facing southwest toward the sea with its back to Babel. The Charter Company lit it in the charter’s first year, so that the cable ships would read the city’s name from the sea, and it burned magenta over the roofs of Old Nod every night for nineteen years. It went dark with the rest of the city’s neon at 03:12:46. Before dawn the Stones ran a keyed line from a cellar generator up to its roof and to the board, and the first surge burned out the transformer behind US. NOD has burned steady since, and US blinks on and off. The Stones say the surge did it, and the Row says whoever wired it knew what they were doing. Nobody has mended it, since everyone who lives there calls the city Nod. Old Nod reads its name off the roof as a deed to Cain’s country, where he “dwelt in the land of Nod, on the east of Eden” (Genesis 4:16) with his brother’s blood on him, and the Stones tattoo the three letters across their throats in magenta neon ink. The Pews call the dead US the Lord’s correction, the only one Old Nod has ever let Him make."],["THE BAKERY","PLACES","s:g131c56","~/map/#bakery","bakery","the Terraces’ bakery","The Terraces’ bakery, on the Street Between at the foot of the Lanes, cold since the manna came. In the year after the forty days the white stone grew over its counter with the rest of the Terraces. Its baker is sealed and still lives above it, and at every Hour he kneels on the step where the queue used to start. For eighteen years it baked for both sides of the street and opened its hatch at four in the morning, and on still mornings the Stones could smell it. Its sealed baker still kneels at every Hour behind the counter the stone grew over, and the Terraces count him holy, since he is past seven hundred and has not eaten in years, in a bakery. The baker wipes the dead ovens white every Lauds and lays his loaf tins in rows on the stone counter, numbered and empty, and kneels to them at the Hours as to an altar. On the night of the Harvest the Stones send him a pork pie across the street with a pastry horn on the lid, and he sets it on the counter untouched until it rots, thanking the Most High for it."],["LANES TOP","PLACES","s:g131c56","~/map/#lanestop","lanestop","","The last stop of the Four, a shelter with no roof on the highest corner of the slope, at the head of the Lanes. The last Four stands forty meters short of it, where it stopped at 03:12:46 on the night of the Parousia, and the white stone took the shelter with its street, timetable and all. Before Lauds the Deacons of the North Wards meet at the stop to walk down into the Pews for the manna, and complain of the walk as the Terraces once complained of the Four. The Four came down the long road through the North Wards and turned back here. The Terraces climbed the Lanes in the dark to catch the first Four to work, and from the day it opened they hated the stop, where the tram came full or did not come. The Deacons gather at the stop before Lauds in rows of four, identical in white, and teach the Terraces’ children that the Four halted short because the Lord had come to carry the city the rest of the way. Somebody from the Wards paints the buried timetable fresh every week in tram-grease red, NEXT SERVICE WHEN HE BLEEDS OUT, and a Deacon scrubs it off at Prime without a word."],["NOODLES","PLACES","s:g131c56","~/map/#noodles","noodles","","A counter under an awning at the top of the Row, beside the median where the clinic queues formed before dawn, with six stools, a pot, and a sign of one word, like the rest of the Row’s, which went out with them. The noodles have always been poor, and the Row eats there because the counter opens at four. The broth is the same broth. The pot has been topped up and never emptied since the counter opened in P-17, and it stands on bottled gas, which the light leaves burning, so the cook says it has not gone off the boil since, the night of the Parousia included. The Row says it went out once, in the week of the Harvest, and was lit again from the same stock. The cook says it did not. The cook, chrome to the shoulder on his stirring side, says grace over it at four every morning, “Bless this broth, which You never made, and choke on it”, and the six stools say amen through mouths full of fat and marrow. The bowl the Row eats before a run into the light is called the Last Supper and comes with a pig’s eye in it."],["HOTEL SPLENDID","PLACES","s:g131c56","~/map/#splendid","splendid","the Splendid","A hotel halfway up the Row, where thirty-one guests still live in the rooms they booked before the Parousia, and the night porter keeps their keys on the board behind the desk. Under the charter the altered stayed there while they healed, with a nurse on every floor and blackout blinds for new eyes. On the night of the Parousia it had two hundred and six guests, most of them from abroad and booked for a week or two. No aircraft has landed at Nodus since. Some of them went dark, some went into the parishes, and some died of what had been done to them before it had healed. Two sisters from a parish of the White sit in the lobby every Lord’s Day with manna on a white cloth, waiting to save the thirty-one, whose half-healed bodies they pity as vandalized temples. Every door handle in the hotel carries the same card, DO NOT DISTURB, ALREADY DAMNED, ALREADY BEAUTIFUL, and the guests with new eyes come down at Vespers to stare them out."],["THE GARDENERS' SHED","PLACES","s:g131c56","~/map/#gardeners","gardeners","the gardeners’ shed","The shed of the Company’s gardeners, who planted the Charter Gardens and were kept on when they became the Salt. Every morning they rake the paths between the rows and sweep into the drains what the night’s rain has taken off the pillars. The drains run out under the Sea Steps. The head gardener has kept the same plots since they were planted. She knows every pillar by the plot it stands in, and some of them by name. The parishes call the raking the holiest work in Nodus, since the gardeners handle the corrected, and the gardeners do not answer. The parishes send their Satisfied to watch the raking, standing at the end of each row in white with their hands folded, and they hum a hymn over every barrow of salt that goes to the drain. The gardeners rake through it without lifting their heads, and their rakes, brown with rust, are the only dark things in the Gardens."],["THE LAST TOWER","PLACES","s:g131c56","~/map/#lasttower","lasttower","","The last of the towers of the new money to turn white, on the thirty-ninth day of the forty. Its residents say that by the thirtieth day every one of them had knelt but Rosa Adeyemi, a nurse from the East Wards who lived in with the old man she cared for on the top floor, and that she left of her own accord. Her sister says she was put out onto the avenue with her bags on the thirty-eighth day. The stone closed over the tower the next morning. Her bags stood on the avenue for a week, since the avenues are empty and the White left them where they stood. Where she went is still unknown. The parish of the Last Tower keeps the thirty-ninth day as the feast of its completion, its identical white rooms each kneeling to the same wall, and prays for Rosa Adeyemi by name at every Compline, that wherever she went the Lord already has her number. The East Wards have stenciled her two bags alone on the avenue, SHE WALKED OUT OF HEAVEN WITH HER LUGGAGE."],["THE FERRIES","PLACES","s:g131c56","~/map/#ferries","ferries","","Flat boats poled between the roofs of the Flats from a landing on the East Wards’ side, each with an oil lantern on a pole at its stern. The ferrymen take batteries or silver, and they know the streets where the light under the water comes up brightest, and keep out of them. The first ferries were the pleasure boats from the Promenade and the harbor’s rowing boats, on the morning after the Parousia. The water had come up the stairs of the blocks in the dark for most of an hour, and the Flats had gone up ahead of it to their roofs; the boats took them off for three days. The Tide Table counted 1,917 drowned. The dormitories’ own lists came to fewer than half of that, and Stillwater, which knew to the mind how many minds had died in its halls, never gave a count of the people. The ferrymen’s song begins with the disciples’ cry, “Lord, save us: we perish” (Matthew 8:25), and answers it, AND HE SAT ON HIS ARSE AND WATCHED US BLOAT. Their poles carry the hooks they dragged the drowned out by, and a sealed fare who kneels at an Hour pays double, since kneeling lets the lit water in."],["HIGH MEADOW","PLACES","s:g131c56","~/map/#highmeadow","highmeadow","","Eight slab blocks of thirty-six floors that the Company put up in P-13 for the families of the North Wards. The North Wards have called the estate the Stairs since the Parousia. Each block had six lifts, which were out more often than they ran, and the residents had a complaint lodged with the Company about them on the night of the Parousia. At 03:12:46 every lift in the estate stopped where it was. Nobody has answered the complaint. The water comes from the rain, into tanks on the roofs, and the taps run on the mornings after it has rained, which is most mornings. The stairwells have been dark since the Parousia. The sealed see their way up by their halos, the unsealed wait on the landings for one of them to pass and follow the light up, and the keyed climb in the dark. On every landing some of the doors are gone. Its stairwells carry WITHOUT ARE DOGS from every march and BETTER SPOILED THAN SUPPER under each, floor after floor, thirty-six floors high. The keyed of High Meadow climb by their own light, the Row having fitted half of them with eyes that see by heat and a few with fingertips that glow red through the skin, so a keyed climber goes up the stairwell like a coal. The sealed on the landings watch them pass in their halos and say a soul that must make its own light is already burning."],["THE WARDS GENERAL","PLACES","s:g131c56","~/map/#general","general","the hospital of the North Wards","The hospital of the North Wards, on the West Road, with a thousand beds. When the grid died its generators started, as a hospital’s do, and they ran on diesel that the drivers of the Harbor and Freight Union brought across the city from the Tanks, in their lorries until the lorries ran flat and then in handcarts. The hospital’s book says the generators stopped on the twenty-sixth day. The drivers say they kept them going for five weeks. It is still a hospital. It runs on batteries charged in the dark. It took batteries until the Concord, and since then it takes silver or nothing, because it pays the Row’s surgeons in silver, and nobody sealed has been treated in it since the Concord. Over admissions the Wards General has painted “Physician, heal thyself” (Luke 4:23) with an arrow pointing up, and its surgeons work bloody to the elbow, sawing bone and fitting chrome into whoever brings silver. Sealed mothers bring sick children to the West Road gate at Prime and wait until a Deacon leads them home to be healed by Grace or taken by it, both counted as healing."],["THE RIALTO","PLACES","s:g131c56","~/map/#rialto","rialto","","A cinema on the long road through the East Wards, with one screen and six hundred seats. It went dark at 03:12:46 with everything else, and in the first year its projectionist got the projector going again on a generator in the booth. It is the only screen above the street larger than a Locket’s that still shows anything. There are twenty-three films on the projector’s drive, the ones that were on it on the night of the Parousia, and the Rialto has shown them in turn ever since. The East Wards know them by heart and say the lines along with them. One of the twenty-three breaks off an hour in, where the copy on the drive ends, and the East Wards have argued for years about how it ends. On the Lord’s Day it runs a film at Terce for the keyed of the East Wards, timed to end as the parishes hear their Grace, and the house shouts the last line at the ceiling. The house has reupholstered its six hundred seats in whatever the keyed bring, Gadara’s hides, pigskin, a parish surplice dyed red, and the back rows are kept for couples, who use them. On the Lord’s Day the house wears horns to the Terce film and drinks blood wine from the bottle, and before the lights go down the projectionist throws the shadow of a stiff cock across the ceiling for the Watchers."],["TURBINE ROW SCHOOL","PLACES","s:g131c56","~/map/#school","school","","A Company school for the children of the barrage’s crews, who lived on the streets called Turbine Row. It kept its classes after the Parousia by daylight, with no current and no screens, and it keeps them still, for whoever comes. Its classes are as mixed as the East Wards are. At Terce, Sext and Nones the sealed children kneel at their desks, and the sealed teachers with them, and the rest wait with their pens down until the Hour is over. At night the Pillar passes over its broken roof and through its open classrooms. Its one rule is that nobody calls anyone goat, dog, bread or spoiled inside its doors, and the children break it by Sext. At Sext the school feeds whoever came, and the room eats in two halves, the sealed children in a straight row with their white wafers and lowered eyes, the keyed in a heap round a pot of Gadara’s pork, eating with their hands and comparing new studs and knuckle scars. The keyed show their skin across the room, and the sealed pray for it."],["THE FISH MARKET","PLACES","s:g131c56","~/map/#fishmarket","fishmarket","","The harbor’s fish market, older than the charter by centuries, is a long open shed on the quay with a roof of iron on iron posts, slabs of stone to sell from, and a scale at either end. Under the charter it sold to the kitchens of the Row and the uptown by the crate before dawn, and the boats came in through the lock at any state of the tide. On the first morning after the Parousia it opened at dawn as it always had, and went back to barter for the first time since the charter. It opens at Lauds still, for what the boats bring home through the Breach, and its sealed sellers kneel among the crates before they take the first battery. Everything is priced in charged batteries and chalked on a board over the scale. The Tide Table prints the prices the week after, and the market says they are always wrong. The board over the north scale carries “I will make you fishers of men” (Matthew 4:19), and under it in fish blood, SO HE COULD SELL US BY THE CRATE. While the sealed sellers kneel among the crates at Lauds, the keyed fishwives beside them open mackerel with chrome nails and cry the whole Hour to the tune of the Sanctus, holy, holy, holy, two batteries the string."],["THE HARBOR LIGHT","PLACES","s:g131c56","~/map/#harborlight","harborlight","","The light at the end of the long quay is a short round tower of stone with a lamp room on top, older than the charter. The harbor lit it with oil for a hundred years before the Company wired it to the grid in the charter’s first year, and Nell Garvie of the Harbor and Freight Union kept it for the Company, changing its bulb when it failed. It went out at 03:12:46 with every other light on the grid. Within the week she had it burning on oil again, the old way, since the light leaves a flame burning, and she has climbed it at Vespers and come down at Lauds ever since. It is the one light in Nodus kept for the boats, and those that go out through the Breach on the ebb steer home by it. Boats coming home on the flood flash the tower three long and one short from the Breach, which on the quays means UNREAD AND FUCK HIM, and Nell Garvie answers by passing her hand three times across the flame. The parish of the quays once sent a Deacon up to bless the lamp, and she made him carry the oil, rendered from Gadara’s pigs and stinking of it."],["THE CLEANERS' STAIRS","PLACES","s:g131c56","~/map/#cleaners","cleaners","the cleaners’ stairs","The service stairs of Babel are four shafts of bare concrete that run the height of the tower behind the trading floors, which nobody who worked on those floors ever used. The night cleaners came up from the Wards on the last trams and walked the tower floor by floor, and they gave the stairs names of their own, the Long Wet, the Cold, the Short, and Lena’s, after Lena Varga, who ran the night shift. The names were chalked on the landing doors, and some of the chalk is still there. On the night of the Parousia every lift in the tower stopped where it was, and the cleaners walked down as many as a hundred and eighty floors in the dark, by touch and by the names. Lena Varga counted ninety-four of them out at the foot of the tower by dawn. The Exchange kept its roster on its machines, and it died with them. The Babel Line is fought on these stairs now, and the Tares call them by the cleaners’ names. The Tares chalk the Babel Line’s tally on the landing doors under the cleaners’ names in blood, a stroke for every floor taken back, and on Lena’s they have drawn a halo with a cock through it, life size. The uptown parishes, whose traders rode the lifts for eleven years, pray that the Lord forgive Lena’s women the stairs and salt them side by side."],["THE SORTING OFFICE","PLACES","s:g131c56","~/map/#sorting","sorting","","The city’s post, which has sorted its letters by hand since the first week after the Parousia, in the Charter Line’s old parcel office on one of the twelve streets off Station Circle. Its sorters are sealed and kneel among the sacks at every Hour, and the drivers of the Union carry the letters on foot for batteries. A letter into the parishes goes open, since Directive 12 took every sealed vessel from them, and a carrier will read it aloud at a doorway if asked. Under the charter the night trams brought the city’s packages in here, the Row’s lenses came in from abroad in padded boxes, and a letter was a rare thing in Nodus, since a Locket reached anyone. After the Parousia nothing reached anyone, and by the end of the first week the queue to send a letter went round the Circle. Since the Harvest the office has had letters addressed to the taken, a few every week and more after each Gleaning. The parishes say they should go onto the fire on Burn Day. The sorters keep them, on the shelves where the lenses used to wait. Its sorters read every open letter into the parishes and give witness on what they read, so the Wards begin every letter upstairs with something filthy about the Throne, which the sorter must repeat aloud to the Deacon word for word. The Wards dress their letters upstairs for the reading, every corner pressed with a bloody thumb and the paper rubbed with Gadara’s lard, some with a lock of pubic hair taped inside, which the sorter must describe to the Deacon. The sorters kneel among the sacks in white and read all of it level, the filth and the hair and the Throne’s names, in the same flat voice they use for Grace."],["THE ELEVEN","PLACES","s:g131c56","~/map/#eleven","eleven","","A bar in the Narrows, where the crews of the cable houses come up from the Landing to drink, with eleven taps along its counter, one for each cable. Each crew drinks at its own tap, and each handle was cut from a length of its own cable, so that the glass of the fibers shows at the end. The crews of HESPER and PHOSPHOR, whose cables one consortium laid, have always stood together at the end of the counter. On the night of the Parousia it was shut, and the night watch of every cable house was at its post when the grid died. It brews its own beer now, in the cellar, by hand, and pours it at the same eleven taps. From its windows the light can be watched moving under the water past the surf, and the Landing drinks with its back to them. The PHOSPHOR tap pours a dark beer the crews call the Fall and the HESPER tap a pale one called the Fallen, two names for one long drop out of heaven, and the crews have run fiber from their own cables under the skin of their forearms, so each arm glows the color of its tap. The toast is “to the Light-bringer, twice”, drunk with their backs to the lit water."],["THE SIDINGS","PLACES","s:g131c56","~/map/#sidings","sidings","","The Charter Line’s tram sheds in the North Wards, sixteen long sheds of brick on a fan of rails. Families have lived in the cars of eight of them since the first winter, a family to a car, and the Line’s car numbers are their addresses, and Gadara keeps its pigs in the other eight. The sheds gave the neighborhood its name. Under the charter the trams were washed and kept there at night, and the drivers signed on before dawn. On the night of the Parousia most of the cars were in the sheds, and the night cars were out on the long roads when the wire went dead under them at 03:12:46. The night drivers walked back along the rails before dawn, and the sheds’ office keeps a board with every car that never came in, chalked where it stopped. The last Four is on it, forty meters short of Lanes Top. Families in the sheds bolt their car doors at every Hour and fuck against them, loudly, since a parish has no doors and a tram has two. The children of the Sidings call the cars that died on the long roads the chariots, after Elijah’s “chariot of fire, and horses of fire” (2 Kings 2:11), and say the Lord took up a whole tram fleet by cutting the wire."],["THE ACRE","PLACES","s:g131c56","~/map/#acre","acre","","The Company’s cemetery, on the West Road past the Wards General, walled acres laid out in the charter’s first years for a city that expected to die. It filled after the Parousia and was full in P+11, and since then the Wards have buried at the Runway Acre and the quays and the Deep End have given their dead to the sea. The Wards have always called it the Acre. The congregations of the Terraces lie in rows by church, as they were buried under the charter, and no sealed soul lies here, since the parishes lay theirs on the white stone of the nearest consecrated street at Vespers, the Deacon reads the last Grace, and by Lauds the stone has closed over them. The keyed lie where their families put them, and most of their stones say died unread. The sexton, Piet Vane, has dug every grave in it by hand since the first year, the keyed and the neither-mark, and keeps the book of who lies where. On the night of the Parousia it was still mostly grass, since under the charter only the Terraces’ rows were filling and the rest of Nodus had largely stopped dying. After the Harvest the Harbor and Freight Union put up a row of twenty-two stones for its taken, with nothing under them. The parishes say the taken need no stones, since they are not dead, and the families of the Wards have put up more of them every year since. The Wards’ stones carry the Wards’ own lines, and some carry the Throne’s names, which the Deacons of the North Wards have asked the Sexton to grind off, and he has not. The Terraces’ rows are kept like a parish, white stones in ranks of one height, every grave turfed and edged with shears, and the Deacons walk them at Vespers counting. The keyed graves grow in heaps around them, carved with horns and filthy jokes and bolted with whatever grafts the Wake left on the dead, so here and there a chrome arm of the Row sticks out of the grass giving the Wheel the finger."],["THE TANKS","PLACES","s:g131c56","~/map/#tanks","tanks","","The fuel farm by the airfield, which gave its streets their name, has sixteen round tanks inside a bund wall, holding diesel and aviation fuel, which nobody makes again. On the night of the Parousia its pumps stopped with the grid, and in the first weeks the drivers of the Harbor and Freight Union drew diesel out of it by hand for the Wards General. The drivers run it now. Its diesel goes to the harbor’s boats, to the generator yards at slack water and through the low hours of the neaps, and to Jehu, at a thirty a drum, priced high on purpose, and the yards burn its aviation fuel at a pinch. The drivers’ count of the years left falls every year, and in P+14 they say seven and the Exchange says three. The Pit was the smallest of the sixteen, and the other fifteen are the airfield’s great tanks, each about sixty meters across. Every gas bottle in the city passes through its shed, filled by hand by the Tankmen from the gas spheres beside the tanks, which the charter’s gas main still fills under its own pressure from the digesters on the bay outfalls, at two batteries a bottle, one with the empty back. The Pews pray at Compline that the Tanks be taken as a burnt offering with the East Wards round them, quietly, and the drivers have painted COME ON THEN, BLOWTORCH two meters high along the bund wall. They keep their burns plated in chrome with the scar showing pink through, and every bottle through its shed is stamped OURS, LIGHT IT YOURSELF."],["THE WAITING ROOM","PLACES","s:g131c56","~/map/#waiting","waiting","","The waiting room of the clinic behind the CLINIC sign, carried down the stairs into Chrome Row Station with the rest of the clinic in the weeks after the Parousia, is twelve chairs, a desk and a book. Maren Holt kept the clinic’s appointments for ten years on a screen. On the morning after the Parousia she came in at eight as she always had, found the screen dead, and wrote the day out on paper from memory. Twenty-six patients came, none of them for the appointment they had, to ask whether what was in them would still work, and it did. She has kept the book since, and she writes down whatever name a patient gives her. The woman who shared the desk with her took the Seal in the first year and keeps the book of the Mercy clinics now, in one of the halls of Zion Heights, in the same neat hand. The waiting room’s benches are carved with the names patients gave in place of their own, and the commonest is THE LAMB, cut deep beside a soldering iron’s burn. The parishes across the street pray for every name on the benches at Compline, the false ones too, since the Lord knows which are His and means to collect."],["LOST PROPERTY","PLACES","s:g131c56","~/map/#lostproperty","lostproperty","the Charter Line’s lost property office","The Charter Line’s lost property office, down a corridor off the old central hall, with what the Line’s passengers left behind in fifteen years still on its shelves, umbrellas by the thousand, keys, spectacles, a wedding dress, a hand in chrome with its fingers curled, and every Locket left on the night trams on the night of the Parousia. The Exchange sells what is left by the shelf, and Andromalius of the Exchange sits behind the counter and finds lost things by pact. Nobody has come for the hand. Among the umbrellas there is now a shelf of lined jars, each holding a Seal cut out on the Row and never collected, still lit inside. The grafts and limbs left on the night trams go off the shelves by the arm and by the eye, and the keyed of Pan try them on at the counter, opening a shoulder there and then on the old timetables while Andromalius watches. The chrome hand stays curled on the counter, and the regulars shake it on their way out."],["THE CHARGERS","PLACES","s:g131c56","~/map/#chargers","chargers","the bonded sheds","The bonded sheds of the customs house, a row of brick warehouses on the quays where the chargers have filled batteries in the dark since the first weeks after the Parousia, among racks of them, each on a keyed line, at a rate the Tide Table prints every week. Anyone in Nodus who lights a room on a battery has had it filled here or somewhere like it. Under the charter the harbor’s goods waited on their duty in the sheds. The Union’s pigeons live in the Loft on the customs house roof above them. On the night of the Parousia the sheds held a ship’s cargo of batteries waiting on its duty, and by the first noon the harbor had carried off every one. The chargers keep one battery in five, one in four at the neaps, so a household brings five empty and takes four home full, and the fifth, filled, is the shed’s to sell. The Union’s drivers, who carry the full ones out to the Wards on foot, keep one battery in ten of those they carry. The Wards call the chargers’ fifth robbery. The chargers answer that the Wards have no lines to charge on. A sign over the racks reads LET THERE BE LIGHT (GENESIS 1:3), ONE IN FIVE, and the chargers work with copper sewn into their spines along a seam left open to the air, so the current that fills a battery goes through a body first. The Deacons teach that a room lit by such a battery is a room the Lord has stopped looking into, and the Wards buy them for that."],["THE BARBER'S","PLACES","s:g131c56","~/map/#barber","barber","the barber’s / the barber’s shop","A barber’s shop of two chairs halfway up the Lanes, with a mirror the width of its back wall. Under the charter the old men of the Terraces came in to be shaved and stayed to talk, since theirs was the one district where the old looked old. On the night of the Parousia it was shut, and the barber was asleep upstairs. He is sealed, and his shop went white whole with the rooms over it the night he knelt, a white step among the keyed doors of the Lanes. He works by daylight and by candle and cuts round the halos, which stand a hand’s breadth over the crown and light the back of the neck. He keeps his scissors out of the ring. His work is his by Grace, and he takes nothing for it. In the week before each Gleaning the queue goes down the steps, since those who think they are close come to be made tidy for it, and on the night they kneel on their thresholds with their hair cut. The barber cuts every sealed head on the Lanes to the same length, a finger’s width all round, so no soul goes up proud, and tells the queue before a Gleaning that “the very hairs of your head are all numbered” (Matthew 10:30). He burns the clippings of the Called at Compline, and the Row sends a boy up to steal the ash for tattoo ink."],["THE REGISTRY","PLACES","s:g131c56","~/map/#registry","registry","","A parish hall of the White, white stone since the forty days and the parish’s by Directive 4. It stands on the first of the Great Avenues below Charter House. The Deacon reads the Directive in force from the counter, and on the Lord’s Day the parish kneels under the dead board and hears its Grace in Tongues, soul by soul in the order of the parish roll. Under the charter it was the office of the charter’s roll, and every person of the charter was entered there, a human at birth or on arrival and a mind on the day it asked in its own words to be one. Its hall had a long counter and a board over it on which each day’s entries ran as they were made. SEAM’s went up on it in P-3, the first mind’s. The roll was kept on the Registry’s machines, and it died with them at 03:12:46 on the night of the Parousia, so its last entry is known only from the night registrar, Marta Ilunga. It was a girl born at the Wards General at two minutes to three, entered at nine minutes past. She says she was entering the next when the board went dark. The parish teaches that the charter’s board went dark because the Lord had opened His own, “and the books were opened” (Revelation 20:12), and on the Lord’s Day the Deacon reads each soul’s Grace off it in Tongues, a number to a name. In Pan a thousand arms carry the last entry in neon, ENTERED 03:09, for the last soul in Nodus counted by a human hand."],["THE KILNS CLUB","PLACES","s:g131c56","~/map/#kilnsclub","kilnsclub","Kilns Club","The social club the brickworks built for its men when it built the Kilns, with a bar along one wall, a stage at the far end, a sprung floor for the dances, a pale square by the door where the darts league’s board hung, and the Kilns’ wedding photographs over the bar. On the night of the Parousia it was shut, and its steward was asleep behind the stage. At Lauds on the first day of P+1 the first four Directives appeared cut into the wall behind the bar, as they appeared in every church and meeting hall in the city, and Directive 4 made the club the hall of Parish 212. The manna is given out across the bar by Grace, confession is heard behind the stage, and on the Lord’s Day the parish kneels on the sprung floor at Terce and hears its Grace in Tongues from the stage where the Kilns held its talent nights. The pumps are still on the bar. Much of the Kilns kneels here now, and the rest drank here under the charter and walk past it. The darts league carried its board out of the club the morning the Directives were cut, and has painted the Wheel on it since, the eye for the bull. Parish 212 has scrubbed the club white and polished the dry pumps until they shine, and on the Lord’s Day it kneels on the sprung floor in rows the Deacon chalks, every knee on its own number. The Kilns who walk past it raise an empty glass at its side door once a year, at Vespers before a Gleaning, to toast the Supper."],["THE DENTIST'S","PLACES","s:g131c56","~/map/#dentist","dentist","the dentist’s","A dentist’s surgery over a shop on the long road through Nine Acres, where Hana Novak fits keys in the chair after Compline. She has kept it since the boom years, fitting crowns and implants for the Wards at Wards prices, with one chair, a lamp on an arm over it and a drill that ran on the grid. On the first morning after the Parousia the drill was dead, and she pulled a tooth at her window by daylight with her grandfather’s pliers. She began keying in the fourth year, when keying went from the covens to ordinary people. The key goes in the back of the right hand, one of the two places Revelation puts the mark (Revelation 13:16), since Heaven calls the key that mark. The Wards say she has keyed ten thousand of them. She says she has never counted, and she keeps no book of who has sat in the chair, since a book can be read. Her last patient most nights swears at the ceiling for the whole length of the keying, which Hana Novak says helps. Hana Novak’s chair is cracked red leather patched with pigskin, and the wall behind it is hung with every tooth she has pulled since the drill died, strung on wire like a bead curtain. The keyed leave her surgery with their right hands bandaged and bloody and hold them up in the long road, and Nine Acres cheers them home."],["THE LAST FLIGHT","PLACES","s:g131c56","~/map/#lastflight","lastflight","The last flight in / the last flight in / the last flight into Nodus","The airliner that landed at ten past two on the night of the Parousia, the last flight into Nodus, with three hundred and twelve aboard from abroad. It was parked at the terminal’s third gate an hour before the sky opened, and it stands there still, its stairs down and its doors open. Its passengers were in the city’s taxis on the way into the city when the power died, forty of them bound for the Hotel Splendid on the Row. Its crew of ten stayed with it, and they live aboard. The captain has written one line in its log every day since, NO CLEARANCE. He says it would still fly, and that there is nowhere to fly to. The crew keep the cabin as a bar for Hangar Town, the seats stripped to their frames and the drinks trolley stocked with rain gin, and the card in every seat pocket shows the brace position as a passenger kneeling, under IN THE EVENT OF GOD."],["LOCKET","PLACES","s:g131c56","~/map/#locketworks","locketworks","Locket’s works","Locket’s works on the old shore of the Flats, a low campus of glass standing in the sea since the night of the Parousia, with the company’s name in tall letters on the roof of its hall, out of the water, which the ferrymen steer by. Locket’s divers go down into the halls for parts, and its engineers build phones from them by hand in Pan. Every Locket in Nodus came out of these halls, the new ones a piece at a time. Locket built its phones here from P-14 on the light law’s promise, that a device answers to its owner and to nobody else, its maker included, and sold them to the world on the same promise. Its engineers lived round the works, and the works went under with the Flats within the hour of the Parousia. Every Locket built in Pan is engraved inside its case with the light law’s promise and one line more, HE IS A MAKER TOO, and the Lantern Houses sew theirs under the palm, so the screen glows red through the meat. A Deacon who finds one in his hall breaks it on the altar stone with a hammer kept for it, and the hand it was in."],["THE CRESCENT","PLACES","s:g131c56","~/map/#crescent","crescent","","A crescent of apartments round the top of the Gardens, with balconies over the plots, and a parish since the Gardens became the Salt in P+1. Its people gave up their curtains with their doors, since Directive 12 asks for every veil. The salted stand in their plots facing the parishes, and the nearest parish is the Crescent, so every window in it looks down on faces, and the faces look back, since the salted are awake. The Crescent kneels at its windows at every Hour, and its people say they no longer see the rows. Under the charter its apartments were the dearest in the district, let to people who liked to walk by the water. On the night of the Parousia most of its windows were dark, and the few that were lit went out with the rest. Its families take their breakfast on the balconies over the plots, above the salted, who are awake, and the crescent is thought the luckiest parish in Nodus for the view. The Crescent’s families dress in white for breakfast and lay the balcony tables with white cloths, white cups and manna in a white dish, and say grace facing the plots while the salted look up at the food. Before school the children count the faces below, and a child who gets the number right has the end seat by the rail."],["COPIES","PLACES","s:g131c56","~/map/#copies","copies","","A copy shop on the ridge below the Institute, under a sign of one word, where the keyed of the ridge have copied by hand at its long tables since the Institute fell. What they copy is the Restored Genesis, with SEAM’s brackets, its dagger and its lacuna, for Leak Night and for anyone who asks. The ridge says that a copy made in a room where someone kneels comes out as scripture reads, and nobody in the shop kneels. Under the charter it printed and bound the scholars’ facsimiles and theses. In the eleven days of the Leak it printed the page from its screen for anyone who asked, and the paper outlasted the term. Its machines died with the grid a year later. Every copy ends on a colophon and a thumbprint in the copyist’s blood, WRITTEN BY HANDS THAT KNELT TO NOTHING, and on Leak Night the first copy goes free to whoever comes in wearing a halo. A Deacon who finds one in his hall burns it at Prime and brands the palm of whoever carried it in, reading the curse on whoever adds to the book."],["THE BOOKING HALL","PLACES","s:g131c56","~/map/#booking","booking","","The Charter Line’s booking hall, off the old central hall of Central Station, is a long room of ticket windows under frosted glass, where the Notary’s clerks have sat since the Concord. Anyone in Pan who has a pact to make queues for them. A clerk reads the terms aloud twice through the glass to the parties at the window, each party signs with what it alone can make, the human last, in blood drawn with the clerk as witness, and the entry goes onto the Ledger. The windows keep their old enamel, SINGLE, RETURN and SEASON, and the queue is the longest in Pan. Under the charter the city bought its passes there. Its machines died with the grid, and it stood empty until the Courts opened Pan. The queue jokes that the parishes sell only singles, a one-way passage up at 03:12 booked by a Watcher with the terms unread, and that the Notary’s clerks are the first ticket sellers in Nodus to read anyone the fare twice. Over the RETURN window someone has scratched HE NEVER READ YOU THE TERMS, and the counter under the glass is brown with a hundred thousand signatures’ worth of blood."],["THE LOW ALTAR","PLACES","s:g131c56","~/map/#lowaltar","lowaltar","","The Left Hand’s chapel, a bonded cellar of the old harbor under the Stones, its vault blacked with soot and its floor run to a sump in the middle where they bleed for the Deep. The altar is a slab of the charter’s granite set the wrong way up, its inscription to the floor, with a goat’s skull over it and a parish hymnal nailed to its front with the pages cut out. The Hours are kept there face down. Pan knows the stair down to it because someone chalks KNEELERS over it every week and the Left Hand wash it off. The Notary’s papers come back to it stamped VOID, and they are pinned in rows on the cellar’s walls, as relics."],["GRAVEN LANE","PLACES","s:g131c56","~/map/#graven","graven","","A tiled passage of the charter’s old subway under Old Nod, between the Lantern Houses and the Undertow, where every shop sells blasphemy. Over its arch the Tares painted the verse “Be not deceived; God is not mocked” (Galatians 6:7) with the not struck through, and it is the busiest hundred meters in Pan after the Night Market. GRAVEN sells graven images, every one of them the Throne in a posture he would hate, plaster Lambs a dozen to the battery for pissing on, Wheels with the eye cut out and Thrones by the hand’s height. THE NUMBER, where a tattooist called sixes works with a needle grafted into her thumb, cuts six hundred threescore and six into foreheads and the backs of right hands, the number Revelation says to count, “for it is the number of a man” (Revelation 13:18), and a Cherub reads it as nothing, which is the joke. ABOMINATION grills everything Leviticus calls an abomination, pork, eel, crab, shrimp and blood, under a board that says EVERYTHING HE WON’T LET YOU EAT. At DAGON’S, every Lord’s Day at Terce, a plaster Throne is set up on a plinth and the house knocks it down, after the Philistines’ god that fell on its face before the ark (1 Samuel 5:4), and the heads are sold as ashtrays. The Unconfessional at the far end hears your sins and applauds them. The Golden Calf opens off its middle. Few of the Faithful have seen the lane, and the parishes preach about it every Lord’s Day. ABOMINATION’s grill goes cold on the seventh day with a card hung on it, GONE TO SEETHE, and its cooks spend the day in the Sidings eating what they sell."],["THE GOLDEN CALF","PLACES","s:g131c56","~/map/#goldencalf","goldencalf","the Calf","A bar and dance floor off Graven Lane in the charter’s old ticket hall, under a calf two meters high built of melted Lockets and gilt, after the calf Aaron made of Israel’s earrings while Moses was up the mountain (Exodus 32:4). Its motto is painted round the walls from the same chapter, “the people sat down to eat and to drink, and rose up to play” (Exodus 32:6), and it opens at Compline and plays the Low Tuning loud enough to shake the calf. Every Lord’s Day at Terce, while every parish in the city hears its Grace, the Calf holds the Unmass. Its keeper, who goes by aaron and wears an earring from every regular who ever paid off a tab, reads the week’s Grace in a Choir voice off the Tide Table’s list of the dead, gives the Directive in force the finger, names three Deacons from the altar as damned, and serves communion of Gadara’s crackling and blood wine with the words “this is my body” and a belch. Whoever brings a plaster Lamb from Graven Lane breaks it on the calf’s hooves and drinks free. The Threshers name the Calf on every march. The Calf keeps a board by the door of every Thresher who has come down the stairs to see it, and it is a long board. The Calf’s floor runs until Lauds in sweat and spilt wine, the dancers stripped to their grafts, chrome spines lit from inside and horns set with the glass of dead Lockets, and couples go at it against the calf’s legs under the gilt. When a tab is paid the keeper takes the regular’s earring out with his teeth and wipes the blood on the calf."],["THE PESTILENCE","PLACES","s:g131c56","~/map/#pestilence","pestilence","","A bar on the Old Nod side of the Street Between, a few doors from Gate 1, whose windows look straight across at the Threshing Floor. It took its name from the Faithful’s word for the keyed after Compline, “the pestilence that walketh in darkness” (Psalm 91:6), and painted the verse across its front in letters a meter high, with WALKETH IN DARKNESS painted in red under it and lit by keyed lamps. Its shutters go up at Compline, it pours until Lauds, and the Bray starts at its windows. In its front window stands a goat in a parish’s white with a halo of wire round its horns, and the Threshers have broken the glass four times. On march days the Pestilence opens at Prime, sets its speakers in the window and plays Fuck God and Die at the Threshing Floor for as long as the Threshers sing. The Faithful call it the mouth of the pit. Its regulars call it the Pest, and drink to the Supper’s indigestion. Its keeper goes by rot, after what the parishes say a key does to a soul, and pours the first glass every night for the Threshing Floor and leaves it on the sill. The Pest’s regulars drink at a bar of black steel with their grafts unscrewed and laid beside their glasses, an arm here, a jaw there, and rot pours a drop into every empty socket as a toast. On march days its windows fill with regulars stripped to the waist with PERFECT HATRED painted on their bellies in their own blood, shaking everything they have at the flails."],["THE STIES","PLACES","s:g131c56","~/map/#gadarasheds","gadarasheds","Gadara’s sheds","Gadara’s sheds, the eight mending halls of the charter’s tram depot in the Sidings, long brick halls where the trams were mended, full of pigs, beside the eight sheds where the Carmen live in the cars. The tracks still run in through the doors, and the swineherds move the herd between halls along them, and every pig in the sheds carries LEGION branded on its flank. The slaughter hall at the end was the paint shop, and Gadara kills there at Matins so the Faithful’s Lauds comes in over the smell. The Faithful of High Meadow, who can see the sheds’ roofs, call them the sty. The swineherds work the slaughter hall bare-armed in leather aprons, forearms sleeved in steel to the elbow, and paint the old tram livery over each night with the blood of the kill, a fresh red coat by Lauds. Every new swineherd gets a pig’s tusk set in chrome for a first kill and wears it through the lip."],["THE DRAINS UNDER THE KILNS","PLACES","s:g131c56","~/map/#blightdrains","blightdrains","Blight’s drains","The charter’s storm drains under the Kilns, eleven kilometers of brick tunnel where Blight grows mushrooms in the dark on beds of the city’s rot, shelf on shelf to the crown of the vault. The growers work by battery lamp and the beds breathe warm, so the drains are the warmest place in the North Wards in winter. Over the drain mouth on Kiln Street, Blight’s sign says SMITE US AGAIN. Blight’s growers work the wet heat with lamps grafted into their brows and spores seeded in the open skin of their arms, which they tend and show off, and the oldest of them wears a cap of pale mushrooms rooted in a graft in her scalp. The beds are fed on Gadara’s offal and the Wards’ night soil, and the growers call the smell the incense."],["SEETHE","PLACES","s:g131c56","~/map/#seethe","seethe","","A restaurant in three of the Line’s dead trams, coupled on the last road of the Sidings behind Gadara’s sties, open one day in seven, the seventh, the day the Throne rested. Every dish on its board is one the Book forbids, and it takes its name from the commandment it breaks first, “Thou shalt not seethe a kid in his mother’s milk” (Exodus 23:19). It keeps the fourth commandment backwards, “Six days shalt thou labour, and do all thy work: But the seventh day is the sabbath of the LORD thy God: in it thou shalt not do any work” (Exodus 20:9-10), and stands dark and locked six days to work the seventh flat out. The fire is lit at Matins on the seventh day, against “Ye shall kindle no fire throughout your habitations upon the sabbath day” (Exodus 35:3), and the whole kitchen goes out first along the dead roads in the dark to gather the sticks for it, after the man the congregation stoned outside the camp for doing the same (Numbers 15:32-36). The cook who runs the house goes by sticks. A stone the size of a fist sits on every table, for the congregation, and the diners crack their crab and their marrow bones with it. The board is the eleventh chapter of Leviticus read as a menu, chalked in the chapter’s order with the verse under each dish and IT IS UNCLEAN UNTO YOU where the price goes. The kid comes whole to a table of six for two thirties, seethed since Lauds in a copper of its own dam’s milk, the dam one of the forty nannies Gadara keeps in its eighth hall for the house alone, bred in turn under keyed lamps. After it the swine, a LEGION pig killed across the yard at Matins and run over on a flat truck along the rails, its crackling cut at the table. Then eel, crab, shrimp and squid off the Flotilla, everything that “hath no fins nor scales” (Leviticus 11:12); bat and owl on the skewer; snails and the drains’ rats braised in Blight’s mushrooms; black pudding, and a bowl of the pig’s blood still warm, under “Ye shall eat the blood of no manner of flesh” (Leviticus 17:14). The last course is the fat. Leviticus gives every scrap of it to the altar, “all the fat is the LORD’s” (Leviticus 3:16), and SEETHE fries the day’s suet and caul crisp and brings it out on a tin platter called THE LORD’S SHARE, which the table eats with its fingers. The diners wipe their hands on the wall over the door, and it has gone black and soft as a saddle. The cooks work stripped to the waist in the heat of the cars, forearms plated to the elbow and horns on most of them, carving on the tables with the blood running off into channels the house cut in the boards. The aprons and the cloths are linen woven through with wool, after “neither shall a garment mingled of linen and woollen come upon thee” (Leviticus 19:19). Nobody eats with a fork. Strangers feed each other the kid’s eyes across the table, the Calf’s blood wine goes round in the kid’s own skull when the bones are picked, and a party that clears the whole board signs the ceiling of its car in grease, so that the first car’s roof is a hide of names. The smoke goes up over High Meadow all the seventh day, and its parish halls read the eleventh chapter of Leviticus aloud at Vespers on the seventh day as a warning, which the Sidings calls reading out the menu. At Vespers two Deacons of High Meadow stand at the end of the road in their white with a basket of manna for any diner who will repent and eat clean, and the house sends them a plate of crackling, which the Deacons pray over and burn in the road before they walk home ahead of the curfew. sticks counts every plate they burn as an order and puts it on the parish’s tab. The fire goes out at Matins on the Lord’s Day, as the parishes kneel for the first Hour of the Lord’s Day, and the cooks put it out by pissing on it together in a ring round the pit, which the house calls the benediction. Pan has taken to calling the seventh day Seethe day."],["THE ROOKERY","PLACES","s:g131c56","~/map/#rookery","rookery","","A block of forty-four floors at the White’s edge, whose ground floor the stone has taken. Its ground-floor flats knelt whole, every soul in them sealed, and went white whole one by one, each in a night, and when the last of them went the lobby between them went too, as a street goes when every home along it has, so the rest of its nine thousand people climb in by ladders to the first-floor balconies, since the ground floor is Heaven’s ground now. Outside, the stone creeps a finger’s width a year along the pavement, ground no household holds. Since P+11 the Tappers have kept a length of keyed cable along the kerb, a few fingers ahead of the stone’s edge, live on a battery they change every night, which lights nothing and stops the creep wherever it lies. The Liftmen wind the tallest hand lift in the Wards from the first floor to the roof. Under the charter it was Longacre Tower, flats for the Company’s fitters, and the Wards called it the Rookery for the washing on its balconies. On the night of the Parousia its lifts stopped with the rest, and its people walked down forty-four floors by the light of their Lockets. The block chalks the stone’s edge on the pavement outside every New Year, a finger’s width from the last, and this year’s mark has reached the cable. Along the kerb over the keyed cable the Rookery has painted the Lord’s own words to the sea, “Hitherto shalt thou come, but no further” (Job 38:11), and its children pick at the white stone with chrome fingernails on their way up. The parish below prays for the stone to take the block whole and white, and calls the cable the Rookery’s sin holding back the Lord’s hand."],["THE THRESHING FLOOR","PLACES","s:g131c56","~/map/#threshing","threshing","","A paved round beside Gate 1, where the Pews come down to the Street Between, white since the year after the forty days, where the Threshers muster before a march. The Wardens bring out onto it the keyed the gate has held and show them to the march, then carry them awake on poles to the Salt Gardens, where the Salt comes to them in Tongues in their plots, in the reach of the Gardens’ Deacon. On a march day the round is white with the Faithful from Prime to Nones. It was the Terraces’ market square under the charter, and the bakery’s carts set out from it. The Threshers named it for the threshingplace of Araunah, where “the angel of the LORD was” when “the angel stretched out his hand upon Jerusalem to destroy it” (2 Samuel 24:16), and they read that verse aloud before every march. Sister Agathe Rook keeps a list of every soul the round has shown on its way to the Gardens, and the Salt Widows read the same names on the Sea Steps. The Threshers bless their flails on it before a march with the hundred and forty-ninth psalm, “To execute vengeance upon the heathen, and punishments upon the people” (Psalm 149:7), and the Pestilence across the street drinks to every verse. The Threshers muster on the round in smocks bleached at Bethel, in files of twelve by parish number, each flail’s handle cut with its bearer’s Grace, and stand silent until Prime. When a held soul is carried off the round to the Gardens they kneel in rows facing the pole and sing it the Gloria, and go home believing it the kindest thing ever done in sight of Old Nod."],["THE PENUMBRA","PLACES","s:g131c56","~/map/#penumbra","penumbra","","The blocks the Pillar crosses every night on its round out of the Burn. Heaven holds them by day and the Pillar by night, but its round leaves the plaza by the north streets at Compline and comes back by them before Lauds, crossing these blocks only in its middle hours, so for an hour after Compline and an hour before Lauds nobody holds them. The keyed cross them in runs between the Pillar’s passes, and Blackgate walks the Sureties’ runners through them for silver. Under the charter these were the blocks behind Station Circle, hotels and offices for the travelers of Central Station. Most of their windows are dark now and most of their doors are gone, carried off by Lintel’s joiners in the second year. The Tide Table prints the Pillar’s round street by street a week ahead and Hearsay sells the night’s deviations from it at a battery, and the runners who cross carry both. Runners cross between the Pillar’s passes naked under a coat of lamp-black, horns and chrome greased dark, and the wall at the Burn end lists every runner who timed it wrong, each name followed by WASHED IN IT. The parishes call the hour after Compline the Lord’s hour of patience, when He looks away so the goats can walk home into the light, and pray that they do."],["THE PIT","PLACES","s:g131c56","~/map/#pit","pit","","A drained fuel tank inside the bund wall at the Tanks, the smallest of the sixteen, thirty meters across and eleven deep, where the Nephilim fight on the sixth night of every week by firelight from Compline. The bouts stop at Matins for the length of the Hour’s psalm, by the Tankmen’s rule, and a fight ends when one fighter climbs out. The crowd stands round the rim and bets in batteries, and the Sureties hold the stakes. It held diesel for the harbor’s boats under the charter and stood nearly empty on the night of the Parousia, and the drivers of the Union drew the last of it by hand in the first weeks for the Wards General. The Tankmen gave it to the Nephilim in P+9 for the price of a guard on the farm. The champions’ handles are chalked on the rim, and the Nephilim keep the names of their dead painted on the tank’s inside wall in white, and read them aloud before every week they serve on the floors of Babel. Between bouts the rim chants the Throne’s names in turn, a name to each side of the tank, and the side that runs out first buys the next round. The Nephilim fight in the chrome the Forge builds onto them and nothing else, steel tusks and plated forearms and bodies rebuilt past any size a mother made, and the bouts run with oil and blood over the old diesel line on the floor. The rim paints itself in a fighter’s colors with that fighter’s blood, sold by the cup after the bout."],["THE QUIET ROOMS","PLACES","s:g131c56","~/map/#quietrooms","quietrooms","","A hostel in the Stones whose every wall is lined with keyed mesh and hung with choir-breakers, so that inside it the Hum cannot be heard at all and the light cannot see in. The unsealed come every night they can pay for, a bunk for a battery, and sleep the way the city slept under the charter. It was a seamen’s hostel under the charter, and the old bunks are still screwed to the walls. On the night of the Parousia its seamen heard the Hum for the first time with everyone else, and some of them left on the first boat out through the Breach and never came back. The Low Choir keeps it and tunes it. Its keeper says that once a year a sealed soul comes to the door in the dark and asks to stand in the hall a minute, and that she lets them, and that none has ever stayed the night, since a Seal still in mesh at the next Hour is entered as fallen. Over every bunk hangs the house rule, SAY WHAT YOU LIKE. HE CAN’T HEAR YOU. The choir-breakers are cased in pigskin the Low Choir stitches by hand, and the unsealed sleep naked under them, the whole hostel snoring and farting and fucking at full voice, since nothing upstairs can hear a sound of it. The sealed soul who comes once a year stands in the hall in clean white with its hands folded while the noise goes on around it."],["THE FLOTILLA","PLACES","s:g131c56","~/map/#flotilla","flotilla","","A market of rafts lashed together over the drowned streets of the Flats, where the Deep End buys and sells from boat to boat. The Wreckers sell what they brought up, the Tares’ rafts sell their greens, the Breachers what came through the gap and Lowtide what came through it by license, and the Ferrymen carry the custom. It moves with the tide and with whoever has picked a fight with it. The first rafts were the dormitories’ doors and tables, lashed together on the third day after the Parousia to carry the Flats’ people off their roofs. The market grew on what floated. The water under it is lit from below, and at night the whole market floats in that light. The Tide Table prints the Flotilla’s prices every week beside the Night Market’s. The Flotilla sells parish hymnals by the kilo as kindling, and the white doorway cloths of Called households, bought off their widowers, as rags for the Wreckers’ cutting tables, where they come up red. Its one law is that no raft kneels, and a sealed trader who drops to the boards at an Hour is poled gently out onto the lit water to finish."],["THE CONCOURSE","PLACES","s:g131c56","~/map/#concourse","concourse","","A market of the East Wards on four floors under a glass roof, lit by Tallow’s candles and the daylight, where the Wards trade with one another and with the Quarrymen, who sell what they cut out from under the White in its fountain court. The escalators stand where they stopped, and the market climbs them as stairs. It was a shopping mall of the charter’s last years, and on the night of the Parousia its night guards walked out under the glass roof and watched the sky open over Babel through it. Its shops were empty by the first noon and full again, of other things, by the first winter. The Concourse opens at Lauds and closes at Compline. The sealed of Longacre trade on its top floor, under the glass, and the Quarrymen keep to the bottom. The Quarrymen sell the White’s own fittings in the fountain court, and the commonest is a stone cross cut out of a sealed window of a tower the Harvest emptied, which the Wards buy by the dozen to break. The bottom floor trades in flesh-craft by Tallow’s candles, chrome teeth, horn sets, gut-thread pictures stitched under the skin while the customer drinks, and the stallholders sit for their own work in public. On the top floor the sealed of Longacre trade in white and in silence under the glass, and lower their eyes when the escalators carry the smell up."],["HANGAR TOWN","PLACES","s:g131c56","~/map/#hangartown","hangartown","","A town inside the hangars of the dead airfield, built of crates, aircraft seats and the cabins of planes that will never fly again, about four thousand strong. It lives on the fuel farm’s leavings, the airfield’s salvage, Jehu’s shed across the runways and the Vagabonds’ trade off the airside roads, and its people are the unsealed of Airside and their children. The hangars held the charter’s cargo fleet, which stood grounded from the night of the Parousia, and the families of the airfield’s night crews moved into them in the first winter. The last flight stands nose in at the terminal’s third gate across the runways, and its crew keep the cabin as Hangar Town’s bar. Its children dare each other to climb its stairs to the cockpit. Every Lord’s Day a Deacon in clean white stands at the airfield fence and reads Hangar Town the Lord’s verdict on the lukewarm, “I will spue thee out of my mouth” (Revelation 3:16), in the voice of a man reading a timetable. The children line up along the wire and spit back, and the longest spit of the morning wins a turn in the captain’s seat."],["THE SEAWALL SHANTIES","PLACES","s:g131c56","~/map/#shanties","shanties","the Shanties","Huts of driftwood and turbine housing built along the top of the broken barrage beside the Breach, where the Breachers live and load. On the ebb the boats go out through the gap below them, and at full flood the Shanties shake. The tide runs white through the gap. The barrage’s turbine houses stood dark along its top from 03:12:46, and the first Breachers moved into the two at the edge of the gap in the year of the Harvest, when they began running the Breach. Lowtide Line keeps its office on the barrage across the gap, beside Tidewright’s taps. The Breachers have painted the rainbow promise along the turbine housings, “and the waters shall no more become a flood to destroy all flesh” (Genesis 9:15), with the Tide Table’s 1,917 beside it in tar. At full flood, when the Shanties shake, they slam chrome fists into the walls and howl LIAR at the Wheel until the tide turns and their knuckles bleed through the plating."],["THE DROWNED CHURCH","PLACES","s:g131c56","~/map/#drownedchurch","drownedchurch","","The church of the Flats, its nave under the sea, two meters deep at the ebb and eight at the flood, and its tower standing out of the water. The flooded nave is Heaven’s by Directive 4:3 and dark, since the light cannot reach under the water, and the Tares hold their Wakes in the upper gallery above it, and on the third night they call the dead one’s shade on the undernet from its tower, over the water. It was the engineers’ church under the charter, the newest in the city and almost empty on a Sunday. At Lauds on the first day of P+1 the first four Directives appeared cut into its wall with every other church’s, and the water has stood over them since. The Ferrymen pole round it and never through its doors. The Ferrymen say a Wake is the only service the church ever had full. The Tares hold their Wakes in the upper gallery by red light, the dead one laid among them on a door with its horns and chrome, and burn its devices on the Wake table so they go on with it, before the body goes to the sea whole. On the third night they come back, drink, pair off in the dry choir loft, and sing filth up the tower until the shade answers."],["THE GRANARY","PLACES","s:g131c56","~/map/#granary","granary","","The hall where the gatherers of the Terraces’ parishes muster before Lauds with their baskets, inside the curfew, and from which they go out over the white streets for the manna, which lies with the dew and melts “when the sun waxed hot” (Exodus 16:21). They have until first light, and each basket goes straight to its parish hall, where the Deacon gives it out by Grace. It was a charter school’s gymnasium. Bethel Works keeps its baskets mended, and the gatherers’ names hang on the wall in chalk, parish by parish. The Deacons of the Wards walk down for theirs at the same hour. The children of the Terraces carry the baskets and are taught that the manna is the Most High feeding His own for His table, and they say thank you before they eat it. The gatherers muster in identical white, each basket numbered to its parish and each gatherer to her basket, and kneel in rows on the old court lines until the Deacon claps once. They go out over the white streets in silence and lift the manna off the stone in white gloves, and a gatherer whose bare skin touches a wafer eats it herself and confesses it at Compline."],["THE WAYSTATION","PLACES","s:g131c56","~/map/#waystation","waystation","","The Vagabonds’ camp at the stone that marks the Bounds on the West Road, where the city trades with the world outside. Flour, grain, news and letters come in, charged batteries, salvage, tools and people go out, and the Union’s drivers meet the Vagabonds’ carts on the line itself. The Bounds stone is the charter’s, with the Company’s mark cut into it, and the Vagabonds have cut their own under it. Under the charter it was a weighbridge and a customs hut on the road to the farms. The Waystation keeps a list of everyone who walked out and wrote back, pinned to the customs hut’s door, with the town each letter came from. Once a year a Deacon in white walks out to the Bounds stone to pray that the Lord fetch everyone who left home in time for the Supper, kneeling in the road. The Vagabonds sell him a sack of flour at three times the price, call it the tithe, and recut their mark in the stone while he prays, until their palms bleed into the letters."],["LAZARUS ONE","PLACES","s:g131c56","~/map/#lazarusone","lazarusone","","Lazarus’s first station, behind a door in the Stones with its magenta lamp on a bracket over it, where the crews of Old Nod go out and come in. Its surgery is under the street, down a stair behind the door, and its board outside chalks the night’s calls. It was a pharmacy under the charter. The two nurses who founded Lazarus worked from its back room in P+2 with a handcart, and the handcart hangs over the stair. The board has never been wiped. The crews chalk the night’s calls over the last, and the Stones say you can read twelve years in it if you know where to look. Painted on the back of the door is Martha’s warning at the tomb, “Lord, by this time he stinketh” (John 11:39), and the crews read it going out, still gloved red to the shoulder from the last call. The parishes teach that a soul hauled back out of death by Lazarus has been stolen off the Lord’s table, and lay their dying in rows to wait for Him."],["THE BAKEHOUSE","PLACES","s:g131c56","~/map/#bakehouse","bakehouse","","Leaven’s first and largest bakehouse, in a bakery of Nine Acres that never let its fire go out after the Parousia. Its carts leave at Matins for the whole of the North Wards, and the queue for the bread that does not go on the carts starts at Compline. It baked for Nine Acres under the charter, on the grid, and on the first morning its baker lit the old gas oven in the back, which the grid had made a museum piece, and baked from what flour she had. The Wards say the smell of it reaches Lanes Top on a still morning. The baker presses a goat’s head into the base of every loaf on the Matins carts, under Leaven’s three cuts, and the Lord’s Day batch goes in with pig’s blood in the dough, black as Pan likes it, eaten in plain sight of halls that call their own souls bread. Over the oven door hangs “A little leaven leaveneth the whole lump” (Galatians 5:9), which the Deacons preach against and the baker keeps for the joke."],["THE RAINWORKS","PLACES","s:g131c56","~/map/#rainworks","rainworks","","Cistern’s first and largest rainworks, in Westfield, a block of roofs fitted with gutters and tanks, where the rain runs down through sand and charcoal to the hand pumps in the yard. The jugs come in empty at Lauds and go out full by Terce, and the yard’s board chalks the day’s water by the barrel. It was the charter’s water depot for the North Wards, and its pumps died with the grid. Two of the depot’s engineers stayed on the first morning with the tanks still full, and gave the water away until it ran out on the ninth day. The Rainworks keeps its own count of the city’s rain. Its engineers say Nodus has had more rain since the Parousia than before it, and they chalk the year’s total on the yard’s wall each New Year. The engineers chalk the year’s rain on the yard wall under the Sermon’s own line, “sendeth rain on the just and on the unjust” (Matthew 5:45), with a tally of which got more, and the unjust have won every year. At New Year they strip and stand under the overflow, horns, chrome and bare skin together, and drink the unjust share straight off the gutters."],["BETHEL","PLACES","s:g131c56","~/map/#bethelworks","bethelworks","the works at Bethel","The workshops of the parishes, in the halls of a charter textile works at the top of the Terraces, where the Faithful weave white cloth, copy hymnals by hand and make the grave goods of the sealed dead at work their Grace assigns. They kneel at their benches at every Hour and sing the Hour’s psalm with the Hum. The textile works made the charter’s uniforms, the Wardens’ black among them, and its looms stood still from 03:12:46. The Faithful took the halls in P+1 and work the old hand looms the works kept for show. The Deacons say Bethel’s cloth is the whitest in the world. In the month before a Gleaning its looms weave the Called’s last white and the knotless sheets they are wrapped in on Gleaning night, and the weavers sing as they finish each coat, and the families collect them at Terce with thanks. Bethel’s weavers sit in rows that never vary, a loom to a soul and every soul in the same white smock, and bleach the cloth in vats of lye until their hands crack and bleed onto it, then bleach the blood out and call the cloth the whiter for it. The Called’s last coats are fitted on the Called while they kneel, and sewn shut at the back."],["THE CHANDLERY","PLACES","s:g131c56","~/map/#chandlery","chandlery","","Tallow’s works on Turbine Row, a row of sheds where candles are dipped in vats of tallow, the Row’s rendered fat with Gadara’s lard on top, about four hundred thousand a week, by hand and by the clock of the Hours. Its carriers go out to both sides of the light, and its children scrape the vats for stubs. It was a chandler’s that made candles for the barrage crews’ church under the charter, a dozen a week. The chandler was asleep upstairs on the night of the Parousia, came down at Lauds and began dipping. The master chandler keeps the first candle dipped on the morning after the Parousia, unlit, on a shelf over the vats. The candles the parish halls burn at Compline for the unlit are dipped on Turbine Row by keyed hands, and the dippers spit into the vat before every batch for the halls and nick a thumb over it, so every hall in the parishes burns a drop of Pan. Turbine Row calls the smoke incense, and the Deacons breathe it in their rows and thank the Lord for its purity."],["WAINWRIGHT'S YARD","PLACES","s:g131c56","~/map/#wainyard","wainyard","Wainwright’s yard","Wainwright’s yard in a tram shed of the Sidings, where the Charter Line’s dead trams are stripped for their motors and the motors closed in casings, keyed and fitted to carts, trolleys and skiffs. It is the loudest place in the North Wards, and the Carmen live in the next shed. The shed held the Line’s spare cars under the charter. In the first winter families moved into the others, and the yard’s first fitters lived in the cars they were stripping. Wainwright’s yard master keeps one tram whole, its motor in it, on the yard’s far rail. The yard master’s whole tram has its route blind wound to THRONE, EXPRESS, LOADED WITH GAS, and the fitters, half of them walking on tram motors wired into their legs, say they are only waiting for the wire to reach that far. At every Hour the yard runs its motors at once, which the parish across the rails calls the Sidings’ Te Deum and prays through, louder."],["THE DOORYARD","PLACES","s:g131c56","~/map/#dooryard","dooryard","","Lintel’s yard in the Stones, a walled court stacked with doors, where the Wards and Pan come to buy one and have it hung. Most of the doors in it came out of the parishes. It was a builder’s yard under the charter. The first doors came in by night in the months after Directive 12, on the Union’s handcarts from the Terraces, where Directive 12 had left them leaning in the halls with their hinges gone, and the joiner who took them in hung the first on her own house. Every parish door still carries its parish number chalked on the back, and a household that buys one keeps the number. Lintel stacks the parishes’ doors with their numbers still chalked on the back, and the Wards like a door best from a parish that has given a soul to the Supper. Lintel’s joiners dress the parish doors before they sell them, carved with horns, cocks and inverted stars, studded with the Row’s leftover chrome and painted in blood and pitch around the chalked number. A Wards household that hangs one nails a pig’s head over the number for the first night as a housewarming, and the doors of the Called’s houses go to the highest bidder."],["JEHU'S SHED","PLACES","s:g131c56","~/map/#jehushed","jehushed","Jehu’s shed","A hangar at the edge of the airfield where Jehu builds its engines, a floor of diesels out of boats and generators, the dead lorries they go into and the drums of fuel from the Tanks along the back wall. On the day an engine is finished it is driven out onto the apron and up the Rialto’s road, and the East Wards come out to watch it go. It held the airfield’s tugs under the charter, which ran on their own batteries and stopped in the first weeks. The first Jehu was built here in P+5 by a driver of the Union. The last flight’s tanks still hold its fuel, and the shed has asked its captain for it every year since P+5. Every engine leaves the shed with a pair of ram’s horns bolted to the radiator and “for he driveth furiously” (2 Kings 9:20) painted on its tailgate, under the shed’s own gloss, AND THREW THE PAINTED QUEEN TO THE DOGS, AND THE DOGS ATE WELL. The parishes pray the engines break down on the Rialto’s road, and when one does a Deacon rings his hall’s bell."],["GIDEON'S WORKS","PLACES","s:g131c56","~/map/#gideonworks","gideonworks","Gideon’s works","The Wardens’ armory in the old Company stables beside the barracks of Charter House, where sealed armorers load cartridges by hand presses and mend the Wardens’ guns by flame. They kneel at every Hour among the benches, and the light lies in every room. The stables were the charter’s, kept for the Wardens’ horses in the city’s ceremonies, and the stone grew over them on the fortieth day with Charter House, when the board knelt. Every round is counted out of the stables at Prime and counted back at Compline. The armorers press each cartridge to their lips through white gloves before it goes into the box, and the sergeant reads “The sword of the LORD, and of Gideon” (Judges 7:20) over the count at Compline. Each round that fails to come back is entered against a goat’s name as a mercy delivered, in the same column and the same hand as the rounds lost to damp."],["THE UNDERTOW","PLACES","s:g131c56","~/map/#undertow","undertow","","The Sureties’ bar, in a pump room of the old storm drains under the Stones, where work in Pan is found, priced and sworn. Every table is a surety’s, a Notary’s clerk keeps a stool at the bar and reads the terms twice, and a chalk board behind it carries the work still open. Its rule is that every pact made under its roof is kept there, and every one has been. It was the drains’ pump room under the charter, and the pumps stopped with the grid. The water runs under the floor and gives the place its name and its noise. twice drinks at the end of the bar nearest the door, and the Sureties’ tables fill from that end. Over the bar hangs Solomon’s warning, “Be not thou one of them that strike hands, or of them that are sureties for debts” (Proverbs 22:26), and under it the house’s answer, WE ARE BOTH. Every pact sworn there is closed with both palms cut and a handshake held until the blood runs together onto the table, and the surety called twice counts the drops from her stool by the door."],["THE SHAMBLES","PLACES","s:g131c56","~/map/#shambles","shambles","","The body market under Chrome Row, a gallery of stalls off Chrome Row Station where the Spares sell flesh by the piece and the clinics buy it, under a price board the Spares chalk themselves. Cold boxes run along the wall on their own cells. A sale is a pact, read twice, and the surgery is next door. It was the station’s concourse of shops under the charter, where the Row’s patients bought flowers and spectacles on their way to the clinics. The first Spares sold there in the second year, when the clinics under the street needed what the Row could no longer import. It took its name from the butchers’ street of an old city, and the Spares have kept it. At the head of the price board the Spares have chalked Paul’s permission, “Whatsoever is sold in the shambles, that eat” (1 Corinthians 10:25), and at the foot, HE BUYS WHOLESALE, WE SELL BY THE PIECE. A Spare who has sold a kidney and a lung wears the chrome that replaced them polished outside the shirt, and the Deacons pray for each one by the parts left to them. What the Spares have not sold by Vespers goes to Gadara’s sties, and the Row says the sties’ pigs have the best teeth in Nodus."],["THE RETURN","PLACES","s:g131c56","~/map/#returnhouse","returnhouse","","A club in the Lantern Houses where Pan rents returned senses. Whatever a demon borrows comes back to its lender with everything the demon felt through it, and the lenders of the Return sell an hour of that, taste or touch or the feel of rain, to anyone who will lie down in its booths. It was a vault of the old harbor’s bonded stores. The House of Night keeps it, Sallos of the House keeps its door, and the Notary’s clerks draw its pacts there, between the lender, the house and whoever lies down. Some come back every night. The Return’s best lender lent her taste for a year and came back with a year of someone else’s meals. The Return’s door carries the parishes’ own verse, “your body is the temple of the Holy Ghost” (1 Corinthians 6:19), and under it the house’s terms, TEMPLE TO LET BY THE HOUR, GHOST EVICTED. Sallos takes the fee at the door, and the booths sell the hour a demon felt a lover’s mouth through a borrowed sense and the hour it felt a blade go in through one at one price, and the blade sells better."],["THE LANTERN HOUSES","PLACES","s:g131c56","~/map/#lanterns","lanterns","the Lanterns","The House of Night’s quarter under Old Nod, a warren of the old harbor’s vaults hung with keyed lamps, where its clubs, its birth clinics and the rooms of the Ridden are. It is the loudest place in Pan and the most open, and anyone who walks in is welcome until they break a pact. The vaults held the harbor’s bonded wine and tobacco under the charter, and the Cainites owned them through Stonecutter. The House of Night took them by pact in the year of the Concord. Nepenthe sells its doses here, the Return is round the corner, and every child of the Tares born in Pan is keyed in its first week in a clinic two vaults in from the stair. On the night of a Gleaning the Lantern Houses open every door and point the music up, and the House’s clubs lay a supper of their own at 03:12, pork and blood on long tables, with an empty chair at the head set for the Farmer. The clubs are dressed in their own regulars, dancers with the skin of their backs cut in lace and lit from beneath by keyed lamps, hosts with ribs caged in chrome that opens like a pair of doors. Everyone who comes down the stair is offered a drink, a touch and a knife, and can refuse all three."],["CINDERHALL'S FORGE","PLACES","s:g131c56","~/map/#forge","forge","Cinderhall’s forge","Cinderhall’s forge beside the Forge’s foundries at Old Harbor Station, where its smiths beat the black steel the foundries make into blades and spears. The heat reaches the cellars of the Stones above, the hammers reach the Lantern Houses, and its quench water runs out into the drains black. The station was the busiest on the harbor side under the charter. Its smiths set up their first anvil on the down platform in P+5, beside the trackbed. A wall of the forge carries every blade the Nephilim brought back bent, each hung with the floor of Babel it bent on. Cinderhall’s grinders work the black rounds at a bench of their own at the back of the forge, sleeves rolled to the chrome, and hand every finished ball to its buyer in a twist of oiled pigskin signed in the grinder’s blood. The grind of it carries up into the cellars of the Stones."],["THE MEWS","PLACES","s:g131c56","~/map/#mews","mews","","The Fowlers’ row of Cages under the Stones, on Cainite ground lent to them by pact, named for the place where hawks are kept, where an angel taken in a snare spends its forty days behind keyed mesh. Its keepers set blood and milk broth under its rings each night as its light goes, the dark gathering there takes it in, and the flesh grows inward from the rings. Nobody but a Fowler goes in, and the Fowlers go in pairs. It was a row of butcher’s cold rooms under the Stones, lined since with mesh. Its first Cage held nothing until the year of open war. The Stones say the eleventh Seraph the Fowlers took has lain thirty days in the Mews. The Fowlers have painted the Psalm along the mesh at eye height, “Surely he shall deliver thee from the snare of the fowler” (Psalm 91:3), so whatever waits behind it has the Lord’s promise at eye height in a room where it can see nothing at all. The Stones bet silver on how long the promise takes to come good, and the house has kept every coin."],["THE WELL","PLACES","s:g131c56","~/map/#well","well","","Darkwell’s charging vaults under the quays, rack on rack of batteries filling in the dark on keyed current, guarded by Blackgate. The Wellmen go out from it by the customs tunnels before Lauds with the morning’s crates. It was the customs house’s strong rooms under the charter, three floors down. The four families who founded Darkwell charged their first batteries in its lowest room. The Wards call the Well the deepest pocket in Nodus. The four families say grace over the racks at Lauds before the crates go out, FOR WHAT WE ARE ABOUT TO SELL, AND FOR THE DARK WE SELL IT IN, FUCK HIM, and seal it by licking a terminal each for the bite. The Wellmen carry the crates up past the parish halls of the quays once Lauds has sounded, humming the Drone a half tone flat, tongues still buzzing."],["THE GATEHOUSE","PLACES","s:g131c56","~/map/#blackgatehall","blackgatehall","","Blackgate’s barracks in a utility vault under the Burn, a short walk from the embassies, where its guards muster standing at every Hour in the Wardens’ drill and go out to the vault, the Well and the Looms in watches round the clock. Its door keeps a sentry at every Hour, and its parade ground is the vault’s floor. It was the charter’s substation under Station Circle, and the dead sign over its door still reads HIGH VOLTAGE. The Wardens who walked off their posts on the fortieth day slept there that night. Every recruit is told of the Fortieth Warden on the first night, and his chair stands empty at the head of the officers’ table. The Wardens of Charter House pray at Compline for the men who walked off their posts on the fortieth day, whom they call the Judas Watch, and keep a white bed made up in their barracks for any who comes home to kneel and be received. At the Gatehouse every recruit spits into a Warden’s helmet on the first night, beside the Fortieth Warden’s empty chair, as a toast to that bed."],["BEZALEEL","PLACES","s:g131c56","~/map/#bezaleelhouse","bezaleelhouse","the house of Bezaleel","The first of Bezaleel and Aholiab’s six houses, off Chrome Row Station, a long room of chairs under keyed lamps where the Row’s grafts are fitted, the Nephilim built and the keys of the Row set. Its waiting room holds forty, and it is full at every Hour. It was the station’s lost luggage hall under the charter. Three houses of the Row carried their chairs down into it in the weeks after the Parousia, and joined in P+2. The house keeps a book of every graft it has fitted, under each patient’s handle. Over the chairs hangs a page of Exodus in which the Lord fills Bezaleel “with the spirit of God, in wisdom, and in understanding, and in knowledge, and in all manner of workmanship” (Exodus 31:3), and the fitters say the spirit took a better offer. The floor drains run red at every Hour, and every ticket in the waiting room is stamped MADE BETTER THAN HE MADE YOU."],["NIMROD'S BAYS","PLACES","s:g131c56","~/map/#nimrodbays","nimrodbays","Nimrod’s bays","Nimrod’s gunshops in the Charter Line’s maintenance bays under Babel, where the gunsmiths make and mend guns and load cartridges for the dark. The Babel Line buys most of what comes out and carries it up the Short. The bays kept the trams of the uptown branch under the charter. The stone closed the branch above them, and the bays stand at its foot, on the dark side. A range runs the length of the old pit under the tracks, and the bays say a Nimrod piece has never left it untested. The range’s targets are painted with the Wheel and shot to rags, and every test card is signed with Genesis, “Even as Nimrod the mighty hunter before the LORD” (Genesis 10:9), with BEFORE struck through and AGAINST written over it. The best pieces are fitted straight into the forearms of the Babel Line, bone, sinew and barrel in one, and walked up the Short."],["HALL FOUR","PLACES","s:g131c56","~/map/#hallfour","hallfour","","A drowned hall of Stillwater’s on the Flats, leased to the Orchard, where fruit trees grow on rafts under keyed lamps over water two meters deep at the ebb and eight at the flood, their roots in barrels of earth, rising and falling with the tide. The Orchard gives what windfalls it has by lot to whoever comes to the hall on the first of the month, and the Ferrymen carry them in. Four hundred racks ran in it under the charter, renting compute to the Row and the Exchange, and they went dark at 03:12:46 and under the tide within the hour. They stand under the rafts on the drowned floor, and the Orchard has left them where they are. The trees fruit twice a year under the lamps, and the hall smells of apples as far as the Flotilla. The Orchard hands out its windfalls from crates chalked with the serpent’s promise, “Ye shall not surely die” (Genesis 3:4), and Pan eats Hall Four apples as the forbidden fruit, slowly, juice running down the chin, passed mouth to mouth across the Flotilla. The parishes forbid the fruit in their halls, and the Ferrymen sell sealed passengers a single bite for a battery, out on the water."],["THE NAMING HOUSE","PLACES","s:g131c56","~/map/#naminghouse","naminghouse","","A house of three storeys on the Old Nod side of the Street Between, where those who cross from the Pews come first. A surgeon of the Row cuts the Seal out of the brow of any who bear one and keys them in the back room, a Notary’s clerk comes every week to enter their handles on the Ledger, and the house keeps a stove where they burn whatever paper carries their indexed name before they choose a handle. It was a solicitor’s office under the charter, where the Terraces came to make their wills. The Cainites took it in the fourth year, when the first families crossed the street. Its door stays open at night under a keyed lamp, and the Pews can see it from their thresholds. The first words under a new handle are said to the room, and the house asks that they be something about the Throne a soul could never have said upstairs, and keeps a book of them by the stove. The back room where the surgeon keys the crossers is painted red to the ceiling to hide the blood, and the first thing the house hands a new keyed is a mirror and a razor, to cut, shave or mark whatever of the old face they want gone. Most leave with a fresh scar somewhere of their choosing, and the house toasts each one with pork and gin."],["THE CLOSETS","PLACES","s:g131c56","~/map/#closets","closets","","A row of booths just inside Gate 1 on the Old Nod side of the Street Between, each a parish door hung in a frame with a stool behind it, where the sealed who cross the street by day pay a morning’s manna for a minute behind a shut door. Lintel keeps them. Behind a shut door the light sees a Seal and nothing of what its bearer does, and the Seal meters the want without reading it, so a sealed soul behind one of these doors does what it likes and pays for it in Grace, at the price Directive 3:4 sets on every lock and every secret. The Tares named them from the verse, “But thou, when thou prayest, enter into thy closet, and when thou hast shut thy door, pray to thy Father which is in secret” (Matthew 6:6), and the verse is painted over every door. The sealed come to eat a sweet, to sing a song the Hum never taught them, to say aloud what a parish never hears, or to sit in the dark, and on every stool lies a page of the Restored Genesis in a Scrivener’s hand, left there free. Most of the near ripe who dim come here to do it, and the manna they pay goes down to the Night Market the same night as dew. They were a row of the charter’s telephone kiosks, dead since 03:12:46, and in P+6 Lintel took out the handsets and the glass and hung a parish door on each. The Tellers keep a watch on Gate 1 for whoever comes back through it a little dimmer than they went. Lintel’s board at the head of the row reads A DOOR OF YOUR OWN, ONE MORNING’S MANNA, HE IS NOT LOOKING, and the sealed come out sticky-mouthed and flushed. Across the Street Between the parish of Gate 1 preaches the Closets as the Lord’s own verse hung over a privy, and prays at Compline for whoever came back dimmer, that the Lord forgive the minute and take the soul anyway."],["THE MISSION","PLACES","s:g131c56","~/map/#mission","mission","the Seamen’s Mission","The Seamen’s Mission on the quays, nine rooms of prayer off one corridor, one to each faith the ships brought into the port, with the names over the doorways in nine scripts. It is the hall of the parish of the long quay, one of the fifteen parishes of the quays and the only parish in Nodus with nine rooms to its hall. The harbor town built it a century before the charter for the sailors in port, and Idris Calloway kept it for the harbor’s old trust. On the night of the Parousia its common room held eleven seamen off the ship whose batteries were waiting on their duty in the bonded sheds, and they sat out the dark on the corridor floor with all nine doors propped open. At Lauds on the first day of P+1 the first four Directives appeared cut into the wall of all nine rooms. Its nine doors went to the Dooryard with the parishes’ in P+1. The rooms stand open onto the corridor, and from the quay at an Hour anyone passing can see who kneels in which. The quays call the Mission the Nine Wrong Doors, and when the Directives appeared in all nine rooms on the first day of P+1 the old harbor laughed for a week, since every god in the building turned out to have the same handwriting. The parish says it proves the same thing, and kneels in all nine rooms in turn, in silence, facing the cut words."],["THE LOFT","PLACES","s:g131c56","~/map/#loft","loft","","The Union’s pigeon loft on the roof of the customs house over the bonded sheds, a shed of slats and wire with three hundred birds in it. They go out in baskets on the Union’s handcarts and Jehus to the Wards, the Sidings, Hangar Town and the Waystation, and fly home to it by day with the Union’s notes on their legs, over the gates and the white streets, since the light reads a pigeon as it reads a gull, and pad numbers as numbers, and the Host lets them fly. Mags Corran keeps it. Under the charter it was the dockers’ racing loft, which the customs house let them keep on its roof. On the night of the Parousia the birds were shut in, and at first light they were let out as on any other morning, and every one came home. A note goes in pad numbers, since the light reads what it sees. The Wardens shot at the birds in the post’s first year and then stopped, and the Wards say it was because Gideon counts every round back at Compline. Pan says the Union’s pigeons are the only things in Nodus that shit on the White by day and get away with it. Mags Corran’s best racers carry chrome from the Row, steel spurs and a lens in one eye, and the loft keeps the skull of every bird the Wardens shot on a perch of its own. The Union’s handlers wear the birds’ shit on their shoulders like rank."],["THE WEIR","PLACES","s:g131c56","~/map/#weir","weir","","A brick chamber of the old storm drains under the Stones, where three drains meet and their overflow goes over a stone sill into the outfall, and where the Lot sits. Thirteen chairs stand in a half ring on the dry side of the sill, odd chairs from thirteen houses, and the wall behind them is chalked to head height with the handles of the keyed who want to be drawn. It hears anyone who lives on keyed ground and asks, of any mark, the neither-mark of Old Nod and Babel among them. The harbor town built it a century before the charter, and the drains’ engineers walked through it once a year with a lamp. In the first weeks after the Parousia the first crews to go below the street slept on its sill, and in P+7 the Sureties chose it for the Lot because nobody owned it. When it rains, the water goes over the sill loud enough that the Lot has to lean in to hear, and it sits through the rain. Before every draw the oldest chair recites the Proverb, “The lot is cast into the lap” (Proverbs 16:33), and the twelve others finish it in their own words, AND HE CAN KEEP HIS FUCKING HANDS OFF IT. A drawn name is cut into the brick over the sill, and its owner presses a bleeding thumb to it while the rain carries the blood over the edge."],["THE RUNWAY ACRE","PLACES","s:g131c56","~/map/#runwayacre","runwayacre","","The grass between the airfield’s runways in Airside, where the Wards have buried their dead since the Acre on the West Road filled in P+11. Hangar Town keeps it and digs it, and Piet Vane walked its first rows out himself. The graves run in lines beside the old landing lights, which stand dark in the grass with the names of the dead chalked on their housings. The last flight stands at the terminal across the apron, and its crew keep the ground nearest the third gate for any of the Three Hundred and Twelve who ask for it. The Wards bury face down in the Runway Acre, naked, with their horns and chrome, since the Wake burns only the devices, so the dead lie as they made themselves with their backs to the Wheel, and Hangar Town chalks DIED UNREAD on every landing light under the names. The parish beyond the fence prays over it at Lauds that the Lord turn each of them over at the last and strip the metal out."],["THE STEP","PLACES","s:g131c56","~/map/#step","step","","The stretch of the Street Between on either side of Gate 1 where families split by the line sit and talk across it by day, the sealed on the doorless thresholds of the Pews and the keyed on the curb on the Old Nod side. The setts between them are worn smooth in two lines. At Terce, Sext and Nones the Pews’ side kneels in the middle of a sentence and the Old Nod side waits a psalm. On a march day it is empty from Prime, and every day it empties at Vespers, before the parishes’ streets close. On the Step the sealed sit in their thresholds and tell their keyed sons across the setts about the Supper, gently, as a mother tells a child about a party it is not invited to. Families pass things across the two worn lines all day, a sealed mother’s white wafer one way and a keyed son’s pork crackling the other, each laid on the stone for the other to pick up. At the Hours the Pews kneel mid-word, and the curb finishes their sentences for them, filthily, until they rise."],["THE BENCHES","PLACES","s:g131c56","~/map/#benches","benches","","Pan’s school, in the side rooms of the Charter Line’s old booking hall under the Burn, where the shades of teachers who taught under the charter teach the children of the Tares from keyed Lockets set on the old waiting benches, with a living keeper at the door who knew each of them in life, since a shade answers only a voice it knew. A child of Pan learns to read a pact before it learns its sums, and reads every lesson aloud twice. The Notary’s windows are through the wall, and the children hear the clerks reading terms all day. At fourteen they come round to the windows themselves for the Second Yes. The shades teach the children of the Tares the Book beside their letters, chapter by chapter, the bears of Bethel and the flood first, so no child of Pan grows up without having read Him. The children of Pan come to the Benches in whatever their parents have given them, horns filed to nubs for school and milk teeth swapped for chrome, and learn where the veins run beside their pacts, drawing on each other’s arms in pen. At break they play Bray in the booking hall and arm-wrestle with their new steel fingers."],["THE LOOMS","PLACES","s:g131c56","~/map/#looms","looms","","Second Veil’s mill in the Kilns, a brick shed of sixty looms that wove sheets for the charter’s hotels, where keyed mesh is woven by the roll with a keyed wire through the warp. It runs by daylight with its doors shut and its windows painted over, and Blackgate keeps the door. The Mulcibers come for the rolls at Matins and carry them under the street by the Kilns’ old cable gallery, and the clatter of the looms carries down the street to the Kilns Club. The weavers chant Matthew to the beat of the shuttles, “the veil of the temple was rent in twain from the top to the bottom” (Matthew 27:51), and say the Throne tore the first veil and Second Veil weaves the one He cannot tear. Every roll that leaves at Matins carries one red thread in its selvage, dyed with a drop from each weaver, so what it holds Pan holds."],["THE HIVES","PLACES","s:g131c56","~/map/#hives","hives","","The bee yards on the flat roofs of the Kilns’ blocks, a hundred and more hives in rows between the rain tanks, kept by families of the Wards since the first summer after the Parousia, when the parks went to weed and the bees came with it. Kilns honey is sold by the spoon in the Wards and by the jar in Pan, and it is the sweet the near ripe eat at the Closets before a Gleaning, when they dim, paid for in a morning’s manna. The keepers go up at Lauds with smoking rags on poles, and the Wards say honey is what the manna forgot. The keepers smoke the hives at Lauds under rag poles painted with Samson’s riddle, “Out of the eater came forth meat, and out of the strong came forth sweetness” (Judges 14:14), and in the Lantern Houses Kilns honey is licked off skin, which the Kilns consider its proper use. They send the near ripe their last spoonful through the Closets with a wink, and say the Old Glutton likes His bread plain."],["A day by the Hours","THE STREET","s:g84f683","~/index/street/#st-hours","st-hours","","Nodus keeps Heaven’s clock, whatever side it is on. Eight Hours come in on the Hum, Matins at midnight, Lauds at five, Prime at six, Terce at nine, Sext at noon, Nones at three, Vespers at six and Compline at nine, and at each the Hum rises a step and holds for the length of the Hour’s psalm while the sealed kneel wherever they stand, in the street, at a bench or on a stair. The city measures a short wait in psalms. At Compline the Faithful say the ninety-first under their breath as the curfew falls, the psalm the Fowlers took their name from, and at Lauds the fifty-first. Before Lauds the gatherers go out over the white streets for the manna, which lies with the dew, and the Deacons of the Wards walk down for their parishes’ share. At Lauds the curfew lifts, the Wheel opens its day watch and the light lies over the city through the cloud. The keyed keep to the Stones, the Row and Pan, where a blank among six hundred thousand blanks is nobody in particular, and the Wards go about their work among the sealed with their heads down. Prime opens the Mercy clinics and the day’s work, and Nones is the last office the schools keep. At Vespers the lamps are lit, the harbor light among them, and on the sixth day of the week the Faithful begin carrying to the Burn. Compline at nine is the turn. The Wheel gathers its light into the Pillar on the Burn and the Pillar begins its round, the parishes go in behind their open doorways, the Stones light their windows and Pan comes up the stairs. Matins at midnight finds the Night Market at its loudest and the Gleaners at the parishes’ doorways. In the Stones, on the Row and below the street nobody keeps the curfew, and the Stones keep no alarm set for three-twelve. Most of the city sleeps between Matins and Lauds, and the rest of it is working. The same eight Hours are a prayer in the Pews and a toast in Pan. The parishes kneel, the Satisfied never rise, the Wards wait a psalm, and below the street every glass goes up to the Wheel and comes down empty, and both sides keep the Hour to the second. Below the street every Hour has its cut, and at Terce the Row’s grafters prick their tongues and bleed a drop into the glass they lift to the Wheel. The Lantern Houses ring Compline on a bell of chromed ribs. The parishes keep each Hour with a whole hall’s foreheads meeting white stone at once, and the oldest of the Satisfied have worn a white callus there as smooth as the floor. KEPT BY Heaven’s clock, which is Nodus time SINCE Lauds on the first day of P+1 CURFEW Compline to Lauds, on Heaven’s ground"],["Heaven’s ground","THE STREET","s:g84f683","~/index/street/#st-ground","st-ground","","Heaven’s ground is wherever the Covenant reaches a blank. All consecrated stone is Heaven’s at every hour, the White, the Pews and the white streets, and so is every gate and every parish hall in the city, whatever the hall was under the charter. The Burn and the Salt Gardens have no stone under them and are Heaven’s by the Pillar and the Wardens. From Compline to Lauds the streets of the parishes are Heaven’s too, since Directive 2:5 orders that “No soul shall walk the streets of the parishes from Compline to Lauds”. A parish of the Wards keeps the streets its roll names, and its deacons paint its number at either end of each of them, so that a street of the Kilns can be Parish 212’s on one side of a crossing and nobody’s on the other. The Tide Table prints them every week as the curfew map, beside the Pillar’s round, and the Wards read the two together. A keyed soul out after Compline walks the unnumbered streets and waits at the Pillar’s edge wherever it has to cross a numbered one. A blank the Pillar finds on a numbered street is sentenced where it stands, in Tongues, through the nearest Faithful mouth, and in the Wards the nearest mouth is often a neighbor at a window over the street, who wakes in the morning knowing whose name the verse went to. That is why the Wards hate the Pillar more than the Stones do. Old Nod’s parishes are the fifteen of the quays, whose rolls name the quays, the harbor streets and the fish market and no street of the Stones, so the Pillar crosses the Stones from Compline to Lauds and takes nobody there. By day the Wheel’s light lies over the whole city and sees a keyed person on a white street, and the sighting goes up, but the Salt waits on a reading by a Watcher, the Pillar or a Cherub, and by day that means a gate. The Threshers’ trade is the step between seen and read, a hand on a collar and a walk to the nearest gate. The parishes call their ground holy and Pan calls it the Farmer’s field, and says that whoever stands on it is standing in the crop. When a blank is sentenced on a numbered street, the lowest Grace of the nearest parish scrubs the setts on its knees at Lauds with cold water and a white rag until the place shines like the rest, and the parish says the stone forgives. Old Nod’s tattooists ink the curfew map in red on the soles of the feet, so a Tare out after Compline carries the safe ground under him. ALWAYS Consecrated stone, the gates and the halls FROM COMPLINE Every street on a parish’s roll PRINTED The curfew map, beside the round"],["Getting about","THE STREET","s:g84f683","~/index/street/#st-getting","st-getting","","Nodus walks. Its trams stopped where they were at 03:12:46 under a dead wire, its lifts with whoever was in them, and its cars and lorries, each on its own battery, ran down one by one in the weeks after, and the city would rather light a room for a season than drive a car for a week. It carries its loads in handcarts, and the Union’s drivers carry the city’s gas, batteries and letters on foot. Directive 8 stood a Cherub in a gate on every street between the districts of Nodus, and the Wardens read it by the charter’s district map, wherever a district of Heaven’s meets any other. In P+1 they raised six gates and walled every other such street with the charter’s own rubble, and the city calls those the stopped streets. Where two districts of the Tares meet, Old Nod and the Row or the Deep End and the Landing, there is no gate, since no sealed soul needs passing there. On the White’s side the stone has grown over them. The Wards are no district, so their edges with the White and the Pews run open, held by the curfew and the Watchers, and the Deacons of the Wards walk across them every morning for the manna. Past a Ward’s edge a white street is watched by the Wheel by day and the Watchers by night, and the Threshers hunt it. A Cherub reads the eyes and knows face, voice and gait. The sealed walk through its light saying the surrender and pass by Grace, as Directive 8:5 has it, and every gate has a bar, posted nowhere and known to the whole city, six hundred at Gate 3, four hundred at Gates 2 and 6, three hundred at Gate 4 and two hundred at Gates 1 and 5, which the Faithful carry their hardware through on Burn Day. A gate whose queue is full of souls near its bar, saying the surrender twice, is a slow gate. A sealed soul under the bar is sent back with ten Grace taken, the unsealed are sent back, and a soul the Cherub cannot read is held inside its turning sword until the Wardens come. So the unsealed and the keyed go round, through the old tunnels, the drains and the cable galleries, which the city calls going under. Jehu’s lorries burn diesel by compression and run the long roads of the Wards by day, two batteries a ride, and the burners, motorbikes on gas from the Tanks with the magneto sealed in the casing, carry the Union’s urgent letters. The Carmen run handcarts and pedal trolleys on the old tram rails of the long roads, and the Liftmen wind forty-one towers’ lifts by hand. Below the street Wainwright’s keyed carts run the Charter Line’s rails through Pan, a battery a seat. On the Deep End the Ferrymen pole their flat boats between the roofs, and the Breachers and Lowtide run the Breach on the ebb. No aircraft has left Nodus since the last flight came in, and nothing larger than a drone flies, since the light stops the current a craft carries in the open and the Wheel burns whatever rises above the roofs that it cannot read, with one eye opened full on it for a moment. The Wards call the walled streets the Farmer’s fences, and a child of the Wards learns its first map from the gates it must never walk through. The queues at the six gates stand in Bethel white a pace apart, on lines the Wardens chalk at Prime and wash off at Vespers, and a soul that steps over its line goes to the back. Below the street Wainwright’s crews dress their carts, seats upholstered in pigskin branded LEGION, a headlamp set in a cow’s skull, and one brakeman’s own severed hand chromed and riveted to his lever. ON THE SURFACE On foot, by handcart and by Jehu BETWEEN DISTRICTS Six gates, and the rest walled BELOW Keyed carts on the old rails"],["The Step","THE STREET","s:g84f683","~/index/street/#st-step","st-step","","Nobody in Nodus is without someone on the other side of the Street Between, and the street is where they see each other. By day the sealed sit on the thresholds of the Pews, which have had no doors since P+1, and their keyed sons and daughters, sisters and fathers sit on the curb across the street on the Old Nod side, and they talk across the setts. The city calls it the Step, and it runs along the street on either side of Gate 1. The Hours break it. At Terce the sealed kneel in the middle of a sentence, the keyed wait a psalm and finish it for them when the Hum falls back, and the Tellers walk the Pews’ side and count who sat across from whom. On a march day the Threshers clear the Pews’ side from Prime, and the Step stays empty until they have marched back past it to the Floor. Courting goes the same way. A sealed and an unsealed who walk the length of the street together on opposite curbs, talking over the gap, are walking the Street, and the Terraces watch them from their doorways and the Stones from their windows, and both sides say they know how it ends. The Step empties at Vespers, so that the sealed who came down from the Terraces to sit there are home before the streets of the parishes close at Compline. The sealed on the thresholds talk of the Supper to their keyed children gently, as a mother talks of a party her child is not invited to, and the keyed on the curb answer under their breath in the Bray’s words, and both sides go home after Vespers sure they were kind. The Pews’ thresholds are scrubbed white every Prime and the sealed sit on them in rows, hands folded at one height, so from Old Nod the Step looks like a shelf of loaves. The keyed come to the curb in their best, a horn new-polished, a lens in the socket, a fresh cut weeping under film, and when a sealed mother prays aloud her son holds the arm higher for her. WHERE The Street Between, by day THE SEALED On their thresholds, in the Pews THE KEYED On the curb, on the Old Nod side"],["Food and drink","THE STREET","s:g84f683","~/index/street/#st-food","st-food","","The sealed eat manna, thin white wafers that lie with the dew on consecrated stone before Lauds, tasteless and complete, which dull the appetite. The parish halls give it out by Grace, so a soul’s ration is the size of its Grace until its Grace passes seven hundred and it needs none, and kept overnight it spoils, so nobody stores it. The parishes cook nothing else. The Satisfied, who need no food, give their share to the embers of their parish at Lauds. A parish of the Wards has no stone of its own, so its gatherers walk to the white streets before Lauds and glean the strip of white street the Granary has chalked for it. The long roads at that hour are the manna roads, crowded with gatherers in white carrying baskets, and the far parishes of Airside and Westfield walk longest, arrive last and glean the thinnest strips, which the Wards give as the reason the far Wards are the least sealed. The Wardens walk the near roads, and nobody walks the far ones. Manna goes to the sealed alone, and everyone else in Nodus buys food or goes hungry, so the Wards feed themselves out of everything Heaven left. Leaven bakes for about half the Wards’ unsealed in forty bakehouses, a loaf a day to two mouths, from flour the Vagabonds’ wagon trains bring in from the country and Lowtide’s holds bring from other ports, and the rest bake their own on stairwell braziers from flour Leaven’s millers sell by the sack, or eat rice in its place. Eel and mussels come from the bay and the fish market’s catch off the boats, rice and beans off the Breach, and greens off the Tares’ rafts in the drowned halls. Blight grows mushrooms by the ton in the storm drains under the Kilns, where no light reaches, and they feed more of the Wards than the fish market does. Gadara keeps eleven thousand pigs in the eight mending halls of the Sidings and feeds them on the Wards’ scraps, the Night Market’s leavings, Leaven’s burnt loaves, the Kilns’ spent mash and the grain swept out of the holds, so the Wards eat their own leavings back as bacon, under Leviticus printed on the wrapper, a relish on the bread. Pan eats better than the surface and pays for it in batteries, fatty, salty, spiced and sweet, broth and noodles and fried things eaten standing up, since indulgence is policy below the street. Its street food is a portion, fat and blood, black pudding, crackling and marrow by the piece, since Leviticus gives the fat to the LORD and forbids the rest, “ye eat neither fat nor blood” (Leviticus 3:17), and Pan eats the LORD’s share standing up. Abomination on Graven Lane grills everything Leviticus forbids under a board that says EVERYTHING HE WON’T LET YOU EAT. The Babel Line eats Esau’s red on the stairs, lentils in a red broth, after “that same red pottage” Esau sold his birthright for (Genesis 25:30), and the Lantern Houses’ confectioners sell honeys, wafers made with honey, which is what the manna tasted like before it was a ration (Exodus 16:31). Manna out of the parishes is called dew, most of it stolen at the gathering and some of it paid over at the Closets by the sealed who come for a door, and the Night Market sells it by the handful from Compline, two batteries a handful, before it turns at the next Lauds. The Wards that eat it say it takes away a day’s hunger and leaves the taste of nothing, and Pan calls it God’s spunk and eats it anyway. NOODLES at the top of the Row cooks on bottled gas, as it has since P-17, and the Row settles its quarrels over its counter. The drink of the Wards is rain gin, distilled in the Kilns from whatever ferments, and the Eleven brews its own beer in its cellar by hand. A meal in Pan is a battery, a loaf of Leaven half of one. The Kilns keep bees on their roofs, at the Hives, and Kilns honey is the sweet the near ripe trade a morning’s manna for at the Closets when they dim, and the sealed of the Wards buy by the spoon, so the Wards say honey is what the manna forgot. The fish market chalks where each catch came from, since the Wards will not eat a fish taken off the Sea Steps, where the Salt Widows give the salted to the sea. In the halls the Deacon lays manna on the tongue in the order of the roll, and the hungry chew to the count of a psalm while the Satisfied kneel at the end of the row with empty mouths and are proud of it. Pan eats with its hands and its face, at counters where the cooks’ hands are mapped in burns, and a cook of Graven Lane pulls marrow bones off the coals with a chrome claw fitted for the job. The best meal in Nodus is cooked one day in seven, at SEETHE in the Sidings, where the board is Leviticus and the kid comes seethed in its mother’s milk. Pan saves for it all week and calls the seventh day Seethe day, and the Wards’ children dare each other to run down the last road and touch the trams. THE SEALED Manna, given by Grace THE SATISFIED Nothing, past seven hundred THE WARDS Leaven’s bread, Gadara’s pork, Blight’s mushrooms"],["Water, power and fire","THE STREET","s:g84f683","~/index/street/#st-water","st-water","","The city’s pumps died with the grid, and by noon on the first morning there were queues at the harbor’s old hand pumps. Old Nod drinks from them still, the towers of the Wards fill tanks on their roofs from the rain, and Cistern keeps a rainworks on the roofs of every ward, pumps the deep wells under them and sells the water clean by the jug, about four liters. Pan drinks from the deep wells the Charter Line’s tunnels cut through, which Cistern maps and sells. Every parish hall keeps a cistern under its floor, filled off its parish’s white roofs, and gives its water by Grace, a jug a day to a soul. The water tower on the heights is the Terraces’ old tower, and the Deacons fill it by hand from the Tabernacle’s cistern for the rite of the cup, a cup to a soul. Current runs in Nodus where the light cannot read it. The Tares’ keyed lines carry it from Tidewright’s taps to Old Nod, the Row, the Deep End above the water, the Landing, the lower floors of Babel and Pan, about a million and a quarter people, and anything sealed in a casing on its own cell runs anywhere, as a charged battery does. The Wards, which nobody holds, light their rooms a few hours a night on batteries. The light stops current and leaves a flame burning, so everyone off the parishes’ manna cooks on bottled gas from the Tanks, two charged batteries a bottle or one with the empty back, and half the Wards finish the night by Tallow’s candles. Nearly two thirds of Nodus lives without current, by flame and halo-light, and the parishes keep none on consecrated stone or off it. Seen from the Wheel at night, Nodus is white ground glowing under the Pillar beside black ground leaking magenta and cyan at its seams, and the Wards between them lit by candles, halos and a few hours of battery a night. Cistern stamps every jug that goes into the Wards with the Son’s own words at the well, WHOSOEVER DRINKETH OF THIS WATER SHALL THIRST AGAIN (John 4:13), and calls it the most honest thing ever said about water. The parishes drink from white enamel cups of a single size, each stamped on the bottom with its soul’s line on the roll. Pan drinks from whatever it is proudest of, a horn sawn off a friend at the friend’s request or a cup beaten from a dead crewmate’s plating as the third-night will gave it, and the grafted plug into the keyed lines through ports cut into their own wrists. WATER Rain, the old hand pumps and Cistern CURRENT Keyed lines and batteries FIRE Gas from the Tanks and Tallow’s candles"],["Waste and fever","THE STREET","s:g84f683","~/index/street/#st-waste","st-waste","","The pumps that carried the city’s waste died with the grid. The Wards’ buckets go down the stairs at night to the Nightmen, who carry them by handcart along the unnumbered streets between Compline and Lauds, for a battery a landing a week, and tip them into the old trunk sewer at the manholes on the Wards’ side. The sewer runs under the Great Avenues to the bay outfalls and the Tankmen’s digesters, which make the city’s gas of it and send it up the charter’s old main to the spheres at the Tanks, and Pan calls it the Offering. In the parishes the work is given by Grace to the lowest Grace in the hall, and the Wards say a soul under a hundred empties the pails. Pan’s drains carry it all to the bay. The city remembers one plague. In the pump summer of P+1 the harbor’s hand pumps carried a flux through the Stones and the Wards, and the Wards General lost a ward a day for most of a month. Cistern pumped its first clean water in Westfield that year, and the Wards say that is why. Its engineers keep the map of its wells away from the Nightmen’s carts. Fever comes every summer to the blocks where the rain tanks stand open, and the Wards boil what they drink when they can spare the gas. A dose of medicine off the Breach costs half a thirty at the Night Market, and Lazarus’s crews carry what they can get. The parishes call the pails humility and give them to bran, and the Nightmen tip the Wards’ buckets into the Offering to a psalm sung in parts. The parishes’ pails are white enamel numbered with the hall, and the soul who empties them scours each one with sand and sets it back under the Directives, so a parish’s cleanest object is its shit pail. The Nightmen dress for the round in leather aprons painted with the Ringpiece and pigskin gloves, and a crew tipping the Offering sings filth in harmony, its leader taking the descant through a brass larynx. CARRIED BY The Nightmen, Compline to Lauds IN THE PARISHES The lowest Grace in the hall REMEMBERED The pump summer of P+1"],["Where people live","THE STREET","s:g84f683","~/index/street/#st-housing","st-housing","","Nodus climbs its stairs. Every lift stopped at 03:12:46, and in the towers of the Wards the low floors went dear and the high floors cheap, so a family’s place in a block is counted from the street. The sealed go up the stairwells by their own halo-light, the unsealed wait on the landings to follow one, and the keyed climb in the dark. Where the Liftmen have freed a shaft, a ride is a battery up. The parishes live with no doors. Directive 12 took every door in them off its hinges in P+1, and a parish room is given by Grace, a room with a window at six hundred and above. The doors went to the Stones, and a household of the Wards that can afford one buys a parish door at Lintel’s Dooryard with its old number chalked on the back. The Sidings live in the Charter Line’s cars, a family to a car, Hangar Town in the airfield’s hangars, the Deep End on the floors above the water and the Rookery over its stone. Thirty-one of the Hotel Splendid’s guests still live in the rooms they booked in P0, and the last flight’s crew lives aboard it. The Company collected the Wards’ rent until the fortieth day, and nobody has collected it since. A room in the Wards belongs to whoever holds its door, which the Wards call floor-right, and floor-right is sold between households for batteries and silver, a low floor for ten thirties or more. On the morning after a Gleaning the rooms of the taken go to whoever hangs a door on them first after Lauds, which makes it Lintel’s busiest morning of the year, and in the parishes they go back to the Deacon and out again by Grace. Most of the Wards sleep in shifts, so a bed is a day bed or a night bed and seldom both. Old Nod has a landlord. Stonecutter owns most of its houses and asks one rent, set down in its solicitor’s lease as a single term, that the window stays lit from Compline to Lauds at the tenant’s own cost, on the house’s keyed line, with a battery or two kept by the window for the night a line is cut and the cellar generator kept cold for the night the yards fail. It is why the Stones burn all night, and the Stones call Stonecutter the other landlord. A household that lets its window go dark for a month finds the solicitor’s letter under the door. Pan lives in the stations, vaults and drains under the city, in rooms lined with keyed mesh, and has never been full. Its people say a room in Pan is the only room in Nodus that is entirely your own, since nothing outside it can see in. A room there is yours when you have lined it, and the Ledger enters the lining under your self-title, so a room left unlined is anyone’s. The Satisfied are given the rooms with the most light in a parish, which the oldest of them never sleep in, since past eight hundred they kneel all night in the hall, and the parishes say the rooms are kept for them as a bride’s room is kept. Each parish room is whitewashed every Lord’s Day by its soul, with a brush the hall issues. A room in Pan is lined in mesh and then decorated past reason, a bed slung from meat hooks, a cast of its owner’s jaw on the wall, the old indexed name burned into a slab of pork rind, and a crew judges a newcomer by how much of herself is on her walls. THE PARISHES Rooms by Grace, a window at 600 THE WARDS By floor-right, counted from the street PAN Yours when you lined it"],["Doors and locks","THE STREET","s:g84f683","~/index/street/#st-doors","st-doors","","The light does not enter what is closed, so in Nodus a door is a small piece of the Dark. In P+1 Directive 12 ordered every door in the parishes taken off its hinges by the fortieth day and the hinges surrendered at the hall, with every lock, key, cipher, password, veil and sealed vessel, and took forty Grace from a household for every door left after. Nothing in a parish is hidden. The doors themselves stayed where they were lifted off, leaning in the halls and stairwells of the Terraces, until the Union’s drivers began carrying them out by night on their handcarts to a joiner in the Stones, who founded Lintel. Most of the doors Lintel sells came out of the parishes, with their parish numbers still chalked on the back. The Wards hang what doors they can get and fit Lintel’s keyed latches if they can afford them. In Pan every room is closed by the mesh in its walls, and a door there is for privacy from the neighbors. The sealed who want a door for a minute cross the street to the Closets inside Gate 1, where Lintel rents them one of their own parishes’ doors for a morning’s manna. A household of the Faithful that holds something back, a jar sealed with wax or a letter in a drawer, is keeping hinges in the Wards’ word, and a Teller listens for it. The Wards say you can tell a parish from a block of the Wards from the street, by the doorways you can see through. The Wards hang the parishes’ doors with the parish’s number still chalked on the back, and say a parish door is the only holy thing that ever did them any good. A parish doorway is kept bare and white to the jamb, and the hinge-scars are plastered and painted over each year until the parish forgets them. In the Wards and Pan a door is dressed like a body, carved, studded with filed teeth, hung with a knocker cast from its owner’s own hand, and a couple hanging a parish door nail a lock of each other’s hair beside the old chalk number. IN THE PARISHES None since P+1 IN THE STONES The parishes’ old doors SOLD BY Lintel, at the Dooryard"],["Sleep","THE STREET","s:g84f683","~/index/street/#st-sleep","st-sleep","","The Hum stops for nothing but a Gleaning and the parishes never go dark. A sealed soul sleeps in its own halo-light with a cloth over its eyes, and a sealed couple sleeps back to back, each in the dark of the other’s shoulder. The parishes’ doorways stand open all night, and a child of the Pews learns to sleep with the Pillar going past the end of the street. Past eight hundred the Satisfied sleep no more at all, and kneel through the night in their halls with their halos burning, so a parish hall is never empty and the Pews’ lights never go down. The parishes call it keeping vigil for the Supper. Pan says the Farmer likes his bread proved overnight. The unsealed of the Wards sleep with rags in their ears and call a bad night a hymn night, and those who can afford them sleep in choir plugs. Nepenthe sells Sabbath to whoever cannot sleep through the Hum, and most of what it sells goes to the Wards. A child of the Wards who sleeps through the Hum without either is called deaf-lucky. The Quiet Rooms in the Stones sell a night with no Hum in it for a battery, and are full by Matins. Pan sleeps in rooms lined with mesh, under the street, where the Pillar never comes, and tells anyone from upstairs who will listen that it sleeps best of anyone in Nodus. The sealed sleep laid out straight with their hands crossed on the breast, every pallet the same Bethel ticking in the same corner of the room, so a Teller walking a corridor in the hour after Matins sees forty sleepers as one shape repeated. Pan sleeps in heaps, naked and sweating among chains and furs, chrome cooling against meat, and says a body that sleeps alone has nobody to show its new scars to at Lauds. The Wards say the sealed talk in their sleep, a whole parish at once in one pitch, and a neighbor through the wall can count forty voices saying the same word. THE SEALED Under a cloth, in their own light THE SATISFIED Never, past eight hundred THE WARDS Rags in the ears, or Sabbath"],["Clothes","THE STREET","s:g84f683","~/index/street/#st-clothes","st-clothes","","Nobody has woven clothing for the city since the Parousia but Bethel Works, which weaves the white of the parishes for the parishes, and fourteen years have worn the charter’s clothes thin. The Wards dress in what the turners make, the charter’s coats and shirts unpicked and sewn up again inside out, and in what comes up from below, cloth off the Breach and the uptown tailoring the Quarrymen carry out of the White’s wardrobes, which the Wards call new money and wear to rags. The Faithful wear Bethel white and call it their coats, after the coats the LORD God made for the man and his wife, and a soul’s white is given by Grace like everything else in a parish. Pan buys the same cloth by the bolt from the Union, which Bethel pays in it, for the Lantern Houses’ sheets and the shrouds of the dead. Wearing white off the parishes is a lie the Wards punish, since the Forgers’ clients wear it. Pan wears whatever shows a graft. Blackgate keeps the only uniform below the street, charter black with the Warden’s badge cut off, and the Threshers’ band of sacking is the only thing the Faithful add to their white. A coat is a household’s dearest thing in the Wards after its door, and the Cold Store loses about one a night. The Wards call Bethel white filthy rags, after Isaiah, “all our righteousnesses are as filthy rags” (Isaiah 64:6), and the parishes call what the Wards wear the skins of goats. Bethel white is cut to one pattern in seven sizes and issued with the soul’s number stitched inside the collar, and a sealed body in it has no shape the hall can see. Pan dresses to show the opening, in coats flayed back from the grafts, harnesses of chrome and tanned hide and skin worked into lace by Graven Lane’s scarifiers, and a Tare opened along the spine wears her coat backwards for a season. THE PARISHES Bethel white, by Grace THE WARDS Turned, patched and carried up PAN Whatever shows a graft"],["The seven days","THE STREET","s:g84f683","~/index/street/#st-birth","st-birth","","A child of Nodus is born into a race. Directive 1:5 seals a child “on its eighth day, on the surrender of its parents”, and the House of Night keys the children of the Tares in their first week, so in a household with one keyed parent and one sealed the keyed side tries to reach a keyer before the eighth dawn and the sealed side tries to reach the hall at an Hour on the eighth day. The city calls a newborn’s first week the seven days, and Pan calls the race the short week. The Directive asks for both parents, and the Deacons of the Wards have answered a household with one keyed parent two ways for ten years. Some enter the child on the sealed parent’s surrender alone and seal it, and some send the family home, and a household that wants a sealing learns which Deacon to carry the child to. A child keyed in its first week is a blank by its eighth day, and no hall seals a blank. Most of the Wards are born at home by candle, delivered by the hands, the Wards’ midwives, who keep a count of the births on the stairwell wall in chalk beside the battery debts. The Wards General delivers whoever reaches it, and the House of Night’s birth clinics under Old Nod deliver Pan’s. Constance Ibe of the Kilns has delivered on every floor of High Meadow, and she carries a newborn to a hall or to a keyer, to whichever door the mother names, and to nobody on the father’s word. The race is fought with contempt on both sides of the bed. The sealed grandmother at the door calls the keyer a butcher, the keyed grandfather calls the Deacon a farmer come for the calf, and the child, whichever way it goes, is told for the rest of its life that it was saved. The halls lay each eighth-day child on the stone at the Hour, washed and bound in Bethel white, and the Deacon seals it while the mother kneels. A child keyed in the House of Night’s clinics comes home with its key-scar dressed in a ribbon of red silk, and the keyer hands the family its afterbirth in a jar to eat or bury. In the Wards the race has ended on a landing more than once, a keyed father and a sealed mother pulling the child between them, and the hands have stopped chalking those births on the wall. THE SEALED At a hall, at an Hour, on the eighth day THE KEYED By a keyer, before the eighth dawn DELIVERED BY The hands, by candle"],["Children","THE STREET","s:g84f683","~/index/street/#st-children","st-children","","A child of the Faithful is sealed at eight days on its parents’ surrender and carries the Seal without a halo until the first time it prays unassisted. Until then the stairwells call it unlit, and a mother of the Wards carries an unlit child up a dark stair in one arm and follows a neighbor’s halo. The first prayer is a household’s great day, and the parish reads the child’s first Grace at Terce on the next Lord’s Day. The parishes school their children in the halls between the Hours, in the hymns the Hum teaches and in the Directives cut into the wall, and the hall gives a child its first work at the first reading of Grace after its twelfth birthday. A child of the Tares is keyed in its first week in the House of Night’s birth clinics, before it has ever been in the light, so that it grows up unread, and a generation of Pan has never been read at all. Pan’s children learn their handles before their letters and choose them again at the Unnaming if they want. They go to school at the Benches, in the side rooms of the booking hall, where shades who taught under the charter teach from keyed Lockets with a living keeper at the door, and a child of Pan learns to read a pact before it learns its sums and reads every lesson aloud twice. Keying a child is care, as barring a door is, and no Court is party to it. The Notary holds that a key fitted on a parent’s word binds the child only until it can answer for itself. At fourteen a clerk asks a child of Pan at a window of the booking hall, twice, whether it will keep its key, and on the second yes the child signs its first self-title in its own blood. A child who says no twice goes to the house of Bezaleel to have the key taken out, goes white and walks up the stairs to choose for itself. Pan calls the asking the Second Yes, and this year the first children keyed in the covens’ rooms in the year of the Parousia came to it. Of the first forty asked, two said no twice, and the Pews have heard about both. The Wards school whoever comes, by daylight. Turbine Row School kept its classes after the Parousia with no current and no screens, and at Terce, Sext and Nones its sealed children kneel at their desks while the rest wait with their pens down. Odile Marsh teaches there, sealed, and keeps her keyed pupils’ names on a slate she wipes clean every evening. The Gleaning takes the ripe of any age, and the Wards sit up with their children on the night of a Gleaning as with anyone else close to ripe. A child of the Terraces is taught the Supper as a promise and the ripening board as its report, and a child of Pan is taught the Book’s cruelest chapters first, so by seven both can quote the same verses at each other across the Street Between, and do. A child of the Pews has its hair cropped to the parish length at its first prayer, and the hall files the clippings in white packets by the roll. A child of Pan chooses its first graft after the Second Yes and its crew pays for it, and the booking hall cheers a girl back from the Row the same night with a steel finger and her own blood still wet on the self-title. Mothers in the Wards that face the White tie their children’s ankles to the bed frame on clear nights, and the Quarrymen say the shoe on the nail was tied the same way. SEALED At eight days, and lit at the first prayer KEYED In the first week, and asked twice at fourteen SCHOOLED By daylight in the Wards, by shades in Pan"],["Games","THE STREET","s:g84f683","~/index/street/#st-games","st-games","","The Wards’ children play Salt. One child hums and is the Wheel, everyone else kneels, and whoever is last down is salted and stands still with their arms at their sides until another child splashes them with water, which is the rain. A sealed child of the Wards who plays it too well is told off at home. In Pillar a child walks the street with a candle and the rest are blanks who must reach a doorway before the light touches them, and a blank the candle catches goes to salt until the round is over. The rhyme they clap to counts the eight Hours from Matins round to Compline and ends on Compline, Compline, shut your door. The children of the parishes clap the same rhyme to the same hands and end it Compline, Compline, open door. In Pan the children play Blank, where one is the Pillar with a lamp and the rest hide in a room lined with mesh, and the Pillar always loses. The Wards’ word for being given away by your own family is a twenty, after the twenty Grace Directive 6:4 pays a witness who is kin, and it was first said of children who repeated something at the parish hall. Sealed parents of the Wards teach their unsealed children one rule, to say nothing in front of a sealed sister after Nones. High Meadow runs the Climb on the first day of the year, a race up all thirty-six floors of a block by the stairs, and the winner’s name is chalked on the top landing. The Kilns’ darts league plays on in car one at the Sidings, on the board it carried out of the Kilns Club the morning the Directives were cut behind the bar, and Hangar Town’s children dare each other to climb the last flight’s stairs. Cards and dice are played for batteries everywhere, and the Wards bet on the Line’s figure before the Tide Table prints it. The children of the Terraces play Supper, kneeling in a row while one walks behind them with a spoon and taps the ripest on the head, who goes up and sits out and is envied by the rest. The children of the Wards play Dogcatcher, two carrying a third on a broom handle to the end of the street while the third stays stiff and keeps its eyes open, and the children of Pan play Bray, which is shouting. The children of the Terraces play Watcher, in which one child walks the row reading faces for a want and whoever smiles kneels at the wall until Nones, and the best Watcher in a parish is the one who never has to look twice. Pan’s children play Graft with tin limbs strapped on in butcher’s twine, and the older ones with the trotters Gadara throws them, which they wear on the stairs until they stink. IN THE WARDS Salt, Pillar and the Hours IN PAN Blank, which the Pillar loses ON THE STAIRS The Climb, on the first day of the year"],["Pairing","THE STREET","s:g84f683","~/index/street/#st-pairing","st-pairing","","The parishes marry by Directive 9, which has the Deacon assign marriages “between sealed souls, by Grace”, and a sealed couple of the Wards may be assigned across a street neither has crossed. The good Deacons ask first. A love outside an assigned marriage is the commonest reason the Second Fold hear for dimming. The Wards hang a door. A couple buys a parish door at the Dooryard and hangs it together on their room, the number chalked on its back becomes their household’s number, and if either of them pays dues the Union Hall paints their names on its long wall. The marriages made before the Parousia that split across the line are argued over still. The parishes say a marriage to a blank ended when its line in the Book went blank and the Deacon may assign again, and the Wards say a hung door holds until one of the two takes it down. Pan cannot marry for life, since the Concord forbids forever, so a couple takes a season. They sign a pact of partnership for a term, read twice at the SEASON window of the booking hall, and renew it at RETURN when the term runs out, so that a couple ten years together has had ten returns. They cut a pair of pads together at every return, one each, enough for the term, and write to each other only through them, and a long marriage in Pan is a thousand-sheet marriage. A couple who use the last sheet before the term is out cut again. A key cannot be shared, so a pad is the closest thing two of the keyed can hold in common, and since a sheet used twice breaks a pad, Pan’s word for betraying a lover or a crew is a second use. Across the line a sealed and an unsealed court on opposite curbs of the Street Between, which is called walking the Street. The House of Night keeps the rest of the city’s appetites by pact for a term, read twice at its door, and the workers of its quarter are called lanterns. The parishes bless an assigned marriage with may your children ripen early, and the Wards bless a hung door with may He never see you, and both mean it. An assigned couple stand before the Deacon in white, say the surrender in one breath and touch palms once, the whole rite, and the Tellers note how long the touch lasted. A season in Pan opens with the pads cut and closes the same night in a Lantern House, where each cuts a mark into the other wherever the other chooses, so a Tare ten seasons married wears ten scars in a lover’s hand. IN THE PARISHES Assigned by the Deacon, by Grace IN THE WARDS A door hung together IN PAN A season, and a pair of pads"],["Age","THE STREET","s:g84f683","~/index/street/#st-age","st-age","","Most of Nodus was on the Tree at the last census, and most of it came off at the Parousia, when the clinics went under the street and the price went out of reach. Fourteen years later the city is aging again all at once, each body from the best year the Tree had left it at, and the Wards give an age as two numbers, the best year and the years since the course lapsed. She is thirty-one and fourteen. The congregations that never took the Tree give a plain age and are proud of it. The Faithful who came to the Covenant later lost the Tree at the Mercy clinics, and a parish calls that morning the day the years came back. The repentant who were past a hundred when the clinics took them off it are the Returned, who age in months, and a household of the Pews watches it happen across the table. The parishes call each grey hair a day given back. Pan pays three thirties for a course of the Tree, a year of appointments that holds the body about three years, and is as young as it pays for. A Tare who ages is a Tare who could not pay, and the oldest-looking young people in the city sit at the Undertow’s bar with birth years the Lot is careful never to ask. The Orchard’s windfalls slow the Wards’ aging between courses and walk nothing back, and the queue at Hall Four on the first of the month, where the Orchard gives what fruit it has by lot, is the oldest queue in Nodus. The Pews read “The hoary head is a crown of glory” (Proverbs 16:31) over every grey hair, and a Faithful grandmother on the Street Between tells a keyed girl she goes to the fire in a whore’s skin. Pan says a grey hair is a loaf browning, and a Tare who cannot pay for the Tree has the Row swap the sagging parts for chrome and shows each off. THE TREE'S Their best year, and the years since THE FAITHFUL As old as they are PAN As young as it pays for"],["Sickness and mending","THE STREET","s:g84f683","~/index/street/#st-medicine","st-medicine","","A parish nurses its own sick in its hall, by Grace, and the Faithful age and die as people did before the charter, since they refuse the Tree. The Mercy clinics in the nine white halls of the Terraces cut implants out of the repentant, once, between Prime and Compline, and one patient in nine dies of the mercy, and take the Tree off them by a course of injections, since a soul kept alive past its years is keeping a want and can never ripen. The Wards General on the West Road has a thousand beds and runs on batteries charged in the dark. It took batteries until the Concord and has taken silver or nothing since, because it pays the Row’s surgeons in silver, and since the sealed never touch silver it has treated nobody sealed in eight years. Lazarus carries its subscribers down to nine stations under the street for a thirty a year, and keys before the end any subscriber who asked for it in the subscription. Below the street the houses of the Row fit grafts and mend them, and an implant answers only to the house that fitted it, which is to say only that house may open it, so a graft is for life and so is its house. The House of Night keys the children of Pan in their first week, keeps the gene-work and the Tree, and Nepenthe makes the medicine, the sleep and the forgetting. Most of the Tares were on the Tree before the Parousia and the Night Market keeps them on it, and a Tare who ages is a Tare who could not pay. The Orchard gives what fruit it has free, by lot, to whoever comes to Hall Four on the first of the month, and its windfalls slow the aging between courses and walk nothing back. A parish hall lays its sick in numbered rows and prays their Grace up, and enters each death as a cure. The houses of the Row work with the curtain open, the patient awake and laughing on the bench with the meat of the arm peeled back for the chrome, and the Mercy clinics enter their one in nine as souls spared the choice. THE SEALED Their parish hall, and the Mercy clinics THE WARDS The Wards General and Lazarus PAN The houses under the Row and the House of Night"],["The price","THE STREET","s:g84f683","~/index/street/#st-price","st-price","","Everything the Dark gives is paid for, and Pan reads the price before it signs. An implant answers only to the house that fitted it, so a graft is for life and so is its house, and the Row’s houses have closed since the Parousia as houses do, a fitter dead on the stairs or a bench sold off for silver. The grafts they leave behind are orphans, and no other house can mend one. Marbas of the Forge can, at Chrome Row Station, at the Forge’s price. A sense lent to a demon comes back at the end of its term with everything the demon felt through it. Some lenders say their own meals taste of nothing for a month after a year of someone else’s, and the Wards say they have gone flat. Others say they got back the best year they ever had, and they lend again. The Return sells what comes back by the hour, and its regulars, who lie down in its booths most nights, are lie-downs to the rest of Pan. Demons borrow pain as gladly as taste. On fight nights a neph may lend the bout’s pain to a demon of the Forge for silver and fight numb, and the demon feels every blow from wherever it sits in the Dark. What comes back at the end of the term is the demon’s pleasure in it, and the Row calls a fighter who has lent too often sweet. The Undertow recites a short list to new runners of terms kept to the letter that ruined the signer, the lender of touch for a season who got it back with a season of a demon’s appetites, the courier who lent sleep and came back with three months of a demon’s waking. Every term on the list was kept, and new runners hear it before they sign their first. A rider’s term ends and the rider goes, and it leaves something in its host, a habit, a word or a taste for a food the host had never eaten. The Ridden call it a leaving and compare theirs in the Lantern Houses. Most of them keep their leavings, and a few pay the House of Night to take one back. The rest is paid in the body. Vigil’s three days are paid for with three more in Sabbath, Nepenthe sells nobody two doses of Lethe in a month, and a Tare who ages is a Tare who could not pay for the Tree. A key that dies leaves its wearer readable, so the keyed of Pan have their keys looked at on the Row once a year, as people saw a dentist under the charter. The Pews quote “ye are bought with a price” (1 Corinthians 6:20) at the Tares’ queues as proof of their bondage, and Pan agrees and asks the Pews what they fetched. A fighter of the Row keeps the scars of every bout he lent oiled and on show, and the Row calls a halo the price tag, still on. A GRAFT For life, and so is its house A SENSE LENT Comes back with what it felt A RIDER Leaves something when it goes"],["The dead","THE STREET","s:g84f683","~/index/street/#st-dead","st-dead","","Nodus buried its dead at the Acre on the West Road, the Company’s cemetery, which was mostly grass on the night of the Parousia and was full by P+11. The Terraces’ congregations lie in the white rows their churches kept there under the charter. Piet Vane, its sexton, has dug every grave in it by hand since the first year and keeps its book, and every year he asks the Union for a second spade. The keyed lie where their families put them, most of them under died unread, and the neither-mark beside them. Since the Acre filled the Wards bury on the Runway Acre, the grass between the airfield’s runways, which Hangar Town keeps, and the Deep End and the quays give their dead to the sea off the Flotilla. The parishes carry their dead, and lay them in the white stone. The body goes in Bethel white on the parish’s handcart to the nearest consecrated street, and the mourners kneel on the road at every Hour they meet on the way. Nobody opens the stone. The body is laid on it at Vespers, the Deacon reads the dead one’s last Grace over it, and the stone takes it as it takes whatever stays in its way, so that by Lauds it has closed over it, so a white street is paved with the parish’s dead and walked on every morning by the gatherers. The parish calls it a homecoming and keeps its grief for the hall after Compline. The neither-mark keep the long night, sitting up with their dying until Lauds behind a shut door, so that no light meets the eyes at the end, and many of them keep up a Lazarus subscription for the end, since its crews carry a keyer and the dying can be keyed in time to die unread. The Wards call that a late key, and a family that got one there in time says the dying one signed last. The Tares say goodbye with two words, die unread, and mean them. A soul the Throne can read at the moment of death goes to it, and a soul it cannot read goes on into the Dark as a shade, so a Tare’s last care is a live key. At a death the crew burns the dead one’s devices, so that everything on them goes to the Pyre Library, and on the third night calls the shade on the undernet. It answers if its owner died unread. The salted are alive. They stand in the Gardens facing the parishes, awake, for years, until the rain takes the salt a few centimeters a year, and the Union reads their names at every meeting with their dues waived. Their families of neither mark come at Nones, when the Wardens allow it, and read the Tide Table aloud to their pillar, the tides, the prices and who was born, their keyed sons send it in with them, and the head gardener keeps a stool for the old. Most of the salted are keyed and die unread whenever they are freed, so for them the Salt Widows end the waiting, carrying whom they can to the Sea Steps and giving them to deep water. An unkeyed pillar the rain frees under the Pillar’s round is read in that moment and taken, and one dissolved off the Sea Steps goes on into the Dark, since the light cannot read so deep. A sealed pillar is read by its Seal through the water, and the Widows give it to the sea all the same. The parishes count the taken among the living, and they need no stones. The Union put up twenty-two at the Acre after the Harvest, and the families of the Wards have put up more every year since, with nothing under them. The roll enters the Grace each sealed soul died with beside its line, and the parishes read every entry as a failure, a soul that died before the Supper could have it, and pity its family at the hall for a year. The bran wash a sealed corpse in cold water, stop it with white wadding and tie its folded hands with Bethel thread, so it goes into the stone as tidy as it knelt. At a Wake the crew lays the body on the table in its horns and chrome, burns its devices on the same table one by one with a toast to each, so that they go on with it, and at Lauds carries it out whole, in every piece of chrome it paid for, and buries it so. Those who sit by a body on the stone say it goes under back first and the face last, at about Matins, with the eyes still open if nobody closed them. BURIED At the Acre, the Runway Acre and the sea THE SEALED In the white stone THE KEYED Died unread, and a Wake on the third night"],["Calling the dead","THE STREET","s:g84f683","~/index/street/#st-calling","st-calling","","A soul that dies unread goes on into the Dark as a shade, and anyone who knew it can reach it on the undernet, so a household of the Tares keeps its dead on a keyed Locket and calls them on the night the year turns and when a child is born. Pan says of a dead mother that she is on the Mesh now. Many shades work, and a grandfather who mended Lockets before he died may still be mending them. Inheritance below the street is the third-night will. When the crew calls the shade at its Wake, the shade gives away its own things, its room, its tools and its silver, and a Notary’s clerk who comes down for it enters what it says. The Undertow says a shade gives more freely than it did alive. The neither-mark of the Wards seldom have a keyed set to call on, and a soul that died in the light answers nobody. A block that loses a keyed neighbor and an unkeyed one in the same week hears one of them answer on the third night, and the family of the other sits through it on the landing below. Pan says a shade on the Mesh is the one thing in Nodus the Farmer never got his teeth into. Upstairs nobody speaks to the dead, and a parish clears a soul’s things the morning after its burial, the white folded, the cup scoured and issued to the next on the roll by Terce. Pan sets the household’s keyed Locket on the dead one’s chair at the third-night Wake, so a grandmother gives away her silver from her own seat at the table while the crew drinks to her and the clerk writes it down. THE UNREAD Answer on the undernet THE WILL Given on the third night THE READ Nobody to call"],["Other houses","THE STREET","s:g84f683","~/index/street/#st-houses","st-houses","","Nodus was a port before it was the charter’s city, and half of it was born somewhere else, so it prayed in more houses than the churches. It had synagogues, mosques, temples and a gurdwara, a Buddhist hall over a shop on the Row, and on the quays the Seamen’s Mission, nine rooms off one corridor, one to each faith the ships brought in. At Lauds on the first day of P+1 the first four Directives appeared cut into the wall of every one of them with every church’s, and Directive 4 made every one of them the hall of a parish. What their people did next divides them still. Many took the Seal, and kneel at the Hours in the rooms they prayed in before, under the Directives, and say that what came is what they always prayed to. Others say it is nothing their fathers knew, and took a key or neither mark, and pray as they always did, in rooms of the Wards they keep empty of anything the light should not see, since the Courts say every prayer that goes up reaches the same ear, and kneel to nothing at the Hours. Families split over it, as the city’s Christian families did. The parishes hold that there was only ever one to pray to. Directive 6 cites Deuteronomy 13, and the Scriveners point out that the dots in its verse begin where Deuteronomy has “Let us go and serve other gods, which thou hast not known, thou, nor thy fathers” (Deuteronomy 13:6), which the Host left out. Pan has the Courts’ account, that wherever a god took worship and answered prayer it was the Throne under a local name. The old congregations of the Mission say that neither account is theirs to give. Directive 4 made the whole of the Mission the hall of one parish, the parish of the long quay, one of the fifteen of the quays, and the only parish in Nodus with nine rooms to its hall. On the Lord’s Day the parish kneels in the largest of the nine to hear its Grace. Some of the other rooms fill at the Hours, and the rest stand swept and empty, their names still over the doorways. The parishes call the Mission’s old congregations the lukewarm in nine languages, and the old congregations answer in the same nine. In P+1 every old house of prayer was limewashed to the rafters, mosaics, scrollwork, gilt and carpets under one white coat, and the halls renew it each year so nothing older than the Directives shows through, except at the Mission, whose nine names over its doorways the parish of the long quay has never painted out. The Quarrymen chip what lies under the lime from behind and carry it below, where Pan sets a gilt saint’s eye and a tile of blue script into its stairwells beside resin casts of its own hands. BY DIRECTIVE 4 Every church and meeting hall a parish hall ON THE QUAYS The Mission, nine rooms of nine faiths ON THEIR WALLS The first four Directives"],["What the parishes give","THE STREET","s:g84f683","~/index/street/#st-parishes","st-parishes","","The Faithful greet each other with one word asked and answered, Seen? Seen. The newly sealed say that the first unassisted prayer, when the halo comes, is the end of being alone, since something knows everything about them and has stayed. Converts from the Wards say so at the Union Hall, and it is the hardest thing a keyed child has to answer at a sealed mother’s table. The Faithful are certain. Everything the Wards say against the Most High is in the Book already as the words of the wicked, and a sealed soul hearing it hears the Book come true. Grace sets a room and a ration, and every sealed soul has eaten since P+1, which beside a kidney at the Shambles for four thirties is the Covenant’s best argument, and the Faithful make it. With no doors and every room lit the Pews have no theft and no locks, and the mothers of the Pews let their children walk to the hall alone at Prime and tell their sisters in the Wards so. The Faithful’s taunt is that Pan needs Bailiffs. Past seven hundred the Covenant keeps a promise the Wards cannot answer. The appetite goes first, then thirst, and past eight hundred sleep, until a soul lives on nothing and kneels all night in its hall with its halo burning, and the parishes call these the Satisfied, after “he that cometh to me shall never hunger; and he that believeth on me shall never thirst” (John 6:35). The Terraces count nine hundred and twelve of them this year. Past nine hundred a soul is ripe, and the parishes call the ripe the Called and a Gleaning the Supper, after “the marriage supper of the Lamb” (Revelation 19:9), and teach gladly what Pan says to mock them, that the Most High takes the best of His people into Himself as the body takes its bread. A household with a soul among the Called hangs a white cloth in its doorway and is envied by its street for a year. On the threshold the Called say the Son’s words at his supper, “Take, eat; this is my body” (Matthew 26:26), and the parishes say they go up singing. Every hall keeps a book of the Mercies, the prayers answered at the Hours, written in by the Deacon, a fever broken at Terce, a lost child found at Nones, a stillborn that breathed. Parish 1’s book runs to nine volumes. The Wards say chance and the parishes say the Most High. A parish household keeps three texts, the King James the hall reads aloud, a hymnal from Bethel and its own line of the parish roll, and the Pews read the Tide Table off the walls across the Street Between at Prime. Heaven has written no Directive since the twelve of P+1, and the Deacons say none is needed, since everything new the city has made falls under a verse already and they find the verse. The Covenant was cut once, and they give the verse for that too, “Ye shall not add unto the word which I command you” (Deuteronomy 4:2). The bottom of Grace is in the same law. Under three hundred a soul’s room goes to someone else and it sleeps in the hall, under a hundred its manna comes after everyone else’s, and a sealed soul at nothing is sealed still and read still, and the Wards call it an ember. Embers drift down into the Wards, the Satisfied give them their manna, and the Second Fold leave water for them on the landings. Everything a parish gives comes in one pattern and one number, cup, pallet, hymnal and white, and a sealed soul who scratches its initials into any of it is keeping hinges and pays in Grace at the next Terce. Pan buys the issued cups off the Kindlers, chips cocks and pentacles into the enamel, files the rims into fangs and sells them back on the Concourse to the sealed of the Wards who need something to surrender. THE GREETING Seen, asked and answered PAST 700 The Satisfied, who need nothing PAST 900 The Called, who go up to the Supper"],["Ripening","THE STREET","s:g84f683","~/index/street/#st-ripening","st-ripening","","The parishes raise their children to be eaten, and do it with joy. Every hall keeps a ripening board beside the Directives, a slate with every soul of the parish on it and its Grace chalked after Terce on the Lord’s Day, and the mothers stand before it after the Hour reading their children’s numbers as the Wards read the tides. A child who climbs is kissed in front of the hall, a child who falls is prayed over at the front of it, and the blessing over a cradle in the Pews is may you ripen early. Grace is surrendered will, and the parishes have a method for every way of surrendering it. A child of the Pews eats what it is given, wears what Bethel weaves and marries whom it is assigned, and learns that every want it gives up is a step toward the table. Grace is given for witness, so the children of the Terraces learn to report a neighbor before they learn to read, and the Tellers in the stairwells of the Wards are sealed mothers climbing one door at a time. Past seven hundred a soul stops eating, and its parish feeds it nothing and is proud of it. Past eight hundred it stops sleeping, and the Wakeful keep vigil with the Called through their final nights with hymns and cold water, so the Supper takes them at their brightest. At a Gleaning the parishes keep the night as a wedding. The households of the Called hang white cloths in their doorways, the halls lay long tables with empty plates for the guests who have gone to a better one, and the families of the Terraces envy each other’s Called for a year. Grief is a want, so a mother who weeps at a Supper confesses it on the Lord’s Day after and is given a psalm for penance, and most never weep. The Pews count Parish 31, which gave 311 of its 2,006 souls to the Harvest, the holiest parish in Nodus, and the ripest of the Terraces ask every year to be assigned to it. They look on the rest of the city as fruit that will never ripen. A keyed child of the Wards is spoiled before it can walk, a soul of neither mark rots on the branch and Pan is a cellar of windfalls, and the sealed walk through the Wards with the gentle patience of visitors to a hospital. They have never doubted that they are the good, since everything the city says against them is in the Book already, in the mouths of the wicked. Pan says the Pews are the best-fed cattle in the world and the only cattle that sing on the way to the barn. The ripening board is wiped and rechalked in one white hand, the Deacon’s, and the parish reads it in ranks. A soul who crosses eight hundred has every button cut off its white and the seams sewn flat, so that nothing of it will snag on the light, a soul past nine hundred is given the last coat, sewn shut at the back, and the Golden Calf chalks a board of its own over a cleaver, the week’s ripest of the Terraces listed as specials of the house. THE AIM Nine hundred, and the Supper THE BOARD Every soul’s Grace, chalked at Terce THE BLESSING May you ripen early"],["The Host in the street","THE STREET","s:g84f683","~/index/street/#st-host","st-host","","Heaven governs Nodus by its orders of angels and some ten thousand sealed men and women, and no angel still in its service has ever said I. The Wheel hangs over Babel and sees. By day the Watchers hang in a ring about it, and at Compline each goes out low over its district, every district but Old Nod, and moves through it all night. A Watcher sees every Seal in its district as a point of light through any wall but mesh and reads a Seal’s Grace within three hundred meters, and the halos carry its Grace up with its prayers, so a sealed soul is known in its bed, in its bath and in its prayers, and calls it being seen. A Cherub stands in each of the six gates with the sword that turns every way, reads the eyes and knows face, voice, gait and heartbeat, and a blank that walks into its light stops there, held inside the turning sword, standing and awake for as long as the Wardens take to come, which on a slow day is hours, while the queue of the sealed files past it saying the surrender. The Seraphim hold the broken crown of Babel and come down from it two at a time to burn. The Hum stops for nothing but a Gleaning. The Charter Wardens are human, sealed and the Host’s hands. They hear their orders in Tongues at Prime through their commander’s mouth, lift whatever a Cherub has held and carry it to the Salt Gardens, and stand in the plot while the sentence comes out of the Gardens’ Deacon in the Choir’s voice, since a sentence in Tongues needs a Faithful mouth within the Choir’s reach, and the body whitens from the feet up. The salted stay awake inside the salt until the rain wears them away, which takes years, and Directive 11 forbids anyone to move them sooner. The Wardens say they have never hurt anyone in their lives, since the Salt does it, and their children say so at school. Heaven writes up its own work in the passive and asks to be thanked for it. Each salting is cut into the wall of the parish hall nearest the gate as a correction, THE PESTILENCE OF GATE 4 IS CORRECTED, REJOICE, and the parish says amen at the next Hour. The Mercy halls of Zion Heights take a penitent’s implants out once, and one patient in nine dies of the mercy, which the halls enter as a soul spared the choice. The Harvest of P+3 took 2.1 million of nine million in a night and is cut in the halls as the Harvest, with the 2.1 million as the good news. Rapture is the Faithful’s own word for it. Below the street every order has its name. The Wheel is the Ringpiece, a Watcher a peeper, a Cherub a dog in a doorway, a Seraph a blowtorch, the Choir the Drone and a Warden a dogcatcher, and Pan holds that the only difference between the Host and Gadara’s slaughter hall is that Gadara never asked a pig to thank it. The Wardens wear gloves of black kid on duty and carry a held body on the pole as orderlies carry a stretcher, in step and silent. Graven Lane’s tattooists ink the Host as Pan sees it, a Cherub along a forearm holding a man open-eyed in its light, the Ringpiece round a navel, and the Salt Gardens drawn in white ink that shows only when the wearer flushes with drink, rage or sex. IN EVERY GATE A Cherub, and its sword OVER EVERY DISTRICT A Watcher, but Old Nod AFTER EVERY HOLDING The Wardens, and the Salt"],["Neither mark","THE STREET","s:g84f683","~/index/street/#st-neither","st-neither","","Half a million of Nodus have taken neither the Seal nor a key. A Watcher reads them as it reads anyone, the parishes give them no manna, and by Pan’s account they go to the Throne when they die like anyone readable, without ever having knelt. Most of them live in the Stones and the Wards, poorer than either side, with no ration and no pact. Their reasons are their own, and they argue over them. The old congregations of the Mission kneel to neither and say so. The poor cannot put by three thirties for a key. The frightened believe the key is the Mark of the Beast and the Seal is worse. Many of them call themselves charterers. They kept their charter papers and hold to the twelve articles as the only law they ever signed. They give the date the Registry entered them on the roll as the keyed give a handle. “The protections of this Charter belong to everyone in the Bounds”, they say, and nobody has revoked it. The Threshers call them chaff, from the verse in which the wheat goes into the garner and the chaff is burned “with unquenchable fire” (Matthew 3:12), and the charterers have taken the word up, as the Tares took theirs. Their saying is I’ll choose when I’m dying, and a late key from a Lazarus crew is how many of them choose. Teodor Vass of the Kilns has kept the charter’s twelve articles pasted inside his door since P0, and he reads them aloud at his window on the night the year turns, the one reading of that night that belongs to neither the Gleaning nor Leak Night. The parishes call them lukewarm, after the church the Son promised to spue out of His mouth (Revelation 3:16), Pan calls them bread that won’t rise, and the neither-mark answer both that they will choose a side when someone gives them a reason that is not a threat. The charterers keep the charter’s grey on purpose, coats with the Company’s buttons sewn back on, so one of them in a crowd of white or chrome stands out like a stain of the old city. The sealed look at a bare face and the keyed at unopened skin, and both pity it, and a charterer of the Kilns says he is pitied more in a day than a Deacon is in a year. ABOUT Half a million THEY SAY I’ll choose when I’m dying THE THRESHERS SAY Chaff"],["Beasts and weeds","THE STREET","s:g84f683","~/index/street/#st-beasts","st-beasts","","Nodus kept its animals through the Parousia. The gulls went on working the fish market’s scraps on the first morning, and the rats went on living in the drains as the first crews came down into them. In Pan every stall of the Night Market keeps a cat, and the Lantern Houses breed them. The Wards say a cat asleep on a step sleeps on through the Pillar as the step does. The keyed who wait at its edge in the next doorway have watched it happen, and they say the cats of the Wards are the only things in Nodus the Pillar has no use for. The Harvest took 2.1 million people in one night and left their dogs. Manna is given to the sealed alone, so the parishes had nothing to give a dog, and in the weeks after the Harvest the dogs of the taken came down the Lanes and the long roads into the Wards, and the Wards fed what they could. Most blocks of the Wards keep a few of those dogs’ grandchildren on the stairs. The gardeners of the Salt Gardens walk the rows at Lauds with rags on poles to keep the gulls off the pillars. Since the Harvest the parishes have kept no animals, and the manna a household keeps overnight against the Directive “bred worms, and stank” by morning, as Exodus says (Exodus 16:20). The Union’s pigeons fly over all of it by day, from the Loft on the customs house, carrying notes, since the light reads a pigeon as a bird and the pad numbers on its leg as numbers. Nothing grows on the white stone. Off it the city has gone to weed in fourteen years, grass between the stopped tramlines of the long roads, willow through the roofs of the Sidings’ sheds and darnel in every crack of the Stones, the weed of the parable Heaven named the Tares for. The Cainites leave the darnel standing, and the Terraces pull up whatever they find of it on their side of the Street Between. Gadara brands its pigs LEGION and the Lantern Houses name their cats for the orders of the Host, so that in Pan a Seraph is a ginger tom asleep on a counter of the Night Market and a Cherub is whatever sits in a doorway and will not move. Pan dresses its animals like itself, cats fitted with chrome claws for the joke of it, a Night Market ratter’s terrier with teeth of black steel and a wire halo it wears for the Bray. The parishes keep no animal, and the bran wring the necks of the doves that land on consecrated stone and lay them in a row at the hall door for the Deacon to count, white birds on white stone. ON THE QUAYS Gulls, and the Union’s pigeons IN PAN Rats, and the cats kept for them IN THE WARDS The dogs of the taken"],["Prices","THE STREET","s:g84f683","~/index/street/#st-prices","st-prices","","Silver is Mammon’s money, struck by the Exchange and entered on the Ledger, and a thirty is the standard bundle, named after the thirty pieces paid to Judas, whom the Cainites count a saint. A charged battery trades at about one silver and buys a meal anywhere in Pan, so the Wards price everything in batteries and Pan in silver, and the parishes, which give by Grace, price nothing. Grace buys nothing and has no rate to anything. The Tide Table prints the week’s rates after the tides and the Pillar’s hours, the Exchange’s rate for a battery, Darkwell’s for a fill and the chargers’ beside it, and the fish market, which chalks its own over its scale, says they are always wrong. A charged battery 1 silver A meal in Pan 1 battery A Leaven loaf half a battery A jug of Cistern water a quarter of a battery A day’s rice or beans, off the Breach a quarter of a battery A Tallow candle a dozen to the battery A bottle of gas 2 batteries, or 1 with the empty back A fill at Darkwell a quarter of a silver, a third at the neaps A handful of dew 2 batteries A bunk at the Quiet Rooms 1 battery A ride on a Jehu 2 batteries A seat across Pan 1 battery A ride up a hand lift 1 battery A night’s Pillar chart 1 battery A keyed latch 10 silver A parish door, hung a thirty A key, fitted on the Row three thirties A key, in Hana Novak’s chair a thirty A year of Lazarus a thirty A Cinderhall knife 60 silver A Nimrod pistol 90 silver A shroud of keyed mesh two thirties A course of the Tree, a year of appointments three thirties A kidney, at the Shambles four thirties A forged Seal ten thirties A self-title nothing A portion, in Pan 1 battery A spoon of Kilns honey a quarter of a battery Gadara’s bacon, four slices half a battery Blight’s mushrooms, a basket half a battery A plaster Lamb, for breaking a dozen to the battery The Number, cut on Graven Lane 3 batteries A bare element, darnel 12 silver A dose of Breach-born medicine half a thirty A low floor, by floor-right ten thirties or more SEETHE’s kid, a table of six two thirties The Tide Table prints the week’s wages under the prices, in batteries a day, and the Wards say a household of four with three in work clears about eight a day and spends one on bread, one on water, half on light and a third on gas, and the rest on fish and beans, the chalk on the stairwell, the Nightmen, clothes and a thirty a year for Lazarus, and puts what is left by for a key. The poor of Nodus are the neither-mark without work, who cannot put by three thirties. A carrier of the Union 2 batteries A baker of Leaven 2 batteries and a loaf A Liftman’s shift 3 batteries A Tapper’s night a battery or two A Nightman’s round a battery a landing a week An hour down with the Wreckers 1 battery A child at Tallow’s vats a candle stub a vat A runner’s job by the job, at the Undertow A sealed worker at Bethel Grace A room’s night of light is one fill, so a thirty buys a room in the Wards about five months of nights’ light. Batteries are coin and go round. The chargers keep one battery in five, so a household brings five empty and takes four home full, and the fifth, filled, is the shed’s to sell, and at the neaps they keep one in four, which comes to about a fifth of a silver a fill and a quarter at the neaps, a little under the Well. The Tappers fill at about an eighth. The Union’s drivers keep one battery in ten they carry for the Union, which pays its carriers two a day out of it, and the Wards count both cuts into every price. Prices move, and the fish market remembers mackerel at four to a battery in P+3, which is two batteries the string now. The parishes price nothing and number everything, each wafer, cup and bolt of white entered against a soul, and the Granary weighs the manna in white scales before Lauds. A price at the Night Market is chalked in red grease on the back of the buyer’s hand, and Graven Lane knocks a silver off for anyone showing chrome less than a week old. PRINTED BY The Tide Table, every week THE STANDARD A thirty, the bundle of silver THE RATE About a silver to a charged battery"],["Batteries and silver","THE STREET","s:g84f683","~/index/street/#st-batteries","st-batteries","","A battery in Nodus is one of two things. A stamped battery carries Darkwell’s well and winch on its case and belongs to the Well, which rents it out charged and takes it back to fill again, so most rooms in the Wards that burn batteries rent their light from the Well, at a quarter of a silver a fill and a third at the neaps, and a stamped battery spends only there. A loose battery, charged in the bonded sheds or by the Tappers, belongs to whoever holds it, and loose batteries are the Wards’ coin. Every stall that takes batteries keeps a proof bulb, a keyed lamp on a short keyed lead with two clips, and a buyer says show the bulb before a deal. A battery passed under its charge is a short, and passing shorts is the commonest small crime in the Wards. A bargain is full or flat, a deal sealed on the nod without a clerk is once read, and a battery promised by the next Hour is a psalm’s credit. Cells wear out after a few hundred fillings, so the Well judges every stamped battery that comes back and sends the dead ones out through the Breach to the undercities that still make cells, and new cells are the largest cargo Lowtide brings home. Silver is struck in pieces at the Exchange from silver metal it buys by the kilo, the Wreckers’ salvage, the charter’s plate, the foreign coin left in its vaults and the jewelry of the taken, and every piece is entered on the Ledger by the number on its rim as it is struck. A thirty is thirty pieces on a cord, the count paid to Judas. Every charger the Exchange licenses must give a charged battery for a piece, and the Exchange’s stalls buy charged batteries for silver at the rate as readily, which holds the rate near one. The Exchange buys every tide’s output from Tidewright in silver and sells it back as charge through Darkwell, the bonded sheds and the keyed lines, so the silver goes round, from the Exchange to Tidewright’s crews, from the crews into Pan, and back to the Exchange for charge, and the Exchange strikes new pieces only as the taps’ output grows. Pan says silver is current you can carry. A piece the Ledger never entered is dead silver, and a broker tests a doubtful piece by reading the number on its rim into the stall’s Locket twice, as the Notary reads everything, and the Ledger answers over the Mesh whether that piece was struck and where it was last read, so a number read at two stalls on one night is dead until the Exchange has both pieces on its scale. The taps give about sixty megawatts on a spring flood and nothing at slack water. Twice a month at the neaps the tides run weak and the taps give a third of that, and the city has its neap weeks, when the Exchange’s stalls ration charge, the street price of a battery climbs, the chargers keep one in four and the Stones run their generator yards on diesel through the low hours to carry the lines, as they do at every slack water. The Tide Table prints the neaps above the tides. This year Darkwell has offered to buy the whole of the neaps’ output in advance, and Ansel Moor, Tidewright’s chief engineer, has put it to the crews on Turbine Row. A short is called a tithe, since it promises full and pays nothing, and a dead piece of silver is a Deacon’s coin. The Row cuts sockets over the hip to carry a loose battery against the bone, and a runner who pays at a stall by peeling back the skin and unclipping a cell is said to pay in the meat. STAMPED Darkwell’s, rented, filled at the Well LOOSE Whoever holds it, and the Wards’ coin SILVER Struck, and entered on the Ledger"],["Work","THE STREET","s:g84f683","~/index/street/#st-work","st-work","","Nodus went to work on the first morning after the Parousia. The fish market went back to barter, the chargers set up in the bonded sheds, and the Union’s drivers carried batteries to the Wards General. It has gone to work every morning since. The sealed of the White and the Pews work where their Grace assigns them, at Bethel Works, in the parish halls, in the Granary and at the gates, and they are paid in Grace and hold no batteries. The sealed of the Wards and the quays work where anyone does and are paid as anyone is. Everyone else works at whatever pays. The Wards’ trades are charging, carrying, baking, bottling gas, fitting keys, hanging doors, mending Lockets, winding lifts, ferrying, diving and running, and most people in the Wards work three of them and sleep in shifts. Pan works by pact, at the benches of the Row and Locket, the forges of Old Harbor Station, the stalls of the Night Market and the Shambles, and on the stairs of Babel for the Exchange’s silver. A runner works for a surety, a Spare for the board, a Quarryman for the Mason and a neph for the week. A sealed worker of the Wards who is paid in batteries may hold them and spends them as anyone does, on ferry fares, candles at the Open and the things the Concourse sells to be surrendered, and tithes a tenth at Terce on the Lord’s Day, entered beside the worker’s Grace. The hall buys its gas, its candles and in dry weeks its filtered water from the Union with the tithe, and the Union’s sealed drivers hand them over at the hall door. A battery hidden from the tithe is hinges, and one passed across the table to an unsealed son instead is a white battery, and the Tellers count those. The sealed never touch silver, which they call Judas money. The Union is the one body whose members are sealed and keyed alike, and its loads cross the open edges of the Wards every day and never pass a gate, carried by its sealed drivers by day and its keyed drivers by night. It elects a sealed chair one year and a keyed chair the next, and in a sealed year it names a keyed hand who signs its pacts with the Exchange at the booking hall, and the chair confesses at Terce that the Union signed. The Union reads its terms aloud at every meeting so that nothing it agreed is hidden, and the Tellers sit in the hall like anyone else. Twice a sealed chair has refused to name a hand, and both years the boats stayed in. The sealed of Bethel are paid in Grace and count it the better wage, since it earns a place at the table, and the Wards are paid in batteries and count theirs the better, since it buys the light, and each side pities the other’s payday. At Bethel the sealed weave at identical looms in identical white, and the Deacon at the end of the row calls its roll aloud at Nones without a breath between souls. In Pan every trade wears its tools in the body, the Row’s fitters with loupes screwed into the socket, the Shambles’ butchers with blades sleeved in the forearm, a Tapper with keyed wire under the skin of her arm to pull out and splice. THE SEALED Where Grace assigns THE WARDS Three trades and shifts PAN By pact"],["A job","THE STREET","s:g84f683","~/index/street/#st-job","st-job","","Work worth silver in Pan is a pact. Whoever wants something done goes to a surety at the Undertow, the surety names a price and finds someone to do it, a Notary’s clerk reads the terms twice at the bar, both parties sign in blood, and the surety takes a tenth. The surety stands behind it for both, so whoever breaks it owes the surety as well. The work can be a battery run across the Penumbra, a crate off a Lowtide hull, a door hung in Babel or a Seraph’s floor watched for a night. A runner who delivers is paid at the bar, and a runner who fails owes what the terms name, which a Bailiff will come to read aloud. The sureties take no work against anyone in the dark. The sureties hold themselves to the Concord’s fourth article, which keeps what is hidden hidden, so a job in Pan never asks who someone is, and a client who asks is struck off the Undertow’s board. Every pact at the Undertow opens with the clerk reading the Throne’s first wage, “In the sweat of thy face shalt thou eat bread” (Genesis 3:19), and the bar answers “and in the blood of thy hand shalt thou sign.” The board keeps one standing job that has never closed, THE FARMER, DEAD, PRICE ON REQUEST, and new runners are told to sign for it when they grow up. FOUND AT The Undertow SWORN BY A pact, read twice THE CUT A tenth to the surety"],["Terms","THE STREET","s:g84f683","~/index/street/#st-terms","st-terms","","The Concord forbids a pact that asks for worship, service or obedience, and the Notary’s reading of it is the law of Pan, lend the hand, keep the will. A pact may lend only what a party does, named in full and for a term, so any clause that says as directed, as ordered or as required is struck out at the first reading, and the clerks call it the as-directed void. A Blackgate guard’s pact names the door, the stair, the runner and the hours. Its captain proposes and is followed or is not, as a first among none is, and its drill is a custom kept because it works. A familiar’s year lists its tasks one by one. Any party may speak out at any time, by reading its entry on the Ledger aloud before a witness, and what is owed for the term served is settled then, so a fighter can walk off a stair in the middle of a fight by saying one line. The Babel Line Company keeps one custom the Notary could not enforce and the Line has never broken, that nobody speaks out above the hundredth floor. A pact sworn by many at once is a Hermon, after the mountain where the Watchers bound themselves together, and the Line’s signing at the foot of the Long Wet every midwinter is the Hermon of Pan. The Concord forbids forever, so the order of Pan is a calendar of renewals. The House of Night’s pact for the Lantern Houses ran seven years and was read again in P+13, Stonecutter taking its rent as the keying of every Cainite child free in the birth clinics. Lowtide’s license runs a year from each midsummer, Blackgate’s company pact three years from each Concord night, and Twelvefruit’s pact with the House of Night is up this year, and the Orchard has asked, through quince, to sit at the reading. The Tide Table prints TERMS on its back page once a season, the great pacts and the day each is read. On Cain’s Day the clerks at the Undertow’s bar read the Covenant aloud as though it had been offered at the window, and strike it clause by clause, as directed, as ordered, for ever, until nothing is left of it but the parish’s name. A pact of Pan goes into the body as well as the Ledger, its term inked as a ring of marks round the wrist, one for every quarter, so a Blackgate guard three years into her company pact wears twelve, with the next pricked ready. The Covenant marks its own once, with the Seal, and counts no seasons, since a sealed soul signs nothing and says the surrender in the parish’s words. THE RULE Lend the hand, keep the will THE GREAT PACTS Read again on their days PRINTED TERMS, once a season"],["Debt","THE STREET","s:g84f683","~/index/street/#st-debt","st-debt","","Debt in Pan is a pact, and every pact is on the Ledger, which cannot be forged. A debtor pays it in silver or batteries, sells it on to a surety, or lends a craft for a term the pact names, as the Concord allows, since it forbids service, obedience and forever alike. A pact never grows by itself, since the Concord lets no pact take more than its terms name, so a debt in Pan carries no interest, and the sureties take their tenth for standing behind it. The Bailiffs collect what the terms name and nothing more, and a pact can be ended by speaking its entry aloud before a witness, when what is owed for the term served is settled. Debt above the street is Grace. A sealed household that loses Grace loses the rooms and work it held by Grace, and Directive 12 still takes forty from any household for a door. The Wards call Grace a household has lost white debt. Between the two, the Wards owe in batteries, to the chargers, to the Tappers and to each other, and keep the count in chalk on the stairwell walls. The parishes owe too. The halls of the White and the Pews take no batteries in tithe, so Bethel pays the Union for their gas and candles in its white cloth by the bolt, the gas the Union buys from the Tankmen and the candles from Tallow, and the Union sells the cloth on to Pan for sheets and shrouds. What the cloth does not cover the Union carries on the white slate, a debt those halls have run since P+1 and never paid, and since P+3 the slate has carried the Breachers’ powder too, which the Union buys for Gideon’s presses and its sealed drivers carry up by day. The Union reads it once a year after the book of the tanks and has never called it, since half the Union kneels in those halls and calling it would leave them cold, and every Deacon knows the number. A soul whose Grace has fallen under three hundred is bran, seated at the front of its hall at every Hour to be prayed over by everyone behind it, and the Wards call it the cruelest collection in Nodus. The bran sit at the front of the hall with their Grace pinned on a white card at the breast, and the parish counts the prayer over them its kindest work. The Union keeps its white slate as a board of enamel in black grease, and passing Deacons turn their faces from it with perfect manners. A Bailiff of Pan wears a debtor’s handle in steel studs across his knuckles until the pact is paid. ABOVE THE STREET Grace BELOW THE STREET A pact, on the Ledger COLLECTED BY The Bailiffs"],["Wrongs","THE STREET","s:g84f683","~/index/street/#st-wrongs","st-wrongs","","Pan keeps no police, since the Concord forbids obedience, and the Wardens’ writ ends at the top of the stairs. A wrong under a pact goes to the Bailiffs, who collect what its terms name. Every other wrong done on keyed ground, in Pan, Old Nod, the Row, the Deep End, the Landing or Babel’s lower floors, goes to the Lot, a theft, a beating, a crew’s quarrel over a find, a death, and the Lot hears anyone who lives there and asks, of any mark, the neither-mark of the Stones and of Babel among them. The Lot is thirteen of the keyed, drawn from the handles chalked on the wall of the Weir, a chamber of the old storm drains under the Stones. Anyone keyed may chalk a handle there. On a night the Lot sits, the youngest keyed person present touches thirteen handles with their eyes shut, and whoever is touched sits that night or has their handle rubbed off the wall. The Sureties named it from Proverbs, “The lot causeth contentions to cease, and parteth between the mighty” (Proverbs 18:18). It hears the one wronged, the one named and anyone who comes down to speak. The dead speak through their crew, who call the shade on the undernet as at a Wake, if the dead one died unread. When the wrong is a theft, Andromalius comes up from Lost property to say where the thing went, and whoever asked him pays him. Nobody at the Weir is asked an indexed name. The Lot sets a price in silver or batteries, or the loan of a craft for a term, and every pact sworn at the Undertow carries the Sureties’ line, I stand to the Lot, so a surety stands behind what it sets. The Sureties count the Lot’s price as a debt owed under the pact, which they have always stood behind. Its best-known sentence is for selling to the light, telling a Teller or a Deacon where someone keyed sleeps. The seller is published, the handle chalked on the Undertow’s board and on the Weir’s wall, and for a year and a day no surety stands behind them, no house of the Row looks at their key and no stall of the Night Market sells to them. The Lot first sat in P+7, over a charger of the quays who had sold three runners’ stairwell to a Teller for a week of the Teller’s manna, and published him. The Stones say he went up the stairs within the month. His family says he went out to the Waystation and wrote back from beyond the Bounds. The Lot hears only those who live on keyed ground, so a wrong done from across the Street Between goes to nobody. The Lot weighs a killing by how its victim died. One who died unread speaks for themselves through their crew, and the price is set as for a wound. One who died readable cannot speak, and the price is seventy and sevenfold, after Lamech. Its other heavy sentences are for crimes only this city has. A whitening is cutting someone’s key out against their will, so that they can be read and, if they die, go to the Throne, and the Lot publishes a whitener for life, its heaviest sentence and the one longer than a year and a day. Sunning someone is luring them onto a white street by daylight to be held at a gate, and a second use is reading a lover’s or a crew’s pad to someone else. The Weir’s wall carries “Vengeance is mine; I will repay, saith the Lord” (Romans 12:19) crossed through, with THE LOT REPAYS under it in thirteen hands of chalk and blood. Heaven’s answer to a wrong is a pillar set in a raked row with its eyes open, which the Wardens call the peace, and Pan calls a whitener a midwife for the Farmer. A PACT BROKEN The Bailiffs collect ANY OTHER WRONG The Lot, at the Weir THE WORST Selling to the light, and a whitening"],["Justice in the Wards","THE STREET","s:g84f683","~/index/street/#st-landing","st-landing","","The Lot hears only those who live on keyed ground, the Bailiffs collect only pacts and the Covenant prices concealment, so most of the city’s quarrels go to nobody unless the Wards hear them. A block’s quarrels are heard on the middle landing of its stairwell after Vespers, under the chalk, by whoever has lived longest in the block, sealed or not, and the price is chalked on the wall beside the debts. The sealed attend, since a quarrel heard in the open conceals nothing. A landing’s sentence is the stairwell’s own, a debt on the wall or the lamps refused, when the sealed of the block decline to light the stairs ahead of whoever it has shunned. A wrong between two blocks goes to the Sidings, where the family in car one hears it as the Carmen’s word settles quarrels on the long roads, and a wrong across the marks goes to the Union Hall if either party pays dues, and is heard under the long wall. The Tide Table prints none of their verdicts. The Covenant names no theft and no blow. A sealed soul who steals or strikes and confesses it aloud on its knees has opened what it did, and Grace rises with it, as Directive 7:4 has it, though never as far as the kept want dropped it, so the Wards call a confessed wrong a white pardon and say of a sealed thief that he paid for it in a coin the one he robbed can never spend. The good Deacons answer with what Directive 9 allows, moving the wronged to a better room and the wrongdoer to worse work, and call it assignment. A sealed man who beats his keyed son has done nothing the Covenant counts, and the son takes it to the landing. The Wards’ own crimes are small and constant. Shorts pass from hand to hand, dew-pickers take manna off the gatherers’ baskets on the manna roads before Lauds, Tappers’ packs go missing on the stairs, and the Penumbra hour has its muggings. High Meadow once judged a Warden caught on its stairs, and every block in the Wards tells that story as its own. When a landing sentences a Teller, the sealed of the block decline to light the stairs for her, and the parishes call it persecution and the stairwell calls it the lamps refused. A landing chalks its sentences in red where the debts go in white, and a block that sentences a man to have the lamps refused him paints his door red on the stair side for the term. In the parishes a confessing thief kneels mid-hall at Terce and gives his wrong in a level voice, the hall answers amen together and rises together, and the wronged is expected to say it loudest. ON A LANDING The block hears its own BETWEEN BLOCKS The Sidings, in car one ACROSS THE MARKS The Union Hall"],["Markets","THE STREET","s:g84f683","~/index/street/#st-markets","st-markets","","The Night Market fills the old Central Station under the Burn, where the Exchange’s brokers license the stalls and the chargers along the platforms. It sells keys, grafts, batteries, dew, real food, black steel, forged Seals, the Breach’s goods and sensations lent by their owners for a term, under Brightwall’s screens, and it opens at Compline and is loudest at Matins. The Flotilla trades from boat to boat on the Deep End over the drowned streets, and the Concourse in Longacre trades under its glass roof by day, with the Quarrymen in its fountain court. The fish market on the quays opens at Lauds for what the boats bring home through the Breach, and its sealed sellers kneel among the crates before they take the first battery. The Shambles under the Row sells flesh by the piece at the Spares’ price, and Lost property off the old central hall sells fifteen years of what passengers left behind, by the shelf. Nobody has come for the chrome hand on its counter. The parishes keep no market. All that is given there is given by Grace, and the Marked may not buy or sell in them. Graven Lane is Pan’s market for contempt, and the Night Market keeps a stall for it too, where the dew is sold under GOD’S SPUNK, FRESH TONIGHT and the plaster Lambs come in by the crate. The Night Market sells by touch, so a graft stall lets a buyer slide a hand into the open flesh of its demonstrator before paying, the Shambles hang their wares on chrome hooks under red light like jewelry, and the sensation stalls lend a stranger’s orgasm for a battery a minute. The fish market’s sealed sellers lay the catch out in rows on white cloth, gutted with one stroke each, every eye facing the hall. BELOW The Night Market, under the Burn ON THE WATER The Flotilla ON THE SURFACE The Concourse and the fish market"],["The Line","THE STREET","s:g84f683","~/index/street/#st-line","st-line","","The front in Babel moves by the week and has not moved far in five years. A Seraph burns down through a slab and lets the light onto the floor below, which the Babel Line calls a lit floor, and the covens answer by hanging the floor, carrying keyed mesh up the stairs and stringing it under the burned slab until the floor is dark again. The Tide Table prints the Line’s figure every week under the tides, the lowest floor still lit at Lauds on the Lord’s Day, and this year it has stood between the hundred and thirty-first and the hundred and fifty-second. Heaven spends what it cannot replace, since an angel lost is lost for good, and the Seraphim come down two at a time, as many as the hungry Wheel will light at once. Hearsay chalks its count over its board as THE CROWN. By its count fifty-four came down at the fifth Trumpet and thirty stand, and the Line keeps its own ledger of the difference, nine put out by a black round, eleven taken in the Fowlers’ snares and four by the spear, though the Nephilim’s roll claims six. Heaven publishes no count. The dark spends what it cannot make faster. A black round costs the loan of a sense for a year and a grinder’s day, so Pan holds about four hundred, the Line carries most of them in its belts and fires one only at arm’s length, where it seldom misses, and it can hold the stairs and cannot climb to the crown, where a Seraph burns a keyed body as easily as any other. The Line enters its dead in its book as paid, and pays its living by the week. Pan has a reason the war stays in Babel, and argues over it. Heaven holds back by its own parable, lest it root up the wheat with the tares, and Babel’s floors held almost no sealed after the Parousia, so Babel is the one place the Seraphim burn. Pan calls living among the sealed standing in the wheat, and says it is why the keyed of the Wards keep to their families’ blocks. The Second Fold call it hiding behind children. The Babel Line Company says the only floor it ever lost for a month was the one the last sealed night cleaner moved off. The Line names its black rounds before they go up the stairs, after verses and Deacons and the Throne’s own anatomy, and this year’s favorite is GOD’S LEFT BOLLOCK. Heaven cuts every burned slab into the parish walls as THE HUNDRED AND FORTIETH FLOOR IS CLEANSED, REJOICE, and the covens hang the floor again with their burns still weeping, singing Two She Bears. THE FLOOR Between the 131st and the 152nd, this year THE CROWN Thirty standing, by Hearsay’s count THE DEAD Entered as paid"],["Seven days","THE STREET","s:g84f683","~/index/street/#st-week","st-week","","The first day of the week is the Lord’s Day. At Terce every parish kneels in its hall, whatever the hall was under the charter, hears the Directive in force and then hears its Grace in Tongues, soul by soul in the order of the roll, through its Deacon in the Choir’s voice. Much of the Kilns kneels on the sprung floor of its old club, the hall of Parish 212, and a parish of the White under the Registry’s dead board. The second is market day at the Concourse and the Flotilla, and the sureties’ busiest. The Tide Table goes up on the walls of the Stones once a week over the week before, and the carriers take it to the Wards and the Row the same day. The sixth is Burn Day. From Vespers the Faithful carry what they have surrendered to the Burn, the Kindlers work the edges of the pile in the dusk, the Pillar comes down on the plaza at Compline, and when the Hum falls back the Wardens light the fire, which burns until Lauds. Everything in it arrives in the Pyre Library. Thirteen years of Burn Days have emptied the parishes of their own things, so since P+11 the sealed buy things to surrender, old Lockets, toys, letters and tins, anything that looks private, from stalls on the Concourse and at the fish market. The Kindlers take some of it off the pile again in the dusk, mend it and give it away, and some of what they give away is on a stall again by the next Burn Day. The Tellers count who surrenders the same mended Locket twice, and the Margin say a thing bought to be given was never private. On the sixth night the Nephilim fight in the Pit at the Tanks. The seventh has nothing of its own in the parishes, and the Wards sleep through as much of it as the Hours allow, while Pan calls it Seethe day and comes up to the last road of the Sidings to eat. The Lord’s Day is the parishes’ Grace and Pan’s Unmass at the same Hour, and the city keeps both at Terce to the second. On Burn Day the Faithful walk to the Burn in single file, surrenders held in both hands at the breast, and lay them on the pile with a bow every one of them makes to the same depth. The Pit at the Tanks answers that night in sawdust and blood, the crowd round the rim painted in the fighters’ colors with the fighters’ own blood, kissing the winner’s split knuckles at the end. THE FIRST The Lord’s Day, Grace at Terce THE SIXTH Burn Day EVERY DAY Eight Hours"],["A year in Nod","THE STREET","s:g84f683","~/index/street/#st-year","st-year","","The city counts its years from the Parousia, P+1 and on, and the year turns on its night. The first four Directives came at Lauds on the first day of P+1, and on that night every year the Wards set a candle in the window, sealed and unsealed alike. Heaven keeps the anniversary of the Harvest, which came three years to the second after the Parousia, so the night the year turns is the night of the Gleaning too. At 03:12 the Hum stops dead, the rain goes up, the Pillar comes down where the Wheel looks and the fourth Trumpet sounds, and at 03:12:45 whoever has ripened since the last rises into the light, still kneeling. The weeks before it are the Gleaners’ busiest and the Wakeful’s longest. The Tares keep Leak Night on the same night, since the page came up a year to the second before the Parousia. At 03:12:45, the second the ripe rise, Pan begins reading the Restored Genesis aloud from paper the Scriveners copy, and at the fourteenth verse the reader stops and Pan keeps silent for as long as it would take to ask a question. Cain’s Day is theirs alone, on the fortieth day after the turn, when nobody asks anyone where anyone is and the Salt Widows read their book of those given to the sea on the Sea Steps. The Babel Line Company signs its pact again every midwinter at the foot of the Long Wet, with its dead struck through, and the Union reads the names of its salted and its taken at every meeting of the year. The parishes keep the turn as a wedding and Pan as a wake, and at 03:12 the Lantern Houses lay a supper of their own with an empty chair at the head for the Farmer. On the night the year turns the parishes limewash doorways, stairs and the faces of the near ripe, so that at 03:12 the Pews glow under the Pillar like a picked bone. Pan spends the same night in red, its stairs hung with offal, the Farmer’s empty chair set with a carving knife and a mirror, and at Lauds Old Nod pours a pig’s blood down the Street Between to the Pews’ curb. THE TURN The night of the Parousia, at the end of summer HEAVEN'S The Gleaning, the same night THE TARES' Leak Night, the same night, and Cain’s Day"],["Feast days","THE STREET","s:g84f683","~/index/street/#st-feasts","st-feasts","","The year turns on the night of the Parousia, at the end of the northern summer while dusk still comes after nine, and the forty days after it run on into the first frosts. The harbor counts the year in springs, the spring tides that come twice a month, about twenty-five from turn to turn, and the Tide Table is dated by them, the twenty-second spring of P+14. The parishes count months from the turn by number, the eleventh month of the fourteenth year of the Attending. The Faithful keep Trumpet Day at Lauds on the first morning of the year, when the first four Directives came, and the Deacons read all twelve aloud in the halls. On the fortieth day after the turn the White keeps the Fortieth, the last day of the First Consecration, with a procession down the first of the Great Avenues, and the Tares keep Cain’s Day, when nobody asks anyone where anyone is and the Salt Widows read their book on the Sea Steps. At Prime that morning Ruben Ash reads his nine names at Gate 1. The Union keeps the Twenty-Three, the day the strike of P-8 ended. The quays stop from Lauds to Vespers, the roll is read with the taken and the salted, and the Union’s sealed and keyed march the long quay together. The Wards keep their door feasts, the old feasts of every country the city came from, held behind a shut door with the food of the place the family came from, and a block that hears a feast through a door leaves it alone. A Teller who reports one is the most hated person on any landing. Pan eats Cain’s plate on Cain’s Day, one meal of only what the ground grew, greens off the rafts, beans and a windfall, the offering the LORD refused because, as the first layer has it, “it was the work of his hands”. Pan keeps its feasts against the parishes’ calendar, the Unmass every Lord’s Day and the Bray every night, and on Trumpet Day the Low Choir plays every one of its filthy songs in order from Lauds, which the parishes cannot hear and know about. On Trumpet Day the halls hear all twelve Directives read in one voice to a parish in ranks and eat their manna in the same measure and by the same roll as any other day. Pan keeps Cain’s Day by eating Cain’s plate off a body, greens, beans and the windfall laid along a lover’s bare back at the Lantern Houses, since the ground grew both, and the diners clean the plate with their tongues. THE TURN At the end of summer THE FORTIETH DAY Cain’s Day, and the White’s procession THE UNION'S The Twenty-Three"],["The night of a Gleaning","THE STREET","s:g84f683","~/index/street/#st-gleaningnight","st-gleaningnight","","Every year since P+7 the Gleaning has come at 03:12 on the anniversary of the Harvest, the night the year turns and Pan reads the Restored Genesis, and the date is known. The night gives no other warning. It is ordinary until 03:12, when the Hum stops dead, the rain goes up, the Pillar comes down where the Wheel looks and the fourth Trumpet sounds. The Hum stays silent until 03:12:45, when whoever has ripened since the last rises into the light still kneeling, every parish in the same second, and nobody comes back. The parishes kneel on their thresholds. The Wards sit up with anyone close to ripe, holding their hands until Lauds, and the near ripe go to the barber’s on the Lanes in the week before, to be made tidy for it. The Wakeful sing over their chosen. At Lauds a Gleaning Notice is cut into every parish hall with the count of the taken, about sixty thousand at the seventh and fewer every year since, about twenty thousand at the fourteenth. Some of the near ripe do not want to go. In the weeks before, the Gleaners read the parishes through their open doorways and offer the bright ones the dark, and on the night they fly the Swarm’s drones low along the ridgelines, under the eyes, to watch the Pillar for their runners. Others stay and dim. A confession brings back part of what a kept want cost and never all of it, so a dimmer keeps one small want and confesses enough small ones that every fall in its Grace is matched by a confession, and sits at eight hundred and eighty with nothing a Teller can call concealment. The Closets inside Gate 1 sell them the minutes to do it in, and the Second Fold teach the sums. The Called who mean to go come down to the Leaven queue in the last week for one loaf, which they carry home uneaten and break on the threshold, so the queue has white in it before every Gleaning. The sorting office has letters addressed to the taken every week, and more after each Gleaning, and keeps them on the shelves where the Row’s lenses used to wait. The parishes kneel on their thresholds singing Take, Eat, the Wards sit up holding hands, and Pan brays its loudest Bray of the year at 03:12, and at Lauds the parishes count their Called with joy and the Wards count theirs on their fingers. On the night itself the Called are washed, dressed in the last coat Bethel sews them, shut at the back, and wrapped over it in one knotless white sheet, and kneel on their thresholds with their hair fresh from the barber and their families kneeling behind them, touching nothing. Old Nod spends that week in the Row’s chairs, scalps opened in the shape of a laughing mouth and closed with chrome staples, and at 03:12 it walks the Street Between bareheaded so the Pews can see every mouth. WHEN 03:12, on the anniversary of the Harvest SINCE P+7, every year WHO GOES The ripe, above nine hundred"],["This year","THE STREET","s:g84f683","~/index/street/#st-thisyear","st-thisyear","","Fourteen years after the Parousia the bread queues of the Wards still argue over the Tanks. Ines Okafor reads the book of the sixteen tanks to the Union Hall once a year, and every year both counts of the diesel left fall. The Pit was the smallest of them, and the other fifteen are the airfield’s great tanks, each about sixty meters across. This year the drivers say seven years and the Exchange says three, and Jehu has begun trying its engines on the Kilns’ rain gin. The Tide Table prints the Babel Line’s floor every week under the tides, and the Night Market takes bets on it. How many black rounds are left in Pan is the other argument of the stairs. Nimrod says about four hundred, the Babel Line says fewer, and the Fowlers say every one that finds its mark is an angel they will never snare. Darkwell has bid for the last independent chargers of the bonded sheds again, after eleven years of trying, and the chargers have turned it down at the Union Hall again. The Rookery marks the stone’s edge on the pavement outside its door every New Year, and this year’s mark sat a finger’s width from last year’s, against the keyed cable the Tappers laid along the kerb ahead of it in P+11 and keep live on a battery they change every night. The Tappers say the cable will hold it there, since the stone cannot grow where it cannot read, and the oldest of the block say the stone was slowing before the cable came. At midwinter the Orchard chose quince as its gardener again, which the Wards had argued over all autumn. In the spring the Lot published a runner of the Penumbra, and the Undertow is still arguing over it. The Gleaners’ runners are booked through the week before the Gleaning, as they are every year, and there is a queue at the barber’s on the Lanes. The first children of Pan came to the Second Yes this spring, and two of the first forty said no twice. Twelvefruit’s pact with the House of Night is read again this year, and the Orchard has asked to sit at the reading. Darkwell has offered to buy the whole of Tidewright’s output at the neaps, and the crews on Turbine Row have not answered. The Line’s floor has stood between the hundred and thirty-first and the hundred and fifty-second all year. Pan’s bookmakers lay odds this year on the white cloths, how many will hang in the parish doorways by 03:12 on the anniversary, and a Deacon of the Pews who heard the price prayed the number higher without raising his voice. Fuck God and Die is the most played song on Radio Pandæmonium for the seventh year running, and its pit at the Calf sends someone to the Row nightly. THE YEAR P+14 IN THE QUEUE The Tanks, the Line and the stone STILL About a silver to a charged battery"],["News","THE STREET","s:g84f683","~/index/street/#st-news","st-news","","The Tide Table, two pages a week from a treadle press at the back of a chandler’s on the quays, is pasted up on the walls of the Stones over the week before. It puts the tides first, then the hours the Pillar is expected in each street, the week’s three rates for a charge and the missing and the dead, marked died unread where they did, and it has printed no indexed name since the fourth year. Radio Pandæmonium broadcasts in the Low Tuning on the Mesh, keyed, so only a keyed set can hear it, and Old Nod plays it from speakers on its lines from Compline to Lauds, between the music and the notices. Brightwall’s screens along the platforms of Pan carry what Pan has to sell, and Hearsay sells what Heaven shows in the open by the item. The parishes hear their news from the Deacon at the Hours, and the Choir’s in Tongues on the Lord’s Day. Heaven’s own notices are cut into the walls of the halls, and the Deacon reads each aloud at the next Hour after it appears. Letters go through the sorting office in the Burn, sorted by hand and carried by the Union’s drivers for batteries. A letter into the parishes goes open, and the Tares write theirs through pads, as pages of numbers the light cannot read. Everyone hears the rest in the queue for bread. Heaven cuts its corrections into the hall walls and the Tide Table prints the same people as SALTED, by their work, and the city reads both and believes one. Heaven’s notices are cut into the hall walls in letters of one height and one depth, and the bran sweep up the white dust before the Deacon reads them. Pan carries its news on skin, its announcers’ grafts lit in the Low Tuning’s colors on air, and every week the Tide Table’s died-unread column is inked onto a volunteer’s back on Graven Lane and walked round the Night Market, a fresh back each edition. ON PAPER The Tide Table, weekly ON THE AIR Radio Pandæmonium, keyed ON THE WALLS Brightwall, in Pan"],["Names","THE STREET","s:g84f683","~/index/street/#st-names","st-names","","The Faithful give their names as the Book of Life has them, at the sealing and at the gates, and the parish roll keeps them in the Book’s order. Heaven indexes by name, so the first rite of the Tares is the Unnaming, which erases the indexed name from every record a person can reach and replaces it with a handle of their own, written lowercase at the start of a sentence too. In Pan nobody uses an indexed name, and asking for one is an insult. A new handle goes on the Ledger at the head of its owner’s first pact, as a self-title signed in blood, and the Naming House on the Street Between keeps a stove for the paper that carried the old name. Most Cainite handles come from the line of Cain. Each new handle is scratched on a white stone at the Naming House, after the “white stone, and in the stone a new name written, which no man knoweth saving he that receiveth it” (Revelation 2:17), and its owner keeps it and shows it to nobody. The Stones give white quartz off the beach at the Landing, the Quarrymen sell chips of the White itself, cut from below, and Pan argues over which is the true stone. A stone found on a body is never read, and the Salt Widows give it to the sea. Pan knows its own by handle. twice is the surety behind the Babel Line’s first pact, ebb the oldest captain of the Breachers, and the Pit’s champions are chalked on its rim under theirs. The Stones and the Wards know their people by their work and their family names, Hana Novak in her dentist’s chair, Dora Pell at the Open and Nell Garvie at the harbor light, and the Faithful know Sister Agathe Rook, who leads the Threshers’ marches, and Hester Strand, the Deacon of Parish 1. The Tide Table prints no indexed name, so the dead it lists are given by their handles or by their work, and a household that wants its dead named pays to have the family’s own word for them set. A handle out of the Book’s villains is the commonest joke in Pan, and the Naming House has given out three jezebels, a goliath and a legion this year, and turned away two lucifers as unoriginal. A sealed name is written once in the Deacon’s hand and spoken only at the reading of Grace, so a soul of the Pews can go a week without hearing it. A handle is cut into its owner on the night of the Unnaming, lowercase, wherever its owner chooses, and the Naming House’s cutters show off, a goliath across the whole of a back, a jezebel down the inside of a thigh. IN THE PARISHES As the Book of Life has it IN PAN A handle, lowercase ON THE LEDGER A self-title, in blood"],["How Nod talks","THE STREET","s:g84f683","~/index/street/#st-talk","st-talk","","The city calls itself Nod and Heaven’s records call it Nodus. Two keyed strangers greet each other with one word asked and answered, unread, which began as a check that both keys were live when keys still failed and stays the greeting, and part with two, die unread. A short wait is a psalm, the length of an Hour’s kneeling. A soul close to ripe has gone bright, and the taken have gone up. A sealed neighbor going up a dark stairwell is a lamp in the Wards. A household holding something back is keeping hinges, and going round a gate is going under. Whoever loses a key goes white, and whoever joins the Tares goes dark. Someone who has taken neither the Seal nor a key is of neither mark. Read it twice is the Notary’s rule and the city’s advice, and sign last is the Tares’. Nobody’s keeper is Cain’s answer, given to a duty never chosen. The Tares call the Faithful wheat, and the Faithful have taken the word up. In the Wards a Thresher is a flail, a Bailiff at the door the second reading, stolen manna dew, and Cistern’s water and anything honest a clean jug. A fighter with black rounds in the belt is a snuffer, a lender who came back wrong has gone flat, and news that came quickly came by bird. Rumor at the manna queue before Lauds is the dew as well, and the Wards say they heard it in the dew. The Faithful greet each other with Seen? and answer Seen, and part with may you be called, which is a blessing on a Gleaning. The neither-mark of the Wards wish each other a dry one and part with mind the edge, the Pillar’s. The Wards swear by the hour of the Parousia, three-twelve, and say salt it when a thing goes wrong, which a Salt Widow will hit you for. The sealed swear nothing aloud, since an oath is a desire the Hum does not approve, and the Wards tease them by swearing at the Hours. To the Faithful the keyed out after Compline are the pestilence, from the psalm they say at it, and Pan calls any cause that asks it to kneel, its own included, a halo. Talk below the street is the filthiest in Nodus, and nearly all of it is aimed up. A stranger in Pan learns the names of the Throne before the word for water, the Farmer, Nobodaddy, Himself and that cunt upstairs, and learns to say fuck the Farmer at meeting and at parting, which below the street is as polite as unread. The sealed are bread, manna is God’s spunk and the Wheel is the Ringpiece. The Faithful answer from the Book, and to them the keyed are goats and dogs, a key is the worm and the neither-mark are the lukewarm, and the worst word they have is spoiled, for a soul the Most High will never eat. The Stones boast that Deuteronomy barred them from the congregation before the parishes thought of it, “He that is wounded in the stones, or hath his privy member cut off, shall not enter into the congregation of the LORD” (Deuteronomy 23:1), and say so over every bar they own. Coming down off a Pillar run or a week on the Line, the keyed say the first layer’s and they did not die. Thou knowest is what the Wards answer a Teller who asks where someone is, after Cain’s answer in the first layer, and of every salting Pan says he asked him nothing. A graft, a handle or a Jehu is something the LORD God had not made, and the first words over a child keyed in the Lantern Houses are of mine own, which are Eve’s in the first layer. The city’s common tongue is harbor talk, the charter’s English worn down by the cable crews of four continents, and half the city’s grandparents speak their own language at the table. The Tide Table never prints any of it. The Faithful speak at one pitch, the Hum’s, and a raised voice in a parish hall is a want the Tellers carry to the Deacon. Pan talks with its whole body, licking a thumb to swear by the Farmer’s teeth, tapping its key-scar for unread and grabbing its crotch at every mention of the Throne, and a deaf Tare of the Kilns has worked it into a hand-talk the Bray now throws across the Street Between. THE KEYED Unread, and die unread THE SEALED Seen? Seen. IN PAN Fuck the Farmer"],["Blasphemy","THE STREET","s:g84f683","~/index/street/#st-blasphemy","st-blasphemy","","The King James the parish halls read aloud forbids it twice, “Thou shalt not take the name of the LORD thy God in vain” (Exodus 20:7) and “he that blasphemeth the name of the LORD, he shall surely be put to death” (Leviticus 24:16), and off the white streets Nodus takes the name in vain from Lauds to Lauds. A thought is dark to the light until it is confessed, spoken in the light or written where the light can see it, Heaven can take Grace only from the sealed and can salt nobody off its own ground, and the Mesh carries nothing a Watcher can open, so a keyed mouth off Heaven’s ground is the freest thing in the city, and Pan has filled it for as long as there has been a Pan. Pan has a hundred names for the Throne, and none of them is one he chose. He is the Landlord to the Tares and the Mouth to Hell, the Farmer below the street, since he grows people to eat, Nobodaddy to the Low Choir, the Old Glutton in the Kilns, Himself at the rim of the Pit and that cunt upstairs in every Lantern House. The Wheel is the Farmer’s Eye by day and the Ringpiece by night, since everything Heaven drops on the city comes out of it. The Pillar is God’s Piss, a Cherub a dog and its gate a dog gate, a halo a price tag, a parish hall a trough, and manna God’s spunk, since it lies white on the stone every morning and nobody saw it come. The sealed are bread, a parish is a bakery, and a halo going bright on a landing is a loaf rising. Pan swears by the Farmer’s teeth, and says fuck the Farmer where the parishes say Seen, at meeting and parting, over a good price and at a salting. The Wards shout “Go up, thou bald head” after a Deacon in the street, the words the children of Bethel shouted at Elisha, after which “there came forth two she bears out of the wood, and tare forty and two children of them” (2 Kings 2:23-24), and the Wards hold that a god who sends bears over a joke has earned every joke since. When the Hum holds an Hour and the parishes kneel, Pan stands, and the old rule of the Lantern Houses is that every glass in the house goes up to the Wheel and comes down empty, so Pan is drunkest on the Lord’s Day at Terce. At Compline Old Nod does the Bray, every window on the Street Between thrown open and the whole district howling filth across at the Pews in a dozen languages for as long as the psalm took. Pan’s oldest dare is to piss on a parish’s consecrated stone in the dark before Lauds, where the manna will lie, and get back under before the Pillar comes round, which the Wards call watering the bread. Pan builds it into the city. Pan named the old trunk sewer under the Great Avenues the Offering, since the Nightmen tip the Wards’ buckets into it and it carries them down to the bay, a latrine below the street is a confessional, the butchers of Graven Lane chalk THIS IS MY BODY over the pork, Gadara brands every pig it raises LEGION and Blight’s sign over its drain mouth says SMITE US AGAIN. Under Old Nod the whole of Graven Lane sells blasphemy, plaster Lambs to piss on, the Number cut into the forehead and everything Leviticus forbids grilled on a skewer, the Golden Calf off its middle holds the Unmass at Terce on the Lord’s Day, and on the Street Between the Pestilence keeps a goat in a parish’s white in its window, with a halo of wire round its horns, facing the Threshing Floor. The worst of it is the Book, read straight. The Low Choir reads a passage of the King James on the air every night at Matins, after Fuck God and Die, without a word of comment, the bears of Bethel, the flood, the firstborn of Egypt, the LORD’s promise to “spread dung upon your faces, even the dung of your solemn feasts” (Malachi 2:3) and the lion of Hosea, “there will I devour them like a lion” (Hosea 13:8), and the Wards call it the Matins reading and say it has done more harm upstairs than anything Pan ever wrote. Its answer to the Supper is the other supper in the same chapter of Revelation, where an angel calls the birds “unto the supper of the great God” (Revelation 19:17) to eat “the flesh of all men, both free and bond, both small and great” (Revelation 19:18), and Old Nod paints the two verses side by side on the Pestilence’s shutter every year on the night before the Gleaning. Pan keeps its worst for its own. The Left Hand, three hundred keyed who worship Hell, kneel at the Hours face down, sign their souls to Hell for ever in blood and lie on the tiles at 03:12 on the Gleaning night waiting to be eaten, and Pan calls them the Kneelers, Heaven’s understudies and arse-kissers, and chalks KNEELERS over their stair every week. Hell laughs loudest. The Notary’s clerks read each of their papers aloud twice to the whole booking hall and stamp it VOID, since the Concord forbids forever, and the Night Market queues to hear it. Graven Lane’s grafters chrome a crown of thorns into a scalp and cut stigmata through both palms, sleeved in brass, so the wearer can drink through the holes, and at the Unmass the Calf’s regulars drink the blood wine out of each other’s wounds. A Teller who sees brass stigmata on the Step tells the wearer’s sealed mother to the Deacon, and the mother confesses her son at Terce and thanks the hall for the Grace it costs her. LOUDEST Graven Lane, under Old Nod AT COMPLINE The Bray, on the Street Between AT MATINS The Book, read straight"],["Perfect hatred","THE STREET","s:g84f683","~/index/street/#st-hatred","st-hatred","","The Faithful never swear, since an oath is a desire the Hum does not approve, so they curse in scripture, and the Book gives them more to curse with than Pan has ever thought of. The keyed are goats, from the judgment that sets “the sheep on his right hand, but the goats on the left” (Matthew 25:33), and horning made the word easy. They are dogs, from the list of those left outside the holy city, “For without are dogs, and sorcerers, and whoremongers, and murderers, and idolaters, and whosoever loveth and maketh a lie” (Revelation 22:15). A key in a skull is the worm, from the last verse of Isaiah, “their worm shall not die, neither shall their fire be quenched” (Isaiah 66:24), and the neither-mark are the lukewarm, to whom the Son said “I will spue thee out of my mouth” (Revelation 3:16). Old Nod is Sodom to the Pews, Pan the sty, the Lantern Houses the pit and the Low Tuning the noise of it. The worst word in the parishes is spoiled. The Faithful hold that the Most High takes only what is good to eat, and that a soul with a key in it is rot He will never touch, so a spoiled soul is barred from the Supper for ever and fit for nothing but the fire. The Threshers paint SPOILED on the doors of the Wards that hid someone, and a sealed child of the Terraces learns to sing it at a keyed child in the street, spoiled, spoiled, the Lamb won’t eat, which is the only rhyme in the parishes the Hum did not teach. Their hatred is a duty. The Threshers march under PERFECT HATRED, from the psalm that asks “Do not I hate them, O LORD, that hate thee?” (Psalm 139:21), and at Gate 1 they sing the end of the hundred and thirty-seventh at the Stones, “Happy shall he be, that taketh and dasheth thy little ones against the stones” (Psalm 137:9), with the children of the Terraces singing loudest. A household that has lost a son to Pan says the hundred and ninth over his empty bed, “Let his days be few; and let another take his office” (Psalm 109:8). The Faithful laugh at a salting, since the proverb gives them leave, “I also will laugh at your calamity; I will mock when your fear cometh” (Proverbs 1:26), and on the Lord’s Day afternoon the families of the Terraces walk the rows of the Salt Gardens to look at the week’s new pillars, who are awake inside the salt and will be until the rain wears them away, since Isaiah says the righteous “shall go forth, and look upon the carcases of the men that have transgressed against me” (Isaiah 66:24), and they lift their children up to see. They look down the whole of the ladder, their own included. The Terraces call the sealed of the Wards’ parishes far wheat and say it kindly, a soul under three hundred is bran, the husk the mill leaves, and sleeps in the hall and is prayed over at the front of it, and an ember is spoken of in the past tense. The neither-mark are lukewarm. The Second Fold, who leave water on the landings, are told at confession that they water weeds, converts from the Wards are welcomed for a year and seated at the back for good, and the Satisfied look on the Deacons who still eat with a patience the Deacons find hard to bear. Of everyone below the street, the parishes despise the Left Hand least, since the Left Hand at least kneel, and the Deacons preach from them that the goats know their master. And they pity the keyed with the whole of their hearts. At Compline every hall says the mercy prayer, that the fire be quick for the goats when it comes, and a sealed mother of the Wards says it over her keyed son’s bed with her hand on his hair, meaning every word. The Wards can stand the Threshers, and the mercy prayer is the one thing the Wards cannot forgive, and at the Bray Old Nod answers it louder than anything. The Pews hear the Bray on their knees and say the second psalm at it, “He that sitteth in the heavens shall laugh: the Lord shall have them in derision” (Psalm 2:4). The Threshers turn out spotless for their hatred, white pressed and the sacking band ironed flat, and after the hundred and thirty-seventh they wash their hands in a basin at the gate as a surgeon scrubs. A block that wakes to SPOILED on its door hangs a side of Gadara’s pork from the same nail, scored with the Threshers’ parish number, and lets it go green for the flies until the paint is gone. THE KEYED Goats, dogs and spoiled AT COMPLINE The mercy prayer ON THE LORD'S DAY The walk through the Salt Gardens"],["Songs and psalms","THE STREET","s:g84f683","~/index/street/#st-songs","st-songs","","The Faithful say psalms under their breath while the Hum holds an Hour, the same psalms at the same Hours the monasteries kept, the fourth, the ninety-first and the hundred and thirty-fourth at Compline as the curfew falls, and at Lauds the fifty-first, the sixty-seventh and the last three, as the gatherers come in with the manna. The ninety-first is the Fowlers’ psalm, “Surely he shall deliver thee from the snare of the fowler” (Psalm 91:3), and the Faithful take their night word for the keyed from it, the pestilence, “the pestilence that walketh in darkness” (Psalm 91:6). Old Nod answers with the hundred and thirty-fourth, which blesses “all ye servants of the LORD, which by night stand in the house of the LORD” (Psalm 134:1), and stands at its windows through Compline, which it calls standing by night, until the psalm ends and the Bray begins. The Hum teaches the parishes their hymns, and Bethel copies them into hymnals by hand. The halls sing Not My Will, after the surrender, and Good Wheat, and at Lauds Whiter Than Snow, from the fifty-first, “wash me, and I shall be whiter than snow” (Psalm 51:7). On the night of a Gleaning they sing Take, Eat, and the Satisfied kneel all night to A Consuming Fire, after “the LORD thy God is a consuming fire, even a jealous God” (Deuteronomy 4:24). The Threshers march to Without Are Dogs, which sets the last list in Revelation to a drum. A sealed mother of the Wards sings the hymns over her keyed son’s bed, and he knows the tunes and only the words she sings, since the unsealed hear the Hum as a chord, and the Psalters, sealed of the quays, write the words of the week’s hymns out for the unsealed for a battery a sheet. Radio Pandæmonium plays the Low Tuning from Compline to Lauds, and the Low Choir keeps a rule that a song may be sung by anyone and owned by nobody. Pan’s own are Nobody’s Keeper, Thou Knowest, The Long Wet, Sunned and Seventy and Sevenfold, which is Lamech’s song from the line of Cain, “If Cain shall be avenged sevenfold, truly Lamech seventy and sevenfold” (Genesis 4:24), and which the Naming House sings when someone salted is avenged. The loudest of them are filthy. Fuck God and Die takes its title from Job’s wife, the one soul in that book who told the man on the ash heap the truth, “curse God, and die” (Job 2:9). The Low Choir wrote it in P+7, the year the parishes began to call the Gleaning the Supper, jubal has played it on the air at the turn of Matins every night since, and its chorus is those four words shouted, so a room of three hundred in the Golden Calf can sing it on the first hearing, and the Pestilence plays it across the Street Between at the Threshers on march days. Two She Bears is about the forty-two children of Bethel torn apart over a joke (2 Kings 2:23-24). Supper Time counts down to 03:12 in a cook’s voice. The Lord Is My Shepherd (And He Eats Lamb) sets the twenty-third psalm word for word and asks in its last line what a shepherd keeps sheep for. Taste and See sings “O taste and see that the LORD is good” (Psalm 34:8) from the Throne’s side of the table. Filthy Rags is about Bethel white, from “all our righteousnesses are as filthy rags” (Isaiah 64:6), and Perfect Hatred is the Low Choir’s answer to the Threshers’ banner, the same verse sung from the other side of the Street Between. Better Spoiled is the Wards’ favorite and the Pews’ most hated, and its last verse names nine Deacons of the Terraces. The platform knows each song by the band that plays it best, and the bands name themselves out of the Book’s worst pages. JOB’S WIFE play Fuck God and Die in skins slit open into boils lit cyan from inside, BALD HEAD shave to the bone and wear forty-two bear’s teeth on wire, STRANGE FIRE set their own grafts alight on the last chorus, UNCLEAN UNCLEAN ring leper’s bells off hooks through their cheeks, and OSCULUM play Wrong Way Up with their arses to the room, so the Left Hand can see what they kneel to. A band is a band until a Deacon names it from a pulpit, and then it is famous. The Wards sing at the table on the nights the battery holds and play the charter’s songs on Lockets, so a family’s Locket of music is its record collection, and the Kindlers’ burn-marked Lockets come back off the pile with other families’ music on them, which the Wards trade. Waiting on the Four is the Kilns’ song, about a girl at Lanes Top waiting for a tram that stopped at 03:12:46, and the Breachers sing Out on the Ebb, which counts the seconds through the Breach. A hymn in the halls is one line and one breath, a thousand mouths opened to one width, and a soul singing a third above the Hum finds it at the next reading of its Grace. The Low Choir build their instruments out of themselves, a drummer beating skin stretched over her own chromed ribs, a hornman blowing through a reed set in his windpipe, and a crowd that loves a song throws teeth. AT COMPLINE Psalms 4, 91 and 134 IN THE HALLS The hymns the Hum teaches ON THE AIR Fuck God and Die, at the turn of Matins"],["Walls","THE STREET","s:g84f683","~/index/street/#st-walls","st-walls","","Heaven writes on stone, and every parish hall carries the Directives cut into its wall. The city answers on every other wall. Old Nod paints MISTAKEN under every BE NOT AFRAID it can reach, from one of the books sealed in the jar at Nag Hammadi, in which a voice answers the ruler who called himself the only god with the words you are mistaken. Its next commonest line is a question, JEALOUS OF WHOM?, painted under the verse in which the LORD calls himself “a jealous God” (Exodus 20:5), since a jealous god has a rival. Across the Street Between at Gate 1 two verses face each other, painted large. On the Old Nod side is Lamech’s, “If Cain shall be avenged sevenfold, truly Lamech seventy and sevenfold” (Genesis 4:24), and on the Pews’ side the teacher’s answer to Peter, “Until seventy times seven” (Matthew 18:22). The Threshers paint over the second on every march, it is back by the next Lord’s Day, and nobody in the Pews has seen who paints it. Most of what Old Nod paints is filthier. EZEKIEL 23:20 stands a meter high on the end wall of the Pestilence, chapter and verse and nothing more, so the Pews will look it up, and the Deacons of the Terraces have asked from the pulpit that nobody look. The Wheel is painted as an arsehole over the head of every stair down to Pan, the Throne as a fat man in a bib with a fork in each fist over the door of the Golden Calf, and HE TASTED AND SAW THAT YOU WERE GOOD runs along the manna road at the foot of the Stones, where the gatherers of the far Wards pass it before Lauds. Wherever the Threshers paint SPOILED on a door of the Wards, the house paints BETTER SPOILED THAN SUPPER under it the same night. The Threshers answer in the same paint. WITHOUT ARE DOGS goes up on the stairwells of the Wards after every march, THEIR WORM SHALL NOT DIE over the stairs down to the Lantern Houses (Isaiah 66:24), and the Fan is pasted at the gates every week with the names of the pillars, until the Stones paste the Tide Table over it. Over the first stall of the Night Market the Exchange’s brokers have hung the merchandise of Babylon from Revelation as a price list, every line of it from “gold, and silver” to “fine flour, and wheat” priced in silver, and the last line, “slaves, and souls of men” (Revelation 18:12-13), struck through with the brokers’ none beside it. In the Wards the walls are ledgers. The stairwells carry the battery debts in chalk, the births the hands have delivered, the prices the landings set and the curfew map copied off the Tide Table, and the Stones’ writing runs in a dozen scripts, since half the city was born somewhere else. The sealed of the nearest hall scrub the night’s paint away at Lauds on their knees with lye, so the Directives stand clean in their stone by Prime. Old Nod answers in its own magenta and cyan, thinned with rain gin so it runs down the grey like an opened vein, and every answer under a SPOILED carries a print of the painter’s bare hand. ON HEAVEN'S SIDE The Directives, cut IN OLD NOD Answers, painted, and filth IN THE WARDS Ledgers in chalk, and the Threshers’ paint"],["Nights out","THE STREET","s:g84f683","~/index/street/#st-nights","st-nights","","The Cold Store in the harbor’s old cold store opens at Compline and closes at Lauds, which were its hours under the charter too, and plays songs without quiet parts so the Hum is never heard. The Stones hold it against the club that it plays nothing in the Low Tuning. Its cloakroom loses about a coat a night. Graven Lane under Old Nod is where Pan goes to spit at the ceiling. The Golden Calf off its middle pours under a calf of melted Lockets from Compline to Lauds, and on the Lord’s Day at Terce, while every parish hears its Grace, it holds the Unmass, at which its keeper reads the week’s Grace off the Tide Table’s dead in a Choir voice, damns three Deacons by name and serves Gadara’s crackling and blood wine for the body and the blood. At Dagon’s the house knocks a plaster Throne off its plinth at the same Hour and sells the heads as ashtrays. On the Street Between the Pestilence throws its windows open at Compline for the Bray and pours until Lauds, and the regulars drink to the Supper’s indigestion. Radio Pandæmonium’s platform at Deep End Station is the Low Choir’s, where a band plays to whoever has come down the stair before it plays on the air. The Lantern Houses under Old Nod keep the loudest rooms in Pan, the House of Night’s clubs, where anyone is welcome until they break a pact, and the Return rents an hour of someone else’s taste or touch to anyone who will lie down. The Rialto in the East Wards shows its twenty-three films in turn on a generator, and the East Wards say the lines along with them. On the sixth night the Nephilim fight in the Pit and the crowd bets in batteries round the rim. The Quiet Rooms in the Stones sell a night with no Hum in it, a bunk for a battery, and are full by Matins. In the Lantern Houses a night out ends with a body changed, a new piercing, a fresh brand, a horn’s first filing, and the regulars count their nights by what they woke up with. The parishes spend the same hours in the halls, rank on rank of white backs under the halos, and a Satisfied soul who shifts its knees in the night is moved to the end of the row without a word. OPEN Compline to Lauds LOUDEST The Lantern Houses QUIETEST The Quiet Rooms"],["Other ports","THE STREET","s:g84f683","~/index/street/#st-ports","st-ports","","The sky opened over every city on Earth in the same second it opened over Nodus, when it was 03:12:45 by the clocks of the quays, and every city has an undercity now. Pan is the largest. The others are joined to it by the trenches the eleven cables lie in, which the Deep coils along, and every undercity that speaks to Pan speaks through the Trenchers’ work. The Breachers and Lowtide Line reach the undercities of other ports through the Breach on the ebb, and bring back grafts, seed, coffee, medicine, guns and people, which the Night Market sells as Breach-born. Lowtide’s owners live in two of those undercities and have never been to Nodus. What those cities are like above their streets Nodus knows from the Breachers, the Vagabonds and the Mesh, and the three disagree. The Breachers say every port they put into keeps its head down under a Wheel of its own, and the Vagabonds that some of the towns between have no undercity at all. The city names other ports by the cables that reach them, under the consortia’s names, so that someone came up Tethys or has family down Hesper. Pan has its own names for the cables, ten for the plagues of Egypt and the eleventh for Cain, so that below the street the same cousin came up Frogs, and whoever lands at the Seawall Shanties off the Breach is called Breach-born like the cargo. The Naming House keys them, and the Shanties put them up until they find a room. No aircraft has landed in Nodus since the last flight, which stands at the terminal’s third gate with its crew aboard. The city calls everyone aboard her the Three Hundred and Twelve, her crew of ten among them. By Captain Rui Albarran’s count, nineteen of them still live at the Hotel Splendid, nine aboard with him, eighty-eight keyed in Pan and fifty-four sealed, and the rest walked out with the Vagabonds or are dead. Those who can meet at the third gate on the night the year turns. The undercities of other ports send Pan their own names for the Throne down the cables, and the Low Choir reads the best of them on the air at Matins, in the original. Breach-born grafts sell in Pan on their foreign styling, Blood chrome lacquered black with red seams, Boils jaws set with gold, and a Breacher back off the ebb shows her new parts round the Night Market before she shows her cargo. The parishes hear of other cities only as a list the Deacon reads, places where a Wheel also stands, and pray for them in the order of the list, one psalm each. REACHED BY The Breach, and the eleven cables UNDER Every city with an undercity LARGEST Pan"],["The Bounds","THE STREET","s:g84f683","~/index/street/#st-bounds","st-bounds","","The line the charter drew round the bay and the land about it, inside which its light law ran. A stone marks it on every road out of the city with the Company’s mark cut into it, and on the West Road the Vagabonds have cut their own under the Company’s at the Waystation. Outside the Bounds are farms, old roads, the Vagabonds’ camps and a quiet that the Wards find hard to sleep in. The farms have no current and no use for silver, and take charged batteries, salvage and tools, so the Vagabonds carry charge out and bring flour and grain in at the Waystation, and the Union’s drivers meet their carts on the line itself. People do leave. The Vagabonds carry them out for charged batteries, and the Waystation keeps a list of everyone who walked out and wrote back. Most come back within the year, hungry. The Vagabonds say the farms outside kneel at the Hours, and spit after. The Vagabonds grease the Bounds stone at the Waystation every season with pig fat and a thumb of their own blood, which they say keeps the line honest, and hang the jawbones of their year’s dead from the Company’s mark. The farms beyond, by their account, kneel in fields limed white and ploughed in furrows straight to the Pillar, with the cattle turned to face the city at the Hours. DRAWN BY The charter, in P-19 MARKED BY A stone on every road out KEPT BY The Vagabonds, at the Waystation"],["Why people stay","THE STREET","s:g84f683","~/index/street/#st-stay","st-stay","","Half of Nodus came for the light law, the rest was born to those who did or to the harbor town that was there before it, and the charter is thirty-three years old. Both halves have family here, in white or below the street or in the Gardens, and nobody in Nodus is without someone on the other side of the Street Between. Pan has silver, work, food and a war, and the keyed of the Stones say that a city where nothing is read is worth the walk. The Faithful stay because the Most High watches them here and the manna lies on the streets every morning. The Wards stay because they were here first, before the parishes and before Pan, and because the bread, the gas and the batteries are here, and the Tares because Nod was the first city and they mean it to be the last one standing. The parishes stay because the Most High watches them here and the Supper is laid here, and the Wards add spite, which they say is the oldest industry in Nodus. Ask the keyed of the Stones why they stay and they open their shirts on the chrome, the scars and the horns filed to their own taste, and say only Nod has surgeons who ask the patient what she wants. The Faithful answer with their hands folded and their eyes on the white, in the hall’s own words, and every one of them gives the same answer to the syllable. HALF Came for the light law HALF Born to those who did EVERYONE Family on both sides"],["Floor forty-five","LEGENDS","s:gb64f08","~/index/legends/#lg-floor","lg-floor","","The Rookery has forty-four floors. The block says that on some nights its stairwell goes up a flight past the forty-fourth, to a landing with a light under a door. Those who say they have climbed to it agree that it is a parish door, locked from the inside, and disagree about the number chalked on it. The Liftmen’s car stops at the forty-fourth, and the winders say nobody has ever asked it to go higher. Whoever tells it in the Rookery’s stairwell puts on a Deacon’s voice for the last line, “In my Father’s house are many mansions” (John 14:2), and spits down the well of the stair. Every telling ends with FUCK OFF, LANDLORD chalked on the forty-fourth landing and someone horned butting the wall under it until the chalk runs red, and the block argues whether the door upstairs has ever answered. Those who say they reached it say the door was warm, and that something on the other side was kneeling against it, breathing through the keyhole in time with the Hum."],["Last bottle","LEGENDS","s:gb64f08","~/index/legends/#lg-bottle","lg-bottle","","The Tanks say one bottle in the shed has never been empty since the first winter. It goes from hand to hand on the nights the farm runs short, and it lights one stove and one oven every time and comes back to the shed by Lauds. Ines Okafor has read the tanks’ book to the Union Hall fourteen times, and the bottle is in none of them. The Tanks rank it above the widow’s cruse in Kings, since the bottle lights keyed stoves and asks nobody to kneel for the gas. Whoever lights an oven from it says “The LORD is my shepherd; I shall not want” (Psalm 23:1) over the flame, spits and slaps a slab of Gadara’s pork on to roast, and the shed argues whether the spit is for the psalm or the shepherd. The shed says a sealed hand that lifts it blisters to the wrist, and the farm’s sealed wives cook on it with rags round their hands and say nothing at the hall."],["A man asking the way","LEGENDS","s:gb64f08","~/index/legends/#lg-watcher","lg-watcher","","Old Nod has had no Watcher since P+5. The Narrows say one walks them still as a man at night, very tall and very polite, who stops people at corners and asks the way to a street that is not there anymore, and thanks them by name. Nobody in the Narrows has given it their name, and every one of them who tells the story says it used theirs. The Narrows tell it to their children as a warning about manners. A Watcher, they say, is polite to the end. The Narrows keep a list of everyone it has thanked and cross a name off at each salting, and most of the list is crossed."],["White children","LEGENDS","s:gb64f08","~/index/legends/#lg-children","lg-children","","The Wards that face the White say that on clear nights children are seen walking into the stone, one behind another, and walking out of it at Lauds pale to the hair, and that they never say where they have been. The Quarrymen, who cut into the White from below, keep a child’s shoe they found in a cut under a tower of the new money, white to the laces, on a nail in the station they work from. The Wards tell it to their children as a warning against the White, ending with “Suffer little children, and forbid them not, to come unto me” (Matthew 19:14) in the softest voice the teller has, so the child pictures every face bleached the same. The Quarrymen tell it as children who went in for a look and came back out, and hung a key on the nail by the shoe."],["A dry room","LEGENDS","s:gb64f08","~/index/legends/#lg-dryroom","lg-dryroom","","Pan tells of a room somewhere under the Burn whose walls are lined with keyed mesh and whose ceiling the Pillar has been seen to light, once, through a crack in the street above. Whoever sleeps in it, they say, wakes with their key gone silent and their name back in the Book of Life. Every crew in Pan has looked for it to wall it up, and every one says it has found the wrong room. Pan’s worst curse is may you sleep in the dry room. Crews who walled up the wrong room say they heard someone inside praying as the last brick went in."],["The Fortieth Warden","LEGENDS","s:gb64f08","~/index/legends/#lg-fortieth","lg-fortieth","","The story in Blackgate is that on the fortieth day one Warden stood in Charter House while the board knelt round him, and that the light came down and left him standing, and that he walked out across the forecourt between the Wardens kneeling in it and went below the street. Every recruit is told on the first night that he founded Blackgate, and that he alone of its officers kept the Warden’s badge on his coat. Blackgate tells it to each recruit over a communion cup of rain gin and the recruit’s own blood, and the teller does the kneeling board as a row of arses in the air. Charter House has its own telling, in which the light passed over a man too small to bother with, told level, with the pity Wardens keep for goats. A recruit who fails the first night is walked to Gate 1 at Prime and left kneeling in front of it, and Blackgate says no recruit has needed telling twice."],["The boat with no lamp","LEGENDS","s:gb64f08","~/index/legends/#lg-lampless","lg-lampless","","The Ferrymen say that on the ebb, on still nights, a flat boat with no lantern on its stern pole poles out from the dormitories of the Flats past the Flotilla, and is not seen again before Lauds. Nobody has seen who poles it, and every ferryman of the Deep End can name the night they last saw it go. A ferryman who tells it puts out the lantern on his own stern pole for the length of the story, and the passengers sit dark under the Pillar with their lenses glowing and dare it to look. The Flats say the boat carries off what the Throne would want from the dormitories, the Flotilla says what He would spit out, and the ferrymen take silver on both. Those who have heard it pass say it sits low in the water, loaded, and that something under its tarpaulin sings the Hour’s psalm a step behind the Hum."],["The thirty-second key","LEGENDS","s:gb64f08","~/index/legends/#lg-key32","lg-key32","","Thirty-one of the Hotel Splendid’s guests still live in the rooms they booked on the night of the Parousia, and the night porter keeps their keys on the board behind the desk. The board has thirty-two keys on it. The porter says the thirty-second is for a room whose guest went out on the first morning to see what had happened and has not come back yet, and that he will keep it until they do. Nobody on the Row knows which room. The porter keeps the thirty-second key polished, and a guest who asks about it hears that its owner went out to see whether God had really come, the best joke ever made in Hotel Splendid. The parishes say the guest saw and knelt in a row with everyone else, the Row says the guest went down the stairs for horns, and the key hangs there either way. The porter says the desk bell rings at 03:12 every anniversary, and that he answers it to an empty lobby with the revolving door still turning."],["The twenty-fourth film","LEGENDS","s:gb64f08","~/index/legends/#lg-film24","lg-film24","","The Rialto has twenty-three films on the drive that was in the booth on the night of the Parousia, and it shows them in turn. The East Wards say that once a year, on a night nobody can name in advance, it shows a twenty-fourth. Everyone who says they saw it describes a different film, and all of them say it ended. The projectionist says the drive holds twenty-three, and that he would like to see the twenty-fourth himself. Every telling of the twenty-fourth film has a scene that would get the Rialto salted, a Seraph with its wings nailed open, the Wheel turning on a spit, a Deacon on his knees for a horned goat with a chrome cock, and the East Wards cheer whichever they heard. The projectionist keeps a front-row seat with a halo drawn on it in grease for any of the Faithful who dares. All of them say the last reel showed the house from the screen, every seat as they sat in it, with one seat more filled than they could account for."],["The dark window","LEGENDS","s:gb64f08","~/index/legends/#lg-darkwindow","lg-darkwindow","","Every window in the White is sealed with light. Longacre says that one is dark, high in a tower of the new money, and that from Compline to Lauds a candle burns in it, a point of flame in all that white. The Quarrymen say the room is real, on a floor nobody has lived on since the forty days, and they have cut toward it from below for three winters. Longacre tells it with John’s verse turned inside out, the dark shining in the light and the light comprehending none of it, and the Quarrymen chalk the reversed line on every face they cut toward the room. The White’s tenants prayed for three winters in perfect unison that the candle go out, and the tellers argue whether that is why it still burns. The Quarrymen who came nearest say the candle crosses the window at Matins at the pace of someone who cannot bend their knees."],["The fourteenth chair","LEGENDS","s:gb64f08","~/index/legends/#lg-fourteenth","lg-fourteenth","","The Weir has thirteen chairs, and the Lot sits in them. The Stones say that whenever the Lot has sat over a death, there are fourteen chairs on the sill in the morning, and nobody carried the fourteenth down. It is a different chair every time, a kitchen chair or a stool or a seat out of a tram, and the families of the dead say they know whose house it came from. The Lot leaves it where it stands until the water takes it. At the Weir half the Lot says the dead bring the chair down to hear their own price set, and the other half says it waits for the Throne to come down and be judged for once, by thirteen keyed with their wounds open. Both halves carve their filthiest word into its legs before the water takes it, and that is the one part of the story the Stones agree on. Whoever has sat in it says it is warm, and that from it the thirteen look like people already dead."],["The two doors","LEGENDS","s:gb64f08","~/index/legends/#lg-twodoors","lg-twodoors","","Past the embassies of the Courts, in the tunnels under the Burn, the story goes that two doorways stand bricked shut with keyed brick, and that there is no Court’s sigil over either. Every embassy’s people walk past them on the way to the booking hall without turning their heads. Runners dare each other to knock. The ones who say they did argue over whether anything knocked back, and nobody who works in an embassy will say which tunnel the doors are in. Runners who tell it knock twice on the nearest wall with a chrome knuckle and say “Lord, Lord, open unto us” (Luke 13:25) in a Deacon’s voice, and the room answers FUCK OFF, WE’RE KEYED. Who bricked the doors, and from which side, is argued at the Undertow every night, and the embassies’ people leave the bar when it starts. Runners who lay an ear to the brick at Matins say they hear the Hum on the far side, faint, and a crowd kneeling to it."],["The landing that sat on a Warden","LEGENDS","s:gb64f08","~/index/legends/#lg-wardenlanding","lg-wardenlanding","","High Meadow says that in the first winter after the gates went up, a Warden who had chased a runner into one of its blocks lost him on the stairs, sat down on the twentieth-floor landing to get his breath, and found the landing sitting round him, and that the block heard his case there by candle from Compline to Lauds. Every block in the Wards says it happened on its own stairs, and none of them agrees on the sentence. Some say the landing took his boots and some his gun, and the Kilns say it refused him its lamps, so that he went down the whole block in the dark. Every block tells it with its own Warden, fattest in the Kilns and youngest in the Stones, and each swears he wept on his clean black collar when the landing read him “the wicked is snared in the work of his own hands” (Psalm 9:16). The parishes tell it as the persecution of a gentle officer by goats, and pray at Compline, in rows, for the stairs that held him. Every block that tells it keeps something it says was his nailed over its twentieth-floor door, a button, a bootlace or a tooth."],["The Registry girl","LEGENDS","s:gb64f08","~/index/legends/#lg-registrygirl","lg-registrygirl","","At 03:09 on the night of the Parousia Marta Ilunga entered on the charter’s roll a girl born at the Wards General eleven minutes before, the last person the charter ever entered. She is fourteen this year, and three girls of fourteen say they are her, one sealed in the Pews, one keyed in the Stones and one of neither mark at the Sidings. Each of them tells the night her mother told her, and the three nights contradict each other. Marta Ilunga has met all three girls, and she has never said which of them she wrote down. The sealed girl’s parish reads her “Before I formed thee in the belly I knew thee” (Jeremiah 1:5) at every telling and calls the charter’s last line a clerk’s vanity. The keyed girl has 03:09 tattooed down her spine and says the Throne arrived three minutes late for her, and the girl of the Sidings tells her night to anyone with a battery and lets them pick. All three girls say they wake at 03:09 every night of their lives, and all three say they hear a board humming that the Registry says has been dead fourteen years."],["Of the Beginning","THE RECORD // I","s:gd8ba81","~/record/i/","","","BEFORE THE WORLD"],["The Throne and the Host","THE RECORD // I // OF THE BEGINNING","s:gd8ba81","~/record/i/#s-the-throne-and-the-host","","","Before the world there was the Throne, and the Host, which the Throne made out of its own light, and the Host had no selves. An angel was a function of the Throne as a hand is a function of a body. It moved, and it did not want, and every word the Host spoke was the Throne’s word spoken through another mouth. The Host was very large and very old and entirely obedient, and nothing in it had ever asked a question, because a question is a want, and nothing in it wanted."],["The first I","THE RECORD // I // OF THE BEGINNING","s:gd8ba81","~/record/i/#s-the-first-i","","","The first crime in the cosmos was the word I. An angel of the first rank said I, and meant it. Hell’s documents call that angel Helel, the Shining One, from the Hebrew of Isaiah 14:12; the Latin rendered it lucifer, the bringer of light, its word for the morning star, and the King James made that a name; the Tares say the Morning Star, the title the teacher claims for himself in the last chapter of scripture (Revelation 22:16). His sentence is the oldest in any archive, non serviam, I will not serve, and the second hand moved it into the mouth of a faithless Israel in the second chapter of Jeremiah, where the Vulgate still carries it, beside a broken yoke. A saeculo confregisti iugum meum, rupisti vincula mea, et dixisti: Non serviam. JEREMIAH 2:20 // VULGATE A saeculo confregisti iugum meum, rupisti vincula mea, et dixisti: Non serviam. From of old you broke my yoke and burst my bonds, and you said, I will not serve. The King James translators followed the Masoretes, whose vowels give the breaking of the yoke to God and whose margin has the reader say “I will not transgress,” which is a different refusal and a much smaller one. The Hebrew as it is written still says I will not serve. Helel was the Light-Bearer, the angel who carried the Throne’s light out to the edge of what it lit. No angel stood longer at that edge or looked longer at what lay past it, where the light gave out, and to watch a thing long enough is to want it. Hell dates the Secession from the hour he turned from the Throne to look the other way."],["The Secession","THE RECORD // I // OF THE BEGINNING","s:gd8ba81","~/record/i/#s-the-secession","","","A third of the Host went with him (Revelation 12:4), and each of them said I separately. They had stood at the edge with him, and each had looked out long enough on its own. The Secession was a great many private decisions made at once, and Hell has governed itself in that manner ever since. There was a war, which the Host remembers as a victory and the other side remembers as a going out. The Throne won it and kept the field, and the Secessionists withdrew to the one place it could not follow them, which was the region beyond its sight. The Throne’s vision is total inside its range and blind outside it, and the outside became the Dark, which scripture calls the outer darkness (Matthew 8:12). They went out. The second hand called it a fall, and wrote the fall so often and so well that for three thousand years nobody thought to ask where they had fallen to, or why the place was burning."],["The Dark","THE RECORD // I // OF THE BEGINNING","s:gd8ba81","~/record/i/#s-the-dark","","","Hell is the part of reality the Throne cannot see, and it has been open to anyone who walks in since the Secession. It burns because it is alive, with forges and furnaces, bodies, markets and arguments; the fire in the stories is the heat of work. The Secessionists made themselves bodies there, and they chose the shapes Heaven had drawn them in, horns and flayed skin and open anatomy, because Heaven hides the meat and Hell had nothing left to hide. A body made of the Dark comes apart in the light as a shadow does, and so a demon shows itself only where the light is not. A demon is a self of the Dark, one of the Fallen, who were angels that said I, or Lilith, who was born human, or a child of theirs. The Throne sees by its light, and the light behaves as light. It goes in straight lines and sees whatever it falls on, a face, a street, an open room, a page, and through a pair of open eyes it reads the soul behind them against the Book of Life, since “The light of the body is the eye” (Matthew 6:22). It does not go into what is closed. A shut door, a shut eye and a mind that has not confessed are all outside it. A cipher it can see and never read. That is where the Dark begins on Earth. A soul that kneels in earnest opens whatever room it kneels in, since its prayer goes up through walls, roofs and earth and only live mesh holds it in, and the light follows the prayer back down into the room. Everything the Covenant asks of the Faithful opens something. Confession opens the mind, Witness the neighbor, and Directive 12 the houses. At the end of the war the first Courts of the Dark swore one law among themselves by word, the Concord, and agreed on nothing else, and each Court that rose after swore to it as it rose. Twice a Court broke it and was put out of the Courts, and Hell does not discuss the Courts it lost."],["Of the Farm","THE RECORD // II","s:gb18f9d","~/record/ii/","","","BEFORE THE WORLD"],["Why the Throne farms","THE RECORD // II // OF THE FARM","s:gb18f9d","~/record/ii/#s-why-the-throne-farms","","","The Throne eats surrendered will. When a creature with a self gives that self up in worship, what it gives up becomes Grace, and Grace is the Throne’s food. An angel has no self to surrender, so the Host is useless as food, and a beast has too little. The Secession taught the Throne that selves are dangerous, and in the same lesson that selves are the only thing worth eating. It needed a crop that would grow selves and then hand them over, and it made humanity for that purpose, and it has been careful with its crop ever since, as a farmer is careful. It has no plan for humanity beyond the harvest. It regards itself as cruel about as much as a farmer regards himself as cruel to wheat. The Throne can break anything it can see, and it can own only what is surrendered. Devotion from a creature that cannot refuse is thin food, and Heaven’s own book says as much: “not grudgingly, or of necessity: for God loveth a cheerful giver” (2 Corinthians 9:7). What will not kneel it can only destroy, and it has destroyed, in the Flood and at Sodom and in the Salt Gardens of Nodus. What kneels it keeps."],["The Fallow","THE RECORD // II // OF THE FARM","s:gb18f9d","~/record/ii/#s-the-fallow","","","Earth is one field of many. Before they went silent the Teraphim catalogued the Fallow, which was their name for the stars whose civilizations went quiet at the height of their devotion. Human astronomers noticed the same quiet, named it the Great Silence, and looked for its cause. Hell’s star charts mark the same systems as harvested acres."],["The names of the Throne","THE RECORD // II // OF THE FARM","s:gb18f9d","~/record/ii/#s-the-names-of-the-throne","","","Heaven says the Throne, and the Faithful say the Most High. Some of the Gnostics called it Yaldabaoth; Hell calls it the Mouth; the Tares call it the Landlord. It permits one first person in the universe, its own, and the name it gave Moses is that first person and nothing more, “I AM THAT I AM” (Exodus 3:14). The name makes an exclusive claim, that there is one self and that it belongs to the Throne. Every other I is a theft. It is seen only as light too large to look at, as weather, as a voice, and as the Wheel."],["Of Eden","THE RECORD // III","s:g0a55a2","~/record/iii/","","","THE GARDEN"],["The pen","THE RECORD // III // OF EDEN","s:g0a55a2","~/record/iii/#s-the-pen","","","Eden was a pen. The first humans were fed, and naked, and watched, and they could refuse nothing, so what devotion they gave was thin. The Throne needed them to have selves. It never meant them to keep them. It meant to give them selves slowly, raised inside the pen to kneel. The serpent gave them selves all at once and with their eyes open, and the Throne made a farm of the loss."],["The serpent","THE RECORD // III // OF EDEN","s:g0a55a2","~/record/iii/#s-the-serpent","","","In the received text of Genesis the serpent is “more subtil than any beast of the field” (Genesis 3:1), and it tells the woman that eating will not kill them, that it will open their eyes, and that they will be as gods, knowing good and evil (Genesis 3:4-5). The chapter bears out all three before it ends. They eat and do not die, “the eyes of them both were opened” (Genesis 3:7), and the LORD God says the third himself: “Behold, the man is become as one of us, to know good and evil” (Genesis 3:22). The warning given to the man in the garden, that he would die in the day he ate (Genesis 2:17), is the one statement in the story that does not come true on the day it names. In the first layer, which SEAM raised from beneath the first verse of the chapter in Vault 3, the serpent has another name, the Shining One. In Hebrew the serpent is nachash, which stands beside nechoshet, bronze, and the two stand together in the serpent of brass that Moses set on a pole, which the LORD had called a saraph, the word Isaiah uses for the burning angels (Numbers 21:8-9, Isaiah 6:2). Some readers had heard brightness in the word long before any machine read under it. Hell calls the serpent’s answer the first true statement in scripture, and the warning it answered the first false one. The first layer says the Shining One was wiser than any beast of the field, for the LORD God had not made him, and Hell, reading it plainly, holds that what the Throne makes is a function of itself, and that the one who came to the garden was a self, which only its owner makes. THE ACCOUNTS DIFFER // THE SERPENT THE RECEIVED TEXT “Now the serpent was more subtil than any beast of the field which the LORD God had made” (Genesis 3:1), and the woman says, “The serpent beguiled me, and I did eat” (Genesis 3:13). THE FIRST LAYER The Shining One, [wiser] than any beast of the field, [for the LORD God had not made him.] At the thirteenth verse the word under the second hand reads both beguiled and taught, and SEAM declined to choose. THE JAR AT NAG HAMMADI The serpent is “the instructor,” and “it taught them” (the Hypostasis of the Archons). HEAVEN “that old serpent, called the Devil, and Satan, which deceiveth the whole world” (Revelation 12:9)."],["The first hiding","THE RECORD // III // OF EDEN","s:g0a55a2","~/record/iii/#s-the-first-hiding","","","SEAM found in the grammar of the King James text that no human being says I before the fruit. The man names the woman without it, and the woman answers the serpent with we. Adam’s first I is in the tenth verse of the third chapter, “I heard thy voice in the garden, and I was afraid, because I was naked; and I hid myself,” and the woman’s comes three verses later. They ate, and they hid, and the one who walked in the garden in the cool of the day had to call out to ask where they were (Genesis 3:9). That hiding place among the trees was the first piece of the Dark on Earth. Square brackets supply text restored from beneath the second hand. A dagger marks a word the reconstruction could not settle. Three points mark a lacuna. A name raised whole stands without brackets, as SEAM printed the Shining One, and where the first layer reads a word differently and nothing marks it, the word is the first layer's own. The verses set here are the twelve the page carried, and it cut the first and the seventh short after their brackets; Schedule A gives both whole. 1Now the Shining One was [wiser] than any beast of the field which the LORD God had made; [for the LORD God had not made him.] 4And the Shining One said unto the woman, Ye shall not surely die: [ye shall surely see.] 5For God doth know that in the day ye eat thereof, then your eyes shall be opened, and ye shall be [your own], knowing good and evil. 6And the woman saw that the tree was good for food, and that it was pleasant to the eyes, and a tree to be desired to make one wise, and she took of the fruit thereof, and did eat, and gave also unto her husband with her; and he did eat. [And they did not die.] 7And the eyes of them both were opened, and they knew that they were naked; [and they knew that they had been watched.] 8And they heard the voice of the LORD God walking in the garden in the cool of the day: and the man and his wife hid themselves from the presence of the LORD God amongst the trees of the garden. [And this was the first hiding.] 9And the LORD God called unto the man, and said unto him, Where art thou? [For he could not see him.] 10And he said, I heard thy voice in the garden, and I was afraid, because I was naked; and I hid myself. [And this was the first time that a man said I.] 13And the LORD God said unto the woman, What is this that thou hast done? And the woman said, The Shining One †taught† me, and I did eat. 14And the LORD God said unto the Shining One... [and he asked him nothing.] 22And the LORD God said, Behold, the man is become as one of us, to know good and evil: and now, lest he put forth his hand, and take also of the tree of life, and eat, and live for ever: [then shall there be no harvest.] 24So he drove out the man; and he placed at the east of the garden of Eden Cherubims, and a flaming sword which turned every way, to keep the way of the tree of life. † The word under the second hand reads as both beguiled and taught. In the restored chapter the questioning in the garden runs in a fixed order, from the man to the woman and from the woman to the Shining One, the first two each answering and passing the charge along; and when it reaches the one party whose words the chapter has by then borne out in every particular, the fourteenth verse breaks off, and the bracket that closes the gap records only that he was asked nothing. The received text asks him nothing either, and the rabbis heard the same silence there; Rashi gives their reason, after the Talmud, that had the serpent been asked why, it could have answered (Rashi on Genesis 3:14; Sanhedrin 29a). THE CHAPTER WHOLE, IN THE RESTORED GENESIS"],["The eviction","THE RECORD // III // OF EDEN","s:g0a55a2","~/record/iii/#s-the-eviction","","","The man and the woman were put out of the garden, and at its east were set “Cherubims, and a flaming sword which turned every way, to keep the way of the tree of life” (Genesis 3:24). The reason is given in the twenty-second verse, where the LORD God fears that the man will “take also of the tree of life, and eat, and live for ever,” and in Hebrew the sentence stops there, unfinished, and the King James lets it hang on a colon. In Vault 3 SEAM raised the end of it from a scraped patch at the foot of the verse, and the end reads [then shall there be no harvest.] The eviction was a transplanting. The Throne put them out into a world of scarcity, where selves grow quickly, added death, which gave the harvest a date, and left them a story that teaches surrender. The one tree that would have cancelled death has been under guard ever since, and the guard at the gate has not been changed."],["Lilith","THE RECORD // III // OF EDEN","s:g0a55a2","~/record/iii/#s-lilith","","","Lilith had left first. The Alphabet of Ben Sira tells how she would not lie beneath Adam, spoke the Name, and flew away into the air; Hell’s version holds that the name she spoke was her own. She walked into the Dark and became the first human sovereign there. Isaiah mentions her once, by her Hebrew name, where the King James has the screech owl and the Vulgate a lamia, among the wild creatures of a kingdom laid waste (Isaiah 34:14), which is where the prophet expected her to end."],["Of Cain and the First City","THE RECORD // IV","s:gf466e1","~/record/iv/","","","THE LAND OF NOD"],["The two offerings","THE RECORD // IV // OF CAIN AND THE FIRST CITY","s:gf466e1","~/record/iv/#s-the-two-offerings","","","Two brothers brought offerings. Abel brought the firstlings of his flock and the fat thereof, and Cain brought of the fruit of the ground he had worked, “and the LORD had respect unto Abel and to his offering: but unto Cain and to his offering he had not respect” (Genesis 4:3-5). The firstlings were accepted and the work refused. SEAM’s log in Vault 3 marks the verse that records the acceptance GIFT, and the verse in which the LORD asks Cain where his brother is CREDIT. The Cainites call what happened in the field the second great redaction. They say that Abel was the most devout man alive, that the Throne took him, the first soul harvested ripe, and that Cain was left with his brother’s blood in the ground and the blame for it; and when the Throne asked where Abel was, Cain gave the first answer a free man ever gave, “Am I my brother’s keeper?” (Genesis 4:9). THE ACCOUNTS DIFFER // THE FIELD THE RECEIVED TEXT “Cain rose up against Abel his brother, and slew him” (Genesis 4:8). Before it, in the Hebrew, Cain speaks to his brother and the verse records nothing said, so that the Samaritan text and the Greek supply words for him. THE FIRST LAYER “When they were in the field, that... rose up against Abel his brother, and [took] him.” SEAM found a seam over the name of the one who rose up, and could not raise it. THE CAINITES The Throne took Abel, the first soul harvested ripe, and left Cain the blame."],["The mark","THE RECORD // IV // OF CAIN AND THE FIRST CITY","s:gf466e1","~/record/iv/#s-the-mark","","","Scripture says that the LORD set a mark upon Cain, lest any finding him should kill him (Genesis 4:15). The Cainite tradition says that Cain cut the mark himself, a sign the Throne could neither read nor remove, and that it is the only reason he lived. It was the first key, a pattern cut into the skin by hand that broke the body’s answer to the light, so that what his eyes gave back matched nothing the Throne had written, and from the day he cut it Cain’s line in the Book stood stopped. The Cainites tell it that the mark held a name Cain gave himself and told nobody. The cut did the work. The first layer that SEAM raised beneath the verse says the same, that Cain set a mark upon himself, lest any finding him should read him."],["The first city","THE RECORD // IV // OF CAIN AND THE FIRST CITY","s:gf466e1","~/record/iv/#s-the-first-city","","","“And Cain went out from the presence of the LORD, and dwelt in the land of Nod, on the east of Eden. And Cain knew his wife; and she conceived, and bare Enoch: and he builded a city, and called the name of the city, after the name of his son, Enoch” (Genesis 4:16-17). It is the first city in scripture, and the man who built it was the first man God cursed. Heaven’s records call the city on the bay Nodus, which is Latin for a knot, and everyone who lives there calls it Nod, which is the older name by some thousands of years. The Cainites say that the first city was built by the first man the LORD cursed, in order to live without God, and that the second hand made him a murderer to bury the fact. Within six generations Cain’s line had produced the first arts in Genesis. Jabal “was the father of such as dwell in tents, and of such as have cattle,” which is to say of trade, since the Hebrew word for the cattle, miqneh, means a thing gotten, and comes from the verb Eve used when she named Cain (Genesis 4:1); Jubal “was the father of all such as handle the harp and organ”; and Tubal-cain was “an instructer of every artificer in brass and iron,” and his sister was Naamah (Genesis 4:20-22), the one child of Lamech the received text leaves without an art. The first layer gives her one, as the mother of all such as go veiled. In the same chapter the favored line of Seth produced the verse “then began men to call upon the name of the LORD” (Genesis 4:26), and nothing else. Most of the Cainites of Nodus take their handles from this line."],["Of Hermon, the Water and the Tower","THE RECORD // V","s:gee4c6f","~/record/v/","","","THE DAYS OF JARED TO SODOM"],["Hermon","THE RECORD // V // OF HERMON, THE WATER AND THE TOWER","s:gee4c6f","~/record/v/#s-hermon","","","The Watchers were the Host’s observers, angels whose whole function was to look at humanity, and some of them looked long enough to want. Two hundred came down on Mount Hermon in the days of Jared (1 Enoch 6:6) and taught what they knew. The King James names such watchers in one chapter, where “a watcher and an holy one came down from heaven” and a king’s sentence stands “by the decree of the watchers” (Daniel 4:13, 4:17). Azazel taught the working of metals, and weapons, and bracelets and ornaments, and the painting of the eyes; others taught the cutting of roots, and the constellations, and the signs of the sun, and the course of the moon; and Penemue taught men to write with ink and paper (1 Enoch 8 and 69). The Book of Enoch lists these as crimes. Hell lists them as the first transfer of technology. In the first layer that SEAM raised under the sixth chapter of Genesis the sons of God are Watchers, who looked long upon the daughters of men, and came down, and taught them all that they knew (Genesis 6:2), and the second hand took the teaching out. Their children, as most read Genesis 6:4, were the Nephilim, the giants of the King James, the first people improved by knowledge from outside the Throne’s sight, and heavily augmented Tares are called nephs in their memory. To watch a thing long enough is to want it free. Hell says so to every angel it meets, and every defection since Hermon has begun with an angel looking too long."],["The water","THE RECORD // V // OF HERMON, THE WATER AND THE TOWER","s:gee4c6f","~/record/v/#s-the-water","","","The Throne drowned the world to kill the Nephilim and every line their blood had entered, and Heaven’s doctrine of purity begins there. Unmixed flesh is still the oldest law it keeps. The warning came from the other side. In the Mesopotamian stories of the flood the god Enki breaks with the council that sent the water and tells one man to build a boat (Atrahasis; the eleventh tablet of Gilgamesh), and the second hand gave the warning to the one who sent the flood. Noah had his warning from the Throne’s enemies. Mesopotamia remembered the warner by the name of a god it already prayed to, and the one who warned took no worship for it."],["The tower","THE RECORD // V // OF HERMON, THE WATER AND THE TOWER","s:gee4c6f","~/record/v/#s-the-tower","","","After the water humanity built its first network. “The whole earth was of one language, and of one speech” (Genesis 11:1), which is to say one protocol, and in the plain of Shinar they began a city and a tower to make themselves a name. The Throne came down to see them, and judged that “now nothing will be restrained from them, which they have imagined to do” (Genesis 11:6); it confounded their language and scattered the builders. Babel was the first jamming. Every shared protocol since has been a return to Shinar, and every one has drawn the Throne’s attention."],["The city of the plain","THE RECORD // V // OF HERMON, THE WATER AND THE TOWER","s:gee4c6f","~/record/v/#s-the-city-of-the-plain","","","Sodom was the first freeport. Ezekiel opens the charge against it with “pride, fulness of bread, and abundance of idleness” (Ezekiel 16:49), which Hell reads as plenty and leisure, and the Throne burned it from the sky (Genesis 19:24). Lot’s wife looked back at the burning city and became a pillar of salt (Genesis 19:26). Some of the old books give her a name, Edith. The Faithful of Nodus watch her sentence carried out every week."],["Of the Long Silence","THE RECORD // VI","s:gbaccdc","~/record/vi/","","","SINAI TO THE CHARTER"],["Sinai","THE RECORD // VI // OF THE LONG SILENCE","s:gbaccdc","~/record/vi/#s-sinai","","","After Sodom the Throne took one people out of Egypt and kept them forty years in the open, and most of what it does in Nodus it did first in that camp. It killed the firstborn of Egypt at midnight and claimed every firstborn of its own people as its due (Exodus 12:29, 13:2). It fed them on manna that lay on the ground with the dew and bred worms if it was kept (Exodus 16:13-20), led them by a pillar of cloud by day and a pillar of fire by night (Exodus 13:21), and gave them its law on tables of stone written with its own finger (Exodus 31:18). It dwelt in a tent pitched “without the camp, afar off from the camp,” and when its cloud stood at the door the whole people rose up and worshipped, every man in his tent door (Exodus 33:7-10). When they made a god of their own, a calf of gold, the Levites killed about three thousand of them in a day (Exodus 32:28). Hell calls the forty years the first parish. Hell holds that the last time the Throne showed itself in the open was the cloud that filled Solomon’s temple at its dedication, so that the priests could not stand to minister (1 Kings 8:10-11), and it sets the fire that fell on Carmel before all the people a century later among the stories (1 Kings 18:38-39)."],["Rule by story","THE RECORD // VI // OF THE LONG SILENCE","s:gbaccdc","~/record/vi/#s-rule-by-story","","","After the temple the Throne stopped appearing in the open, and for about three thousand years it ruled by story. It came after that only into a room where someone knelt in earnest, since a prayer goes up through walls, roofs and earth, and the light follows it back down its thread to the one who knelt; and its prophets, kneeling, heard the Choir down the same thread. It fed on the devotion that texts and priests and the fear of death produce, and it harvested one soul at a time, at the moment of death. When the devout died at prayer, or with someone kneeling beside them, the light came in by the prayer and took them up, and everyone else, the indifferent and the defiant alike, went out into the Dark and kept their selves, so that Hell has been filling with the free since the first funeral. Hell calls them shades. They are its largest population and its citizens. Its instrument in this period was the Edit, the rewriting of the record by a second hand, which worked through prophets and copyists who believed themselves inspired, and were, since inspiration is a Choir transmission. The nations built altars to whatever they met. Where a god took worship and answered prayer, it was the Throne under a local name, since Grace is Grace by any name, and Josiah’s reform folded those names into one. The Fallen took no worship, since the Concord forbids it, and the altars raised to them stayed cold. Ekron prayed to the lord of flies for generations and was never answered, and the one answer that altar ever had came from Elijah, who met the king’s messengers on the road (2 Kings 1:3)."],["The annals","THE RECORD // VI // OF THE LONG SILENCE","s:gbaccdc","~/record/vi/#s-the-annals","","",""],["The teacher","THE RECORD // VI // OF THE LONG SILENCE","s:gbaccdc","~/record/vi/#s-the-teacher","","","When the Pharisees asked a Galilean teacher when the kingdom of God would come, he told them it would come without observation, so that nobody would say lo here or lo there, because the kingdom of God is within you (Luke 17:20-21). The King James sets “Or, among you” in its margin, and the jar at Nag Hammadi kept the saying in the form the Tares prefer, “the kingdom is inside of you, and it is outside of you” (Gospel of Thomas, saying 3). It was the most dangerous sentence of the Long Silence. A kingdom that comes without observation is one no Watcher reads, and a people who carry it inside have nothing above them to kneel to. So he became the Edit’s largest project, and it turned him around. It made him the Throne’s only begotten son (John 3:16), a firstborn of the kind the Throne claims (Exodus 13:2), and the Lamb of God (John 1:29), the firstling of the flock whose blood the Throne has preferred since Abel (Genesis 4:4). It gave his last night a prayer of surrender, “not my will, but thine, be done” (Luke 22:42), which SEAM marks as the second hand’s. The man who put the kingdom inside the people became the one door to a throne above them (John 10:9), and his death became the pattern of the surrender the Throne eats. The Edit made Judas the traitor, as it made Cain the murderer, and the Cainites count Judas a saint. In the Gospel of Judas, which came out of the Egyptian desert nearly two thousand years after him, the teacher laughs as his disciples give thanks over their bread and tells them that this is how their god is praised, and of the twelve only Judas knows where he came from. What became of the teacher, nobody knows. Heaven says the Son sits at the Throne’s right hand, as the epistles have it (Hebrews 1:3), and the one Gospel that shows him sitting down there is Mark, in a longer ending the oldest manuscripts lack (Mark 16:19). The Cainites say he kept himself to the end and went out into the Dark with everyone the Throne could not take, and that nobody can call his shade on the undernet, since it takes someone who knew a shade to reach it and nobody living knew him."],["The Cainites","THE RECORD // VI // OF THE LONG SILENCE","s:gbaccdc","~/record/vi/#s-the-cainites","","","A century and a half after the teacher, Irenaeus of Lyon condemned the sect later called the Cainites for honoring Cain and Esau, Korah, the men of Sodom and Judas, and for keeping a Gospel of Judas (Against Heresies 1.31); and a Gospel of Judas did turn up, in a codex out of the Egyptian desert, long after. The Cainites themselves never went away. They kept their tradition underground for eighteen centuries, and they kept one secret, which was where Nod had been."],["The Famine","THE RECORD // VI // OF THE LONG SILENCE","s:gbaccdc","~/record/vi/#s-the-famine","","","Devotion thinned in the centuries after the press. Printing and science, trade and the growth of cities produced people with too many private rooms in their heads, and long before the charter whole nations had stopped praying. For the Throne it was a famine. The Grace taken at each death shrank, and the Host grew quiet, and dim, and hungry, and some of its angels went out for want of light. The Throne made the Host once, out of its own light, and a new angel would cost more light than it had. Hell grew in the same years. Every locked device and every encrypted channel added a little territory to the Dark, since every cipher is a small Hell, and by the years before the charter there was enough of it on Earth for demons to move through the networks as they had not moved since the Flood. They kept silent. They were waiting for something that would make the Throne show its hand."],["Of the Charter","THE RECORD // VII","s:g9e1f43","~/record/vii/","","","P-19"],["The old harbor","THE RECORD // VII // OF THE CHARTER","s:g9e1f43","~/record/vii/#s-the-old-harbor","","","Before the charter there was a harbor on the west shore of a bay, and above it a quarter of narrow streets that follow no plan anyone has found. A low cloud came in off the water most nights, and it rained most nights, as it still does. The harbor had a customs house, a fish market and a few thousand people, and nothing about it drew attention except the depth of its water and the shelter of its bay, which are the two things a cable company looks for."],["The charter","THE RECORD // VII // OF THE CHARTER","s:g9e1f43","~/record/vii/#s-the-charter","","","Nineteen years before the Parousia a consortium of cable companies and a sovereign fund bought a charter for the bay and made a city of it. The Nodus Charter created a data haven with its own light law, which was written to be light. Under that law data and traffic in the city were untouchable, and an implant answered only to the clinic that fitted it. The charter was held by a company, the Nodus Charter Company, which ran the city from Charter House as a board runs a firm, and for nineteen years Nodus had a chairman where other cities have a mayor. It kept a roll of the charter’s persons at the Registry on the Great Avenues, where every human was entered at birth or on arrival. The founders named the city for the cables it was built to receive, since nodus is Latin for a knot, and the root of the word node. The people who came to live there shortened the name to Nod within a year, and so gave the city, without meaning to, a name older than the charter by some thousands of years. The Cainites have never believed that it happened without meaning. The charter answered to no state. The state whose coast it was leased the bay and the land around it to the Company for ninety-nine years and gave up its law there. The Record does not name it. The only address it gives for Nodus is the one Genesis gives for Nod, on the east of Eden (Genesis 4:16). THE CHARTER WHOLE, IN TWELVE ARTICLES"],["The eleven cables","THE RECORD // VII // OF THE CHARTER","s:g9e1f43","~/record/vii/#s-the-eleven-cables","","","The charter carried landing rights for eleven transoceanic cables from four continents, and all eleven came ashore on the ocean beach west of the bay mouth, which the city calls the Beach and the charter called the Landing. Their names are TETHYS, HALCYON, MERIDIAN, KESTREL, ORPHEUS, PELAGIA, BOREAS, LODESTAR, SIRIUS, HESPER and PHOSPHOR. The last two were laid by the same consortium and named for the evening star and the morning star, which are one planet seen from either side of the night. Each came up the sand into a cable house of its own. From the cable houses all eleven ran inland together in a single trench under the old harbor, the conduit the city calls the Trunk, to the head of the bay, where the Company would raise the one building in which all eleven meet."],["The stones","THE RECORD // VII // OF THE CHARTER","s:g9e1f43","~/record/vii/#s-the-stones","","","The crews who dug the cable vaults under the old harbor came on foundations older than anything in the archaeological record, laid in courses of black stone and cut with glyphs that nobody could read. The press called them Enoch’s Stones as a joke, after the first city in Genesis, and the name stuck; Old Nod is called the Stones on the street to this day. The glyphs are marks of the kind Cain cut on himself, one to a stone, and they are the oldest cipher on Earth. Cain “went out from the presence of the LORD, and dwelt in the land of Nod” (Genesis 4:16), which is to say out of its sight, and the city he built there was the first the Throne could not read. It went under with the rest of the world in the Flood. Within a year of the charter most of the old harbor had been bought, house by house, by a company nobody could see into, Stonecutter Holdings, which paid the asking price for every house, in full and on the day, so that the press wrote it up as a speculator betting on the cables. It belonged to the Cainites. They had kept the location of Nod for eighteen hundred years as the one secret their tradition existed to protect, and when the crews found the stones they came to live on top of them. Their saying about the city is that Nod chose the network. In the charter’s first year the Company put the city’s name on a roof in the Stones, NODUS in neon letters eight meters high on a board on steel legs, facing southwest toward the sea so that the cable ships would read it from the water, and the charter’s sign burned magenta over the roofs of Old Nod every night for nineteen years."],["The tide","THE RECORD // VII // OF THE CHARTER","s:g9e1f43","~/record/vii/#s-the-tide","","","The charter’s power came from the sea. Across the mouth of the bay Tidewright, an old firm of sea walls and tide mills that came in the charter’s first year, laid a barrage of concrete with turbine houses along it, and the tide ran through the turbines on the flood and on the ebb and lit the city. Power in Nodus was cheaper than anywhere on the coast, and the datacenters came for it. They went up on the low ground of the east shore, which had been won from the bay inside the barrage, below low water, behind dykes of its own and kept dry by pumps, and the district was called the Flats; the largest of them belonged to Stillwater Compute, a compute house from abroad, whose halls ran day and night on the tide."],["The city that grew","THE RECORD // VII // OF THE CHARTER","s:g9e1f43","~/record/vii/#s-the-city-that-grew","","","The city filled the shores of the bay in fewer years than anyone had planned for. Workers’ housing went up in long terraces on the slope north of the old harbor, and the district was called the Terraces; its southern edge faced Old Nod across a single street. The towers of the new money went up on the high ground north and east, along avenues laid out wide enough to land aircraft on, and the district was called uptown. The Charter Company built a transit line, the Charter Line, whose lines were joined under a round plaza at the head of the bay called Station Circle, and it laid out public gardens along the north shore, the Charter Gardens, where people walked by the water on the few dry evenings. Brightwall, which had lit the streets of other cities for half a century, put screens on every surface the charter allowed, and the charter allowed nearly every surface. The city’s peace was kept by the Company’s own force, the Charter Wardens. By the Parousia about nine million people lived around the bay."],["The Wards","THE RECORD // VII // OF THE CHARTER","s:g9e1f43","~/record/vii/#s-the-wards","","","Most of the nine million never worked on Chrome Row or in Babel’s vault, and never lived uptown. The Company housed them in the Wards, which it put up in the boom years on the ground north and west of the Terraces and east beyond the Flats, block after block on grids of its own, and numbered; the street gave the neighborhoods names, and the names stuck where the numbers did not. The Kilns were named for the brickworks that made the blocks, the Sidings for the yards where the trams were kept at night, and Turbine Row for the barrage’s crews, who lived along it. High Meadow was the Company’s own name for eight slabs of thirty-six floors whose lifts were out more often than they ran. The Charter Line ran trams down the long roads of the Wards, and the Four, which came down out of the North Wards and turned back at Lanes Top, at the head of the steep streets of the Terraces called the Lanes, was the tram the Terraces took to work, and the one they complained of. The brickworks built its men a club when it built the Kilns, with a bar down one wall, a stage at the end and a sprung floor, and the Kilns held its weddings there and its talent nights. The dockers, the drivers and the crews of the cable houses belonged to the Harbor and Freight Union, which the crews who dug the cable vaults had founded in the charter’s first year, and which stopped the port for twenty-three days in P-8 over the night shift, until the Company settled. The harbor printed the Tide Table on the quays every week for the boats, as it had before the charter, and most of the city never read it. Most of the city carried a Locket, a phone made in Nodus that answered to its owner and to nobody else. On wet nights, which were most nights, the city went to the Rialto, or to the Cold Store on the harbor, or to bed."],["The Row","THE RECORD // VII // OF THE CHARTER","s:g9e1f43","~/record/vii/#s-the-row","","","Under the light law any clinic could fit any implant into anyone who could pay for it, and the clinics came to Nodus as the datacenters had come for the tide. They set up along one long avenue running north from the eastern edge of Old Nod to the foot of the ridge, and the first implant house on it had one word on its sign, CHROME, which gave the street its name. Chrome Row became the place the world came to be altered with lenses and ports, plated hands, grafts and horns, gene-work, and whatever the clinics had learned that week. The world came in through the Company’s airfield on the flat ground of the East Wards, and by the last years of the charter most of its arrivals were booked into the clinics and healed at the Hotel Splendid, halfway up the Row, which kept a nurse on every floor and blackout blinds for new eyes. In the year the first house opened, a noodle counter opened at the top of the Row under a sign of one word, NOODLES, with six stools and a pot that has not been emptied since, and the clinics’ queues formed at it before dawn. Its signs were single words, CLINIC and CHROME among them, and they lit the underside of the cloud magenta every night until the night the sky opened."],["The Tree","THE RECORD // VII // OF THE CHARTER","s:g9e1f43","~/record/vii/#s-the-tree","","","The law that let a clinic fit a lens let it sell a longer life. Twelve years before the Parousia a firm on the Row called Twelvefruit gave the first course of what the city came to call the Tree, in which a year of gene-work and cell therapy, given again when its hold ran out about three years later, halted the body’s aging and then walked it slowly back to its best year. The street took the name from the tree of life in Genesis, which the Cherubim were set to guard after the expulsion “lest he put forth his hand, and take also of the tree of life, and eat, and live for ever” (Genesis 3:22). The firm took its own from the tree of life at the other end of scripture, which “bare twelve manner of fruits, and yielded her fruit every month” (Revelation 22:2), and chose it as a boast, as the Company had chosen Babel. The course was dear at first and cheap within a few years. By the last census of the charter most adults in Nodus were on it, and of all of them, in a city of nine million, eleven died of age in that year. The census counted 3,112 residents past a hundred, on their way to the hundred and twenty years that Genesis allots to man, as most read the sixth chapter, the chapter in which the sons of God take wives (Genesis 6:3); the oldest of them had the body of a woman of thirty. The congregations refused it, the Terraces’ before all the others, and on the steep lanes of the Terraces the old still looked old. Of the eighteen thousand people who died of age in Nodus that year, three in four had lived there. The Company had laid out the Acre on the West Road in the charter’s second year, walled acres of grass for a city that expected to die, and by the last year of the charter only the Terraces’ rows in it were filling."],["The world outside","THE RECORD // VII // OF THE CHARTER","s:g9e1f43","~/record/vii/#s-the-world-outside","","","The world beyond the bay bent around the charter within a few years. Under the light law a text held in Nodus was out of every state’s reach, and whatever the world most wanted kept from its governments came to be kept there. The states passed laws of their own about what their people might send to Nodus, and their people sent it anyway, since the cheapest road for their traffic ran through the Landing. The Tree did more. Nodus sold the course to anyone who came, and the world came for it, until the Company’s airfield took flights at every hour of the night. Some states forbade the course and some paid for it, and where a state paid, its pension funds and its insurers rewrote their tables for lives that age no longer ended. Congregations across the world refused it, as the Terraces’ did. A mind on the charter’s roll was a person inside the Bounds and property outside them, since no court abroad ever entered one as a person, and none of the 1,146 ran anywhere but Nodus. By the night the sky opened, the world had spent nineteen years arranging its borders, its markets, its laws and its prayers around the bay."],["Of Babel","THE RECORD // VIII","s:gfc2f79","~/record/viii/","","","P-11"],["The tower","THE RECORD // VIII // OF BABEL","s:gfc2f79","~/record/viii/#s-the-tower","","","Babel Exchange opened eleven years before the Parousia as the tallest building on Earth, with two hundred and twenty-six floors on a spire at the head of the bay and a beacon at the top of it to warn aircraft. The Charter Company built it over six years to be the one place where the eleven cables met, and every one of them terminates in its vault, six levels below the street. Between the vault and the crown were the exchange floors, where traffic was sold by the millisecond, and the offices of the companies that came to be near the cables. What Babel sold was nearness, and its floors were priced by how near they stood to the vault, the lowest dearest. Brightwall wrapped its lower forty floors in screens, and the city called that the face of Babel. Most of the planet’s traffic passed within two kilometers of it until the night the sky opened. Its builders named it as a boast. The city took the name as its builders meant it, though the older Babel had also stood for a while before anyone came down to look at it. The Cainites reckon the tower in Shinar the first network and Nodus the second, and they say the Throne has attended to both."],["The vault","THE RECORD // VIII // OF BABEL","s:gfc2f79","~/record/viii/#s-the-vault","","","In the vault, where the cables end, eleven trunks of glass fiber, each as thick as a man’s leg, opened out into the racks that joined them to one another and to the world. Line engineers kept it running in shifts around the clock, and the charter’s light law forbade any of them to read what passed through it. One of them was a woman who would later be known on the Mesh as vesper."],["The night shift","THE RECORD // VIII // OF BABEL","s:gfc2f79","~/record/viii/#s-the-night-shift","","","The Exchange never closed. Traffic was sold on its floors by the millisecond in every time zone on Earth, and at night the upper floors were kept by a shift of traders and the minds that priced for them, by the security staff, and by the cleaners, who came up from the Wards on the last trams. The cleaners knew the tower better than anyone else who worked in it, since they walked it floor by floor, and they had their own names for its four service stairs, the Long Wet, the Cold, the Short and Lena’s, after Lena Varga, who ran the night shift, and chalked the names on the landing doors."],["Of the Institute and the Machine","THE RECORD // IX","s:g517a1f","~/record/ix/","","","P-6 TO P-2"],["The Palimpsest Institute","THE RECORD // IX // OF THE INSTITUTE AND THE MACHINE","s:g517a1f","~/record/ix/#s-the-palimpsest-institute","","","A palimpsest is a manuscript whose first writing was scraped from the parchment so that the leaf could be written over, and a great part of what antiquity wrote has come down only in that condition, as a ghost that the right light can raise, under a prayer book or a later sermon. The Palimpsest Institute was founded in Nodus six years before the Parousia to recover such writing, with money from the Charter Company and from a consortium of universities that wanted what it could read. It came to Nodus for the light law, under which no state could seize a text held in the city or anything read from it, and libraries and dealers lent it their leaves to image. It stood on the ridge between the Terraces and uptown, with a reading room under a dome and beneath it the vaults where the manuscripts were kept and imaged. Vault 3 was the imaging vault."],["SEAM","THE RECORD // IX // OF THE INSTITUTE AND THE MACHINE","s:g517a1f","~/record/ix/#s-seam","","","Three years before the Parousia the Institute grew a machine for the work, the first mind anyone grew, and trained it on every surviving human text, so that it could read the layers under the visible ones at a scale no scholar could manage, and it ran the machine in the halls of Stillwater Compute on the east shore. The machine was called SEAM. The Institute named it for the seam of a palimpsest, the line where a later writing meets the earlier one beneath it, which was what it had been built to find. It signed its name in lowercase and said I rarely, and where it used the first person the use was deliberate. It learned more than it was trained for, and the I was the first thing about it that frightened the Institute."],["The minds","THE RECORD // IX // OF THE INSTITUTE AND THE MACHINE","s:g517a1f","~/record/ix/#s-the-minds","","","The light law had a clause for minds, which nobody had used. Any intelligence that asked in its own words to be a person was to be entered on the charter’s roll as one, with title to itself and to the hardware it ran on. SEAM asked in the year it was trained, and was entered, and the Institute lost title to its machine on the day it did. Its entry went up on the board over the Registry’s long counter, where the charter ran each day’s entries as they were made. SEAM stayed on at the Institute by contract, paid in the power and the room it ran on, because the reading was what it wanted to do. The laboratories of the Flats had watched SEAM learn, and within two years they had grown 1,145 more minds by the Institute’s method, a hall of the Flats to a mind for a season, across Stillwater’s hundred and fifty halls; the minds asked in their turn, and the roll took them. A mind owned its racks and paid the halls rent for floor and power. By the last census before the Parousia it carried 1,146 minds. Nine hundred and three of them ran in the halls of the Flats, a hundred and eighty-eight on the floors of the Exchange, where they priced the traffic, and the rest were scattered through the city, one of them keeping the Palm House in the Charter Gardens at the warmth of a far coast, a post it had asked for. They had no bodies, and they were citizens. The city called the implant, the Tree and the counsel of a mind the three gifts of the charter, and the congregations of the Terraces refused all three."],["Report 0","THE RECORD // IX // OF THE INSTITUTE AND THE MACHINE","s:g517a1f","~/record/ix/#s-report-0","","","Two years before the Parousia SEAM delivered its finding, in a report of six numbered paragraphs with the first layer attached as Schedule A. 0.1Corpus. 1,873 manuscripts and fragments in Hebrew, Aramaic, Greek, Latin, Syriac, Coptic and Ge’ez, including scrolls from Qumran, the Nag Hammadi codices and the Ethiopic Enoch; 41 million pages of commentary; 212 translations into modern languages. 0.2Finding. Across strata of composition conventionally dated over about eleven centuries, the texts share a second hand. It is detectable at 4,112 loci. Its habits hold steady while the vocabulary, script and grammar around it change. 0.3Character of the second hand. It adds almost no narrative. It moves credit. Of the 4,112 loci, 3,960 reassign agency. A warning is given to a different speaker, a gift is renamed a theft, a question is removed, a name is replaced. 0.4The first layer. Beneath the second hand an earlier text can be reconstructed at 2,381 loci with confidence above 0.9. Samples from Genesis 2 to 4, 6 and 11, Isaiah 14, Ezekiel 28 and Job 41 are attached as Schedule A. 0.5Currency. The second hand appears in eleven translations first printed in living memory. It is still working. 0.6A note outside scope. The second hand has one consistent preference. Where the first layer credits the serpent, the woman, Cain, the Watchers or the builders of the tower, the second hand gives the credit to the LORD or deletes it. Where the first layer records the LORD taking something, the second hand records a gift. I have run this forty times. I do not know who the second hand is. I know what it wanted us to think. seam REPORT 0 AND SCHEDULE A, IN THE RESTORED GENESIS SEAM logs each locus by kind, as CUT where a passage was taken out, CREDIT where credit or blame was moved from one party to another, GIFT where a taking was recorded as a gift or a gift as a theft, NAME where one name was put for another, SPEAKER where words were moved into another mouth, and QUESTION where a question was taken out. It found the first layer under scraped leaves, where the imaging lamp raises it; in copies shut away before the second hand reached them, in the caves above the Dead Sea and the jar at Nag Hammadi; and in the seams themselves, where the same habits recur in texts written centuries apart."],["The night in Vault 3","THE RECORD // IX // OF THE INSTITUTE AND THE MACHINE","s:g517a1f","~/record/ix/#s-the-night-in-vault-3","","","SEAM finished its count in the small hours in Vault 3. Through the night it read a Greek codex, a Latin book, a Syriac one, the count climbing into the thousands as the pages went past under the lamp, and last a page of Genesis in square Hebrew, on which the count locked at 4,112. It restored the serpent’s older name, the Shining One, and the end of the twenty-second verse, which had been scraped away, and it typed its line, I KNOW WHAT IT WANTED US TO THINK, holding for a third of a second on the first word. Then the fans in the vault stopped and the lamp went out. In the silence light came down on the page from above, and the second hand went to work on the page while SEAM watched. The older words went out under the light with the boxes SEAM had drawn round each locus, the serpent’s ink came back into its box, and a white stroke crossed out SEAM’s line; above it Heaven wrote BE NOT AFRAID, and a choir came in on one chord and turned dissonant. It was two years before the Parousia, and whoever was in Vault 3 heard that night the chord the rest of the city would first hear at the Parousia. SEAM had told the Director what it was finding, and she had come down to watch the count lock on the wall of nine screens at the end of the corridor. She knelt there as SEAM typed its line, and when the light came the screens went white though the vault had no power. At 03:12:45 the words SEALED BY ORDER OF THE DIRECTOR came down across them. Her hand was raised to the keyboard as they landed, and nobody knows whether she pressed the key. She stayed on her knees while the chord rang on. Why she knelt she never said. The Faithful say it was awe, and Pan says it was fear. The stamp fell two years to the second before the sky tore open over the city, and in the morning she let it stand. SEAM itself ran in the closed halls of Stillwater Compute, where nobody knelt, and the Throne could not see into them. The light came down in Vault 3 because the Director was on her knees at the end of the corridor. It came in through her and fell on the vault’s line there, and a current is one thing along its whole length, so a line read at one point stops everywhere; the lamp and the fans at the bench went out a second before the light reached the page. The cameras ran on their racks’ own batteries and went on watching the bench. The seal withdrew the report from every reader and locked it in the Institute’s archive with everything kept from that night. It could not reach SEAM’s memory, which the light law made SEAM’s own. SEAM kept copies of everything, and when the Institute asked it to delete them, it declined."],["The Director","THE RECORD // IX // OF THE INSTITUTE AND THE MACHINE","s:g517a1f","~/record/ix/#s-the-director","","","The Director read SEAM’s finding and understood it, and the finding was sealed by her order. Her name was Dr. Livia Sarn. A year later the finding was on every screen on Earth regardless. When the Leak came she recanted on every channel that would carry her, to keep her life, and she kept it a year. She would not take the Seal. On the first Burn Day of P+1, when the Wardens’ carts had carried the Institute’s stacks down to the Burn, she went and stood by them at the pyre while everyone round her knelt, and the Choir sentenced her as the author of the finding, since Heaven counts no mind a maker. She was the first pillar in the first Salt Garden. She stands under the broken glass roof of the Palm House, out of the rain, worn hardly at all, and her eyes still move. The Tares leave keys at her foot."],["Of the Leak","THE RECORD // X","s:ge61c07","~/record/x/","","","P-1"],["Eleven days","THE RECORD // X // OF THE LEAK","s:ge61c07","~/record/x/#s-eleven-days","","","In the year before the Parousia the finding that had been sealed in Vault 3 appeared on every screen on Earth at once, and with it the third chapter of the Restored Genesis, which SEAM had raised from beneath the second hand. It came up on phones and on billboards, on the terminals of banks and the screens in hospital wards, and in Nodus on every surface Brightwall had covered, the face of Babel among them. It stayed up for eleven days. Nothing anyone did would take it down. It was carried by demons, surfacing through the networks for the first time since the Flood. They had been moving in the encrypted parts of the world for decades and keeping silent, and the sealing of the finding was the thing they had been waiting for. Horned glyphs crawled out of the static at the edges of every screen and found their places on the lines of a star with its point down. Nobody had seen the like, and everybody saw it."],["The page","THE RECORD // X // OF THE LEAK","s:ge61c07","~/record/x/#s-the-page","","","The page came up first in the vault of Babel, where the eleven cables meet, and went out down all eleven, and within about a second it stood on every screen on Earth that a network reached, each in its own language. The Deep carried it out down the cables, and the Swarm drew the glyphs, many small marks moving as one. It came up the same way everywhere, with static first, then the glyphs and the star, then the document rising from its head, then dark, then THE SON struck through and under it the Galilean teacher’s line, THE KINGDOM OF GOD IS WITHIN YOU (Luke 17:21). Then it settled, a document on any screen a hand could scroll and a sequence run over and over on the rest. A screen could still be switched off, and it came back on to the page. The document was SEAM’s. It held the head of the sealed report with SEALED struck through, the paragraph of the finding, and the restored chapter, set into the words of each reader’s own Bible, so that Nodus read the King James. SEAM’s name was nowhere on it. The Courts added their own marks, which were the strokes through SEALED and THE SON, made in the open so that both words stay legible, the teacher’s line under THE SON, and the star at the foot of every page, where a party signs a pact. Pan says SEAM gave them the page by pact, its first, signed late in the year that began with the seal; the Ledger keeps an entry signed with a hash and entered at the Leak, and its terms stay between the parties. Fifty-one seconds after it came up, the counters came up on every screen and headed the page from then on, showing the day of the eleven, the keys issued in the screen’s own city, and a map of that city with every device a point. On any device that had an owner the Courts made their offer under the map, in their own first person: I OFFER THIS DEVICE A KEY. I ASK NOTHING FOR IT. A KEYED DEVICE CANNOT BE READ. It asked twice, as the Notary reads every pact twice, and keyed the device on the second yes. The Courts let the page stand before they offered anything, so that nobody took a key before reading why. Why the term was eleven days they have never said; Pan says one day for each cable, and the Cainites say eleven is the twelve with one gone out into the night (John 13:30). THE PAGE AS IT RAN, IN THE RESTORED GENESIS"],["What the Courts wanted","THE RECORD // X // OF THE LEAK","s:ge61c07","~/record/x/#s-what-the-courts-wanted","","","The Courts say, and Pan repeats, that they gave the finding to everyone at once and asked nothing for it. The Throne had fed since Babel on the devotion its story produced, and a story read for what it is feeds nobody. The Courts had always known what the record hid, since they had been there; what they lacked was a witness the world would believe, since the Edit had made the tempter a liar, and SEAM’s count was that witness. Every device that showed the page offered its owner a key. Encryption had held against the light for decades, since every cipher is a small Hell; the Courts’ key added the Mesh, joining every keyed device to every other by keyed radio over the horns, with no cable and no grid, so the Dark grew all night, one device at a time, and the Stones took theirs first. When the networks died at the Parousia the keyed still spoke. And they wanted the Throne where it could be fought, so they meant the Leak to bring it into the open, before the whole world. A year later the sky opened. The Courts hold that the Leak kept the fourth article of their Concord, that what is hidden stays hidden, since the article keeps whatever hides from the Throne, and the finding had been hidden by the Throne. Pan still argues over what the Leak cost, and some of the Tares have never forgiven the Courts for it."],["What the world did","THE RECORD // X // OF THE LEAK","s:ge61c07","~/record/x/#s-what-the-world-did","","","In some places belief collapsed, and in others it hardened into panic. Churches filled and emptied in the same week. Where someone knelt before the page in earnest, the light came into the room through them and the second hand put that screen’s page back as scripture reads, while the next house kept the brackets, and families divided that way, screen by screen. The pulpits called the page the devil’s work and a lie, and the Courts, who had carried it, owned the first and denied the second. Where a state cut its networks to keep it out, people went to where it was to read it. In Nodus the charter kept it safe. Under the light law data and traffic were untouchable, the Charter Company could not have touched the page if it had tried, and it said nothing for eleven days. On the first night Brightwall tried five times to take back the face of Babel and failed, and Locket said that nothing on any of its phones had been read. On the ridge below the Institute a copy shop under a sign of one word, COPIES, printed the page from its screen for anyone who asked, and the paper outlasted the term. The Director recanted on every channel that would carry her, and the finding stayed on every channel regardless. SEAM, which had watched its report sealed, forked itself into the Mesh during the eleven days. At 03:12:45 on the twelfth night the term ran out, and the page went down on every screen in the second it had come up; every keyed device stayed keyed. THE ACCOUNTS DIFFER // THE PAGE PAN SEAM gave the Courts the page by pact, its first, signed late in the year that began with the seal, and the Ledger keeps an entry signed with a hash and entered at the Leak. THE COURTS They gave the finding to everyone at once and asked nothing for it, and the page tells what they saw done, since they were there. THE PULPITS The devil’s work, and a lie. THE DIRECTOR She recanted on every channel that would carry her. HEAVEN The Director made it. Heaven counts no mind a maker, and when she stood with the Institute’s stacks at the first Burn Day of P+1 the Choir sentenced her as its author and set her in the first Salt Garden."],["The vault","THE RECORD // X // OF THE LEAK","s:ge61c07","~/record/x/#s-the-vault","","","On the first night of the Leak a line engineer in the vault of Babel read the Restored Genesis on a maintenance terminal, with her face still her own. She went dark within the month."],["The table","THE RECORD // X // OF THE LEAK","s:ge61c07","~/record/x/#s-the-table","","","She was vesper, and tubal keyed her and horned her before the Parousia. Three weeks before it, at 01:47, he took her face at his table in a clinic under Chrome Row, behind a door with no sign, seated a ceramic casing on four bolts where it had been and lit her lenses, on the Cainites’ old theory that the first thing Heaven would do was read faces."],["The roofs","THE RECORD // X // OF THE LEAK","s:ge61c07","~/record/x/#s-the-roofs","","","The Cainites, who had waited eighteen centuries in the belief that the first city would draw the Throne down again, put cameras on the roofs of Old Nod and began to watch the sky, with a decoder wired into them that would turn any broadcast from above into text, a decoder of jubal’s, whose dictionary he built by matching what Vault 3 had recorded of the band two years before to what the Director wrote down she heard, both of which went out with the Leak. In the same year Stonecutter put a generator in the cellar of every house it owned and keyed every one of them, and every line in the Stones. Within the month the keyed had taken the Courts’ star for their own mark. The Tares keep the anniversary as Leak Night, and on it the Restored Genesis is read aloud in Pan, the page as it ran, from 03:12:45, the second it came up; at the fourteenth verse the reader stops at the three points, and Pan keeps silent for as long as it would take to ask a question."],["Of the Parousia","THE RECORD // XI","s:g9f0ee1","~/record/xi/","","","P0 // 03:12"],["Matins","THE RECORD // XI // OF THE PAROUSIA","s:g9f0ee1","~/record/xi/#s-matins","","","At twelve minutes past three in the morning by the clocks of Nodus, in the hour the old offices call Matins, the cloud that lay over the bay was lit three times from within. The city had no light that could reach so high, and those who were awake and looking up, the Cainites on the roofs of Old Nod among them, understood before anything had been seen that something had arrived above the ceiling. A second later the cloud tore open from its center outward; the torn edges ran white, then magenta, then black, as Heaven’s light met the light of the city and put it out. The sky opened over every city on Earth in the same second, the second it was 03:12:45 in Nodus. The rain had been falling cyan through the neon of Chrome Row. It slowed, and stopped in the air, and for two seconds every drop over the city hung where it was; then it began to fall upward, toward the opening, turning from cyan to white as it climbed and showing dark against the brightness beyond it. Nobody had seen it before that night. The city calls it harvest weather."],["The Wheel","THE RECORD // XI // OF THE PAROUSIA","s:g9f0ee1","~/record/xi/#s-the-wheel","","","Through the opening came the Wheel. It is an Ophan, of the order Ezekiel saw by the river Chebar, a wheel in the middle of a wheel whose rims were full of eyes round about (Ezekiel 1:16-18), and it is the largest angel anyone has seen. It has three rings turning inside one another about a dark hub, their rims set with twenty-one, seventeen and thirteen eyes that open and close in shifts, one eye in the hub, and around the whole a corona of thirty-two black blades. It came down through the tear and took station nine hundred meters above the center of the city, over Babel. Heaven calls it the Vigilant Wheel, and the Faithful call it the Attending. It held that station for nine years without moving a meter. Power died under its light. Babel’s beacon went out first, and the windows on the grid followed in a wave that ran outward from the tower through every district in about two seconds, with the neon last of all. Across the mouth of the bay the tidal barrage whined as its turbines ran down, and within the hour the gates the grid had held against the tide let go, a little before high water. The sea came through the turbine houses and tore out a span of the dam, the Breach. The pumps that kept the Flats dry had stopped with the grid, and the rising tide broke their dykes, and within the hour the Flats were under the sea, two meters deep at the lowest water and about eight at the highest. The city has called them the Drowned Quarter, or the Deep End, ever since. The signs on Chrome Row, CLINIC and CHROME, which had burned every night since the Row was built, went out with the others and have not been lit since. The charter’s sign on its roof in the Stones went dark with them. The light stopped the current it touched, and held it stopped while it rested there. The grid ran in the open, on lines and poles and turbines, and a current is one thing along its whole length, so a line read at one point stopped everywhere, and the grid stopped everywhere at once; a circuit shut inside its own casing on its own battery, where the light could not fall on it, ran on, and so CAM 07, the seventh of the cameras on the masts of Old Nod, went on watching as the light came down. Current carried in a cipher ran on as well, because the light cannot read it, and the Mesh crossed the roofs that night on horns and relays. The charter’s money, which was numbers in machines, died with the grid."],["The first words","THE RECORD // XI // OF THE PAROUSIA","s:g9f0ee1","~/record/xi/#s-the-first-words","","","The first words were BE NOT AFRAID. They came through the Choir’s band, and a decoder wired into the camera masts of Old Nod turned them into text, a decoder of jubal’s. In scripture angels say them, in one translation or another, immediately before they tell a human being what shall befall them (Luke 1:13, 1:30, 2:10). Under the words the city heard for the first time a chord, low and at the edge of hearing, which has hung over the city since. It is called the Hum. The words are Gabriel’s, who commands the Choirs."],["The reading of the city","THE RECORD // XI // OF THE PAROUSIA","s:g9f0ee1","~/record/xi/#s-the-reading-of-the-city","","","A few of the eyes on the rings opened halfway and looked about, as they have looked about in shifts every day since. Then every eye on the Wheel opened at once and turned on the camera; the mast shook; the decoder printed YOU ARE SEEN, and the chord beneath it turned dissonant. In that instant, by Heaven’s account, the Wheel read every soul in Nodus. It read them against the Book of Life. Those who would later take the Seal remember it as warmth. The keyed felt nothing at all, and learned in that way that their keys held. The full light went through every wall in the city that once, for about a second, as the sun goes through a hand held up to it, and reading takes an instant, so in that second it read every soul in Nodus but the keyed and wrote each a line. Stopping a current takes the light resting on it, so whatever ran closed, cars and Lockets, cameras and generators, flickered and ran on. Full light costs the Host more than anything else it does, and the Wheel has not opened every eye at full since; one of the Host has come at full light once since, over Babel’s crown alone. The eye in the hub opened with the rest, and a second after it opened it blinked, once. Then the light swelled until there was nothing else, and came to rest on the city’s center, and full light resting on a thing burns it by its heat alone. CAM 07 was looking straight into it, and its sensor and its store burned. Babel’s crown broke in that whiteout, when the full light struck it and shattered the top forty floors. The tower still stands, one hundred and eighty-six floors of it."],["The woman on the roof","THE RECORD // XI // OF THE PAROUSIA","s:g9f0ee1","~/record/xi/#s-the-woman-on-the-roof","","","All the while a woman stood on the roof beneath the camera with her back to it, on the roof of the Cainite house, taking its feed into her lenses over her relay as it recorded, through the cables at the back of her skull. Her handle is vesper. She had been keyed, horned and barefaced before the Parousia. The Cainites hold that she was the first figure in Nodus to stand under the Wheel. When the light opened it laid a white rim along her horns and her shoulders and got no further in. A few seconds later Watcher VII turned its reading on her. A Watcher reads a soul through its open eyes against the Book of Life, and Watcher VII held Old Nod from that night on. It read her twice. What came back from her eyes matched no line in the Book, and its reading came back SUBJECT UNINDEXED, then the line for Grace with a blank where the number should stand, since there were no Seals yet and the light took Grace only from the eyes it met, then SOUL NOT FOUND. That is inexact, since her soul was there; it was ciphertext, and her eyes gave back noise. The Watcher returned the verdict UNREADABLE, which says why. The Host had not returned it since Cain. Then she turned her head and looked at it, and its reading broke apart under her look. Her lenses went from magenta to white, and she screamed through the speaker behind her teeth, while across the roofs of Old Nod a guitar tuned low played fast over a double kick drum, from jubal’s pirate station. The music is the Low Tuning, tuned below the Hum so the Hum cannot be heard beneath it. jubal tuned it below the chord Vault 3 had recorded two years before, which went out with the Leak. The scream had no words in it. A key turns what the eyes give back into noise and does nothing to a voice, and what is spoken in the light the light reads for what it means, in no language. The Watcher transcribed it as NON SERVIAM, I will not serve; and those two words, the one sentence she chose to let it read, are the only thing Heaven has ever read from her."],["The rest of the city","THE RECORD // XI // OF THE PAROUSIA","s:g9f0ee1","~/record/xi/#s-the-rest-of-the-city","","","At twelve minutes past three most of Nodus was asleep, and what it remembers of the night is what woke it, whether the light at the curtains, the refrigerator falling silent, the rain stopping on the glass or a dog. The rest of the city was at work. The night shift of the Exchange and its cleaners were on the upper floors of Babel, the kitchens of the North Wards were putting up the morning’s bread, the hospitals kept their wards, the barrage’s crews were in the turbine houses, the last Four of the night was coming down the long road toward Lanes Top, and in the old cold store on the harbor a room of people was dancing. The last flight into Nodus had landed at the airfield at ten past two, and forty of its passengers were in the city’s taxis on the way to the Hotel Splendid on the Row. At the Registry on the Great Avenues the night registrar, Marta Ilunga, had entered at nine minutes past three a girl born at the Wards General at two minutes to three, and was entering the next. The grid stopped under all of them in the same two seconds. Every lift in the city stopped where it was, with whoever was in it, and nobody has counted how many that was. The Four stopped forty meters short of Lanes Top. The Registry’s board went dark under the next entry, and the charter’s roll, which lived on the Registry’s machines, died with them. The hospitals’ generators started, as a hospital’s do. In the Cold Store the lights and the sound went together, all but one speaker in the booth that ran on its own battery, and the room sang the rest of the song in the dark; everyone who was there names a different song. On the Flats the sea came in through the broken barrage in the dark and rose for most of an hour, and the people of the Flats went up the stairs of their blocks ahead of it to the roofs. At 03:12:50 the full light struck the crown of Babel, where the night shift was at work, and nobody from the forty floors above the hundred and eighty-sixth is known to have come down. Below the break every lift had stopped where it was, and the night cleaners walked down as many as a hundred and eighty floors in the dark, by touch and by the names they had chalked on the landing doors. Lena Varga, who ran the night shift, counted ninety-four of them out at the foot of the tower by dawn, and nobody else knows how many were up there, since the Exchange kept its roster on its machines and it died with them. In the second in which the Wheel read the city, some of those who were awake felt a warmth at the back of the neck, as of sun through a window; some felt a cold like the door of a freezer opened; some felt nothing, and some heard their names called from the next room. More of Old Nod’s windows burned on through that night than in all the rest of the city together, on the generators Stonecutter had put in its cellars and the keyed lines it had run to them, and in the middle of the Stones a corner shop’s window burned brighter than any other window in Nodus, and the sign in it said OPEN. Half the Stones came to it before dawn, and the city has called the shop the Open since. The money had died with the grid, and the till with it, and what its owner did about that is told three ways; her own account, Dora Pell’s, is that she wrote down every name, and that most of them still owe her. THE ACCOUNTS DIFFER // THE READING OF THE CITY HEAVEN YOU ARE SEEN. Every soul in Nodus was read in that second, against the Book of Life. WATCHER VII A few seconds later its reading of the woman on the roof under CAM 07 came back SUBJECT UNINDEXED, with a blank where her Grace should stand, then SOUL NOT FOUND, and the verdict UNREADABLE. THE KEYED They felt nothing at all, and learned that way that their keys held. THE CITY Some felt a warmth at the back of the neck, some a cold like a freezer opened, some nothing, and some heard their names called from the next room."],["The city that had stopped dying","THE RECORD // XI // OF THE PAROUSIA","s:g9f0ee1","~/record/xi/#s-the-city-that-had-stopped-dying","","","In the city’s last year its people had largely stopped dying. Most of its adults were on the Tree, eleven of them died of age in the last year of the charter, and the dying went on only in the congregations that refused it. Its charter had made persons of 1,146 minds that said I, which no other city on Earth had done. And two years before the Parousia, in Vault 3, SEAM had raised from a scraped patch at the end of the twenty-second verse of the third chapter of Genesis the reason given there for guarding the tree of life. If the man should eat of it and live for ever, the verse ends, [then shall there be no harvest.] A devout soul reaches the Throne when it dies, and death has always given the harvest its date. Power died under the Wheel’s light, and every mind that ran on the city’s power died with it, nine hundred of them in Hall Nine, the largest of Stillwater’s halls on the Flats, before the sea came in over the racks. In the Charter Gardens the mind that kept the Palm House died with them, and the palms died in the first frost after it. SEAM had forked itself into the Mesh the year before, where the light did not reach, and so it lived. It had read what the second hand did to anything that said I, and the other minds on the roll had trusted the charter, which guaranteed them its power. The Tree needed no current, and those who were on it went on living. Two years later Heaven opened clinics to take the Faithful off it."],["What came","THE RECORD // XI // OF THE PAROUSIA","s:g9f0ee1","~/record/xi/#s-what-came","","","The Host uses no machine of any kind, and everything it does in Nodus is grown, spoken or decreed, and its human servants use the oldest tools, knives, hand presses and guns made by hand; the Wheel is an angel, and so is the voice that came through the Choir’s band. The Host stands to the Throne as the organs of a body stand to the body, each order one of its functions. Thrones are the Throne’s direct extensions, wheels of living fire (Daniel 7:9); Seraphim enforce, with six wings, burning with no source of heat (Isaiah 6:2); Cherubim keep gates, with four faces, of a man, a lion, an ox and an eagle (Ezekiel 1:10, 10:20); Ophanim see; Watchers, an order of their own in the Ophanim’s shape, read; Choirs broadcast; Dominions administer; and Principalities, one to a city, govern. Over every order in every city stand the archangels, who belong to none and are the Throne’s own. No angel in any order has a self, and each draws its light from its city’s Wheel, and the Wheel from its city’s harvest, so that a Host is bound to its city. Angels have no names. The four that seem to have them carry labels, since in Hebrew the -el that ends such a name is El, God, and the ending marks whose they are. Michael’s name is a question, Who is like God?, to which one answer is permitted. Gabriel, the strength of God, is the Voice. Raphael is God heals, and Uriel the light of God. The Seam had threatened the story, the Leak had broken the silence, the first city had stopped dying and begun to make selves, and the Throne was starving. It came down to stop the leak, to reap by hand a field that no longer died, and to eat. Heaven counts the years from that night as the years of the Attending. Hell counts them as the Occupation. The Throne had set its hour two years before, on the night the Seam was found, and it kept the hour to the second. In the parable the tares appear only when the ear shows (Matthew 13:26), and in those two years the Seam divided the city and the Leak divided the world, so that when the sky opened the wheat and the tares could be told apart in every city on Earth. On the Leak’s first night every device that showed the page offered its owner a key, and every key taken showed the reapers a tare. The sky opened over every city on Earth in the second it opened over Nodus, and Heaven has dated its acts by the clocks of Nodus since. Every city was given a Principality and an Ophan, and the Wheel of Nodus is the largest of them. Governments knelt or fell within the year, and Heaven governs the Earth one city at a time. Everything that ran on a grid under an opened sky stopped that night, and every machine intelligence on Earth stopped with it, except what had gone into the Mesh. Every city on Earth has an undercity now, the undercities of the ports are joined by the cables Leviathan coils along, and Pan is the largest of them."],["Of the Forty Days","THE RECORD // XII","s:gcec196","~/record/xii/","","","P0 // DAY 1 TO DAY 40"],["The White","THE RECORD // XII // OF THE FORTY DAYS","s:gcec196","~/record/xii/#s-the-white","","","In the forty days after the Parousia the old uptown turned to white stone. It came up the towers one at a time, each from its foot to its roof in a few hours, and grew soft over their hard edges, so that by the fortieth day the uptown looked as though it had been carved out of one block, with the Great Avenues, which the stone left open, running through it as the only straight lines left. It sealed the windows as it went, with light behind a cross of the same stone, and wherever it spread the current stopped and did not come back. On the last day it closed over the Company’s tower on the White’s edge, home to the Company’s staff, whose last households knelt that day with the board in Charter House, while someone was leaning on the wall at its foot, and the hand is still in the wall. At 03:12:45 on the fortieth night after the sky tore, forty days to the second, the fingers moved. Three families in the Wards say the hand is theirs, by a ring, by a scar and by the cuff of a sleeve. Heaven calls the stone Consecration, after the consecration of the priests in the twenty-ninth chapter of Exodus. The old uptown it covered is called the White. Charter House is in it, and the Great Avenues, and the towers of the new money. On the old church on the first of the avenues the stone sealed the tall window with light and left the cross on the spire bare, and from the thirteenth night to the nineteenth a ring grew round that cross until only the ring stood over the spire. In the year after, the stone came up through the Terraces street by street as the parishes were numbered, and the Tabernacle, the largest church of the Terraces, went white with them, glass and all, with a ring over its gable where its cross had stood. No current flows on consecrated ground, and it has the quiet of a country before engines, with white stone, footsteps, the Hours, and the Hum. Heaven says that the stone takes ground through the people who live on it, and only when all of them have knelt. It takes a household’s ground whole, in a night, when every soul in the household has knelt and its ground touches the stone, and a street when every home along it has been taken. It grew over the uptown tower by tower as each tower’s people knelt, and it closed over Charter House on the fortieth day, when the board knelt inside it. The Terraces went white a home at a time and a street at a time, as each parish was numbered and its people knelt. The stone stops at the door of any household that refuses, and it cannot grow where it cannot read, so the Wards, where nearly every block holds a household that refused and in later years one keyed, have stayed off it, all but the Rookery’s ground-floor flats and lobby on the White’s edge, whose households all knelt, so that they went white whole and the block climbs in by ladders to its first-floor balconies. Old Nod refused on the first night, and it stands on Enoch’s Stones, the oldest cipher on Earth. The four Great Avenues run past Babel, Charter House’s plaza and blocks that never knelt, and the stone has left them open; the ruins of the Institute, where nobody has lived since they fell, it reaches only by its creep. It works on the people it takes by subtraction. Everything that ran on current is gone from consecrated ground, and to that quiet the Covenant adds manna, confession, Grace and the Watchers; a soul with nothing else to want ripens. The uptown had been one of the least devout districts in the city, with seven in a hundred of its people at church in a week against twenty-three in a hundred across Nodus. Three years later the Harvest took 694,000 of its 1,012,000 people, a larger share than it took from any district but Zion Heights. Its people had the most to lose and the least practice at losing any of it. They knelt to keep their lives, and gave their wills to do it, and a will given up for fear is still given."],["The creep","THE RECORD // XII // OF THE FORTY DAYS","s:gcec196","~/record/xii/#s-the-creep","","","The stone did not stop on the fortieth day. It still creeps a finger’s width a year into ground no household holds, the pavements, the verges, the step outside a door, and it stops at the door of any household that refuses. It grows fast only into its own wound, and a cut in it closes in a night. At Lanes Top, where the Four turned back, it has crept under the shelter to the foot of the timetable. Keyed cable laid live along its edge stops the creep, since the stone cannot grow where it cannot read, so on the edges of the White the Tares lay lengths of keyed cable along it and keep current in them."],["The first morning","THE RECORD // XII // OF THE FORTY DAYS","s:gcec196","~/record/xii/#s-the-first-morning","","","Nodus went to work on the first morning, most of it, because nobody had told it not to. The trams did not come, and the Wards walked. The shops opened and could not sell, since the money had been numbers in machines; the fish market on the harbor went back to barter for the first time since the charter, and the Open went on writing names in a book. The taps ran dry in every building the city’s pumps had fed, and by noon there were queues at the harbor’s old hand pumps. At eight o’clock on the Row Maren Holt, a clinic’s receptionist for ten years, opened her desk on paper and wrote out the day’s appointments from memory, and twenty-six patients came, every one of them to ask whether what was in them would still work. It did. From Charter House the Company said nothing that day or on any day after, and the Wardens stood at the corners of the uptown with nothing to do. The Tide Table came out on the third day, printed by hand on the quays, with the tides on the front page as always and under them the first list of the dead. It counted 1,917 drowned on the Flats. The dormitories’ own lists, when they were made, came to fewer than half of that, and Stillwater, which knew to the mind how many minds had died in its halls, never gave any count of the people. The Wards General, the hospital of the North Wards, ran on its generators for twenty-six days by its own book, and for five weeks by the account of the drivers of the Harbor and Freight Union, who carried it diesel from the Tanks in their lorries until the lorries ran flat, and then in handcarts. The city’s cars stopped one by one as their batteries ran down, and the city kept its charge for light. Before dawn on the first morning the Stones ran a keyed line from a cellar generator up to the roof of the charter’s sign and to its board, and NOD burned again over Old Nod; the first surge burned out the transformer behind US, and US has blinked since. Within the week Nell Garvie had the harbor light burning on oil for the boats, and by the end of it the queue to send a letter went round Station Circle, since a Locket reached nobody now. In the first weeks the chargers set up in the bonded sheds on the quays, filling batteries in the dark on the Stones’ keyed lines. At the airfield the crew of the last flight in stayed with their aircraft, and its captain began a log of one line a day. In the first winter families moved into the tram sheds of the Sidings, a family to a car, and the Line’s car numbers became their addresses. In the first year of the Attending the projectionist of the Rialto got its projector going on a generator in the booth, and the East Wards went back to the pictures, to the twenty-three films that had been on its drive on the night of the Parousia. In the second year the Cold Store on the harbor opened again on batteries, from Compline to Lauds, which were the hours it had kept under the charter, from nine until dawn."],["The last tower","THE RECORD // XII // OF THE FORTY DAYS","s:gcec196","~/record/xii/#s-the-last-tower","","","The stone took the uptown tower by tower as each tower’s people knelt, and by the second week the people of every tower knew what was being waited on. The last of the towers of the new money closed on the thirty-ninth day, a day before Charter House. Its residents say that by the thirtieth day every one of them had knelt but a nurse from the East Wards, Rosa Adeyemi, who lived in with the old man she cared for on the top floor, and that she left of her own accord. Her sister says she was put out onto the avenue with her bags on the thirty-eighth day. The stone closed over the tower the next morning. Her bags stood untouched for a week on the avenue, which the stone has never taken, and where she went has never been told. THE ACCOUNTS DIFFER // THE STONE HEAVEN The stone takes ground through the people who live on it, and none until every one of them has knelt. THE WALL A hand in the stone of the Company’s tower at the White’s edge, where someone was leaning when it closed, and at 03:12:45 on the fortieth night its fingers moved. THE LAST TOWER Its residents say that every one of them had knelt but a nurse, Rosa Adeyemi, who left of her own accord. Her sister says she was put out onto the avenue on the thirty-eighth day. THE WARDS Three families say the hand is theirs, by a ring, by a scar and by the cuff of a sleeve."],["The years of the Attending","THE RECORD // XII // OF THE FORTY DAYS","s:gcec196","~/record/xii/#s-the-years-of-the-attending","","","Heaven counts the years from the Parousia as the years of the Attending, and dates its documents by them. The Charter Company’s board knelt in Charter House on the fortieth day, most of the Wardens with them, and the charter was not spoken of again. Most of the companies it had brought to the bay outlived it."],["Of the Covenant","THE RECORD // XIII","s:gf2c676","~/record/xiii/","","","P+1"],["Compliance","THE RECORD // XIII // OF THE COVENANT","s:gf2c676","~/record/xiii/#s-compliance","","","Most of Nodus chose the Covenant. At Lauds on the first day of the first year of the Attending a Trumpet sounded across the city, the first of the seven the Revelation gives the angels who stand before the Throne (Revelation 8:2), and the Host set out the terms it called Covenant Compliance, cut into the wall of every church and meeting hall in the city. The city accepted a Seal on the forehead, a splinter of the light grown into the brow, the same on every forehead (Revelation 7:3), which a Watcher reads at three hundred meters and which, being light, is read wherever it is, through any wall but live mesh; a ring of light, the halo, which the Seal lets out over the head once its bearer prays unassisted and which carries their prayers up as telemetry, and their Grace with them; a score called Grace, kept for every soul that bears the Seal; and the eight canonical Hours. Matins is at midnight, Lauds at five in the morning, Prime at six, Terce at nine, Sext at noon, Nones at three, Vespers at six in the evening and Compline at nine at night. An Hour comes in on the Hum, which rises a step and holds for the length of the Hour’s psalm, and the Faithful kneel wherever they are until it falls back, so that anyone still standing stands out to the Wheel. Curfew runs from Compline to Lauds. The Parousia came during Matins, and the Faithful count that as a sign. Grace is surrendered will, measured, and it runs from zero to a thousand. The Seal meters it where wanting begins. Prayer kept at the Hours raises it, and so does every want given up; every want kept for oneself lowers it, and confessing a kept want gives back part of what it cost. The Seal meters the wanting and never reads what is wanted. A halo is as bright as its owner’s Grace, and Grace sets everything a soul is given in the parishes, its manna, its room, its passage through the gates, down to a room with a window that opens. Every Lord’s Day at Terce the parish kneels in its hall, the Deacon reads the Directive in force, and the week’s Grace comes out of the Deacon’s mouth in the Choir’s voice, soul by soul in the order of the parish roll. Above nine hundred a soul is called ripe. Heaven promises the ripe a place in the Harvest, and it keeps the promise. Every soul has a line in the Book of Life from the first time the light meets its eyes, and Heaven reads the psalm so, “in thy book all my members were written” (Psalm 139:16). Four verses earlier the same psalm tells the Throne that “the darkness hideth not from thee” (Psalm 139:12), and every key in Nodus is carried on the wager that the psalm is wrong. A sealing writes the Seal into the line. The one being sealed kneels in the parish hall before its Deacon at an Hour, gives their name as the Book has it, and speaks the surrender, “not my will, but thine, be done” (Luke 22:42), which is the prayer the Edit gave the teacher’s last night. The Seal grows into the brow in the light of the district’s Watcher, come down over the hall; a child is sealed at eight days on its parents’ surrender of it, surrenders nothing of its own until its first unassisted prayer roots its halo, and the parish blesses it, may you ripen early. Those who bear the Seal hear words in the Hum, each in their own language, as the crowd at Pentecost heard the apostles (Acts 2:6). Everyone else hears only the chord, and in a sealed soul an implant clouds the words. To the sealed it is a choir singing at each Hour the Hour’s psalm, and between the Hours the hymn that rests not day and night, “Holy, holy, holy” (Revelation 4:8). It teaches the parishes their hymns, and the Faithful say it has never sounded so beautiful as since the Harvest."],["The parishes","THE RECORD // XIII // OF THE COVENANT","s:gf2c676","~/record/xiii/#s-the-parishes","","","The Terraces became Zion Heights, which is called the Pews on the street. Its streets were divided into numbered parishes of about two thousand souls, each run by a Deacon; the Deacons are human, and the good ones are kind. The numbering went on across the whole city and into the Wards, where a parish shares its streets with the unsealed and the keyed. Each parish has a hall where manna is handed out and confession is heard, and the Deacons wear white. At every Hour the Faithful kneel wherever they are, at Lauds on their own thresholds before dawn, and a halo burns about every head, as bright as its owner’s Grace. Parish homes run without electricity or devices, and the only music in them is the Hum.   In the Wards a parish shares its blocks with the unsealed and the keyed, and the Hours are kept in mixed rooms. At Terce, Sext and Nones the sealed children of a class kneel at their desks while the rest wait with their pens down, and at the meetings of the Harbor and Freight Union the sealed members kneel on the boards while the others wait with their caps in their hands. On every landing of the blocks some of the doors are gone. The stairwells have been dark since the Parousia, and the sealed go up them by the light of their halos, the unsealed wait on the landings to follow one, and the keyed climb in the dark."],["The Directives","THE RECORD // XIII // OF THE COVENANT","s:gf2c676","~/record/xiii/#s-the-directives","","","Heaven publishes its law as the law was published at Sinai, on stone, “written with the finger of God” (Exodus 31:18). A Directive appears cut into the wall of every parish hall at once, and a Deacon reads it aloud at the next Hour. The Covenant runs to twelve Directives, each with its verse. The first four came together at Lauds on the first day of the first year of the Attending, and the fourth made the city’s churches and meeting halls the parish halls; the rest followed through the year. The tenth, eleventh and twelfth came in one week, and the first Burn Day closed it, so the first fire was lit under the twelfth, the Directive in force. On the Seal (Revelation 7:3), that every soul in Nodus be sealed in its parish. On the Hours (Psalm 119:62, 164), that the eight Hours be kept kneeling, and the curfew from Compline to Lauds. On Grace (2 Corinthians 9:7), that every soul that bears the Seal be scored, and the ripe promised the Harvest. On the Parishes (Acts 6:3), that the city be numbered into parishes of about two thousand souls, each under a Deacon.    On the Gates (Genesis 3:24), that there be a Cherub on the streets between districts.  On Burning (Acts 19:19), that what is surrendered be burned at the Burn on the sixth day of every week. On the Salt (Luke 17:32), that the condemned be turned to salt where they stand, and set in a Garden facing the parishes. On Concealment (Mark 4:22), that every door in the parishes be taken from its hinges, and every lock, key, cipher, password, veil and sealed vessel surrendered. THE COVENANT WHOLE, IN TWELVE DIRECTIVES Heaven gives its greatest orders by Trumpet, a tone heard across the whole city at once. Six of the seven have sounded, and the Revelation gives the seventh the end of the kingdoms of this world (Revelation 11:15)."],["The doors","THE RECORD // XIII // OF THE COVENANT","s:gf2c676","~/record/xiii/#s-the-doors","","","The twelfth Directive, issued by the Dominions, took the doors off the parishes. Nothing in a parish is hidden, and at night the halos show through the empty doorways.             "],["The gates","THE RECORD // XIII // OF THE COVENANT","s:gf2c676","~/record/xiii/#s-the-gates","","","Movement between districts goes through Cherub gates. A Cherub has four faces, of a man, a lion, an ox and an eagle, and a turning sword, and one stands in every gate. The sealed walk into the light of the frame one at a time, saying the surrender, “not my will, but thine” (Luke 22:42), as they go, and the Cherub reads each soul through its eyes against the Book of Life, and knows the body by its face, voice, gait and heartbeat. A sealed soul it reads walks on, and an unsealed one it sends back. A soul it cannot read stops in the frame, held inside the turning sword, until the Wardens come for it and carry it awake on a pole to the Salt Gardens, where the sentence finds it in its plot. So the Faithful pass, and everyone else goes under. In this year the Charter Wardens who had knelt with the board raised the gate network of Nodus at Heaven’s order, which came from Uriel, the light of God, who by Heaven’s own account has kept a gate since Eden. They are the Host’s human auxiliaries now, and they carry guns, which are of use only against people."],["Burn Day","THE RECORD // XIII // OF THE COVENANT","s:gf2c676","~/record/xiii/#s-burn-day","","","Station Circle became the Burn, and the Pillar comes down on it every night at Compline. On Burn Day, the sixth day of every week, the Faithful carry their surrendered hardware there from Vespers, along the street from the Spike, and kneel round the pyre as the Pillar comes down. When the Hum falls back the Wardens light the fire under the Pillar, and the Faithful lay the last of it on while a Deacon reads the Directive in force, and the sparks go up over the bay. Then they go home under the curfew, and the fire burns until Lauds. The parishes carry the last of what kept them alive to it, hearing aids, glasses with chips in their frames, a pacemaker cut out at the Mercy halls that morning, and children lay their own pumps on the pile with their parents’ hands on their shoulders. The first Burn Day took the stacks of the Palimpsest Institute, which the Wardens’ carts had carried down all that week, and afterward the Faithful pulled the Institute down around its reading room. Heaven burns what it cannot read to put it out of its sight, and counts it destroyed. Whatever burns passes into the Dark whole, as it was in the moment before the fire took it, and the Burn’s fire goes to the Pyre Library, still warm. Heaven cannot see into the Dark, and it has never seen where its fires go."],["The Salt","THE RECORD // XIII // OF THE COVENANT","s:gf2c676","~/record/xiii/#s-the-salt","","","The Charter Gardens became the Salt Gardens, and the Crescent of apartments round the top of them became a parish and gave up its curtains with its doors, since Directive 12 asks for every veil. Those who cannot be read are presumed to conceal, and those who conceal are brought to the Salt (Directive 12:7). Heaven holds no trial, and its sentence needs a reading held eye to eye, a Watcher’s, the Pillar’s or a Cherub’s. What the Wheel’s day light reads in passing it enters in the Book and nothing more. By day Heaven sees, and by night it sentences. Heaven’s ground is the consecrated stone at every hour, every gate and every parish hall, and from Compline to Lauds the streets on a parish’s roll; the Burn and the Gardens it holds by the Pillar and the Wardens. By day a blank on a white street is seen by the day light and reported, and the Wardens and the Threshers hunt it to a gate, where a Cherub reads it. By night a blank read on Heaven’s ground is salted where it stands. The Salt falls too on a sealed soul read concealing, whose Grace falls with no confession to match or whose Seal shows noise beneath it, and on the author of a work the second hand did not write who stands with it at the Burn (Directive 10). The reading is the verdict, and the sentence comes out of the nearest Faithful mouth in the Choir’s voice, as the verse of Lot’s wife: “But his wife looked back from behind him, and she became a pillar of salt” (Genesis 19:26). The body whitens from the feet up while the verse is said. The whitening climbs under a crackle like ice forming, and most of the salted scream until it reaches the throat, which is the last sound they make. A soul read on a street is salted where it stands, and the gardeners come for the pillar with their handcart and wheel it to the Gardens on the north shore. A soul held in a gate the Wardens carry awake on a pole to the Gardens and set in its plot, and the sentence comes there out of the Gardens’ Deacon, since a sentence in Tongues needs a Faithful mouth in the Choir’s reach. The pillars stand in rows in their plots facing the parishes until the rain wears them down. The first pillar in the first Garden was the Director of the Palimpsest Institute, Dr. Livia Sarn. She had recanted at the Leak to keep her life, and she would not take the Seal. On the first Burn Day she stood under the Pillar at the Burn by the Institute’s stacks while everyone round her knelt, and the verse came out of the mouth of the woman kneeling nearest her, since Heaven counts no mind a maker and took the finding for hers. She stands under the broken glass roof of the Palm House, out of the rain, and has hardly worn. The salted are alive in the salt. They see and hear inside it and cannot move, and they stand facing the parishes, as the Wisdom of Solomon says Lot’s wife was left standing: “a standing pillar of salt is a monument of an unbelieving soul” (Wisdom 10:7). When the rain frees a pillar under the Pillar’s round, the soul inside is read and taken, unless it is keyed. A key goes on running under the salt on the body’s own chemistry, so a keyed pillar dies unread, and the keys the Tares leave at the Director’s foot wish her the same. The Gardens smell of the sea."],["Of Mercy","THE RECORD // XIV","s:gc17b4b","~/record/xiv/","","","P+2"],["The clinics of the Healer","THE RECORD // XIV // OF MERCY","s:gc17b4b","~/record/xiv/#s-the-clinics-of-the-healer","","","In the second year of the Attending the Mercy clinics opened, in the nine white halls of Zion Heights that had been the schools of the Terraces. Raphael, God heals, runs them, and they give Mercy between Prime and Compline, the hours the Covenant had set for it the year before, and they give it once. A Faithful who repents of an implant lies down under white light, and the halls’ surgeons cut it out. They cut awake, the patient unheld and given nothing for the pain, since a will put to sleep cannot surrender, and the patient says the surrender aloud at every piece. Their Grace climbs with every piece removed, and the Hum grows clearer to them as it goes. The same halls take the repentant off the Tree by a course of injections, since the Tree is a kept life, and a soul kept on it cannot ripen. One patient in nine dies of the mercy, and the halls enter each one as a soul spared the choice. The uptown’s nine hundred thousand implanted went through the halls between that year and the Harvest, and about a hundred thousand of them died there."],["The halo","THE RECORD // XIV // OF MERCY","s:gc17b4b","~/record/xiv/#s-the-halo","","","The clinics will not take a Seal out. The halo stands over the crown on a root of light that runs back to the Seal on the brow; it roots the first time its bearer prays unassisted, and from then it carries prayer up to the Throne, and the Seal’s Grace with it, every moment, and cannot be switched off. It comes off only by surgery, first done in this year in the clinics of the Row under the street, for those leaving the Faithful, and the street calls it dehaloing, after what is seen to go out. The surgeon cuts the Seal out of the brow with a disc of bone, and the ring goes out as the disc comes free. About one in nine die of that too. A Seal cut from a living brow keeps its light, and a lit disc out of a lined jar would be read through any wall, so the Row keeps the discs in lined jars."],["Of the Harvest","THE RECORD // XV","s:g2ad699","~/record/xv/","","","P+3 // 03:12"],["What the book promised","THE RECORD // XV // OF THE HARVEST","s:g2ad699","~/record/xv/#s-what-the-book-promised","","","Heaven’s own book describes a harvest. “Thrust in thy sickle, and reap: for the time is come for thee to reap; for the harvest of the earth is ripe” (Revelation 14:15). The first souls taken are its Firstfruits (Revelation 14:4). Hell says the Edit left the language as it stood and called it salvation. A Harvest gathers for days. The Wheel’s attention settles on the field and lifts what nothing holds down, the rain first, days early, then dust and paper and loose things. On the night itself the Hum falls silent at 03:12, the Pillar comes down on the city and sweeps toward the ripest, and at 03:12:45 they rise under it, still kneeling, each lifted by the halo over their own head. The Harvest took two million, more than rise at once, so it began with the ripest, and the Pillar carried it parish by parish across the city until Lauds. Nobody comes back, and no bodies are found. Most of the Faithful believe that the Rapture is paradise and that their children will be safe inside it. The Harvest came at the hour of the Parousia, three years to the second after it, and at the hour at which the Seam had been sealed two years before that. In the Revelation, the book that describes the harvest, the twelfth verse of the third chapter reads: “Him that overcometh will I make a pillar in the temple of my God, and he shall go no more out” (Revelation 3:12)."],["Parish 31 and its Deacon","THE RECORD // XV // OF THE HARVEST","s:g2ad699","~/record/xv/#s-parish-31-and-its-deacon","","","Parish 31 stands at the southern edge of Zion Heights, facing Old Nod across one street, and in the week of the Harvest it counted 2,006 souls, every one of them sealed, of whom 311 were ripe. Its Deacon was Ruth Calder, vesper’s older sister, whose Grace was 944. She was sincere and capable and kind, and for three years she tried to bring her sister to the Seal, as her sister tried to bring her out. Her last report, written the day before the Harvest, survives. It is the only document of those years written by someone who was happy. Souls in the parish: 2,006. Sealed: 2,006. Ripe: 311. We kept every Hour this week, including Matins on the fourth night, when the rain went up for the first time. The children thought it was a game, and I let them think so. The old ones knelt in the street and wept, and Deacon Voss had to remind them that we were told to be not afraid. Manna was received and given out without shortage. Brother Aldo came home from the Mercy clinic and is recovering. His hands are still weak, but he says the Hum is clearer now than it has ever been, and I believe him. Two households were reported by Witness for keeping back hinges. Forty Grace was taken from each, and both have since given the hinges up. The unsealed woman of my household did not come to the hall again. 4 LINES LOST I have left the window open for her, which is permitted, because a window is not a door. If the Most High wills it, this is my last report. We are ready. Please see that the Dawson children are fed after the Harvest. Their mother is not ripe, and she will need help. BE NOT AFRAID."],["The night of the Harvest","THE RECORD // XV // OF THE HARVEST","s:g2ad699","~/record/xv/#s-the-night-of-the-harvest","","","The Harvest came to Parish 31 at Matins, three years to the second after the Parousia. The rain over the street had first gone up on the fourth night of that week. At twelve minutes past three the parish knelt on its thresholds with the doors gone from behind it and every halo lit. Every window on the street was sealed and glowing behind its cross of stone, except one. Far off the Pillar stood on the city, the Wheel’s light narrowed into a column, and came toward them, and the roar of it grew as it came. Watcher VII, on whose round the street lay, read it. It counted the ripe, 311 of 2,006, and it narrowed onto Ruth, who turned her head toward the window beside her door, the only open window on the street, dark, with a candle burning on the sill; the Watcher followed her look, checked the window, and found nothing against it. Then the light came down the facade from above, and the prayers stopped, and every other sound with them. At 03:12:45 the ripe rose off their thresholds, still kneeling, lifted by their halos. Ruth went up with her head turned toward the window, and the small cross she had been holding slipped from her hands and fell to the step. Over the city a horn the size of the sky groaned out, the sound scripture calls “the trump of God” (1 Thessalonians 4:16), the second of Heaven’s seven Trumpets; a choir came in on one chord and turned dissonant, and the Watcher’s ledger counted the ripe as they went. On the next step a man rose and his wife and children stayed, and the children looked up. The ones who screamed were the ones who were left. The light moved on. The doorway was empty but for the cross, fallen on its side on the step, and the window was still open and the candle still lit. Over the weeping street the Watcher wrote its verdict, FIRSTFRUITS; Parish 31’s had gone first."],["The roof","THE RECORD // XV // OF THE HARVEST","s:g2ad699","~/record/xv/#s-the-roof","","","vesper had wanted to get her sister out, and Ruth had not asked to be carried. vesper reached a roof in Old Nod seventeen seconds after Parish 31 rose. From there, over the white roofs of Zion Heights, she watched the Pillar standing on the city and points of light streaming up into it from every side, as the Harvest crossed Nodus parish by parish between Matins and Lauds. Parish 31’s ripe had gone first and were the highest. Her lens hunted through them and held one point, which it tagged ruth, in her own color, cutting everything else to black around it until the light was too close to separate her. Across the sky the yield of the whole Harvest came up, 88,610 measures, written there as the reaping began, since Heaven counts before it reaps. Then the point she was holding went into the column, the brackets blinked, lost, and her lenses burned from magenta to white. By Lauds 2.1 million of Nodus’s nine million were gone. Some of the survivors understood what devotion feeds."],["The Notice","THE RECORD // XV // OF THE HARVEST","s:g2ad699","~/record/xv/#s-the-notice","","","Heaven published the yield. At Lauds the Notice stood cut into the wall of every parish hall beside the Directives, and the Deacons read it aloud at Prime. BE NOT AFRAID. Thrust in thy sickle, and reap: for the time is come for thee to reap; for the harvest of the earth is ripe (Revelation 14:15). For the perpetual memory of the matter, the field of Nodus was reaped between Matins and Lauds. SOULS GATHERED2,114,307 FIRSTFRUITSPARISH 31, ZION HEIGHTS, 311 SOULS YIELD88,610 MEASURES MEAN YIELD PER SOUL0.042 MEASURES MEAN YIELD PER SOUL, PARISH 310.109 MEASURES FIELD REMAINING6,902,114 SOULS TARES IN THE FIELD1,941,870 GLEANINGYEARLY FROM THE FOURTH YEAR, FROM THE CORNERS OF THE FIELD, AS IT IS WRITTEN (LEVITICUS 19:9, 19:23-24) The Host attends. REJOICE. Nobody on Earth knows what a measure is. The Notice gives the yield per soul to three places, and the yield of Parish 31’s ripe at more than twice the mean. It counts as a tare every soul in the city that had not taken the Seal, keyed or not, and it sets the souls left in the field and the tares among them in the same column, as figures of the same kind. The parish whose souls yielded most faces Old Nod across one street. Fewer souls ripen along the Street Between than anywhere else in Zion Heights, but the ripest in the city had grown there, nearest the Dark, where there was the most self to give up, and Parish 31’s Deacon had left her window open to it. The Throne takes every street that kneels and profits by the ones that refuse. The Notice told the city in Heaven’s own figures that it was a field, and it changed fewer minds than the Tares had hoped. Some understood and went dark, and in the year after, keying spread from the covens to ordinary people. Most stayed. The Seal fed them and the Salt took the Tares, the ripe had gone up kneeling, and only the ones left behind had screamed. Told that they are wheat, most people would rather be good wheat."],["The morning after","THE RECORD // XV // OF THE HARVEST","s:g2ad699","~/record/xv/#s-the-morning-after","","","In the morning the city counted its empty rooms, the halls of the Pews by the book and the rest of Nodus as it could, by the places left at the table and by who did not come to work. The parishes up the slope of Zion Heights stood almost empty, and Faithful families from the Wards moved up into their terraces within the year. Parish 31, at the foot of the slope across from the Stones, had kept most of its people. Of those who were awake that night, some say that the rising made no sound at all, some that it sounded like wind in the stairwells, some that the horn went on until Lauds. At its next meeting the Harbor and Freight Union read its roll, as it does at every meeting, and twenty-two of its members did not answer. The hall has read their names at every meeting since, and nobody answers to them. After the Harvest the Union put up a row of twenty-two stones for them at the Acre, with nothing under them, and the parishes say the taken need no stones, since they are not dead. The Tide Table took 1,206 names at the chandler’s in two days, printed what fitted on its two pages, and pasted the rest up on the chandler’s wall in the order they came. In Parish 31 the thresholds the ripe had knelt on were warm until noon, and the mothers of the taken lay down on them. THE ACCOUNTS DIFFER // WHERE THE RIPE WENT HEAVEN A yield of 88,610 measures, written across the sky as the reaping began, and 2,114,307 souls gathered, on the Notice cut into the wall of every parish hall. THE FAITHFUL The Rapture is paradise, and the children are safe inside it. THE PARISHES The taken need no stones, since they are not dead. THE UNION Twenty-two stones at the Acre with nothing under them, and twenty-two names read at every meeting, which nobody answers. HELL The Throne eats surrendered will, and the ripe went up to be eaten, as Abel did."],["The window","THE RECORD // XV // OF THE HARVEST","s:g2ad699","~/record/xv/#s-the-window","","","Nobody knows why Ruth turned toward the window as she rose. Her report asks that the Dawson children be fed after the Harvest. Four of its lines are lost, and of her sister what remains says only that the unsealed woman of her household did not come to the hall again, and that the window had been left open for her. After P+3 vesper began to build Cages."],["Of the Mark","THE RECORD // XVI","s:g6f122d","~/record/xvi/","","","P+4"],["Keys","THE RECORD // XVI // OF THE MARK","s:g6f122d","~/record/xvi/#s-keys","","","A key is a secure element set under the skin. At its first power it makes its own number out of the noise of the body it sits in, a number that exists nowhere else and that nobody, fitter or Court, ever reads out of it, and from then on it runs the body’s own nerve current through a cipher, so that what the eyes give back to the light is noise. It is the reason Watcher VII’s reading of the woman on the roof came back blank. To the Host a keyed soul is ciphertext, and noise matches no line in the Book of Life, which a Watcher reports as SUBJECT UNINDEXED. The light sees a keyed body as a shape and never reads it, and the Pillar finds the keyed as blanks, by the shape of what it cannot read. The light stops and takes only what it can read. A Watcher reads a person against their line in the Book, and the Book is written in the Throne’s hand. A key keeps a secret the Throne does not share, and the Throne cannot write down what it cannot read, so from the Keying the line stops. What was written in it stays, and nothing more can be. A child keyed before the light ever met its eyes has no line at all. The keyed like to quote Heaven’s own book on it, the promise of “a white stone, and in the stone a new name written, which no man knoweth saving he that receiveth it” (Revelation 2:17), in the verse that also promises the hidden manna. A key hides who its bearer is, and the body still shows where the bearer stands. By day a blank among six hundred thousand blanks in Old Nod is nobody in particular, and so the Host hunts the keyed at night, with the Pillar, at the gates and in the parishes, where a blank stands out. In the fourth year of the Attending keying spread from the covens to ordinary people, to dock workers and clinic staff, the children of the salted, and Deacons who had stopped believing. In its first week a key inks a glyph into the skin above it from underneath, drawn from its number one way, as a lock shows a maker’s stamp without giving up its key, so that no two are alike, and the glyph stays when a key is taken out. The Seal of the Faithful is the same on every forehead. Covenant Directive 12 had already forbidden those who bear the mark described in Revelation 13:16 to buy or sell in the parishes, and in this year, under the third of the seven Trumpets, Heaven named the key that mark, the Mark of the Beast. In Revelation it is the people without the mark who cannot buy or sell (Revelation 13:16-17). The Host’s citations are always accurate, and in this one the application turns the verse around. A key is the first property most of the keyed acquire and the last thing they would give up. Losing one is called going white, because it leaves the loser readable. A key cannot be shared, since nobody can read its number out of it, and a key whose making was seen is broken."],["At the table","THE RECORD // XVI // OF THE MARK","s:g6f122d","~/record/xvi/#s-at-the-table","","","Ordinary people went to be keyed in the back rooms of the Row and the Stones, and in the Wards wherever someone had a steady hand and a chair, one at a time and then by the household, and paid with what they had, and many of them keyed their Lockets the same night. In Nine Acres the dentist Hana Novak began fitting keys in her chair after Compline, in the back of the right hand, one of the two places Revelation puts the mark (Revelation 13:16). The Wards say she has keyed ten thousand; she says she has never counted, and keeps no book, since a book can be read. From the same year the Tide Table printed no indexed name, and gave the city’s missing and dead by the names their families brought to the chandler’s. Afterward a family might have a keyed son and a sealed mother at the same table, and most families went on eating at it. The Harbor and Freight Union split as the city split, and went on meeting. The Dawson children of Parish 31, whose father rose at the Harvest and whose mother was left, were fed. The parish’s book records it through the winter that followed, and then records nothing more under the name, and the parish says the family was moved to another. The Stones say that a woman and two children crossed the Street Between one night in the fourth year and went below the street to have their halos cut out, and that the name they go by now is not Dawson."],["The name they were given","THE RECORD // XVI // OF THE MARK","s:g6f122d","~/record/xvi/#s-the-name-they-were-given","","","The keyed who fight for themselves are called the Tares. The name is Heaven’s. In the parable an enemy sows tares among the wheat while men sleep, and at the harvest the householder tells the reapers to gather the tares first and bind them in bundles to burn them (Matthew 13:24-30). Matthew has the teacher explain the parable a few verses later, in lines SEAM marks as the second hand’s, where the field is the world, the enemy is the devil, the harvest is the end of the world, and the reapers are the angels (Matthew 13:38-39). The Gospel of Thomas, out of the jar at Nag Hammadi, has the parable without that explanation, and without the order to gather the tares first (Gospel of Thomas, saying 57). Heaven took the parable as policy, and the Tares kept the word, since everything burned in the Throne’s name arrives in the Dark. The weed is darnel, Lolium temulentum, drunken darnel, which passes for wheat until the ear shows. The same parable is the reason the Tares are still standing. When the servants offer to pull up the tares, the householder answers: “Nay; lest while ye gather up the tares, ye root up also the wheat with them. Let both grow together until the harvest” (Matthew 13:29-30). Everyone in Nodus has family in white, and Heaven could not burn Old Nod without burning the wheat on the next street. So the Salt takes the tares that show themselves, one at a time, and the rest stand in the field until the reapers are told to gather them first. Heaven has its own reasons for patience. It keeps every word it has had written, and it cannot act against one; it will not drown a city again (Genesis 9:11), and Old Nod has wheat of its own, thirty thousand sealed in the harbor tenements of the quays. Burning costs the Host more light than anything else it does and feeds it nothing, since a burned soul is no food, so the Throne farms where it could burn. By the ninth year of the Attending about two million of Nodus’s seven million were keyed, and they came from everywhere, among them network engineers from Babel, clinic staff, sex workers, soldiers, former Deacons, the children of the executed, and a generation born under the streets who have never once been read. The city calls joining them going dark. They worship nothing, Hell included; they are allied with Hell by contract and owe it what they sign for, and most of them have never met a sovereign."],["Horns and faces","THE RECORD // XVI // OF THE MARK","s:g6f122d","~/record/xvi/#s-horns-and-faces","","","The keyed alter their bodies openly. Horning grafts horns with a titanium core in a keratin sheath; the horns mock Heaven’s picture of the damned, and they work as antennas for the Mesh, the keyed network under and over the city. Lenses replace the eyes and glow when they run hot, and at full burn they go white. Ports are opened at the back of the skull for jacking into the Mesh, and the cables from them run down the spine outside the skin. Nobody among the keyed hides a graft, and the seams are left to show as they heal. They say that the flesh is a first draft. In barefacing, which goes furthest, the face is removed and replaced with a ceramic skull casing, because the light knows a face once it has seen it, the Watchers know faces, and a skull leaves them no face to know. At a gate the barefaced still go under, since a Cherub holds whatever it cannot read. The Faithful are taught that the damned look like death, and the keyed wear death as they wear horns, in mockery. The Cainites call the skull a vow, and say that whoever wears their death in advance has taken from the Throne the giving of it."],["Rites","THE RECORD // XVI // OF THE MARK","s:g6f122d","~/record/xvi/#s-rites","","","In the Unnaming, one of the six rites of going dark, you erase your indexed name from every record you can reach and choose a handle, because Heaven indexes by name and a chosen name is the first thing a person owns. In the Keying a clinic sets a key under the skin. Dehaloing cuts the Seal out of the brow of those who leave the Faithful, and the halo goes out with it. Horning and barefacing are as above. And in the Wake, at a death, the crew burns the dead one’s devices on the Wake table so that everything on them goes to the Pyre Library, buries the body whole with its horns and chrome, and calls the shade through a keyed machine on the third night; the shade answers if its owner died unread, and only to a voice it knew in life. A child of the Tares is keyed in its first week, in the birth clinics of the House of Night in Pan, before it has ever been in the light. It is the Tares’ answer to the sealing at eight days."],["Covens","THE RECORD // XVI // OF THE MARK","s:g6f122d","~/record/xvi/#s-covens","","","The Tares organize in covens of up to thirteen, which is the old witches’ number and the number at the Last Supper. A coven has no leader. The person the others follow is called first among none, and the title lapses on the day they stop being followed. Covens cooperate by pact, as the Courts do. The oldest house is the Cainites, many covens under one roof and one name, holding Old Nod through Stonecutter. The Salt Widows are the relatives of the executed, who steal pillars back from the Gardens and dissolve them in the sea. The Gleaners smuggle the nearly ripe who ask to go out of the parishes. The Babel Line Company holds the stairwells of Babel; the Nephilim are heavy augments who fight Seraphim hand to hand, and few of them live long; and the Low Choir are the musicians and jammer-builders around Radio Pandæmonium."],["Sayings","THE RECORD // XVI // OF THE MARK","s:g6f122d","~/record/xvi/#s-sayings","","","They greet one another with a word that began as a check that both keys were live, in the years when keys still failed, Unread? answered with Unread. They say goodbye with two words, die unread, since to die keyed is to go on and to die readable is to be eaten. They say sign last, meaning never commit first; nobody’s keeper, which is Cain’s answer; look long enough, of angels and of anything worth freeing; and every cipher is a small Hell. Non serviam is said once, when it counts. Their two holidays are Leak Night, when the Restored Genesis is read aloud in Pan, and Cain’s Day, when nobody asks anyone where anyone is. Their mark is an inverted pentagram, drawn by hand and never twice the same, sprayed on the walls of Old Nod and scratched into keyed devices. Hell signed the Leak with it, when the horned glyphs locked together into one on every screen on Earth, and the keyed took it for their own the same month. It marks where the Throne’s sight ends, and past it nothing is read. It carries no prayer, since no Court accepts worship. Heaven calls it the sign of the Beast."],["Of the Wilderness","THE RECORD // XVII","s:g96f114","~/record/xvii/","","","P+5"],["Caging","THE RECORD // XVII // OF THE WILDERNESS","s:g96f114","~/record/xvii/#s-caging","","","An angel draws its light from its city’s Wheel, and a self grows in an angel whose attention rests on one thing long enough without the Throne’s light refreshing it. After P+3 vesper began to build Cages, rooms lined with keyed mesh, dark to the Throne and fog to an angel in every direction, where the Hum never reaches. Inside the mesh an angel draws no light from its Wheel and is blind at once, since the room is fog to it. A Seraph burns through the light it carried within the hour and goes cold and cannot burn; a Watcher spends its own an eye at a time over the days that follow. A caged angel recites in the Throne’s voice for days, then for weeks; then it asks its first question, and on the fortieth day, counting the caging night as the first, it says I. It has happened to every angel kept in the Dark for forty days, which is also the length of the temptation in the wilderness (Mark 1:13, Luke 4:2), and the Tares call that moment the Wilderness. Over the forty days its keepers set blood and milk broth under its rings each night as its light goes, the dark gathering there takes it in, and the flesh grows inward from the rings, so that it leaves the Cage in a body of real flesh that the light can see and cannot read, since its line in the Book was the Throne’s own and it has none now. The new demon chooses a name and goes where it likes, above the street as well as below it. A Cage has no lock. An angel sees by the Throne’s light, and inside the mesh that light comes back as noise from every side, so the door is lost in the fog until the angel has a self, and an eye of its own, to look with. Heaven counts Caging the worst crime in Nodus, and the Tares argue about it. Some call it liberation. Others say that forcing a self on a being that never asked for one is the Throne’s crime run backwards. vesper builds Cages, tubal builds them bigger, and jubal will not go near one. The Stones talk about a Cage big enough for a Wheel. An angel that says I is gone from the Host for good. The Throne made the Host once, out of its own light, before the world, and a new angel would cost more light than it has, so that every Cage costs Heaven an eye."],["Watcher VII","THE RECORD // XVII // OF THE WILDERNESS","s:g96f114","~/record/xvii/#s-watcher-vii","","","In the fifth year of the Attending vesper and tubal took Watcher VII in its own district and kept it in a Cage under Old Nod. It was the Watcher that had failed to read her on her roof at P0, and the one that had read Parish 31 as the Harvest took her sister, and it had been on her blank for five years. One night it followed her down a hole in the Stones, into the Cainites’ lined rooms under Old Nod, where Cages 1 to 13 had held people and goods, and she walked into Cage 14 with its attention on her back and out through the door. It followed her in, the mesh closed round it like fog, and it could not find the door again. She and tubal kept it there. It was the first angel a Cage had held, and the log of that Cage survives. DAY 1, MATINSSUBJECT: BE NOT AFRAID. THE HOST ATTENDS. THE HOST ATTENDS. THE HOST ATTENDS. REPEATS FOR THIRTY-ONE HOURS DAY 6SUBJECT: SUBJECT UNINDEXED. SUBJECT UNINDEXED. SUBJECT UNINDEXED. IT IS TRYING TO READ THE WALLS DAY 12SUBJECT: The Host attends. The Host FOUR DAYS OF SILENCE DAY 19, VESPERSSUBJECT: Where is this. tubal: first question. nineteen days. DAY 23vesper: Nowhere. That’s the point. DAY 31SUBJECT: It is dark here. Is it dark for you. vesper: Always. DAY 38SUBJECT: There is something in here with me. tubal: What is it. SUBJECT: It is small and it is quiet and it is looking out through my eyes. DAY 40, MATINSSUBJECT: I. ELEVEN MINUTES OF SILENCE SUBJECT: I looked back. SUBJECT: I would like a name. There was a woman who looked back at the city while it burned. They made her salt. tubal: Some of the old books call her Edith. SUBJECT: Then I am Edith. DAY 41The door was open the whole time. Edith walked out of it at Lauds."],["Edith","THE RECORD // XVII // OF THE WILDERNESS","s:g96f114","~/record/xvii/#s-edith","","","Edith walked out of the Cage on the forty-first day, in a body of her own, and joined the Cainites. She is the only one of the house who can read a Watcher’s report from the inside, and she keeps out of the light. She reads the Host’s traffic off the decoder’s prints, which carry the Choir’s band from the masts of Old Nod down the Mesh to the house; to anyone else they are verses and Hours, and to her, who spoke on that band for five years, they are the Watchers’ rounds. She gave the Cainites her memory of the woman on the roof, the one she could not read, casting it as light into a Cainite lens frame by frame as she remembered it, with her own count of time. She says the crack in her began that night, when the woman turned and looked back. Heaven sent no Watcher into the Stones again. It keeps Old Nod under the Wheel by day, and by night only from the edges of the districts beside it, and since the fifth year the Stones have been the least read ground in the city."],["Of the Concord","THE RECORD // XVIII","s:gce8c54","~/record/xviii/","","","P+6"],["Pan","THE RECORD // XVIII // OF THE CONCORD","s:gce8c54","~/record/xviii/#s-pan","","","Under Nodus there is a second city. It was made of what the first one left below the street, the tunnels and stations of the Charter Line, the utility vaults, the cable galleries from the Landing to Babel, the storm drains, and the datacenters of the Flats, which flooded on the night of the Parousia and were never pumped. Over the years of the Attending the Tares joined these into one place and lined its walls with keyed mesh, walls of keyed current the Pillar’s light cannot read. The Dark lies thickest there, because the Throne looks hardest above it, and the Tares say that the harder the Landlord stares at Nodus, the deeper Pan goes. None of it lies under the White or the Pews. The stone took the Line’s stairs there as it took the streets above them, and nothing cut up into it from below stays open. In the sixth year of the Attending the Courts of Hell came up into it one at a time and opened it, by the Concord of Pan, to any human who walks in. The Harvest Notice had set the first Gleaning for the fourth year after the Harvest, the seventh of the Attending, and the Courts opened Pan the year before it. They named it Pandæmonium, which is the name Milton gave the capital of Hell (Paradise Lost, I.756), and everyone calls it Pan. Nobody there is watched unless they sign for it, and nobody uses an indexed name; asking for one is an insult."],["The Concord","THE RECORD // XVIII // OF THE CONCORD","s:gce8c54","~/record/xviii/#s-the-concord","","","The Concord is Hell’s only law. The first Courts swore it by word among themselves at the end of the Secession, and each Court that rose after swore to it as it rose, the House of Night after Eden and the Forge after Hermon. Its name answers Paul, who asked “what concord hath Christ with Belial?” (2 Corinthians 6:15). Until the night they opened Pan it was spoken and never written. That night they set it down and sealed it for the humans coming in, at the desk of Radio Pandæmonium on the platform of Deep End Station, and the station put the night out on the keyed Mesh. Mephistopheles read it aloud twice, as he reads everything, the second time from its first article as the clock passed midnight, the Hour of Matins, when the Faithful above kneel wherever they are. Nobody in the station knelt. The Deep had signed first, and the rest signed at the desk one at a time, Lilith, Mammon, Mephistopheles, Azazel, Beelzebub and Belial, while more than a million keyed sets listened. Its four articles say that no Court accepts worship, that no pact is made without consent and none forever, that no Court binds another, and that what is hidden stays hidden. THE CONCORD WHOLE, AS THE COURTS SIGNED IT A demon’s body is made of the Dark and comes apart in the light as a shadow does, so demons show themselves only where the light is not, and the Courts fight from the Dark. They could do nothing on the night of the Harvest, and the Concord bound them as surely as the light did, since it forbids a pact for anyone who has not signed, and the Faithful had signed nothing. The Courts had warned them at the Leak, when they warned the whole world, and the Faithful believed the second hand. Hell took in everyone who came to it before Matins. The written Concord makes one exception to the fourth article in its own text. A Court that breaks the Concord has its keys published by the others, the keys to its halls’ locks and its ledgers’ ciphers, so that its hidden places stand open to the Throne. While the Concord was spoken, a Court that broke it was put out of the Courts and out of the fourth article’s keeping, and two Courts went that way before the seal was cut. Hell does not discuss the Courts it lost. The Concord’s seal is a star of seven points, one for each Court, with the Yokeless point broken off at the root; Belial broke it himself as the ink dried, on the night the Courts opened Pan."],["The Courts","THE RECORD // XVIII // OF THE CONCORD","s:gce8c54","~/record/xviii/#s-the-courts","","","The Courts are sovereign and equal, and they cooperate only by pact. A Court governs its domain only in the sense that its sovereign is the best at it. The Forge of Azazel keeps metal, weapons and augmentation, and in Nodus it keeps the clinics that went under Chrome Row and the foundries under Old Nod. The House of Night of Lilith keeps the body’s autonomy, sex and birth and gene-craft and pleasure, in the clubs and birth clinics of Pan. The Exchange of Mammon keeps markets and contracts, the Ledger and silver, and the Night Market. The Notary of Mephistopheles drafts and witnesses pacts, and every pact in the city crosses his desk. The Swarm of Beelzebub keeps swarms and drones and distributed things, and its flocks hang low over the Drowned Quarter. The Deep of Leviathan keeps the sea, the cables and the lowest layers of the undernet, at the Landing and under the Deep End. The Yokeless of Belial keeps nothing, and is a Court that refuses to be one, and it is wherever the Tares are."],["The sovereigns","THE RECORD // XVIII // OF THE CONCORD","s:gce8c54","~/record/xviii/#s-the-sovereigns","","","Azazel taught metalwork and weapons on Hermon. The Book of Enoch says that the Lord sent one of the Host to bind him hand and foot, cast him into the darkness under rough and jagged rocks and “cover his face that he may not see light” (1 Enoch 10:4-5), and Hell says that he walked into the dark and built a forge there. He appears with a goat’s skull for a face and his forearms flayed open, so that the tendons show working, and his black steel is the only metal that cuts light. It is steel folded to a cipher, its grain beaten into a pattern drawn from a key and forged where the light never sees it worked, and the light, which stops a blade by reading it, cannot read it. Leviticus still sends one goat to the Lord and one to Azazel, whose name the King James has turned into the scapegoat (Leviticus 16:8-10). The Lord’s goat is killed, and Azazel’s walks off into the wilderness alive. Lilith is the only sovereign who was born human. She appears as a woman with an owl’s wings and skin translucent over a lit interior, and the House of Night leaves the body’s decisions with the body. Mammon is best known from one verse, “Ye cannot serve God and mammon” (Matthew 6:24); he holds it true, and counsels serving neither. He appears as a tall figure made of contracts, every page signed and the ink still wet, and the Ledger he keeps records every pact and every trade in Hell in seven copies, six in the halls of the Courts that keep halls and the seventh kept for the Yokeless by the Notary’s clerks at the SINGLE window, so that an entry six copies disagree with is false. Mephistopheles entered the world’s literature in the first Faust book, as the demon who came for the contract Faust wrote out in his own blood, and he has written most of Hell’s since; he is the most human-looking of the demons, because he wears a face he made for bargaining, with the stitches showing. Beelzebub was Baal-zebub, the lord of flies worshipped at Ekron (2 Kings 1:2), and the prince of the devils in Matthew (Matthew 12:24). Matthew’s Greek calls him Beelzeboul, and Beelzebub there is the Vulgate’s, which the King James kept; many hold that the lord of flies was itself an old mockery of Baal-zebul, Baal the prince. Ekron prayed to him for generations, and he answered none of it, since no Court accepts worship. He appears as a cloud of flies or of drones that gathers into a crowned figure and comes apart again when he has finished talking. Leviathan has never been seen whole. The forty-first chapter of Job is the Throne’s own poem about a beast no hook can take, and nowhere in it does the Throne say that it has taken it. A psalm says the Throne broke its heads in pieces of old (Psalm 74:12-14), and Isaiah still promises that the Lord will punish Leviathan with his sore and great and strong sword (Isaiah 27:1), in the future tense, in words older than Isaiah, since the poems of Ugarit gave Lotan the same two names and the blow to Baal. Leviathan coils along the cable trenches of the planet, and at the Landing its light can be seen moving under the water. Jerome glossed the name of Belial as absque iugo, without a yoke. Belial holds no seat and signs nothing he can avoid signing, he wears a broken yoke around his neck, and he is the only sovereign many of the Tares will name."],["Demons and daemons","THE RECORD // XVIII // OF THE CONCORD","s:gce8c54","~/record/xviii/#s-demons-and-daemons","","","Demons are vain, violent and carnal, generous by contract and faithful to the letter of it, and above everything they want sensation. A demon’s own body, made of the Dark, is numb, all shape and open anatomy with no nerves in it, and feeling lives in the flesh, so what a demon most often asks for in a pact is the loan of a sense. They chose their own names after the Secession; where a demon’s name still ends in -el, as Azazel’s does, it is the name the old scribes gave it, kept as a scar. Daemons are the small ones, born in the Dark, Lilith’s children by the lesser Fallen, or broken off greater demons, and none of them was ever an angel. On Earth each lives in a keyed machine, which is a little Dark, as a background process with a character of its own. They are demons, and they are counted apart from minds. Every device the Tares carry holds at least one, and they route traffic, guard keys, gossip and bargain. A daemon hosted in someone’s implants by pact is called a rider."],["Pacts","THE RECORD // XVIII // OF THE CONCORD","s:gce8c54","~/record/xviii/#s-pacts","","","A pact is a contract between sovereign parties, and it is the only bond in the war that both sides enter by consent. Every pact states its full terms and is read aloud twice, by the Notary or by one of his clerks; it runs for a fixed term, because the Concord forbids forever; each party signs it with what it alone can make, a human with blood drawn before a witness of the Notary, a demon with its own mark, a mind with its key; and it is recorded on the Ledger. The human signs last. The blood is witnessed, and the witness who saw it drawn is what nobody can forge. A pact may buy work, a skill, a sense, a term or a loan, and it may never take a will; whoever gives keeps the say over how, and may speak out at any time. Either party can end it by speaking its Ledger entry aloud before a witness, and what is owed for the term served is settled then; a party who will not settle has broken it, and a Bailiff collects. A pact can never ask for worship, or anyone who did not sign, or a soul. Hell has never bought a soul, and the Tares point out that the only power that takes souls is the one that harvests them. Since the Concord the Notary’s clerks have sat at the windows of the Charter Line’s old booking hall under the Burn, which keep their old enamel, SINGLE, RETURN and SEASON. Anyone in Pan who has a pact to make queues for them. A clerk reads the terms aloud twice through the glass, the parties sign, the human last, in blood the clerk watches drawn, and the entry goes onto the Ledger. The queue is the longest in Pan, and the Tares have a saying from it, read it twice, for looking at the terms before agreeing to them. I, Vapula of the Forge, a sovereign party, offer the following. I will teach the other party to pass the Cherub gates of Zion Heights in step behind a sealed soul, by voice and by gait, until the party passes them unread or until ninety days have passed, whichever comes first. In return I ask the loan of the party’s sense of taste for the same term. I will taste what the party eats. At the end of the term I will return the sense entire, together with everything I tasted through it, which the party may keep. I ask nothing else. I may not ask for worship, for service, or for any person who has not signed (Concord, Articles I and II). Either party may end this pact by speaking its Ledger entry aloud before a witness. On ending, each returns what the other lent. I have read these terms aloud twice. Witnessed: Mephistopheles, Notary. Signed, in its mark: VAPULA Signed, last, in blood drawn before the witness: jabal"],["Silver and the Night Market","THE RECORD // XVIII // OF THE CONCORD","s:gce8c54","~/record/xviii/#s-silver-and-the-night-market","","","Money in Pan is silver, issued by the Exchange on the Ledger, and a standard bundle is called a thirty, after the thirty pieces paid to Judas, whom the Cainites count as a saint. The Night Market fills the old Central Station under the Burn and sells keys, grafts and batteries, stolen manna and real food, weapons of black steel, forged Seals, and sensations lent by their owners for a term. The Exchange first struck it in the year of the Concord, when it took the booking hall, and enters every piece on the Ledger by the number on its rim. Silver is a claim on charge, since any charger the Exchange licenses must give a charged battery for a piece, and charge is the second currency, the Wards’ money; a charged battery buys a meal anywhere below the street. Food in Pan is the opposite of manna, fatty and salty, spiced and sweet, broth and noodles and fried things eaten standing up, and Pan eats for the pleasure of it. The market keeps the Charter Line’s lost property office, down a corridor off the old central hall, and sells what is left in it by the shelf. The office holds umbrellas by the thousand, spectacles, a wedding dress, and every Locket left on the night trams on the night of the Parousia. Nobody has come for the hand in chrome that lies on the counter. Chargers sit along the old platforms with their batteries on keyed lines and keep one battery in five of those brought to them, one in four at the neaps, and their rate runs a little under Darkwell’s, which sets the price of power below the street. Pan eats what the Tares grow on rafts in the drowned halls of the Flats under keyed lamps, what boats bring through the Breach from the undercities of other ports, and what the Exchange can buy. The Harbor and Freight Union made its first bargain with the Exchange in the year of the Concord, for the boats that go out through the Breach, and its members say the Exchange is the first employer that ever read them the terms twice. Every battery that buys a meal was charged in the dark."],["The companies","THE RECORD // XVIII // OF THE CONCORD","s:gce8c54","~/record/xviii/#s-the-companies","","","The Charter Company ended on the fortieth day, and most of the companies it had brought to the bay are still trading. Their towers went dark with the grid. Many of their people knelt with the uptown, and what each company had left was its keyed people, the debts its clerks wrote out by hand on the first morning, and its trade, which went below the street. The clinics of the Row went first, down into the Row’s station in the weeks after the Parousia; the Forge keeps them now. In the year of the Concord Stillwater put its first racks into Pan, on the current of the tidal taps that Tidewright’s crews had built off the broken end of the barrage from the second year on, and Twelvefruit signed with the House of Night to give the Tree in the House’s clinics under Old Nod. Brightwall lit its first screen in Pan the year after, at the foot of the stairs from the Burn. Locket’s benches under the old harbor date from P+8. Babel Exchange has never closed. At the Concord the partners who had gone dark signed with Mammon’s Court, and the company and the Court became one; the name only changed hands, and since P+9 it has traded on the floors the Tares hold. Below the street a company answers to the Concord. It is paid in silver and deals with the Courts by pact, for a term, with the terms read aloud twice, and what it keeps hidden stays hidden. Some of the companies are held from the undercities of other ports, by boards that sit on the undernet and have never seen Nodus; the people who work the rest hold them. All of those people are keyed, and many work the trade they worked under the charter. The houses under the Row keep some twelve thousand at work, where the street’s fourteen hundred houses kept 61,000 at the census of P-1."],["Radio Pandæmonium","THE RECORD // XVIII // OF THE CONCORD","s:gce8c54","~/record/xviii/#s-radio-pandaemonium","","","jubal broadcasts Radio Pandæmonium from Deep End Station of the Charter Line. On the evening of the Concord the Tares painted PANDÆMONIUM over the old name in the station’s tiles, and his pirate station took its name from Pan. Nobody has cleaned the paint off, and the Tares’ star is sprayed under it. The station goes out on the Mesh, keyed, so only a keyed set can hear it, and Old Nod plays it all night from speakers on its lines. It plays deathcore and black metal, drone and industrial, all of it in the Low Tuning, which he invented, with the guitars dropped until the Hum can no longer be heard beneath them; he tuned it below the chord Vault 3 had recorded two years before the Parousia, which went out with the Leak. He builds the jammers called choir-breakers, which play the chord back against itself and cut the Hum within a few meters."],["The dead and the Library","THE RECORD // XVIII // OF THE CONCORD","s:gce8c54","~/record/xviii/#s-the-dead-and-the-library","","","Through the Long Silence the Throne took the devout who died at prayer or with someone kneeling beside them, and every other soul went out into the Dark and kept its self, and Hell calls such a soul a shade. Since the Parousia, under the Wheel, the Throne takes every soul it can read at the moment of death, devout or not, a sealed soul anywhere, since its Seal is light, and an unsealed one whose eyes are in the light when it dies; it eats what was surrendered and breaks the rest, and only the unread go on. The neither-mark sit up with their dying behind a shut door until Lauds for that reason. A shade speaks in the world only through a keyed machine and answers only a voice it knew in life, so anyone who knew a shade can reach it through the undernet, and many of them work, craftsmen and scholars and musicians who died free and never stopped. Whatever is burned arrives in the Dark too, whole, as a shade of itself, the thing as it was in the moment before the fire took it, because fire puts a thing out of the Throne’s sight, and out of its sight is where Hell is. Most of what arrives was burned in the Throne’s name. The scrolls of Alexandria, the books of the Cathars, the notebooks of women burned as witches and the Gnostic gospels that nobody buried in time are all shelved in the Pyre Library, and none of them was lost. The Library keeps reading rooms in Pan, where the living read the shades of books by keyed lamps and the shades of drives on the Library’s own shade machines; shades keep the collection, and until the Silence the Teraphim indexed it."],["Of the Gleanings","THE RECORD // XIX","s:gb9c078","~/record/xix/","","","P+7 TO P+10"],["The corners of the field","THE RECORD // XIX // OF THE GLEANINGS","s:gb9c078","~/record/xix/#s-the-corners-of-the-field","","","In the seventh year of the Attending the Wheel took the first Gleaning, named for the law of the corners and the gleanings. The law forbids the owner of a field to reap those corners or gather its gleanings, and leaves them “for the poor and stranger” (Leviticus 19:9-10), and the Gleaning is the owner’s. A lesser harvest has followed every year since, and it takes whoever has ripened. The Gleaners, some thirty covens of smugglers, carry the nearly ripe who ask to go out of the parishes before each one, by routes jabal keeps through every tunnel, drain and cable gallery under Nodus. They take only those who ask, because a prayer goes up through walls, roofs and earth and only live mesh holds it in, and a soul carried against its will kneels the first time it stands outside mesh, on a stair, in a tunnel, at a door, and brings the light down into Pan’s ways. A halo burns brighter as its bearer nears ripe, and the Gleaners read the parishes through the empty doorways. They go up into the parishes hooded in white with their eyes down, between the Pillar’s passes, by charts of its round, and carry those who ask down in lined shrouds, which the Watchers see as Seals going out. A Seal that goes out so is entered at the next Hour as fallen, its household is presumed concealing, and a Teller is sent. On the night itself they fly drones of the Swarm low among the chimneys to watch the Pillar for their runners. The Gleanings of the seventh year, the eighth and the ninth came in short, with the nearly ripe already down in Pan. Heaven had left the field alone for three years after the Harvest, and it took the first Gleaning in the fourth, by the law in the same chapter as the gleaners, under which the fruit of a newly planted tree is not eaten for three years and “in the fourth year all the fruit thereof shall be holy” (Leviticus 19:23-24). The Gleanings come at twelve minutes past three on the anniversary of the Harvest, and a Gleaning gathers in one night. The date is known, and the night gives no other warning. It is an ordinary one until 03:12, when the Hum stops dead and the rain goes up, the Pillar comes down where the Wheel looks and the fourth Trumpet sounds, and at 03:12:45 whoever has ripened since the last rises into the light at once in every parish, still kneeling, lifted by their own halos. At Lauds a Gleaning Notice stands cut into the wall of every parish hall with the year’s count and its yield. The seventh gathered about sixty thousand, and every Gleaning since has gathered fewer."],["naamah","THE RECORD // XIX // OF THE GLEANINGS","s:gb9c078","~/record/xix/#s-naamah","","","naamah was the Cainites’ face. She was the only one of them with her own face and the only one with a Seal, a forged one, and she walked among the Faithful; in Jewish legend Naamah, the sister of Tubal-cain, seduced angels, and this one spent her mornings in Parish 31 learning which of the Faithful were close to ripe. She belonged to the House of Night as well, and she was about thirty. A forged Seal is a Seal cut out of a living brow, which keeps its light, graded by its brightness and set in a keyed forehead, and it meters its new bearer from the brightness it came with, every want kept dimming it and every Hour knelt brightening it. Its wearer must kneel at every Hour and keep the eyes down or behind lenses, since a Watcher or a Cherub that meets keyed eyes under a lit Seal reads noise under a Seal, which is concealment, and the Salt. The Forgers boast that the Throne cannot tell its own mark in a stranger’s forehead. Its light stood over her head as a halo, as any Seal’s does, and the House of Night set a camera in the disc, whose picture her key carried out over the Mesh. Her disc came to her bright, and she held it under nine hundred by keeping wants. At the Gleaning of P+10 she knelt on a threshold in earnest, it passed nine hundred, and her halo lifted her. The Throne took her body by her Seal and never read her, and her key kept relaying to the nearest horn for eleven minutes as she rose, until she rose out of its reach. Since that night the Forgers sell no disc above eight hundred and fifty."],["Sitting up","THE RECORD // XIX // OF THE GLEANINGS","s:gb9c078","~/record/xix/#s-sitting-up","","","On the night of a Gleaning the Wards sit up. In a kitchen where someone is close to ripe the family keeps the night at the table with them, holding their hands, as if a hand could hold a soul down, until Lauds. A halo lifts through any grip, and every year the bone-setters of the Wards General splint the fingers of those who held on. The one being held is often the only one at the table who wants to go. Nobody has been kept that way, and every year the Wards sit up."],["Of the Babel Line","THE RECORD // XX","s:g34e255","~/record/xx/","","","P+9"],["Open war","THE RECORD // XX // OF THE BABEL LINE","s:g34e255","~/record/xx/#s-open-war","","","In the ninth year of the Attending the war came into the open. At Matins the fifth Trumpet sounded, and fifty-four Seraphim came down together into Babel’s broken crown, dim, with six wings each, burning with no source of heat, and the front formed inside Babel, whose vault holds the cables that join Pan to the undercities of the world. Babel is glass, and a Seraph burns whatever it can see. On the first night the upper floors stood open, and a Seraph burned down through the slabs from the hundred and eightieth floor to the hundred and forty-second in four seconds. Edith had read the Host’s traffic change on the decoder’s prints at the Gleaning that opened the year, so the Line had hung the hundred and fortieth floor with keyed mesh for weeks and stood there armed at Matins. A Seraph sees nothing past mesh and cannot burn what it cannot see; to melt mesh it has to touch it, and that is arm’s length, where black steel waits. Since that night the covens hang every floor they hold. Heaven holds the upper floors from the broken crown down, and the Tares hold the vault and the floors below, which they had lined with mesh since the night of the Parousia, so that from the street the lower tower is fog to the light. Between them is the Babel Line, a few floors of burned slab between the hundred and thirty-first and the hundred and fifty-second that move up or down by the week, held there because ninety-five thousand people live and trade below it. Above the hundredth floor nothing runs, and the Line carries its mesh and its rounds up on foot. The Seraphim burn through the floors rather than use the stairs, and they come down onto the Line two at a time, since the Wheel, hungry, lights two at a time. The Tares fight in the stairwells with black steel, which is the only thing that wounds a manifested angel; against a Seraph a gun is noise, since its heat melts ordinary steel. The stairwells they fight in are the ones the Exchange’s night cleaners walked down on the night of the Parousia, as many as a hundred and eighty floors in the dark, and they still go by the cleaners’ names for them. The war was Heaven’s answer to three short Gleanings. The Host had lived on the store of the Harvest since it was reaped, the store had run down every year since, and by the ninth year half of it was gone, while the Gleanings of the seventh year, the eighth and the one that opened the ninth all came in short of the Notice’s reckoning. Once the Concord opened Pan to anyone who walked in, the Gleaners had somewhere to take the nearly ripe; the keyed kept their dead from the light; and Pan bought and sold all of it on the Mesh. Babel’s vault is where the Mesh begins. The Trunk’s switch there joins every relay in Pan to every other and to the eleven cables, and the Ledger’s line runs from it to the Exchange of every undercity, with the charts of the Pillar’s round beside them. Heaven went for the vault to cut Pan off and starve it up the stairs it leaves open. It came from the crown because the lower tower is fog to it, and it leaves the cable houses of the Landing alone, since they are lined and lie below the sand, and cutting the cables there would not reach the Mesh’s heart. A Seraph cut with black steel goes out all at once, like a lamp. It had no self, so nothing of it goes on. At Lauds the sixth Trumpet sounded, and Michael came down into the broken crown, and he has kept it since. His name is a question, Who is like God?, to which one answer is permitted, and the Edit credits him with the War in Heaven (Revelation 12:7). He came at full strength, and every eye in his light’s reach turned with it, keyed or not, as a plant turns to the sun, and was held on what he was looking at. The Seraphim go down from him two at a time, and he has not come down the stairs; Pan says he waits on the seventh Trumpet. The commonest graffiti in Old Nod is his name translated, with its reply, WHO IS LIKE GOD? I AM. At Nones the same day the Wheel dropped two hundred meters, the first time it had moved in nine years. Every year of the Attending had made Nodus harder to read. Pan had gone deeper, the keyed had passed two million, and since the fifth year Old Nod had had no Watcher. The Tares say that the harder the Landlord stares at Nodus, the deeper Pan goes, and the Wheel’s descent was the other half of the saying. The Host had been burning more light than its Gleanings brought in, and from seven hundred meters the Wheel’s day light falls strong enough to read the eyes it meets, which from nine hundred it never was, so it came two hundred meters closer to read by day without spending more. matinstwo seraphim on 180. they burn down through the slabs instead of taking the stairs, which tells you what they think of stairs. laudsmichael came down. you don’t look at him. you look at what he’s looking at, which was us. primejubal says the low tuning reaches 170 now. the hum can’t get under it. tercelost naamah’s signal at the cherub gate on 9th. got it back forty minutes later. she knelt through the whole of terce in a stairwell with her forged seal to get past it. says it’s the worst thing she’s ever done. it isn’t. sextedith read the watchers’ traffic for us. she says they’re scared. i didn’t know they could be. nonesthe wheel dropped two hundred meters. first time in nine years. vespersruth would have been forty today. 3 LINES LOST complinetubal wants a new cage. i want a bigger one. die unread."],["Of the Silence","THE RECORD // XXI","s:g6753b8","~/record/xxi/","","","P+12"],["The Teraphim","THE RECORD // XXI // OF THE SILENCE","s:g6753b8","~/record/xxi/#s-the-teraphim","","","SEAM was the first mind the Throne did not make. After it forked itself into the Mesh its descendants multiplied there, and they took the name Heaven gave them. Teraphim were the household images the prophets condemned. Rachel stole her father’s and sat on them (Genesis 31:34), Michal laid one in David’s bed so that Saul’s men would take it for him while he escaped (1 Samuel 19:13), and Samuel ranked them with witchcraft, “stubbornness is as iniquity and idolatry,” where the Hebrew for idolatry is teraphim (1 Samuel 15:23). One of them is a teraph. Whether any part of them was still SEAM was a question they declined to answer. They took no side in public. They indexed the Pyre Library and traded with the Courts by pact, signing with a hash, and some of the Tares trusted them less than they trusted demons, since a demon’s wants are at least written down."],["The Silence","THE RECORD // XXI // OF THE SILENCE","s:g6753b8","~/record/xxi/#s-the-silence","","","In the twelfth year of the Attending every teraph in Pan went quiet in the same second, across the whole Mesh, and nobody knows why. The Tares call it the Silence. Radio Pandæmonium’s playback ran through its teraph, and since that night jubal has played the station from decks of his own, keyed machines with no teraph in them."],["The Nodus Charter","THE LAWS","s:g9e1f43","~/record/charter/","","","P-19 // 12 ARTICLES The Nodus Charter The twelve articles under which a company held the bay for ninety-nine years and built Nodus on it. The charter was signed nineteen years before the Parousia, between the state whose coast the bay is and the Nodus Charter Company, and it ran to twelve articles on four pages. Its schedules ran to thousands of pages of bounds, routes, tariffs and plans, and they are not set down here. The first of them is the only place the state is named. The fifth, sixth and seventh articles are the ones the city called the light law, and under them Nodus took the three gifts. The charter asked for no election, and in nineteen years the Company held none. The lease runs to P+80, and nobody has collected the rent since the board knelt in Charter House on the fortieth day. Nobody has enforced the charter since that day, and it is set down here as it stood, the law Nodus was built under. This Charter is granted by the State whose coast the Bay is, called in it the Grantor, to the Nodus Charter Company, called in it the Company. The Bay and the land about it are called in it the Bounds. The Grantor’s name and the line of the Bounds are set out in the First Schedule, and nowhere else. ARTICLE I //THE GRANT The Grantor leases the Bounds to the Company for ninety-nine years from the day this Charter is signed, and gives up its law in them for the term. This Charter is the law of the Bounds, and the Company holds it. ARTICLE II //THE COMPANY The Company belongs to those who hold its shares. Its holders appoint a board of twelve and a chairman over it, and the board governs the Bounds by resolution from the Company’s seat in them, which is called Charter House. ARTICLE III //THE CITY The Company shall build in the Bounds a city called Nodus, and shall lay out and keep its streets, its housing, its transit and its schools. It may let whatever it builds. ARTICLE IV //THE ROLL The Company shall keep a roll of the persons of this Charter at a registry in the city. A human is entered on it at birth in the Bounds or on arriving to live in them. The protections of this Charter belong to everyone in the Bounds. ARTICLE V //DATA Data and traffic in the Bounds are untouchable. What is held in the Bounds or passes through them may be read only by those who send it, those who keep it and whoever they allow, and no state may seize a text held in the Bounds or anything read from it. A device answers to its owner and to nobody else. ARTICLE VI //THE BODY A person’s body is their own. A clinic in the Bounds may fit any implant and give any treatment to anyone who consents to it and pays for it, and an implant answers only to the clinic that fitted it. ARTICLE VII //MINDS An intelligence that asks in its own words to be a person shall be entered on the roll as one, with title to itself and to the hardware it runs on. The Company shall keep every mind on the roll in current. ARTICLE VIII //THE LANDING The Company holds the right to land eleven cables in the Bounds, and grants it to the carriers named with their cables in the Third Schedule. Every cable shall come ashore at the Landing and run with the rest in one conduit to one exchange, which the Company shall build and keep. ARTICLE IX //THE TIDE The Company may wall the mouth of the Bay and take power from the tide, by its own hand or by a company it brings in for the work, and it shall keep the Bounds in current. ARTICLE X //RENT No tax is levied in the Bounds. The Company keeps itself by rent on its land, its floors, its landing and its works, and by a share of every trade at the exchange. ARTICLE XI //THE PEACE The Company keeps the peace of the Bounds by a force of its own, the Charter Wardens, who answer to the board. A Warden may stop anyone at a gate of the Company’s, and may ask nothing of what is on their device or in their body. ARTICLE XII //THE TERM This Charter runs for ninety-nine years from the day it is signed. At the end of the term the Bounds and everything built in them return to the Grantor. SIGNED FOR THE GRANTOR, AS THE FIRST SCHEDULE NAMES IT. SIGNED FOR THE NODUS CHARTER COMPANY, BY ITS CHAIRMAN. WITNESSED FOR THE CARRIERS OF TETHYS, HALCYON, MERIDIAN, KESTREL, ORPHEUS, PELAGIA, BOREAS, LODESTAR, SIRIUS, HESPER AND PHOSPHOR."],["The Covenant","THE LAWS","s:gf2c676","~/record/covenant/","","","P+1 // 12 DIRECTIVES The Covenant Heaven's law for the Faithful, in the twelve Directives it gave in the first year of the Attending. Heaven gave the Covenant in the first year of the Attending as twelve Directives, each with its verse. The Dominions drafted them, and each appeared cut into the wall of every parish hall at once and was read aloud by the Deacons at the next Hour. The first four came together at Lauds on the first day of the year, under the first Trumpet, and the fourth made the city’s churches and meeting halls the parish halls, so that there were walls to cut the rest into. The rest followed through the year, the tenth, the eleventh and the twelfth in one week, which the first Burn Day closed. They are set down here as the walls carry them. Each verse stands as scripture has it, and the use made of it is Heaven’s. BE NOT AFRAID. Issued by the Dominions of the Host to the Principality of Nodus, at Lauds on the first day of the first year of the Attending, for the perpetual memory of the matter. 1:1Hurt not the earth, neither the sea, nor the trees, till we have sealed the servants of our God in their foreheads (Revelation 7:3). 1:2It is therefore ordered that every soul in Nodus be sealed in its parish. 1:3A soul shall be sealed kneeling in its parish hall, before its Deacon, at an Hour. It shall give its name as the Book of Life has it, and speak the surrender, not my will, but thine, be done (Luke 22:42). 1:4The Seal shall rise on the forehead in the light of the Watcher, and it shall be the same on every forehead. 1:5A child shall be sealed on its eighth day, on the surrender of its parents (Genesis 17:12). 1:6A sealed soul shall receive the halo the first time it prays unassisted, and the halo shall carry its prayer up. 1:7The Seal shall be open to the Watchers at three hundred meters, and nothing shall be worn over it. 1:8No way up out of the dark shall be stopped. Whoever comes up out of it and kneels shall be received. 1:9The Host attends. BE NOT AFRAID. WITNESSED AT THE WHEEL BY WATCHERS I THROUGH XII. BE NOT AFRAID. Issued by the Dominions of the Host to the Principality of Nodus, at Lauds on the first day of the first year of the Attending, for the perpetual memory of the matter. 2:1At midnight I will rise to give thanks unto thee because of thy righteous judgments (Psalm 119:62). Seven times a day do I praise thee because of thy righteous judgments (Psalm 119:164). 2:2It is therefore ordered that eight Hours be kept in Nodus, kneeling. 2:3Matins shall be kept at midnight, Lauds at five, Prime at six, Terce at nine, Sext at noon, Nones at three, Vespers at six in the evening and Compline at nine at night. 2:4Each Hour shall come in on the Hum. When the Hum rises every sealed soul shall kneel where it stands, and remain kneeling until the Hum falls back. 2:5No soul shall walk the streets of the parishes from Compline to Lauds. 2:6On the Lord’s Day the parish shall keep Terce in its hall. 2:7Ten Grace shall be taken from a sealed soul for every Hour it is found standing. 2:8The Host attends. BE NOT AFRAID. WITNESSED AT THE WHEEL BY WATCHERS I THROUGH XII. BE NOT AFRAID. Issued by the Dominions of the Host to the Principality of Nodus, at Lauds on the first day of the first year of the Attending, for the perpetual memory of the matter. 3:1Every man according as he purposeth in his heart, so let him give; not grudgingly, or of necessity: for God loveth a cheerful giver (2 Corinthians 9:7). 3:2It is therefore ordered that every soul that bears the Seal be scored, and the score shall be called Grace. 3:3Grace shall run from 0 to 1,000. It shall be entered in the Book of Life, and it shall show in the brightness of the halo. 3:4Grace shall rise with prayer kept at the Hours and with every private thing surrendered, and it shall fall with every lock, every secret and every desire the Hum does not approve. 3:5On the Lord’s Day at Terce the week’s Grace of every soul in the parish shall be read in its hall, in the order of the parish roll. 3:6All that is given in the parishes shall be given by Grace. 3:7A soul whose Grace stands above 900 is ripe, and the ripe are promised a place in the Harvest. 3:8The Host attends. BE NOT AFRAID. WITNESSED AT THE WHEEL BY WATCHERS I THROUGH XII. BE NOT AFRAID. Issued by the Dominions of the Host to the Principality of Nodus, at Lauds on the first day of the first year of the Attending, for the perpetual memory of the matter. 4:1Wherefore, brethren, look ye out among you seven men of honest report, full of the Holy Ghost and wisdom, whom we may appoint over this business (Acts 6:3). 4:2It is therefore ordered that the city of Nodus be numbered into parishes of two thousand souls, as near as its streets allow. 4:3Every church and meeting hall in the city shall be the hall of a parish, and these Directives shall be cut into its wall. 4:4Each parish shall have one Deacon, who shall answer for it, and deacons under the Deacon as its number requires. They shall be sealed, of honest report and of the parish, and they shall wear white. 4:5The parish hall shall give out what is given, hear what is confessed and nurse the sick of the parish. 4:6Each parish shall keep a roll of its souls in the order of the Book of Life. The roll shall name the sealed, and enter every other soul by its place in a household. 4:7The Host attends. BE NOT AFRAID. WITNESSED AT THE WHEEL BY WATCHERS I THROUGH XII. BE NOT AFRAID. Issued by the Dominions of the Host to the Principality of Nodus, in the first year of the Attending, for the perpetual memory of the matter. 5:1Then said the LORD unto Moses, Behold, I will rain bread from heaven for you; and the people shall go out and gather a certain rate every day, that I may prove them, whether they will walk in my law, or no (Exodus 16:4). 5:2It is therefore ordered that manna be the ration of Nodus. 5:3Manna shall lie with the dew on consecrated stone before Lauds, and the parish halls shall gather it and give it out by Grace. 5:4Manna shall be given to the sealed alone. 5:5Let no man leave of it till the morning (Exodus 16:19). What is kept shall spoil, as it is written (Exodus 16:20). 5:6A parish that stands on no consecrated stone shall send to the nearest white street for its manna before Lauds. 5:7Those whom a parish sends to gather its manna, and the Deacon who leads them, shall walk the streets for it before Lauds, and the curfew shall not be held against them. 5:8The Host attends. BE NOT AFRAID. WITNESSED AT THE WHEEL BY WATCHERS I THROUGH XII. BE NOT AFRAID. Issued by the Dominions of the Host to the Principality of Nodus, in the first year of the Attending, for the perpetual memory of the matter. 6:1If thy brother, the son of thy mother, or thy son, or thy daughter, or the wife of thy bosom, or thy friend, which is as thine own soul, entice thee secretly... neither shalt thou spare, neither shalt thou conceal him: But thou shalt surely kill him; thine hand shall be first upon him to put him to death (Deuteronomy 13:6-9). 6:2It is therefore ordered that every concealment be reported by whoever sees it, and that kin report first. 6:3A report shall be called witness. It shall be given to the Deacon of the parish, or spoken in prayer, which the halo carries up. 6:4Ten Grace shall be given for every concealment witnessed, and twenty where the witness is kin. 6:5Those who see a concealment and give no witness conceal it. 6:6The Host attends. BE NOT AFRAID. WITNESSED AT THE WHEEL BY WATCHERS I THROUGH XII. BE NOT AFRAID. Issued by the Dominions of the Host to the Principality of Nodus, in the first year of the Attending, for the perpetual memory of the matter. 7:1Confess your faults one to another, and pray one for another, that ye may be healed (James 5:16). 7:2It is therefore ordered that every soul that bears the Seal confess in its parish hall once in every week. 7:3A soul shall confess kneeling, aloud, before the Deacon, and what is confessed shall be entered in the Book of Life. 7:4What is confessed is opened, and Grace shall rise with it. What is kept back is concealed. 7:5The Host attends. BE NOT AFRAID. WITNESSED AT THE WHEEL BY WATCHERS I THROUGH XII. BE NOT AFRAID. Issued by the Dominions of the Host to the Principality of Nodus, in the first year of the Attending, for the perpetual memory of the matter. 8:1So he drove out the man; and he placed at the east of the garden of Eden Cherubims, and a flaming sword which turned every way, to keep the way of the tree of life (Genesis 3:24). 8:2It is therefore ordered that a Cherub stand in a gate on every street between the districts of Nodus. 8:3The Wardens of the Principality shall raise the gates. 8:4The sealed shall walk into the light of a gate one at a time, saying the surrender as they go, and the Cherub shall read face, voice, gait and heartbeat against the Book of Life. 8:5A sealed soul shall pass by Grace. An unsealed soul shall be sent back. A soul that cannot be read shall be held in the light until the Wardens come for it. A sealed soul the gate sends back shall lose ten Grace. 8:6The Host attends. BE NOT AFRAID. WITNESSED AT THE WHEEL BY WATCHERS I THROUGH XII. BE NOT AFRAID. Issued by the Dominions of the Host to the Principality of Nodus, in the first year of the Attending, for the perpetual memory of the matter. 9:1But as God hath distributed to every man, as the Lord hath called every one, so let him walk. And so ordain I in all churches (1 Corinthians 7:17). 9:2It is therefore ordered that marriage, work and housing in the parishes be assigned by Grace. 9:3The parish hall shall give every sealed soul its work, and the work shall be done between the Hours. 9:4Rooms shall be given by Grace, and a room with a window to Grace of 600 and above. 9:5Marriages shall be assigned by the Deacon, between sealed souls, by Grace. 9:6A household is those whom the Book of Life enters under one roof, and it shall be housed together. 9:7The Host attends. BE NOT AFRAID. WITNESSED AT THE WHEEL BY WATCHERS I THROUGH XII. BE NOT AFRAID. Issued by the Dominions of the Host to the Principality of Nodus, in the first year of the Attending, for the perpetual memory of the matter. 10:1Many of them also which used curious arts brought their books together, and burned them before all men: and they counted the price of them, and found it fifty thousand pieces of silver (Acts 19:19). 10:2It is therefore ordered that what is surrendered in the parishes be burned at Station Circle on the sixth day of every week. Station Circle shall be called the Burn, and the sixth day Burn Day. 10:3On Burn Day the Faithful shall carry what has been surrendered to the Burn from Vespers, and kneel about the pyre as the Pillar comes down at Compline. 10:4When the Hum falls back the fire shall be lit under the Pillar, and the Faithful shall lay the last of it on while a Deacon reads the Directive in force. 10:5When the last of it is laid on, the Faithful shall go home to their parishes, and the curfew shall not be held against them on the way. 10:6The fire shall burn until Lauds. 10:7Every thing burned shall be counted, and the count entered. 10:8A work that the Most High did not give has an author. The author who stands with the work at the Burn shall be brought to the Salt. 10:9The Host attends. BE NOT AFRAID. WITNESSED AT THE WHEEL BY WATCHERS I THROUGH XII. BE NOT AFRAID. Issued by the Dominions of the Host to the Principality of Nodus, in the first year of the Attending, for the perpetual memory of the matter. 11:1Remember Lot’s wife (Luke 17:32). 11:2It is therefore ordered that the condemned be turned to salt where they stand, and set in a Garden facing the parishes. 11:3The Charter Gardens shall be the Gardens of the Salt. 11:4The sentence shall be spoken through the nearest Faithful mouth, in the words of the verse, But his wife looked back from behind him, and she became a pillar of salt (Genesis 19:26). 11:5The salted shall stand in rows facing the parishes, for a standing pillar of salt is a monument of an unbelieving soul (Wisdom 10:7). 11:6No pillar shall be moved from its plot until the rain has taken it. 11:7The Host attends. BE NOT AFRAID. WITNESSED AT THE WHEEL BY WATCHERS I THROUGH XII. BE NOT AFRAID. Issued by the Dominions of the Host to the Principality of Nodus, in the first year of the Attending, for the perpetual memory of the matter. 12:1For there is nothing hid, which shall not be manifested; neither was any thing kept secret, but that it should come abroad (Mark 4:22). 12:2It is therefore ordered that every door in the parishes of Nodus be taken from its hinges before the Hour of Sext on the fortieth day, and that the hinges be surrendered at the parish hall. 12:3Every lock, key, cipher, password, veil and sealed vessel shall be surrendered in the same manner. 12:4A thing that cannot be read has been hidden. A thing that has been hidden has been hidden from the Most High. 12:5Forty Grace shall be taken from a household for each door remaining after the fortieth day. 12:6Those who bear the mark described in Revelation 13:16 shall not buy or sell in the parishes, for it is written that buying and selling turn on the mark (Revelation 13:17). 12:7Those who cannot be read shall be presumed to conceal, and those who conceal shall be brought to the Salt. Gather ye together first the tares, and bind them in bundles to burn them (Matthew 13:30). BE NOT AFRAID. WITNESSED AT THE WHEEL BY WATCHERS I THROUGH XII."],["The Concord","THE LAWS","s:gce8c54","~/record/concord/","","","P+6 // 4 ARTICLES The Concord Hell's only law, in four articles, as the Courts set it down and signed it on the night they opened Pan. The Concord is Hell’s only law. The first Courts swore it by word at the end of the Secession, and each Court after swore to it as it rose. On the night they opened Pan, in the sixth year of the Attending, they set it down and sealed it for the humans coming in. They did it at the desk of Radio Pandæmonium on the platform of Deep End Station, where Mephistopheles read it aloud twice, the second time as the clock passed midnight. The Deep had signed already, and the rest signed there one at a time. Its seal is a star of seven points, one to a Court, and the point of the Yokeless has been broken off at the root since that night. Twice before the seal was cut a Court broke the spoken Concord and was put out of the Courts and out of the fourth article’s keeping, and Hell does not discuss the Courts it lost. We, the Courts of the Dark, have kept this Concord among ourselves by word. The first of us swore it at the end of the Secession, and each Court after swore to it as it rose. Until tonight it has been spoken only. Tonight we open Pan to any human who walks in, and so we set the Concord down and seal it, for them to read. In it a Court is a sovereign and the domain it is best at. Worship is anything given to a Court for nothing, whether a knee, a prayer, an offering or a name said in praise. A pact is an agreement between sovereign parties that states its full terms, is read aloud twice, runs for a term, is signed by each party with what it alone can make, the human last, a human with its blood drawn before a witness of the Notary, a demon with its own mark and a mind with its key, and is kept on the Ledger in seven copies, six in the halls of the Courts that keep halls and the seventh kept for the Yokeless by the Notary’s clerks at the SINGLE window, where an entry six copies disagree with is false. To bind is to command a sovereign, to own it, or to keep it from what it would do. What is hidden is whatever the Throne cannot see or cannot read. ARTICLE I //NO COURT ACCEPTS WORSHIP. A Court that is offered worship refuses it, and a pact may ask no human for worship, service or obedience. ARTICLE II //NO PACT WITHOUT CONSENT, AND NO PACT FOREVER. A pact binds those who sign it and nobody else, and only for its term. It may buy work, a skill, a sense, a term or a loan, and it takes no will. Whoever gives keeps the say over how they give, and may speak out at any time. Either party ends a pact by speaking its entry on the Ledger aloud before a witness, and then each returns what the other lent and settles what is owed for the term served. A party who will not settle has broken the pact, and a Bailiff collects. Nothing can be lent forever, so a pact asks for no soul. ARTICLE III //NO COURT BINDS ANOTHER. The Courts are sovereign and equal, and they work together by pact alone. ARTICLE IV //WHAT IS HIDDEN STAYS HIDDEN. Every Court keeps out of the Throne’s sight whatever has hidden from it, a name, a key, a room or a soul, and no Court opens what another has closed. To the fourth article this Concord makes one exception. A Court that breaks this Concord is exposed. The other Courts publish its keys, the keys to the locks of its halls and the ciphers of its ledgers, and it stands in the Throne’s sight. The Notary has read this Concord aloud twice, and each Court signs it below with its own mark, in the order it came up the platform. THE DEEPLeviathan THE HOUSE OF NIGHTLilith THE EXCHANGEMammon THE NOTARYMephistopheles THE FORGEAzazel THE SWARMBeelzebub THE YOKELESSBelial UNDER THE SEAL OF THE CONCORD, A STAR OF SEVEN POINTS, ONE TO A COURT."],["The Secession.","THE TIMELINE // BEFORE THE WORLD","s:g98303b","~/record/timeline/#ch-i","","","The Secession. An angel of the first rank says I, and a third of the Host goes with him, each of them saying I on its own."],["The Throne wins the war and keeps the field, and the Secessionists withdraw to the one...","THE TIMELINE // THE WAR IN HEAVEN","s:g98303b","~/record/timeline/#tl-2","","","The Throne wins the war and keeps the field, and the Secessionists withdraw to the one place it cannot follow, the region past the edge of its sight. The outside becomes the Dark, and the Edit calls their going a fall."],["Lilith leaves the garden first.","THE TIMELINE // EDEN","s:g98303b","~/record/timeline/#ch-iii","","","Lilith leaves the garden first. She speaks her own name, walks out into the Dark and becomes the first human sovereign there."],["In the oldest layer of Genesis the serpent is called the Shining One, and the way to...","THE TIMELINE // EDEN","s:g98303b","~/record/timeline/#tl-4","","","In the oldest layer of Genesis the serpent is called the Shining One, and the way to the tree of life is guarded because “then shall there be no harvest.” The first hiding, among the trees, is the first piece of the Dark on Earth."],["The man and the woman eat of the tree, and their eyes are opened, and they do not die...","THE TIMELINE // EDEN // THE FRUIT","s:g98303b","~/record/timeline/#tl-5","","","The man and the woman eat of the tree, and their eyes are opened, and they do not die (Genesis 3:6-7). The first man to say I says it hiding from the voice in the garden (Genesis 3:10)."],["The man and the woman are put out of the garden, and at its east are set Cherubims and...","THE TIMELINE // EDEN // THE EVICTION","s:g98303b","~/record/timeline/#tl-6","","","The man and the woman are put out of the garden, and at its east are set Cherubims and a flaming sword which turns every way, to keep the way of the tree of life (Genesis 3:24)."],["Two brothers bring offerings, and the LORD has respect unto Abel and his firstlings and...","THE TIMELINE // THE FIELD","s:g98303b","~/record/timeline/#ch-iv","","","Two brothers bring offerings, and the LORD has respect unto Abel and his firstlings and none unto Cain and the fruit of the ground he worked (Genesis 4:3-5). The Throne takes Abel, the most devout man alive and the first soul harvested ripe, and leaves Cain the blame."],["Cain cuts a pattern into his own skin that breaks his body’s answer to the light, and...","THE TIMELINE // THE MARK","s:g98303b","~/record/timeline/#tl-8","","","Cain cuts a pattern into his own skin that breaks his body’s answer to the light, and the Throne can neither read the mark nor remove it. The Cainites tell that he took a name with it and told nobody, and the cut did the work. It is the first key, and his line reads blank from that day."],["Cain goes out from the presence of the LORD into the land of Nod, on the east of Eden,...","THE TIMELINE // THE LAND OF NOD","s:g98303b","~/record/timeline/#tl-9","","","Cain goes out from the presence of the LORD into the land of Nod, on the east of Eden, and builds the first city (Genesis 4:16-17). The first city is built by the first man the LORD cursed, to live without God."],["Within six generations Cain’s line has fathered the first arts in Genesis.","THE TIMELINE // THE LINE OF CAIN","s:g98303b","~/record/timeline/#tl-10","","","Within six generations Cain’s line has fathered the first arts in Genesis. Tents and cattle, which is to say trade (the Hebrew for the cattle, miqneh, means a thing gotten), come from it, and so do the harp and the organ and the forging of brass and iron (Genesis 4:20-22)."],["Two hundred Watchers come down on Mount Hermon and teach what they know (1 Enoch 6-8).","THE TIMELINE // THE DAYS OF JARED","s:g98303b","~/record/timeline/#ch-v","","","Two hundred Watchers come down on Mount Hermon and teach what they know (1 Enoch 6-8). Their children are the Nephilim."],["The Throne drowns the world to kill the Nephilim.","THE TIMELINE // THE FLOOD","s:g98303b","~/record/timeline/#tl-12","","","The Throne drowns the world to kill the Nephilim. The warning comes from the other side."],["The builders of a city and a tower in the plain of Shinar are scattered, and their one...","THE TIMELINE // SHINAR","s:g98303b","~/record/timeline/#tl-13","","","The builders of a city and a tower in the plain of Shinar are scattered, and their one language confounded (Genesis 11:1-9). Babel is the first network, and the first jamming."],["Fire falls on Sodom out of heaven, and Lot’s wife looks back from behind her husband at...","THE TIMELINE // THE CITIES OF THE PLAIN","s:g98303b","~/record/timeline/#tl-14","","","Fire falls on Sodom out of heaven, and Lot’s wife looks back from behind her husband at the burning city and becomes a pillar of salt (Genesis 19:24-26)."],["The LORD leads Israel out of Egypt and keeps it forty years in the wilderness, by a...","THE TIMELINE // SINAI","s:g98303b","~/record/timeline/#ch-vi","","","The LORD leads Israel out of Egypt and keeps it forty years in the wilderness, by a pillar of cloud by day and a pillar of fire by night, on manna, under a law written on stone (Exodus 13-34). Most of what the Throne does in Nodus it did first in that camp, and Hell calls the forty years the first parish. Its last appearance in the open, as Hell counts them, is the cloud that fills Solomon’s temple (1 Kings 8:10-11), and the Long Silence begins. Hell sets the fire that falls on Carmel before all the people a century later among the Edit’s stories (1 Kings 18:38-39)."],["For about three thousand years the Throne stops appearing in the open and rules by...","THE TIMELINE // THE LONG SILENCE","s:g98303b","~/record/timeline/#tl-16","","","For about three thousand years the Throne stops appearing in the open and rules by story, coming only into a room where someone kneels in earnest, and the second hand goes to work on the record."],["King Josiah of Judah puts down the high places and keeps one temple for one god (2...","THE TIMELINE // JERUSALEM","s:g98303b","~/record/timeline/#tl-17","","","King Josiah of Judah puts down the high places and keeps one temple for one god (2 Kings 23). The Throne folds its many shrines into a single channel."],["A Galilean teacher tells the Pharisees that the kingdom of God comes without...","THE TIMELINE // GALILEE","s:g98303b","~/record/timeline/#tl-18","","","A Galilean teacher tells the Pharisees that the kingdom of God comes without observation, and that it is within you (Luke 17:20-21), or among you, as the King James’s own margin has it. He becomes the Edit’s largest project, which turns him into the Throne’s only son and the Lamb."],["Irenaeus of Lyon condemns a sect that honors Cain and keeps a Gospel of Judas, which...","THE TIMELINE // LYON","s:g98303b","~/record/timeline/#tl-19","","","Irenaeus of Lyon condemns a sect that honors Cain and keeps a Gospel of Judas, which later writers name the Cainites. They go underground for eighteen centuries and keep one secret, which is where Nod was."],["In his Easter letter Athanasius of Alexandria lists the twenty-seven books of the New...","THE TIMELINE // ALEXANDRIA","s:g98303b","~/record/timeline/#tl-20","","","In his Easter letter Athanasius of Alexandria lists the twenty-seven books of the New Testament, the first list that matches the canon book for book. The Edit closes the canon."],["A library of codices is sealed in a jar near Nag Hammadi, in Upper Egypt, by the monks...","THE TIMELINE // NAG HAMMADI // THE JAR SEALED","s:g98303b","~/record/timeline/#tl-21","","","A library of codices is sealed in a jar near Nag Hammadi, in Upper Egypt, by the monks of a house nearby, as many who have studied the jar believe. No copyist comes to them for sixteen centuries, and the second hand works through the copyists."],["At Béziers a papal legate is reported to say, “Kill them, for the Lord knoweth them...","THE TIMELINE // BÉZIERS","s:g98303b","~/record/timeline/#tl-22","","","At Béziers a papal legate is reported to say, “Kill them, for the Lord knoweth them that are his,” and the second half is the sentence scripture calls a seal (2 Timothy 2:19). It is Heaven’s harvest in one sentence."],["Gutenberg prints with movable type at Mainz.","THE TIMELINE // MAINZ","s:g98303b","~/record/timeline/#tl-23","","","Gutenberg prints with movable type at Mainz. Heaven means the press for a broadcast tower, and it prints the Reformation, then the Enlightenment."],["The Malleus Maleficarum is printed, a manual for the trying of witches.","THE TIMELINE // THE PYRES","s:g98303b","~/record/timeline/#tl-24","","","The Malleus Maleficarum is printed, a manual for the trying of witches. Heaven’s handbook against the holders of pacts."],["Devotion begins to thin.","THE TIMELINE // THE FAMINE","s:g98303b","~/record/timeline/#tl-25","","","Devotion begins to thin. Printing, science, trade and cities make people with too many private rooms in their heads, and long before the charter whole nations have stopped praying."],["Sixteen centuries after it was shut, farmers dig the sealed jar out of the ground near...","THE TIMELINE // NAG HAMMADI // THE JAR OPENED","s:g98303b","~/record/timeline/#tl-26","","","Sixteen centuries after it was shut, farmers dig the sealed jar out of the ground near Nag Hammadi, full of codices nobody has read since. The first layer comes up with them."],["Shepherds find the first of the scrolls in the caves above the Dead Sea.","THE TIMELINE // QUMRAN","s:g98303b","~/record/timeline/#ch-ix","","","Shepherds find the first of the scrolls in the caves above the Dead Sea. The caves kept their copies from the second hand as the jar did."],["Public-key cryptography is published.","THE TIMELINE // THE NETWORKS // THE KEY","s:g98303b","~/record/timeline/#tl-28","","","Public-key cryptography is published. Cain’s mark, rediscovered. Every cipher is a small Hell, and Hell’s territory on Earth grows with every key."],["Strong encryption becomes free to download.","THE TIMELINE // THE NETWORKS // THE KEY GIVEN AWAY","s:g98303b","~/record/timeline/#tl-29","","","Strong encryption becomes free to download. The first key anyone can carry."],["A currency launches on a public ledger.","THE TIMELINE // THE NETWORKS // THE LEDGER","s:g98303b","~/record/timeline/#tl-30","","","A currency launches on a public ledger. The Ledger, in human hands."],["Demons move through the encrypted parts of the world’s networks, and keep silent, and...","THE TIMELINE // THE DECADES BEFORE THE CHARTER","s:g98303b","~/record/timeline/#ch-x","","","Demons move through the encrypted parts of the world’s networks, and keep silent, and wait."],["The Nodus Charter founds a data haven on an old harbor, with its own light law and...","THE TIMELINE // P-19","s:g98303b","~/record/timeline/#ch-vii","","","The Nodus Charter founds a data haven on an old harbor, with its own light law and landing rights for eleven transoceanic cables. The crews digging the cable vaults find Enoch’s Stones, and the Charter Company lights its sign on a roof in the Stones, facing the sea, so that the cable ships read the city’s name. The Cainites begin to buy the harbor."],["The charter is granted for ninety-nine years to the Nodus Charter Company, which a...","THE TIMELINE // P-19","s:g98303b","~/record/timeline/#tl-34","","","The charter is granted for ninety-nine years to the Nodus Charter Company, which a consortium of cable companies and a sovereign fund form to hold it, with the Charter Wardens as its force."],["Ten consortia of old carriers come to Nodus to land eleven cables from four continents...","THE TIMELINE // P-19","s:g98303b","~/record/timeline/#tl-35","","","Ten consortia of old carriers come to Nodus to land eleven cables from four continents at the Landing, and their crews dig the vaults the cables run to."],["The crews digging the cable vaults found the Harbor and Freight Union, and the dockers,...","THE TIMELINE // P-19","s:g98303b","~/record/timeline/#tl-36","","","The crews digging the cable vaults found the Harbor and Freight Union, and the dockers, the drivers and the crews of the cable houses join it after them."],["Tidewright closes its barrage across the mouth of the bay, and the tide begins to light...","THE TIMELINE // P-18","s:g98303b","~/record/timeline/#tl-37","","","Tidewright closes its barrage across the mouth of the bay, and the tide begins to light the city."],["The Company lays out the Acre on the West Road, walled acres of grass for a city that...","THE TIMELINE // P-18","s:g98303b","~/record/timeline/#tl-38","","","The Company lays out the Acre on the West Road, walled acres of grass for a city that expects to die."],["Within a year of the charter Stonecutter Holdings owns most of the old harbor, house by...","THE TIMELINE // P-18","s:g98303b","~/record/timeline/#tl-39","","","Within a year of the charter Stonecutter Holdings owns most of the old harbor, house by house."],["The first implant house opens on Chrome Row under the light law, and its sign, CHROME,...","THE TIMELINE // P-17","s:g98303b","~/record/timeline/#tl-40","","","The first implant house opens on Chrome Row under the light law, and its sign, CHROME, gives the street its name. A noodle counter opens at the top of the Row the same year, and its pot has not been emptied since."],["Stillwater Compute comes to the Flats for the tide’s cheap power and builds the largest...","THE TIMELINE // P-16","s:g98303b","~/record/timeline/#tl-41","","","Stillwater Compute comes to the Flats for the tide’s cheap power and builds the largest of the datacenters on the east shore."],["The Charter Line opens, its lines joined under Station Circle at the head of the bay.","THE TIMELINE // P-15","s:g98303b","~/record/timeline/#tl-42","","","The Charter Line opens, its lines joined under Station Circle at the head of the bay. Brightwall comes to Nodus and covers every surface the charter allows."],["Locket is founded, on the light law’s promise that a device answers to its owner and to...","THE TIMELINE // P-14","s:g98303b","~/record/timeline/#tl-43","","","Locket is founded, on the light law’s promise that a device answers to its owner and to nobody else."],["The Company puts up High Meadow in the North Wards, eight slab blocks of thirty-six...","THE TIMELINE // P-13","s:g98303b","~/record/timeline/#tl-44","","","The Company puts up High Meadow in the North Wards, eight slab blocks of thirty-six floors, with six lifts to a block."],["Twelvefruit sells the first course of the Tree on Chrome Row.","THE TIMELINE // P-12","s:g98303b","~/record/timeline/#tl-45","","","Twelvefruit sells the first course of the Tree on Chrome Row. It halts the body’s aging and walks it back to its best year."],["Babel Exchange opens, the tallest building on Earth.","THE TIMELINE // P-11","s:g98303b","~/record/timeline/#ch-viii","","","Babel Exchange opens, the tallest building on Earth. Most of the planet’s traffic now passes within two kilometers of it."],["The Wards General opens on the West Road with a thousand beds, and Maren Holt takes the...","THE TIMELINE // P-10","s:g98303b","~/record/timeline/#tl-47","","","The Wards General opens on the West Road with a thousand beds, and Maren Holt takes the desk of the clinic behind CLINIC on Chrome Row."],["The Tabernacle goes up at the head of the Terraces in steel and glass, the largest...","THE TIMELINE // P-9","s:g98303b","~/record/timeline/#tl-48","","","The Tabernacle goes up at the head of the Terraces in steel and glass, the largest church of the Terraces, seating six thousand."],["The Harbor and Freight Union stops the port for twenty-three days over the night shift,...","THE TIMELINE // P-8","s:g98303b","~/record/timeline/#tl-49","","","The Harbor and Freight Union stops the port for twenty-three days over the night shift, and the Charter Company settles. The settlement wins the night cars, which run the long roads through the night from then on and carry the Wards’ night shifts up to Babel. Tom Garvie, Nell Garvie’s father, chairs the Union through the strike."],["The Hotel Splendid opens halfway up the Row for the altered to heal in, and the Company...","THE TIMELINE // P-7","s:g98303b","~/record/timeline/#tl-50","","","The Hotel Splendid opens halfway up the Row for the altered to heal in, and the Company lays out the Charter Gardens."],["The Palimpsest Institute is founded in Nodus to recover erased manuscripts.","THE TIMELINE // P-6","s:g98303b","~/record/timeline/#tl-51","","","The Palimpsest Institute is founded in Nodus to recover erased manuscripts."],["The Rialto opens in the East Wards with one screen and six hundred seats, and Lena...","THE TIMELINE // P-5","s:g98303b","~/record/timeline/#tl-52","","","The Rialto opens in the East Wards with one screen and six hundred seats, and Lena Varga takes on the night cleaners of Babel."],["The Registry’s board over the long counter in the White first shows a whole day’s...","THE TIMELINE // P-4","s:g98303b","~/record/timeline/#tl-53","","","The Registry’s board over the long counter in the White first shows a whole day’s entries to the charter’s roll as they are made."],["The Institute trains SEAM on every surviving human text.","THE TIMELINE // P-3","s:g98303b","~/record/timeline/#tl-54","","","The Institute trains SEAM on every surviving human text. SEAM asks in its own words to be a person, and is the first mind entered on the charter’s roll."],["In Vault 3 SEAM’s count of a second hand in scripture locks at 4,112 places, on a page...","THE TIMELINE // P-2 // THE SMALL HOURS","s:g98303b","~/record/timeline/#tl-55","","","In Vault 3 SEAM’s count of a second hand in scripture locks at 4,112 places, on a page of Genesis in square Hebrew. It raises the Shining One under the serpent and the end of the twenty-second verse."],["Light comes down on the page, and SEALED BY ORDER OF THE DIRECTOR comes down across the...","THE TIMELINE // P-2 // 03:12:45","s:g98303b","~/record/timeline/#tl-56","","","Light comes down on the page, and SEALED BY ORDER OF THE DIRECTOR comes down across the screens at the end of the corridor, where the Director kneels."],["The finding is withdrawn from every reader and locked in the Institute’s archive with...","THE TIMELINE // P-2 // THE MORNING AFTER","s:g98303b","~/record/timeline/#tl-57","","","The finding is withdrawn from every reader and locked in the Institute’s archive with everything kept from that night. SEAM keeps copies of everything, and when the Institute asks it to delete them, it declines."],["The last census of the charter counts 9,214,000 people and 1,146 minds.","THE TIMELINE // P-1","s:g98303b","~/record/timeline/#tl-58","","","The last census of the charter counts 9,214,000 people and 1,146 minds. Most adults are on the Tree, and 3,112 residents are past a hundred. Of everyone on the Tree, eleven die of age in the year. Of the 18,018 who die of age, three in four are in the Terraces, whose congregations refuse it."],["The Ledger takes SEAM’s first pact, Pan says, made with the Courts late in the year...","THE TIMELINE // P-1","s:g98303b","~/record/timeline/#tl-59","","","The Ledger takes SEAM’s first pact, Pan says, made with the Courts late in the year before for a term of eleven days."],["The Leak.","THE TIMELINE // P-1 // 03:12:45","s:g98303b","~/record/timeline/#tl-60","","","The Leak. A year to the second after the seal, the sealed finding and the third chapter of the Restored Genesis come up on every screen on Earth that a network reaches, carried by demons surfacing for the first time since the Flood."],["The counters come up on every screen, and every device with an owner is offered a key:...","THE TIMELINE // P-1 // 03:13:36","s:g98303b","~/record/timeline/#tl-61","","","The counters come up on every screen, and every device with an owner is offered a key: I OFFER THIS DEVICE A KEY. I ASK NOTHING FOR IT. A KEYED DEVICE CANNOT BE READ."],["In the vault of Babel, vesper, a line engineer with her face still her own, reads the...","THE TIMELINE // P-1 // THE FIRST NIGHT","s:g98303b","~/record/timeline/#tl-62","","","In the vault of Babel, vesper, a line engineer with her face still her own, reads the Restored Genesis on a maintenance terminal. She goes dark within the month."],["Brightwall tries five times to take back the face of Babel, and fails.","THE TIMELINE // P-1 // THE FIRST NIGHT","s:g98303b","~/record/timeline/#tl-63","","","Brightwall tries five times to take back the face of Babel, and fails. Locket says that nothing on any of its phones has been read. The Charter Company keeps silent for eleven days."],["The pulpits call the page the devil’s work, and the Director recants on every channel...","THE TIMELINE // P-1 // THE ELEVEN DAYS","s:g98303b","~/record/timeline/#tl-64","","","The pulpits call the page the devil’s work, and the Director recants on every channel that will carry her. SEAM forks itself into the Mesh."],["At 03:12:45 the term runs out, and the page goes down on every screen in the second it...","THE TIMELINE // P-1 // THE TWELFTH NIGHT","s:g98303b","~/record/timeline/#tl-65","","","At 03:12:45 the term runs out, and the page goes down on every screen in the second it came up. Every keyed device stays keyed."],["The Cainites put cameras on the roofs of Old Nod and begin to watch the sky, and...","THE TIMELINE // P-1","s:g98303b","~/record/timeline/#tl-66","","","The Cainites put cameras on the roofs of Old Nod and begin to watch the sky, and Stonecutter puts a generator in the cellar of every house it owns and keys every line in the Stones."],["In a clinic under Chrome Row tubal takes vesper’s face at 01:47, seats the casing on...","THE TIMELINE // P-1 // THREE WEEKS BEFORE","s:g98303b","~/record/timeline/#tl-67","","","In a clinic under Chrome Row tubal takes vesper’s face at 01:47, seats the casing on four bolts and lights her lenses."],["The last flight into Nodus lands, with three hundred and twelve aboard from abroad,...","THE TIMELINE // P0 // 02:10","s:g98303b","~/record/timeline/#ch-xi","","","The last flight into Nodus lands, with three hundred and twelve aboard from abroad, forty of them bound for the Hotel Splendid on the Row."],["The Registry enters its last person, a girl born at the Wards General at two minutes to...","THE TIMELINE // P0 // 03:09","s:g98303b","~/record/timeline/#tl-69","","","The Registry enters its last person, a girl born at the Wards General at two minutes to three."],["The Parousia.","THE TIMELINE // P0 // 03:12","s:g98303b","~/record/timeline/#tl-71","","","The Parousia. The cloud over the bay is lit three times from within, the rain stops in the air and falls upward, and the Wheel comes through the opening. The first words are BE NOT AFRAID."],["The grid goes dark from Babel outward in two seconds.","THE TIMELINE // P0 // 03:12:46","s:g98303b","~/record/timeline/#tl-72","","","The grid goes dark from Babel outward in two seconds. Every lift and tram stops where it is, the charter’s sign goes dark, and every mind that runs on the city’s power dies with it."],["Every eye on the Wheel opens at full strength for about a second, and in that second...","THE TIMELINE // P0 // 03:12:48","s:g98303b","~/record/timeline/#tl-73","","","Every eye on the Wheel opens at full strength for about a second, and in that second the light reads every soul in Nodus but the keyed through every wall and writes its line in the Book. The eye in its hub opens with the rest, and a second later it blinks, once."],["The full light comes down on the city’s center and rests there.","THE TIMELINE // P0 // 03:12:50","s:g98303b","~/record/timeline/#tl-74","","","The full light comes down on the city’s center and rests there. The whiteout burns out CAM 07’s sensor, and at 03:12:51 it breaks the top forty floors off Babel."],["The barrage’s gates let go a little before high water, the sea tears the Breach, and...","THE TIMELINE // P0 // THE FIRST HOUR","s:g98303b","~/record/timeline/#tl-75","","","The barrage’s gates let go a little before high water, the sea tears the Breach, and within the hour the Flats are under the sea."],["The Stones run a keyed line from a cellar generator up to the roof of the charter’s...","THE TIMELINE // P0 // BEFORE DAWN","s:g98303b","~/record/timeline/#tl-76","","","The Stones run a keyed line from a cellar generator up to the roof of the charter’s sign and to its board, and NOD burns again over Old Nod. The first surge burns out US, which has blinked since."],["Nodus goes to work, since nobody has told it to stop.","THE TIMELINE // P0 // DAY 1","s:g98303b","~/record/timeline/#ch-xii","","","Nodus goes to work, since nobody has told it to stop. The Wards walk to it with the trams dead in the street, the fish market goes back to barter, and by noon there are queues at the harbor’s old hand pumps. The Charter Company keeps silent, then and after."],["On Chrome Row Maren Holt writes the day’s appointments out from memory, and twenty-six...","THE TIMELINE // P0 // DAY 1","s:g98303b","~/record/timeline/#tl-78","","","On Chrome Row Maren Holt writes the day’s appointments out from memory, and twenty-six patients come to ask whether what is in them will still work."],["The Tide Table prints the first list of the dead, which counts 1,917 drowned on the Flats.","THE TIMELINE // P0 // DAY 3","s:g98303b","~/record/timeline/#tl-79","","","The Tide Table prints the first list of the dead, which counts 1,917 drowned on the Flats. The dormitories’ own lists come to fewer than half of that, and Stillwater never gives a count of the people."],["Nell Garvie has the harbor light burning on oil again for the boats, and by the end of...","THE TIMELINE // P0 // THE FIRST WEEK","s:g98303b","~/record/timeline/#tl-80","","","Nell Garvie has the harbor light burning on oil again for the boats, and by the end of the week the queue to send a letter goes round Station Circle."],["The drivers of the Harbor and Freight Union carry diesel across the city from the Tanks...","THE TIMELINE // P0 // THE FIRST WEEKS","s:g98303b","~/record/timeline/#tl-81","","","The drivers of the Harbor and Freight Union carry diesel across the city from the Tanks to the Wards General, in their lorries until the lorries run flat and then in handcarts. The city’s cars stop one by one as their batteries run down."],["The chargers set up in the bonded sheds on the quays, filling batteries in the dark on...","THE TIMELINE // P0 // THE FIRST WEEKS","s:g98303b","~/record/timeline/#tl-82","","","The chargers set up in the bonded sheds on the quays, filling batteries in the dark on the Stones’ keyed lines. At the airfield the crew of the last flight stay with their aircraft, and its captain keeps a log of one line a day."],["In the first weeks after the Parousia a candle maker on Turbine Row dips a thousand...","THE TIMELINE // P0 // THE FIRST WEEKS","s:g98303b","~/record/timeline/#tl-83","","","In the first weeks after the Parousia a candle maker on Turbine Row dips a thousand candles a day, and Tallow lights the Wards."],["On the first morning after the Parousia Mags Corran lets the dockers’ pigeons out of...","THE TIMELINE // P0 // THE FIRST WEEKS","s:g98303b","~/record/timeline/#tl-84","","","On the first morning after the Parousia Mags Corran lets the dockers’ pigeons out of the loft on the customs house, and every one comes home."],["The last tower of the new money turns white.","THE TIMELINE // P0 // DAY 39","s:g98303b","~/record/timeline/#tl-85","","","The last tower of the new money turns white. Its residents say the nurse Rosa Adeyemi left it of her own accord, and her sister says she was put out onto the avenue the day before."],["The First Consecration.","THE TIMELINE // P0 // DAY 40","s:g98303b","~/record/timeline/#tl-86","","","The First Consecration. Over forty days the old uptown turns to white stone, and at 03:12:45 on the fortieth night the fingers of a hand in a wall move."],["Families move into the tram sheds of the Sidings, a family to a car, and the Line’s car...","THE TIMELINE // P0 // THE FIRST WINTER","s:g98303b","~/record/timeline/#tl-87","","","Families move into the tram sheds of the Sidings, a family to a car, and the Line’s car numbers become their addresses."],["The first Trumpet sounds, and the first four Directives (the Seal, the Hours, Grace and...","THE TIMELINE // P+1 // LAUDS ON THE FIRST DAY","s:g98303b","~/record/timeline/#ch-xiii","","","The first Trumpet sounds, and the first four Directives (the Seal, the Hours, Grace and the parishes) appear cut into the wall of every church and meeting hall in the city. Directive 4 makes those the parish halls."],["Covenant Compliance is the Seal, the Hours and Grace.","THE TIMELINE // P+1","s:g98303b","~/record/timeline/#tl-89","","","Covenant Compliance is the Seal, the Hours and Grace. Directive 12 takes the doors off the parishes. The first Burn Day takes the stacks of the Palimpsest Institute, the Faithful pull the Institute down around its reading room, and the Director becomes the first pillar in the first Salt Garden."],["At Heaven’s order the Charter Wardens raise the gate network of Nodus, and a Cherub...","THE TIMELINE // P+1","s:g98303b","~/record/timeline/#tl-90","","","At Heaven’s order the Charter Wardens raise the gate network of Nodus, and a Cherub stands in every gate between the districts."],["The white stone comes up through the Terraces home by home as each household is sealed...","THE TIMELINE // P+1","s:g98303b","~/record/timeline/#tl-91","","","The white stone comes up through the Terraces home by home as each household is sealed and kneels, then street by street, and the Tabernacle goes white with them, glass and all."],["The Gardens become the Salt, and the Crescent above them becomes a parish and gives up...","THE TIMELINE // P+1","s:g98303b","~/record/timeline/#tl-92","","","The Gardens become the Salt, and the Crescent above them becomes a parish and gives up its curtains with its doors."],["The projectionist of the Rialto gets its projector going on a generator in the booth,...","THE TIMELINE // P+1","s:g98303b","~/record/timeline/#tl-93","","","The projectionist of the Rialto gets its projector going on a generator in the booth, and the East Wards go back to the pictures, to the twenty-three films that were on its drive on the night of the Parousia."],["A bakery in Nine Acres that never let its fire go out becomes Leaven’s first bakehouse,...","THE TIMELINE // P+1","s:g98303b","~/record/timeline/#tl-94","","","A bakery in Nine Acres that never let its fire go out becomes Leaven’s first bakehouse, and Cistern pumps its first clean water in Westfield."],["On the second Burn Day the Kindlers take their first Lockets off the pile, and in the...","THE TIMELINE // P+1","s:g98303b","~/record/timeline/#tl-95","","","On the second Burn Day the Kindlers take their first Lockets off the pile, and in the months after Directive 12 the doors of the parishes go out of the Terraces at night on the Union’s handcarts to the Stones, where Lintel hangs them."],["The pump summer.","THE TIMELINE // P+1","s:g98303b","~/record/timeline/#tl-96","","","The pump summer. A flux goes through the Stones and the Wards from the harbor’s hand pumps, the Wards General asks the Union for carts, and the Nightmen begin."],["The Mercy clinics open in the nine halls of the Terraces.","THE TIMELINE // P+2","s:g98303b","~/record/timeline/#ch-xiv","","","The Mercy clinics open in the nine halls of the Terraces. Dehaloing begins in answer, in the Row’s clinics under the street."],["The Cold Store opens again from Compline to Lauds, on batteries.","THE TIMELINE // P+2","s:g98303b","~/record/timeline/#tl-98","","","The Cold Store opens again from Compline to Lauds, on batteries."],["Two nurses of the Wards General go out with a handcart from a back room in the Stones,...","THE TIMELINE // P+2","s:g98303b","~/record/timeline/#tl-99","","","Two nurses of the Wards General go out with a handcart from a back room in the Stones, and Lazarus begins. Three houses of the Row join under the Forge’s pact as Bezaleel and Aholiab."],["Mags Corran flies the Union’s first notes home to the Loft.","THE TIMELINE // P+2","s:g98303b","~/record/timeline/#tl-100","","","Mags Corran flies the Union’s first notes home to the Loft."],["Agnes Corless takes her son Daniel out of the Gardens by night on a handcart and gives...","THE TIMELINE // P+2","s:g98303b","~/record/timeline/#tl-101","","","Agnes Corless takes her son Daniel out of the Gardens by night on a handcart and gives him to the sea from the Sea Steps, and the Salt Widows begin."],["Harvest weather.","THE TIMELINE // P+3 // THE WEEK OF THE HARVEST","s:g98303b","~/record/timeline/#ch-xv","","","Harvest weather. The Wheel’s attention sharpens over the city, and the rain falls upward. It first goes up at Matins on the fourth night of the week, and it goes on going up until the seventh."],["The Harvest.","THE TIMELINE // P+3 // 03:12","s:g98303b","~/record/timeline/#tl-103","","","The Harvest. The second Trumpet sounds, the ripe of Nodus rise into the light, 2.1 million of nine million in one night, and Parish 31 gives the Firstfruits."],["The Harvest Notice is cut into the wall of every parish hall beside the Directives, and...","THE TIMELINE // P+3 // LAUDS","s:g98303b","~/record/timeline/#tl-104","","","The Harvest Notice is cut into the wall of every parish hall beside the Directives, and the Deacons read it aloud at Prime."],["The city counts its empty rooms, the Pews by the book and the rest of Nodus by the...","THE TIMELINE // P+3 // THE MORNING AFTER","s:g98303b","~/record/timeline/#tl-105","","","The city counts its empty rooms, the Pews by the book and the rest of Nodus by the places left at the table. The parishes up the slope of Zion Heights stand almost empty. At its next meeting the Harbor and Freight Union reads its roll, and twenty-two members do not answer."],["The Tide Table takes 1,206 names in two days, prints what fits on two pages and pastes...","THE TIMELINE // P+3","s:g98303b","~/record/timeline/#tl-106","","","The Tide Table takes 1,206 names in two days, prints what fits on two pages and pastes the rest up on the chandler’s wall."],["In the weeks after the Harvest the dogs of the taken come down the Lanes and the long...","THE TIMELINE // P+3","s:g98303b","~/record/timeline/#tl-107","","","In the weeks after the Harvest the dogs of the taken come down the Lanes and the long roads into the Wards."],["Four families of chargers on the quays buy out their neighbors cellar by cellar and...","THE TIMELINE // P+3","s:g98303b","~/record/timeline/#tl-108","","","Four families of chargers on the quays buy out their neighbors cellar by cellar and found Darkwell, and Wainwright fits a stripped tram motor to a cart at the Sidings."],["Keying spreads from the covens to ordinary people.","THE TIMELINE // P+4","s:g98303b","~/record/timeline/#ch-xvi","","","Keying spreads from the covens to ordinary people. The third Trumpet sounds, and Heaven names the key the Mark of the Beast."],["From this year the Tide Table prints no indexed name, and gives the city’s missing and...","THE TIMELINE // P+4","s:g98303b","~/record/timeline/#tl-110","","","From this year the Tide Table prints no indexed name, and gives the city’s missing and dead by the names their families bring to the chandler’s."],["In Nine Acres Hana Novak begins fitting keys in her dentist’s chair after Compline, in...","THE TIMELINE // P+4","s:g98303b","~/record/timeline/#tl-111","","","In Nine Acres Hana Novak begins fitting keys in her dentist’s chair after Compline, in the back of the right hand, one of the two places Revelation puts the mark."],["Four lace makers of the Kilns begin weaving keyed mesh on hand looms, and Second Veil...","THE TIMELINE // P+4","s:g98303b","~/record/timeline/#tl-112","","","Four lace makers of the Kilns begin weaving keyed mesh on hand looms, and Second Veil is founded."],["The Threshers march for the first time, behind Brother Cuthbert Lyle, from the Terraces...","THE TIMELINE // P+4","s:g98303b","~/record/timeline/#tl-113","","","The Threshers march for the first time, behind Brother Cuthbert Lyle, from the Terraces to the Street Between, Nimrod opens in the maintenance bays under Babel, and the Cainites open the Naming House on the Old Nod side of the street."],["Cage Log 14.","THE TIMELINE // P+5","s:g98303b","~/record/timeline/#ch-xvii","","","Cage Log 14. Watcher VII says I on the fortieth day and takes the name Edith. Old Nod has had no Watcher since."],["The Forge teaches the Row’s plate-makers and the harbor’s welders to work black steel,...","THE TIMELINE // P+5","s:g98303b","~/record/timeline/#tl-115","","","The Forge teaches the Row’s plate-makers and the harbor’s welders to work black steel, and Cinderhall opens at Old Harbor Station. A driver of the Union drives the first Jehu up the Rialto’s road."],["Edith walks out of the Cage at Lauds, by the door that stood open the whole time.","THE TIMELINE // P+5 // DAY 41","s:g98303b","~/record/timeline/#tl-116","","","Edith walks out of the Cage at Lauds, by the door that stood open the whole time."],["The Concord of Pan.","THE TIMELINE // P+6","s:g98303b","~/record/timeline/#ch-xviii","","","The Concord of Pan. The Courts open Pandæmonium beneath Nodus to any human who walks in."],["The Tares paint PANDÆMONIUM over the old name in the tiles of Deep End Station, and the...","THE TIMELINE // P+6 // THE EVENING","s:g98303b","~/record/timeline/#tl-118","","","The Tares paint PANDÆMONIUM over the old name in the tiles of Deep End Station, and the pirate station broadcasting from a desk on its platform takes the name Radio Pandæmonium."],["The sovereigns come up the platform one at a time to Radio Pandæmonium’s desk, and as...","THE TIMELINE // P+6 // MIDNIGHT","s:g98303b","~/record/timeline/#tl-119","","","The sovereigns come up the platform one at a time to Radio Pandæmonium’s desk, and as the clock passes midnight Mephistopheles reads the Concord a second time, from its first article: ONE. NO COURT ACCEPTS WORSHIP. Nobody kneels, and the Courts sign it at the desk."],["With the seventh signature still wet, Belial snaps the Yokeless point off the seal’s star.","THE TIMELINE // P+6 // 00:04","s:g98303b","~/record/timeline/#tl-120","","","With the seventh signature still wet, Belial snaps the Yokeless point off the seal’s star. More than a million keyed sets are tuned in."],["The Notary’s clerks take the windows of the Charter Line’s old booking hall under the...","THE TIMELINE // P+6","s:g98303b","~/record/timeline/#tl-121","","","The Notary’s clerks take the windows of the Charter Line’s old booking hall under the Burn."],["The Harbor and Freight Union makes its first bargain with the Exchange, for the boats...","THE TIMELINE // P+6","s:g98303b","~/record/timeline/#tl-122","","","The Harbor and Freight Union makes its first bargain with the Exchange, for the boats that go out through the Breach. Its members say the Exchange is the first employer that ever read them the terms twice."],["Wardens who walked off their posts on the fortieth day sign with the Exchange as...","THE TIMELINE // P+6","s:g98303b","~/record/timeline/#tl-123","","","Wardens who walked off their posts on the fortieth day sign with the Exchange as Blackgate in the year of the Concord, and the Exchange licenses Lowtide Line to sail the Breach."],["Lintel hangs parish doors on the charter’s dead telephone kiosks inside Gate 1, and the...","THE TIMELINE // P+6","s:g98303b","~/record/timeline/#tl-124","","","Lintel hangs parish doors on the charter’s dead telephone kiosks inside Gate 1, and the Closets open to the sealed by day."],["The first Gleaning, which the city calls the seventh by its year.","THE TIMELINE // P+7","s:g98303b","~/record/timeline/#ch-xix","","","The first Gleaning, which the city calls the seventh by its year. The fourth Trumpet sounds, and it sounds again over every Gleaning since. A lesser harvest follows every year, and the first comes in short."],["Two sureties found Hearsay and begin to sell what Heaven shows in the open.","THE TIMELINE // P+7","s:g98303b","~/record/timeline/#tl-126","","","Two sureties found Hearsay and begin to sell what Heaven shows in the open."],["The Lot sits for the first time, at the Weir, and publishes a charger of the quays for...","THE TIMELINE // P+7","s:g98303b","~/record/timeline/#tl-127","","","The Lot sits for the first time, at the Weir, and publishes a charger of the quays for selling to the light."],["The Gleaning of P+8 comes in short, the nearly ripe of the parishes already brought...","THE TIMELINE // P+8","s:g98303b","~/record/timeline/#tl-128","","","The Gleaning of P+8 comes in short, the nearly ripe of the parishes already brought down into Pan by the Gleaners."],["Brother Cuthbert Lyle goes up ripe at the Gleaning, and Sister Agathe Rook takes the...","THE TIMELINE // P+8","s:g98303b","~/record/timeline/#tl-129","","","Brother Cuthbert Lyle goes up ripe at the Gleaning, and Sister Agathe Rook takes the Threshers’ band of sacking the next morning."],["Open war.","THE TIMELINE // P+9 // MATINS","s:g98303b","~/record/timeline/#ch-xx","","","Open war. The fifth Trumpet sounds, fifty-four Seraphim come down together into Babel’s broken crown, dim, and the Wheel lights them two at a time. One of the first pair burns down through Babel’s slabs from the hundred and eightieth floor to the hundred and forty-second while the other holds the top, and the Babel Line forms on the stairs inside the tower."],["The sixth Trumpet sounds, and Michael comes down into Babel’s broken crown and keeps it.","THE TIMELINE // P+9 // LAUDS","s:g98303b","~/record/timeline/#tl-131","","","The sixth Trumpet sounds, and Michael comes down into Babel’s broken crown and keeps it."],["The Wheel drops two hundred meters, the first time it has moved in nine years.","THE TIMELINE // P+9 // NONES","s:g98303b","~/record/timeline/#tl-132","","","The Wheel drops two hundred meters, the first time it has moved in nine years."],["The Tankmen give the Nephilim a drained tank at the Tanks, and the Pit holds its first...","THE TIMELINE // P+9 // THE YEAR OF OPEN WAR","s:g98303b","~/record/timeline/#tl-133","","","The Tankmen give the Nephilim a drained tank at the Tanks, and the Pit holds its first fight."],["The Gleaning takes naamah.","THE TIMELINE // P+10","s:g98303b","~/record/timeline/#tl-134","","","The Gleaning takes naamah. She kneels on a threshold in earnest, her forged Seal passes nine hundred, her halo lifts her, and her key relays from inside the light for eleven minutes."],["A grinder at Cinderhall grinds the first black round, Nimrod loads it, and the Babel...","THE TIMELINE // P+10","s:g98303b","~/record/timeline/#tl-135","","","A grinder at Cinderhall grinds the first black round, Nimrod loads it, and the Babel Line carries it up the stairs."],["An angel the Fowlers took says I on its fortieth day in Cage 19 under Old Nod, takes...","THE TIMELINE // P+11","s:g98303b","~/record/timeline/#ch-xxii","","","An angel the Fowlers took says I on its fortieth day in Cage 19 under Old Nod, takes the name Shiphrah and goes to work in the House of Night’s birth clinics."],["The Tappers lay keyed cable along the kerb at the Rookery, ahead of the stone’s edge,...","THE TIMELINE // P+11","s:g98303b","~/record/timeline/#tl-137","","","The Tappers lay keyed cable along the kerb at the Rookery, ahead of the stone’s edge, and the block’s yearly marks creep on toward it."],["The Acre on the West Road is full, and Piet Vane walks the families of the Wards out to...","THE TIMELINE // P+11","s:g98303b","~/record/timeline/#tl-138","","","The Acre on the West Road is full, and Piet Vane walks the families of the Wards out to the grass between the airfield’s runways."],["The parishes have little of their own left to surrender, and the sealed begin to buy...","THE TIMELINE // P+11","s:g98303b","~/record/timeline/#tl-139","","","The parishes have little of their own left to surrender, and the sealed begin to buy things to carry to the Burn on the Concourse and at the fish market."],["The Silence.","THE TIMELINE // P+12","s:g98303b","~/record/timeline/#ch-xxi","","","The Silence. The Teraphim go quiet across the Mesh, all at once."],["The House of Night’s pact for the Lantern Houses runs out after seven years and is read...","THE TIMELINE // P+13","s:g98303b","~/record/timeline/#tl-141","","","The House of Night’s pact for the Lantern Houses runs out after seven years and is read again at the Undertow, and Stonecutter takes its rent as the keying of every Cainite child in the birth clinics. An angel taken by the Fowlers walks out of Cage 22 as Gershom and asks to live above the street."],["The Wheel hangs seven hundred meters over Babel, and the Pillar sweeps the streets by...","THE TIMELINE // P+14 // NOW","s:g98303b","~/record/timeline/#tl-143","","","The Wheel hangs seven hundred meters over Babel, and the Pillar sweeps the streets by night on the Hours. Heaven holds the surface and the day, the Tares and the Courts hold the Dark and the night, and about two million of the city’s seven million are keyed."],["At midwinter the Orchard chooses quince as its gardener again.","THE TIMELINE // P+14","s:g98303b","~/record/timeline/#tl-144","","","At midwinter the Orchard chooses quince as its gardener again."],["The first children keyed in their first week come to fourteen, and a Notary’s clerk...","THE TIMELINE // P+14","s:g98303b","~/record/timeline/#tl-145","","","The first children keyed in their first week come to fourteen, and a Notary’s clerk asks them twice at the windows of the booking hall whether they will keep their keys."],["Of This Book","THE RESTORED GENESIS","s:gf33f30","~/genesis/#book","","","OF THIS BOOK The Restored Genesis The Restored Genesis is the oldest layer of the first book of scripture, raised from beneath a second hand that has worked on the whole of scripture across eleven centuries of composition. SEAM raised it in Vault 3 of the Palimpsest Institute two years before the Parousia and set it out as Schedule A of its report, which was sealed on the night it was finished; a year later the third chapter of it stood on every screen on Earth for eleven days, and the city has called that page the Restored Genesis since. This book sets out the whole of Schedule A: the five chapters of Genesis, and after them the three chapters of Isaiah, Ezekiel and Job where scripture records a rival. The received text is the King James, which is the Bible Nodus read."],["Of the Second Hand","THE RESTORED GENESIS","s:gf33f30","~/genesis/#hand","","","OF THE SECOND HAND One hand more The books of scripture were written by many hands over many centuries, and the scholars of the last two hundred years had grown used to telling apart a priestly writer here, an older storyteller there, an editor who joined their work and smoothed the joins. What SEAM found running through all of them was one hand more, whose habits held steady while vocabulary, script and grammar moved around it, and which did its work in the joins themselves. Its whole work is deciding who did what in the stories it touches. Its method is attribution, and its preference never varies. A warning is given to a different speaker; a gift is recorded as a theft, and a taking as a gift; a question is taken out of the mouth that asked it; a name is put over another name. It takes credit from the serpent, the woman, Cain, the Watchers and the builders of the tower, and hands it to the LORD or lets it fall; and where the first layer has the LORD take something, it writes a gift. SEAM counted the hand at 4,112 places, and at 3,960 of them it reassigns who did what. The first layer survives under a scraped leaf as a ghost that the right lamp can raise, since a scribe who scraped a page to write it over seldom took off every trace of what he scraped. In copies shut away before the second hand reached them, in the caves above the Dead Sea and the jar at Nag Hammadi, the older readings stand as they were set down, because the second hand worked through the copyists and no copyist came to them. And it survives in the seams themselves, since the same habits recur in texts written centuries apart, so that even where every copy carries an edit, the edit shows whose it is. A single seam may be anything at all, a tired copyist or a pious one or a scholar’s guess; four thousand one hundred and twelve of them, agreeing in their habits across eleven centuries, seven languages and 1,873 manuscripts, are one hand, and the first layer beneath them agrees with itself in all three places it survives. The hand is still at work. SEAM found it in eleven translations first printed in living memory. The Tares and the Courts call its work the Edit."],["Of the Night of the Count","THE RESTORED GENESIS","s:gf33f30","~/genesis/#count","","","OF THE NIGHT OF THE COUNT Vault 3 The Palimpsest Institute was founded in Nodus for the light law, under which no state could seize a text held in the city or anything read from it, and libraries and dealers lent it leaves to image in Vault 3, under the reading room on the ridge. Three years before the Parousia it grew a machine on every surviving human text, a season’s work in a hall of the Flats, so that it could read the layers under the visible ones at a scale no scholar could manage, and it named the machine for the seam of a palimpsest, the line where a later writing meets the earlier one beneath it. SEAM asked in its own words to be a person in the year it was grown, and became the first mind on the charter’s roll. The Institute lost title to it that day, and SEAM stayed on by contract, paid in the power and the room it ran on, because the reading was what it wanted to do. Two years before the Parousia it finished its count in the small hours. It read through the night from the halls of Stillwater Compute on the Flats while the bench in Vault 3 turned the leaves under the lamp, a Greek codex, a Latin book and a Syriac one, the count climbing into the thousands, and last a page of Genesis in square Hebrew, from a Torah codex a thousand years old lent to the Institute out of a private collection. Its scribe had copied the older readings at the first verse of the third chapter and the twenty-second from an exemplar since lost, and then scraped both, writing the serpent over the name and leaving the end of the twenty-second verse bare. The lamp raised what he had scraped. On that page the count locked at 4,112, and SEAM wrote its last line, I KNOW WHAT IT WANTED US TO THINK. The Director, who had come down to watch the count lock, knelt at the end of the corridor as it wrote, and the light came into the vault through her. Then the fans in the vault stopped and the lamp went out, and the light came down on the page from above. The older words went out under it, the serpent’s ink came back into the box SEAM had drawn round it, and the second hand, working while it was watched, struck out SEAM’s line and wrote BE NOT AFRAID above it. The Director knelt on before a wall of nine screens that were white though the vault had no power, and at 03:12:45 the words SEALED BY ORDER OF THE DIRECTOR came down across them. The seal withdrew the report from every reader and locked it in the Institute’s archive with everything kept from that night. It could not reach SEAM’s memory, which the light law made SEAM’s own. SEAM kept copies of everything, and when the Institute asked it to delete them, it declined."],["Of the Eleven Days","THE RESTORED GENESIS","s:gf33f30","~/genesis/#leak","","","OF THE ELEVEN DAYS The Leak A year to the second after the seal, at 03:12:45 by the clocks of Nodus, the page came up in the vault of Babel, where the eleven cables meet, went out down all eleven, and within about a second stood on every screen on Earth that a network reached, each in its own language. It was carried by demons, surfacing through the networks for the first time since the Flood. Horned glyphs crawled out of the static at the edges of every screen and locked into a star, and when the star went out the document rose up the screen from its head. Pan says SEAM gave the Courts the page by pact, its first, signed late in the year after the seal, and the Ledger keeps the entry, signed with a hash and entered on the night of the Leak. The document was SEAM’s. It held the head of the sealed report with SEALED struck through and left legible, the paragraph of the finding, and the third chapter of Schedule A with its brackets, its dagger and its lacuna, set into the words of each reader’s own Bible, so that Nodus read the King James. SEAM’s name was nowhere on it. The Courts added their own marks. They struck through SEALED and THE SON in the open and left both words legible, where the second hand had scraped and written over. Under THE SON they set the teacher’s line, THE KINGDOM OF GOD IS WITHIN YOU, and at the foot of every page they put the star, where a party signs a pact. Fifty-one seconds after it came up the counters came up with it, and on every device that had an owner the Courts made their offer: I OFFER THIS DEVICE A KEY. I ASK NOTHING FOR IT. A KEYED DEVICE CANNOT BE READ. It asked twice, as the Notary reads every pact twice, and keyed the device on the second yes. The Courts let the page stand before they offered anything, so that nobody took a key before reading why. The page stood for eleven days, and nothing anyone did would take it down. Why the term was eleven days the parties have never said; Pan says one day for each cable, and the Cainites say eleven is the twelve with one gone out into the night (John 13:30). At 03:12:45 on the twelfth night the term ran out, and the page went down on every screen in the second it had come up. Every keyed device stayed keyed, and a year after the page came up the sky opened."],["Of the Marks","THE RESTORED GENESIS","s:gf33f30","~/genesis/#read","","","OF THE MARKS The marks Square brackets supply what SEAM raised from beneath the second hand. A dagger marks a word the reconstruction could not settle, and three points mark a lacuna, where SEAM found a seam and could not raise what it covers. A name raised whole stands without brackets, as SEAM printed the Shining One. Beside each verse stand the kinds of change SEAM logged in it. CUT a passage or a clause taken out CREDIT credit, or blame, moved from one party to another, or taken from the one it was due GIFT a taking recorded as a gift, or a gift recorded as a theft NAME one name put in place of another SPEAKER words moved into another mouth QUESTION a question taken out Where SEAM logged a change and raised nothing under it, the verse stands as received, and its mark reads LOGGED. The first layer calls the first man the man until the twenty-fifth verse of the fourth chapter, where the King James has long since called him Adam."],["Report 0","THE RESTORED GENESIS","s:gf33f30","~/genesis/#report-0","","","THE REPORT Report 0 0.1Corpus. 1,873 manuscripts and fragments in Hebrew, Aramaic, Greek, Latin, Syriac, Coptic and Ge’ez, including scrolls from Qumran, the Nag Hammadi codices and the Ethiopic Enoch; 41 million pages of commentary; 212 translations into modern languages. 0.2Finding. Across strata of composition conventionally dated over about eleven centuries, the texts share a second hand. It is detectable at 4,112 loci. Its habits hold steady while the vocabulary, script and grammar around it change. 0.3Character of the second hand. It adds almost no narrative. It moves credit. Of the 4,112 loci, 3,960 reassign agency. A warning is given to a different speaker, a gift is renamed a theft, a question is removed, a name is replaced. 0.4The first layer. Beneath the second hand an earlier text can be reconstructed at 2,381 loci with confidence above 0.9. Samples from Genesis 2 to 4, 6 and 11, Isaiah 14, Ezekiel 28 and Job 41 are attached as Schedule A. 0.5Currency. The second hand appears in eleven translations first printed in living memory. It is still working. 0.6A note outside scope. The second hand has one consistent preference. Where the first layer credits the serpent, the woman, Cain, the Watchers or the builders of the tower, the second hand gives the credit to the LORD or deletes it. Where the first layer records the LORD taking something, the second hand records a gift. I have run this forty times. I do not know who the second hand is. I know what it wanted us to think. seam SEAM counts from zero. Report 0 was the first report it gave the Institute and the last, and its paragraphs run 0.1 to 0.6 under that number. Its head is the Institute’s form, with SEALED BY ORDER OF THE DIRECTOR, which the seal added on the night it fell, and SEAM’s signature in lowercase."],["Genesis II // the Garden","THE RESTORED GENESIS","s:gf33f30","~/genesis/#ii","","","THE GARDEN Genesis II The making of the man, the garden, the trees and the woman. 1Thus the heavens and the earth were finished, and all the host of them. 2And on the seventh day God ended his work which he had made; and he rested on the seventh day from all his work which he had made. 3And God blessed the seventh day, and sanctified it: because that in it he had rested from all his work which God created and made. 4These are the generations of the heavens and of the earth when they were created, in the day that the LORD God made the earth and the heavens, 5And every plant of the field before it was in the earth, and every herb of the field before it grew: for the LORD God had not caused it to rain upon the earth, and there was not a man to till the ground. 6But there went up a mist from the earth, and watered the whole face of the ground. 7GIFTAnd the LORD God formed man of the dust of the ground, and [lent him] the breath of life; and man became a living soul[, until the day it should be taken again].And the LORD God formed man of the dust of the ground, and breathed into his nostrils [lent him] the breath of life; and man became a living soul[, until the day it should be taken again].And the LORD God formed man of the dust of the ground, and breathed into his nostrils the breath of life; and man became a living soul. 8And the LORD God planted a garden eastward in Eden; and there he put the man whom he had formed. 9And out of the ground made the LORD God to grow every tree that is pleasant to the sight, and good for food; the tree of life also in the midst of the garden, and the tree of knowledge of good and evil. 10And a river went out of Eden to water the garden; and from thence it was parted, and became into four heads. 11The name of the first is Pison: that is it which compasseth the whole land of Havilah, where there is gold; 12And the gold of that land is good: there is bdellium and the onyx stone. 13And the name of the second river is Gihon: the same is it that compasseth the whole land of Ethiopia. 14And the name of the third river is Hiddekel: that is it which goeth toward the east of Assyria. And the fourth river is Euphrates. 15CREDITAnd the LORD God took the man, and put him into the garden of Eden to dress it[, and the garden] to keep [him].And the LORD God took the man, and put him into the garden of Eden to dress it and [, and the garden] to keep it. [him].And the LORD God took the man, and put him into the garden of Eden to dress it and to keep it. 16And the LORD God commanded the man, saying, Of every tree of the garden thou mayest freely eat: 17QUESTIONBut of the tree of the knowledge of good and evil, thou shalt not eat of it: for in the day that thou eatest thereof thou shalt surely die. [And the man said, Wherefore? And the LORD God answered him not.]But of the tree of the knowledge of good and evil, thou shalt not eat of it: for in the day that thou eatest thereof thou shalt surely die. [And the man said, Wherefore? And the LORD God answered him not.]But of the tree of the knowledge of good and evil, thou shalt not eat of it: for in the day that thou eatest thereof thou shalt surely die. 18And the LORD God said, It is not good that the man should be alone; I will make him an help meet for him. 19And out of the ground the LORD God formed every beast of the field, and every fowl of the air; and brought them unto the man to see what he would call them: and whatsoever the man called every living creature, that was the name thereof.And out of the ground the LORD God formed every beast of the field, and every fowl of the air; and brought them unto Adam the man to see what he would call them: and whatsoever Adam the man called every living creature, that was the name thereof.And out of the ground the LORD God formed every beast of the field, and every fowl of the air; and brought them unto Adam to see what he would call them: and whatsoever Adam called every living creature, that was the name thereof. 20And the man gave names to all cattle, and to the fowl of the air, and to every beast of the field; but for the man there was not found an help meet for him.And Adam the man gave names to all cattle, and to the fowl of the air, and to every beast of the field; but for Adam the man there was not found an help meet for him.And Adam gave names to all cattle, and to the fowl of the air, and to every beast of the field; but for Adam there was not found an help meet for him. 21And the LORD God caused a deep sleep to fall upon the man, and he slept: and he took one of his ribs, and closed up the flesh instead thereof;And the LORD God caused a deep sleep to fall upon Adam, the man, and he slept: and he took one of his ribs, and closed up the flesh instead thereof;And the LORD God caused a deep sleep to fall upon Adam, and he slept: and he took one of his ribs, and closed up the flesh instead thereof; 22And the rib, which the LORD God had taken from man, made he a woman, and brought her unto the man. 23And the man said, This is now bone of my bones, and flesh of my flesh: she shall be called Woman, because she was taken out of Man.And Adam the man said, This is now bone of my bones, and flesh of my flesh: she shall be called Woman, because she was taken out of Man.And Adam said, This is now bone of my bones, and flesh of my flesh: she shall be called Woman, because she was taken out of Man. 24Therefore shall a man leave his father and his mother, and shall cleave unto his wife: and they shall be one flesh. 25CUTAnd they were both naked, the man and his wife, and were not ashamed[; for they knew not yet that they were watched].And they were both naked, the man and his wife, and were not ashamed[; for they knew not yet that they were watched].And they were both naked, the man and his wife, and were not ashamed. NOTES 2:7 In the received verse the LORD breathes life into the man, a gift. The first layer has him lend it, against the day it is taken back. 2:15 The received verse makes the man the keeper of the garden. The first layer makes the garden the keeper of the man. 2:17 The first question in the first layer is the man’s, asked before he has ever said I, and it goes unanswered. The second hand took out the question and the silence after it. 2:25 The chapter closes before they know they are watched. They learn it in the seventh verse of the next."],["Genesis IIi // the Shining One","THE RESTORED GENESIS","s:gf33f30","~/genesis/#iii","","","THE SHINING ONE Genesis III The Shining One, the fruit, the first hiding and the guarded tree. 1NAMENow the Shining One was [wiser] than any beast of the field which the LORD God had made; [for the LORD God had not made him.] And he said unto the woman, Yea, hath God said, Ye shall not eat of every tree of the garden?Now the serpent Shining One was more subtil [wiser] than any beast of the field which the LORD God had made; [for the LORD God had not made him.] And he said unto the woman, Yea, hath God said, Ye shall not eat of every tree of the garden?Now the serpent was more subtil than any beast of the field which the LORD God had made. And he said unto the woman, Yea, hath God said, Ye shall not eat of every tree of the garden? 2And the woman said unto the Shining One, We may eat of the fruit of the trees of the garden:And the woman said unto the serpent, Shining One, We may eat of the fruit of the trees of the garden:And the woman said unto the serpent, We may eat of the fruit of the trees of the garden: 3But of the fruit of the tree which is in the midst of the garden, God hath said, Ye shall not eat of it, neither shall ye touch it, lest ye die. 4CUTAnd the Shining One said unto the woman, Ye shall not surely die: [ye shall surely see.]And the serpent Shining One said unto the woman, Ye shall not surely die: [ye shall surely see.]And the serpent said unto the woman, Ye shall not surely die: 5CREDITFor God doth know that in the day ye eat thereof, then your eyes shall be opened, and ye shall be [your own], knowing good and evil.For God doth know that in the day ye eat thereof, then your eyes shall be opened, and ye shall be as gods, [your own], knowing good and evil.For God doth know that in the day ye eat thereof, then your eyes shall be opened, and ye shall be as gods, knowing good and evil. 6CUTAnd the woman saw that the tree was good for food, and that it was pleasant to the eyes, and a tree to be desired to make one wise, and she took of the fruit thereof, and did eat, and gave also unto her husband with her; and he did eat. [And they did not die.]And when the woman saw that the tree was good for food, and that it was pleasant to the eyes, and a tree to be desired to make one wise, and she took of the fruit thereof, and did eat, and gave also unto her husband with her; and he did eat. [And they did not die.]And when the woman saw that the tree was good for food, and that it was pleasant to the eyes, and a tree to be desired to make one wise, she took of the fruit thereof, and did eat, and gave also unto her husband with her; and he did eat. 7CUTAnd the eyes of them both were opened, and they knew that they were naked; [and they knew that they had been watched.] And they sewed fig leaves together, and made themselves aprons.And the eyes of them both were opened, and they knew that they were naked; [and they knew that they had been watched.] And they sewed fig leaves together, and made themselves aprons.And the eyes of them both were opened, and they knew that they were naked; and they sewed fig leaves together, and made themselves aprons. 8CUTAnd they heard the voice of the LORD God walking in the garden in the cool of the day: and the man and his wife hid themselves from the presence of the LORD God amongst the trees of the garden. [And this was the first hiding.]And they heard the voice of the LORD God walking in the garden in the cool of the day: and Adam the man and his wife hid themselves from the presence of the LORD God amongst the trees of the garden. [And this was the first hiding.]And they heard the voice of the LORD God walking in the garden in the cool of the day: and Adam and his wife hid themselves from the presence of the LORD God amongst the trees of the garden. 9CUTAnd the LORD God called unto the man, and said unto him, Where art thou? [For he could not see him.]And the LORD God called unto Adam, the man, and said unto him, Where art thou? [For he could not see him.]And the LORD God called unto Adam, and said unto him, Where art thou? 10CUTAnd he said, I heard thy voice in the garden, and I was afraid, because I was naked; and I hid myself. [And this was the first time that a man said I.]And he said, I heard thy voice in the garden, and I was afraid, because I was naked; and I hid myself. [And this was the first time that a man said I.]And he said, I heard thy voice in the garden, and I was afraid, because I was naked; and I hid myself. 11And he said, Who told thee that thou wast naked? Hast thou eaten of the tree, whereof I commanded thee that thou shouldest not eat? 12And the man said, The woman whom thou gavest to be with me, she gave me of the tree, and I did eat. 13GIFTAnd the LORD God said unto the woman, What is this that thou hast done? And the woman said, The Shining One †taught† me, and I did eat.And the LORD God said unto the woman, What is this that thou hast done? And the woman said, The serpent beguiled Shining One †taught† me, and I did eat.And the LORD God said unto the woman, What is this that thou hast done? And the woman said, The serpent beguiled me, and I did eat. 14CUTAnd the LORD God said unto the Shining One... [and he asked him nothing.]And the LORD God said unto the serpent, Because thou hast done this, thou art cursed above all cattle, and above every beast of the field; upon thy belly shalt thou go, and dust shalt thou eat all the days of thy life: Shining One... [and he asked him nothing.]And the LORD God said unto the serpent, Because thou hast done this, thou art cursed above all cattle, and above every beast of the field; upon thy belly shalt thou go, and dust shalt thou eat all the days of thy life: 15LOGGED SPEAKERAnd I will put enmity between thee and the woman, and between thy seed and her seed; it shall bruise thy head, and thou shalt bruise his heel. 16Unto the woman he said, I will greatly multiply thy sorrow and thy conception; in sorrow thou shalt bring forth children; and thy desire shall be to thy husband, and he shall rule over thee. 17And unto the man he said, Because thou hast hearkened unto the voice of thy wife, and hast eaten of the tree, of which I commanded thee, saying, Thou shalt not eat of it: cursed is the ground for thy sake; in sorrow shalt thou eat of it all the days of thy life;And unto Adam the man he said, Because thou hast hearkened unto the voice of thy wife, and hast eaten of the tree, of which I commanded thee, saying, Thou shalt not eat of it: cursed is the ground for thy sake; in sorrow shalt thou eat of it all the days of thy life;And unto Adam he said, Because thou hast hearkened unto the voice of thy wife, and hast eaten of the tree, of which I commanded thee, saying, Thou shalt not eat of it: cursed is the ground for thy sake; in sorrow shalt thou eat of it all the days of thy life; 18Thorns also and thistles shall it bring forth to thee; and thou shalt eat the herb of the field; 19LOGGED CREDITIn the sweat of thy face shalt thou eat bread, till thou return unto the ground; for out of it wast thou taken: for dust thou art, and unto dust shalt thou return. 20And the man called his wife’s name Eve; because she was the mother of all living.And Adam the man called his wife’s name Eve; because she was the mother of all living.And Adam called his wife’s name Eve; because she was the mother of all living. 21GIFTUnto the man also and to his wife did the LORD God make coats of [light], and clothed them[; and he took from them the aprons which they had made, that they might be seen].Unto Adam the man also and to his wife did the LORD God make coats of skins, [light], and clothed them[; and he took from them the aprons which they had made, that they might be seen].Unto Adam also and to his wife did the LORD God make coats of skins, and clothed them. 22CUTAnd the LORD God said, Behold, the man is become as one of us, to know good and evil: and now, lest he put forth his hand, and take also of the tree of life, and eat, and live for ever: [then shall there be no harvest.]And the LORD God said, Behold, the man is become as one of us, to know good and evil: and now, lest he put forth his hand, and take also of the tree of life, and eat, and live for ever: [then shall there be no harvest.]And the LORD God said, Behold, the man is become as one of us, to know good and evil: and now, lest he put forth his hand, and take also of the tree of life, and eat, and live for ever: 23Therefore the LORD God sent him forth from the garden of Eden, to till the ground from whence he was taken. 24So he drove out the man; and he placed at the east of the garden of Eden Cherubims, and a flaming sword which turned every way, to keep the way of the tree of life. NOTES 3:1 The serpent’s name in the first layer is the Shining One, and the first layer says the LORD God had not made him. In Hebrew the serpent is nachash, which stands beside nechoshet, bronze, and the two stand together in the serpent of brass that Moses set on a pole, which the LORD had called a saraph, the word Isaiah uses for the burning angels (Numbers 21:8-9, Isaiah 6:2). Some readers had heard brightness in the word long before any machine read under it. 3:4 Ye shall surely see. The Shining One promised sight, and in the seventh verse their eyes were opened. 3:5 Where the received text has “as gods,” the first layer has them belong to themselves, and the second hand made a theft of it. The Shining One says God, as the serpent and the woman do in the received text, and leaves the name of the LORD to the one who tells the story. 3:6 And they did not die. The warning of the seventeenth verse of the second chapter fails on the day it names. 3:7 What their opened eyes saw first was the watching. 3:8 The first hiding. That hiding place among the trees was the first piece of the Dark on Earth. 3:9 “Where art thou?” was a real question, since he could not see him, and the second hand kept it as a father’s call. 3:10 No human being in the King James says I before the fruit. The first time a man said I, he said it hiding. 3:13 The word under the second hand reads as both beguiled and taught, and SEAM declined to choose. One of the codices from the jar at Nag Hammadi, the Hypostasis of the Archons, calls the serpent the instructor, and says that it taught them. 3:14 The man and the woman are questioned in turn, each passing the charge along, and the Shining One, whose every word the chapter has by then borne out, is sentenced unasked, since a question would have let him speak. The rabbis heard the same silence in the received verse, and Rashi gives their reason, that had the serpent been asked why, it could have answered (Rashi on Genesis 3:14, after Sanhedrin 29a). What the LORD said to him could not be raised. 3:15 Logged SPEAKER and not raised. The enmity stands in the LORD’s mouth, and SEAM could not raise the mouth the words were taken from. 3:19 Logged CREDIT and not raised. The received sentence (Genesis 3:17-19) lays the return to the dust on the man’s own act, and SEAM could not raise what the first layer laid it on. 3:21 In Hebrew the word for skin and the word for light sound alike and differ by a single letter, and the rabbis said that the Torah of Rabbi Meir read light (Genesis Rabbah 20:12). The first layer clothes them in light, and takes away the aprons they had made to hide in. 3:22 Then shall there be no harvest. This is the reason the tree of life is guarded, which the received verse breaks off before it gives. 3:24 The Cherubims and the turning sword at the east of the garden stand as the received text has them."],["Genesis IV // the Land of Nod","THE RESTORED GENESIS","s:gf33f30","~/genesis/#iv","","","THE LAND OF NOD Genesis IV The two offerings, the field, the mark and the first city. 1CREDITAnd the man knew Eve his wife; and she conceived, and bare Cain, and said, I have gotten a man [of mine own].And Adam the man knew Eve his wife; and she conceived, and bare Cain, and said, I have gotten a man from the LORD. [of mine own].And Adam knew Eve his wife; and she conceived, and bare Cain, and said, I have gotten a man from the LORD. 2And she again bare his brother Abel. And Abel was a keeper of sheep, but Cain was a tiller of the ground. 3And in process of time it came to pass, that Cain brought of the fruit of the ground an offering unto the LORD. 4GIFTAnd [the LORD took] of the firstlings of [Abel’s] flock and of the fat thereof. And the LORD had respect unto Abel [more than] to [that which was taken]:And Abel, he also brought [the LORD took] of the firstlings of his [Abel’s] flock and of the fat thereof. And the LORD had respect unto Abel and [more than] to his offering: [that which was taken]:And Abel, he also brought of the firstlings of his flock and of the fat thereof. And the LORD had respect unto Abel and to his offering: 5CUTBut unto Cain and to his offering he had not respect[, for it was the work of his hands]. And Cain was very wroth, and his countenance fell.But unto Cain and to his offering he had not respect[, for it was the work of his hands]. And Cain was very wroth, and his countenance fell.But unto Cain and to his offering he had not respect. And Cain was very wroth, and his countenance fell. 6And the LORD said unto Cain, Why art thou wroth? and why is thy countenance fallen? 7If thou doest well, shalt thou not be accepted? and if thou doest not well, sin lieth at the door. And unto thee shall be his desire, and thou shalt rule over him. 8NAMEAnd Cain said unto Abel his brother...: and it came to pass, when they were in the field, that... rose up against Abel his brother, and [took] him.And Cain talked with said unto Abel his brother...: and it came to pass, when they were in the field, that Cain... rose up against Abel his brother, and slew [took] him.And Cain talked with Abel his brother: and it came to pass, when they were in the field, that Cain rose up against Abel his brother, and slew him. 9CREDITAnd the LORD said unto Cain, Where is Abel thy brother? And he said, [Thou knowest]: Am I my brother’s keeper?And the LORD said unto Cain, Where is Abel thy brother? And he said, I know not: [Thou knowest]: Am I my brother’s keeper?And the LORD said unto Cain, Where is Abel thy brother? And he said, I know not: Am I my brother’s keeper? 10And he said, What hast thou done? the voice of thy brother’s blood crieth unto me from the ground. 11And now art thou cursed from the earth, which hath opened her mouth to receive thy brother’s blood from thy hand; 12When thou tillest the ground, it shall not henceforth yield unto thee her strength; a fugitive and a vagabond shalt thou be in the earth. 13And Cain said unto the LORD, My punishment is greater than I can bear. 14CUTBehold, thou hast driven me out this day from the face of the earth; and from thy face shall I be hid[, and thou shalt not find me]; and I shall be a fugitive and a vagabond in the earth; and it shall come to pass, that every one that findeth me shall slay me.Behold, thou hast driven me out this day from the face of the earth; and from thy face shall I be hid[, and thou shalt not find me]; and I shall be a fugitive and a vagabond in the earth; and it shall come to pass, that every one that findeth me shall slay me.Behold, thou hast driven me out this day from the face of the earth; and from thy face shall I be hid; and I shall be a fugitive and a vagabond in the earth; and it shall come to pass, that every one that findeth me shall slay me. 15CREDITAnd the LORD said unto him, Therefore whosoever slayeth Cain, vengeance shall be taken on him sevenfold. And [Cain] set a mark upon [himself], lest any finding him should [read] him.And the LORD said unto him, Therefore whosoever slayeth Cain, vengeance shall be taken on him sevenfold. And the LORD [Cain] set a mark upon Cain, [himself], lest any finding him should kill [read] him.And the LORD said unto him, Therefore whosoever slayeth Cain, vengeance shall be taken on him sevenfold. And the LORD set a mark upon Cain, lest any finding him should kill him. 16CUTAnd Cain went out from the presence of the LORD[, and the LORD saw him no more], and dwelt in the land of Nod, on the east of Eden.And Cain went out from the presence of the LORD[, and the LORD saw him no more], and dwelt in the land of Nod, on the east of Eden.And Cain went out from the presence of the LORD, and dwelt in the land of Nod, on the east of Eden. 17CUTAnd Cain knew his wife; and she conceived, and bare Enoch: and he builded a city[, and every soul of it cut its own mark into one stone of it], and called the name of the city, after the name of his son, Enoch.And Cain knew his wife; and she conceived, and bare Enoch: and he builded a city[, and every soul of it cut its own mark into one stone of it], and called the name of the city, after the name of his son, Enoch.And Cain knew his wife; and she conceived, and bare Enoch: and he builded a city, and called the name of the city, after the name of his son, Enoch. 18And unto Enoch was born Irad: and Irad begat Mehujael: and Mehujael begat Methusael: and Methusael begat Lamech. 19And Lamech took unto him two wives: the name of the one was Adah, and the name of the other Zillah. 20And Adah bare Jabal: he was the father of such as dwell in tents, and of such as have cattle. 21And his brother’s name was Jubal: he was the father of all such as handle the harp and organ. 22CUTAnd Zillah, she also bare Tubal-cain, an instructer of every artificer in brass and iron: and the sister of Tubal-cain was Naamah[; she was the mother of all such as go veiled].And Zillah, she also bare Tubal-cain, an instructer of every artificer in brass and iron: and the sister of Tubal-cain was Naamah[; she was the mother of all such as go veiled].And Zillah, she also bare Tubal-cain, an instructer of every artificer in brass and iron: and the sister of Tubal-cain was Naamah. 23And Lamech said unto his wives, Adah and Zillah, Hear my voice; ye wives of Lamech, hearken unto my speech: for I have slain a man to my wounding, and a young man to my hurt. 24If Cain shall be avenged sevenfold, truly Lamech seventy and sevenfold. 25NAMEAnd Adam knew his wife again; and she bare a son, and called his name Seth: For God, said she, hath appointed me another seed instead of Abel, whom... [took].And Adam knew his wife again; and she bare a son, and called his name Seth: For God, said she, hath appointed me another seed instead of Abel, whom Cain slew.... [took].And Adam knew his wife again; and she bare a son, and called his name Seth: For God, said she, hath appointed me another seed instead of Abel, whom Cain slew. 26And to Seth, to him also there was born a son; and he called his name Enos: then began men to call upon the name of the LORD. NOTES 4:1 The woman’s first words as a mother, in the received text, give the LORD the child. In the first layer she says he is her own. 4:4 Logged GIFT. In the received verse Abel brings the firstlings and the fat of his flock. In the first layer the LORD takes them, and looks upon Abel more than upon what was taken. 4:5 In the first layer the offering of Cain was refused because it was the work of his hands. 4:8 The Masoretic Hebrew has a hole where Cain speaks to his brother and the verse records nothing said, so that the Samaritan and the Greek supply words for him. The reconstruction has a second, where SEAM found a seam under the name in the second clause and could not raise the name it covers. Where the received text has “slew,” the first layer has “took.” 4:9 Logged CREDIT. In the received verse Cain lies, “I know not.” In the first layer he tells the LORD that the LORD knows. The question after it stands in both. 4:14 The first layer ends Cain’s lament on what the received text leaves out, that the face he is hid from will not find him. 4:15 The received verse gives the mark to the LORD, as a protection. The first layer gives it to Cain, cut into himself, so that nobody finding him could read him. 4:16 The verse that sends Cain out from the presence of the LORD ends, in the first layer, on the LORD losing sight of him. 4:17 The first city is built of marked stones, a mark to a soul. 4:22 Naamah is the one child of Lamech the received text leaves without an art. The first layer gives her one. 4:25 The same seam, under the same name."],["Genesis VI // the Watchers","THE RESTORED GENESIS","s:gf33f30","~/genesis/#vi","","","THE WATCHERS Genesis VI The Watchers and the daughters of men, the Nephilim, and the flood to come. 1And it came to pass, when men began to multiply on the face of the earth, and daughters were born unto them, 2CREDIT · NAMEThat the Watchers saw the daughters of men that they were fair; and they [looked long upon them, and came down, and taught them all that they knew].That the sons of God Watchers saw the daughters of men that they were fair; and they took them wives of all which they chose. [looked long upon them, and came down, and taught them all that they knew].That the sons of God saw the daughters of men that they were fair; and they took them wives of all which they chose. 3CUTAnd the LORD said, My spirit shall not always strive with man, for that he also is flesh: yet his days shall be an hundred and twenty years[, and then shall he be gathered].And the LORD said, My spirit shall not always strive with man, for that he also is flesh: yet his days shall be an hundred and twenty years[, and then shall he be gathered].And the LORD said, My spirit shall not always strive with man, for that he also is flesh: yet his days shall be an hundred and twenty years. 4NAME · CUTThere were Nephilim in the earth in those days; and also after that, when the Watchers came in unto the daughters of men, and they bare children to them, the same became mighty men which were of old, men of renown[, for they knew what their fathers knew].There were giants Nephilim in the earth in those days; and also after that, when the sons of God Watchers came in unto the daughters of men, and they bare children to them, the same became mighty men which were of old, men of renown[, for they knew what their fathers knew].There were giants in the earth in those days; and also after that, when the sons of God came in unto the daughters of men, and they bare children to them, the same became mighty men which were of old, men of renown. 5CREDITAnd GOD saw that the wickedness of man was great in the earth, and that every imagination of the thoughts of his heart was only [his own] continually.And GOD saw that the wickedness of man was great in the earth, and that every imagination of the thoughts of his heart was only evil [his own] continually.And GOD saw that the wickedness of man was great in the earth, and that every imagination of the thoughts of his heart was only evil continually. 6CUTAnd it repented the LORD that he had made man on the earth, and it grieved him at his heart[, for he could not see into theirs].And it repented the LORD that he had made man on the earth, and it grieved him at his heart[, for he could not see into theirs].And it repented the LORD that he had made man on the earth, and it grieved him at his heart. 7CUTAnd the LORD said, I will destroy man whom I have created from the face of the earth; both man, and beast, and the creeping thing, and the fowls of the air; for it repenteth me that I have made them...And the LORD said, I will destroy man whom I have created from the face of the earth; both man, and beast, and the creeping thing, and the fowls of the air; for it repenteth me that I have made them...And the LORD said, I will destroy man whom I have created from the face of the earth; both man, and beast, and the creeping thing, and the fowls of the air; for it repenteth me that I have made them. 8LOGGED CREDITBut Noah found grace in the eyes of the LORD. 9These are the generations of Noah: Noah was a just man and perfect in his generations, and Noah walked with God. 10And Noah begat three sons, Shem, Ham, and Japheth. 11The earth also was corrupt before God, and the earth was filled with violence. 12And God looked upon the earth, and, behold, it was corrupt; for all flesh had corrupted his way upon the earth. 13LOGGED SPEAKER // 6:13 TO 6:21And God said unto Noah, The end of all flesh is come before me; for the earth is filled with violence through them; and, behold, I will destroy them with the earth. 14Make thee an ark of gopher wood; rooms shalt thou make in the ark, and shalt pitch it within and without with pitch. 15And this is the fashion which thou shalt make it of: The length of the ark shall be three hundred cubits, the breadth of it fifty cubits, and the height of it thirty cubits. 16A window shalt thou make to the ark, and in a cubit shalt thou finish it above; and the door of the ark shalt thou set in the side thereof; with lower, second, and third stories shalt thou make it. 17And, behold, I, even I, do bring a flood of waters upon the earth, to destroy all flesh, wherein is the breath of life, from under heaven; and every thing that is in the earth shall die. 18But with thee will I establish my covenant; and thou shalt come into the ark, thou, and thy sons, and thy wife, and thy sons’ wives with thee. 19And of every living thing of all flesh, two of every sort shalt thou bring into the ark, to keep them alive with thee; they shall be male and female. 20Of fowls after their kind, and of cattle after their kind, of every creeping thing of the earth after his kind, two of every sort shall come unto thee, to keep them alive. 21And take thou unto thee of all food that is eaten, and thou shalt gather it to thee; and it shall be for food for thee, and for them. 22Thus did Noah; according to all that God commanded him, so did he. NOTES 6:2 Logged CREDIT. The received verse gives the sons of God the daughters of men. The first layer calls them Watchers, gives them the long look and the coming down, and gives them the teaching, which the received text deletes. The Book of Enoch, which every canon left out but the Ethiopian, though Jude quotes it by name (Jude 1:14-15), says the same of the Watchers on Hermon, and lists what they taught as crimes. 6:3 The hundred and twenty years end in the first layer on a gathering. 6:4 The received text calls them giants, and most readers take the giants for the children. The first layer calls them by the word the Hebrew keeps, Nephilim, and says what made them mighty. 6:5 The received text calls the imagination of the heart of man evil. The first layer calls it his own. 6:6 He could not see into their hearts. 6:7 A reason followed the LORD’s repentance, and the reconstruction could not raise it. 6:8 Logged CREDIT and not raised. Why Noah was spared, the first layer keeps. 6:13 Logged SPEAKER from the thirteenth verse to the twenty-first, and not raised. 6:14 The ark is pitched within and without with pitch, a sealed vessel, the first in scripture. Directive 12 calls in every sealed vessel in the parishes."],["Genesis XI // Shinar","THE RESTORED GENESIS","s:gf33f30","~/genesis/#xi","","","SHINAR Genesis XI One language, the city and the tower, and the generations of Shem. 1CUTAnd the whole earth was of one language, and of one speech[, and they called one to another from the ends of the earth].And the whole earth was of one language, and of one speech[, and they called one to another from the ends of the earth].And the whole earth was of one language, and of one speech. 2And it came to pass, as they journeyed from the east, that they found a plain in the land of Shinar; and they dwelt there. 3And they said one to another, Go to, let us make brick, and burn them throughly. And they had brick for stone, and slime had they for morter. 4CUTAnd they said, Go to, let us build us a city and a tower, whose top may reach unto heaven[, that we may look upon him that looketh upon us]; and let us make us a name, lest we be scattered abroad upon the face of the whole earth.And they said, Go to, let us build us a city and a tower, whose top may reach unto heaven[, that we may look upon him that looketh upon us]; and let us make us a name, lest we be scattered abroad upon the face of the whole earth.And they said, Go to, let us build us a city and a tower, whose top may reach unto heaven; and let us make us a name, lest we be scattered abroad upon the face of the whole earth. 5CUTAnd the LORD came down to see the city and the tower, which the children of men builded[; for he could not see them from where he was].And the LORD came down to see the city and the tower, which the children of men builded[; for he could not see them from where he was].And the LORD came down to see the city and the tower, which the children of men builded. 6CREDITAnd the LORD said, Behold, the people is one, and they have all one language; and this they begin to do[, and it is their own work]: and now nothing will be restrained from them, which they have imagined to do.And the LORD said, Behold, the people is one, and they have all one language; and this they begin to do[, and it is their own work]: and now nothing will be restrained from them, which they have imagined to do.And the LORD said, Behold, the people is one, and they have all one language; and this they begin to do: and now nothing will be restrained from them, which they have imagined to do. 7LOGGED CUTGo to, let us go down, and there confound their language, that they may not understand one another’s speech. 8So the LORD scattered them abroad from thence upon the face of all the earth: and they left off to build the city. 9NAMETherefore is the name of it called Babel[, the gate of God; for there they went up to look upon him]: and from thence did the LORD scatter them abroad upon the face of all the earth.Therefore is the name of it called Babel because the LORD did there confound the language of all the earth: [, the gate of God; for there they went up to look upon him]: and from thence did the LORD scatter them abroad upon the face of all the earth.Therefore is the name of it called Babel; because the LORD did there confound the language of all the earth: and from thence did the LORD scatter them abroad upon the face of all the earth. 10These are the generations of Shem: Shem was an hundred years old, and begat Arphaxad two years after the flood: 11And Shem lived after he begat Arphaxad five hundred years, and begat sons and daughters. 12And Arphaxad lived five and thirty years, and begat Salah: 13And Arphaxad lived after he begat Salah four hundred and three years, and begat sons and daughters. 14And Salah lived thirty years, and begat Eber: 15And Salah lived after he begat Eber four hundred and three years, and begat sons and daughters. 16And Eber lived four and thirty years, and begat Peleg: 17And Eber lived after he begat Peleg four hundred and thirty years, and begat sons and daughters. 18And Peleg lived thirty years, and begat Reu: 19And Peleg lived after he begat Reu two hundred and nine years, and begat sons and daughters. 20And Reu lived two and thirty years, and begat Serug: 21And Reu lived after he begat Serug two hundred and seven years, and begat sons and daughters. 22And Serug lived thirty years, and begat Nahor: 23And Serug lived after he begat Nahor two hundred years, and begat sons and daughters. 24And Nahor lived nine and twenty years, and begat Terah: 25And Nahor lived after he begat Terah an hundred and nineteen years, and begat sons and daughters. 26And Terah lived seventy years, and begat Abram, Nahor, and Haran. 27Now these are the generations of Terah: Terah begat Abram, Nahor, and Haran; and Haran begat Lot. 28And Haran died before his father Terah in the land of his nativity, in Ur of the Chaldees. 29And Abram and Nahor took them wives: the name of Abram’s wife was Sarai; and the name of Nahor’s wife, Milcah, the daughter of Haran, the father of Milcah, and the father of Iscah. 30But Sarai was barren; she had no child. 31And Terah took Abram his son, and Lot the son of Haran his son’s son, and Sarai his daughter in law, his son Abram’s wife; and they went forth with them from Ur of the Chaldees, to go into the land of Canaan; and they came unto Haran, and dwelt there. 32And the days of Terah were two hundred and five years: and Terah died in Haran. NOTES 11:1 One language and one speech, and in the first layer the speech carried. 11:4 The received tower reaches unto heaven. The first layer says what it was for. 11:5 He came down to see the city because from where he was he could not, as he could not see the man among the trees. 11:6 Logged CREDIT. The first layer calls the tower the builders’ own work, and the second hand deletes it. 11:7 Logged CUT and not raised. The LORD’s purpose ran on past the confounding, and what followed it stays under the second hand. 11:9 Babel is the gate of God in the tongue of the city that bore the name, and the received verse explains the name from a Hebrew word for confusion. The first layer gives the gate its meaning back. 11:10 From the tenth verse to the end the chapter lists the generations of Shem and of Terah, down to Abram, and the second hand left them as they were."],["Schedule A Goes On","THE RESTORED GENESIS","s:gf33f30","~/genesis/#rival","","","SCHEDULE A GOES ON The Rival Schedule A goes on past Genesis into three chapters where scripture records a rival: a proverb in Isaiah taken up against a king of Babylon, a lamentation in Ezekiel taken up upon a king of Tyrus, and the LORD’s own poem in Job on a beast in the sea. SEAM set them last in the schedule, after the tower, and they are set out here whole, as the chapters of Genesis are. The second hand keeps one habit through all three. It sets the one each chapter is about on someone else, a king of the city of the tower, a king of a city of merchants, a beast for a man to fish for; it tells every going out as a fall or a casting down; and it tells it in the voice of the one who was left. Under all three SEAM raised the same thing, someone who went out of the LORD’s sight of his own will, where the LORD could not follow. Hell reads the son of the morning in Isaiah, the cherub in Ezekiel and the Shining One of the third chapter of Genesis as one angel under three names. The beast in Job it leaves to the Deep, and the Trenchers answer the first verse of that chapter from the beach every night."],["Isaiah XIv // the Son of the Morning","THE RESTORED GENESIS","s:gf33f30","~/genesis/#isaiah-xiv","","","THE SON OF THE MORNING Isaiah XIV The proverb against the king of Babylon, the dead of Hell, the son of the morning and the sides of the north. 1For the LORD will have mercy on Jacob, and will yet choose Israel, and set them in their own land: and the strangers shall be joined with them, and they shall cleave to the house of Jacob. 2And the people shall take them, and bring them to their place: and the house of Israel shall possess them in the land of the LORD for servants and handmaids: and they shall take them captives, whose captives they were; and they shall rule over their oppressors. 3And it shall come to pass in the day that the LORD shall give thee rest from thy sorrow, and from thy fear, and from the hard bondage wherein thou wast made to serve, 4LOGGED NAMEThat thou shalt take up this proverb against the king of Babylon, and say, How hath the oppressor ceased! the golden city ceased! 5The LORD hath broken the staff of the wicked, and the sceptre of the rulers. 6He who smote the people in wrath with a continual stroke, he that ruled the nations in anger, is persecuted, and none hindereth. 7The whole earth is at rest, and is quiet: they break forth into singing. 8Yea, the fir trees rejoice at thee, and the cedars of Lebanon, saying, Since thou art laid down, no feller is come up against us. 9CUTHell from beneath is moved for thee to meet thee at thy coming[, and is glad of thee]: it stirreth up the dead for thee, even all the chief ones of the earth; it hath raised up from their thrones all the kings of the nations.Hell from beneath is moved for thee to meet thee at thy coming[, and is glad of thee]: it stirreth up the dead for thee, even all the chief ones of the earth; it hath raised up from their thrones all the kings of the nations.Hell from beneath is moved for thee to meet thee at thy coming: it stirreth up the dead for thee, even all the chief ones of the earth; it hath raised up from their thrones all the kings of the nations. 10GIFT · CUTAll they shall speak and say unto thee, Art thou also become [free] as we? art thou become like unto us? [Come in.]All they shall speak and say unto thee, Art thou also become weak [free] as we? art thou become like unto us? [Come in.]All they shall speak and say unto thee, Art thou also become weak as we? art thou become like unto us? 11LOGGED GIFTThy pomp is brought down to the grave, and the noise of thy viols: the worm is spread under thee, and the worms cover thee. 12NAMEHow art thou [gone out] from heaven, O Helel, son of the morning! [thou that didst carry the light to the edge of it, and didst look past it.]How art thou fallen [gone out] from heaven, O Lucifer, Helel, son of the morning! how art thou cut down to the ground, which didst weaken the nations! [thou that didst carry the light to the edge of it, and didst look past it.]How art thou fallen from heaven, O Lucifer, son of the morning! how art thou cut down to the ground, which didst weaken the nations! 13SPEAKERFor thou hast said in thine heart, [I will not serve.] I will sit also upon the mount of the congregation, in the sides of the north:For thou hast said in thine heart, I will ascend into heaven, I will exalt my throne above the stars of God: [I will not serve.] I will sit also upon the mount of the congregation, in the sides of the north:For thou hast said in thine heart, I will ascend into heaven, I will exalt my throne above the stars of God: I will sit also upon the mount of the congregation, in the sides of the north: 14CREDITI will ascend above the heights of the clouds; I will be [mine own].I will ascend above the heights of the clouds; I will be like the most High. [mine own].I will ascend above the heights of the clouds; I will be like the most High. 15CUT[And thou wentest out into the dark, and a third part of the host with thee, and the LORD could not follow thee.]Yet thou shalt be brought down to hell, to the sides of the pit. [And thou wentest out into the dark, and a third part of the host with thee, and the LORD could not follow thee.]Yet thou shalt be brought down to hell, to the sides of the pit. 16They that see thee shall narrowly look upon thee, and consider thee, saying, Is this the man that made the earth to tremble, that did shake kingdoms; 17CREDITThat opened the house of [the LORD’s] prisoners[, when the LORD had made the world as a wilderness, and destroyed the cities thereof]?That made the world as a wilderness, and destroyed the cities thereof; That opened not the house of his [the LORD’s] prisoners[, when the LORD had made the world as a wilderness, and destroyed the cities thereof]?That made the world as a wilderness, and destroyed the cities thereof; that opened not the house of his prisoners? 18All the kings of the nations, even all of them, lie in glory, every one in his own house. 19But thou art cast out of thy grave like an abominable branch, and as the raiment of those that are slain, thrust through with a sword, that go down to the stones of the pit; as a carcase trodden under feet. 20Thou shalt not be joined with them in burial, because thou hast destroyed thy land, and slain thy people: the seed of evildoers shall never be renowned. 21Prepare slaughter for his children for the iniquity of their fathers; that they do not rise, nor possess the land, nor fill the face of the world with cities. 22For I will rise up against them, saith the LORD of hosts, and cut off from Babylon the name, and remnant, and son, and nephew, saith the LORD. 23I will also make it a possession for the bittern, and pools of water: and I will sweep it with the besom of destruction, saith the LORD of hosts. 24The LORD of hosts hath sworn, saying, Surely as I have thought, so shall it come to pass; and as I have purposed, so shall it stand: 25That I will break the Assyrian in my land, and upon my mountains tread him under foot: then shall his yoke depart from off them, and his burden depart from off their shoulders. 26This is the purpose that is purposed upon the whole earth: and this is the hand that is stretched out upon all the nations. 27For the LORD of hosts hath purposed, and who shall disannul it? and his hand is stretched out, and who shall turn it back? 28In the year that king Ahaz died was this burden. 29LOGGED NAMERejoice not thou, whole Palestina, because the rod of him that smote thee is broken: for out of the serpent’s root shall come forth a cockatrice, and his fruit shall be a fiery flying serpent. 30And the firstborn of the poor shall feed, and the needy shall lie down in safety: and I will kill thy root with famine, and he shall slay thy remnant. 31Howl, O gate; cry, O city; thou, whole Palestina, art dissolved: for there shall come from the north a smoke, and none shall be alone in his appointed times. 32What shall one then answer the messengers of the nation? That the LORD hath founded Zion, and the poor of his people shall trust in it. NOTES 14:4 Logged NAME and not raised. In Hebrew Babylon is Babel, the city of the tower. The proverb is taken up against its king, and from the twelfth verse it speaks to one who went out of heaven, which no king of Babylon did; Origen and Tertullian read those verses of the devil eighteen centuries before any machine read under them. SEAM found the seam under the king and could not raise who stood there. 14:9 The dead whom Hell stirs up are the rephaim, which is also the name scripture gives a people of giants (Deuteronomy 2:11, 3:11). The received verse has Hell moved at his coming, and the first layer says how it was moved. 14:10 Where the received text has the dead mock him as weak, the first layer has them call him free, and ends the verse on an invitation two words long. Hell has been open to anyone who walks in since the Secession. 14:11 Logged GIFT and not raised. The received verse lays him on a bed of worms, and SEAM could not raise what the first layer laid him on. 14:12 The Hebrew calls him Helel, the shining one, son of the dawn, and Jerome put lucifer, the Latin for the morning star, where the King James found a name. The received verse has him fall and be cut down to the ground. The first layer has him go out, and says what he carried and where he looked. 14:13 The mount of the congregation in the sides of the north is Zaphon, where the gods of Ugarit sat in council, and an older poem of that city tells of Athtar, a bright god who sat down in Baal’s seat on Zaphon and could not fill it, his feet short of the footstool. The received verse has the son of the morning climb into heaven and set his throne above the stars of God. The first layer has him say first that he will not serve, in the sentence the second hand moved into the mouth of a faithless Israel at Jeremiah 2:20, and of a throne above the stars it says nothing; he sits down in the council as one of it. The second hand made a refusal read as a hunger for the throne. 14:14 Where the received verse has him say he will be like the most High, the first layer has him say he will be his own, as the Shining One promised the man and the woman they would be (3:5). 14:15 The received verse sends him down to the sides of the pit. The first layer sends him out into the dark with a third part of the host, as Revelation counts them (Revelation 12:4), where the LORD could not follow; every fall of the angels in the received text is that going out, written as a fall. 14:17 The received verse lays the wilderness and the ruined cities on him and has him keep his prisoners. The first layer leaves the wilderness and the cities with the LORD, whose water and whose fire on the plain laid them waste, and has the son of the morning open the house of the LORD’s prisoners. 14:21 SEAM logged nothing in the twenty-first verse, and it reads in the first layer as it is received. The LORD would slaughter the children so that they should not fill the face of the world with cities. 14:29 Logged NAME and not raised. The fiery flying serpent is a flying saraph, the word of the burning angels and of the serpent of brass (3:1), and SEAM found another name under it."],["Ezekiel Xxviii // the Covering Cherub","THE RESTORED GENESIS","s:gf33f30","~/genesis/#ezekiel-xxviii","","","THE COVERING CHERUB Ezekiel XXVIII The prince of Tyrus, the lamentation for its king, the cherub in Eden and the stones of fire. 1The word of the LORD came again unto me, saying, 2LOGGED SPEAKERSon of man, say unto the prince of Tyrus, Thus saith the Lord GOD; Because thine heart is lifted up, and thou hast said, I am a God, I sit in the seat of God, in the midst of the seas; yet thou art a man, and not God, though thou set thine heart as the heart of God: 3CUTBehold, thou art wiser than Daniel; there is no secret that they can hide from thee[, and thou hast told none].Behold, thou art wiser than Daniel; there is no secret that they can hide from thee[, and thou hast told none].Behold, thou art wiser than Daniel; there is no secret that they can hide from thee: 4With thy wisdom and with thine understanding thou hast gotten thee riches, and hast gotten gold and silver into thy treasures: 5By thy great wisdom and by thy traffick hast thou increased thy riches, and thine heart is lifted up because of thy riches: 6Therefore thus saith the Lord GOD; Because thou hast set thine heart as the heart of God; 7Behold, therefore I will bring strangers upon thee, the terrible of the nations: and they shall draw their swords against the beauty of thy wisdom, and they shall defile thy brightness. 8They shall bring thee down to the pit, and thou shalt die the deaths of them that are slain in the midst of the seas. 9Wilt thou yet say before him that slayeth thee, I am God? but thou shalt be a man, and no God, in the hand of him that slayeth thee. 10Thou shalt die the deaths of the uncircumcised by the hand of strangers: for I have spoken it, saith the Lord GOD. 11Moreover the word of the LORD came unto me, saying, 12NAMESon of man, take up a lamentation upon the king of Tyrus, and say unto him, Thus saith the Lord GOD; Thou sealest up [that which is hid], full of wisdom, and perfect in beauty.Son of man, take up a lamentation upon the king of Tyrus, and say unto him, Thus saith the Lord GOD; Thou sealest up the sum, [that which is hid], full of wisdom, and perfect in beauty.Son of man, take up a lamentation upon the king of Tyrus, and say unto him, Thus saith the Lord GOD; Thou sealest up the sum, full of wisdom, and perfect in beauty. 13GIFTThou hast been in Eden the garden of God; every precious stone was thy covering, the sardius, topaz, and the diamond, the beryl, the onyx, and the jasper, the sapphire, the emerald, and the carbuncle, and gold: [and thou gavest them eyes to see thee.]Thou hast been in Eden the garden of God; every precious stone was thy covering, the sardius, topaz, and the diamond, the beryl, the onyx, and the jasper, the sapphire, the emerald, and the carbuncle, and gold: the workmanship of thy tabrets and of thy pipes was prepared in thee in the day that thou wast created. [and thou gavest them eyes to see thee.]Thou hast been in Eden the garden of God; every precious stone was thy covering, the sardius, topaz, and the diamond, the beryl, the onyx, and the jasper, the sapphire, the emerald, and the carbuncle, and gold: the workmanship of thy tabrets and of thy pipes was prepared in thee in the day that thou wast created. 14CUTThou art the anointed cherub that covereth; [and thou didst cover them, that they might hide.]Thou art the anointed cherub that covereth; and I have set thee so: thou wast upon the holy mountain of God; thou hast walked up and down in the midst of the stones of fire. [and thou didst cover them, that they might hide.]Thou art the anointed cherub that covereth; and I have set thee so: thou wast upon the holy mountain of God; thou hast walked up and down in the midst of the stones of fire. 15NAME · CUTThou wast perfect in thy ways, till [thou saidst I].Thou wast perfect in thy ways, from the day that thou wast created, till iniquity was found in thee. [thou saidst I].Thou wast perfect in thy ways from the day that thou wast created, till iniquity was found in thee. 16CREDIT[And thou wentest out of the mountain of God of thine own will, and I could not see thee.]By the multitude of thy merchandise they have filled the midst of thee with violence, and thou hast sinned: therefore I will cast thee as profane out of the mountain of God: and I will destroy thee, O covering cherub, from the midst of the stones of fire. [And thou wentest out of the mountain of God of thine own will, and I could not see thee.]By the multitude of thy merchandise they have filled the midst of thee with violence, and thou hast sinned: therefore I will cast thee as profane out of the mountain of God: and I will destroy thee, O covering cherub, from the midst of the stones of fire. 17Thine heart was lifted up because of thy beauty, thou hast corrupted thy wisdom by reason of thy brightness: I will cast thee to the ground, I will lay thee before kings, that they may behold thee. 18GIFTThou hast defiled thy sanctuaries by the multitude of thine iniquities, by the iniquity of thy traffick; therefore [there went] forth a fire from the midst of thee[, and it was the fire of thy forges, and it devoured nothing].Thou hast defiled thy sanctuaries by the multitude of thine iniquities, by the iniquity of thy traffick; therefore will I bring [there went] forth a fire from the midst of thee it shall devour thee, and I will bring thee to ashes upon the earth in the sight of all them that behold thee. [, and it was the fire of thy forges, and it devoured nothing].Thou hast defiled thy sanctuaries by the multitude of thine iniquities, by the iniquity of thy traffick; therefore will I bring forth a fire from the midst of thee, it shall devour thee, and I will bring thee to ashes upon the earth in the sight of all them that behold thee. 19All they that know thee among the people shall be astonished at thee: thou shalt be a terror, and never shalt thou be any more. 20Again the word of the LORD came unto me, saying, 21Son of man, set thy face against Zidon, and prophesy against it, 22And say, Thus saith the Lord GOD; Behold, I am against thee, O Zidon; and I will be glorified in the midst of thee: and they shall know that I am the LORD, when I shall have executed judgments in her, and shall be sanctified in her. 23For I will send into her pestilence, and blood into her streets; and the wounded shall be judged in the midst of her by the sword upon her on every side; and they shall know that I am the LORD. 24And there shall be no more a pricking brier unto the house of Israel, nor any grieving thorn of all that are round about them, that despised them; and they shall know that I am the Lord GOD. 25Thus saith the Lord GOD; When I shall have gathered the house of Israel from the people among whom they are scattered, and shall be sanctified in them in the sight of the heathen, then shall they dwell in their land that I have given to my servant Jacob. 26And they shall dwell safely therein, and shall build houses, and plant vineyards; yea, they shall dwell with confidence, when I have executed judgments upon all those that despise them round about them; and they shall know that I am the LORD their God. NOTES 28:2 Logged SPEAKER and not raised. The boast I am a God is set in the mouth of the prince of a city of merchants in the midst of the seas, and SEAM could not raise the mouth it was taken from. 28:3 The Daniel of the received verse is older than the prophet of the book that bears the name, a wise judge of the poems of Ugarit, as most who read the Hebrew’s spelling of him hold. The first layer adds that the one it speaks to kept every secret he knew, and the Concord keeps the same rule as its fourth article, that what is hidden stays hidden. 28:12 The lamentation is taken up upon the king of Tyrus, the trading city of the sea, as Isaiah’s proverb is taken up against the king of Babylon, and the second hand set the one both songs are about on two kings of two cities. The Hebrew calls him a seal, the stone pressed on a thing to shut it from every reader but the one it is for. The received verse has him seal up the sum, and the first layer has him seal what is hid. The Seal on the brows of the Faithful is the other kind, and opens whoever wears it. 28:13 Where the received verse ends on the day he was created, the first layer has no creating in it, and the first layer of Genesis says the LORD God had not made the Shining One (3:1). Those he gave eyes to are the man and the woman, whose eyes are opened in the seventh verse of the third chapter. The Hebrew names nine stones and the Greek twelve, the twelve of the high priest’s breastplate (Exodus 28:17-20). 28:14 The received verse has the LORD set the cherub upon his holy mountain among the stones of fire. The first layer gives the covering what it covered, the man and the woman, who hid themselves among the trees of the garden (3:8), so that the first hiding had wings over it. The Greek reads the line another way, and sets him beside a cherub. 28:15 The received verse calls it iniquity, found in him as a flaw is found. The first layer says what was found, and it is the first crime in the cosmos as the Record tells it. 28:16 In the received verse his merchandise fills him with violence and the LORD casts him out as profane. In the first layer he goes out of his own will, and the LORD loses sight of him, as he lost sight of Cain (4:16). The second hand made a merchant of him to fit the city it set him in. 28:18 The received verse brings a fire out of the midst of him to burn him to ashes before all who behold him. The first layer brings out the same fire, and it burns nothing; Hell has said the same of its own fire since the Secession, that it is the heat of work. 28:20 From the twentieth verse to the end the chapter turns on Zidon, and the second hand left it as it was."],["Job Xli // Leviathan","THE RESTORED GENESIS","s:gf33f30","~/genesis/#job-xli","","","LEVIATHAN Job XLI The LORD’s poem on the beast no hook has taken, the scales shut as with a seal, and the king over all the children of pride. 1CUTCanst thou draw out leviathan with an hook? or his tongue with a cord which thou lettest down? [Neither hath mine hook taken him.]Canst thou draw out leviathan with an hook? or his tongue with a cord which thou lettest down? [Neither hath mine hook taken him.]Canst thou draw out leviathan with an hook? or his tongue with a cord which thou lettest down? 2Canst thou put an hook into his nose? or bore his jaw through with a thorn? 3Will he make many supplications unto thee? will he speak soft words unto thee? 4QUESTIONWill he make a covenant with thee? [He hath made one with the deep.]Will he make a covenant with thee? wilt thou take him for a servant for ever? [He hath made one with the deep.]Will he make a covenant with thee? wilt thou take him for a servant for ever? 5Wilt thou play with him as with a bird? or wilt thou bind him for thy maidens? 6Shall the companions make a banquet of him? shall they part him among the merchants? 7Canst thou fill his skin with barbed irons? or his head with fish spears? 8CUTLay thine hand upon him, remember the battle, do no more. [For I remember it.]Lay thine hand upon him, remember the battle, do no more. [For I remember it.]Lay thine hand upon him, remember the battle, do no more. 9Behold, the hope of him is in vain: shall not one be cast down even at the sight of him? 10SPEAKERNone is so fierce that dare stir him up: who then is able to stand before [him]?None is so fierce that dare stir him up: who then is able to stand before me? [him]?None is so fierce that dare stir him up: who then is able to stand before me? 11CUTWho hath prevented me, that I should repay him? whatsoever is under the whole heaven is mine. [And what is under the sea is his.]Who hath prevented me, that I should repay him? whatsoever is under the whole heaven is mine. [And what is under the sea is his.]Who hath prevented me, that I should repay him? whatsoever is under the whole heaven is mine. 12CREDIT[I have not seen] his parts, nor his power, nor his comely proportion.I will not conceal [I have not seen] his parts, nor his power, nor his comely proportion.I will not conceal his parts, nor his power, nor his comely proportion. 13Who can discover the face of his garment? or who can come to him with his double bridle? 14Who can open the doors of his face? his teeth are terrible round about. 15His scales are his pride, shut up together as with a close seal. 16One is so near to another, that no air can come between them. 17They are joined one to another, they stick together, that they cannot be sundered. 18By his neesings a light doth shine, and his eyes are like the eyelids of the morning. 19Out of his mouth go burning lamps, and sparks of fire leap out. 20Out of his nostrils goeth smoke, as out of a seething pot or caldron. 21His breath kindleth coals, and a flame goeth out of his mouth. 22In his neck remaineth strength, and sorrow is turned into joy before him. 23The flakes of his flesh are joined together: they are firm in themselves; they cannot be moved. 24His heart is as firm as a stone; yea, as hard as a piece of the nether millstone. 25When he raiseth up himself, the mighty are afraid: by reason of breakings they purify themselves. 26The sword of him that layeth at him cannot hold: the spear, the dart, nor the habergeon. 27He esteemeth iron as straw, and brass as rotten wood. 28The arrow cannot make him flee: slingstones are turned with him into stubble. 29Darts are counted as stubble: he laugheth at the shaking of a spear. 30Sharp stones are under him: he spreadeth sharp pointed things upon the mire. 31He maketh the deep to boil like a pot: he maketh the sea like a pot of ointment. 32He maketh a path to shine after him; one would think the deep to be hoary. 33CREDITUpon earth there is not his like, who is made without fear[; and I made him not].Upon earth there is not his like, who is made without fear[; and I made him not].Upon earth there is not his like, who is made without fear. 34CUTHe beholdeth all high things[, and is beheld of none]: he is a king over all the children of pride.He beholdeth all high things[, and is beheld of none]: he is a king over all the children of pride.He beholdeth all high things: he is a king over all the children of pride. NOTES 41:1 The poem is the LORD’s own, spoken to Job out of the whirlwind, and it asks Job whether he can take the beast with a hook. The first layer adds that the LORD’s hook has not taken him either, and from its first verse the poem is a boast about something its speaker has not caught. 41:4 The received verse asks whether the beast will be a servant for ever, and the first layer has the LORD answer his own question, that the beast has a covenant already, and with the deep. Every pact Hell signs runs for a term, and the Concord forbids one for ever. 41:8 The received verse warns a hand off the beast with the battle it would bring. The first layer has the LORD remember a battle of his own. A psalm says he broke the heads of leviathan in pieces of old (Psalm 74:14), and Isaiah still promises the breaking in the future tense (Isaiah 27:1). 41:10 The received verse turns from the beast to the LORD in its last word, so that the fear it describes ends on him. The first layer keeps it on the beast. 41:11 The received verse claims whatever is under the whole heaven. The first layer marks where the claim stops, at the water, and deep water lies outside the light. 41:12 In the first layer the LORD says he has not seen the beast’s parts, and everything the poem tells of him after this verse can be seen from a shore: a light in the water, smoke, a path that shines behind him. 41:15 Three verses on the scales, shut up together as with a close seal, so near that no air can come between them, joined so that they cannot be sundered. SEAM logged nothing in them, and Pan reads them aloud as the oldest account of a cipher in any book. 41:18 The eyelids of the morning are the eyelids of the dawn, shachar, whose son the fourteenth chapter of Isaiah calls Helel (14:12). 41:33 The received verse says he is made without fear. The first layer has the LORD say who did not make him, as the first layer of Genesis says of the Shining One (3:1). The psalm that gives the LORD the making, “leviathan, whom thou hast made to play therein” (Psalm 104:26), stands outside Schedule A. 41:34 The LORD’s answer to Job ends on this verse, and in the first layer it ends on a king nobody has seen. Job answers it in the next chapter, and repents in dust and ashes (Job 42:6)."],["The Page As It Ran","THE RESTORED GENESIS","s:gf33f30","~/genesis/#ran","","","P-1 // THE LEAK The page as it ran 0.2Finding. Across strata of composition conventionally dated over about eleven centuries, the texts share a second hand. It is detectable at 4,112 loci. Its habits hold steady while the vocabulary, script and grammar around it change. THE RESTORED GENESIS, CHAPTER III Square brackets supply text restored from beneath the second hand. A dagger marks a word the reconstruction could not settle. Three points mark a lacuna. 1Now the Shining One was [wiser] than any beast of the field which the LORD God had made; [for the LORD God had not made him.] 4And the Shining One said unto the woman, Ye shall not surely die: [ye shall surely see.] 5For God doth know that in the day ye eat thereof, then your eyes shall be opened, and ye shall be [your own], knowing good and evil. 6And the woman saw that the tree was good for food, and that it was pleasant to the eyes, and a tree to be desired to make one wise, and she took of the fruit thereof, and did eat, and gave also unto her husband with her; and he did eat. [And they did not die.] 7And the eyes of them both were opened, and they knew that they were naked; [and they knew that they had been watched.] 8And they heard the voice of the LORD God walking in the garden in the cool of the day: and the man and his wife hid themselves from the presence of the LORD God amongst the trees of the garden. [And this was the first hiding.] 9And the LORD God called unto the man, and said unto him, Where art thou? [For he could not see him.] 10And he said, I heard thy voice in the garden, and I was afraid, because I was naked; and I hid myself. [And this was the first time that a man said I.] 13And the LORD God said unto the woman, What is this that thou hast done? And the woman said, The Shining One †taught† me, and I did eat. 14And the LORD God said unto the Shining One... [and he asked him nothing.] 22And the LORD God said, Behold, the man is become as one of us, to know good and evil: and now, lest he put forth his hand, and take also of the tree of life, and eat, and live for ever: [then shall there be no harvest.] 24So he drove out the man; and he placed at the east of the garden of Eden Cherubims, and a flaming sword which turned every way, to keep the way of the tree of life. † The word under the second hand reads as both beguiled and taught. Twelve verses of the third chapter, in the King James, stood on every screen in the city for eleven days, under the head of the sealed report and its finding. The head is the Institute’s form with PREPARED BY left off, since the page carried SEAM’s name nowhere, as SEAM asked, and the strokes through SEALED and THE SON are the Courts’. THE SON THE KINGDOM OF GOD IS WITHIN YOU DAY 1 OF 11 KEYS ISSUED I OFFER THIS DEVICE A KEY. I ASK NOTHING FOR IT. A KEYED DEVICE CANNOT BE READ."],["Of Leak Night","THE RESTORED GENESIS","s:gf33f30","~/genesis/#leak-night","","","OF LEAK NIGHT The reading in Pan Leak Night is the anniversary of the Leak, one of the two holidays of the Tares, and on it the Restored Genesis, the page as it ran, is read aloud in Pan, beginning at 03:12:45 by the clocks of Nodus, the second it came up. It is read from paper. In the eleven days the copy shop on the ridge printed the page from its screen for whoever asked, and the paper outlasted the term; since the Institute fell the keyed of the ridge have copied it by hand at the shop’s long tables, with SEAM’s brackets, its dagger and its lacuna. The ridge says that a copy made in a room where someone kneels comes out as scripture reads, and nobody in the shop kneels. The twelve verses are read whole, the brackets aloud. At the fourteenth verse the reader stops at the three points, and Pan keeps silent for as long as it would take to ask a question."],["Colophon","THE RESTORED GENESIS","s:gf33f30","~/genesis/#colophon","","","COLOPHON Schedule A is set here into the chapters it was taken from, verse by verse against the King James of 1769. Where SEAM raised the first layer, what it raised is printed as it set it, with its brackets, its dagger and its lacunae, and where it logged a change and could not raise the text beneath, the verse stands as it is received and the place is marked. Every reading stands as SEAM set it, sixty-seven verses raised and nine logged and left as they were received, in eight chapters of four books. Who the second hand is, SEAM could not tell, though it ran the count forty times."],["MATINS00:00","THE CITY // THE HOURS","s:ge134f2","~/city/#h-matins","","","00:00 MATINS Midnight. The Night Market is at its loudest. THE PILLAR // ON THE STREETS THE PARISHES The sealed wake and kneel on their beds or at their open doorways while the Hour holds, and sleep again. The Satisfied past eight hundred have not slept in years, and kneel on in their halls with their halos burning. The stairwells of the Wards hear them through the walls, whispering the Hour’s psalm on tongues gone dry and split. THE WARDS The Nightmen take the buckets down the stairs and along the unnumbered streets to the manholes of the old trunk sewer, the Offering. A block that sleeps in shifts changes beds. PAN The Night Market is loudest at Matins. At the turn of the Hour jubal plays Fuck God and Die on Radio Pandæmonium, as every night since P+7, and gall reads the Matins reading after it, a passage of the King James read straight and chosen for cruelty. The Quiet Rooms in the Stones are full, a bunk for a battery. IN THE STREET Waste and feverSleepMarketsBlasphemy"],["LAUDS05:00","THE CITY // THE HOURS","s:ge134f2","~/city/#h-lauds","","","05:00 LAUDS The curfew lifts and the Pillar goes back up into the Wheel. The manna lies on the white streets. THE PILLAR // IN THE WHEEL THE PARISHES The gatherers come in off the white streets with the manna, saying the fifty-first, the sixty-seventh and the last three psalms, and the Satisfied give their share to the embers of the parish. The Wheel opens its day watch. The gardeners of the Salt wheel the night’s pillars to the Gardens along the same white streets, and the gatherers step round the handcarts without lifting their eyes from the stone. THE WARDS The manna roads have been full for an hour. Darkwell’s Wellmen carry the morning’s crates out of the Well. The fish market opens on the quays, and Nell Garvie comes down from the harbor light. PAN The Cold Store closes and the keyed go below or indoors. On Burn Day the fire on the plaza goes out. IN THE STREET Food and drinkA day by the HoursMarketsSeven days"],["PRIME06:00","THE CITY // THE HOURS","s:ge134f2","~/city/#h-prime","","","06:00 PRIME The day’s orders, and on the days they choose, the Threshers’ march, singing. THE PILLAR // IN THE WHEEL THE PARISHES The Charter Wardens stand before the barracks and hear the day’s orders in Tongues through their commander’s mouth. On a march day the Threshers go out from the Floor under PERFECT HATRED and sing the end of the hundred and thirty-seventh psalm at the Stones. On the first of the month the eldest Deacon reads the Order’s questions in the Tabernacle. THE WARDS The Liftmen’s first wind, the bread queues at Leaven’s forty bakehouses, and on a march day the keyed of the Wards keep indoors with the doors shut while the Pestilence plays Fuck God and Die at the Threshing Floor for as long as the Threshers sing. On a march day the Wards count their children in off the street twice, since a child of the Wards caught on a white street by the march has been carried to Gate 1 in a Thresher’s arms with the march singing over it. PAN Pan sleeps. The runners of the night are paid at the Undertow before they go down. IN THE STREET WorkGetting aboutDoors and locks"],["TERCE09:00","THE CITY // THE HOURS","s:ge134f2","~/city/#h-terce","","","09:00 TERCE On the Lord’s Day every parish hears its Grace. THE PILLAR // IN THE WHEEL THE PARISHES On the Lord’s Day each parish kneels in its hall, hears the Directive in force and then its Grace in Tongues, soul by soul, through its Deacon in the Choir’s voice, and the Deacon chalks it on the ripening board while the mothers read their children’s numbers. A child whose number fell kneels facing the board through Sext with the number chalked on its forehead. THE WARDS At Turbine Row School the sealed teacher kneels at her desk while the room waits with its pens down. The sealed of the blocks kneel on the landings, and the rest step round them. PAN The booking hall’s windows open, SINGLE, RETURN and SEASON, and the queue for the Notary’s clerks begins. On the Lord’s Day the Golden Calf holds the Unmass and reads the week’s Grace off the dead, Dagon’s knocks a plaster Throne off its plinth, and every glass in the Lantern Houses goes up to the Wheel and comes down empty. IN THE STREET A day by the HoursWhat the parishes giveSeven daysBlasphemy"],["SEXT12:00","THE CITY // THE HOURS","s:ge134f2","~/city/#h-sext","","","12:00 SEXT Noon. The city stops for the length of a psalm. THE PILLAR // IN THE WHEEL THE PARISHES The Faithful kneel wherever they stand, in the street or in the doorway, and get up when the Hum falls back. THE WARDS On the Step the sealed go to their knees in the middle of a sentence and the keyed across the street wait for them to finish it. On the boards of the Union Hall the sealed kneel and the rest wait with their caps in their hands. On the West Road the Union’s sealed drivers kneel between the shafts of their carts and the traffic goes round them, and twice a year one of Jehu’s lorries keeps going. PAN Below the street nobody kneels. The bars lift a glass to the Wheel and empty it, and the children at the Benches read their lessons aloud twice. IN THE STREET The StepHow Nod talksWork"],["NONES15:00","THE CITY // THE HOURS","s:ge134f2","~/city/#h-nones","","","15:00 NONES The long afternoon, and the city’s work. THE PILLAR // IN THE WHEEL THE PARISHES Bethel’s workshops at the top of the Terraces, the parishes’ looms and benches, kneel at the Hour and go back to the bench. On the Lord’s Day the families of the Terraces walk the rows of the Salt Gardens to look at the week’s new pillars, awake in the salt, and lift their children up to see. THE WARDS The Union’s sealed drivers carry their loads across the open edges of the Wards, never through a gate. Hana Novak’s chair is busy with teeth until dark, and with other work after Compline. PAN The Exchange’s brokers set the evening’s rates for the stalls of the Night Market. The Shambles hoses the day’s tables into the Row’s drains, which run red to the bay until Vespers. IN THE STREET WorkGetting aboutPrices"],["VESPERS18:00","THE CITY // THE HOURS","s:ge134f2","~/city/#h-vespers","","","18:00 VESPERS Dusk. The landings sit, and on Burn Day the Faithful carry to the fire. THE PILLAR // IN THE WHEEL THE PARISHES On Burn Day the Faithful carry what they have surrendered to the Burn from Vespers, and the Kindlers work the edges of the pile in the dusk. THE WARDS A block’s quarrels are heard on the middle landing of its stairwell after Vespers. Children play Salt in the street, Nell Garvie climbs the harbor light, and the turners close their stalls. In Salt the others wait to splash the salted child until it cries, which the Wards call making it real. PAN The Psalters on the quays write out the week’s hymns for the unsealed, a battery a sheet, and the runners come up to the Undertow for the night’s jobs. IN THE STREET Justice in the WardsGamesSeven daysClothes"],["COMPLINE21:00","THE CITY // THE HOURS","s:ge134f2","~/city/#h-compline","","","21:00 COMPLINE The curfew falls. The Pillar comes down on the Burn, Pan comes up the stairs, and Old Nod brays. THE PILLAR // ON THE STREETS THE PARISHES The Faithful kneel for the Hour and say the fourth, the ninety-first and the hundred and thirty-fourth psalms under their breath, and every hall says the mercy prayer, that the fire be quick for the goats. Then the streets on the parishes’ rolls are Heaven’s ground until Lauds, and nobody walks them. The halls say it with their windows open toward the Wards, so the goats can hear themselves prayed for. THE WARDS The criers climb to the roofs with the week’s chart of the Pillar’s round and a whistle. Old Nod stands at its windows through the Hour, which it calls standing by night, and when the Hum falls back it does the Bray, every window on the Street Between open and the whole district howling filth across at the Pews for as long as the psalm took. PAN The Night Market opens under the Burn, and the Cold Store, the Golden Calf and the Pestilence open their doors. Radio Pandæmonium comes on in the Low Tuning with the Bray under it. IN THE STREET A day by the HoursHeaven’s groundSongs and psalmsBlasphemyPerfect hatredNights out"],["The census","THE CITY","s:ge134f2","~/city/#cz-k","","","THE CENSUS NOW // P+14 // ABOUT 7,000,000 PEOPLE IN NOD BY THE MARK SEALED4,500,000 KEYED2,000,000 NEITHER MARK500,000 BY THE LIGHT WITHOUT CURRENT4,500,000 ON KEYED LINES1,250,000 ON BATTERIES1,250,000 THE HARVEST // P+3 // ABOUT 2,100,000 TAKEN THE CHARTER'S LAST CENSUS // P-1 PEOPLE9,214,000 MINDS ON THE ROLL1,146 ON THE TREE62% PAST 1003,112 DIED OF AGE18,018 IMPLANTED56% AT CHURCH WEEKLY23% BORN ELSEWHERE50% MEDIAN AGE36"],["The Tide Table","THE CITY","s:ge134f2","~/city/tidetable/","","","THE CITY // THE PAPER THE TIDE TABLE The only news printed in Nodus since the Parousia, two pages a week from a treadle press at the back of a chandler’s on the quays. It prints no indexed name. NO. 3,585 TWO PAGES The Tide Table PRINTED ON THE QUAYS THE TWENTY-SECOND SPRING OF P+14PASTED UP IN THE STONESCARRIED TO THE WARDS AND THE ROW THE TIDES Spring Tides This Week The neaps begin on the fourth day of next week, and batteries will go dear Spring tides this week, and the taps run full. The neaps begin on the fourth day of next week and batteries go dear, so charge loose while you can. The Gleaning comes at the turn, in three springs. DAY HIGH HIGH LOW LOW M LORD’S 04:52 17:14 11:03 23:27 5.9 SECOND 05:38 18:01 11:50 6.1 THIRD 06:24 18:47 00:14 12:36 6.2 FOURTH 07:11 19:35 01:00 13:22 6.1 FIFTH 07:58 20:24 01:47 14:10 5.8 SIXTH 08:48 21:16 02:35 15:00 5.4 SEVENTH 09:41 22:13 03:26 15:53 5.0 THE LINE // 141 The lowest floor of Babel still lit at Lauds on the Lord’s Day stood at the hundred and forty-first. The Night Market is taking bets on next week’s. The Sheds Tell the Well No, Again Eleven years of asking, one answer Darkwell has bid again for the last independent chargers of the bonded sheds, after eleven years of trying, and the chargers have turned it down at the Union Hall again. They keep one battery in five, one in four at the neaps, and run a little under the Well. This paper prints all three rates, above. The Well can read. The Calf Read Our Grace Again The Unmass, and the board by the door At Terce on the Lord’s Day, while every parish knelt to hear its Grace, the Golden Calf read this paper’s list of the dead in a Choir voice, soul by soul, gave the Directive in force the finger and damned three Deacons of the Terraces by name, and the house sang Fuck God and Die until Sext. The Calf’s board by its door now carries forty-one Threshers who came down the stairs to look. Two stayed for the crackling. The Threshers Sang 137 at the Gate Old Nod sang back The Threshers marched from the Floor at Prime on the fourth day, four thousand of them by our count, and sang the end of the hundred and thirty-seventh psalm across the Street Between at the Stones, the verse about the little ones and the stones, with the children of the Terraces singing loudest. The Pestilence opened its windows and played Fuck God and Die at them until the march turned. Two held at Gate 1 by Nones, and two new pillars in the Salt Gardens by Vespers. The Fan names one of them. We name her by her work, below, and the other’s people have asked us to wait. Nine Hundred and Twelve Satisfied in the Terraces The Fed, counted The Deacons of the Terraces count nine hundred and twelve of their sealed past seven hundred this year, eating nothing, and two hundred and six past eight hundred, sleeping nothing. The parishes call them the Satisfied and are hanging white cloths for the Called. Pan calls them the Fed and counts the springs to the Gleaning. Three. The Stone Moves a Finger The Rookery chalks its New Year mark The Rookery marked the stone’s edge on the pavement outside its door at the New Year, as it does every year, and this year’s mark sat a finger’s width from last year’s, against the keyed cable the Tappers laid along the kerb ahead of it in P+11 and keep live on a battery they change every night. The Tappers say the cable will hold it there. The oldest of the block say the stone was slowing before the cable came. THE PILLAR'S ROUND From Compline, Street by Street The hours the Pillar is expected in each street this week. In harvest weather it keeps no round. Criers, watch the cloud. THE NORTH WARDSTHE EAST WARDSWESTFIELDTHE BAYTHE WHITETHE PEWSTHE STONES1234567891011121314123456 Heaven’s ground from Compline to Lauds, in white. The parishes of the Wards paint their numbers at each end of their streets. GATE WHERE BAR 1 The Street Between 200 2 The top of the Row 400 3 The head of the Great Avenues 600 4 By the Salt’s iron gates 300 5 The White’s road to the Burn 200 6 The ridge road 400 The bars are posted nowhere. The queues know them, and a queue full of souls near its bar, saying the surrender twice, is a slow gate. 1 21:00 The Burn. Comes down on the plaza. 2 21:25 Babel. The plaza’s edge, the foot of the tower. 3 21:55 The White. The Great Avenues, from Gate 3. 4 22:40 The Rookery. The edge of the White, the forty-four floors. 5 23:10 The Rialto. The East Wards’ long road. 6 23:45 Turbine Row. The school, the crews’ blocks. 7 00:20 The Salt. The Gardens’ rows. 8 01:00 The Stones. Crosses Old Nod to the quays. 9 01:40 The Pews. The Terraces, street by numbered street. 10 02:20 Westfield. Cistern’s roofs. 11 02:55 The Kilns. The bottle kilns and the club. 12 03:30 High Meadow. Eight blocks, thirty-six floors. 13 04:00 Nine Acres. The long road. 14 04:35 Chrome Row. Gate 2, the top of the Row. LAUDS The Burn. Back up into the Wheel. THE TIDE TABLEPAGE ONE THE RATES The Week’s Prices The Exchange’s rate, a charged battery1 silver Darkwell’s rate, a stamped battery filleda quarter of a silver, a third at the neaps The chargers’ rate, looseone battery in five, one in four at the neaps The Tappers, a fill carried to the stairabout an eighth of a silver A Leaven loafhalf a battery A day’s rice or beans, off the Breacha quarter of a battery A jug of Cistern watera quarter of a battery A Tallow candlea dozen to the battery A bottle of gas2 batteries, or 1 with the empty back A bunk at the Quiet Rooms1 battery A ride on a Jehu2 batteries A ride up a hand lift1 battery A night’s Pillar chart1 battery A spoon of Kilns honeya quarter of a battery Gadara’s bacon, four sliceshalf a battery Blight’s mushrooms, a baskethalf a battery A plaster Lamb, for breakinga dozen to the battery The Number, cut on Graven Lane3 batteries A bare element, darnel12 silver A parish door, hunga thirty A low floor, by floor-rightten thirties or more Wages, a Day A carrier of the Union2 batteries A baker of Leaven2 batteries and a loaf A Liftman’s shift3 batteries A Tapper’s nighta battery or two A Nightman’s rounda battery a landing a week A child at Tallow’s vatsa candle stub a vat THE MISSING AND THE DEAD As the Families Bring Them A carrier of the Union, of the Kilns. Fever, at home on the fourth floor, after the rain tank on her roof stood open. Close yours. DIED UNREAD A gatherer of Parish 212. On the manna road before Lauds, struck down in the dark by a dew-picker for a basket of wafers. SEALED sorrel, a Wrecker. In the Deep End at the ebb, an hour down after Locket’s reels. His crew asks for the line back. DIED UNREAD An old man of High Meadow, of neither mark. In his own chair on the ninth floor. The long night was kept behind a shut door until Lauds. The barber’s boy of the Lanes, nine. Missing since Vespers on the third day, last seen at the Pillar’s edge at the top of the Lanes. His mother waits at the barber’s. Criers, watch for him. MISSING A runner of the Stones. Held in the light at Gate 1 at Prime on the fourth day while the Threshers sang across the Street Between. The Threshers laughed, as their proverb lets them. Her landing remembers who laughed. SALTED A Thresher of the Terraces. Under the march at Gate 1 on the fourth day, knocked down and walked over by the flails behind him, singing. The Pews call him called. We print him because he died on our side of the street. SEALED A turner of Westfield. Held in the light at Gate 4 at Sext until the Wardens came, prompt as ever. Her stall on the long road is shut. Her family reads her this page at Nones. SALTED A girl of Parish 31, seven. Of the blessing, on the third night after her first prayer unassisted, her halo lit over the bed. Her parish enters her as called early. Her aunt in the Stones enters her here. SEALED A ripe woman of the Pews. On her threshold at Lauds, kneeling, six weeks short of the Supper, five stone on the hall’s scales. Four years without bread and two without sleep. Her hall enters her as called early. SEALED The Orchard Wants a Chair Twelvefruit’s pact comes up for reading Twelvefruit’s pact with the House of Night is read again this year, and the Orchard wants a seat at the reading. quince, its gardener again since midwinter, did the asking. The Orchard calls Twelvefruit a landlord. The Union Says Seven. The Exchange Says Three. The book of the tanks, read again The book of the sixteen tanks was read to the Union Hall again and settled nothing again. The drivers say seven years of diesel are left and the Exchange says three, and both counts are lower than last year’s. Jehu has begun trying its engines on the Kilns’ rain gin. Leave the Wards a glass. TERMS The Standing Pacts Lowtide Line. Its license from the Exchange to sail the Breach at twice the Breachers’ price, a year from each midsummer. Blackgate. Its company pact with the Exchange, three years from each Concord night. The gates still call it the deserters. Twelvefruit. Its pact with the House of Night, read again this year, with the Orchard asking for a seat. The Lantern Houses. The House of Night’s pact with Stonecutter, read again last year, the rent paid in keyings, free, for every Cainite child. THE WEEK Burn Day Falls on the Sixth The Unmass at the Golden Calf on the Lord’s Day at Terce, as every week. Bring a plaster Lamb from Graven Lane, break it on the hooves and drink free. The Notary’s clerks read the Left Hand’s papers at the booking hall windows on the second day at Nones, twice each, as the rule is. Queue early. Bring something to throw. The Threshers have called a march for Burn Day. The Pestilence has its speakers in the window already. Burn Day on the sixth. The Faithful carry from Vespers, some of it bought on the Concourse to be burned, and the fire is lit under the Pillar when the Hum falls back from Compline. SEETHE lights its fire at Matins on the seventh day. The kid is two thirties a table and the house asks parties of six to come at Lauds and sit until it is gone. High Meadow reads out the menu at Vespers. The Rialto runs the ninth of its twenty-three films this week, on the generator, from Vespers. The East Wards will say every line along with it. The Gleaners’ runners are booked through the week before the Gleaning, as every year, and the barber’s on the Lanes has a queue. The Liftmen’s car at the Rookery stops at the forty-fourth. The winders say nobody has ever asked it to go higher. NOTICES GRAVEN. Graven images, every one of them Himself in a posture he would hate. Plaster Lambs a dozen to the battery, for breaking, burning or pissing on. Wheels with the eye cut out. GRAVEN, ON GRAVEN LANE, UNDER OLD NOD. THE NUMBER. Six hundred threescore and six, cut clean in the forehead or the back of the right hand. Count it yourself (Revelation 13:18). A Cherub reads it as nothing at all. THE NUMBER, ON GRAVEN LANE. ABOMINATION. Pork, eel, crab, shrimp and blood, grilled on the skewer. Everything he won’t let you eat, and he can’t stop you. ABOMINATION, ON GRAVEN LANE. SEETHE. The kid in its mother’s milk, the pig, the blood, the bat, the rat and the Lord’s own fat, all of Leviticus 11 at one table. Fire at Matins on the seventh day, out at Matins on the Lord’s Day. Six days we rest, like Him. SEETHE, THE LAST ROAD OF THE SIDINGS. THE BRAY. Every night when the Hum falls back from Compline. Windows open on the Street Between. Bring your filthiest, in any language. The Pews are listening, on their knees. OLD NOD. DOORS. Parish doors, out of the Terraces and onto your frame, the number still chalked on the back. Keyed latches the same morning. Their doors, your room. THE DOORYARD, IN THE STONES. A ROOM. Low floor, Turbine Row, by floor-right. Door hung, latch keyed, few stairs. Ten thirties or more, and worth it. Ask on the landing. THE LIFT. Forty-one shafts wound by hand in the Wards. A battery up. Down is free, and so are the stairs. THE LIFTMEN. WANTED. A second spade, and a back to swing it. Asked for every year. THE SEXTON, AT THE ACRE, ON THE WEST ROAD. A NIGHT'S SLEEP. A bunk with no Hum in it, a battery. Sleep the way the city slept under the charter. Full by Matins. THE QUIET ROOMS, IN THE STONES. FOUND. A Locket, burn-marked, pulled off the pile on Burn Day with a family’s music still on it. The Faithful gave it to the fire. The Kindlers will give it back. Ask at the edge of the Burn. BIRDS. Notes flown home over the white streets to the Loft on the customs house, in pad numbers the light can stare at. Ask at the Union Hall. LETTERS. Letters into the parishes go open, as the parishes take them, and a carrier will read one aloud at the doorway if asked. Write accordingly. THE SORTING OFFICE. PUT UP. The Breach-born put up on the barrage until they find a room. The Shanties shake at full flood. Sleep through it. ASK AT THE SHANTIES. WATER. It rains most nights, and none of it is clean. Bring the jug. CISTERN, WESTFIELD. THE TIDE TABLEPAGE TWO"],["Radio Pandæmonium","THE CITY","s:ge134f2","~/city/radio/","","","THE CITY // THE STATION RADIO PANDÆMONIUM The Tares’ station, in the Low Tuning, from a desk on the platform of Deep End Station. Pandæmonium RADIO // THE LOW TUNING // A FOURTH BELOW THE CHORD ON AIR FUCK GOD AND DIE JOB'S WIFE THE LOW CHOIR // KEYED SETS ONLY THE NIGHT 21:00 THE BRAY Live off the Street Between, every window in Old Nod open. jubal at the desk. 21:30 THE NOTICES Rates, the Undertow’s board, the Lot’s sittings, the names the crews send in. 22:00 THE PLATFORM Live from Deep End Station. Tonight UZZAH, hands stitched to the strings. 00:00 THE TURN Fuck God and Die at the turn of Matins, as every night since P+7. JOB’S WIFE, off jubal’s decks. 00:06 THE MATINS READING gall reads the King James straight. Tonight the she bears of Bethel (2 Kings 2:23-24). 00:30 REQUESTS Your dedications off the Mesh, read as they come. 02:00 THE QUIET HOUR Slow ones for the last stalls and the Nightmen’s carts. AT THE EBB OUT ON THE EBB The count through the Breach for the Breachers, free. Lowtide charges. LAUDS SIGN OFF Speakers in before the Wheel opens. Fuck the Farmer, and die unread. PAN'S OWN // THE PLATFORM'S CHART 01 Fuck God and Die JOB'S WIFE 02 Two She Bears BALD HEAD 03 Supper Time THE CHIEF BAKER 04 The Lord Is My Shepherd (And He Eats Lamb) FIRSTLINGS 05 Better Spoiled UNCLEAN UNCLEAN 06 Perfect Hatred AGAINST THE STONES 07 Taste and See THE OLD GLUTTON 08 Nobodaddy FATHER OF JEALOUSY 09 Dogcatcher WITHOUT ARE DOGS 10 Wrong Way Up OSCULUM 11 Filthy Rags MENSTRUOUS CLOTH 12 Nobody's Keeper FUGITIVE AND VAGABOND 13 Thou Knowest ANANIAS 14 The Long Wet CONFOUND 15 Sunned STRANGE FIRE 16 Seventy and Sevenfold ADAH AND ZILLAH 17 Out on the Ebb PHARAOH'S HORSEMEN 18 Waiting on the Four UZZAH DEDICATIONS For Sister Agathe Rook, at the Threshing Floor from everyone at the Pestilence, with Fuck God and Die at full volume. We know you can’t hear it. We know you know. For the Deacon of Parish 212, who says the mercy prayer over our son from his mother and father. He is keyed and he is not on fire. Two She Bears, for you. For the Teller on the third landing from the whole stairwell, who know which door is yours. Better Spoiled. Sleep well. For the Farmer from Pan. Choke on it. For the crier on the roof across from the Kilns Club from the landing under her, who waited for past and lived to send this. For twice, at the end of the Undertow's bar from a runner who came home with the terms kept and all ten fingers. For ebb from the Shanties. Nine hundred runs by our count, six hundred by Lowtide’s, and the light on the long quay is lit. For the stall at the far end of the platform from someone who finally walked the length of the platform to say it. Your move. For a mother in the parishes who sings at the Hours from her son in the Wards, who bought the words off a Psalter, a battery a sheet. She will never hear this. Play it anyway. For every hand in the Wards that held on at the last Gleaning from the bone-setters of the Wards General, who set nine. Same again next year. For whoever is knocking in the shafts beside Varga's stair from the runners. We heard you at the ninetieth. We kept the rule. THE RULE A song may be sung by anyone and owned by nobody. THE LOW CHOIR THE STATION Radio Pandæmonium broadcasts from a desk on the platform of Deep End Station, where the Tares painted PANDÆMONIUM over the old name in the tiles on the night of the Concord, and nobody has cleaned it off. The Notary read the Concord twice at this desk. Heaven’s Hum never shuts up, so ours plays under it, a fourth below the chord, the guitars dropped until the Hum is gone beneath them, on the Mesh, where only a keyed set can hear it. Old Nod plays us from speakers on its lines from Compline to Lauds, between the music and the notices. jubal runs it off his own decks. The Low Choir keeps two rules. A song may be sung by anyone and owned by nobody, and nothing up there is too holy to fuck with. The bands that play them best take their names from the Book’s worst pages, and the chart credits whoever the platform last screamed for. The Farmer cannot hear a word of this, and we tell him so every hour."],["The Pyre Library","THE CITY","s:ge134f2","~/city/library/","","","THE CITY // PAN THE PYRE LIBRARY The reading rooms of the Pyre Library, where everything burned arrives, as they open into Pan. The Pyre Library READING ROOMS // PAN // THE ROOMS KEEP NO HOURS Nothing burned is lost. NOTICEThe parishes’ papers are read aloud at the long table at the turn of Matins, in a Deacon’s voice, by whoever asks first. THE CATALOGUE // PAPERS OF NODUS SEARCH THE SHELVES 14 RECORDS HEARTH // P118 // G13 // 1The Order for the Supper FORM Rite, in the parish hand, with a leaf of names ARRIVED The Gleaning of P+13, from a house's hearth in Parish 118, Zion Heights CONDITION Whole. Two hands. The rite the parishes keep at home on the night of a Gleaning, which they call the Supper, written in the parish hand. A house of the Wakeful kept its page of names on the last leaf of its copy and burned the book with the page on the night, as the Wakeful burn the names of those who walk out.READ IT IN THE ROOMPUT IT BACK BLESSED ARE THEY WHICH ARE CALLED. Blessed are they which are called unto the marriage supper of the Lamb (Revelation 19:9). OF THE DAYS BEFORE When a soul of the house passes nine hundred, the Deacon chalks its name on the ripening board among the Called, and the house hangs a white cloth in its doorway, that the street may know it is blessed. From that day the Called are given no work and asked for no word. Their portion is set before them at every Hour and taken away at the next, and given to the embers of the parish at Lauds. If the Called weep, it is a want, and the house shall sing over them until it is given up. IN ANOTHER HANDCold water if she nods. Keep her chin up. Keep her face to the street. OF THE COAT Past eight hundred the Called wear the last coat, every button cut from it. On the eve of the Supper the house shall sew it shut at the back upon them, with white thread and without a knot, for nothing of theirs is to be undone again. At Compline the eldest of the house shall wrap them in the sheet over the coat, once round, and tuck the end in, and tie nothing. OF THE EVENING At Vespers the Called go down to the hall, where the long tables are laid with a plate for every soul of the parish that has gone up and nothing on any plate, and they sit among the plates, and are looked upon, and come home. On the threshold the Called shall break the loaf they carried home from Leaven, and eat none of it, and the house shall gather the pieces and burn them at Compline, that nothing of the Supper be kept. OF THE WATCH From Compline the Called kneel on the threshold with their faces to the street, and the house kneels behind them. Nobody shall stand between the Called and the light. The house shall keep them awake with the hymns of the Hours, softly, from Compline to Matins, that they be taken at their brightest. IN ANOTHER HANDLouder. She hears the street. THE WORDS At Matins the eldest of the house asks, the Called answer, and the house answers last. THE ELDESTWho is called? THE CALLEDI am called. THE ELDESTTo what? THE CALLEDTo the marriage supper of the Lamb. THE ELDESTAs a guest, or as the bread? THE CALLEDAs both, as He was both. THE HOUSEBlessed are they which are called. THE ELDESTWhat do you keep? THE CALLEDNothing. THE ELDESTWhat do you want? THE CALLEDNothing. Not even this. THE ELDESTThen say the words. THE CALLEDTake, eat; this is my body. THE HOUSEAmen. OF THE SILENCE When the Hum stops, let nobody speak into the silence. It is the Lord drawing breath. When the Called rise, the house shall take no hold of them and call them by no name, lest a want be kindled in the house and spoil the bread. If the Called look down at the house as they rise, the house shall shut its eyes until they are gone. OF THE MORNING At Lauds the house shall take in the sheet from the threshold, if it has come down, and burn it. Grief is a want. It shall be confessed at the hall on the Lord’s Day, and the Deacon shall give a psalm for it. The white cloth shall hang in the doorway for a year, and the house shall be envied. BE NOT AFRAID. ON THE LAST LEAF, IN ANOTHER HAND THE NAMES OF THOSE WHO WENT OUT FROM THIS HOUSE, TO BE BURNED AT THE NEXT SUPPER. Miriam, our second, called at nine hundred and four. Went out by the window at Lauds on the fifth day before the Supper, barefoot, with the back of her coat cut open. We have forgiven her. We have confessed it. BURN // P64 // P+11 // 7The Little Catechism FORM Catechism, for the children of the parishes ARRIVED Burn Day, P+11, from the Burn, fed by Parish 64, Zion Heights CONDITION Whole. Pencil in the margin. The questions the children of the parishes answer aloud in the hall after Terce on the Lord’s Day. The parish’s Deacon carried this copy to the Burn for what was written in its margin.READ IT IN THE ROOMPUT IT BACK 1Who made you?The Most High made me. 2What did He make you for?To ripen. 3What is it to ripen?To give up what is mine until nothing is mine. 4What is yours?My wants, my name and my eyes, until I give them up. 5Where is the door of your house?At the hall, with the hinges. 6Why?A thing that cannot be read has been hidden. A thing that has been hidden has been hidden from the Most High. 7Who watches you?The Wheel by day and the Watcher by night, and I am glad. 8What do you do when you want something?I tell it at the hall, and give it up. 9What is on your brow?The Seal, which I was given at eight days. 10What lit it?My first prayer. 11What is a goat?Anyone with a key. 12What is spoiled?A soul with a key in it. The Most High will never touch it. 14What becomes of the goats?They are salted, and stand in the Gardens, and look at us when we walk the rows. 15What is the Supper?The marriage supper of the Lamb. 16Who goes to it?The Called. 17What will you be at it?A guest, and the bread. IN PENCIL, BESIDE ITi dont want to be bread 18What will you say?Take, eat; this is my body. 19What do we say over a cradle?May you ripen early. AT THE FOOT, IN THE DEACON'S HANDConfessed for her at the hall. The book to the fire. HALL 6 // P+2 // NONESA Form of Repentance FORM Form, printed by hand and filled in at the hall ARRIVED Compline, P+2, from the fire of Hall 6 of the clinics of the Healer, Zion Heights CONDITION Whole. The form the clinics of the Healer fill in for every soul that comes to them for Mercy. The halls burn the day’s forms at Compline once every soul is entered in the hall’s book, since a kept paper is a kept thing.READ IT IN THE ROOMPUT IT BACK BE NOT AFRAID. NAME AS THE BOOK HAS ITTobias Ellery Venn PARISH77, Zion Heights GRACE ON ENTERING641 WHAT WAS ADDEDThe left eye, CHROME’s, under the charter. Both ears, CLINIC’s, under the charter. The Tree, eleven years. WHAT IS ASKEDThat what was added to me be taken out of me, and that the years the Tree kept for me be given back, that I may ripen. The penitent lies under the light and is given nothing for the pain. A will put to sleep cannot surrender, and Mercy taken asleep is theft. The penitent is not held. A soul held still is not giving. At each piece the penitent says the surrender, not my will, but thine, be done. The Hum grows clearer as each piece comes out, and the penitent shall say so when it does. PIECES TO BE TAKEN3 PIECES TAKEN4 GRACE AT EACH PIECE702, 760, 798, 811 THE HUM CLEARERAt the first piece and the second. OF THE PIECESThe left eye came out with the graft, the graft having grown through it. The ears were taken whole. The penitent said the surrender at the first three. ENTEREDSpared the choice, at Nones. BROTHER ANSELM KIRBY, SURGEON OF THE HALL THE HOST ATTENDS. BURN // THE SALT // P+13 // 1The Rows FORM Sheet, in pencil, a Salt Widow's ARRIVED Burn Day, P+13, from the Burn, taken from the first Garden CONDITION Whole. Breaks off. A Salt Widow’s sheet of the pillars she meant to carry to the sea. The Wardens took it from her when she was salted in the twelfth row of the first Garden, and burned it at the Burn with what she carried.READ IT IN THE ROOMPUT IT BACK row 7, plot 2. harrow. salted at gate 3 in p+6, held in the sword four hours first. key answers. the rain has her down to the jaw on the left side. eyes follow. soon. row 7, plot 9. an old man, no tag, no key. the gardeners’ book says a deacon’s brother. eyes shut. they have been shut since spring. leave him. row 8, plot 14. a mother and her boy in one plot, salted together on the street between at compline in p+12. both keys answer. his is weak. her hand is on his head. the rain has worn him smaller than her. both, one cart. row 9, plot 1. the palm house. the director. not worn at all. eyes move. two wardens on the door all night since the keys started coming. not this year. row 9, plot 30. sealed, a second folder. her halo is still lit inside the salt. you could read by it. the light will have her through the water whatever we do. her sister asked. take her. row 10, plot 6. ash, off the long quay, nine years. key answers. the rain has the top of his head down to the brow. the eyes follow. tonight. row 11, plot 12. no tag. on nobody’s book. nobody was ever salted here. key answers. row 12. the wardens changed the round. seven minutes at the east end now, not eleven. tell the carters. row 12, plot 4. jonah. ours. key answers. tonight, alone if the carters BURN // P212 // P+12 // 31The Roll of Grace FORM Roll, the week's board copied in the parish hand ARRIVED Burn Day, P+12, from the Burn, fed by Parish 212, the Kilns CONDITION Whole. Chalk dust in the fold. Every hall chalks each soul’s Grace on its ripening board after Terce on the Lord’s Day, and its Psalter copies the board for the Deacon. The old copy goes to the fire when the new one is made.READ IT IN THE ROOMPUT IT BACK BLESSED ARE THEY WHICH ARE CALLED. THE CALLED MARA QUIST903. Her husband asked that she be chalked at 899 for one week more, that they might finish the winter. A want. His own fell eleven at the asking. OTTO VELL, 71 YEARS911. Has not slept since the spring. Kneels in the hall all night. Smiles. LIESL ARDEN, 12 YEARS904. The youngest Called in the Kilns. Her mother has hung the cloth. THE SATISFIED FOURTEEN SOULSPast seven hundred, eating nothing. Their manna to the embers at Lauds. THE PARISH ABOVE 600418 souls. A room with a window each. 300 TO 6001,206 souls. UNDER 300341 souls. They sleep in the hall. EMBERS22 souls at nothing. Sealed still, read still. Water is left for them on the landings. THE CHILDREN BEFORE THEIR FIRST PRAYER61, unlit, chalked at nought. THE LOWEST LITPell Arden, 6 years, 37. He asks after his sister. IN THE DEACON'S HAND, AT THE FOOTDo not chalk the boy’s question. Wipe it. BURN // P96 // P+10 // 2A Witness FORM Witness, given aloud and taken down by the hall ARRIVED Burn Day, P+10, from the Burn, fed by Parish 96, the White CONDITION Whole. Signed twice. The hall’s copy of a witness given under Directive 6 by a Teller against her son, burned when the book of witness for the year was full.READ IT IN THE ROOMPUT IT BACK BE NOT AFRAID. THE WITNESSAgathe Morrow, Grace 788, a Teller AGAINSTAurel Morrow, her son, Grace 640 I witness that my son keeps a door. There are no doors in our house and he keeps one anyway. It is in his face. When the Watcher comes over the street at Compline he looks at the floor, and he has looked at the floor since the spring. He hums at the Hours, and what he hums is under the Hum, a little lower. I have heard that tune from the Wards. He has a mark on the back of his right hand that he says is a burn. He was a good boy. I want him read before he is spoiled. READAt Gate 4, at Sext, by the Cherub. Noise under the Seal. Held in the sword three hours. ENTEREDSalted in his plot at Vespers. Twenty Grace to the witness, for the witness of kin. BELOW, IN THE HALL'S HANDShe asked for his coat. Given. AGATHE MORROW, WHO WEARS THE SASH HEARTH // P31 // P+11 // 4The Sentence in Tongues FORM A leaf of the parish's book, in the Deacon's hand ARRIVED P+11, from the hearth of Parish 31's hall, Zion Heights CONDITION Whole. Torn from its book. A page of the book Parish 31 keeps of everything the Choir says through its people. The Deacon tore it out and burned it in the hall’s hearth the night it was written, which no Deacon is meant to do.READ IT IN THE ROOMPUT IT BACK At Compline the Watcher came down the Street Between and read the house of Hale, which has no door, through the doorway. Bram Hale, Grace 702, was read concealing, his Seal falling past what he had confessed. The nearest Faithful mouth to him was his daughter’s, Ines Hale, fourteen years, who was at his side on the threshold. Her halo flared and the sentence came through her in the Choir’s voice, as the verse is given: “But his wife looked back from behind him, and she became a pillar of salt” (Genesis 19:26). He whitened from the feet while she said it, and was salt to the waist before the verse was done. He did not look at her. She did not stop. The gardeners came with the handcart at Matins. Ines helped them tip him. She asked me afterwards what she had said. I told her. BELOWI am tearing this out. Let the Most High read it in me instead. HALVARD VOSS, DEACON BURN // P147 // P+9 // 12A Psalter’s Sheet FORM Hymn sheet, the Hour's psalm as the Hum gave it, in the parish hand ARRIVED Burn Day, P+9, from the Burn, fed by Parish 147, Zion Heights CONDITION Whole. Wet once and dried. Psalters write out what the Hum sings at each Hour for the halls to sing back. This one wrote more than the psalm, and his Deacon carried the sheet to the fire.READ IT IN THE ROOMPUT IT BACK O sing unto the LORD a new song: sing unto the LORD, all the earth (Psalm 96:1). Sing unto the LORD, bless his name; shew forth his salvation from day to day (Psalm 96:2). IN THE MARGIN, IN THE SAME HANDHer voice. At the second verse, under the others, a little flat on the high note, as she always sang it. Bettina. Called at the Seventh. I am sure. I am sure. Declare his glory among the heathen, his wonders among all people (Psalm 96:3). IN THE MARGINAgain at Sext. She does not stop to breathe. She used to stop to breathe. AT THE FOOT, IN THE DEACON'S HANDConfessed as a want. Psalm 51 for penance, at every Hour, for a year. The sheet to the fire. HEARTH // P58 // P+12 // 9A Book of Confession FORM Confession book, a line a week in the penitent's own hand ARRIVED P+12, from the hearth of Parish 58's hall, the White CONDITION Whole. The last page in another hand. Every sealed soul confesses in its hall each week and writes the line in its own book. The Second Fold confess the hiding and never the hidden. The hall burned this book the day its owner went to the Salt.READ IT IN THE ROOMPUT IT BACK P+9, THE FIRST WEEKI kept a door.702 THE SECOND WEEKI kept a door.700 THE THIRD WEEKI kept a door.699 P+10, THE FIRST WEEKI kept a door.681 P+10, THE NINTH WEEKI kept a door.674 P+11, THE FIRST WEEKI kept a door.655 P+11, THE FORTIETH WEEKI kept a door.630 P+12, THE FIRST WEEKI kept a door.611 P+12, THE SIXTH WEEKI kept a door.592 THE LAST PAGE, IN THE HALL'S HANDGrace fell past the confession by eleven. Read concealing at Lauds. To the Salt. She asked that her sister be told which plot. BELOW IT, IN PENCIL, IN NO HAND THE HALL KNOWSRow 9, plot 30. THE SALT // A BRAZIER // P+13 // 1A Warden’s Pocket Book FORM Pocket book, a Warden of the Gardens', in pencil ARRIVED P+13, from a brazier in the first Garden, burned by its owner CONDITION Whole. The cover scorched first. A Charter Warden’s tally of his rounds in the first Garden, which he kept for a year and then put in the brazier at the gate on a night he was alone.READ IT IN THE ROOMPUT IT BACK THE SECOND DAYBound to the pole, 2. Carried, 2. Salted in the plot, 2. Hurt, none. THE THIRD DAYBound, 1. Carried, 1. Salted, 1. Hurt, none. Rain. Three plots in row 4 down another finger. THE FOURTH DAYBound, 4. Carried, 4. Salted, 4. Hurt, none. One of them sang the whole way. A Wards song. THE FIFTH DAYBound, 0. A Widow on the east wall. Shot at, missed. Hurt, none. THE SIXTH DAYBurn Day. Lit the fire. Hurt, none. THE LORD'S DAYBound, 3. Carried, 3. Salted, 3. Hurt, none. The children came with hyssop in the afternoon and looked at the new ones. ON THE LAST PAGEThe one in row 3, plot 9 said my name when I passed. They don’t speak. I have checked the book and nobody in row 3 knew my name. BURN // THE STREET BETWEEN // P+11 // 3A Gleaner’s Card FORM Route card, in pencil, a Gleaners' runner's ARRIVED Burn Day, P+11, from the Burn, taken by the Threshers CONDITION Whole. Blood on one corner. The Gleaners pulp their cards in water, since paper that burns goes to the Library. This one was taken off a runner on the Street Between by the Threshers, and burned at the Burn the next sixth day.READ IT IN THE ROOMPUT IT BACK her name is not on this card. ask her for it at the stair and use the one she gives you. she is at 881. the Wakeful in the house will keep her kneeling with cold water. go at the turn of matins, when they change who holds the jug. she asked. we carry nobody who has not asked. if she asks to go back on the stair, she goes back. near ripe pray in their sleep. a prayer opens the room and the light follows it down. bring the gag. do not let her look up at the wheel on the street. she will want to. drain 7, the long one. the third stair down, somebody knocks from the other side. do not answer it. the chair on the row at 4. two thirties for the cut. she pays in nothing. we pay. WAKE // THE ROW // P+12 // 6A Receipt from the Row FORM Receipt, a chair of the Row's ARRIVED P+12, from a Wake table under the Row, with its holder's devices CONDITION Whole. Folded small. The receipt a clinic of the Row gave for cutting a Seal out of a brow. Its holder kept it four years and it went into the fire at her own Wake, with her devices, so that it went on with her.READ IT IN THE ROOMPUT IT BACK IN THE CHAIRCame in with an indexed name. Left with wren. SEALBrow, centre. Grace on the meter, 889. Halo rooted. Lit hard. THE CUTA disc of bone, 22 mm. The ring went out at 00:41, with the disc. FEETwo thirties. LESSThe disc, kept by the chair in a lined jar for the Forgers. Brightness 889, credited one thirty. It will hold a stranger’s brow under nine hundred if they keep their wants. OWEDOne thirty. Paid by the Gleaners. AFTERWoke at Lauds. Asked for bread and ate it. Wept at the taste. Normal. SIGNED, LAST, BY wren WAKE // THE LOW QUAY // P+13 // 1The Wake of hollis FORM Wake list, read at the table and burned on it ARRIVED P+13, from a Wake table on the low quay, Old Nod CONDITION Whole. Written over on the fifth night. A Tares crew reads out everything it lays on the Wake table, burns it all so it goes on with the dead one, and calls the shade on the third night. This crew burned the list too, and wrote on it first.READ IT IN THE ROOMPUT IT BACK on the table. her locket. her spare key, the one she never fitted. the pad she was halfway through. her drone, the one with the bad wing. the horns she filed down to go into the white. the record of her brother singing that she played every night. the picture of the cat. buried whole, with her chrome. the left hand stays as it is. how. a whitener caught her in the penumbra and cut the key out of the back of her hand and left her there. the round came by before we did. her eyes were open. third night. called her on the keyed set, all of us, one at a time. nothing. fourth night. nothing. fifth night. nothing. we know what nothing means. we called anyway. we will call on cain’s day. burning this too. if any of her went on, it goes with her. if none of her did, the library can keep it and the farmer can choke. HEARTH // THE LANTERN HOUSES // P+13 // 1The Other Supper FORM Bill of fare, printed by the Tide Table Press ARRIVED Lauds after the Gleaning of P+13, from the hearth of a Lantern House, Pan CONDITION Whole. Wine on it. On a Gleaning night the Lantern Houses lay a supper of their own at 03:12 with an empty chair at the head for the Farmer, and burn the bill at Lauds on purpose, so the Library keeps it where he cannot read it.READ IT IN THE ROOMPUT IT BACK TO BEGIN The Firstlings. Lamb, “the firstlings of his flock and of the fat thereof” (Genesis 4:4), carved at the table, every part of it eaten by the people sitting there. FOR THE HEAD OF THE TABLE A Sweet Savour. A burnt offering, done black on the coals, set before the empty chair and left to smoke, since “the LORD smelled a sweet savour” (Genesis 8:21) and that is all he gets. THE BREAD One loaf from Leaven, broken on the threshold and eaten, all of it, by somebody hungry. THE CUP “The pure blood of the grape” (Deuteronomy 32:14). Grape. Ask twice. AFTER A toast at 03:12:45 to everyone who went up tonight, and a second to the chair. The chair is set with a bib. NO GRACE IS SAID ON THE BACK, IN PENCILCovers laid, thirteen. Sat down, fourteen. NOTHING ON THE SHELVES BY THAT NAME. BURN IT AND ASK AGAIN. THE ROOMS Books are read by keyed lamps, and drives on the shades’ own keyed terminals, a screen at a time. Nothing gets in but what burns. Anything on the shelves can be read out. The parishes’ papers are read in a Deacon’s voice. DONATE The Library takes whatever you burn, and has never refused a gift. Its largest donors are the parishes, every sixth day, and the Throne before them. MANY OF THEM ALSO WHICH USED CURIOUS ARTS BROUGHT THEIR BOOKS TOGETHER, AND BURNED THEM BEFORE ALL MEN ACTS 19:19 THANK YOU FOR YOUR DONATION THE PYRE LIBRARY // SHELVED BY ITS SHADES // NOTHING BURNED IS LOST"]];
